fn documentChunk(j: usize) -> StringView => match (j) {
    0 =>
        "# Async tasks\n\nA program that waits for many things at once (timers, sockets, files, other tasks) can run them as **tasks** on one\nthread instead of one thread each. An `async fn` is a function whose call is a task; `await` runs a task and gives its\nresult; while it waits, other tasks of the same thread run. CeeSafe's tasks are **structured**: every task a program\nstarts has returned before `Async.run` returns, so a task may borrow its caller's locals, just as a function call may.\n\n## Async functions and `await`\n\n```ceesafe\nasync fn add(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 => a + b;\n\nasync fn checked(x: i32) -> Result<i32, StringView> {\n    let y = await add(x, x);\n    if (y > 100) {\n        return Err(\"too big\");\n    }\n    return Ok(y);\n}\n\nasync fn total(values: Slice<i32>) -> i32 {\n    var sum = 0;\n    for (v in values) {\n        sum = await add(sum, v);\n    }\n    return sum;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let values = [1, 2, 3];\n    Console.writeLine(Async.run(total(values[..])));\n    match (Async.run(checked(20))) {\n        Ok(v) => { Console.writeLine(v); }\n        Err(e) => { Console.writeLine(e); }\n    }\n    match (Async.run(checked(60))) {\n        Ok(v) => { Console.writeLine(v); }\n        Err(e) => { Console.writeLine(e); }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```text\n6\n40\ntoo big\n```\n\n`Async.run(task)` runs a task on this thread until it returns, from ordinary (sync) code. Inside an async fn, `await`\ndoes the same, and `?` works on an awaited `Result` as on any other. A call of an async fn does nothing by itself: it\nmust be awaited, run or spawned. There is no `Task` or `Future` value to store or return, so a task never outlives the\ncode that started it:\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0342 -->\n```ceesafe\nasync fn add(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 => a + b;\n\nfn main() {\n    let pending = add(1, 2);\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:4:19: error[N0342]: a call of the async fn 'add' is a task: await it, or pass it to 'Async.run' (type-system.md §40.1)\n  |\n4 |     let pending = add(1, 2);\n  |                   ^^^^^^^^^\n```\n\n`await` is allowed only in an async fn or an async closure (N0341). An async fn may await itself, directly, through\nother async fns, or through `dyn` (middleware wrapping middleware): each await on such a cycle takes its callee's frame\nfrom the task's own frame stack, reused from task to task, so recursion is written as in sync code. Running out of\nmemory for it stops the program, as a deep sync recursion does; `Async.boxed(alloc, f(…))` gives a call a frame from an\nallocator instead, with the failure as a value. In freestanding code, which has no frame stack, such recursion is N0343\nand `Async.boxed` is the way.\n\n## Running tasks at once: scopes\n\n`Async.scope` starts **children** that run while the scope waits, and returns once every child has. A child is an\nasync closure, and it may borrow the awaiting function's locals, because the scope cannot return before it does:\n\n```ceesafe\nenum Failure {\n    OutOfMemory,\n    Bad,\n}\n\nasync fn pause(ms: u64) -> Result<void, Failure> {\n    match (await Timer.sleep(ms)) {\n        Ok() => { return Ok(); }\n        Err(_) => { return Err(Failure.Bad); }\n    }\n}\n\nasync fn say(text: StringView) -> Result<void, Failure> {\n    match (await AsyncConsole.writeLine(text)) {\n        Ok() => { return Ok(); }\n        Err(_) => { return Err(Failure.Bad); }\n    }\n}\n\nasync fn gather() -> Result<u32, Failure> {\n    var slow: u32 = 0;\n    var fast: u32 = 0;\n    await Async.scope(Failure.OutOfMemory, (s) => {\n        s.spawn(async () => {\n            await pause(30)?;\n            await say(\"slow done\")?;\n            slow = 100;\n            return Ok();\n        });\n        s.spawn(async () => {\n            await pause(10)?;\n            await say(\"fast done\")?;\n            fast = 20;\n            return Ok();\n        });\n    })?;\n    return Ok(slow + fast); // both children have returned\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    match (Async.run(gather())) {\n        Ok(v) => { Console.writeLine(v); }\n        Err(_) => { Console.writeLine(\"failed\"); }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```text\nfast don",
    1 =>
        "e\nslow done\n120\n```\n\nThe two children wait at the same time, so the whole takes about 30 ms, not 40. They write with `AsyncConsole`:\n`Console.writeLine` blocks its thread, which async code may not do (N0295, below). The rules that keep this safe are the\nborrow checker's:\n\n- two children cannot both capture a local that either changes (N0241), and the scope's body cannot use a local a\n  child has captured until the scope returns;\n- a child cannot borrow a local of the scope's body, which ends before the child may (N0344); a loop that spawns a\n  child per item copies what each child reads, or moves it in with `async move () => …`;\n- every child returns `Result<void, E>` with one `E`: the first `Err` cancels the other children and is the scope's\n  result, after all of them have returned. `oom`, the scope's first argument, is the error if a child's frame cannot\n  be allocated.\n\n`Async.scopeIn(alloc, oom, body)` takes the children's frames from an allocator of your choice; `Async.scope` uses the\nsystem heap.\n\n## Starting tasks as you go: task sets\n\nA scope's children are started in its body, from the variables in reach. A server starts a task per connection as\nconnections come, from a loop that may be in another function. That is a **task set**:\n\n```ceesafe\nenum WorkError {\n    Failed(u32),\n    NoMemory,\n}\n\nasync fn work(n: u32) -> Result<void, WorkError> {\n    match (await Timer.sleep(1)) {\n        Ok() => {}\n        Err(_) => { return Err(WorkError.Failed(n)); }\n    }\n    return if (n == 3) Err(WorkError.Failed(n)) else Ok();\n}\n\n/// Starts five tasks through the set it is given; at most two run at once.\nasync fn startAll(set: borrow TaskSet<WorkError>) -> Result<void, WorkError> {\n    for (n in 0..5u32) {\n        await set.spawn(async move () => await work(n))?;\n    }\n    return Ok();\n}\n\nasync fn run() -> Result<void, WorkError> {\n    return await Async.tasks(2, WorkError.NoMemory, async (set) => await startAll(set));\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    match (Async.run(run())) {\n        Ok() => { Console.writeLine(\"all done\"); }\n        Err(WorkError.Failed(n)) => {\n            Console.write(\"task \");\n            Console.write(n);\n            Console.writeLine(\" failed; the others ran to the end\");\n        }\n        Err(WorkError.NoMemory) => { Console.writeLine(\"no memory\"); }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```text\ntask 3 failed; the others ran to the end\n```\n\n- `Async.tasks(limit, oom, body)` runs `body` with the set, then waits for every task it started. The set's error type\n  is `oom`'s, and must be `Copy`.\n- `await set.spawn(async move () => …)` waits while `limit` tasks run; `set.trySpawn(…)` is `false` instead.\n- One task's error does not stop the others: the set reports the first. The body's error cancels them all.\n- A task may outlive the function that started it, so it owns what it captures: no references or views (N0349).\n  Share through `Arc` or a `Channel`, or pass values in.\n\n## Racing and joining two tasks\n\n`Async.race(a, b)` runs two async closures and keeps the first to finish; `Async.all(a, b)` runs both and fails with\nthe first error. Neither allocates: their children's frames are in the caller's.\n\n```ceesafe\nasync fn after(ms: u64, v: u32) -> Result<u32, AsyncError> {\n    await Timer.sleep(ms)?;\n    return Ok(v);\n}\n\nasync fn fastest() -> Result<u32, AsyncError> {\n    let r: Result<Raced<u32>, AsyncError> = await Async.race(\n        async () => await after(30, 1),\n        async () => await after(5, 2),\n    );\n    let raced = r?;\n    return Ok(raced.winner * 10 + raced.value); // the second child (winner 1) returned 2\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    match (Async.run(fastest())) {\n        Ok(v) => { Console.writeLine(v); }\n        Err(_) => { Console.writeLine(\"failed\"); }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```text\n12\n```\n\nThe loser is cancelled and awaited before `race` returns; if it also returned `Ok`, `raced.other` holds its value.\n\n## Cancellation\n\nA task is cancelled when a sibling fails, when it loses a race, or when the task that owns its scope is cancelled.\nCancelli",
    2 =>
        "ng only sets a flag: the task's next waiting operation (a sleep, a read, a channel receive) returns\n`Err(AsyncError.Cancelled)` at once, and the task returns through its own `?`s, dropping its locals as any function\ndoes. Code between two `await`s is never interrupted. A long computation can look with `Async.isCancelled()` or\n`Async.checkpoint(error)`.\n\n```ceesafe\nasync fn work() -> Result<u32, AsyncError> {\n    var saw: u32 = 0;\n    let outcome = await Async.scope(AsyncError.Cancelled, (s) => {\n        s.spawn(async () => {\n            await Timer.sleep(5)?;\n            return Err(AsyncError.TimedOut); // fails first, so its sibling is cancelled\n        });\n        s.spawn(async () => {\n            match (await Timer.sleep(1000)) {\n                Ok() => { saw = 1; }\n                Err(AsyncError.Cancelled) => { saw = 2; }\n                Err(_) => { saw = 3; }\n            }\n            return Ok();\n        });\n    });\n    match (outcome) {\n        Err(AsyncError.TimedOut) => { return Ok(saw); }\n        _ => { return Ok(0); }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    match (Async.run(work())) {\n        Ok(v) => { Console.writeLine(v); }\n        Err(_) => { Console.writeLine(\"failed\"); }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```text\n2\n```\n\n`Async.shield(ms, body)` runs cleanup that must finish: inside it the flag is ignored, for at most `ms` milliseconds.\n\n## Deadlines\n\n`Async.timeout(ms, timedOut, body)` runs `body` with a deadline. If the body has not returned after `ms`\nmilliseconds, it is cancelled and awaited, and the result is `Err(timedOut)`, an error of the body's own type.\n`Async.deadline(at, timedOut, body)` is the same with a time on `Timer.now()`'s clock. Neither allocates.\n\n```ceesafe\nenum FetchError {\n    TimedOut,\n    Async(AsyncError),\n}\n\nasync fn after(ms: u64, v: u32) -> Result<u32, FetchError> {\n    match (await Timer.sleep(ms)) {\n        Ok() => { return Ok(v); }\n        Err(e) => { return Err(FetchError.Async(e)); }\n    }\n}\n\nasync fn fetch() -> Result<u32, FetchError> {\n    let quick = await Async.timeout(50, FetchError.TimedOut, async () => await after(5, 1))?;\n    match (await Async.timeout(5, FetchError.TimedOut, async () => await after(50, 2))) {\n        Ok(v) => { return Ok(quick * 10 + v); }\n        Err(FetchError.TimedOut) => { return Ok(quick * 10 + 9); } // the slow one ran out of time\n        Err(e) => { return Err(e); }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    match (Async.run(fetch())) {\n        Ok(v) => { Console.writeLine(v); }\n        Err(_) => { Console.writeLine(\"failed\"); }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```text\n19\n```\n\nA body that returned `Ok` before it saw the cancellation keeps its value. If the caller itself is cancelled, the\nresult is the body's own error, never `timedOut`: a timeout reports only its own deadline.\n\n## Waiting for the outside world\n\nThe library's waiting operations are async functions, in the hosted profile:\n\n| What | Operations |\n|---|---|\n| Time | `Timer.sleep(ms)`; `Timer.now()` (not async), a monotonic clock for waits and measurements; `Clock.now()`, the date as a `UnixTime` (seconds and nanoseconds since 1970), which may jump |\n| Random bytes | `Random.fill(bytes)` (not async, and cheap enough per message): bytes from the system's cryptographic source, for keys, masks and tokens |\n| Console | `AsyncConsole.write(text)`, `writeLine(text)`, `writeError(text)` |\n| Files | `AsyncFiles.open`, `create`, `read`, `write` (each on a worker thread while the task waits) |\n| Sockets | `AsyncTcpListener.bind` or `bindWith(address, options)` (not async), `accept`; `AsyncTcpStream.connect` or `connectTo(address)`, `read`, `write`, `writeVectored(parts)` and `writeAllVectored(parts)` (an `IoParts`, up to eight byte parts sent in one call); `sendFile(file, offset, length)` and `sendFileAll(file, offset, length)`, a file's bytes sent by the kernel where it can (`sendfile`, `TransmitFile`), the file's position untouched; `AsyncUdpSocket.bind(address, options)`, `sendTo`, `recvFrom`, and `sendMsg`/`recvMsg` with the local address, ECN bits, truncation an",
    3 =>
        "d segments; `AsyncDns.resolve(host, port)`, a name's addresses, cancellable; the options (`setNoDelay`, `setKeepAlive`, buffers, `setLinger`) and `peerAddress`/`localAddress`, not async |\n| Tasks to tasks | `Channel<T>`: `sendAsync`, `recvAsync`, `close` (`send` and `recv` block, for threads); `trySend` and `tryRecv`, which never wait (a full or empty channel answers at once), and `isClosed()`; `AsyncMutex<T>`: `lock`, whose guard may be held across `await` |\n| Blocking calls | `Async.blocking(() => …)`: any sync code on a worker thread while the task waits |\n| Signals | `AsyncSignal`: one task waits, any thread or interrupt handler raises |\n\nEach returns `Result<T, AsyncError>`, whose cases are `Cancelled`, `TimedOut` and `Io(IoError)`. A socket's address is\na `SocketAddress`, `V4` or `V6`, made from text by `SocketAddresses.parse(\"[::1]:8080\")` or\n`SocketAddresses.parseIp(\"::1\", 8080)`, and written back with `a.write(mut w)`. A listener on `SocketAddresses.v6Any(port)`\nalso accepts IPv4 clients, whose `peerAddress()` is `V4`. Network failures have their own `IoError` kinds,\n`ConnectionReset`, `TimedOut`, `HostUnreachable` and the rest, the same on every platform. A blocking call made\ndirectly in async code would stop every task of the thread, so it is a compile error (N0295): a `File` read or a\n`Mutex` lock belongs in `Async.blocking`.\n\nA static file goes to a socket with `await stream.sendFileAll(borrow file, offset, length)`: the kernel copies it from\nthe page cache (`sendfile`, or `TransmitFile` on Windows), and where it cannot, lib/std reads 64 KiB at a time on a\nworker thread and writes them, with the same result. The offset is explicit and the file's position stays where it was,\nso one open file can be sent on many connections at once. If the file is shorter than `offset + length`, the result\nis `Io(InvalidInput)`. `sendFile` is one step and returns how many bytes went.\n\n## How it runs\n\nEach async fn has a **frame** holding its locals and what it is waiting for. Its size is known at compile time\n(`ceesafec build --explain-frames` prints it), and it never moves, which is why a reference to a local may be held\nacross an `await`. An awaited call's frame is inside its caller's, so `await` itself allocates nothing; children of a\nscope get theirs from the scope's allocator.\n\nThe executor waits with kqueue on macOS and the BSDs, epoll on Linux (io_uring when asked for), and the console and\nfile APIs on Windows. `Async.run` uses one thread; `Async.runWorkers(n, task)` and `s.spawnSend(…)` spread a scope's\nchildren over `n` threads ([Threads](threads.md#async-tasks-on-several-threads)). In the interpreter tasks run in a\nfixed order with a virtual clock, so the examples above print the same every time, and quickly; there `Clock.now()`\nis 2000-01-01T00:00:00Z plus that clock, and `Random.fill` gives the same bytes every run, which are not secret.\n\nAsync code also runs in CosmoOS user programs (without async sockets) and in kernel code, over an executor the kernel\nprovides ([Kernel code](kernel.md#async-tasks-in-kernel-code)). The debugger lists a program's tasks and where each\nwaits ([Debugging](debugging.md)). The rules in full are the specification's §40\n([type-system.md](../spec/type-system.md)) and ADRs 0031 to 0040.\n\n## Next\n\n[Unsafe code and C](unsafe-and-c.md).\n",
    4 =>
        "# Basics\n\nThis chapter covers what every program uses: values and types, variables, functions, operators, and control flow.\nEach example is a whole program you can run with `ceesafec run --interp file.cee`.\n\n## A program\n\nA program is one or more `.cee` files with a `main` function. `main` takes nothing and returns nothing or an `i32`\nexit status.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    Console.writeLine(\"Hello, CeeSafe\");\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n```text\nHello, CeeSafe\n```\n\n`Console.write` and `Console.writeLine` print integers, booleans, floats and text.\n\n## Types\n\n| Type | Values |\n|---|---|\n| `i8` `i16` `i32` `i64` | signed integers, two's complement |\n| `u8` `u16` `u32` `u64` | unsigned integers |\n| `isize` `usize` | the target's pointer width; lengths and indexes are `usize` |\n| `f32` `f64` | IEEE 754 floating point |\n| `bool` | `true`, `false` |\n| `StringView` | a read-only view of UTF-8 text; string literals have this type |\n\nLater chapters add structs, enums, arrays, references, optionals (`T?`), results (`Result<T, E>`), boxes and\ncollections. There is no `char`: text is UTF-8 bytes, and a character literal such as `'k'` is an integer.\n\n## Variables\n\n`let` declares a variable that cannot be assigned again; `var` one that can. The type is inferred from the\ninitialiser, or written after a colon.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    let name = \"CeeSafe\";              // StringView\n    let year: u16 = 2026;\n    var count = 0;                  // i32: an integer literal's default type\n    count += 1;\n    var total: u64;                 // declared now, assigned before it is read\n    total = 40 + 2;\n    Console.writeLine(name);\n    Console.writeLine(year);\n    Console.writeLine(count);\n    Console.writeLine(total);\n}\n```\n\n```text\nCeeSafe\n2026\n1\n42\n```\n\nReading a variable that might not be assigned yet is a compile error, and so is assigning to a `let`. Two locals in\nscope cannot have the same name: there is no shadowing. `let _ = f();` evaluates `f()` and discards its value.\n\n### Reserved words and built-in names\n\nSome words that look like ordinary names are reserved, so they can't name a variable, parameter or field: `move`,\n`own`, `type`, `namespace`, `macro`, `yield`, `class` and `internal`, among others. The full list is in\n[grammar.md §1.3](../spec/grammar.md). The compiler says so when one is used as a name.\n\nThe built-in types' names are taken in every program, so a program can't declare its own `Handle` (a `SlotMap`'s\nhandle), `Check` (the test helpers), `TestFailure`, or the lib/core names listed in [the library API](builtins.md).\n\n## Integer literals and the expected type\n\nAn integer literal takes its type from where it is used. In `let year: u16 = 2026;` it is a `u16`; in `x + 1` it is\n`x`'s type; with nothing to go by it is an `i32`. A suffix fixes it: `7u8`, `100_000u64`. Literals can be written in\nhex, binary and octal (`0xFF`, `0b1010`, `0o17`), with `_` between digits. A value that does not fit its type is a\ncompile error.\n\nA character literal is the character's Unicode value, typed like any other literal:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn isDigit(c: u8) -> bool => c >= '0' && c <= '9';\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(isDigit('7'));\n    Console.writeLine(isDigit('x'));\n    Console.writeLine('\\n' as u32);\n}\n```\n\n```text\ntrue\nfalse\n10\n```\n\n## Functions\n\n```ceesafe\nfn add(a: i64, b: i64) -> i64 {\n    return a + b;\n}\n\nfn square(x: i64) -> i64 => x * x;        // an expression body\n\nfn greet(name: StringView) {              // no return type: returns nothing\n    Console.write(\"Hello, \");\n    Console.writeLine(name);\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(add(2, square(3)));\n    greet(\"reader\");\n}\n```\n\n```text\n11\nHello, reader\n```\n\nParameters are immutable. Arguments are evaluated left to right. A function that returns a value must return on\nevery path, which the compiler checks.\n\n## Operators\n\nBoth operands of an operator must have the same type: there are **no implicit conversions**, not even between `i32`\nand `i64`.\n\n| Operators | On | Notes |\n",
    5 =>
        "|---|---|---|\n| `+ - * / %` | integers, floats | integer overflow stops the program; so does dividing by zero |\n| `& \\| ^ << >>` | unsigned integers | a shift by the width or more stops the program |\n| `== != < <= > >=` | integers, floats, `bool` (`==`, `!=`), text (`==`, `!=`) | |\n| `== !=` | enums without payloads; arrays and slices of integers, `bool` or such enums | compare the elements |\n| `&& \\|\\| !` | `bool` | `&&` and `\\|\\|` short-circuit |\n| `??` | an optional and a fallback | `o ?? d`: `o`'s value, or `d` when `o` is `null` |\n\nWhen overflow is expected, say what should happen:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    let big: u8 = 250;\n    Console.writeLine(big.wrappingAdd(10));       // 4: modulo 256\n    Console.writeLine(big.saturatingAdd(10));     // 255: clamped\n    match (big.checkedAdd(10)) {                  // u8?: null on overflow\n        Some(sum) => { Console.writeLine(sum); }\n        null => { Console.writeLine(\"overflow\"); }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```text\n4\n255\noverflow\n```\n\nThe same three families exist for `Sub` and `Mul`, on every integer type.\n\n## Conversions\n\n`as` converts only when no value can be lost: `u8 as u32`, `i32 as i64`, `u32 as i64`, `bool as u8`, `i32 as f64`.\nAnything that could lose information is spelled out:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    let big: u32 = 300;\n    Console.writeLine(truncate<u8>(big));             // 44: the low 8 bits\n    let x: f64 = 2.75;\n    Console.writeLine(truncate<i32>(x));              // 2: toward zero; out of range stops the program\n    Console.writeLine(round<f64>(1234567890123u64));  // the nearest f64\n    let n: i32 = -1;\n    Console.writeLine(n as i64);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n44\n2\n1234567890123\n-1\n```\n\n## Control flow\n\n`if`, `while`, `for` and `match` put their condition in parentheses, and their bodies are blocks.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    var n = 0;\n    while (n < 3) {\n        n += 1;\n    }\n    for (i in 0..3) {                    // 0, 1, 2\n        if (i == 1) {\n            continue;\n        }\n        Console.write(i);\n    }\n    Console.writeLine();\n    var tries = 0;\n    loop {                               // until a break\n        tries += 1;\n        if (tries == 5) {\n            break;\n        }\n    }\n    Console.writeLine(n + tries);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n02\n8\n```\n\n`for (x in values)` walks an array, a slice or a vector (see [Collections](collections.md)).\n\n### `if` and `match` are expressions too\n\n```ceesafe\nfn describe(n: i32) -> StringView => match (n) {\n    0 => \"zero\",\n    1 => \"one\",\n    _ => if (n < 0) \"negative\" else \"many\",\n};\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(describe(0));\n    Console.writeLine(describe(7));\n    Console.writeLine(describe(-2));\n    let sign = if (-2 < 0) -1 else 1;\n    Console.writeLine(sign);\n}\n```\n\n```text\nzero\nmany\nnegative\n-1\n```\n\nAn `if` expression needs its `else`. A branch can also be a block. It either ends with its value, written without `;`,\nor it leaves, such as `{ return; }`, which is how an early exit fits inside an expression:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn parse(text: StringView) -> i32 {\n    let n = match (text.parseInt<i32>()) {\n        Some(v) => {\n            let doubled = v * 2;\n            doubled\n        }\n        null => {\n            return -1;\n        }\n    };\n    return n;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(parse(\"21\"));\n    Console.writeLine(parse(\"x\"));\n}\n```\n\n```text\n42\n-1\n```\n\n## Constants\n\n```ceesafe\nconst SECONDS_PER_DAY: u32 = 24 * 60 * 60;\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(SECONDS_PER_DAY);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n86400\n```\n\nA constant is evaluated at compile time. An overflow there is a compile error rather than a run-time stop.\n\nA constant may be text, and in a constant or a static `+` joins constant text, so a prefix is written once:\n\n```ceesafe\nconst TOOL: StringView = \"bun\";\nconst USAGE: StringView = TOOL + \" <command> [options]\";\nconst VERSION_LINE: StringView = TOOL + \" 1.4.4\";\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(USAGE);\n    Console.writeLine(VERSION_LINE);\n}\n```\n\n```text\nbun <command> [options]\nbun 1.4.4\n```\n\nThe joined text is made when th",
    6 =>
        "e program is compiled. Anywhere else `+` on views is an error (N0207): build text at\nrun time by appending to a `String`. With a `c\"…\"` on the right, `TOOL + c\"rc\"` is a C string (a `CStr`,\n[C interop](unsafe-and-c.md)).\n\n## Embedded files\n\n`embed(\"path\")` puts a file's bytes in the program, as a `Slice<u8>`; `embedText(\"path\")` puts them as text, a\n`StringView`, and refuses a file that is not UTF-8 (N0361). The file is read when the package is checked, so the\nprogram needs nothing beside it at run time:\n\n<!-- ceesafe: skip -->\n```ceesafe\nstatic ROOT_CERTS: Slice<u8> = embed(\"certs/roots.der\");\nconst HELP: StringView = embedText(\"help.txt\");\nconst BANNER: StringView = \"bun: \" + embedText(\"banner.txt\");\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(ROOT_CERTS.length);\n    Console.write(HELP);\n}\n```\n\nThe path is relative to the source file that names it, with `/` between directories, and nothing in it is expanded:\nno absolute paths, no `~`, no environment variables. Wherever its symbolic links lead, the file must be inside the\npackage's directory, or inside a directory its manifest lists in `[embed] roots` ([Packages](packages.md)); a file\nchecked on its own embeds only below its own directory. Anything else, a missing file, a directory or a file over\n16 MiB, is N0362, also in the editor and the MCP server, which read the files too.\n\nA constant text's `.bytes()` is constant too, so a byte string is written as text: `const MAGIC: Slice<u8> =\n\"\\u{7f}ELF\".bytes();`. A constant or a static may also be a table of them, `[Slice<u8>; N]`, embedded files included.\n\n## Comments\n\n`//` comments run to the end of the line, `/* … */` comments may span lines, and `///` before an item is its\ndocumentation, which [`ceesafec doc`](formatting-and-docs.md) collects.\n\n## Next\n\n[Structs, enums and patterns](structs-and-enums.md).\n",
    7 =>
        "# Built-in types and functions\n\nThe types and functions below are part of the compiler, not of a library, so they have no page in the library\nreference. The libraries' own types, such as `TextWriter`, `Timer`, `Environment`, `HashMap` and the async\nsockets, are in [lib/core](../spec/api/core.md) and [lib/std](../spec/api/std.md). Every example here is compiled\nand run by the guide's checks, so the names and signatures are the compiler's. With the MCP server,\n`ceesafe_api` looks any of these names up.\n\n## `Console`\n\n| Function | Type |\n|---|---|\n| `Console.write(x)`, `Console.writeLine(x)` | `x` an integer, float, `bool`, `StringView` or `String` |\n| `Console.writeBytes(bytes)` | `bytes: Slice<u8>`, raw bytes to standard output, UTF-8 or not |\n| `Console.writeError(text)`, `Console.writeErrorLine(text)` | `text: StringView`, to standard error |\n| `Console.read(mut buffer)` | `Result<usize, IoError>`; `0` at the end of input |\n\n`Console` blocks its thread: in an `async fn`, use lib/std's `AsyncConsole` (N0295 otherwise).\n\n`Console.write` takes text, which is always UTF-8. Bytes from a file or the network may not be:\n`StringView.fromUtf8(bytes)!` stops the program on them. To pass them through as they are, write them with\n`Console.writeBytes(bytes)`.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    Console.write(\"answer: \");\n    Console.writeLine(42);\n    let bytes = \"raw\\n\";\n    Console.writeBytes(bytes.bytes());\n    Console.writeLine(2.5);\n    Console.writeLine(true);\n}\n```\n\n```text\nanswer: 42\nraw\n2.5\ntrue\n```\n\n## `Process` and program arguments\n\n| Function | Type |\n|---|---|\n| `Process.argumentCount()` | `usize`, including the program name |\n| `Process.argument(i)` | `StringView?`: `null` out of range or for bytes that are not UTF-8 |\n| `Process.exit(status)` | `status: i32`; never returns |\n| `Process.run(program, arguments)` | `Result<i32, IoError>`; `arguments: borrow Vector<StringView>` |\n| `Process.capture(program, arguments, input)` | `Result<ProcessOutput, IoError>` (lib/std) |\n\nEnvironment variables are lib/std's `Environment.variable(name)`, a `StringView?`.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(Process.argumentCount() >= 1);\n    match (Process.argument(1000)) {\n        Some(a) => { Console.writeLine(a); }\n        null => { Console.writeLine(\"no argument 1000\"); }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```text\ntrue\nno argument 1000\n```\n\n## `File`, `TcpListener` and `TcpStream`\n\n| Function | Type |\n|---|---|\n| `File.open(path)`, `File.create(path)` | `Result<File, IoError>` (reading; writing, created or truncated) |\n| `File.remove(path)` | `Result<void, IoError>` |\n| `f.read(mut buffer)` | `Result<usize, IoError>`, `0` at the end; `buffer: SliceMut<u8>` |\n| `f.write(bytes)` | `Result<void, IoError>`: all of them or an error |\n| `File.openReadWrite(path)`, `File.openOrCreate(path)`, `File.createNew(path)` | `Result<File, IoError>` for reading and writing, never truncated: an existing file; created if missing; a new one (`AlreadyExists` otherwise) |\n| `f.readAt(mut buffer, offset)`, `f.writeAt(bytes, offset)` | positional: `usize` (0 past the end); every byte or an error |\n| `f.syncData()`, `f.syncAll()` | durable on disk (`F_FULLFSYNC` on Apple systems); after a failed sync the file's durable contents are unknown |\n| `f.length()`, `f.setLength(n)` | `u64`; truncate or zero-extend |\n| `File.rename(from, to)`, `Directory.sync(path)` | atomic replace; make a directory's entries durable |\n| `Directory.space(path)` | the volume's `total`, `free` and `available` bytes (ADR 0077) |\n| `Console.flush()` | write standard output's buffer now, so it survives a kill |\n| `File.openAppend(path)` | write-only, created if missing, every write at the end |\n| `File.modified(path)`, `File.setPermissions(path, 0o640)` | a `UnixTime`; `chmod` |\n| `File.openRegular(path)` | read-only, only a regular file: never waits on a FIFO; `IsDirectory`, or `InvalidInput` for a device (ADR 0078) |\n| `f.modified()`, `f.kind()` | the open file's time and `PathKind`, from the file, not its path ",
    8 =>
        "|\n| `IoError.StorageFull` | the volume is full (`ENOSPC`, `EDQUOT`) |\n| `TcpListener.bind(address, port)` | `Result<TcpListener, IoError>`; then `port()`, `accept()` |\n| `TcpStream.connect(address, port)` | `Result<TcpStream, IoError>`; `read`, `write`, `shutdownWrite`, `shutdownRead` |\n| `e.message()` | the `IoError`'s text, a `StringView` |\n\nA `File` closes when it is destroyed. These block their thread: async code uses lib/std's `AsyncFiles`,\n`AsyncTcpListener` and `AsyncTcpStream`.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    match (File.open(\"/no/such/file\")) {\n        Ok(_) => { Console.writeLine(\"opened\"); }\n        Err(e) => { Console.writeLine(e.message()); }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```text\nNo such file or directory\n```\n\n## `StringView` and `String`\n\n| Member | Type |\n|---|---|\n| `s.length` | `usize`, bytes |\n| `s[i]`, `s[lo..hi]`, `s.bytes()` | `u8`; a `StringView` (character boundaries checked); `Slice<u8>` |\n| `StringView.fromUtf8(bytes)` | `StringView?`: `null` for bytes that are not UTF-8 |\n| `==`, `!=` | compare the bytes |\n| `String.create()`, `String.withCapacity(n)` | `String`; `Result<String, AllocError>` |\n| `t.append(view)`, `t.appendNumber(n)` | `Result<void, AllocError>` |\n| `t.appendHex(n, minWidth, upper)`, `t.appendOctal(n)`, `t.appendFixed(f, digits)`, `t.appendPadded(view, width, align, fill)` | `Result<void, AllocError>`: Rust's `{:08x}`, `{:o}`, `{:.3}`, `{:>8}` (also on a `Vector<u8>`, and as `VectorBytes.appendHex(mut v, …)`) |\n| `t.view()`, `t.clear()`, `t.length`, `t.capacity` | a `StringView` borrowed from `t`; … |\n| `s.indexOf(t)`, `s.lastIndexOf(t)`, `s.indexOfByte(b)` | `usize?` |\n| `s.contains(t)`, `s.startsWith(t)`, `s.endsWith(t)`, `s.equalsIgnoreAsciiCase(t)` | `bool` |\n| `s.trim()`, `s.trimStart()`, `s.trimEnd()` | the `StringView` without ASCII whitespace |\n| `s.splitOnce(sep)`, `s.split(sep)` | `TextHalves?` (`left`, `right`); a `TextSplit`, whose `next()` gives each piece, then `null` |\n| `s.parseInt<T>()`, `s.parseIntRadix<T>(radix)`, `s.parseFloat<T>()` | `T?`: `null` for text that is not a `T` |\n\nThe searches and parsers work on a `String` too, and on bytes: a `Slice<u8>` (or a `Vector<u8>`) takes and gives\n`Slice<u8>` where a view takes and gives `StringView`. Every view they return stays valid UTF-8. Floats parse\ncorrectly rounded, and accept `inf`, `infinity` and `nan` in any case.\n\nA `StringView` is a reference: a variable holding one is a `let`, and a struct or enum holding one needs a region\nparameter. For formatting into a fixed buffer without allocating, use lib/core's `TextWriter` (`text`, `byte`,\n`bytes`, `decimal` for a `u64`, `signed` for an `i64`, `hex`, then `written`).\n\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    let s: StringView = \"CeeSafe\";\n    Console.writeLine(s.length);\n    Console.writeLine(s[0..3]);\n    var t = String.create();\n    t.append(\"x = \")!;\n    t.appendNumber(-7)!;\n    Console.writeLine(t.view());\n    let bytes = [104u8, 105];\n    match (StringView.fromUtf8(bytes[..])) {\n        Some(v) => { Console.writeLine(v); }\n        null => { Console.writeLine(\"not UTF-8\"); }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```text\n7\nCee\nx = -7\nhi\n```\n\n## `Vector`, arrays and slices\n\n| Member | Type |\n|---|---|\n| `Vector<T>.create()`, `Vector<T>.withCapacity(n)` | `Vector<T>`; `Result<Vector<T>, AllocError>` |\n| `v.push(x)` | `Result<void, AllocError>` |\n| `v.pop()` | `T?` |\n| `v.length`, `v.capacity`, `v[i]` | `usize`; an element place |\n| `v.clear()`, `v.truncate(n)`, `v.swap(i, j)` | |\n| `v.insert(at, x)`, `v.remove(at)`, `v.swapRemove(at)` | `Result<void, AllocError>`; `T`; `T` (the last element fills the gap); the index is checked at the call |\n| `[]` | the empty `Slice<T>` or `SliceMut<T>`, where one is expected |\n| `a.writeLE(0, x)` on an array | an array's methods are its slice's: `a[..].writeLE(0, x)` |\n| `v.view()`, `v.viewMut()` | `Slice<T>`, `SliceMut<T>` |\n| `[x; N]`, `[a, b, c]`, `a[lo..hi]`, `borrow mut a[lo..hi]` | an array; a `Slice<T>`; a `SliceMut<T>` |\n| `d.copyFrom(s)`, `d.fill(v)` | bulk writes into a `SliceMu",
    9 =>
        "t<T>` |\n| `v.extend(s)`, `v.resize(n, fill)`, `v.reserve(extra)` | `Result<void, AllocError>`; one growth at most; `extend` and `resize` need Copy elements |\n| `b.readBE<T>(at)`, `b.readLE<T>(at)` | `T`, an integer from bytes, big- or little-endian |\n| `b.writeBE(at, x)`, `b.writeLE(at, x)` | into a `SliceMut<u8>` (or a mutable `Vector<u8>`) |\n| `v.pushBE(x)`, `v.pushLE(x)` | `Result<void, AllocError>`: `x`'s bytes appended to a `Vector<u8>` |\n\nByte order works on fixed-width integers (`u8` to `u64`, `i8` to `i64`); the value's type gives the width, so a\nliteral needs a suffix (`v.pushBE(5u32)`). A read or write past the end panics at the call, and writes nothing.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    var message = Vector<u8>.create();\n    message.pushBE(0x51u8)!; // 'Q'\n    message.pushBE(0u32)!; // the length, patched below\n    message.extend(\"SELECT 1\".bytes())!;\n    message.pushBE(0u8)!;\n    let length = truncate<u32>(message.length - 1);\n    message.writeBE(1, length);\n    Console.writeLine(message.readBE<u32>(1));\n    var fields = \"r=abc,s=xyz,i=4096\".split(\",\");\n    while (true) {\n        let field = match (fields.next()) {\n            Some(f) => f,\n            null => { break; }\n        };\n        let pair = field.splitOnce(\"=\")!;\n        if (pair.left == \"i\") {\n            Console.writeLine(pair.right.parseInt<u32>()! * 2);\n        }\n    }\n    Console.writeLine(\" 2.5e-3 \".trim().parseFloat<f64>()!);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n13\n8192\n0.0025\n```\n\nIndexes are `usize`. A range of two literals that the body indexes with is a `usize` range. Arrays and slices may\nhold views (`[StringView; 3]`, `Slice<StringView>`), but not `borrow T` references. Slice a variable, not a call's\nresult: bind it to a local first.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn total(xs: Slice<u32>) -> u32 {\n    var n: u32 = 0;\n    for (x in xs) {\n        n += x;\n    }\n    return n;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    var v = Vector<u32>.create();\n    for (i in 0u32..4) {\n        v.push(i * 10)!;\n    }\n    Console.writeLine(total(v.view()));\n    var buffer = [0u32; 4];\n    buffer[1..3].fill(5);\n    Console.writeLine(total(buffer[..]));\n    let names = [\"Low\", \"Medium\", \"High\"];\n    Console.writeLine(names[1]);\n    match (v.pop()) {\n        Some(last) => { Console.writeLine(last); }\n        null => {}\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```text\n60\n10\nMedium\n30\n```\n\n## Integers, floats and conversions\n\n| Operation | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| `+ - * / %`, unary `-` | panic on overflow, and on dividing by zero |\n| `& \\| ^ << >>` | unsigned types only; no `~` (use `x ^ 0xFFFFFFFF`) |\n| `x.wrappingAdd(y)`, `x.checkedAdd(y)`, `x.saturatingAdd(y)` | also `Sub`, `Mul`; `checked…` is a `T?` |\n| `x.min(y)`, `x.max(y)` | every integer type |\n| `x.abs()`, `x.unsignedAbs()` | signed types: `abs` panics at the minimum, `unsignedAbs` is the unsigned type of the width |\n| `x.rotateLeft(n)`, `x.rotateRight(n)`, `x.wrappingShl(n)`, `x.wrappingShr(n)` | unsigned types; `n: u32`, taken modulo the width |\n| `x.leadingZeros()`, `x.trailingZeros()`, `x.countOnes()` | unsigned types; a `u32` (the width for 0) |\n| `x.byteSwap()` | every integer type: the bytes in the other order |\n| `x as T` | only conversions that never lose information; `a as u64 < b` compares |\n| `*r` | the Copy value `r` refers to; `*r = v` writes through a `borrow mut` |\n| `replace(mut place, v)`, `swap(mut a, mut b)` | the old value, which nothing is left without; exchange two places |\n| `assert(cond)`, `assert(cond, message)`, `panic(message)` | panic when false; always (`never`) |\n| `debugAssert(cond)`, `debugAssert(cond, message)` | as `assert`, but only in debug builds, tests and the interpreter; may change nothing that exists before it ([Debugging](debugging.md#debug-assertions)) |\n| `embed(\"path\")`, `embedText(\"path\")` | a file's bytes as a `Slice<u8>`, or as UTF-8 text, read when the package is checked; the path is relative to the source file and stays inside the package ([Constants](basics.md#embedded-files)) |\n| `r.isOk()`, `r.isErr()`, `r.ok()`, `r.err()`, `o.okOr(e)` | a `Result`'s te",
    10 =>
        "sts and conversions; an optional as a `Result` |\n| `enum E { @from Io(IoError), … }` | `?` turns an `IoError` into `E.Io(e)` in a function returning `Result<_, E>` |\n| `truncate<T>(x)` | wraps an integer; a float to an integer rounds toward zero and panics out of range or on NaN |\n| `checkedTruncate<T>(f)`, `saturatingTruncate<T>(f)` | a float to an integer: `T?` (`null` where `truncate` would panic); the nearest bound (NaN gives 0) |\n| `round<T>(x)` | to a float type, rounding to nearest |\n| `f.sqrt()`, `f.abs()`, `f.floor()`, `f.ceil()`, `f.min(g)`, `f.max(g)`, `f.bits()` | floats |\n\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    let big: u8 = 250;\n    Console.writeLine(big.wrappingAdd(10));\n    Console.writeLine(big.saturatingAdd(10));\n    let n: i32 = -7;\n    Console.writeLine(n.abs());\n    Console.writeLine(n.max(3));\n    let wide: i64 = 300;\n    Console.writeLine(truncate<u8>(wide));\n    let f: f64 = 2.75;\n    Console.writeLine(truncate<i32>(f));\n    Console.writeLine(round<f32>(wide));\n    Console.writeLine(f.floor());\n}\n```\n\n```text\n4\n255\n7\n3\n44\n2\n300\n2\n```\n\n## `Box` and memory\n\n| Type or function | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| `Box.create(value)` | `Result<Box<T>, AllocError>`, heap-owned; `Box<dyn I>` for dynamic dispatch |\n| `FixedBufferAllocator.over(mut bytes)` | an allocator over a caller's buffer, for freestanding code |\n| `Arena`, `Scratch`, `Pool<T>`, `SlotMap<T>`, `StableVector<T>` | see [memory](memory.md) and [collections](collections.md) |\n| `Thread.spawn`, `Thread.both`, `Async.run`, `Async.scope` | see [threads](threads.md) and [async](async.md) |\n\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    let b = Box.create(41)!;\n    Console.writeLine(b + 1);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n42\n```\n\n## Every signature above\n\nThis block is type-checked, not run: each call is annotated with the type the tables give, so a table that drifts\nfrom the compiler fails the guide's checks.\n\n<!-- ceesafe: check -->\n```ceesafe\nfn everything() -> Result<void, IoError> {\n    let created: Result<File, IoError> = File.create(\"/tmp/x\");\n    var f = created?;\n    let wrote: Result<void, IoError> = f.write(\"hi\".bytes());\n    var buffer = [0u8; 8];\n    let got: Result<usize, IoError> = f.read(mut buffer[..]);\n    let removed: Result<void, IoError> = File.remove(\"/tmp/x\");\n    let input: Result<usize, IoError> = Console.read(mut buffer[..]);\n    Console.writeError(\"e\");\n    Console.writeErrorLine(\"e\");\n    var listener = TcpListener.bind(\"127.0.0.1\", 0)?;\n    let port: Result<u16, IoError> = listener.port();\n    let accepted: Result<TcpStream, IoError> = listener.accept();\n    var stream = TcpStream.connect(\"127.0.0.1\", 1)?;\n    let n: Result<usize, IoError> = stream.read(mut buffer[..]);\n    let w: Result<void, IoError> = stream.write(buffer[..]);\n    let sw: Result<void, IoError> = stream.shutdownWrite();\n    let sr: Result<void, IoError> = stream.shutdownRead();\n    var arguments = Vector<StringView>.create();\n    let status: Result<i32, IoError> = Process.run(\"true\", borrow arguments);\n    let captured: Result<ProcessOutput, IoError> = Process.capture(\"true\", borrow arguments, \"\".bytes());\n    let home: StringView? = Environment.variable(\"HOME\");\n    return Ok();\n}\nfn collections() -> Result<void, AllocError> {\n    var v: Vector<u32> = Vector<u32>.withCapacity(4)?;\n    v.push(1)?;\n    v.push(2)?;\n    let c: usize = v.capacity;\n    v.swap(0, 1);\n    v.truncate(1);\n    let m: SliceMut<u32> = v.viewMut();\n    v.clear();\n    var t: String = String.withCapacity(8)?;\n    t.clear();\n    let tc: usize = t.capacity;\n    var dst = [0u8; 2];\n    let src = [1u8, 2];\n    dst[..].copyFrom(src[..]);\n    var bytes = Vector<u8>.create();\n    bytes.extend(src[..])?;\n    bytes.resize(8, 0)?;\n    bytes.reserve(8)?;\n    bytes.pushBE(1u16)?;\n    bytes.pushLE(-1i32)?;\n    let word: u32 = bytes.readBE<u32>(0);\n    let back: i16 = bytes.view().readLE<i16>(2);\n    bytes.writeLE(0, 7u64);\n    dst[..].writeBE(0, 3u16);\n    return Ok();\n}\nfn text() {\n    let s: StringView = \" a,b \";\n    let at: usize? = s.indexOf(\",\");\n    ",
    11 =>
        "let last: usize? = s.lastIndexOf(\"b\");\n    let byte: usize? = s.indexOfByte(',');\n    let has: bool = s.contains(\"a\") && s.startsWith(\" \") && s.endsWith(\" \") && s.equalsIgnoreAsciiCase(\" A,B \");\n    let t: StringView = s.trim().trimStart().trimEnd();\n    let halves: TextHalves? = t.splitOnce(\",\");\n    var pieces: TextSplit = t.split(\",\");\n    let piece: StringView? = pieces.next();\n    let n: i64? = \"-12\".parseInt<i64>();\n    let h: u8? = \"ff\".parseIntRadix<u8>(16);\n    let f: f32? = \"1.5\".parseFloat<f32>();\n    let raw: Slice<u8> = s.bytes();\n    let rawAt: usize? = raw.indexOf(\",\".bytes());\n    let rawTrim: Slice<u8> = raw.trim();\n    let rawHalves: ByteHalves? = raw.splitOnce(\",\".bytes());\n    var rawPieces: ByteSplit = raw.split(\",\".bytes());\n    let rawN: u32? = raw.parseInt<u32>();\n}\nfn numbers() {\n    let a: u8 = 250;\n    let s: u8 = a.wrappingSub(1);\n    let m: u8? = a.checkedMul(2);\n    let x: i64 = -5;\n    let u: u64 = x.unsignedAbs();\n    let f: f64 = 2.0;\n    let ct: u8? = checkedTruncate<u8>(f);\n    let st: u8 = saturatingTruncate<u8>(f);\n    let r: f64 = f.sqrt().abs().ceil().min(1.0).max(0.5);\n    let bits: u64 = f.bits();\n    let w: u32 = 0x80000001;\n    let rotated: u32 = w.rotateLeft(1).rotateRight(2).wrappingShl(33).wrappingShr(1);\n    let counts: u32 = w.leadingZeros() + w.trailingZeros() + w.countOnes();\n    let swapped: i16 = (-2i16).byteSwap();\n}\nfn memory() {\n    var storage = [0u8; 64];\n    let arena = FixedBufferAllocator.over(mut storage);\n}\nfn main() {}\n```\n",
    12 =>
        "# The ceesafec command\n\n`ceesafec` is the compiler and every tool around it. `ceesafec --help` prints the same summary as this page.\n\nInside a [package](packages.md), commands take no file names: they use the nearest `ceesafe.toml` in the current\ndirectory or above. Outside one, give the files.\n\n## Commands\n\n| Command | Does |\n|---|---|\n| `ceesafec new <name> [--lib] [--agent <names>] [--no-agent]` | make a package: `ceesafe.toml`, `src/main.cee` (or `src/lib.cee`), and `AGENTS.md` for [AI agents](for-agents.md) |\n| `ceesafec agents [--agent <names>]` | write or refresh the package's agent instructions |\n| `ceesafec fetch [--offline]` | resolve the package's dependencies and write `ceesafe.lock` |\n| `ceesafec check` | parse, type-check and borrow-check; the fastest way to find errors |\n| `ceesafec build` | compile to C, then with the C compiler to `out/<name>` (or `-o`) |\n| `ceesafec run --interp` | run the program in the reference interpreter |\n| `ceesafec test` | run the package's `@test` functions, each as its own process ([Testing](testing.md)) |\n| `ceesafec parse` | parse only, reporting syntax errors |\n| `ceesafec fmt [--check \\| --stdout]` | format in the canonical style ([Formatting](formatting-and-docs.md)) |\n| `ceesafec doc [-o file] [--title t] [--missing]` | a Markdown page of the public items ([Documentation](formatting-and-docs.md#documentation)) |\n| `ceesafec lsp` | the language server, for [editors](editors.md) |\n| `ceesafec fix [--dry-run]` | apply the fixes the compiler is sure of, and check again until none applies |\n| `ceesafec explain <code>` | what a diagnostic code (`N0249`) means |\n| `ceesafec mcp` | the MCP server, for [AI agents](for-agents.md) |\n\n## Building\n\n| Option | Does |\n|---|---|\n| `-o <path>` | the output file (or directory, with `--emit c`) |\n| `--emit exe` | an executable (the default) |\n| `--emit c` | the generated C sources, to look at or to build yourself |\n| `--emit obj` | one relocatable object, for a kernel or another program's link (`--ld ld.lld` joins objects for ELF cross builds) |\n| `--cc <compiler>` | the C compiler; default `$CC`, else `cc`, or on Windows clang, gcc or Visual Studio's `cl`, found and set up ([tooling](../spec/tooling.md) §8) |\n| `--cflag <flag>` | an extra C compiler flag (repeatable); the default is `-O2` |\n| `-g` | a debug build: `#line` directives and `-g -O0` (see [Debugging](debugging.md)) |\n| `--line-directives` | `#line` directives without changing the optimisation level |\n| `--split` | the C in one file per CeeSafe source, `c/<package>/<path>.c`, so it compiles in parallel ([C backend](../spec/c-backend.md) §2.4) |\n| `--jobs <n>` | with `--split` (without it, a usage error): compile at most `n` C files at once; the default is the number of CPUs, and the output is the same for any `n` |\n| `--profile hosted \\| freestanding` | the [profile](kernel.md#the-freestanding-profile) |\n| `--no-float` | reject every float type and literal |\n| `--offline` | in a package: never run `git` |\n\nC files given with the CeeSafe files (`ceesafec build main.cee shim.c`) are compiled and linked with the program.\n\n## Seeing what the compiler decided\n\n| Option | Prints |\n|---|---|\n| `--explain-allocations` | each allocation site, and whether its box lives on the stack or the heap, and why |\n| `--explain-bounds-checks` | each index check, and whether it was removed |\n| `--explain-overflow-checks` | each checked `+`, `-`, `*` and negation, and whether its check was removed |\n| `--no-placement`, `--no-bce`, `--no-oce` | turn off box placement, bounds-check elimination, overflow-check elimination |\n| `--dump-ast`, `--dump-hir`, `--dump-nir` | the compiler's intermediate forms (`parse`, `check`, `build`) |\n\nThe optimisations never remove a check that could fail; their preconditions are in\n[Optimisations](../spec/optimisations.md).\n\n## Exit status and errors\n\n`ceesafec` exits with 0 on success, 1 when the program has errors, and 2 for a usage error. Diagnostics go to standard\nerror a",
    13 =>
        "s `file:line:column: error[code]: message`, followed by the source line, notes, and a hint where there is one.\nThe codes are listed in [Diagnostics](../spec/diagnostics.md).\n\nWith `--diagnostics-format=json` (on `parse`, `check`, `build`, `run`, `fmt` and `fix`), they go to standard output\ninstead, as one JSON document with each diagnostic's position, message and fixes, for programs and\n[AI agents](for-agents.md) ([tooling](../spec/tooling.md) §5).\n\n## The interpreter\n\n`ceesafec run --interp` runs a program without a C compiler, in an interpreter that executes the compiler's own\nintermediate form. It stops with an exit status of 70 on any use of freed, out-of-bounds or uninitialised memory, or\na bad free, which the C build would not detect. The test suite runs every program both ways and compares the results.\n",
    14 =>
        "# Clay: a UI toolkit in CeeSafe\n\n[Clay](https://github.com/CeeSafe/Clay) is ClayWind ported to CeeSafe, with no C code and no dependencies.\nClayWind is a Tailwind-flavoured immediate-mode UI toolkit built on the layout model of\n[Clay by Nic Barker](https://github.com/nicbarker/clay). The port is about 18,000 lines. It is a working example of\nwhat CeeSafe is for: a program that does everything itself, from layout to pixels, and still runs on every desktop.\n\n![The ClayWind demo's dashboard, drawn by the CeeSafe renderer](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/CeeSafe/Clay/main/docs/dashboard.png)\n\n## What it does\n\n- **Layout:** a port of Clay 0.14's layout engine, checked against the C original by a differential fuzzer. Flex\n  rows and columns, grow and fit sizing, wrapping, floating elements, scrolling and transforms.\n- **Rendering:** a software renderer written to match ThorVG's output.\n  - Anti-aliased paths, gradients, images, shadows, blur, filters and every CSS blend mode.\n  - TrueType text, read and rasterized by the toolkit itself.\n- **Speed:**\n  - **Retina:** frames are drawn at the display's pixel ratio.\n  - **Incremental:** only what changed between frames is redrawn, and the result is byte-identical to a full\n    redraw. A 2× frame costs 2–4 ms instead of 64.\n- **Controls:** buttons, text input, sliders, tabs, dropdowns, lists, a context menu and tooltips, with focus and\n  transitions.\n\n## What it uses in CeeSafe\n\n- **Native windows:** lib/std's `Window` ([type-system.md §26.13](../spec/type-system.md)), with AppKit on macOS,\n  Win32 on Windows and X11 on Linux. ClayWind's desktop host presents only the boxes that changed\n  (`Window.presentChanged`). The program itself has no `extern \"C\"`.\n- **Regions:** the renderer and the UI borrow their memory from a `FixedBufferAllocator`, and the borrow checker\n  tracks those loans across the whole frame.\n- **Generic methods and `dyn` interfaces:** `Ui.endFrame<M: TextMetrics>` takes any text measurer, including the\n  renderer's own font engine, and every host's `Platform` is passed as a `dyn Platform`.\n- **Hosts on the standard library:** sockets and async I/O for a browser host (HTTP and WebSocket servers written in\n  CeeSafe), and the console for a terminal host.\n\nSeveral language changes came from this port, or from work it started:\n- **The port's own findings:** generic methods, arrays of views and `dyn I` as a bound.\n- **Follow-ups:** static view tables, and, found while testing them, a soundness fix for `static mut` and stricter\n  rules for `static` in signatures.\n- **What the hosts needed:** native windows and `presentChanged`.\n\nEach has a decision record, ADRs 0063 to 0070.\n\n## Running it\n\nWith [ceesafec 0.14.0 or later](./installation.md):\n\n```sh\ngit clone https://github.com/CeeSafe/Clay.git\ncd Clay/claywind-hosts/desktop\nceesafec build\n./out/desktop\n```\n\nThe same demo also runs headless (for screenshots and tests), in a browser (`claywind-hosts/browser`) and in a\nterminal (`claywind-hosts/terminal`). The repository's README describes each host and the test suite.\n",
    15 =>
        "# Functions and closures\n\n## Functions are values\n\nA function can be stored in a variable and passed as an argument. Its type is written `fn(parameter types) -> result`:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn double(x: i32) -> i32 => x * 2;\nfn negate(x: i32) -> i32 => -x;\n\nfn applyTwice(f: fn(i32) -> i32, value: i32) -> i32 => f(f(value));\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(applyTwice(double, 3));\n    var op: fn(i32) -> i32 = double;\n    op = negate;\n    Console.writeLine(op(5));\n}\n```\n\n```text\n12\n-5\n```\n\nA function value is one pointer, and calling it is an indirect call.\n\n## Closures\n\nA closure is a function written inline, `(parameters) => body`, that can use the variables around it. It borrows\nwhat it captures, so it is a kind of reference: its type is `borrow fn(…) -> R` if it only reads what it captures,\nand `borrow mut fn(…) -> R` if it changes something.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn countIf(values: Slice<i32>, keep: borrow fn(i32) -> bool) -> i32 {\n    var n = 0;\n    for (v in values) {\n        if (keep(v)) {\n            n += 1;\n        }\n    }\n    return n;\n}\n\nfn forEach(values: Slice<i32>, visit: borrow mut fn(i32)) {\n    for (v in values) {\n        visit(v);\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let values = [3, 8, 1, 9, 4];\n    let limit = 4;\n    Console.writeLine(countIf(values[..], (v) => v > limit));     // captures `limit`\n    var total = 0;\n    forEach(values[..], (v) => { total += v; });                   // changes `total`\n    Console.writeLine(total);\n    let twice = (x: i32) => x * 2;                                  // captures nothing: a plain fn\n    Console.writeLine(twice(21));\n}\n```\n\n```text\n2\n25\n42\n```\n\n- Parameter types can be left out where the expected type gives them, as in the calls above.\n- The body is an expression, or a block: `(v) => { total += v; }`.\n- A closure that captures nothing is an ordinary function value, and can be stored anywhere a function can.\n\n### Closures do not outlive their block\n\nBecause a closure borrows its captures, the borrow checker treats it like any reference: it cannot be returned from\nthe function that made it, stored in a struct, or kept after a captured variable changes. That makes closures free\nof allocation and safe to use in kernels.\n\n### Owning closures\n\nA `move` literal moves what it captures into the closure, so the closure can outlive the function that made it. It\nis boxed, with an allocation you can see and handle, as a `Box<dyn fn(…) -> R>`. A closure that changes what it\ncaptured is a `Box<dyn mut fn(…) -> R>`, and calling it needs mutable access.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn adder(n: u64) -> Box<dyn fn(u64) -> u64> {\n    return Box.create(move (x) => x + n)!;\n}\n\nfn counter() -> Box<dyn mut fn() -> u64> {\n    var count: u64 = 0;\n    return Box.create(move () => {\n        count += 1;\n        return count;\n    })!;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let add2 = adder(2);\n    Console.writeLine(add2(3));\n    var next = counter();\n    next();\n    Console.writeLine(next());\n    var steps = Vector<Box<dyn fn(u64) -> u64>>.create();\n    steps.push(adder(1))!;\n    steps.push(adder(10))!;\n    var x: u64 = 0;\n    for (step in steps) {\n        x = step(x);\n    }\n    Console.writeLine(x);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n5\n2\n11\n```\n\n- The literal goes straight into `Box.create(…)` where a `Box<dyn fn…>` is expected; its parameter types come from\n  there.\n- A captured variable is moved in, and cannot be used afterwards (a Copy one is copied). It cannot hold a reference.\n- The body changes the closure's own copy of what it captured, and cannot move it out.\n\n## Methods that take closures\n\nSlices, arrays (through `a[..]`) and vectors have `sortBy`, `forEach`, `any`, `all`, and vectors `retain`:\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Reading {\n    sensor: u32;\n    celsius: f64;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    var readings = Vector<Reading>.create();\n    for (i in 0..8u32) {\n        readings.push(Reading { sensor: i % 3, celsius: 18.5 + (i as f64) * 0.75 })!;\n    }\n    let limit = 20.0;\n    readings.retain((r) => r.celsius > limit);\n    readings.sortBy((a, b) => if (a.celsius > b.celsius) ",
    16 =>
        "-1 else if (a.celsius < b.celsius) 1 else 0);\n    var sum = 0.0;\n    readings.forEach((r) => { sum += r.celsius; });\n    Console.writeLine(readings.length);\n    Console.writeLine(readings[0].celsius);\n    Console.writeLine(readings.any((r) => r.sensor == 0));\n}\n```\n\n```text\n5\n23.75\ntrue\n```\n\n`sortBy` is an introsort: it allocates nothing and runs in O(n log n), but it is not stable. Sorted, reversed and\nduplicate-heavy input take about one pass. Its comparator returns a\nnegative number, zero or a positive number. These methods are written in CeeSafe, in `lib/core/slices.cee`.\n\nIn an async fn, they take async closures too: `await readings.forEach(async (r) => await upload(r))` calls the\nclosure on each element and waits for each call. Such a call is a task, so it needs its `await`. The same holds for\nyour own functions: a function marked `@effectsOf(f)` for its closure parameter `f` waits for an async `f`. It must\nthen only call `f`, or pass it on to another such function. It must not store it.\n\n## Effect lists\n\nIn kernel code, a callback may run where sleeping or allocating is forbidden. A function type can say what its\nfunctions may do: `@effects() fn(i32)` may neither sleep nor allocate, and `@effects(alloc) fn(i32)` may allocate but\nnot sleep. The compiler checks every function and closure given for it. See [Kernel code](kernel.md).\n\n```ceesafe\nfn onEach(values: Slice<u32>, f: borrow mut @effects() fn(u32)) {\n    for (v in values) {\n        f(v);\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    var largest: u32 = 0;\n    let values = [4u32, 11, 7];\n    onEach(values[..], (v) => {\n        if (v > largest) {\n            largest = v;\n        }\n    });\n    Console.writeLine(largest);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n11\n```\n\n## Floats\n\n`f32` and `f64` follow IEEE 754: no operation on them panics, and NaN and the infinities behave as specified. They\nprint as the shortest text that reads back as the same value.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn mean(values: Slice<f64>) -> f64 {\n    var sum = 0.0;\n    for (v in values) {\n        sum += v;\n    }\n    return sum / round<f64>(values.length);\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let samples = [1.5, 2.25, 4.0];\n    let m = mean(samples[..]);\n    Console.writeLine(m);\n    Console.writeLine(m.sqrt());\n    Console.writeLine(truncate<i64>(m * 1000.0));\n    Console.writeLine(0.1 + 0.2);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n2.5833333333333335\n1.6072751268321592\n2583\n0.30000000000000004\n```\n\nIntegers become floats with `as` when exact (`i32 as f64`) or `round<f64>(n)`; floats become integers with\n`truncate<T>(x)` (toward zero; out of range stops the program), `checkedTruncate` or `saturatingTruncate`. The math\nmethods are `sqrt`, `abs`, `floor`, `ceil`, `min` and `max`. Kernel code compiles with `--no-float`, which rejects\nfloat types altogether.\n\n## Next\n\n[Threads](threads.md).\n",
    17 =>
        "# Arrays, slices and collections\n\n## Arrays\n\n`[T; N]` is `N` values of `T`, inline: on the stack, or inside the struct that holds it. Arrays are values (copied\nwhen assigned) and their element type must be Copy.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    var squares = [0u32; 5];               // five zeros\n    for (i in 0..squares.length) {\n        squares[i] = truncate<u32>(i * i);    // usize to u32 could lose bits: say so\n    }\n    let primes = [2, 3, 5, 7];\n    var sum = 0;\n    for (p in primes) {\n        sum += p;\n    }\n    Console.writeLine(squares[4]);\n    Console.writeLine(sum);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n16\n17\n```\n\nEvery index is checked: `a[i]` with `i >= a.length` stops the program. The compiler removes the check where it can\nprove the index is in range, as in both loops above.\n\n## Slices\n\n`Slice<T>` is a borrowed view of consecutive elements, and `SliceMut<T>` a borrowed view that can change them. They\nare a pointer and a length, they follow every rule for references, and they are how functions take \"some elements\":\n\n```ceesafe\nfn total(values: Slice<i64>) -> i64 {\n    var sum: i64 = 0;\n    for (v in values) {\n        sum += v;\n    }\n    return sum;\n}\n\nfn double(values: SliceMut<i64>) {\n    for (i in 0..values.length) {\n        values[i] *= 2;\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    var numbers = [1i64, 2, 3, 4, 5];\n    double(mut numbers[1..3]);              // elements 1 and 2\n    Console.writeLine(total(numbers[..]));\n    Console.writeLine(total(numbers[3..]));\n}\n```\n\n```text\n20\n9\n```\n\n`a[lo..hi]`, `a[lo..]`, `a[..hi]` and `a[..]` take elements `lo` up to `hi`; the range is checked. A slice borrows\nthe whole array it came from, so the array cannot change while the slice is used.\n\nSlices also copy and fill in one checked operation (`d.copyFrom(s)`, `d.fill(v)`), swap elements, and sort and\nsearch with closures (see [Functions and closures](closures.md)).\n\n## Vectors\n\n`Vector<T>` owns a growable list on the heap. Pushing can fail, so `push` returns a `Result`.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    var v = Vector<i32>.create();          // allocates nothing yet\n    for (i in 0..5) {\n        v.push(i * 10)!;\n    }\n    Console.writeLine(v.length);\n    Console.writeLine(v[2]);\n    match (v.pop()) {                      // i32?: null when empty\n        Some(last) => { Console.writeLine(last); }\n        null => {}\n    }\n    v[0] = 99;\n    var sum = 0;\n    for (x in v) {\n        sum += x;\n    }\n    Console.writeLine(sum);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n5\n20\n40\n159\n```\n\nA vector can be passed where a `Slice<T>` is expected (it lends its elements), and `mut v` where a `SliceMut<T>` is.\n`v.view()` and `v.viewMut()` give the slices explicitly. Other members: `capacity`, `clear()`, `truncate(n)`,\n`swap(i, j)`, and `Vector<T>.withCapacity(n)`.\n\n**Invalidation is a compile error.** Growing a vector may move its elements, so while any element or view of it is\nborrowed, `push`, `pop`, `clear` and writes are rejected. This is the most common thing the borrow checker catches\nin code that would crash in C++.\n\n## Text: `StringView` and `String`\n\n`StringView` is a read-only view of UTF-8 text: string literals are views, and so are slices of strings. `String`\nowns growable text.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn greet(name: StringView) -> Result<String, AllocError> {\n    var s = String.withCapacity(name.length + 7)?;\n    s.append(\"Hello, \")?;\n    s.append(name)?;\n    s.append(\"! You are visitor \")?;\n    s.appendNumber(42)?;\n    return Ok(s);\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let message = greet(\"Ada\")!;\n    Console.writeLine(message);\n    let word: StringView = \"CeeSafe language\";\n    Console.writeLine(word[0..7]);\n    Console.writeLine(word.length);\n    Console.writeLine(word == \"CeeSafe language\");\n}\n```\n\n```text\nHello, Ada! You are visitor 42\nCeeSafe\n16\ntrue\n```\n\n- `length` is in bytes; `s[i]` is a byte (`u8`); `s.bytes()` is a `Slice<u8>`.\n- Slicing (`s[lo..hi]`) checks that both ends fall on character boundaries.\n- A `String` can only receive valid UTF-8 (`append` takes a view, which always is), so its contents are always valid\n  ",
    18 =>
        "text.\n- `==` compares bytes, on views and on `String`s.\n\nNumbers in other forms go through lib/core's helpers, which a `String` and a `Vector<u8>` both have (and `VectorBytes`\nas static functions): `appendHex(n, minWidth, upper)`, `appendOctal(n)`, `appendFixed(f, digits)`, rounded half to\neven as Rust's `{:.3}` is, and `appendPadded(text, width, align, fill)` for a field of `width` characters.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    var s = String.create();\n    s.appendHex(48879, 8, true)!;\n    s.append(\" \")!;\n    s.appendOctal(8)!;\n    s.append(\" \")!;\n    s.appendFixed(2.675, 2)!;\n    s.append(\" [\")!;\n    s.appendPadded(\"id\", 6, TextAlign.Right, ' ')!;\n    s.append(\"]\")!;\n    Console.writeLine(s.view());\n}\n```\n\n```text\n0000BEEF 10 2.67 [    id]\n```\n\n`2.675` is `2.67499999…` in binary, so it rounds down, as it does in Rust and C.\n\n## Stable references: `SlotMap` and `StableVector`\n\nSometimes values must refer to each other, as in a graph. A reference stored in a value would borrow the collection\nfor as long as the value lives. A `SlotMap` gives out **handles** instead: plain values that name an element, can be\nstored anywhere, and are checked on every use, so a handle to a removed element finds nothing instead of something\nelse.\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Node {\n    value: i32;\n    next: Handle<Node>?;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    var nodes = SlotMap<Node>.create();\n    let a = nodes.insert(Node { value: 1, next: null })!;\n    let b = nodes.insert(Node { value: 2, next: a })!;\n    match (nodes.get(b)) {\n        Some(node) => { Console.writeLine(node.value); }\n        null => {}\n    }\n    let removed = nodes.remove(a);\n    Console.writeLine(nodes.get(a) == null);             // the handle is stale now\n    Console.writeLine(nodes.length);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n2\ntrue\n1\n```\n\n`StableVector<T>` is a grow-only sequence whose elements never move, so an element can stay borrowed while more are\npushed.\n\n## Maps: `HashMap`\n\n```ceesafe\nfn key(text: StringView) -> String {\n    var s = String.create();\n    s.append(text)!;\n    return s;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    var ages = HashMap<String, u32>.create();\n    ages.put(key(\"ada\"), 36)!;\n    ages.put(key(\"alan\"), 41)!;\n    let previous = ages.put(key(\"ada\"), 37)!; // the old value: Some(36)\n    let ada = key(\"ada\");\n    match (ages.get(borrow ada)) {\n        Some(age) => { Console.writeLine(age); }\n        null => { Console.writeLine(\"unknown\"); }\n    }\n    for (i in 0..ages.length()) {\n        let name = ages.keyAt(i);\n        Console.writeLine(name.view());\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```text\n37\nada\nalan\n```\n\n- **Keys.** A key type implements `HashKey`; lib/std does for integers, `bool`, `String` and `Vector<u8>`.\n- **The hash.** It is keyed by a random value chosen once per process, so a client cannot send keys that all collide.\n- **Order.** Iteration follows insertion, so a program's output never depends on the hash. A removal moves the last\n  entry into the removed one's place.\n\n## Next\n\n[Generics and interfaces](generics.md).\n",
    19 =>
        "# CeeSafe for C and C++ programmers\n\nCeeSafe compiles to C, calls C directly, and is designed to live inside C codebases, kernels included. What it adds is a\ncompiler that tracks who owns each piece of memory and who is looking at it, and rejects the programs that would use\nit wrongly. What it removes is undefined behaviour.\n\n## At a glance\n\n| C / C++ | CeeSafe | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `int32_t x = 1;` | `let x: i32 = 1;` (or `var` to change it) | |\n| `T *`, `const T *` | `borrow mut T`, `borrow T` in safe code; `*mut T`, `*T` in `unsafe` | references are never null and never dangle |\n| `malloc` / `free`, `new` / `delete` | `Box.create(v)`; freed by its owner | allocation failure is a `Result`, not `NULL` or an exception |\n| `std::unique_ptr<T>` | `Box<T>` | |\n| `std::vector<T>` | `Vector<T>` | growing it while an element is referenced is a compile error |\n| `std::string`, `std::string_view` | `String`, `StringView` | UTF-8 only |\n| `std::optional<T>` | `T?` | |\n| destructors, RAII | `drop { … }` blocks; destruction at scope end | moves leave nothing behind to destroy twice |\n| `std::move(x)` | assignment moves by default; `move x` to end a use early | using a moved value is a compile error |\n| exceptions, `errno`, return codes | `Result<T, E>` and `?` | |\n| `assert`, `abort` | panics: bounds, overflow, unwrap | always on |\n| templates | generics with interface bounds | checked once, before use; no SFINAE |\n| `#include`, the preprocessor | packages and `import` | no macros, no conditional compilation |\n| `struct` layout | the same as C's, in declaration order | `@repr(C)` to cross the boundary |\n| `volatile` | `Volatile<T>`, `p.readVolatile()` | |\n| `-fsanitize=address,undefined` | needed only for `unsafe` code; `ceesafec run --interp` checks it | |\n\n## The same program\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <vector>\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector<int> v = {1, 2, 3};\n    int &first = v[0];\n    v.push_back(4);          // may reallocate: `first` now dangles\n    std::printf(\"%d\\n\", first);\n}\n```\n\nIn C++ this compiles and has undefined behaviour. In CeeSafe it does not compile:\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0241 -->\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    var v = Vector<i32>.create();\n    v.push(1)!;\n    let first = borrow v[0];\n    v.push(4)!;\n    Console.writeLine(first);\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:5:5: error[N0241]: cannot borrow 'v' as mutable while 'v[0]' is borrowed\n  |\n5 |     v.push(4)!;\n  |     ^\n  = note: 'v[0]' is borrowed here (4:5)\n  = note: the borrow is used later here (6:5)\n```\n\nThe fixes are the ones you would reach for anyway: copy the value (`let first = v[0];`), or take the reference after\nthe last push.\n\n## No undefined behaviour\n\nEvery operation that is undefined in C is defined in CeeSafe, by a check or by a rule:\n\n| Undefined in C | In CeeSafe |\n|---|---|\n| signed overflow | stops the program (`wrappingAdd` and friends where wrapping is meant) |\n| out-of-bounds access | stops the program |\n| use after free, double free | compile errors |\n| reading uninitialised memory | a compile error |\n| null dereference | there are no null references; optionals must be checked |\n| shift by the width or more | stops the program |\n| unsequenced modifications | evaluation order is left to right |\n| strict aliasing violations | the generated C never relies on them |\n\nThe generated C is written so that it has no undefined behaviour itself, and is warning-free under strict flags, so\nthe C compiler's optimiser has nothing to exploit.\n\n## Working with an existing C codebase\n\n- Call C functions from an `extern \"C\"` block, inside `unsafe`.\n- Export CeeSafe functions with `@export`; `ceesafec` writes `ceesafe_exports.h` for your C code to include.\n- Use your codebase's own structs from its headers (`@header`, `@ctype`) instead of re-declaring them: the C compiler\n  checks every field CeeSafe uses.\n- Build a freestanding object with `--profile freestanding --emit obj` and link it with your build.\n\nSee [Unsafe code and C](../unsafe-and-c.md) and [Kernel code]",
    20 =>
        "(../kernel.md).\n\n## Things you will miss\n\nThe preprocessor, inline assembly, unions (enums with payloads replace tagged unions), bit fields, pointer\narithmetic in safe code, varargs, goto, and in C++, classes with inheritance and virtual functions, operator\noverloading, overloads, exceptions, and `constexpr` evaluation.\n",
    21 =>
        "# CeeSafe for C#, Java and TypeScript programmers\n\nCeeSafe's syntax was chosen to look like C# and TypeScript: braces, `fn name(x: T) -> R`, `let` and `var`, generics in\nangle brackets, `public` and `internal`, `using`, interfaces. Underneath, it works very differently: there is no\ngarbage collector, no classes, no inheritance, no exceptions and no null references. Memory is managed at compile\ntime, by tracking ownership.\n\n## At a glance\n\n| C# / Java / TypeScript | CeeSafe | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `var x = 1;` / `const x = 1;` | `var x = 1;` / `let x = 1;` | |\n| `class` | `struct` with methods | no inheritance; values, not references |\n| `interface`, `class C : I` | `interface`, `impl I for C` | interfaces are generic bounds, not types |\n| `new Point(1, 2)` | `Point { x: 1, y: 2 }` | on the stack, or `Box.create(…)` on the heap |\n| garbage collection | ownership: destroyed when the owner goes away | deterministic, at a point the language defines |\n| `IDisposable`, `using` | `drop { … }`, `using` | every owner runs `drop`, not only `using` |\n| `null` references | `T?`, which must be checked | a plain `T` is never null |\n| exceptions, `try`/`catch` | `Result<T, E>`, `?`, `match` | panics are bugs and cannot be caught |\n| `ref`, `out` | `borrow mut T` parameters, `mut x` at the call | |\n| `List<T>`, `string` | `Vector<T>`, `String` / `StringView` | |\n| LINQ, lambdas | `sortBy`, `forEach`, `any`, `all`, `retain`; `(x) => x > 2` | lambdas borrow and cannot escape |\n| `async` / `await`, `Task.WhenAll`, `Task.WhenAny` | `async fn`, `await`; `Async.scope`, `Async.all`, `Async.race` | a task is awaited where it starts, never stored; see [Async tasks](../async.md) |\n| threads | `Thread.spawn`, `Thread.both`, `Arc`, `Mutex`, atomics | see [Threads](../threads.md) |\n| NuGet, Maven, npm | `ceesafe.toml`, path and git dependencies | |\n\n## The same program\n\n```csharp\nclass Account {\n    public long Balance { get; private set; }\n    public void Deposit(long amount) => Balance += amount;\n}\n\nvar a = new Account();\na.Deposit(100);\nConsole.WriteLine(a.Balance);\n```\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Account {\n    private balance: i64;\n\n    public fn create() -> Account => Account { balance: 0 };\n    public fn deposit(borrow mut this, amount: i64) {\n        this.balance += amount;\n    }\n    public fn current(borrow this) -> i64 => this.balance;     // a method cannot share a field's name\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    var a = Account.create();\n    a.deposit(100);\n    Console.writeLine(a.current());\n}\n```\n\n```text\n100\n```\n\nThe receiver says how a method uses its object: `borrow this` reads it, `borrow mut this` changes it, and `this`\ntakes it by value. Calling `deposit` needs `a` to be a `var`.\n\n## What changes\n\n**Values, not references.** Assigning a struct copies it (if every field is copyable) or moves it, never shares it.\nTwo variables never point to the same object unless one *borrows* the other, and a borrow is checked: while something\nis borrowed mutably, nothing else can use it.\n\n**Destruction is deterministic.** An object is destroyed when its owner goes out of scope, by assignment, or when the\nobject that owns it is destroyed. A `drop` block runs then, exactly once: closing a file or releasing a lock does not\ndepend on a collector or a `finally`.\n\n**Errors are return values.** A function that can fail returns `Result<T, E>`; the caller matches it, or passes it on\nwith `?`. Out of memory is an error value too. Bugs (an index out of range, an overflow) stop the program instead of\nthrowing.\n\n**No null.** `StringView` can never be null; `StringView?` can, and the compiler requires a check before use.\n\n**Generics are compiled per type,** like C++ templates and unlike Java's erasure, and checked once, like C#'s.\nInterfaces constrain type parameters (`<T: Shape>`), but there is no `List<Shape>` holding different shapes: use an\nenum.\n\n## Things you will miss\n\nClasses and inheritance, interfaces as types, reflection, attributes on everything, properties, events, LINQ's ",
    22 =>
        "query\nsyntax, string interpolation, exceptions, a task you can store and await later, and a large standard library.\n",
    23 =>
        "# CeeSafe for Go programmers\n\nGo and CeeSafe share some taste: a small language, errors as values, `defer`, a canonical formatter, packages as\ndirectories, and fast feedback. They differ in how memory is managed. Go has a garbage collector and a runtime; CeeSafe\nhas neither, and tracks ownership at compile time instead, which is what lets it run in a kernel.\n\n## At a glance\n\n| Go | CeeSafe | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `x := 1`, `var x int32` | `let x = 1;`, `var x: i32 = 0;` | `let` cannot be reassigned |\n| `func f(a int) (int, error)` | `fn f(a: i32) -> Result<i32, E>` | |\n| `if err != nil { return err }` | `?` | |\n| `panic`, `recover` | panics abort; no recovery | |\n| `defer` | `defer` | runs at the end of the block, not the function |\n| slices `[]T` (growable) | `Vector<T>` (owns, grows) and `Slice<T>` (borrows) | a slice cannot outlive what it views |\n| maps | not built in yet | `SlotMap` for handles |\n| `nil` | `null`, only for optionals (`T?`) | |\n| interfaces, satisfied implicitly | interfaces, implemented explicitly with `impl` | generic bounds, resolved at compile time |\n| generics `[T any]` | `<T>` | |\n| goroutines, channels | async tasks in a scope, `Channel<T>` (`sendAsync`, `recvAsync`; `send`, `recv` between threads) | a task never outlives its scope; see [Async tasks](../async.md) |\n| `gofmt` | `ceesafec fmt` | one canonical style, no options |\n| `go.mod`, `go.sum` | `ceesafe.toml`, `ceesafe.lock` | path and git dependencies, pinned by commit and hash |\n| `go vet`, the race detector | the compiler: ownership, borrows, context effects | |\n\n## The same program\n\n```go\npackage main\n\nimport (\n    \"errors\"\n    \"fmt\"\n)\n\nfunc divide(a, b int) (int, error) {\n    if b == 0 {\n        return 0, errors.New(\"division by zero\")\n    }\n    return a / b, nil\n}\n\nfunc average(xs []int) (int, error) {\n    sum := 0\n    for _, x := range xs {\n        sum += x\n    }\n    return divide(sum, len(xs))\n}\n\nfunc main() {\n    avg, err := average([]int{3, 5, 10})\n    if err != nil {\n        fmt.Println(err)\n        return\n    }\n    fmt.Println(avg)\n}\n```\n\n```ceesafe\nenum MathError {\n    DivisionByZero,\n}\n\nfn divide(a: i64, b: i64) -> Result<i64, MathError> {\n    if (b == 0) {\n        return Err(MathError.DivisionByZero);\n    }\n    return Ok(a / b);\n}\n\nfn average(xs: Slice<i64>) -> Result<i64, MathError> {\n    var sum: i64 = 0;\n    for (x in xs) {\n        sum += x;\n    }\n    return divide(sum, truncate<i64>(xs.length));    // usize to i64 could lose bits: say so\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let values = [3i64, 5, 10];\n    match (average(values[..])) {\n        Ok(avg) => { Console.writeLine(avg); }\n        Err(MathError.DivisionByZero) => { Console.writeLine(\"division by zero\"); }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```text\n6\n```\n\n`xs.length` is a `usize`, and CeeSafe has no implicit conversions: `as` converts only where nothing can be lost, so a\n`usize` becomes an `i64` through `truncate<i64>`.\n\n## What changes\n\n**No garbage collector.** Every value has one owner and is destroyed when the owner goes away. Where Go lets any\nnumber of pointers share an object and keeps it alive, CeeSafe lets one owner keep it and others *borrow* it for a while,\nand checks that no borrow outlives the object.\n\n**Allocation is visible and fallible.** Values live on the stack unless the code asks for a box, a vector or a string,\nand each of those returns a `Result`: out of memory is an error, not a crash.\n\n**Errors propagate with `?`** instead of `if err != nil`. The error type is part of the signature, usually an enum.\n\n**Interfaces are explicit and static.** A type implements an interface with an `impl` block, and interfaces are used\nas generic bounds, resolved at compile time. There is no interface value holding \"some type\", and no type switch.\n\n**Overflow is checked.** Integer arithmetic that overflows stops the program; Go wraps silently.\n\n## Things you will miss\n\nGoroutines that outlive their caller, `select` over many channels (`Async.race` takes two tasks), maps, interface values and type switch",
    24 => "es, struct embedding, the standard library,\nand Go's fast tooling for large codebases.\n",
    25 =>
        "# Coming from another language\n\nCeeSafe borrows deliberately from several languages, and each will feel partly familiar:\n\n- its **syntax** from C# and TypeScript: braces, `fn name(x: T) -> R`, `let` and `var`, generics in `<…>`;\n- its **ownership and borrowing** from Rust, with less annotation and a simpler checker;\n- its **explicit allocators** from Zig, with use-after-free made a compile error;\n- its **regions** from Cyclone, and its **compile-through-C** design and compiler-managed memory from Goose;\n- its **deterministic destruction** from C++'s RAII, without the undefined behaviour.\n\nThese pages map what you know to CeeSafe, show the same program side by side, and point out the differences that will\ntrip you up.\n\n| If you know | Read | The biggest changes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Rust | [CeeSafe for Rust programmers](rust.md) | no lifetime syntax on plain borrows; allocators per allocation; checks in every build; no macros or traits-as-types |\n| Zig | [CeeSafe for Zig programmers](zig.md) | use-after-free and dangling references are compile errors; checks never turn off; generics instead of `comptime` |\n| C or C++ | [CeeSafe for C and C++ programmers](c-and-cpp.md) | the compiler tracks ownership and borrows; no undefined behaviour; no preprocessor |\n| C#, Java or TypeScript | [CeeSafe for C#, Java and TypeScript programmers](csharp-java-typescript.md) | no garbage collector, no classes or inheritance, no exceptions, no null references |\n| Go | [CeeSafe for Go programmers](go.md) | no garbage collector; errors propagate with `?`; interfaces are explicit and static; no goroutines yet |\n\nWhy CeeSafe is built the way it is, against those languages, is on the repository's\n[README](https://github.com/CeeSafe/CeeSafe#why-ceesafe).\n",
    26 =>
        "# CeeSafe for Rust programmers\n\nCeeSafe's safety model is Rust's: single ownership, moves, shared and exclusive borrows, deterministic destruction, no\ngarbage collector. What differs is how much you write down, where memory comes from, and how much the language does\nfor you. This page assumes you know Rust well.\n\n## At a glance\n\n| Rust | CeeSafe | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `let x = 1;` / `let mut x = 1;` | `let x = 1;` / `var x = 1;` | |\n| shadowing (`let x = x + 1;`) | not allowed | two locals in scope never share a name |\n| `&T`, `&mut T` | `borrow T`, `borrow mut T` | |\n| `f(&x)`, `f(&mut x)` | `f(x)`, `f(mut x)` | a shared borrow is implicit at the call; a mutable one is marked |\n| `fn f<'a>(a: &'a T, b: &T) -> &'a T` | `fn f(a: borrow T, b: borrow T) -> borrow(a) T` | the result names its sources; no lifetime parameters |\n| `struct S<'a> { s: &'a str }` | `struct S<region r> { s: StringView<r>; }` | only types that store references declare regions |\n| `Box<T>`, `Vec<T>`, `String`, `&str` | `Box<T>`, `Vector<T>`, `String`, `StringView` | |\n| `Option<T>`: `Some(x)`, `None` | `T?`: `Some(x)`, `null` | a `T` converts to `T?` where one is expected |\n| `Result<T, E>`, `?` | `Result<T, E>`, `?` | the same |\n| `.unwrap()` | `!` (postfix) | `r!`, `o!` |\n| `panic!` (unwinds by default) | panics always abort | no unwinding, no `catch_unwind` |\n| `impl Drop for S` | `drop { … }` inside the struct | |\n| `trait`, `impl Trait for T`, `T: Trait` | `interface`, `impl Interface for T`, `T: Interface` | default methods, `Self`, associated types and constants, supertraits |\n| `Box<dyn Trait>`, `&dyn Trait` | `Box<dyn I>`, `borrow dyn I` | |\n| `fn f<const N: usize>()`, `f::<3>()`, `f::<{ X + 1 }>()` | `fn f<const N: usize>()`, `f<3>()`, `f<Y>()` with `const Y: usize = X + 1;` | `bool`, integer and fieldless-enum values; a `const fn` is an ordinary `fn` |\n| `#[derive(Clone, Copy)]` | automatic | a struct is Copy when all its fields are and it has no `drop` |\n| `x as u8` (truncates) | `truncate<u8>(x)` | `as` only converts where nothing can be lost |\n| `wrapping_add`, `checked_add`, `saturating_add` | `wrappingAdd`, `checkedAdd`, `saturatingAdd` | |\n| `\\|x\\| x + 1` | `(x) => x + 1` | a closure literal borrows its captures and cannot escape |\n| `Box::new(move \\|x\\| x + n)` | `Box.create(move (x) => x + n)!` | a `Box<dyn fn(u64) -> u64>`, which owns its captures |\n| `match x { A => …, }` | `match (x) { E.A => { … } }` | variants are qualified; statement arms are blocks |\n| `Some(n) if n > 3 =>`, `A \\| B`, `0..=9`, `x @ P` | `Some(n) if (n > 3) =>`, `A \\| B`, `0..=9`, `x @ P` | the guard is parenthesised |\n| `\"get\" =>`, `b\"cjs\" =>`, `[first, rest @ ..] =>` | `\"get\" =>`, `\"cjs\" =>`, `[first, rest @ ..] =>` | a string pattern is typed by the value: a `StringView` or a `Slice<u8>` |\n| `if let Some(v) = e`, `while let …`, `matches!(e, P)` | `if (e is Some(v))`, `while (e is …)`, `e is P` | one operator; it chains with `&&` |\n| `std::thread::spawn(move \\|\\| …)` | `Thread.spawn(move () => …)!` | with `Arc`, `Mutex` and atomics |\n| `Send`, `Sync` | `Send`, `Share` | |\n| `println!(\"{}\", x)` | `Console.writeLine(x)` | no macros, no format strings yet |\n| `#![no_std]` | `--profile freestanding` | |\n| `unsafe { }` with a `// SAFETY:` convention | `unsafe { }` with a required `/// Safety:` doc line | the compiler enforces it |\n| Cargo, `Cargo.toml`, `Cargo.lock` | `ceesafec`, `ceesafe.toml`, `ceesafe.lock` | path, git and registry dependencies |\n| `rustfmt`, `rustdoc` | `ceesafec fmt`, `ceesafec doc` | |\n\n## The same program\n\n```rust\nfn longest_word(text: &str) -> Option<&str> {\n    let mut best: Option<&str> = None;\n    for word in text.split(' ') {\n        if best.map_or(true, |b| word.len() > b.len()) {\n            best = Some(word);\n        }\n    }\n    best\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    match longest_word(\"the quick brownish fox\") {\n        Some(w) => println!(\"{}\", w),\n        None => println!(\"empty\"),\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```ceesafe\nfn longestWord<region r>(t",
    27 =>
        "ext: StringView<r>) -> StringView<r>? {\n    var best: StringView<r>? = null;\n    for (word in text.split(\" \")) {\n        match (best) {\n            Some(b) if (b.length >= word.length) => {}\n            _ => { best = word; }\n        }\n    }\n    return best;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    match (longestWord(\"the quick brownish fox\")) {\n        Some(w) => { Console.writeLine(w); }\n        null => { Console.writeLine(\"empty\"); }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```text\nbrownish\n```\n\nThe CeeSafe version loops over the `split` iterator where Rust would call `map_or` (there are no adapters), tests\nwith a guard, returns explicitly, and its signature says the result views the input through the region `r`. With a\nsingle reference parameter, as here, the region could also be left out:\n`fn longestWord(text: StringView) -> StringView?` means the same.\n\n## Ownership and borrowing: what is the same\n\nMoves, Copy types, \"one writer or many readers\", borrows ending at their last use, two-phase borrows for\n`v.push(v.length)`, references that cannot outlive their referent, and destruction at scope end in reverse order: all\nas in Rust. Error messages name the borrow, where it was made and where it is used later.\n\n## What is different\n\n**Less annotation.** A function that returns a reference names the parameters it may borrow from,\n`-> borrow(a, b) T`, instead of declaring lifetime parameters. Types that store references declare `region`\nparameters, and so do functions that pass such types through; plain functions over references never do. Regions are\nerased: `Token<a>` and `Token<b>` are the same type.\n\n**A simpler checker.** CeeSafe's borrow checker is intraprocedural and keeps one set of loans per value, where Rust\nreasons about lifetimes. Borrows end at their last use, as with NLL, but some programs Rust accepts are rejected; the\nusual fixes are to end a borrow earlier, copy a value out, or use an index or a slot-map handle. Reference locals are\n`let`, never `var`: assigning to a reference writes through it.\n\n**Allocators per allocation.** Every allocating type takes its allocator as a type parameter (`Box<T, A>`,\n`Vector<T, A>`), and every allocation returns a `Result`: there is no infallible `push`. Arenas, pools, fixed buffers\nand scratch frames are built in, and allocations from a region borrow it, so they cannot outlive it.\n\n**Checks in every build.** Integer overflow panics in release builds too; there is no `--release` that turns it off.\nBounds and overflow checks the compiler proves unnecessary are removed, and `--explain-bounds-checks` shows which.\n\n**Interfaces are traits without the corners.** Interfaces have default methods, `Self`, associated types and\nconstants, and supertraits, and `impl<T> I for Wrapper<T>` implements one for a generic type; there are no blanket\n`impl<T: A> B for T` and no `impl Trait` in argument or result position. A collection of different types is an enum or\nholds `Box<dyn I>`.\n\n**Closures borrow, unless they are boxed.** A closure literal borrows what it captures and has a reference type\n(`borrow fn(i32) -> bool`, `borrow mut fn(i32)`), so it cannot be returned or stored. A `move` closure owns its\ncaptures and is boxed where it is made: `Box.create(move (x: u64) => x + n)!` is a `Box<dyn fn(u64) -> u64>`, and one\nthat changes its captures a `Box<dyn mut fn()>`. There is no `Fn`/`FnMut`/`FnOnce` trait or `impl Fn`: a parameter\ntakes `borrow fn(…)` or `borrow mut fn(…)`.\n\n**Threads and tasks.** `Thread.spawn(move () => …)` and `Thread.both` run threads; `Arc`, `Mutex` and lib/core's\natomics share state, and `Send` and `Share` are Rust's `Send` and `Sync`. `async fn` and `await` run tasks on lib/std's\nexecutor, on one thread or several (type-system.md §38, §40).\n\n**No macros, no iterator chains.** `for` walks ranges, arrays, slices, vectors and iterators: lib/core's\n`Iterator<T>` (`fn next(borrow mut this) -> T?`), through a bound `I: Iterator<T>` or a `dyn Iterator<T>`, and any\nstruct with such a `next`. The item is an interface pa",
    28 =>
        "rameter, not an associated type: `Iterator<u8>`, not\n`Iterator<Item = u8>`. There are no adapters: a chain of `map`, `filter` and `collect` is the loop's body, and slices\nand vectors have `sortBy`, `forEach`, `any`, `all` and `retain`.\n\n**Through C.** CeeSafe compiles to C11, which any C toolchain or kernel build can take, including MSVC and compilers for\ntargets LLVM does not support. The generated C is readable and maps back to CeeSafe lines.\n\n**Kernel checking.** Context effects (`@context(atomic)`, `@effects(sleep)`) reject sleeping under a spin lock across\ncalls, at compile time. Rust has no equivalent in the language.\n\n## Things you will miss\n\n`Rc` (CeeSafe has `Arc`, and lib/core's `Cell` and `RefCell`, whose `read()` and `write()` are Rust's `borrow()` and\n`borrow_mut()`: type-system.md §41), macros, iterator adapters, blanket impls, `impl Trait`, shadowing, the\nstandard library's breadth, and crates.io. Several are planned or recorded as open decisions.\n",
    29 =>
        "# CeeSafe for Zig programmers\n\nZig and CeeSafe agree on a lot: no hidden allocation, allocators as explicit values, errors as values, `defer`, no\ngarbage collector, first-class C interop, and code that can run in a kernel. They differ on what guarantees safety.\nZig checks bounds, overflow and null at run time in its safe build modes, and leaves use-after-free and dangling\npointers to the programmer. CeeSafe makes those compile errors, through ownership and borrowing, and keeps its run-time\nchecks on in every build.\n\n## At a glance\n\n| Zig | CeeSafe | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `const x = 1;` / `var x: u32 = 1;` | `let x = 1;` / `var x: u32 = 1;` | |\n| `[4]u8`, `[]u8`, `[]const u8` | `[u8; 4]`, `SliceMut<u8>`, `Slice<u8>` | slices are borrows: they cannot outlive their array |\n| `?T`, `null`, `orelse`, `.?` | `T?`, `null`, `match`, `!` | |\n| `!T`, `error.X`, `try`, `catch` | `Result<T, E>`, `Err(E.X)`, `?`, `match` | errors are ordinary enums |\n| `catch unreachable` | `!` (postfix) | |\n| `defer`, `errdefer` | `defer` | locals are destroyed automatically: most `defer`s are unnecessary |\n| `allocator.create(T)`, `allocator.destroy(p)` | `Box.createIn(allocator, value)`; destroyed automatically | the allocator is part of the box's type |\n| `std.ArrayList(T)` with an allocator | `Vector<T, A>` | |\n| `std.heap.ArenaAllocator` | `Arena` | allocations borrow the arena: none survives it |\n| `std.heap.FixedBufferAllocator` | `FixedBufferAllocator` | |\n| `+%`, `+\\|`, `@addWithOverflow` | `wrappingAdd`, `saturatingAdd`, `checkedAdd` | |\n| `@intCast`, `@truncate` | `as` (lossless only), `truncate<T>` | |\n| `comptime` generics | `<T>` generics with interface bounds | no compile-time execution |\n| `comptime` flags and sizes (`comptime ts: bool`) | const parameters: `fn parse<const TS: bool>()`, `[u8; N]` | `bool`, integer and enum values, one copy per value |\n| `@cImport` | `extern \"C\"` declarations; C structs imported by `@header` | |\n| `export fn`, `callconv(.C)` | `@export`, `@callback` | |\n| Debug / ReleaseSafe / ReleaseFast | one mode, always checked | proved checks are removed |\n| `std.debug.print` | `Console.writeLine` | |\n\n## The same program\n\n```zig\n// Zig 0.13\nconst std = @import(\"std\");\n\nfn sumAll(allocator: std.mem.Allocator, n: usize) !u64 {\n    var list = std.ArrayList(u64).init(allocator);\n    defer list.deinit();\n    var i: u64 = 0;\n    while (i < n) : (i += 1) try list.append(i * i);\n    var total: u64 = 0;\n    for (list.items) |x| total += x;\n    return total;\n}\n\npub fn main() !void {\n    const total = try sumAll(std.heap.page_allocator, 10);\n    std.debug.print(\"{}\\n\", .{total});\n}\n```\n\n```ceesafe\nfn sumAll(n: u64) -> Result<u64, AllocError> {\n    var list = Vector<u64>.create();          // freed at the end of the function, by its owner\n    for (i in 0..n) {\n        list.push(i * i)?;\n    }\n    var total: u64 = 0;\n    for (x in list) {\n        total += x;\n    }\n    return Ok(total);\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(sumAll(10)!);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n285\n```\n\nThere is no `deinit`: the vector is destroyed when its owner goes out of scope, on every path, including the early\nreturn of `?`. Another allocator goes in the type, `Vector<u64, A>` for an allocator type `A`. An arena works the Zig\nway instead: `arena.allocArray(n, 0u64)` returns a slice that borrows the arena, and everything is freed with it.\n\n## What changes\n\n**Lifetimes are checked.** Returning a slice of a local array, keeping a pointer into an `ArrayList` across an\n`append`, or using an arena allocation after `arena.deinit()`: each is a compile error in CeeSafe, with a message naming\nthe borrow. In Zig these compile and fail at run time, if they are noticed at all.\n\n**Destruction is automatic.** A struct with a `drop` block, or that owns a box, vector or file, is destroyed exactly\nonce when its owner goes away. Ownership moves on assignment, so there is no double free to avoid by convention.\n\n**Errors carry types.** An error is any type, usually an enum with payloads ",
    30 =>
        "(`ParseError.BadDigit(c)`), not a global\nerror set. `?` requires the function's error type to match; there are no implicit error-set unions.\n\n**Generics, not `comptime`.** CeeSafe's generics are type parameters checked once, for every type argument, against the\nbounds you declare. There is no compile-time evaluation of arbitrary code, no `@typeInfo` and no reflection. Constants\nare evaluated at compile time; functions are not. A `comptime` flag or size becomes a const parameter,\n`fn scan<const TS: bool>(…)` or `struct Buffer<const N: usize>`, and `if (TS)` in it is folded in each copy.\n\n**Safety is not a build mode.** There is no ReleaseFast. Overflow and bounds checks are always on, and the compiler\nremoves those it proves cannot fail.\n\n## Things you will miss\n\n`comptime`, `@cImport` of whole headers, packed structs and arbitrary-width integers (`u3`), SIMD vectors, inline\nassembly, the cross-compiling toolchain in one binary, and Zig's build system. CeeSafe relies on a C compiler for\ncode generation and cross compilation.\n",
    31 =>
        "# Debugging\n\nA CeeSafe program is debugged as the C it compiles to, with the C mapped back to CeeSafe lines, so lldb and gdb work\nwithout knowing anything about CeeSafe.\n\n## A debug build\n\n```sh\nceesafec build -g main.cee -o program\nlldb ./program          # or: gdb ./program\n```\n\n`-g` writes `#line` directives into the C and compiles it with `-g -O0`. Breakpoints then go on CeeSafe lines, and\nstepping moves through CeeSafe statements.\n\n| | lldb | gdb |\n|---|---|---|\n| Stop at a line | `b main.cee:12` | `break main.cee:12` |\n| Stop when a check fails, before the program exits | `b ceesafe_panic` | `break ceesafe_panic` |\n| Where am I | `bt` | `bt` |\n\n## Names\n\nThe C names follow fixed rules, so a CeeSafe name is easy to find:\n\n| CeeSafe | C |\n|---|---|\n| function `length` | `ceeF_length` |\n| method `Counter.bump` | `ceeM_7Counter_bump` (the type's name, prefixed by its length) |\n| local or parameter `sum` | `v_sum_1` |\n| a temporary the compiler made | `t_3` |\n| field `x` | `f_x` |\n| struct `Point` | `ceeT_Point` |\n| an item of a dependency `geometry` | `ceeF_8geometry_area`, `ceeT_8geometry_Point`, `ceeM_08geometry5Point_norm` (the package's name after its length) |\n\n## CeeSafe names in the debugger\n\n`editors/debug` adds two commands that print with CeeSafe names:\n\n```sh\nlldb ./program -o \"command script import editors/debug/ceesafe_lldb.py\"\ngdb ./program -ex \"source editors/debug/ceesafe_gdb.py\"\n```\n\n- `ceesafe-locals`: the current frame's locals and parameters, without the compiler's temporaries, with struct fields by\n  their CeeSafe names: `p = { x: 3, y: 4 }`.\n- `ceesafe-bt`: the backtrace as `Counter.bump at main.cee:6`.\n- `ceesafe-tasks`: in an async program, the thread's tasks and the line each one waits at, with a scope's children\n  indented under the task that awaits the scope.\n\n```text\n(lldb) b main.cee:12\n(lldb) run\n(lldb) ceesafe-locals\np = { x: 3, y: 4 }\nsum = 7\n(lldb) ceesafe-bt\nlength at main.cee:12\nmain at main.cee:18\n```\n\n## Debug assertions\n\n`debugAssert(condition)` and `debugAssert(condition, message)` check what should always hold, at no cost in a release\nbuild: they are evaluated only in a debug build (`-g`), under `ceesafec test` and in the interpreter, and a release\nbuild leaves them out entirely. `--debug-assertions` and `--no-debug-assertions` turn them on or off in any build.\nA false one panics, as `assert` does.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn isSorted(values: Slice<u32>) -> bool {\n    for (k in 1..values.length) {\n        if (values[k - 1] > values[k]) {\n            return false;\n        }\n    }\n    return true;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    var scores = Vector<u32>.create();\n    scores.push(3)!;\n    scores.push(8)!;\n    debugAssert(isSorted(scores.view()), \"scores out of order\");\n    Console.writeLine(scores.length);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n2\n```\n\nSince a release build leaves the call out, the condition and message must not change anything that exists before\nthem, or the two builds would behave differently. The compiler checks this, through every function they call: no\nassignment, `mut` argument, move or drop of an outer variable, no I/O, no C call, no call through a closure or `dyn`,\nand no way out but their end: no `?`, no `break` or `continue` to a loop outside them, no `Process.exit`. They may\nread, allocate, and change their own locals. To assert on something that changes, do the change first:\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0360 -->\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    var queue = Vector<u32>.create();\n    queue.push(3)!;\n    debugAssert(queue.pop() != null);\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:4:5: error[N0360]: 'debugAssert' must not change what exists before it, since release builds leave it out (ADR 0087 §2.5): this lends as 'mut' 'queue', which exists before the call\n  |\n4 |     debugAssert(queue.pop() != null);\n  |     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n```\n\nHere, `let last = queue.pop(); debugAssert(last != null);` pops in every build. `assert` is always evaluated, so it\nhas no such rule.\n\n## When the program panics\n\nA failed check prints the reason and ",
    32 =>
        "the CeeSafe location, and exits with status 134. Under a debugger, a breakpoint on\n`ceesafe_panic` stops at the moment of the panic, with the whole call stack to inspect:\n\n```text\n(lldb) b ceesafe_panic\n(lldb) run\n(lldb) ceesafe-bt\nceesafe_panic at ceesafe_rt_core.c:207\ncee_add_u8 at rt.h:190\ngrow at main.cee:2\nmain at main.cee:7\n```\n\nIn an async program, stopped in a child task of a scope:\n\n```text\n(lldb) ceesafe-tasks\ngather at main.cee:18 (waiting for its scope)\n  async closure at main.cee:20 (running)\n  async closure at main.cee:25 (waiting for a deadline)\n```\n\nEach line is the task's own async fn and the line where it last suspended. The running task is at the `await` it\nresumed from, and `ceesafe-bt` shows where it is now.\n\nFor memory errors in `unsafe` code, `ceesafec run --interp` is often faster: the interpreter stops at the first invalid\naccess and names it.\n",
    33 =>
        "# Editors\n\n## VS Code\n\nThe extension in `editors/vscode` gives `.cee` files syntax highlighting, and through the language server: errors as\nyou type, the type or signature on hover, go to definition (F12), the outline, and formatting (Shift+Alt+F).\n\nInstall it from the [Visual Studio Marketplace](https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=ceesafe.ceesafe-language):\nsearch for **CeeSafe** in VS Code's Extensions view, or run\n\n```sh\ncode --install-extension ceesafe.ceesafe-language\n```\n\nEvery [release](https://github.com/CeeSafe/CeeSafe/releases) also has it as `ceesafe-language-<version>.vsix`, built and\ntested with that release's `ceesafec` (its checksum is in `SHA256SUMS`), for **Extensions: Install from VSIX…**.\n\nTo build it from the repository instead, with [Vite+](https://viteplus.dev/):\n\n```sh\ncd editors/vscode\nvp install\nvp test                 # the grammar, and ceesafec lsp through VS Code's own client library\nvp run package          # makes ceesafe-language-<version>.vsix\ncode --install-extension ceesafe-language-*.vsix\n```\n\nThe extension starts `ceesafec lsp`. It finds `ceesafec` on your `PATH`, or where the setting `ceesafe.server.path` says.\n\n## Other editors\n\n`ceesafec lsp` is a standard Language Server Protocol server on standard input and output, so any editor with an LSP\nclient can use it: Neovim, Helix, Emacs, Zed, Sublime Text. Configure it to run `ceesafec lsp` for `.cee` files. The\nTextMate grammar in `editors/vscode/syntaxes/ceesafe.tmLanguage.json` works in editors that accept TextMate grammars.\n\n## What the server does\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Diagnostics | every error and note of `ceesafec check`, as you type |\n| Hover | the type of an expression; `let`/`var` and the type of a variable; a function's signature; a field's type |\n| Go to definition | locals, parameters, functions, methods, fields, structs, enums and interfaces in the file |\n| Document symbols | the outline: functions, structs with their fields and methods, enums, interfaces, constants |\n| Formatting | the whole file, in `ceesafec fmt`'s style |\n\nA file that belongs to a package is checked with the package and its locked dependencies; any other file is checked\non its own. The details are in [Tooling §2](../spec/tooling.md#_2-language-server-t3).\n",
    34 =>
        "# Errors\n\nCeeSafe has two kinds of failure, and keeps them apart:\n\n- **Recoverable errors** are values: a function that can fail returns `Result<T, E>`, and the caller decides what to\n  do. Allocation failure and I/O errors are of this kind.\n- **Bugs** stop the program: an index out of bounds, an integer overflow, dividing by zero, unwrapping an error or a\n  `null`. This is a **panic**: the program prints the reason and where it happened, and exits at once. There is no\n  unwinding and no catching.\n\n## `Result`\n\n```ceesafe\nenum ParseError {\n    Empty,\n    BadDigit(u8),\n    TooLarge,\n}\n\nfn parse(text: StringView) -> Result<u32, ParseError> {\n    if (text.length == 0) {\n        return Err(ParseError.Empty);\n    }\n    var value: u32 = 0;\n    for (c in text.bytes()) {\n        if (c < '0' || c > '9') {\n            return Err(ParseError.BadDigit(c));\n        }\n        let next = value.checkedMul(10);\n        if (next == null) {\n            return Err(ParseError.TooLarge);\n        }\n        value = next! + (c - '0') as u32;\n    }\n    return Ok(value);\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    match (parse(\"2026\")) {\n        Ok(n) => { Console.writeLine(n); }\n        Err(ParseError.BadDigit(c)) => {\n            Console.write(\"not a digit: \");\n            Console.writeLine(c);\n        }\n        Err(_) => { Console.writeLine(\"no number\"); }\n    }\n    match (parse(\"20x6\")) {\n        Ok(n) => { Console.writeLine(n); }\n        Err(ParseError.BadDigit(c)) => {\n            Console.write(\"not a digit: \");\n            Console.writeLine(c);\n        }\n        Err(_) => { Console.writeLine(\"no number\"); }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```text\n2026\nnot a digit: 120\n```\n\n`Result<T, E>` is an enum with two variants, `Ok(T)` and `Err(E)`, matched without a type prefix. `T` may be `void`\n(`Result<void, E>`, with `Ok()`); `E` is usually an enum of the ways the function can fail.\n\n## `?`: pass the error on\n\nIn a function that returns a `Result` with the same error type, `e?` is the value inside `Ok`, or returns the `Err`\nto the caller at once. Every local is destroyed on the way out, as for any `return`.\n\n```ceesafe\nenum ParseError {\n    Empty,\n    BadDigit(u8),\n}\n\nfn digit(c: u8) -> Result<u32, ParseError> {\n    if (c < '0' || c > '9') {\n        return Err(ParseError.BadDigit(c));\n    }\n    return Ok((c - '0') as u32);\n}\n\nfn twoDigits(text: StringView) -> Result<u32, ParseError> {\n    if (text.length != 2) {\n        return Err(ParseError.Empty);\n    }\n    return Ok(digit(text[0])? * 10 + digit(text[1])?);\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(twoDigits(\"42\")!);\n    match (twoDigits(\"4x\")) {\n        Ok(n) => { Console.writeLine(n); }\n        Err(_) => { Console.writeLine(\"not two digits\"); }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```text\n42\nnot two digits\n```\n\nThe same works on optionals: `o?` returns `null` from a function that returns an optional.\n\n## `!`: this cannot fail\n\n`e!` is the value inside `Ok` (or a present optional's value). If it is an `Err` (or `null`), the program panics. Use\nit where failure would be a bug, or in small programs and tests where stopping is the right answer:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    var names = Vector<StringView>.create();\n    names.push(\"ada\")!;                  // allocation failure stops this program\n    names.push(\"grace\")!;\n    Console.writeLine(names.length);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n2\n```\n\n## Allocation failure is a value\n\nEvery operation that allocates returns a `Result<…, AllocError>`: `Box.create`, `Vector.push`, `String.append`,\n`Arena.alloc` and the rest. A program that must survive running out of memory, such as a kernel, handles it; a\nprogram that need not, writes `!` or passes it on with `?`:\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Node {\n    value: i32;\n    next: Box<Node>?;\n}\n\nfn push(head: Box<Node>?, value: i32) -> Result<Box<Node>, AllocError> =>\n    Box.create(Node { value: value, next: head });\n\nfn build(n: i32) -> Result<Box<Node>?, AllocError> {\n    var head: Box<Node>? = null;\n    for (i in 0..n) {\n        head = push(move head, i)?;\n    }\n    return Ok(head);\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    match (bui",
    35 =>
        "ld(3)) {\n        Ok(list) => { Console.writeLine(list!.value); }\n        Err(AllocError.OutOfMemory) => { Console.writeLine(\"out of memory\"); }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```text\n2\n```\n\nThe test suite runs every allocating program again with each allocation failing in turn, to check that every\nfailure path cleans up after itself.\n\n## Panics\n\nA panic prints the reason and the source location, and exits with status 134:\n\n<!-- ceesafe: panics -->\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    let values = [1, 2, 3];\n    var i: usize = 0;\n    while (true) {\n        Console.writeLine(values[i]);\n        i += 1;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```text\n1\n2\n3\n```\n\nHere the fourth read stops the program with `panic: index out of bounds` and the file and line of `values[i]`.\nPanics never run `drop` blocks or deferred statements: the program is in a state its author did not expect, so\nnothing more of it runs.\n\n## I/O errors\n\nFiles, sockets and processes report `IoError`, a built-in enum (`NotFound`, `PermissionDenied`, `AlreadyExists`, …,\n`Other(code)`), whose `message()` is the C library's text:\n\n<!-- ceesafe: exits 1 -->\n```ceesafe\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    match (File.open(\"/no/such/file\")) {\n        Ok(file) => {\n            Console.writeLine(\"opened\");\n            return 0;\n        }\n        Err(e) => {\n            Console.writeLine(e.message());\n            return 1;\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```text\nNo such file or directory\n```\n\n## Next\n\n[Memory and allocation](memory.md).\n",
    36 =>
        "# CeeSafe for AI agents\n\nThis page is for AI agents that write CeeSafe, and for the people who set them up. CeeSafe is new, so a model has seen\nlittle of it: it will write C#, TypeScript or Rust that looks like CeeSafe and is not. The compiler is built to correct\nthat quickly. It says exactly what is wrong, fixes what it is sure of, and serves its own specification.\n\nThe guide and the specification are inside `ceesafec`: an agent reads them through `ceesafec explain` and the MCP\nserver's resources (`ceesafe://docs/…`, below), so it needs no network access to read the rules. On the web they are\nalso in machine-readable form: [llms.txt](https://ceesafe.github.io/CeeSafe/llms.txt) (an index) and\n[llms-full.txt](https://ceesafe.github.io/CeeSafe/llms-full.txt) (the guide, the specification and the library\nreference in one file), and every page is available as Markdown at its address with `.md` added. The library reference\nis [lib/core](../spec/api/core.md) and [lib/std](../spec/api/std.md), and the compiler's own types and functions\n(`Console`, `File`, `Vector`, `StringView`, integer methods, …) are on [built-in types and functions](builtins.md).\n\n## In a new package\n\n`ceesafec new` writes `AGENTS.md`, which most coding agents read: what CeeSafe is, the package's layout, the commands that\ncheck the work, how to use the MCP server, and the common mistakes below. Name your agent to get its own file and its\nMCP configuration too:\n\n```sh\nceesafec new hello --agent claude      # also CLAUDE.md (importing AGENTS.md) and .mcp.json\n```\n\n| `--agent` | Writes |\n|---|---|\n| `agents` (the default) | `AGENTS.md` |\n| `claude` | `CLAUDE.md`, `.mcp.json` |\n| `gemini` | `GEMINI.md`, `.gemini/settings.json` |\n| `copilot` | `.github/copilot-instructions.md`, `.vscode/mcp.json` |\n| `cursor` | `.cursor/rules/ceesafe.mdc`, `.cursor/mcp.json` |\n\nCeeSafe's part of `AGENTS.md` sits between `<!--CEESAFE START-->` and `<!--CEESAFE END-->`. After updating `ceesafec`, run\n`ceesafec agents` in the package to refresh that part; whatever you wrote outside it stays as it is.\n\n## Set up the MCP server\n\n`ceesafec mcp` is a [Model Context Protocol](https://modelcontextprotocol.io) server. Add it to your agent once:\n\n```sh\nclaude mcp add ceesafe -- ceesafec mcp          # Claude Code\n```\n\nor, for any MCP client:\n\n```json\n{ \"mcpServers\": { \"ceesafe\": { \"command\": \"ceesafec\", \"args\": [\"mcp\"] } } }\n```\n\nIts tools:\n\n| Tool | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `ceesafe_check` | type-checks code and returns its diagnostics as JSON, with fixes |\n| `ceesafe_fix` | applies every fix the compiler is sure of, and returns the files and what remains |\n| `ceesafe_format` | formats a file in the one canonical style |\n| `ceesafe_run` | runs a program in a sandboxed interpreter: no files, sockets or processes, and a step and memory budget |\n| `ceesafe_emit_c` | shows the generated C, and where allocations, bounds checks and overflow checks went |\n| `ceesafe_explain` | what a diagnostic code means |\n| `ceesafe_spec_search`, `ceesafe_spec_section` | search and read the specification, which is embedded in `ceesafec` |\n| `ceesafe_symbol` | the type and declaration of what is at a position |\n| `ceesafe_api` | a library or built-in name looked up (`TextWriter.decimal`, `Process.argument`): its signature and documentation, or the closest names |\n\nThe prompt `write-ceesafe` gives an agent this page and the loop below. The specification and this guide are resources\n(`ceesafe://docs/…`). The [tooling reference](../spec/tooling.md) §6 has the details.\n\n## The loop\n\n1. Write the code.\n2. Check it: `ceesafe_check`, or `ceesafec check --diagnostics-format=json`.\n3. Apply the fixes: `ceesafe_fix`, or `ceesafec fix`. A fix is offered only where the compiler is sure of it: `mut` before\n   an argument, `let` to `var`, `truncate<T>(…)` for a narrowing cast, a missing `;`.\n4. Fix the rest yourself. Each diagnostic has a code; `ceesafe_explain` (or `ceesafec explain N0249`) says what it means, and\n   th",
    37 =>
        "e message often names the specification section to read. For a function or method you are not sure of, look it\n   up with `ceesafe_api` before writing it, rather than guessing a name from another language.\n5. Repeat until there are no errors. Then run it with `ceesafe_run`, or build it with `ceesafec build`.\n\nWithout MCP, the same loop works from a shell: `ceesafec check --diagnostics-format=json main.cee` prints one JSON\ndocument with every diagnostic, its position and its fixes, and `ceesafec fix main.cee` applies them.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\nThese are the mistakes models make most often in CeeSafe, each with the compiler's answer and what to write instead.\n\n### There is no `~`\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0102 -->\n```ceesafe\nfn invert(x: u32) -> u32 => ~x;\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:1:29: error[N0102]: expected expression, found '~'\n  |\n1 | fn invert(x: u32) -> u32 => ~x;\n  |                             ^\n```\n\nComplement with an exclusive or of all ones:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn invert(x: u32) -> u32 => x ^ 0xFFFFFFFF;\n```\n\n### There are no type aliases\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0101 -->\n```ceesafe\ntype Count = u32;\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:1:1: error[N0101]: expected 'fn', 'const', 'struct' or 'enum', found 'type'\n  |\n1 | type Count = u32;\n  | ^^^^\n```\n\nWrite the type itself, or wrap it in a struct when it deserves a name of its own.\n\n### There are no tuples\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0100 -->\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    let (a, b) = (1, 2);\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:2:9: error[N0100]: expected identifier, found '('\n  |\n2 |     let (a, b) = (1, 2);\n  |         ^\n```\n\nDeclare two variables, or return a struct with named fields.\n\n### Arrays of structs are not compared with `==`\n\n`==` compares arrays and slices of integers, `bool` and fieldless enums. Arrays of structs are a different matter:\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0207 -->\n```ceesafe\nstruct P { x: i32; }\n\nfn same(a: [P; 2], b: [P; 2]) -> bool => a == b;\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:3:42: error[N0207]: '==' compares slices and arrays of integers, bools and fieldless enums, not of 'P' (type-system.md §16.2)\n  |\n3 | fn same(a: [P; 2], b: [P; 2]) -> bool => a == b;\n  |                                          ^^^^^^\n```\n\nCompare element by element:\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct P { x: i32; }\n\nfn same(a: [P; 2], b: [P; 2]) -> bool {\n    for (i in 0..a.length) {\n        if (a[i].x != b[i].x) { return false; }\n    }\n    return true;\n}\n```\n\n### Indexes are `usize`, and a range's type comes from its bounds\n\nA range of two literals that the body indexes with is a `usize` range, so this works as written:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn sum(values: Slice<u64>) -> u64 {\n    var total: u64 = 0;\n    for (i in 0..4) {\n        total += values[i];\n    }\n    return total;\n}\n```\n\nThe variable then is a `usize` everywhere in the body. Using it as an `i32` as well is an error:\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0205 -->\n```ceesafe\nfn fill(values: SliceMut<i32>) {\n    for (i in 0..4) {\n        let v: i32 = i;\n        values[i] = v;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:3:22: error[N0205]: expected 'i32', found 'usize'\n  |\n3 |         let v: i32 = i;\n  |                      ^\n```\n\nA range's type otherwise comes from its bounds: `0..4` with no index use is a range of `i32`, and\n`0..values.length` is a `usize` range. Convert explicitly where the two meet:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn fill(values: SliceMut<i32>) {\n    for (i in 0..values.length) {\n        values[i] = truncate<i32>(i);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### A block has a value only as a branch\n\nA branch of an `if` or `match` expression may be a block that ends with its value, written without `;`:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn sign(x: i32) -> i32 {\n    let s = if (x < 0) {\n        let magnitude = 0 - x;\n        magnitude / magnitude * -1\n    } else 1;\n    return s;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(sign(-4));\n}\n```\n\n```text\n-1\n```\n\nA statement's block has no value: a `match` statement whose arms end in a value is an error.\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0100 -->\n```ceesafe\nfn sign(x: i32) {\n    match (x < 0) {\n        true => { -1 }\n        false => { 1 }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:3:21:",
    38 =>
        " error[N0100]: expected ';', found '}': a block ends in a value only as a branch of an 'if' or 'match' expression (let x = match …)\n  |\n3 |         true => { -1 }\n  |                     ^\n\nmain.cee:4:21: error[N0100]: expected ';', found '}': a block ends in a value only as a branch of an 'if' or 'match' expression (let x = match …)\n  |\n4 |         false => { 1 }\n  |                     ^\n```\n\nMake it an expression, `let s = match (x < 0) { … };`, and return its value.\n\n### A view of a temporary lasts until the end of its statement\n\nA call's result can be borrowed, sliced or measured where it is made, `use(make().view())`, but it is destroyed at the\nend of the statement, so a view of it cannot be kept:\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0243 -->\n```ceesafe\nfn make() -> String {\n    var s = String.create();\n    s.append(\"hello\")!;\n    return s;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let v = make().view();\n    Console.writeLine(v);\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:8:5: error[N0243]: a temporary value is dropped at the end of this statement while still borrowed; bind it to a local first\n  |\n8 |     let v = make().view();\n  |     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  = note: a temporary value is borrowed here (8:5)\n  = note: the borrow is used later here (9:5)\n```\n\nBind the value to a local, and keep the view of the local:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn make() -> String {\n    var s = String.create();\n    s.append(\"hello\")!;\n    return s;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let text = make();\n    let v = text.view();\n    Console.writeLine(v);\n}\n```\n\n```text\nhello\n```\n\n### There is no string interpolation\n\n`{x}` in a string is printed as written. When `x` is a name in scope, the compiler warns (N0400):\n\n<!-- ceesafe: warns N0400 -->\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    let x = 3;\n    Console.writeLine(\"x = {x}\");\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:3:28: warning[N0400]: this string prints '{x}' as written (there is no string interpolation): write the value in a call of its own, or '\\{' to mean the brace\n  |\n3 |     Console.writeLine(\"x = {x}\");\n  |                            ^^^\n```\n\nWrite the value in a call of its own:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    let x = 3;\n    Console.write(\"x = \");\n    Console.writeLine(x);\n}\n```\n\n```text\nx = 3\n```\n\n### A multi-line string is a raw `\"\"\"` literal\n\nA `\"...\"` literal ends on its line. For several lines, open with `\"\"\"` at the end of a line and close with `\"\"\"` on a\nline of its own. The closing line's indentation is removed from every line, there is no final newline, and there are\nno escapes:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    let query = \"\"\"\n        SELECT id, name\n          FROM users\n        \"\"\";\n    Console.writeLine(query);\n}\n```\n\n```text\nSELECT id, name\n  FROM users\n```\n\n### `for` loops over iterators too\n\n`for` takes arrays, slices, ranges, and any value with `fn next(borrow mut this) -> T?`, such as a `split`:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    let text = \"a,b,c\";\n    for (part in text.split(\",\")) {\n        Console.writeLine(part);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```text\na\nb\nc\n```\n\nlib/core's `Iterator<T>` names that shape as an interface, so generic code and `dyn` can iterate too:\n`fn total<I: Iterator<u32>>(it: I) -> u32 { … for (x in it) { … } … }`, and `for (x in boxed)` over a\n`Box<dyn Iterator<u32>>` in a `var`. Rust's iterator chains (`map`, `filter`, `collect`, …) become the loop's body:\nthere is no adapter library.\n\n### A function that always exits returns `never`\n\nA call of a `never` function ends its branch, so a `match` that gives a value needs no value from that branch:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn die(message: StringView) -> never {\n    Console.writeLine(message);\n    Process.exit(1);\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let n: Result<u32, u32> = Ok(7);\n    let value = match (n) {\n        Ok(v) => v,\n        Err(_) => {\n            die(\"no number\");\n        }\n    };\n    Console.writeLine(value);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n7\n```\n\n### Converting with a check: `checkedTruncate` and `saturatingTruncate`\n\n`as` only allows conversions that never lose information, so `usize` to `i64` is not one. `checkedTruncate<T>(x)` is\n`null` when the value does not f",
    39 =>
        "it, and `saturatingTruncate<T>(x)` clamps it:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    let n: usize = 300;\n    let wide = checkedTruncate<i64>(n) ?? -1;\n    let small = checkedTruncate<u8>(n) ?? 0;\n    Console.writeLine(wide);\n    Console.writeLine(small);\n    Console.writeLine(saturatingTruncate<u8>(n));\n}\n```\n\n```text\n300\n0\n255\n```\n\n### A function in a field is not called through the field\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0210 -->\n```ceesafe\nstruct Counter {\n    step: fn(i32) -> i32;\n}\n\nfn advance(c: borrow Counter, n: i32) -> i32 => (c.step)(n);\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:5:49: error[N0210]: this expression cannot be called\n  |\n5 | fn advance(c: borrow Counter, n: i32) -> i32 => (c.step)(n);\n  |                                                 ^^^^^^^^\n```\n\nPass the function or closure as a parameter, and call the parameter:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn advance(step: fn(i32) -> i32, n: i32) -> i32 => step(n);\n```\n\n### A borrowed string in an enum needs a region\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0270 -->\n```ceesafe\nenum Failure {\n    Refused(StringView),\n    Io,\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:2:13: error[N0270]: a payload of scoped type 'StringView' needs a region parameter on the enum: 'enum Failure<region r>' (type-system.md §25.4)\n  |\n2 |     Refused(StringView),\n  |             ^^^^^^^^^^\n```\n\nA `StringView` borrows its text, so a type holding one says from where. For fixed messages, an enum of reasons and a\nfunction that words them is simpler:\n\n```ceesafe\nenum Reason {\n    TooLarge,\n    BadMagic,\n}\n\nenum Failure {\n    Refused(Reason),\n    Io,\n}\n\nfn describe(reason: Reason) -> StringView => match (reason) {\n    Reason.TooLarge => \"too large\",\n    Reason.BadMagic => \"bad magic number\",\n};\n```\n\n### A method cannot share a field's name\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0228 -->\n```ceesafe\nstruct Account {\n    balance: i64;\n\n    fn balance(borrow this) -> i64 => this.balance;\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:4:8: error[N0228]: 'Account' already has a member named 'balance'\n  |\n4 |     fn balance(borrow this) -> i64 => this.balance;\n  |        ^^^^^^^\n```\n\nName the method for what it does (`current`), or make the field `public`.\n\n### Look a name up before deciding it is missing\n\nA model guesses names from the languages it knows (`unsigned`, `args`, `writeLn`). CeeSafe's names are often\ndifferent, and the compiler says which ones exist:\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0200 -->\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    var buffer = [0u8; 32];\n    var w = TextWriter.create(mut buffer[..]);\n    w.unsigned(42);\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:4:7: error[N0200]: 'TextWriter' has no method named 'unsigned'; did you mean 'signed', 'decimal' or 'hex'?\n  |\n4 |     w.unsigned(42);\n  |       ^^^^^^^^\n```\n\n`decimal` is suggested because its documentation says \"Appends an unsigned integer in decimal\". Before writing a call\nyou are unsure of, look the name up: `ceesafe_api` with `TextWriter`, `TextWriter.decimal`, `decimal` or\n`Process.argument` returns the entries, or the closest names. Without MCP, the [built-in types](builtins.md) and the\n[lib/core](../spec/api/core.md) and [lib/std](../spec/api/std.md) pages list every name.\n\n### Reserved words are not names\n\nSome keywords read like ordinary names: `own`, `type`, `move`, `drop`, `where`, `class`, `yield`, `macro`, `using`,\n`static` and `in` are among them ([grammar](../spec/grammar.md) §1.3 lists them all).\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0100 -->\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    let own = 1;\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:2:9: error[N0100]: expected identifier, found 'own', which is a reserved word: choose another name (grammar.md §1.3)\n  |\n2 |     let own = 1;\n  |         ^^^\n```\n\n### A lookup that may find nothing is an optional, not a `Result`\n\n`Process.argument(i)`, `StringView.fromUtf8(bytes)`, `Environment.variable(name)`, `v.pop()` and `x.checkedAdd(y)`\ngive a `T?`: match it with `Some(…)` and `null`, not `Ok` and `Err`. A `!` unwraps an optional as well as a result.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    match (Process.argument(1)) {\n        Some(path) => { Console.writeLine(path); }\n        null => { Console.writeLine(\"no fi",
    40 =>
        "le given\"); }\n    }\n    let bytes = [104u8, 105];\n    let text = StringView.fromUtf8(bytes[..])!; // `!` unwraps an optional too, and panics on null\n    Console.writeLine(text);\n}\n```\n\n```text\nno file given\nhi\n```\n\n### `Console` blocks its thread, so async code uses `AsyncConsole`\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0295 -->\n```ceesafe\nasync fn greet() {\n    Console.writeLine(\"hello\");\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    Async.run(greet());\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:2:5: error[N0295]: 'greet' is an async fn, which must not block its thread (type-system.md §40.1), but this may sleep: it does console I/O\n  |\n2 |     Console.writeLine(\"hello\");\n  |     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n```\n\nThe same holds for `File` and `TcpStream`: async code uses `AsyncFiles`, `AsyncTcpListener` and `AsyncTcpStream`\nfrom lib/std. A call through `dyn` counts as blocking unless the interface method is declared `@effects()`.\n\n```ceesafe\nasync fn greet() -> Result<void, AsyncError> {\n    await AsyncConsole.writeLine(\"hello\")?;\n    return Ok();\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    match (Async.run(greet())) {\n        Ok() => {}\n        Err(_) => { Console.writeLine(\"failed\"); }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```text\nhello\n```\n\n### `mut` at a call, `borrow mut` anywhere else\n\nA mutable borrow passed as an argument is written `mut x` (or `mut a[lo..hi]`). The same borrow in a `let` or any\nother expression is `borrow mut x`.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn bump(n: borrow mut i32) {\n    n += 1;\n}\n\nfn zero(bytes: SliceMut<u8>) {\n    bytes.fill(0);\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    var n = 1;\n    bump(mut n); // an argument: `mut`\n    let r = borrow mut n; // anywhere else: `borrow mut`\n    r += 1;\n    var buffer = [7u8; 4];\n    zero(mut buffer[0..2]);\n    let tail = borrow mut buffer[2..];\n    tail[0] = 9;\n    Console.writeLine(n);\n    Console.writeLine(buffer[2]);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n3\n9\n```\n\n### What the compiler fixes for you\n\n`ceesafe_fix` and `ceesafec fix` repair these without being asked twice: a `let` that is assigned (`var`), an argument for a\n`borrow mut` parameter without `mut`, a `mut` where the parameter is a shared `borrow`, `as` between integer types that\ncan lose information (`truncate<T>(…)`, which wraps), and a `;` missing after a statement.\n\n## Where to read more\n\nThe [guide](introduction.md) teaches the language; [Coming from another language](coming-from/index.md) maps what you know.\n[Built-in types and functions](builtins.md), [lib/core](../spec/api/core.md) and [lib/std](../spec/api/std.md) list every\nname.\nThe rules themselves are the [specification](../spec/type-system.md), with the [grammar](../spec/grammar.md) and every\n[diagnostic](../spec/diagnostics.md).\n",
    41 =>
        "# Formatting and documentation\n\n## Formatting\n\n`ceesafec fmt` rewrites CeeSafe source in one canonical style. It has no options, so code formatted by anyone looks the\nsame. The style is specified in [Formatting](../spec/formatting.md).\n\n```sh\nceesafec fmt                       # the package's sources (or: ceesafec fmt file.cee dir/)\nceesafec fmt --check               # change nothing; list the files that would change; exit 1 if any\nceesafec fmt --stdout file.cee    # print one file formatted\n```\n\nThe style, briefly:\n\n- 4-space indentation, no tabs, at most one blank line in a row, lines up to 120 columns;\n- a block or list you wrote on one line stays on one line if it fits; one you broke across lines is laid out one\n  element per line, with a trailing comma;\n- a line that is too long is broken at the outermost place that helps: an expression body, then a list, then an\n  initialiser, then an operator;\n- `} else {` on one line, and the blocks of an `if`/`else` chain are broken together;\n- trailing comments on consecutive lines are aligned.\n\nIt is safe by construction: the formatter only changes whitespace and trailing commas, and before returning it parses\nits own output and checks that the program and its comments are unchanged. A file with a syntax error is left alone.\n\n## Documentation\n\n`ceesafec doc` writes one Markdown page for a package's public items: each item's signature, as the formatter prints\nit, and its `///` doc comment, which is Markdown.\n\n```sh\nceesafec doc -o api.md --title \"geometry\"\nceesafec doc --missing             # list the public items without a doc comment; exit 1 if any\n```\n\n<!-- ceesafe: skip -->\n```ceesafe\n/// A point in the plane.\n///   - coordinates are exact integers;\n///   - `norm` is the squared length.\npublic struct Point {\n    public x: i64;\n    public y: i64;\n\n    /// The squared distance from the origin.\n    public fn norm(this) -> i64 => this.x * this.x + this.y * this.y;\n}\n```\n\nbecomes a section with `public struct Point { public x: i64; public y: i64; }`, the list, and a subsection for\n`Point.norm`. The [lib/core reference](../spec/api/core.md) on this site is generated this way.\n",
    42 =>
        "# Generics and interfaces\n\n## Generic functions and types\n\nFunctions, structs and enums can take type parameters. Each use with different types compiles to its own copy\n(monomorphisation), so generic code is as fast as hand-written code for each type.\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Pair<T> {\n    first: T;\n    second: T;\n    fn swapped(this) -> Pair<T> => Pair { first: this.second, second: this.first };\n}\n\nenum Maybe<T> {\n    Nothing,\n    Just(T),\n}\n\nfn firstOf<T>(pair: Pair<T>) -> T => pair.first;\n\nfn main() {\n    let p = Pair { first: 1, second: 2 };           // Pair<i32>, inferred\n    let q = p.swapped();\n    Console.writeLine(firstOf(q));                  // T = i32, inferred\n    let names = Pair<StringView> { first: \"x\", second: \"y\" };\n    Console.writeLine(firstOf<StringView>(names));  // written out\n    let m: Maybe<u8> = Maybe.Just(7);\n    match (m) {\n        Maybe.Just(v) => { Console.writeLine(v); }\n        Maybe.Nothing => {}\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```text\n2\nx\n7\n```\n\nType arguments are inferred from the arguments where possible, and written otherwise: `Pair<u8> { … }`,\n`Vector<i32>.create()`, `firstOf<StringView>(names)`.\n\n**Generic code is checked once,** for every possible type argument. Inside it, a value of type `T` can be moved,\nborrowed, stored and returned, but nothing else is known about it: no arithmetic, no fields, no `==`. What it can do\ncomes from its **bounds**.\n\n## Interfaces\n\nAn interface lists methods. A type implements it with an `impl` block, and a generic function bound by it can call\nthose methods.\n\n```ceesafe\ninterface Shape {\n    fn area(borrow this) -> u64;\n    fn name(borrow this) -> StringView;\n}\n\nstruct Square {\n    side: u64;\n}\n\nstruct Rect {\n    w: u64;\n    h: u64;\n}\n\nimpl Shape for Square {\n    fn area(borrow this) -> u64 => this.side * this.side;\n    fn name(borrow this) -> StringView => \"square\";\n}\n\nimpl Shape for Rect {\n    fn area(borrow this) -> u64 => this.w * this.h;\n    fn name(borrow this) -> StringView => \"rect\";\n}\n\nfn total<S: Shape>(shapes: Slice<S>) -> u64 {\n    var sum: u64 = 0;\n    for (shape in shapes) {\n        sum += shape.area();\n    }\n    return sum;\n}\n\nfn describe<S: Shape>(shape: borrow S) {\n    Console.write(shape.name());\n    Console.write(\": \");\n    Console.writeLine(shape.area());\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let squares = [Square { side: 2 }, Square { side: 3 }];\n    var rects = Vector<Rect>.create();\n    rects.push(Rect { w: 4, h: 5 })!;\n    Console.writeLine(total(squares[..]) + total(rects.view()));\n    describe(squares[1]);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n33\nsquare: 9\n```\n\n- Calls through a bound are resolved at compile time: there is no virtual dispatch and no cost.\n- `Copy` is a built-in bound: a `T: Copy` can be used twice and stored in arrays.\n- Several bounds are joined with `+`: `<T: Shape + Copy>`.\n- An interface can itself be generic: `interface Comparable<T> { fn compare(borrow this, other: borrow T) -> i32; }`.\n\n## Default methods and `Self`\n\nAn interface method can have a body: its **default**. An impl that leaves the method out inherits the default; one\nthat writes it overrides it. Inside an interface, `Self` is the implementing type.\n\n```ceesafe\ninterface Watcher {\n    fn onError(borrow mut this, code: u32);\n\n    fn onWatchError(borrow mut this, code: u32) {\n        this.onError(code + 100);\n    }\n\n    fn label(borrow this) -> StringView => \"watcher\";\n\n    fn copyOf(borrow this) -> Self;\n}\n\nstruct Log {\n    total: u32;\n}\n\nimpl Watcher for Log {\n    fn onError(borrow mut this, code: u32) {\n        this.total += code;\n    }\n\n    fn label(borrow this) -> StringView => \"log\";\n\n    fn copyOf(borrow this) -> Self => Self { total: this.total };\n}\n\nfn report<W: Watcher>(w: borrow mut W) {\n    w.onWatchError(1);\n    Console.writeLine(w.label());\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    var log = Log { total: 0 };\n    log.onWatchError(5);\n    report(mut log);\n    Console.writeLine(log.copyOf().total);\n}\n```\n\n```text\nlog\n206\n```\n\n- A default is checked once, where the interface is, with `Self` unknown: it can call the inte",
    43 =>
        "rface's methods but\n  not use fields. An error in it is reported there, even if no impl inherits it.\n- An impl that inherits a default gets its own copy, compiled for the target type: a call on the type, through a\n  bound or through `dyn` calls it directly or through the table, as for a method the impl writes.\n- A type has one method of each name: an inherited default whose name the type already has is an error at the impl.\n- In an `impl` block and in a struct's or enum's body, `Self` is the type itself, with its parameters:\n  `Self { … }`, `Self.make()` and `Self.MAX` work as with the type's name.\n- A method without a receiver is a static function: `fn create(options: borrow Options) -> Self;` is called as\n  `T.create(o)` through a bound `T`, as `Self.create(o)` in a default and as `Rule.create(o)` on a type. A method may\n  also have type parameters of its own, `fn visit<T: Copy>(borrow mut this, value: T)`, instantiated per call.\n\n## Constants and associated types\n\nAn interface can declare constants, with or without a default, and associated types, which each impl chooses:\n\n```ceesafe\ninterface Encoding {\n    const NAME: StringView;\n    const WIDTH: usize = 1;\n    type Unit: Copy;\n\n    fn decode(borrow this, bytes: Slice<u8>) -> Self.Unit?;\n}\n\nstruct Latin1 {\n    unused: u8;\n}\n\nimpl Encoding for Latin1 {\n    const NAME: StringView = \"latin-1\";\n    type Unit = u8;\n\n    fn decode(borrow this, bytes: Slice<u8>) -> u8? => if (bytes.length > 0) bytes[0] else null;\n}\n\nstruct Ucs2 {\n    unused: u8;\n}\n\nimpl Encoding for Ucs2 {\n    const NAME: StringView = \"ucs-2\";\n    const WIDTH: usize = 2;\n    type Unit = u16;\n\n    fn decode(borrow this, bytes: Slice<u8>) -> u16? {\n        if (bytes.length < 2) {\n            return null;\n        }\n        return (bytes[0] as u16) * 256 + (bytes[1] as u16);\n    }\n}\n\nfn first<E: Encoding>(e: borrow E, bytes: Slice<u8>) -> E.Unit? {\n    Console.write(E.NAME);\n    Console.write(\" reads \");\n    Console.write(E.WIDTH);\n    Console.writeLine(\" byte(s)\");\n    return e.decode(bytes);\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let bytes = [1u8, 2u8];\n    Console.writeLine(first(Latin1 { unused: 0 }, bytes[..]) ?? 0);\n    Console.writeLine(first(Ucs2 { unused: 0 }, bytes[..]) ?? 0);\n    Console.writeLine(Ucs2.WIDTH);\n}\n```\n\n```text\nlatin-1 reads 1 byte(s)\n1\nucs-2 reads 2 byte(s)\n258\n2\n```\n\n- `E.NAME` and `E.Unit` name the constant and the type of whatever `E` is; each instance reads its impl's, so a test\n  of a constant such as `if (H.HAS_ON_DATA)` costs nothing at run time.\n- An associated type's bounds (`Copy` here) are checked at each impl, and are all that generic code knows of it.\n- When callers need to fix a type (an iterator's item, say), make it an interface parameter instead:\n  `Iterator<u8>` rather than an associated type.\n- An interface with constants, associated types or static functions has no `dyn` type.\n\nAn interface can build on others, its **supertraits**: `interface Shape: Named { … }`. Every `impl Shape` needs an\n`impl Named` for the same type, and a bound `S: Shape` gives `Named`'s methods too: inside a generic function over\n`S: Shape`, `s.name()` works, and `s` can be passed where a `Named` is wanted. `dyn Shape` holds both interfaces'\nmethods. `Copy` can be a supertrait as well, making every bound by the interface a Copy one.\n\n## Generic impls\n\nAn impl can have type parameters of its own. A wrapper then implements an interface for every inner type that has it:\n\n```ceesafe\ninterface Source {\n    fn next(borrow mut this) -> u32;\n}\n\nstruct Counter {\n    value: u32;\n}\n\nimpl Source for Counter {\n    fn next(borrow mut this) -> u32 {\n        this.value += 1;\n        return this.value;\n    }\n}\n\n/// Any source, with the calls of `next` counted.\nstruct Counted<T> {\n    inner: T;\n    calls: u32;\n}\n\nimpl<T: Source> Source for Counted<T> {\n    fn next(borrow mut this) -> u32 {\n        this.calls += 1;\n        return this.inner.next();\n    }\n}\n\nfn take<S: Source>(s: borrow mut S) -> u32 => s.next() + s.next();\n\nfn main",
    44 =>
        "() {\n    var counted = Counted { inner: Counted { inner: Counter { value: 0 }, calls: 0 }, calls: 0 };\n    Console.writeLine(take(mut counted));\n    Console.writeLine(counted.calls + counted.inner.calls);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n3\n4\n```\n\n- The impl is checked once, then compiled for each instance it applies to: `Counted<Counter>` and\n  `Counted<Counted<Counter>>` here. An instance whose argument does not meet the bound (`Counted<u8>`) does not\n  implement `Source`.\n- Each of the impl's type parameters must appear in its target, and the target is a generic struct or enum.\n- Beside a generic impl, no other impl of the same interface may be written for the same struct or enum: no\n  `impl Source for Counted<Counter>` as well.\n- An impl lives in the package that declares the interface or the type (the orphan rule), so two packages never\n  both provide one.\n- A struct with region parameters implements an interface with its regions named, `impl<region r> Body for\n  RequestBody<r> { … }`, and is used through bounds; it does not convert to `dyn Body`, since a `dyn` value holds no\n  loans.\n\n## Const parameters\n\nA generic item may also take **values**: `const N: T`, with `T` a `bool`, an integer type or a fieldless enum. Each\ndistinct value compiles to its own copy, so a flag or a size known at compile time costs nothing at run time.\n\n```ceesafe\nenum Mode { Plain, Shout }\n\nstruct Buffer<const N: usize> {\n    bytes: [u8; N];\n    used: usize;\n}\n\nfn render<const UPPER: bool>(c: u8) -> u8 {\n    if (UPPER && c >= 'a' && c <= 'z') {\n        return c - 32;\n    }\n    return c;\n}\n\nfn greet<const M: Mode>() -> StringView => match (M) { Mode.Plain => \"hello\", Mode.Shout => \"HELLO\" };\n\nfn sum<const N: usize>(values: [u32; N]) -> u32 {\n    var total: u32 = 0;\n    for (v in values) {\n        total += v;\n    }\n    return total;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let b = Buffer<16> { bytes: [0u8; 16], used: 0 };\n    Console.writeLine(b.bytes.length);\n    let shout = true;\n    let c = if (shout) render<true>('q') else render<false>('q'); // the instance is chosen at run time\n    Console.writeLine(c);\n    Console.writeLine(greet<Mode.Shout>());\n    Console.writeLine(sum([1u32, 2, 3]));                         // N = 3, inferred from the argument\n}\n```\n\n```text\n16\n81\nHELLO\n6\n```\n\n- Inside the generic item, a const parameter is a value of its type: `if (UPPER)`, `match (M)`, `[u8; N]`. Both\n  branches of `if (UPPER)` are checked, and each copy keeps only its own.\n- An argument is `true`, `false`, an integer literal, a constant (`Buffer<SIZE>`), an enum variant\n  (`greet<Mode.Shout>()`) or the caller's own const parameter. A run-time value is not one: choose the instance with\n  an `if`, as above.\n- An array length may also name a `usize` constant: `const SIZE: usize = 64;` then `[u8; SIZE]`.\n- Expressions over a parameter (`[u8; N + 1]`, `render<!UPPER>`) are refused; name a constant, or add a parameter.\n\n## Dynamic dispatch\n\nA bound is resolved at compile time, so a `Slice<S>` holds shapes of one type. For a collection of different types\nthat implement the same interface, use `dyn Shape`: a value of some type implementing `Shape`, whose methods are\ncalled through a table at run time. Its size is not known, so it lives behind a pointer: `borrow dyn Shape`,\n`borrow mut dyn Shape`, or an owning `Box<dyn Shape>`.\n\n```ceesafe\ninterface Shape {\n    fn area(borrow this) -> u64;\n    fn name(borrow this) -> StringView;\n}\n\nstruct Square {\n    side: u64;\n}\n\nstruct Rect {\n    w: u64;\n    h: u64;\n}\n\nimpl Shape for Square {\n    fn area(borrow this) -> u64 => this.side * this.side;\n    fn name(borrow this) -> StringView => \"square\";\n}\n\nimpl Shape for Rect {\n    fn area(borrow this) -> u64 => this.w * this.h;\n    fn name(borrow this) -> StringView => \"rect\";\n}\n\nfn describe(shape: borrow dyn Shape) {\n    Console.write(shape.name());\n    Console.write(\": \");\n    Console.writeLine(shape.area());\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    var shapes = Vector<Box<dyn Shape>>.create();\n    shapes.push(Box.create(Square { sid",
    45 =>
        "e: 3 })!)!; // a Box<Square> becomes a Box<dyn Shape>\n    shapes.push(Box.create(Rect { w: 2, h: 5 })!)!;\n    for (shape in shapes) {\n        describe(shape);\n    }\n    let sq = Square { side: 4 };\n    describe(sq); // a Square, lent as a borrow dyn Shape\n}\n```\n\n```text\nsquare: 9\nrect: 10\nsquare: 16\n```\n\n- A `borrow T` or a `Box<T>` converts to the `dyn` type where one is expected, if `T` implements the interface.\n  There is no way back to `T`.\n- Every method of the interface must take `borrow this` or `borrow mut this`, have no type or const parameters of\n  its own and not name `Self` except as its receiver: a static function or a generic method has no place in a table.\n  Defaults are fine. Async methods are allowed:\n  `await shape.load()` through `dyn` waits for the value's own method, in room the compiler makes for the largest\n  of the types converted to that `dyn` type.\n- A call through `dyn` cannot be inlined. Prefer a bound when one type is enough.\n- An interface method may declare its effects, `@effects() fn visit(borrow mut this, x: u64);`, which every `impl`\n  must keep: kernel code in an atomic context can then call it through `dyn`.\n\n## Next\n\n[Functions and closures](closures.md).\n",
    46 =>
        "# Getting started\n\nThis page makes a package, builds and runs it, and shows the commands you will use every day. It assumes `ceesafec` is\n[installed](installation.md) and on your `PATH`.\n\n## A new package\n\n```sh\nceesafec new hello\ncd hello\n```\n\n`ceesafec new` writes a manifest, a first source file, and instructions for AI coding agents:\n\n```text\nhello/\n├── ceesafe.toml\n├── src/\n│   └── main.cee\n├── AGENTS.md\n└── .gitignore\n```\n\n`AGENTS.md` tells an agent working in the package what CeeSafe is and how to check its work. With\n`ceesafec new hello --agent claude` (or `gemini`, `copilot`, `cursor`) it also writes that agent's own file and\nconfigures CeeSafe's MCP server for it; `--no-agent` writes none. See [CeeSafe for AI agents](for-agents.md).\n\n`ceesafe.toml` names the package and says what it builds:\n\n```toml\n[package]\nname = \"hello\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nkind = \"executable\"\n\n[dependencies]\n```\n\nand `src/main.cee` is a program:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(\"Hello from hello\");\n}\n```\n\n```text\nHello from hello\n```\n\n## Build and run\n\nInside a package, the commands need no file names:\n\n```sh\nceesafec build            # compiles to C, then to out/hello\n./out/hello\nceesafec run --interp     # runs the program in the reference interpreter instead\nceesafec check            # type and borrow checks only: the fastest way to find errors\nceesafec fmt              # formats the package's sources in the canonical style\n```\n\nEvery `.cee` file under `src/` belongs to the package, and all of them share one scope: a function declared in one\nfile can be called from another without an import.\n\n## A slightly larger program\n\nReplace `src/main.cee` with:\n\n```ceesafe\n/// The number of steps the Collatz sequence takes from `n` to 1.\nfn steps(n: u64) -> u32 {\n    var value = n;\n    var count: u32 = 0;\n    while (value != 1) {\n        value = if (value % 2 == 0) value / 2 else 3 * value + 1;\n        count += 1;\n    }\n    return count;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    var longest: u64 = 1;\n    for (n in 1..1000u64) {\n        if (steps(n) > steps(longest)) {\n            longest = n;\n        }\n    }\n    Console.write(\"longest below 1000: \");\n    Console.write(longest);\n    Console.write(\", \");\n    Console.write(steps(longest));\n    Console.writeLine(\" steps\");\n}\n```\n\n```text\nlongest below 1000: 871, 178 steps\n```\n\n`3 * value + 1` is checked: if it overflowed a `u64`, the program would stop with a message naming the line, in every\nbuild. CeeSafe never wraps silently.\n\n## When the compiler says no\n\nErrors name a code, the place, and usually the fix. This program keeps a reference to a vector's element and then\ngrows the vector, which could move the element:\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0241 -->\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    var numbers = Vector<i32>.create();\n    numbers.push(1)!;\n    let first = borrow numbers[0];\n    numbers.push(2)!;\n    Console.writeLine(first);\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:5:5: error[N0241]: cannot borrow 'numbers' as mutable while 'numbers[0]' is borrowed\n  |\n5 |     numbers.push(2)!;\n  |     ^^^^^^^\n  = note: 'numbers[0]' is borrowed here (4:5)\n  = note: the borrow is used later here (6:5)\n```\n\nThe fix is to finish with `first` before the push, or to copy the value (`let first = numbers[0];`). Every code is\nexplained in [Diagnostics](../spec/diagnostics.md).\n\n## Files without a package\n\nFor a quick experiment, a package is not needed: give the files to the command.\n\n```sh\nceesafec build tool.cee helpers.cee -o tool\nceesafec run --interp tool.cee\n```\n\n## Next\n\n- [Basics](basics.md): values, types, functions and control flow.\n- [Packages](packages.md): dependencies, the lock file, and sharing code.\n- [The ceesafec command](ceesafec.md): every command and option.\n",
    47 =>
        "# Installation\n\n## Install ceesafec\n\nOn macOS and Linux:\n\n```sh\ncurl -fsSL https://ceesafe.github.io/CeeSafe/install.sh | sh\n```\n\nOn Windows, in PowerShell:\n\n```powershell\nirm https://ceesafe.github.io/CeeSafe/install.ps1 | iex\n```\n\nThe script downloads the latest release of `ceesafec` for your machine from\n[GitHub](https://github.com/CeeSafe/CeeSafe/releases), checks its SHA-256, and installs it in `~/.ceesafe/bin`\n(`%USERPROFILE%\\.ceesafe\\bin`). On macOS and Linux it then tells you how to add that directory to your `PATH`; on\nWindows it adds it for you. `CEESAFE_VERSION=v0.1.0` installs a given release instead. Run the same line again to\nupdate.\n\nReleases are built for macOS (arm64 and x64, signed and notarized by Apple), Linux (x64 and arm64; glibc 2.35 or newer:\nUbuntu 22.04, Debian 12, Fedora 36 and later) and Windows (x64 and arm64). You can also download an archive from the\n[releases page](https://github.com/CeeSafe/CeeSafe/releases) yourself: each holds `ceesafec` (`ceesafec.exe`), and\n`SHA256SUMS` lists their checksums. `ceesafec` is one native executable: its runtime, its libraries and this\nspecification are inside it, and it needs nothing else to run.\n\n```sh\nceesafec --version\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\n| | For | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| A C compiler: clang, gcc or MSVC | `ceesafec build`, and building `ceesafec` from source | clang and gcc are fully supported; MSVC is best-effort (`/std:c11`). `check`, `run --interp`, `fmt`, `lsp` and `mcp` need none |\n| git | dependencies from git repositories | only for [packages](packages.md) that use them |\n| Node.js and [Vite+](https://viteplus.dev/) | the VS Code extension | optional |\n\n## Build from source\n\nTo work on CeeSafe itself, or on a platform without a release, build `ceesafec` from the repository. It is written in\nCeeSafe and builds itself, so the first build of a checkout needs a `ceesafec`: a release's, or one built from a\nrelease's C seed with a C compiler alone.\n\nWith a released `ceesafec` (any platform's archive from the\n[releases](https://github.com/CeeSafe/CeeSafe/releases), or the one `install.sh` put in `~/.ceesafe/bin`):\n\n```sh\ngit clone https://github.com/CeeSafe/CeeSafe.git\ncd CeeSafe\nsh tools/bootstrap_release.sh ~/.ceesafe/bin/ceesafec HEAD out/ceesafec\nout/ceesafec --version\n```\n\n`tools/bootstrap_release.sh` builds, with the release, the commits recorded for it in the script (a compiler cannot\nalways build a tree whose own sources use library code added after it), then the tree you checked out.\n\nOn a platform with no release, build a `ceesafec` from `ceesafec-<version>-seed.tar.gz`, attached to every release:\nit is ceesafec's own C, and its `README.md` gives the one `cc` (or `cl`) command. That `ceesafec` builds the tree of\nits release's tag directly.\n\nAfter that, `ceesafec` rebuilds itself:\n\n```sh\nout/ceesafec build -o out/ceesafec.new && mv out/ceesafec.new out/ceesafec\n```\n\n`ceesafec` is one native executable: its runtime, its libraries and this specification are inside it, so it can be\ncopied anywhere, for example onto your `PATH` as `~/.local/bin/ceesafec`.\n\nTo check everything, run the contract suite (the repository's `spec/testing.md`; it compiles and runs the corpus\nmany times, checks the fixed point, and sweeps allocation failures):\n\n```sh\npython3 tools/contract_tests.py --ceesafec out/ceesafec\n```\n\n## Check that it works\n\n```sh\ncat > hello.cee <<'EOF'\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(\"Hello, CeeSafe\");\n}\nEOF\nceesafec build hello.cee -o hello && ./hello      # through C, to a native program\nceesafec run --interp hello.cee                   # or in the reference interpreter\n```\n\n`ceesafec build` uses the C compiler in `$CC`, or `cc`; `--cc clang` picks one. On Windows it finds one itself: clang\n(`winget install LLVM.LLVM`), MinGW's gcc, or Visual Studio's `cl`, and it sets up Visual Studio's headers and\nlibraries for clang and `cl` when you are not in a developer prompt. So with LLVM and Visual Studio's \"Desktop\ndevelopment with C++\" workload (or i",
    48 =>
        "ts Build Tools) installed, `ceesafec build` works from any terminal and makes\n`out\\hello.exe` ([tooling](../spec/tooling.md) §8).\n\n## Editor support\n\nThe repository has a VS Code extension in `editors/vscode`: syntax highlighting, and through `ceesafec lsp` errors as you\ntype, hover, go to definition, the outline and formatting. See [Editors](editors.md) to build and install it, and to\nuse the language server from other editors.\n",
    49 =>
        "# What CeeSafe is\n\nCeeSafe is a memory-safe systems programming language. Its syntax will look familiar if you know C# or TypeScript; its\nsafety comes from ownership and borrowing, as in Rust. It is compiled ahead of time to portable C11, so it runs\nwherever a C compiler does: on Linux, macOS and Windows, on bare metal, and inside an operating system kernel.\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Account {\n    id: u64;\n    balance: i64;\n\n    public fn deposit(borrow mut this, amount: i64) {\n        this.balance += amount;\n    }\n\n    drop {\n        Console.write(\"closing account \");\n        Console.writeLine(this.id);\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    var account = Account { id: 7, balance: 0 };\n    account.deposit(100);\n    Console.writeLine(account.balance);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n100\nclosing account 7\n```\n\n## What it promises\n\nIn safe code, which is all code outside an `unsafe` block, CeeSafe rules out:\n\n- **use after free and double free:** every value has one owner, and it is destroyed exactly once;\n- **dangling references:** a reference cannot outlive what it refers to, which the compiler checks without lifetime\n  annotations in most code;\n- **data races between a reference and a write:** while something is borrowed it cannot be changed, and while it is\n  borrowed mutably nothing else can read it;\n- **out-of-bounds access and reading uninitialised memory:** indexes are checked, and a variable must be assigned\n  before it is read;\n- **silent integer overflow:** arithmetic that overflows stops the program, in every build. Where wrapping is meant,\n  the code says so (`x.wrappingAdd(y)`).\n\nIt does this **without a garbage collector or reference counting.** Destruction is deterministic: a value is destroyed\nat the end of its scope, or when its owner is, in an order the language defines.\n\n## What makes it different\n\n- **Lifetimes without annotations, mostly.** A function that takes and returns references names the parameters its\n  result borrows from (`-> borrow(a, b) T`). Only types that store references, and functions over them, declare\n  region parameters.\n- **Allocation is explicit and pluggable.** Each allocation chooses its allocator: the system heap, an arena, a pool,\n  a fixed buffer, a scratch frame, or your own. Allocation failure is a value (`Result<T, AllocError>`), handled or\n  propagated with `?`.\n- **The compiler shows its work.** It can explain which boxes it placed on the stack and which bounds and overflow\n  checks it removed, and why.\n- **C you can read.** The generated C is structured and warning-free, maps back to CeeSafe lines for debuggers, and has\n  no undefined behaviour by design. A reference interpreter runs the same programs and stops on any memory error,\n  which makes it a second implementation to test the compiler against.\n- **Built for kernels too.** A freestanding profile without a C library, context effects that reject sleeping while a\n  spin lock is held, C structs imported from the C codebase's own headers, and lock guards and reference-counted\n  kernel objects. All of this is exercised inside [CosmoOS](https://github.com/vkuttyp/CosmoOS), an operating system\n  written in C.\n\n## Status\n\nCeeSafe is **pre-1.0**. Its compiler, `ceesafec`, is written in CeeSafe and compiles itself. The language and its tools\nwork end to end, and are tested against a corpus of about 550 programs. But nobody has used it in production. What is\ndone and what is next are in the repository's `ROADMAP.md`.\n\nSome things it does not have yet: trigonometry and logarithms, iterator adapters\n(`map`, `filter` and the like; loops do their work). Each is planned, or recorded as an open decision.\n\n## Where to go next\n\n- [Install the compiler](installation.md) and [write a first program](getting-started.md).\n- Read [the language guide](basics.md), chapter by chapter.\n- Coming from another language? Start with [CeeSafe for Rust, Zig, C, C# and Go programmers](coming-from/index.md).\n- The full rules are in the [specification](../spec/README.md): the grammar, the type s",
    50 => "ystem and the ownership rules.\n",
    51 =>
        "# Kernel code\n\nCeeSafe is designed to run inside an operating system kernel as well as in user programs. Its kernel support was\ndeveloped against [CosmoOS](https://github.com/vkuttyp/CosmoOS), a kernel written in C, where the language runs as\nkernel components and as loadable modules on x86-64 and AArch64 (CosmoOS builds those with the ceesafec it pins).\nNothing in it is specific to CosmoOS: the same mechanisms fit any C kernel or firmware.\n\n## The freestanding profile\n\n`--profile freestanding` (or `profile = \"freestanding\"` in a package's manifest) compiles for an environment without a\nC library or an operating system:\n\n- there is no `main`: the entry points are `@export` functions that the environment calls;\n- everything that needs a hosted runtime is unavailable, and naming it is an error: `Console`, files, sockets,\n  `SystemHeap` and the collections built on it;\n- memory comes from an allocator the environment provides, written in CeeSafe over the kernel's C functions;\n- `--no-float` (or `float = false`) rejects floating point, for code compiled without FP registers.\n\n`ceesafec build --emit obj` then writes one relocatable object, with only the undefined symbols the environment\nprovides, ready for the kernel's own build to link.\n\n<!-- ceesafe: freestanding -->\n```ceesafe\n/// A CRC-32C over `n` bytes at `data`, for C callers.\n/// Safety: `data` points to `n` readable bytes, as the C prototype's contract says.\n@export(\"crc32c_sum\")\nunsafe fn crc32c(data: *u8, n: usize) -> u32 {\n    return checksum(Slice.fromRaw(data, n));\n}\n\nfn checksum(bytes: Slice<u8>) -> u32 {\n    var crc: u32 = 0xFFFFFFFF;\n    for (b in bytes) {\n        crc = crc ^ (b as u32);\n        for (bit in 0..8) {\n            let mask = if ((crc & 1) != 0) 0x82F63B78u32 else 0u32;\n            crc = (crc >> 1) ^ mask;\n        }\n    }\n    return crc ^ 0xFFFFFFFF;\n}\n```\n\nThe unsafe part is confined to the boundary: the exported function turns C's pointer and length into a slice under a\nstated contract, and everything behind it is safe, checked CeeSafe.\n\n## Context effects\n\nKernel code runs in contexts with rules: while a spin lock is held it must not sleep; in an interrupt handler it must\nnot sleep or allocate. CeeSafe checks these rules at compile time, across function calls:\n\n- an operation's **effects** are `sleep` (it may block) and `alloc` (it may allocate), inferred for every function\n  from what it does and calls, its destructors included;\n- `@effects(…)` declares what a C function may do, or bounds what a CeeSafe function may do;\n- `@context(atomic)` on a function forbids `sleep` in it; `@context(irq)` forbids `sleep` and `alloc`;\n- `@context(atomic)` on a **struct** makes holding one an atomic context: a spin-lock guard.\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0295 -->\n```ceesafe\n@header(\"kernel/sched.h\")\nextern \"C\" {\n    @effects(sleep)\n    fn cosmo_sleep_ms(ms: u32);\n}\n\n/// Holding a spin-lock guard makes the code that runs meanwhile atomic: it must not sleep.\n@context(atomic)\nstruct SpinGuard { cpu: u32; drop {} }\n\n/// Safety: cosmo_sleep_ms has no preconditions; it may sleep.\nfn backOff() { unsafe { cosmo_sleep_ms(1); } }\n\nfn update(counter: borrow mut u64) {\n    let guard = SpinGuard { cpu: 0 };\n    counter += 1;\n    backOff(); // sleeps while the guard is held\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:17:5: error[N0295]: a 'SpinGuard' is live here, which makes this an atomic context (type-system.md §32.3), but this may sleep: it calls 'backOff', which calls 'cosmo_sleep_ms', declared @effects(sleep)\n   |\n17 |     backOff(); // sleeps while the guard is held\n   |     ^^^^^^^^^^\n```\n\nThis proves that CeeSafe code does nothing its context forbids, given that the `@effects` of C functions are declared\ncorrectly. It does not prove the absence of deadlocks or lock-order inversions: the kernel's own lockdep stays the\ncheck for those.\n\n## Hardware registers\n\n`lib/core` provides `Volatile<T>`, a cell read and written only by volatile accesses, and `MmioPtr<T>`, the address of\nmemory-mapped regi",
    52 =>
        "sters. Making an `MmioPtr` is the one unsafe step; register access through it is safe.\n\n<!-- ceesafe: freestanding -->\n```ceesafe\n@repr(C)\nstruct UartRegs {\n    data: Volatile<u32>;\n    status: Volatile<u32>;\n}\n\nstruct Uart {\n    regs: MmioPtr<UartRegs>;\n\n    /// Safety: `base` is the mapped address of a UART that no other `Uart` aliases.\n    public unsafe fn attach(base: usize) -> Uart => Uart { regs: MmioPtr<UartRegs>.at(base) };\n\n    public fn putByte(borrow mut this, b: u8) {\n        while ((this.regs.get().status.read() & 0x20) != 0) {\n            Cpu.spinHint();\n        }\n        this.regs.getMut().data.write(b as u32);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Kernel allocators, locks and objects\n\nThe CosmoOS integration (the repository's `integrations/cosmoos/`) builds the rest from these pieces:\n\n- `KernelHeap`, an allocator over `kmalloc`/`kfree`, so `Box.createIn(KernelHeap {}, value)` allocates kernel memory\n  and returns `Err` when the kernel has none;\n- `SpinLock<T>`, `IrqSpinLock<T>` and `Mutex<T>` that hold the data they protect: the data is reachable only through a\n  guard, the guard unlocks when it is destroyed, and a spin guard is an atomic context;\n- `KRef<T>`, a reference-counted kernel object whose final release may sleep, so dropping one under a spin lock does\n  not compile;\n- C kernel state (lists, locks, kernel objects) embedded by value in CeeSafe structs, built in place and never moved;\n- a loadable module whose state, `init` and teardown are CeeSafe.\n\nHow these were built and tested inside CosmoOS, including a stress test under lockdep, is in the\nCosmoOS reports and the [profiles](../spec/profiles.md).\n\n## Async tasks in kernel code\n\nAsync tasks run in the freestanding profile too. The environment gives the executor four functions:\n- the time;\n- a way to idle until a deadline;\n- a wake that is safe in an interrupt;\n- a slot for each thread's executor.\n\nA scope's children come from an allocator you name, `await Async.scopeIn(KernelHeap {}, oom, (s) => { … })`, since\nthere is no system heap. Timers, `race`, `all`, `shield` and cancellation work as in a hosted program.\n\nAn interrupt handler resumes a task through an `AsyncSignal`. The task waits with `await signal.wait()`. The handler,\nan `@context(irq)` function, calls `signal.raise()`, which has no effects. A raise that comes before the wait is kept.\n`Async.blocking`, `Async.runWorkers`, `spawnSend` and async I/O need threads or descriptors, so they stay hosted.\nCosmoOS's boot self-test `cee-async` runs these over the kernel's clock, a wait queue and a timer's interrupt.\n\n## Next\n\n[The ceesafec command](ceesafec.md).\n",
    53 =>
        "# Memory and allocation\n\nCeeSafe has no hidden allocation. Values live on the stack unless the program asks for memory, and every request names\nwhere the memory comes from and returns an error when there is none. This chapter goes from the simplest owner of\nheap memory, `Box`, to arenas, pools and scratch frames, and ends with how the compiler moves boxes to the stack when\nit can prove that is safe.\n\nThe rules are in [Memory model](../spec/memory-model.md) and the type system's sections on\n[boxes](../spec/type-system.md#_18-boxes-and-allocators-m3b) and\n[regions](../spec/type-system.md#_19-regions-m4).\n\n## `Box`\n\n`Box<T>` owns one `T` on the heap. It is one pointer, it moves (never copies), and destroying it destroys the `T`\nand frees the memory. Fields and methods are reached through it as if it were the value.\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Account {\n    id: u64;\n    balance: i64;\n    fn deposit(borrow mut this, amount: i64) {\n        this.balance += amount;\n    }\n}\n\nfn open(id: u64) -> Result<Box<Account>, AllocError> => Box.create(Account { id: id, balance: 0 });\n\nfn main() {\n    var account = open(7)!;\n    account.deposit(100);                  // through the box\n    Console.writeLine(account.balance);\n}                                          // the Account is destroyed and its memory freed here\n```\n\n```text\n100\n```\n\nBoxes are how recursive types are built, since a type cannot contain itself directly:\n\n```ceesafe\nenum Tree {\n    Leaf,\n    Node(Box<Tree>, i32, Box<Tree>),\n}\n\nfn sum(t: borrow Tree) -> i32 => match (t) {\n    Tree.Leaf => 0,\n    Tree.Node(left, value, right) => sum(left) + value + sum(right),\n};\n\nfn leaf() -> Box<Tree> => Box.create(Tree.Leaf)!;\n\nfn main() {\n    let tree = Tree.Node(Box.create(Tree.Node(leaf(), 1, leaf()))!, 2, Box.create(Tree.Node(leaf(), 3, leaf()))!);\n    Console.writeLine(sum(tree));\n}\n```\n\n```text\n6\n```\n\nA long list of boxes is destroyed with a loop, not recursion, so it cannot overflow the stack.\n\n## Allocators\n\n`Box<T>` is `Box<T, SystemHeap>`: memory from the platform's `malloc`. The allocator is part of the type, and\n`Box.createIn(allocator, value)` chooses another one:\n\n| Allocator | Gives | Frees |\n|---|---|---|\n| `SystemHeap` | the platform heap | each value when its owner is destroyed |\n| `Arena` | chunks of one region | everything at once, when the arena is destroyed or reset |\n| `FixedBufferAllocator` | a buffer you provide (an array on the stack, say) | nothing: the buffer is reused when the allocator ends |\n| `Scratch` frames | a runtime stack of chunks, last in first out | everything in a frame when the frame ends |\n| `Pool<T>` | slots for up to *n* values of one type | each slot when its box is destroyed |\n| your own | any struct that implements `Allocator` | as you implement it |\n\n`FailingAllocator` is a `SystemHeap` that can be told to fail after *n* allocations, for testing failure paths.\n\n## Arenas\n\nAn arena hands out memory quickly and frees it all at once. Every allocation **borrows the arena**, so none can\noutlive it: the compiler rejects any attempt to keep one after the arena is reset or destroyed.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn histogram(input: Slice<u8>) -> Result<u64, AllocError> {\n    using arena = Arena.create(SystemHeap {}, 65536u64);\n    let counts = arena.allocArray(256usize, 0u32)?;   // a SliceMut<u32> in the arena\n    for (b in input) {\n        counts[b as usize] += 1;\n    }\n    var distinct: u64 = 0;\n    for (c in counts) {\n        if (c != 0) {\n            distinct += 1;\n        }\n    }\n    return Ok(distinct);                               // a number, not arena memory\n}                                                      // the arena releases everything here\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(histogram(\"hello\".bytes())!);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n4\n```\n\nValues in an arena must not need destruction (no `drop` blocks, no boxes inside), since the arena never runs their\ndestructors. Returning `counts` itself would be a compile error: it borrows an arena that ends with the function.\n\n## A ",
    54 =>
        "fixed buffer\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Span {\n    start: u32;\n    length: u32;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    var storage = [0u8; 1024];\n    let fba = FixedBufferAllocator.over(mut storage);       // borrows `storage` mutably\n    let spans = fba.allocArray(16usize, Span { start: 0, length: 0 })!;\n    spans[3].length = 5;\n    Console.writeLine(spans[3].length);\n    Console.writeLine(spans.length);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n5\n16\n```\n\nWhen the buffer is full, an allocation returns `Err(AllocError.OutOfMemory)`; nothing is ever taken from the heap.\n\n## Scratch frames\n\nScratch memory is a stack of frames: a function enters a frame, allocates temporaries from it, and they are all\nreleased when the frame ends. Nested frames borrow their parent, so frames always end in order.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn render(n: usize) -> Result<usize, AllocError> {\n    using frame = Scratch.enter();\n    let buffer = frame.allocArray(n, 0u8)?;\n    {\n        using inner = frame.enter();\n        let temporary = inner.allocArray(n * 2, 0u16)?;\n        temporary[0] = 1;\n    }                                                  // `inner`'s memory is released here\n    return Ok(buffer.length);\n}                                                      // `frame`'s memory is released here\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(render(64)!);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n64\n```\n\n## Pools\n\nA pool holds up to a fixed number of values of one type. `take` returns a box-like `PoolBox` that returns its slot\nwhen destroyed, so pool values may have `drop` blocks.\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Particle {\n    x: i32;\n    v: i32;\n}\n\nfn simulate() -> Result<i32, AllocError> {\n    using pool: Pool<Particle> = Pool.create(SystemHeap {}, 64u32);\n    var a = pool.take(Particle { x: 0, v: 2 })?;\n    let b = pool.take(Particle { x: 10, v: -1 })?;\n    a.x += a.v;\n    return Ok(a.x + b.x);\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(simulate()!);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n12\n```\n\n## Your own allocator\n\nAn allocator is a struct with no fields that implements `lib/core`'s `Allocator` interface: `allocate` returns memory\nfor a `Layout` (size and alignment) or an `AllocError`, and `deallocate`, which is `unsafe` because it must be given\nexactly what `allocate` returned, gives it back. `Box.createIn(MyAllocator {}, value)` then uses it. The CosmoOS\nkernel's `KernelHeap` over `kmalloc` is written this way; see [Kernel code](kernel.md) and the\n[lib/core reference](../spec/api/core.md).\n\n## Placement: boxes on the stack\n\nThe compiler looks for boxes that provably never outlive their function, or one loop iteration, and puts them on the\nstack instead. The program cannot tell the difference: destruction happens at the same points and in the same\norder, and allocation failure can no longer happen for those boxes. `ceesafec build --explain-allocations` lists each\nallocation and the decision; `--no-placement` turns it off. The rules are in\n[Optimisations](../spec/optimisations.md).\n\n## Next\n\n[Arrays, slices and collections](collections.md).\n",
    55 =>
        "# Ownership and borrowing\n\nCeeSafe manages memory without a garbage collector by tracking, at compile time, who owns each value and who is\nlooking at it. This chapter explains the rules, why they hold, and what the compiler says when a program breaks\nthem. The formal rules are in [Ownership](../spec/ownership.md) and\n[References and the borrow checker](../spec/lifetimes.md).\n\n## Copy types and move types\n\nIntegers, floats, `bool`, references and string views are **Copy**: assigning one copies it, and both copies stay\nusable. A struct or enum is Copy when everything in it is Copy and it has no `drop` block.\n\nEverything else is a **move type**: boxes, vectors, strings, files, and any struct that owns one or has a `drop`\nblock. Assigning a move-type value, passing it, returning it or storing it **moves** it. The old place is then empty,\nand using it is a compile error.\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0250 -->\n```ceesafe\nstruct Buffer {\n    size: u32;\n    drop { Console.writeLine(\"freed\"); }\n}\n\nfn consume(b: Buffer) {}\n\nfn main() {\n    let b = Buffer { size: 64 };\n    consume(b);\n    Console.writeLine(b.size);\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:11:5: error[N0250]: 'b.size' is used here after it was moved (on at least one path)\n   |\n11 |     Console.writeLine(b.size);\n   |     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n```\n\nMoves are what make destruction safe: a value has exactly one owner, so it is destroyed exactly once.\n\n## Destruction\n\nA value is destroyed when its owner goes away: at the end of the variable's scope, when a temporary's statement\nends, or when an assignment overwrites it. Destroying a value runs its `drop` block, if it has one, and then destroys\nits fields. Locals are destroyed in the reverse order of their declaration.\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Noisy {\n    id: u32;\n    drop {\n        Console.write(\"drop \");\n        Console.writeLine(this.id);\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let first = Noisy { id: 1 };\n    let second = Noisy { id: 2 };\n    {\n        let inner = Noisy { id: 3 };\n    }                                   // inner is destroyed here\n    var replaced = Noisy { id: 4 };\n    replaced = Noisy { id: 5 };         // 4 is destroyed by the assignment\n    let moved = first;                  // first is moved: it is destroyed once, as `moved`\n    Console.writeLine(\"end of main\");\n}\n```\n\n```text\ndrop 3\ndrop 4\nend of main\ndrop 1\ndrop 5\ndrop 2\n```\n\nAt the end of `main`, the locals are destroyed in reverse order of declaration: `moved` (holding 1), `replaced`\n(holding 5), then `second`. `first` was moved, so it is not destroyed again. A value moved on only some paths gets a\nhidden flag, so it is still destroyed exactly once.\n\nA **panic** or `Process.exit` does not run `drop` blocks: a panic stops the program at once (there is no unwinding).\n\n### `defer` and `using`\n\n`defer s;` runs `s` when the enclosing block exits by any path: its end, `break`, `continue` or `return`.\n`using x = e;` declares a local that cannot be moved away: it lives exactly as long as its block, which documents\nthat its lifetime *is* the scope (a lock guard, an arena, a file).\n\n```ceesafe\nfn work(fail: bool) -> i32 {\n    defer Console.writeLine(\"cleanup\");\n    if (fail) {\n        return 1;\n    }\n    Console.writeLine(\"working\");\n    return 0;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(work(false));\n    Console.writeLine(work(true));\n}\n```\n\n```text\nworking\ncleanup\n0\ncleanup\n1\n```\n\n## Borrowing\n\nMost code does not want to take ownership; it wants to look at a value or change it in place. A **reference** does\nthat without moving:\n\n- `borrow T` is a shared reference: it can read, and any number can exist at once;\n- `borrow mut T` is an exclusive reference: it can read and write, and while it is in use nothing else can touch\n  the value.\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Counter {\n    hits: u64;\n}\n\nfn show(c: borrow Counter) {               // looks at the counter\n    Console.writeLine(c.hits);\n}\n\nfn record(c: borrow mut Counter) {         // changes it in place\n    c.hits += 1;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    var counter = Counter",
    56 =>
        " { hits: 0 };\n    record(mut counter);                   // `mut` at the call: this call changes `counter`\n    record(mut counter);\n    show(counter);                         // borrowed for the call; no marker needed\n}\n```\n\n```text\n2\n```\n\nA `borrow T` parameter accepts the value itself, borrowed implicitly. A `borrow mut T` parameter needs `mut` before\nthe argument, so that every place where a value may change is visible where it is called, as C#'s `ref` is.\n\n### The rule\n\nWhile a borrow is **live**, meaning the reference or anything made from it is still used later, the borrowed value\nmay not be:\n\n- changed, mutably borrowed or destroyed, if the borrow is shared;\n- used in any way, if the borrow is mutable.\n\nThe compiler checks this within each function, on every path. A borrow ends at its last use, not at the end of its\nscope.\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0240 -->\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    var total = 0;\n    let r = borrow total;\n    total += 1;\n    Console.writeLine(r);\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:4:5: error[N0240]: cannot assign to 'total' while 'total' is borrowed\n  |\n4 |     total += 1;\n  |     ^^^^^^^^^^^\n  = note: 'total' is borrowed here (3:5)\n  = note: the borrow is used later here (5:5)\n```\n\nMoving the `Console.writeLine(r)` above the `+=` fixes it: the borrow is then dead before the write. The same rule\nis what stops a vector from growing while an element is borrowed, since growing can move its elements.\n\n### Returning references\n\nA function that returns a reference must return something borrowed from its parameters, never from its own locals.\nWith one reference parameter, that is assumed. With several, the result names the ones it may borrow from:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn larger(a: borrow i64, b: borrow i64) -> borrow(a, b) i64 => if (a > b) a else b;\n\nfn firstPositive(values: Slice<i64>) -> borrow i64 {\n    for (i in 0..values.length) {\n        if (values[i] > 0) {\n            return borrow values[i];\n        }\n    }\n    return borrow values[0];\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let x: i64 = 3;\n    let y: i64 = 7;\n    Console.writeLine(larger(x, y));\n    let numbers = [-1i64, 4, 9];\n    Console.writeLine(firstPositive(numbers[..]));\n}\n```\n\n```text\n7\n4\n```\n\nThe caller then knows how long the result stays valid: as long as the arguments it borrows from. Returning a\nreference to a local is rejected, since the local is gone when the function returns:\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0244 -->\n```ceesafe\nfn pick(a: borrow i32) -> borrow i32 {\n    let copy = a + 1;\n    return borrow copy;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(pick(1));\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:3:5: error[N0244]: cannot return a reference to 'copy', which is local to 'pick'\n  |\n3 |     return borrow copy;\n  |     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  = note: borrowed here (3:12)\n```\n\n### Two mutable borrows\n\nTwo `borrow mut` references to one value can never be live together, even as two arguments of one call:\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0241 -->\n```ceesafe\nfn addTo(target: borrow mut i32, amount: borrow mut i32) {\n    target += amount;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    var x = 1;\n    addTo(mut x, mut x);\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:7:18: error[N0241]: cannot borrow 'x' as mutable while 'x' is mutably borrowed\n  |\n7 |     addTo(mut x, mut x);\n  |                  ^^^^^\n  = note: 'x' is mutably borrowed here (7:11)\n  = note: the borrow is used later here (7:5)\n```\n\n## References in structs: regions\n\nA struct that holds a reference or a view must say so, with a **region parameter**: the region is the lifetime of\nwhat the reference points into. Values of such a type cannot outlive that region.\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Word<region src> {\n    text: StringView<src>;\n    start: usize;\n}\n\nfn firstWord<region src>(line: StringView<src>) -> Word<src> {\n    var end: usize = 0;\n    while (end < line.length && line[end] != ' ') {\n        end += 1;\n    }\n    return Word { text: line[0..end], start: 0 };\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let line = \"hello wide world\";\n    let word = firstWord(line);\n    Console.writeLine(word.text);\n}\n```\n\n```text\nhello\n```\n\nPlain functions that",
    57 =>
        " take and return references never need region parameters; only types that *store* references,\nand functions that pass such types through, do. Regions are a compile-time idea: they cost nothing at run time.\n\n## What the checker does not do\n\nThe borrow checker works within one function at a time, using the signatures of the functions it calls. It is\ndeliberately simpler than Rust's: some correct programs are rejected. When that happens, the usual fixes are to end\na borrow earlier, copy a value out, split a function, or use an index or a [slot map handle](collections.md) instead\nof a stored reference.\n\n## Next\n\n[Errors](errors.md): `Result`, `?`, `!` and panics.\n",
    58 =>
        "# Packages\n\nA package is a directory with a `ceesafe.toml`: a program or a library, its sources, and the packages it depends on.\nDependencies come from local paths or git repositories, pinned to exact commits in `ceesafe.lock`. The full rules are in\n[Tooling §4](../spec/tooling.md#_4-packages-t6-adr-0013) and [ADR 0013](../spec/decisions/0013-m10-modules-and-packages.md).\n\n## The manifest\n\n```toml\n[package]\nname = \"app\"                 # an identifier: what other packages call this one\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nkind = \"executable\"          # \"executable\", \"library\", or \"object\" (one .o for a kernel's build)\nprofile = \"hosted\"           # or \"freestanding\"\nfloat = true                 # false is --no-float\nsources = [\"src\"]            # the default\nc = [\"src/shim.c\"]           # C files built with an executable\nceesafec = \">=0.20.0\"        # the compiler this package needs: an older one stops here, naming both versions\n\n[dependencies]\ngeometry = { path = \"../geometry\" }\nlz4 = { git = \"https://example.org/lz4-cee.git\", tag = \"v1.2.0\" }      # or rev = \"<commit>\", or branch = \"main\"\n```\n\nOnly `name` is required. `ceesafec` takes a version requirement like a dependency's (`>=0.20.0, <0.21.0`); a compiler\nolder than the field reports it as unknown, at the same line. A dependency's key must be the name its own manifest gives it, and a dependency must be a\nlibrary.\n\n## C libraries\n\nA package that wraps a C library names it, and every program that depends on the package links it:\n\n```toml\n[package]\nname = \"tls\"\nkind = \"library\"\nc = [\"c/shim.c\"]\nlinks = [\"ssl\", \"crypto\"]                # -lssl -lcrypto (ssl.lib crypto.lib with cl)\nlib-dirs = [\"$OPENSSL_DIR/lib\"]          # an environment variable, or a literal path\ninclude-dirs = [\"$OPENSSL_DIR/include\"]  # for this package's own C files\n\n[target.windows]                         # added on Windows; also [target.macos], [target.linux]\nlinks = [\"libssl\", \"libcrypto\"]\n```\n\nNothing runs to find the library: no pkg-config, no scripts. A directory is literal, or starts with an environment\nvariable, which must be set. `ceesafec build --emit c` writes the link line to `ceesafe_link.txt`, for building by hand.\n\n## Embedded files\n\n`embed(\"path\")` and `embedText(\"path\")` ([Basics](basics.md#embedded-files)) read files inside the package's directory.\nTo embed what a build generates elsewhere, list the directory:\n\n```toml\n[embed]\nroots = [\"../build/codegen\"]             # relative to the package; no absolute paths or environment variables\n```\n\nA root may be outside the package in your own package and in a path dependency you reach through path dependencies\nalone. A git or registry dependency, and every package reached through one, embeds only from that checkout, whatever\nits roots say: a symbolic link it holds, or a path dependency it names, that leads outside is refused (N0362). So is\nany package in the package cache, even when you open its files in the editor or run `check` there. A missing file and\none outside get the same message, which names the path only as you wrote it.\n\n## Using a dependency\n\nAll files of one package share one scope. Another package's items are reached through its name, and only its\n`public` items are visible:\n\n<!-- ceesafe: skip -->\n```ceesafe\n// geometry/src/lib.cee\npublic struct Point {\n    public x: i64;\n    public y: i64;\n\n    public fn create(x: i64, y: i64) -> Point => Point { x: x, y: y };\n    public fn norm(this) -> i64 => this.x * this.x + this.y * this.y;\n}\n\npublic fn origin() -> Point => Point.create(0, 0);\n\nfn helper() -> i64 => 1;          // internal: only geometry can call it\n```\n\n<!-- ceesafe: skip -->\n```ceesafe\n// app/src/main.cee\nimport geometry.Point;            // one item; `import geometry;` brings all its public items\n\nfn main() {\n    let p = Point.create(3, 4);   // imported\n    let q = geometry.origin();    // qualified\n    Console.writeLine(p.norm() + q.norm());\n}\n```\n\n- `geometry.X` names a dependency's public item, in types, expressions and patterns.\n-",
    59 =>
        " `import geometry.X;` brings one item into the file's scope, and `import geometry;` all its public items. Imports\n  apply to their file only. A name two imports provide is an error where it is used.\n- Within a package, the name of a dependency is reserved: nothing else in the package may be called `geometry`.\n- A public function may not expose a type its users cannot see (a public function returning an internal struct).\n- Two packages may declare the same name: in the generated C, a dependency's items carry its package's name.\n\n## The lock file\n\n```sh\nceesafec fetch          # resolves dependencies and writes ceesafe.lock\nceesafec build          # uses ceesafe.lock; fails if it does not match ceesafe.toml\n```\n\n`ceesafe.lock` records every dependency, direct and indirect: where it comes from, and for a git dependency the exact\ncommit and a SHA-256 of its files. Commit it with `ceesafe.toml`, so that every build of the package uses the same code.\n\n- A `tag` or `branch` is resolved to a commit by `ceesafec fetch` and stays that commit until the next fetch, even if the\n  branch moves.\n- Every build checks each git dependency's files against the lock's hash, so a checkout changed behind your back is\n  an error.\n- Git repositories and checkouts are cached in `~/.ceesafe` (or `$CEESAFE_HOME`). `--offline` builds never touch the network.\n- Nothing runs during a fetch or a build except `git` and the C compiler: packages have no build scripts.\n- Two path or git dependencies that need the same package must agree on its source and commit.\n\n## The registry\n\nA dependency can also be a version requirement, looked up in the package index:\n\n```toml\n[dependencies]\nshapes = \"2.1\"                # any 2.x from 2.1.0 on\ngeometry = \"=1.4.0\"           # exactly\njson = { version = \"0.9\", index = \"https://example.org/my-index.git\" }\n```\n\n`ceesafec fetch` chooses the newest version of each package that satisfies every requirement on it, and records its\ncommit and hash in `ceesafe.lock`, so builds stay pinned until the next fetch. A version already locked is kept while it\nstill fits; `ceesafec fetch --update` (or `--update shapes`) chooses afresh. When no choice works, the error says which\nrequirements clash and where each came from.\n\nTo publish a library, give its manifest a `version`, commit and push it, and run `ceesafec publish`. It checks the package\nand prints the entry to add to the index by pull request.\n\n## Building\n\n```sh\nceesafec check          # the package and its dependencies, checked together\nceesafec build          # out/<name>\nceesafec run --interp\nceesafec fmt            # the package's own sources\nceesafec doc -o api.md  # its public items\n```\n\nA build compiles the package and all its dependencies together into one C program, so generic code from a dependency\nis specialised for its users and checked like the rest.\n\n## Files without a package\n\nEverything also works on files: `ceesafec build a.cee b.cee -o tool`. Files given together are one package with no\ndependencies.\n",
    60 =>
        "# Performance\n\nCeeSafe compiles through C, with every safety check on: array bounds, integer overflow, lossy conversions. This page\nmeasures what that costs against Rust and plain C on sixteen workloads, and says where CeeSafe is ahead or behind and\nwhy.\n\n## The result\n\nOver the sixteen workloads CeeSafe takes **0.96× Rust's time** (geometric mean), and **0.88×** that of Rust built with\noverflow checks, which is the comparison that checks the same things CeeSafe does. Hand-written C without any check takes\n0.94×.\n\n| Workload | CeeSafe | Rust | Rust, overflow checks | C | CeeSafe (ms) | Rust (ms) |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| `heap-alloc` | **0.28** | 1.00 | 0.92 | 0.95 | 121 | 428 |\n| `nbody` | **0.82** | 1.00 | 1.08 | 0.82 | 500 | 611 |\n| `stack-alloc` | **0.84** | 1.00 | 1.70 | 0.85 | 244 | 289 |\n| `vector-growth` | **0.87** | 1.00 | 1.03 | 0.80 | 385 | 443 |\n| `arena` | **0.95** | 1.00 | 0.91 | 0.98 | 241 | 254 |\n| `memory-copy` | 0.99 | 1.00 | 1.06 | 1.13 | 595 | 602 |\n| `linked-list` | 1.01 | 1.00 | 0.99 | 0.90 | 366 | 361 |\n| `large-objects` | 1.02 | 1.00 | 0.97 | 1.02 | 254 | 250 |\n| `small-objects` | 1.03 | 1.00 | 1.00 | 0.89 | 510 | 493 |\n| `string-building` | 1.03 | 1.00 | 1.31 | 0.78 | 299 | 292 |\n| `numeric` | 1.12 | 1.00 | 1.00 | 1.14 | 240 | 214 |\n| `binary-trees` | 1.13 | 1.00 | 1.02 | 0.91 | 561 | 494 |\n| `parsing` | 1.16 | 1.00 | 1.10 | 1.05 | 161 | 139 |\n| `data-oriented` | 1.18 | 1.00 | 1.05 | 1.00 | 381 | 323 |\n| `recursion` | 1.26 | 1.00 | 1.26 | 1.00 | 407 | 323 |\n| `hash-map` | 1.41 | 1.00 | 1.17 | 0.94 | 453 | 321 |\n| **geometric mean** | **0.96** | 1.00 | 1.09 | 0.94 | | |\n\nTimes are each language's median wall time over five runs (after one discarded run) divided by Rust's; lower is\nfaster. Measured 2026-10-31 on an Apple M-series Mac (arm64, macOS), with ceesafec 0.2.0-dev, rustc 1.99.1 and Apple\nclang 21. A run-to-run spread of a few percent is normal: compare ratios, not single milliseconds.\n\nPeak memory is the same as Rust's or lower everywhere (`arena`: 7.8 MiB against 17.7 MiB). Binaries are about 40–56 KiB\nagainst Rust's 460 KiB. ceesafec compiles each program in about a quarter of a second, roughly twice rustc's time on these\nsingle-file programs, since it runs the C compiler after its own work.\n\n## Where CeeSafe is ahead\n\n- **`heap-alloc`, 3.5× faster than Rust.** ceesafec's placement optimisation keeps a box that never escapes its\n  function on the stack, so the program's per-iteration heap array costs no allocation at all. It beats hand-written C,\n  which calls `malloc` and `free` each time.\n- **`stack-alloc`, `nbody`, `vector-growth`.** CeeSafe matches C. Its overflow and bounds checks are removed where\n  ceesafec proves they cannot fail (loop indexes within a length, sums of bounded values), which Rust with overflow\n  checks does not manage on `stack-alloc` (1.70×).\n- **`arena`.** A region's bump allocation is inlined into the program (Decision 0007), and the arena's chunks take less\n  than half the memory of Rust's.\n\n## Where CeeSafe is behind, and why\n\n- **`hash-map` (1.41×).** Rust builds its tables with `vec![0; n]`, so LLVM knows their length and removes the bounds\n  checks on `keys[s & MASK]`. A CeeSafe `Vector` can only be filled by `push`, so the length is unknown and every probe\n  keeps its check: with the checks compiled out the program runs at Rust's speed. A filled-vector constructor would\n  close this gap.\n- **`recursion` (1.26×) and `parsing` (1.16×).** The overflow check on `fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)` and on accumulating\n  digits cannot be proven unnecessary; Rust pays the same when its overflow checks are on (1.26× and 1.10×).\n- **`data-oriented` (1.18×).** As for `hash-map`, the particle arrays are filled by `push`; the bounds checks cost about\n  11%.\n- **`binary-trees` (1.13×) and `small-objects` (1.03×).** Every heap allocation goes through the runtime's\n  `ceesafe_alloc`, which counts allocations (for `CEESAFE_ALLOC_REPORT` and fault injection) b",
    61 =>
        "efore calling `malloc`.\n  Calling `malloc` directly measured 12% faster on both.\n\n## Const parameters\n\n`const-flags` (ADR 0083 §1.3) is a recursive scanner over 10 MB of pseudo-random bytes, run 100 times, with three\nflag tests per byte; its flags are const parameters (`fn scan<const TS: bool, const JSX: bool>`), so each instance\nkeeps only its own tests. It is a workload of `run.py`, measured on its own and not in the table above, whose sixteen\nworkloads were measured with an older ceesafec. Measured 2026-11-09 on the same kind of machine (Apple arm64, Apple\nclang 21, rustc 1.99.1, ceesafec 0.26.0-dev), on a machine shared with other work, so the spread is wider than usual:\n\n| Version | Time |\n|---|---:|\n| CeeSafe, const parameters (`main.cee`) | 1.05× Rust (2.67 s against 2.55 s, `run.py`, median of 5) |\n| Rust, const generics (`main.rs`) | 1.00 |\n| CeeSafe, the same flags as `bool` parameters (`runtime.cee`) | 1.19× CeeSafe's const version (3.68 s against 3.10 s user time, 3 runs each) |\n\nAt `-O2` clang does not clone a recursive function for a constant argument, so a run-time flag keeps every test;\nthe const parameter removes them, as Rust's const generics do. `main.c` takes the flags as arguments (C has no const\nparameters), and in the same three runs took 3.27–3.67 s.\n\n## Run it yourself\n\n```sh\npython3 benchmarks/run.py                                    # every workload: cee, rust, rust-checked, c; 5 runs\npython3 benchmarks/run.py --workloads hash-map,nbody --runs 3\npython3 benchmarks/run.py --cee-flag --no-bce                # with bounds-check elimination off\n```\n\nEach workload is the same algorithm with the same data structures in every language, and prints one checksum, which\nthe runner checks. `benchmarks/README.md` describes the workloads and the build flags. To see which checks ceesafec kept\nin a program, build it with `--explain-bounds-checks`, `--explain-overflow-checks` or `--explain-allocations`\n([The ceesafec command](ceesafec.md)).\n",
    62 =>
        "# Structs, enums and patterns\n\n## Structs\n\nA struct groups named fields. Fields end with `;`. Functions declared inside are its methods (with a `this`\nreceiver) or static functions (without one).\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Point {\n    x: i64;\n    y: i64;\n\n    fn origin() -> Point => Point { x: 0, y: 0 };            // static: Point.origin()\n\n    fn manhattan(this) -> i64 {                               // `this` by value (Point is Copy)\n        return abs(this.x) + abs(this.y);\n    }\n\n    fn moveBy(borrow mut this, dx: i64, dy: i64) {            // changes the point in place\n        this.x += dx;\n        this.y += dy;\n    }\n}\n\nfn abs(v: i64) -> i64 => if (v < 0) -v else v;\n\nfn main() {\n    var p = Point.origin();\n    p.moveBy(3, -4);\n    Console.writeLine(p.manhattan());\n    let q = Point { x: 1, y: 2 };       // every field, in any order\n    Console.writeLine(q.x + q.y);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n7\n3\n```\n\n- A **struct literal** names every field exactly once.\n- The **receiver** says how a method gets its struct: `this` (a copy, or a move), `borrow this` (read-only access) or\n  `borrow mut this` (changes the caller's value). Calling a `borrow mut this` method needs a `var` (or a mutable\n  reference); the call borrows it for the duration of the call.\n- A struct is **Copy**, copied on assignment, when every field is Copy and it has no `drop` block. Otherwise it\n  moves: see [Ownership](ownership.md).\n- Fields are laid out in declaration order with C's rules, so a struct is the same in C.\n\n### Visibility\n\nItems and members are `internal` by default: visible throughout their package. `public` makes them visible to other\npackages (see [Packages](packages.md)); `private` on a field limits it to the file that declares the struct, which\nlets a type guard an invariant.\n\n## Enums\n\nAn enum is one of several variants, each with zero or more positional payloads. Variants are written with the enum's\nname.\n\n```ceesafe\nenum Shape {\n    Empty,\n    Circle(i64),\n    Rect(i64, i64),\n}\n\nfn area(s: Shape) -> i64 => match (s) {\n    Shape.Empty => 0,\n    Shape.Circle(r) => 3 * r * r,\n    Shape.Rect(w, h) => w * h,\n};\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(area(Shape.Circle(2)));\n    Console.writeLine(area(Shape.Rect(3, 4)));\n    Console.writeLine(area(Shape.Empty));\n}\n```\n\n```text\n12\n12\n0\n```\n\nAn enum whose variants have no payloads compares with `==` and `!=`. An enum with payloads compares with `match`, or\nwith `==` once it derives equality (below).\n\n```ceesafe\nenum Kind { User, Alias, List }\n\nfn main() {\n    let kind = Kind.Alias;\n    Console.writeLine(kind == Kind.Alias);\n    Console.writeLine(kind != Kind.User);\n}\n```\n\n```text\ntrue\ntrue\n```\n\n### Deriving equality, hashing and ordering\n\nStructs and enums have no `==` of their own. `@derive(Equal)` gives one: the fields are compared in declaration order\n(an enum's variant, then its payloads), and `!=` is the opposite. `@derive(HashKey)` (which needs `Equal`) makes the\ntype a `HashMap` key, and `@derive(OrderKey)` an `OrderedMap` key ordered field by field.\n\n```ceesafe\n@derive(Equal, HashKey, OrderKey)\nstruct Point {\n    x: i32;\n    y: i32;\n}\n\n@derive(Equal)\nenum Shape {\n    Dot(Point),\n    Box(Point, Point),\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let a = Point { x: 1, y: 2 };\n    let b = Point { x: 1, y: 5 };\n    Console.writeLine(a == b);\n    Console.writeLine(Shape.Box(a, b) == Shape.Box(a, b));\n    Console.writeLine(a.compareKey(b));\n    var seen = HashMap<Point, u32>.create();\n    seen.put(a, 1)!;\n    Console.writeLine(seen.contains(Point { x: 1, y: 2 }));\n}\n```\n\n```text\nfalse\ntrue\n-1\ntrue\n```\n\nEvery field needs `==` itself: numbers, `bool`, strings and views, fieldless enums, byte vectors and slices, optionals\nof these (two `null`s are equal), and other types that derive `Equal`, with arrays, slices and vectors of them (`==`\nworks on those anywhere: same length, equal elements). Floats compare as IEEE values, so a `NaN` field\nmakes a value unequal to itself. A field without `==` is an error at the attribute that names it; write a meth",
    63 =>
        "od of\nyour own for such a type. The derived code is ordinary CeeSafe that `ceesafec check --dump-hir` prints: for `Point`,\n`impl DerivedEqual<Point> for Point { fn derivedEqual(borrow this, other: borrow Point) -> bool => this.x == other.x\n&& this.y == other.y; }`, and `a == b` calls it. For `HashKey` and `OrderKey`, a field whose type is a fieldless enum\nneeds no derive of its own: its variant's position in the declaration is hashed and compared.\n\n## `match`\n\n`match` tries its arms in order. It must be **exhaustive**: every possible value matches some arm, or the compiler\nnames one that does not. An arm that can never be reached is an error too.\n\nAs a statement, each arm is a block; as an expression (above), each arm is a value, separated by commas.\n\n```ceesafe\nenum Command {\n    Move(i32, i32),\n    Wait(u32),\n    Quit,\n}\n\nfn run(command: Command) {\n    match (command) {\n        Command.Move(0, 0) => { Console.writeLine(\"stay\"); }\n        Command.Move(x, _) => {\n            Console.write(\"move x by \");\n            Console.writeLine(x);\n        }\n        Command.Wait(seconds) => {\n            Console.write(\"wait \");\n            Console.writeLine(seconds);\n        }\n        Command.Quit => {}\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    run(Command.Move(0, 0));\n    run(Command.Move(5, 1));\n    run(Command.Wait(3));\n    run(Command.Quit);\n}\n```\n\n```text\nstay\nmove x by 5\nwait 3\n```\n\nPatterns can be nested: a variant inside a variant (`Ok(Shape.Circle(r))`), a literal in a payload's position\n(`Command.Move(0, 0)`), `null` or `Some(…)` for optionals, and `_` for anything. Integers and booleans can be matched\ndirectly, with `_` for the rest.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn kind(n: u8) -> StringView => match (n) {\n    0 => \"zero\",\n    'a' => \"the letter a\",\n    _ => \"something else\",\n};\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(kind(97));\n    Console.writeLine(kind(3));\n}\n```\n\n```text\nthe letter a\nsomething else\n```\n\n### Alternatives\n\n`A | B` matches when either pattern does, at the top of an arm or inside a payload. When the alternatives bind\nnames, each binds the same ones, with the same types.\n\n```ceesafe\nenum Token {\n    Word(u32),\n    Number(u32),\n    End,\n}\n\nfn isSpace(c: u8) -> bool => match (c) {\n    ' ' | '\\t' | '\\n' => true,\n    _ => false,\n};\n\nfn length(t: Token) -> u32 => match (t) {\n    Token.Word(n) | Token.Number(n) => n,\n    Token.End => 0,\n};\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(isSpace('\\t'));\n    Console.writeLine(length(Token.Number(3)));\n}\n```\n\n```text\ntrue\n3\n```\n\n### Constants and names\n\nA bare name in a pattern is a constant when a `const` of that name is in scope, and otherwise binds the value at\nthat position, at the top of an arm too. `Type.NAME` names an associated constant.\n\n```ceesafe\nconst LF: u8 = 10;\n\nfn describe(c: u8) -> StringView => match (c) {\n    LF => \"newline\",\n    other => if (other < 32) \"control\" else \"text\",\n};\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(describe(10));\n    Console.writeLine(describe(65));\n}\n```\n\n```text\nnewline\ntext\n```\n\nA misspelt constant binds instead and matches everything; the compiler then reports the arms after it as\nunreachable.\n\n### Ranges\n\n`lo..=hi` matches the integers from `lo` to `hi`, both included; `lo..hi` leaves `hi` out. The bounds are integer or\ncharacter literals. Literals and ranges that together hold every value of the type need no `_`.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn kind(c: u8) -> StringView => match (c) {\n    'a'..='z' | 'A'..='Z' | '_' => \"letter\",\n    '0'..='9' => \"digit\",\n    _ => \"other\",\n};\n\nfn half(b: u8) -> u32 => match (b) {\n    0x00..=0x7F => 1,\n    0x80..=0xFF => 2,\n};\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(kind('q'));\n    Console.writeLine(kind('7'));\n    Console.writeLine(half(200));\n}\n```\n\n```text\nletter\ndigit\n2\n```\n\nA match on literals and ranges compiles to a C `switch`.\n\n### Naming what matched\n\n`name @ pattern` tests the pattern and binds the value it matched:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn digit(c: u8) -> u32 => match (c) {\n    d @ '0'..='9' => (d - '0') as u32,\n    q @ ('\"' | '\\'') => 100 + q as u32,\n    _ => 999,\n};\n\n",
    64 =>
        "fn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(digit('7'));\n}\n```\n\n```text\n7\n```\n\nA value moves at most once: in a match that moves payloads out, `whole @ Slot.Full(inner)` cannot move the value into\n`whole` and its payload into `inner` too (N0250); bind one of them.\n\n### Guards\n\nAn arm can add a condition after its pattern, `if (…)`: the arm is taken only when the pattern matches and the\ncondition holds; otherwise the next arm is tried. The guard sees the arm's bindings.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn size(x: u32?) -> StringView => match (x) {\n    Some(n) if (n > 100) => \"big\",\n    Some(0) => \"zero\",\n    Some(_) => \"small\",\n    null => \"none\",\n};\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(size(Some(500)));\n    Console.writeLine(size(Some(7)));\n}\n```\n\n```text\nbig\nsmall\n```\n\nA guarded arm never counts toward exhaustiveness: the compiler cannot know when its condition holds, so the other\narms must still cover every value. A guard only reads: it cannot move, assign or lend as `mut` the matched value or\nthe arm's bindings (N0355). In a match that moves a payload into its binding, the move happens only once the guard\nholds.\n\n### Strings and slices\n\nA string literal is a pattern on a `StringView`, and on a `Slice<u8>`, where it matches the text's bytes. A slice\npattern, `[…]`, matches a slice or an array by its elements: `..` stands for any number of them, and `rest @ ..`\nbinds those as a slice.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn command(word: StringView) -> u32 => match (word) {\n    \"get\" | \"head\" => 1,\n    \"put\" => 2,\n    _ => 0,\n};\n\nfn ends(s: Slice<i32>) -> i32 => match (s) {\n    [] => 0,\n    [only] => only,\n    [first, .., last] => first + last,\n};\n\nfn unquote(text: Slice<u8>) -> Slice<u8> => match (text) {\n    ['\"', inner @ .., '\"'] => inner,\n    _ => text,\n};\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(command(\"head\"));\n    let numbers = [3, 5, 8];\n    Console.writeLine(ends(numbers[..]));\n    Console.writeLine(unquote(\"\\\"quoted\\\"\".bytes()).length);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n1\n11\n6\n```\n\nStrings never cover their type, so a match on a `StringView` ends with `_` or a binding. Slice patterns cover by\nlength: `[]` and `[_, ..]` together match every slice, and a missing case is reported with one:\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0256 -->\n```ceesafe\nfn size(s: Slice<i32>) -> u32 => match (s) {\n    [] => 0,\n    [_] => 1,\n};\n\nfn main() {}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:1:41: error[N0256]: this match does not cover [_, _, ..]; add the missing arms or a '_' arm\n  |\n1 | fn size(s: Slice<i32>) -> u32 => match (s) {\n  |                                         ^\n```\n\nA slice pattern never moves an element: one of a Copy type is copied, any other is bound as a `borrow T`.\n\n### Patterns as conditions\n\n`e is P` tests a value against a pattern and gives a `bool`. In an `if` or `while` condition, the names the pattern\nbinds are in scope in the rest of the `&&` chain and in the then-branch or the loop body:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn find(key: u32) -> u32? => if (key > 2) Some(key * 10) else null;\n\nfn main() {\n    let c = 'q';\n    let letter = c is 'a'..='z' | 'A'..='Z';\n    Console.writeLine(letter);\n    if (find(5) is Some(found) && found > 20) {\n        Console.writeLine(found);\n    }\n    var stack = Vector<u32>.create();\n    stack.push(1)!;\n    stack.push(2)!;\n    while (stack.pop() is Some(top)) {\n        Console.writeLine(top);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```text\ntrue\n50\n2\n1\n```\n\nNowhere else is the test known to be true, so a pattern that binds under `!` or `||`, or as a whole `let`\ninitialiser, is an error (N0359). In a chain outside a condition, `let big = find(5) is Some(v) && v > 20;`, the\nnames end with the statement, so a `RefCell` guard bound that way is released before the next one. A test that binds a payload that is not Copy moves the value only when it succeeds:\nafter `if (t is Token.Word(w)) { … } else { … }`, the `else` branch still has `t`.\n\n## Optionals\n\n`T?` is a `T` or `null`. A plain `T` converts to `T?` wherever one is expected.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn find(values: Slice<i32>, wanted: i32) -> usize? {\n    for (i in 0..values.length) {\n        ",
    65 =>
        "if (values[i] == wanted) {\n            return i;                  // converts to usize?\n        }\n    }\n    return null;\n}\n\nfn firstOfTwo(values: Slice<i32>) -> usize? {\n    let a = find(values, 2)?;           // `?`: if null, return null\n    return a;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let numbers = [5, 2, 9];\n    match (find(numbers[..], 9)) {\n        Some(i) => { Console.writeLine(i); }\n        null => { Console.writeLine(\"absent\"); }\n    }\n    Console.writeLine(find(numbers[..], 7) == null);\n    Console.writeLine(firstOfTwo(numbers[..])!);   // `!`: the value, or stop the program if null\n    Console.writeLine(find(numbers[..], 7) ?? 99); // `??`: the value, or a fallback\n    Console.writeLine(find(numbers[..], 5).isSome());\n}\n```\n\n```text\n2\ntrue\n1\n99\ntrue\n```\n\n- `o == null` and `o != null` test for absence; so do `o.isNull()` and `o.isSome()`.\n- `o ?? fallback` is the value, or `fallback` when `o` is `null`. The fallback is evaluated only then, and a chain\n  `a ?? b ?? c` takes the first value present.\n- `o!` is the value; on `null` it stops the program with \"unwrapped null\".\n- `o?` is the value; on `null` the enclosing function returns `null` (it must return an optional).\n- A reference can be optional (`borrow T?`), and so can a box (`Box<T>?`); both are one pointer.\n\n## Results\n\n`Result<T, E>` is the other built-in enum: `Ok(value)` or `Err(error)`. It is how functions report failure; see\n[Errors](errors.md).\n\n## Next\n\n[Ownership and borrowing](ownership.md): what happens when a value is assigned, passed and destroyed.\n",
    66 =>
        "# Testing\n\nA test is a function marked `@test`, and `ceesafec test` runs a package's tests, each as its own process.\n\n## Writing tests\n\n```ceesafe\n/// The bytes 0..n, or the allocation failure.\nfn numbers(n: u64) -> Result<Vector<u8>, AllocError> {\n    var v = Vector<u8>.create();\n    for (k in 0..n) {\n        v.push(truncate<u8>(k))?;\n    }\n    return Ok(v);\n}\n\n@test\nfn counts() -> Result<void, TestFailure> {\n    let made = numbers(3);\n    CheckResult<Vector<u8>, AllocError>.isOk(borrow made, \"three bytes fit\")?;\n    match (made) {\n        Ok(v) => {\n            Check.equalU64(3, v.length as u64)?;\n            let expected = [0u8, 1, 2];\n            Check.equalBytes(expected[..], v.view())?;\n        }\n        Err(_) => { return Check.fail(\"unreachable after isOk\"); }\n    }\n    return Ok();\n}\n\n@test\nasync fn waits() -> Result<void, AsyncError> {\n    await Timer.sleep(1)?;\n    return Ok();\n}\n```\n\n- **Shape.** A test takes no parameters and returns `void` or `Result<void, E>`. An `Err` is its failure, and so is a\n  panic. It may be `async`.\n- **Checks.** `Check`'s functions (`isTrue`, `equalU64`, `equalText`, `equalBytes`, `fail`, …) return\n  `Result<void, TestFailure>`, so a failed check returns from the test with `?`, dropping what it holds as any return\n  does.\n- **Where.** Tests go beside the code, or in the package's `tests/` directory (`tests = [...]` in the manifest). They\n  see the package's private items.\n- **`build` leaves them out**, so calling a test from other code is an error (N0348).\n\n## Running them\n\n```text\nceesafec test                          every test\nceesafec test --filter parse --jobs 4  those whose name contains \"parse\", four at a time\nceesafec test --interp                 in the interpreter: a virtual clock, a fixed run order\nceesafec test --sweep alloc            each passing test again, once per allocation, that allocation failing\nceesafec test --sweep cancel           each passing async test again, cancelled at each of its suspensions\nceesafec test --format json            one JSON document, for CI\n```\n\nEach test runs as its own process, so a panic or a crash ends only that test. Tests run in the order of their names.\nEach is reported as passed, failed, panicked, crashed (a signal), exited (it called `Process.exit` itself), timed out\n(`--timeout`, 60 seconds by default) or leaked, natively and with `--interp`. A leak fails a test: CeeSafe has no\ngarbage collector, so memory still allocated at the end is a bug. A failing test's output is shown under its name, and\na test failing with its own enum names the variant. A `--filter` that matches no test is an error, and `--sweep` runs\nnatively only. `ceesafec test --help` lists the options.\n",
    67 =>
        "# Threads\n\nA program can run work on several threads. The compiler checks that safe code never has a data race: a value moves to\nanother thread only if it is safe there, and threads share memory only through types made for it.\n\n## Spawning and joining\n\n`Thread.spawn` starts a thread running a `move` closure, which owns what it captures. It returns a `Thread<T>`, and\n`join` waits for the thread and gives back its result.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    var numbers = Vector<u64>.create();\n    for (i in 1..101u64) {\n        numbers.push(i)!;\n    }\n    let worker = Thread.spawn(move () => {\n        var sum: u64 = 0;\n        for (n in numbers[..]) {\n            sum += n;\n        }\n        return sum;\n    })!;\n    Console.writeLine(worker.join());\n}\n```\n\n```text\n5050\n```\n\n- The vector is moved into the thread. The spawner cannot use it afterwards, so nothing is shared.\n- Spawning can fail (no memory, no thread): `Thread.spawn` returns a `Result`, here unwrapped with `!`.\n- A `Thread<T>` that is dropped without `join` is joined then: every thread ends before `main` returns.\n- A panic in any thread ends the whole program.\n\n## What may cross: `Send`\n\nA value may move to another thread if it is `Send`. Plain data, strings, vectors and boxes are. References are not,\nnor anything that holds one, nor raw pointers. The compiler infers it, and reports N0334 where it fails:\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0334 -->\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    var n: u64 = 5;\n    let address = borrow n as *u64;\n    let t = Thread.spawn(move () => address.isNull())!;\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:4:26: error[N0334]: this thread's closure captures 'address', a '*u64', which is not Send: it cannot move to another thread (type-system.md §38.1)\n  |\n4 |     let t = Thread.spawn(move () => address.isNull())!;\n  |                          ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n```\n\nA value that two threads *read* at once must also be `Share`. Most `Send` types are, but not a `StableVector`, whose\n`push` changes it through a shared reference, nor a `Box<dyn I + Send>`, whose contents the compiler cannot see, nor\nanything that holds one. Put such a value behind a `Mutex` to share it.\n\n`Send` and `Share` can also bound a generic function: `fn run<T: Send>(value: T)`.\n\n## Sharing: `Arc`, `Mutex` and atomics\n\nTo share a value, give each thread an owner of it: `Arc<T>` counts its owners, and the last one to go frees it. An\n`Arc` gives only shared access, so a value that threads change goes in a `Mutex`, whose guard lends it to one thread\nat a time.\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Tally {\n    count: u64;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let tally = Arc<Mutex<Tally>>.create(Mutex<Tally>.create(Tally { count: 0 })!)!;\n    var threads = Vector<Thread<void>>.create();\n    for (t in 0..4u64) {\n        let mine = tally.clone();\n        threads.push(Thread.spawn(move () => {\n            for (k in 0..250u64) {\n                var guard = mine.get().lock();\n                let data = guard.data();\n                data.count += 1;\n            }\n        })!)!;\n    }\n    threads.clear(); // dropping the threads joins them\n    var guard = tally.get().lock();\n    Console.writeLine(guard.data().count);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n1000\n```\n\nFor a counter or a flag, an atomic is simpler: `AtomicU64`, `AtomicU32`, `AtomicUSize` and `AtomicBool` change\nthrough a shared reference with `load`, `store`, `swap`, `compareExchange`, `fetchAdd` and `fetchSub`, and the\ninteger ones with `fetchOr` and `fetchAnd`.\n\nThose are sequentially consistent: every thread sees every atomic operation in one order. That is the simple rule,\nand on x86-64 it costs a full barrier on each store. When one thread publishes data and another reads it, release\nand acquire are enough, and `storeWith` and `loadWith` say so:\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Message {\n    data: AtomicU64;\n    ready: AtomicBool;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let m = Message { data: AtomicU64.create(0), ready: AtomicBool.create(false) };\n    var got: u64 = 0;\n    Thread.both(() => {\n        while (!m.ready.loadWith(LoadOrdering.Acquire)) {}\n ",
    68 =>
        "       got = m.data.loadWith(LoadOrdering.Relaxed);\n    }, () => {\n        m.data.storeWith(42, StoreOrdering.Relaxed);\n        m.ready.storeWith(true, StoreOrdering.Release);\n    });\n    Console.writeLine(got);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n42\n```\n\nThe release store makes the data written before it visible to a thread whose acquire load reads `true`. The\n`…With` methods take C11's orderings (`Relaxed`, `Acquire`, `Release`, `AcquireRelease`, `SequentiallyConsistent`). A\nweaker ordering never makes a data race, but a wrong one can make a thread read a stale value, and neither\n`ceesafec run --interp` nor ThreadSanitizer will notice. Use the plain methods unless a measurement says otherwise.\n`benchmarks/atomics` has one: a ring between two threads, 5 to 9 times faster on x86-64 with release and acquire.\n\n## Borrowing: `Thread.both`\n\nA spawned thread cannot borrow, since it may outlive what it borrowed. `Thread.both(a, b)` can: it runs `a` on a new\nthread and `b` on this one, and returns only when both have finished. So the two closures may use the caller's data,\nfor example the two halves of a slice:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn sum(values: Slice<u64>) -> u64 {\n    var total: u64 = 0;\n    for (v in values) {\n        total = total + v;\n    }\n    return total;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let values: [u64; 6] = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6];\n    let halves = values[..].splitAt(3);\n    var low: u64 = 0;\n    var high: u64 = 0;\n    Thread.both(() => { low = sum(halves.left); }, () => { high = sum(halves.right); });\n    Console.writeLine(low + high);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n21\n```\n\nThe usual borrowing rules hold between the two closures: both may read a value, but if one changes it the other may\nnot use it. A value both read must be `Share`, and one that a closure changes must be `Send`; a reference counts as\nits referent here. `splitAtMut` gives two halves that each closure may change. If a thread cannot be started, `both`\nruns `a` and then `b` on this thread, so it never fails.\n\n## Async tasks on several threads\n\nAn `Async.run` uses one thread. To spread a scope's children over several, run it with `Async.runWorkers(4, task)`\nand spawn them with `s.spawnSend(async () => …)` instead of `s.spawn`. Another worker may take such a child before it\nstarts. Once started, the child stays on that worker. What the child captures follows the rule of `Thread.both`: a\nvalue it reads must be `Share`, and one it changes must be `Send`. Two children cannot change the same value, so they\nreport results through atomics, or through a value per child. Under a plain `Async.run`, `spawnSend` is `spawn`.\n\n## Where threads run\n\nThreads are part of the hosted profile: Linux, macOS, Windows and CosmoOS user programs. Kernel code uses the\nkernel's own locks instead ([Kernel code](kernel.md)). The interpreter (`ceesafec run --interp`) runs threads one at a\ntime, in a fixed order, so its output is always the same.\n\n## Next\n\n[Async tasks](async.md): many waits on one thread.\n",
    69 =>
        "# Unsafe code and C\n\nSafe CeeSafe cannot express everything a systems program needs: talking to hardware, calling C, implementing an\nallocator. For those there is `unsafe`, kept small and visible. The contract unsafe code must keep is in\n[Unsafe code](../spec/unsafe.md), and the C boundary in the type system's\n[C interoperability](../spec/type-system.md#_28-c-interoperability-m8b) section.\n\n## Raw pointers\n\n`*T` and `*mut T` are raw pointers: plain addresses, Copy, with no borrow attached. **Making** a pointer is safe;\n**using** one (`read`, `write`, `add`, turning it back into a reference) is allowed only inside `unsafe`.\n\n```ceesafe\n/// Safety: `p` points to `n` initialised `u32`s that nothing else writes while this runs.\nunsafe fn sum(p: *u32, n: usize) -> u64 {\n    var total: u64 = 0;\n    for (i in 0..n) {\n        total += p.add(i).read() as u64;\n    }\n    return total;\n}\n\n/// Safety: the pointer comes from a live array and stays inside it.\nfn main() {\n    var values = [1u32, 2, 3, 4];\n    let p = borrow mut values[0] as *mut u32;\n    unsafe {\n        p.add(3).write(10);\n    }\n    Console.writeLine(unsafe { sum(p, values.length) });\n}\n```\n\n```text\n16\n```\n\n## The `Safety:` rule\n\nEvery `unsafe fn`, and every function that contains an `unsafe` block, must say in its doc comment why it is sound, on\na line starting with `Safety:`. The compiler enforces it:\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0283 -->\n```ceesafe\nfn first(p: *u8) -> u8 {\n    return unsafe { p.read() };\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:1:4: error[N0283]: function 'first' uses 'unsafe' and needs a '/// Safety:' doc comment saying why it is sound (type-system.md §27.4)\n  |\n1 | fn first(p: *u8) -> u8 {\n  |    ^^^^^\n```\n\nFor an `unsafe fn`, the line states what callers must guarantee; for a safe function with an `unsafe` block inside,\nwhy the block cannot go wrong. Reviewers read these lines; the compiler checks that they exist.\n\n`unsafe` permits only the pointer operations, calls of unsafe functions and C functions, and volatile accesses.\nEverything else in an unsafe block is checked exactly as in safe code.\n\n## Calling C\n\nC functions are declared in an `extern \"C\"` block and called inside `unsafe`, since CeeSafe cannot check what C does.\nTheir types are C's: integers, `bool`, raw pointers, `*CChar` for C strings, `CChar` for a `char`, and `@repr(C)`\nstructs.\n\n<!-- ceesafe: check -->\n```ceesafe\nextern \"C\" {\n    fn strlen(s: *CChar) -> usize;\n    fn puts(s: *CChar) -> i32;\n}\n\n/// Safety: a `c\"…\"` ends in a zero byte, as `puts` and `strlen` need.\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let text = c\"hello from C\";\n    let p = text.ptr();\n    unsafe {\n        puts(p);\n    }\n    return truncate<i32>(unsafe { strlen(p) });\n}\n```\n\n`c\"…\"` is a C string: the compiler adds the zero byte that ends it, and refuses a `\\0` inside. Its type is lib/core's\n`CStr`, a view with `ptr()` for C, `view()` and `length()` for CeeSafe (without the zero byte). It can be a constant or\na static, and there `PREFIX + c\"…\"` joins constant text before it (`const RC: CStr = HOME + c\"/.bunrc\";`).\n`CStr.fromBytesWithNul(bytes)` makes one from bytes that end in their only zero byte, or gives `null`; like\nany view, it cannot outlive those bytes. `\"…\\0\".ptr as *CChar` also works, but nothing checks the `\\0` is there.\n\nC files are built with the program: `ceesafec build main.cee helpers.c -o program`, or the `c` list of a package's\nmanifest.\n\nCeeSafe can't see what a C function does, so a call of one counts as possibly sleeping and allocating\n(type-system.md §32). Async code may not sleep, so calling such a function from an `async fn` is N0295. When the C\nfunction returns promptly, say so on its declaration: `@effects()` for none, or `@effects(alloc)` if it allocates.\n\n<!-- ceesafe: skip -->\n```ceesafe\nextern \"C\" {\n    @effects()\n    fn crc32_update(crc: u32, data: *u8, length: usize) -> u32;\n}\n```\n\n## Sharing C declarations between packages\n\nA C function has one declaration in a program, so its CeeSafe types can't drift apart: a second",
    70 =>
        " declaration with the\nsame C name is N0201, even in another package, and so is an `@export` with that name. One package owns the\ndeclaration and makes it `public`, and the others call it through that package\n([ADR 0081](../spec/decisions/0081-c-declarations-shared-between-packages.md)):\n\n<!-- ceesafe: skip -->\n```ceesafe\n// sys/src/lib.cee, a library\nextern \"C\" {\n    public fn strlen(s: *CChar) -> usize;\n}\n```\n\n<!-- ceesafe: skip -->\n```ceesafe\n// app/src/main.cee, which depends on sys\n/// Safety: the literal ends in a zero byte, as strlen needs.\nfn main() {\n    let text = \"hello\\0\";\n    Console.writeLine(unsafe { sys.strlen(text.ptr as *CChar) });\n}\n```\n\nIf `app` declares `strlen` too, N0201 is reported at `app`'s declaration, with a note at the one `sys` owns. The\nmessage says what to do: ``use `sys.strlen`, which package 'sys' declares; remove this declaration``, or, when the\ndeclaration in `sys` isn't `public`, to make it `public` there. When the two declarations are identical in CeeSafe\n(the same parameter and result types, variadic-ness and attributes), `ceesafec fix` removes the redundant one. It\ndoesn't change the calls, which still need `sys.` in front.\n\nTo give a C function a stronger type, such as a `borrow` instead of a raw pointer, write a wrapper in the package that\nwants it, rather than a second declaration. The wrapper calls the owner's declaration and says in its `Safety:` line\nwhy that call is sound. Within one package, two declarations with one C name are N0201 too, reported at the later\none in the package's file order:\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0201 -->\n```ceesafe\nextern \"C\" {\n    fn strlen(s: *CChar) -> usize;\n    @cname(\"strlen\")\n    fn length(s: *CChar) -> usize;\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:4:8: error[N0201]: the C name 'strlen' of C function 'strlen' is already used by another C function or export (type-system.md §28.2)\n  |\n4 |     fn length(s: *CChar) -> usize;\n  |        ^^^^^^\n  = note: the other declaration is here (2:8)\n```\n\n## Being called from C\n\n`@export` makes a CeeSafe function callable from C, and `@repr(C)` gives a struct C's layout. `ceesafec` writes\n`ceesafe_exports.h` with their declarations for the C side to include.\n\n<!-- ceesafe: check -->\n```ceesafe\n@repr(C)\nstruct Point {\n    x: i32;\n    y: i32;\n}\n\n/// The Manhattan length of a point, callable from C as `geo_length(struct Point)`.\n@export(\"geo_length\")\nfn length(p: Point) -> i32 => abs(p.x) + abs(p.y);\n\nfn abs(v: i32) -> i32 => if (v < 0) -v else v;\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(length(Point { x: 3, y: -4 }));\n}\n```\n\nIn C, a `@repr(C)` struct or `@repr` enum keeps the name it is declared with, even when it comes from a dependency:\npackage `geo`'s `Point` is `struct Point` in `ceesafe_exports.h`, not `geo.Point`. So, like a C function, a `@repr`\ntype's name belongs to one package in a program: a second `@repr(C) struct Point` in another package is N0201\n(type-system.md §28.5). Ordinary structs and enums have no such limit.\n\n`@callback` gives a function a C address without exporting a symbol, for C APIs that take a function pointer and a\n`void *` argument, as kernels' timer and thread APIs do. `extern \"C\" fn(…) -> R` is the type of such a pointer.\n\n## C structs from the C code's headers\n\nA C codebase's own structs are not re-declared in CeeSafe, where the two copies could drift apart. They are imported from\nits headers: CeeSafe uses them by pointer or embedded by value, names only the fields it uses, and the C compiler checks\neach one at build time.\n\n<!-- ceesafe: skip -->\n```ceesafe\n@header(\"kernel/timer.h\")\nextern \"C\" {\n    struct timer;\n    @effects()\n    fn timer_start(t: *mut timer, delay_ns: u64);\n}\n```\n\nSee [C structs from headers](../spec/decisions/0008-c-struct-bindings-from-headers.md) and\n[Kernel code](kernel.md).\n\n### Arrays, keyword names and tables\n\nA C array member is declared as a CeeSafe array (`uint8_t uuid[16]` is `uuid: [u8; 16]`) and used like one: indexed with\nbounds checks, sliced, copied. A member whose name is ",
    71 =>
        "a CeeSafe keyword takes `@cname`. An array of C structs is indexed in\nplace. A constant table can be exported to C as data with `@export`.\n\n<!-- ceesafe: check -->\n```ceesafe\n@header(\"kernel/gpt.h\")\nextern \"C\" {\n    struct gpt_part {\n        first_lba: u64;\n        @cname(\"type\")\n        kind: [u8; 16];\n        uuid: [u8; 16];\n    }\n    struct gpt_table {\n        nparts: u32;\n        parts: [gpt_part; 32];\n    }\n}\n\n/// `const uint8_t gpt_type_esp[16]` in C.\n@export(\"gpt_type_esp\")\nconst TYPE_ESP: [u8; 16] = [0x28, 0x73, 0x2a, 0xc1, 0x1f, 0xf8, 0xd2, 0x11, 0xba, 0x4b, 0x00, 0xa0, 0xc9, 0x3e, 0xc9, 0x3b];\n\n/// Is partition `i` an EFI system partition?\nfn isEsp(table: borrow gpt_table, i: usize) -> bool {\n    let kind = table.parts[i].kind; // a copy of the C array\n    for (k in 0..kind.length) {\n        if (kind[k] != TYPE_ESP[k]) { return false; }\n    }\n    return true;\n}\n```\n\nThis is how CosmoOS's GPT parser is written (`integrations/cosmoos/c9.py` checks it; the rules are\n[ADR 0014](../spec/decisions/0014-c9-c-arrays-and-exported-constants.md)).\n\n### C enums\n\nA header that types a parameter or a member with an enum is imported with `enum name: I;`, where `I` is the integer the C\ncompiler gives the enum. The type is an integer of its own: `as` converts it to and from `I`, and its\nvalues are constants of the type. CeeSafe spells it `enum name` (or a typedef's name, with `@ctype`) everywhere C sees it,\nso a function pointer type whose C type names the enum, such as an operation table's entry, is the same C type as the\nCeeSafe function's. With `u32` in its place, C would accept the call, but UBSan's `-fsanitize=function` refuses a call\nthrough a pointer of another type.\n\n<!-- ceesafe: check -->\n```ceesafe\n@header(\"kernel/vfs.h\")\nextern \"C\" {\n    enum vnode_type: u32;\n    struct vnode {\n        @cname(\"type\")\n        kind: vnode_type;\n    }\n}\n\nconst VNODE_DIR: vnode_type = 2;\n\nfn isDirectory(vn: borrow vnode) -> bool => vn.kind == VNODE_DIR;\n\n/// A readdir callback: its type, `vfs_dirent_cb`, takes the enum.\n@callback\nfn emit(arg: *mut void, name: *CChar, len: usize, ino: u64, kind: vnode_type) -> i32 => if (kind == VNODE_DIR) 1 else 0;\n```\n\nThe C compiler checks that the enum is the integer declared. That integer depends on the target: GCC and Clang on Linux\nand macOS make an enum without negative values `unsigned int` (`u32`), as this kernel's is, and one with a negative\nvalue `int` (`i32`), while on Windows every enum is `int`. A header meant for every target gives its enum a negative\nvalue, so that it is `i32` everywhere. The rules are [ADR 0079](../spec/decisions/0079-imported-c-enums.md) and\ntype-system.md §31.6.\n\n## C constants, C data and configured code\n\nA port of a C file keeps using what the C side defines: its constants, its globals, its build switches. The\n[C data rules](../spec/decisions/0080-the-kernel-floors.md) let CeeSafe name each of them without a C file of its own.\n\n**Constants C checks.** A constant written in an `extern \"C\"` block is an ordinary CeeSafe constant, and the C compiler\nasserts that the header agrees. `@cvalue` asserts a C expression instead, such as a size or an offset. `const NAME: T;`\nwith no value reads C's own value at run time, for a string macro:\n\n<!-- ceesafe: check -->\n```ceesafe\n@header(\"kernel/vfs.h\")\nextern \"C\" {\n    const EINVAL: i32 = 22;\n    @cvalue(\"sizeof(struct cosmo_stat)\")\n    const STAT_SIZE: usize = 144;\n    const COSMO_VERSION: *CChar;\n}\n\nfn invalid() -> i32 => -EINVAL;\n```\n\n**Data C names, and data C defines.** `@export` on a static makes it C data under that symbol: a plain `static` of a\nC type is C's `const` object, such as an operation table a header declares `extern const`, and a `static mut` is a\nvariable C may write, such as a sysctl's. In an `extern \"C\"` block, `static NAME: T;` reads data C defines:\n\n<!-- ceesafe: check -->\n```ceesafe\n@header(\"kernel/storage.h\")\nextern \"C\" {\n    struct fs_type {\n        flags: u32;\n    }\n    static procnet_names: [*CChar; 8];\n}\n\n/",
    72 =>
        "// `extern const struct fs_type pool_fs_type;` in C.\n@export(\"pool_fs_type\")\nstatic POOL_FS: fs_type = fs_type { flags: 1 };\n\n/// `extern uint32_t g_pool_rate;`: the sysctl writes it.\n@export(\"g_pool_rate\")\nstatic mut RATE: u32 = 100;\n```\n\n**C locals.** `var s: T;` of a C type is C's zero-filled object on the stack, for state a C API keeps in the caller's\nframe, as a hash state is. It is used by its address and never moved:\n\n<!-- ceesafe: check -->\n```ceesafe\n@header(\"kernel/blake2b.h\")\nextern \"C\" {\n    struct blake2b;\n    fn blake2b_init(s: *mut blake2b, outlen: usize);\n    fn blake2b_final(s: *mut blake2b, out: *mut u8);\n}\n\n/// Safety: the state is a live C local for each call.\nfn digest(out: borrow mut [u8; 32]) {\n    var s: blake2b;\n    unsafe {\n        blake2b_init(borrow mut s as *mut blake2b, 32);\n        blake2b_final(borrow mut s as *mut blake2b, borrow mut out[0] as *mut u8);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Code only some builds have.** `if (cfg(\"CONFIG_DEBUG\")) { … } else { … }` is C's `#if CONFIG_DEBUG`. A member or a C\nfunction that only some builds have is declared `@cfg(\"CONFIG_DEBUG\")` and used only inside such a block, which the\ncompiler checks (N0350). Both blocks are checked in every build:\n\n<!-- ceesafe: check -->\n```ceesafe\n@header(\"kernel/cosmofs.h\")\nextern \"C\" {\n    struct cfs {\n        @cfg(\"CONFIG_DEBUG\")\n        test_armed: bool;\n    }\n    @cfg(\"CONFIG_DEBUG\")\n    fn cfs_debug_note(fs: *mut cfs);\n}\n\n/// Safety: `fs` is a live filesystem.\n@export(\"cfs_test_arm\")\nunsafe fn arm(fs: *mut cfs) {\n    if (cfg(\"CONFIG_DEBUG\")) {\n        let f = Ptr.toBorrowMut(fs);\n        f.test_armed = true;\n        cfs_debug_note(fs);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n`CChar` is C's `char` by value as well as behind a pointer (`b as CChar`, `c as u8`), `CLong` and `CULong` are C's\n`long` and `unsigned long`, an imported struct's member may be an array of pointers, and `AtomicU8` is a byte member C\nchanges atomically.\n\n## C code that allocates\n\nA package's C files can allocate through CeeSafe's allocator, so the allocation-failure sweep reaches their\nout-of-memory paths too. `ceesafe/foreign.h` is on every C file's include path:\n\n```c\n#include \"ceesafe/foreign.h\"\n\nvoid *block = ceesafe_alloc(size, CEE_ALLOC_FOREIGN);      /* malloc */\nblock = ceesafe_realloc(block, bigger, CEE_ALLOC_FOREIGN); /* realloc; NULL allocates */\nceesafe_free(block, CEE_ALLOC_FOREIGN);                    /* free; NULL does nothing */\n```\n\nThese allocations are counted and can be failed (`CEESAFE_FAIL_ALLOC_AT`, `ceesafec test --sweep alloc`) like\nCeeSafe's own, but a block C still holds at exit is not a leak, since C libraries keep global state until the process\nends. The allocation report shows those blocks on their own line, `ceesafe alloc: foreign held=<k>`. A library with\nallocation hooks can be pointed at them in test builds; OpenSSL's are `CRYPTO_set_mem_functions`\n([ADR 0060](../spec/decisions/0060-web-random-tryrecv-foreign-host.md)).\n\nSome libraries cannot survive a failed allocation during their one-time setup: OpenSSL's initialisation re-enters\nitself when one fails. Allocate during that setup with `CEE_ALLOC_FOREIGN_INIT` instead: those allocations are never\nfailed and do not count as attempts, so the sweep tests the program's own out-of-memory paths, and the blocks they\nkeep are reported as held, like `CEE_ALLOC_FOREIGN`'s. Either number may reallocate or free a block made under the\nother. Use it for the setup only: an allocation made under it is never tested\n([ADR 0061](../spec/decisions/0061-foreign-allocations-for-initialisation.md)).\n\n## Embedding in a host program\n\nA program whose `main` is not CeeSafe's (a C, C++ or Rust program calling CeeSafe exports) links CeeSafe as a static\nlibrary. `ceesafec build --emit lib -o build/libtext.a` writes one archive holding the generated code and the runtime,\none member per C file; with `--cflag -flto=thin` the members are bitcode and join the host's ThinLTO. Link one archive\nper host program. Beside the archive it writes the ",
    73 =>
        "headers: `build/ceesafe_exports.h` declares the exports, and\n`build/ceesafe/embed.h` is the runtime's interface for a host. A package with a CeeSafe `main` gets no C `main` in the\narchive, since the host has its own (`--lib-main` keeps it).\n\n<!-- ceesafe: check -->\n```ceesafe\n/// The number of bytes of `text` that are ASCII letters.\n/// Safety: the host passes `length` readable bytes at `text`.\n@export(\"text_letters\")\nfn letters(text: *u8, length: usize) -> u64 {\n    let bytes = unsafe { Slice.fromRaw(text, length) };\n    var count: u64 = 0;\n    for (b in bytes) {\n        if ((b >= 'a' && b <= 'z') || (b >= 'A' && b <= 'Z')) {\n            count += 1;\n        }\n    }\n    return count;\n}\n```\n\nThe host calls `ceesafe_init` once, before any thread calls CeeSafe; after it, exports may be called from any of its\nthreads at once. Its config gives the program arguments (copied), the allocation report and failure injection, a\npanic hook, and the host's allocator, and `ceesafe_shutdown` prints the report and returns the blocks still live:\n\n```c\n#include \"ceesafe/embed.h\"\n#include \"ceesafe_exports.h\"\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nstatic void on_panic(void *context, const ceesafe_panic_info *info) {\n    (void)context;\n    fprintf(stderr, \"panic: %s at %s:%u\\n\", info->reason, info->file ? info->file : \"(runtime)\", info->line);\n    abort(); /* a hook must end the process or never return */\n}\n\nint main(int argc, char **argv) {\n    ceesafe_config config = {0};\n    config.size = sizeof config;\n    config.flags = CEESAFE_INIT_ENVIRONMENT; /* CEESAFE_ALLOC_REPORT and CEESAFE_FAIL_ALLOC_AT */\n    config.argc = argc;\n    config.argv = (const char *const *)argv;\n    config.panic_hook = on_panic;\n    if (ceesafe_init(&config) != 0) return 1;\n    printf(\"%llu\\n\", (unsigned long long)text_letters((const uint8_t *)\"a1b2\", 4));\n    return ceesafe_shutdown() == 0 ? 0 : 1; /* 1 if blocks leaked under a report */\n}\n```\n\n`cc -I build host.c build/libtext.a -o host` links it. A panic calls the hook on the thread that panicked, with the\nreason, the message and the CeeSafe location; threads that panic at once are in the hook at once, so a hook that\nwrites a report takes a lock first. The hook must not return into CeeSafe, call an export, `longjmp` out or throw:\nCeeSafe's frames have no unwinding, so after a panic that thread may not call CeeSafe again, and an export called on\nit prints the panic and aborts. Without `ceesafe_init` everything still works, but the panic is only printed, the\nenvironment's settings are ignored and `Process.argument` has no arguments\n([ADR 0088](../spec/decisions/0088-embedding-ceesafe-in-a-host-program.md)).\n\nA host with its own allocator (mimalloc, an arena) gives it in `config.allocator`: four functions with malloc's\ncontracts and a context, called from any thread. Every block CeeSafe and its runtime allocate then comes from it,\nand the report and failure injection work as before: an injected failure never reaches it. Its blocks must be aligned\nto 16 bytes (more than `max_align_t` promises on macOS arm64 and under MSVC); one that is not stops the program\nwith a panic that names the function. Install it before CeeSafe allocates anything, or `ceesafe_init` returns\n`CEESAFE_INIT_LATE`. Each side still frees what it allocated: a block CeeSafe hands out goes back through an export.\n\n```c\n/* mimalloc's process-wide functions: any thread may call them (a mimalloc heap belongs to one thread). */\nstatic void *my_alloc(void *context, size_t size) { (void)context; return mi_malloc_aligned(size, 16); }\nstatic void *my_zeroed(void *context, size_t size) { (void)context; return mi_zalloc_aligned(size, 16); }\nstatic void *my_realloc(void *context, void *p, size_t size) { (void)context; return mi_realloc_aligned(p, size, 16); }\nstatic void my_free(void *context, void *p) { (void)context; mi_free(p); }\n\nstatic const ceesafe_allocator mimalloc_allocator = {my_alloc, my_zeroed, my_realloc, my_free, NULL};\n/* in main, before ceesafe_init: *",
    74 => "/\nconfig.allocator = &mimalloc_allocator;\n```\n\n## Next\n\n[Kernel code](kernel.md).\n",
    75 =>
        "# The CeeSafe Specification\n\nCeeSafe is a memory-safe systems programming language with modern syntax, compiled ahead of time to\nportable C. This directory is its specification. `ceesafec` embeds it: `ceesafec explain` and the MCP server\n(`ceesafe_spec_search`, `ceesafe_spec_section`, the `ceesafe://docs/…` resources) serve this text.\n\n## 1. Documents\n\nNormative, in order of authority after the ADRs:\n\n| Document | What it specifies |\n|---|---|\n| [`decisions/`](decisions/) | the ADRs: accepted decisions override the other documents' proposals (§2) |\n| [`ownership.md`](ownership.md) | the safety invariants (O*, B*, E*, R*, H*, U*) and their formal design |\n| [`language-spec.md`](language-spec.md) | the language: goals, syntax overview, semantics |\n| [`grammar.md`](grammar.md) | the grammar |\n| [`type-system.md`](type-system.md) | types and static semantics, the built-in types and libraries, chapter by chapter |\n| [`lifetimes.md`](lifetimes.md) | references and the borrow checker |\n| [`memory-model.md`](memory-model.md) | allocation, regions, placement |\n| [`unsafe.md`](unsafe.md) | `unsafe`, raw pointers and their contract |\n| [`c-backend.md`](c-backend.md) | the generated C: its rules, names, ABI version, structure |\n| [`optimisations.md`](optimisations.md) | each optimisation's preconditions and checks |\n| [`profiles.md`](profiles.md) | the hosted and freestanding profiles and their runtimes |\n| [`diagnostics.md`](diagnostics.md) | every diagnostic code |\n| [`tooling.md`](tooling.md) | the command line, `doc`, `lsp`, debugging, packages, fixes, `explain`, `mcp`, agent files |\n| [`formatting.md`](formatting.md) | the canonical style `ceesafec fmt` writes |\n| [`api/core.md`](api/core.md) | `lib/core`'s API, generated by `ceesafec doc` |\n\nDescriptive: [`architecture.md`](architecture.md) (how `ceesafec` is built),\n[`intermediate-representation.md`](intermediate-representation.md) (the AST, HIR and NIR as implemented) and\n[`testing.md`](testing.md) (how CeeSafe is tested).\n\nThe [guide](../guide/introduction.md) teaches the language; every example in it is compiled by `tools/guide_check.py`.\nBoth are published at [ceesafe.github.io/CeeSafe](https://ceesafe.github.io/CeeSafe/) (the repository's `site/`).\n\n## 2. Decisions\n\nCeeSafe began as a port of an earlier compiler. `decisions/0001`–`0030` are that compiler's ADRs, inherited with their\ntext unchanged, under a header that says so. CeeSafe's own language decisions continue the series from ADR 0031 in the\nsame directory. The repository's own decisions (names, bootstrap, releases) are \"Decisions\" in its `docs/decisions/`,\na separate series.\n\n## 3. Names\n\nThe compiler, its files and the C it generates use CeeSafe's names:\n\n- the compiler is `ceesafec`; source files are `.cee`; packages are `ceesafe.toml` and `ceesafe.lock`, the cache is\n  `$CEESAFE_HOME` (default `~/.ceesafe`), and the default registry index is `github.com/CeeSafe/CeeSafe-index`;\n- the command line reads only `.cee` and `.c` files (`tooling.md`);\n- the generated C and the runtime use CeeSafe's names: `cee_`, `ceeF_`, `ceeMk_`, … for generated names, and\n  `ceesafe_*`, `CEE_*`, `CEESAFE_*`, `ceesafe/rt.h` for the runtime; programs may not declare C names with those\n  prefixes (N0292); and `ceesafe/rt.h` defines `CEESAFE_ABI_VERSION`, which every generated unit checks\n  (`c-backend.md` §1.1);\n- messages say CeeSafe: `ceesafe panic: …`.\n\n## 4. Reading this text\n\n- **Milestone tags** (M1–M15, C0–C9, K1–K7, G7b, …) name the milestone that introduced a rule. They are kept so a\n  rule can be traced to its history; they say nothing about CeeSafe's plan, which is the repository's `ROADMAP.md`.\n- **CosmoOS.** The kernel profile, `lib/kernel` and the CosmoOS bindings were developed inside CosmoOS, which builds\n  its CeeSafe code with the ceesafec it pins.\n- **Paths.** `src/…` is `ceesafec`'s source. `lib/…` and `runtime/…` name the library and runtime sources ceesafec\n  embeds (`architecture.md` §6). `te",
    76 =>
        "sts/corpus/…`, `benchmarks/…` and `programs/…` are under `tests/reference/`.\n  `integrations/cosmoos/…` holds the CosmoOS checks (`testing.md` §1).\n- **Tests.** Where a rule is checked by a test, the text names its tool (`testing.md`).\n\n## 5. Changing it\n\nSpec before code: a change to the language changes this specification first, with an ADR when it is a decision, and\nthen the compiler, its tests and the guide.\n",
    77 =>
        "# lib/core\n\n- [struct PhysAddr](#struct-physaddr)\n- [struct VirtAddr](#struct-virtaddr)\n- [struct Layout](#struct-layout)\n- [interface Allocator](#interface-allocator)\n- [enum AsyncError](#enum-asyncerror)\n- [struct Timer](#struct-timer)\n- [struct AsyncControl](#struct-asynccontrol)\n- [struct AsyncLeaf](#struct-asyncleaf)\n- [struct Raced](#struct-raced)\n- [struct TaskSet](#struct-taskset)\n- [struct AsyncTasks](#struct-asynctasks)\n- [struct AsyncTasksIn](#struct-asynctasksin)\n- [struct AsyncSignal](#struct-asyncsignal)\n- [enum LoadOrdering](#enum-loadordering)\n- [enum StoreOrdering](#enum-storeordering)\n- [enum Ordering](#enum-ordering)\n- [struct AtomicU64](#struct-atomicu64)\n- [struct AtomicU32](#struct-atomicu32)\n- [struct AtomicU8](#struct-atomicu8)\n- [struct AtomicUSize](#struct-atomicusize)\n- [struct AtomicBool](#struct-atomicbool)\n- [struct ByteOrder](#struct-byteorder)\n- [struct VectorBytes](#struct-vectorbytes)\n- [struct RawCell](#struct-rawcell)\n- [struct Cell](#struct-cell)\n- [struct RefCell](#struct-refcell)\n- [struct RefCellTaking](#struct-refcelltaking)\n- [struct CellRef](#struct-cellref)\n- [struct CellRefMut](#struct-cellrefmut)\n- [struct CStr](#struct-cstr)\n- [interface DerivedEqual](#interface-derivedequal)\n- [struct DerivedEqualElements](#struct-derivedequalelements)\n- [struct TextWriter](#struct-textwriter)\n- [enum TextAlign](#enum-textalign)\n- [struct Formatted](#struct-formatted)\n- [interface AppendFormatted](#interface-appendformatted)\n- [interface Iterator](#interface-iterator)\n- [struct Volatile](#struct-volatile)\n- [struct MmioPtr](#struct-mmioptr)\n- [struct NumberParsing](#struct-numberparsing)\n- [struct ResultMethods](#struct-resultmethods)\n- [struct OptionalResults](#struct-optionalresults)\n- [struct SliceHalves](#struct-slicehalves)\n- [struct SliceHalvesMut](#struct-slicehalvesmut)\n- [struct SliceSplitting](#struct-slicesplitting)\n- [struct SliceAlgorithms](#struct-slicealgorithms)\n- [struct VectorEditing](#struct-vectorediting)\n- [struct TextHalves](#struct-texthalves)\n- [struct ByteHalves](#struct-bytehalves)\n- [struct TextSplit](#struct-textsplit)\n- [struct ByteSplit](#struct-bytesplit)\n- [struct TextSearch](#struct-textsearch)\n- [struct ByteSearch](#struct-bytesearch)\n\n## struct PhysAddr\n\n```ceesafe\npublic struct PhysAddr\n```\n\nA physical address.\n\n### PhysAddr.of\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn of(bits: u64) -> PhysAddr\n```\n\nThe address whose value is `bits`.\n\n### PhysAddr.bits\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn bits(this) -> u64\n```\n\nThe address's value.\n\n### PhysAddr.offset\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn offset(this, n: u64) -> PhysAddr\n```\n\nThe address `n` bytes further on; panics if that overflows a `u64`.\n\n## struct VirtAddr\n\n```ceesafe\npublic struct VirtAddr\n```\n\nA virtual address.\n\n### VirtAddr.of\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn of(bits: u64) -> VirtAddr\n```\n\nThe address whose value is `bits`.\n\n### VirtAddr.bits\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn bits(this) -> u64\n```\n\nThe address's value.\n\n### VirtAddr.offset\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn offset(this, n: u64) -> VirtAddr\n```\n\nThe address `n` bytes further on; panics if that overflows a `u64`.\n\n## struct Layout\n\n```ceesafe\npublic struct Layout\n```\n\nThe size in bytes and the alignment (a power of two) of a block of memory.\n\n## interface Allocator\n\n```ceesafe\npublic interface Allocator {\n    fn allocate(borrow this, layout: Layout) -> Result<*mut u8, AllocError>;\n    unsafe fn deallocate(borrow this, p: *mut u8, layout: Layout);\n}\n```\n\nAn allocator a `Box<T, A>` can use: a struct without fields that implements this (type-system.md §30.2).\n\n## enum AsyncError\n\n```ceesafe\npublic enum AsyncError {\n    Cancelled,\n    TimedOut,\n    Io(IoError),\n}\n```\n\nWhy an async operation failed (type-system.md §40.4, §40.7).\n\n## struct Timer\n\n```ceesafe\npublic struct Timer\n```\n\nTime for tasks: the monotonic clock in milliseconds.\n\n### Timer.now\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn now() -> u64\n```\n\nMilliseconds on the monotonic clock, from an arbitrary start.\nSafety: ceesafe_async_now only reads the clock.\n\n### Timer.sleep\n\n```ceesaf",
    78 =>
        "e\npublic async fn sleep(ms: u64) -> Result<void, AsyncError>\n```\n\nWaits at least `ms` milliseconds without blocking the thread: other tasks run meanwhile.\nSafety: ceesafe_async_wait_until only records the deadline for the executor.\nA cancelled task's sleep returns `Err(AsyncError.Cancelled)` at once (type-system.md §40.7).\n\n## struct AsyncControl\n\n```ceesafe\npublic struct AsyncControl<E>\n```\n\nWhat `Async.isCancelled()` and `Async.checkpoint(cancelled)` call (type-system.md §40.4).\n\n### AsyncControl.isCancelled\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn isCancelled() -> bool\n```\n\nWhether the current task is cancelled and not shielded.\nSafety: ceesafe_async_status only reads the executor's state.\n\n### AsyncControl.enterShield\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn enterShield(ms: u64)\n```\n\n`Async.shield`'s start (type-system.md §40.7).\nSafety: it only counts the current task's shields.\n\n### AsyncControl.leaveShield\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn leaveShield()\n```\n\n`Async.shield`'s end.\nSafety: it only counts the current task's shields, after enterShield.\n\n### AsyncControl.checkpoint\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn checkpoint(cancelled: E) -> Result<void, E>\n```\n\n`Err(cancelled)` if the current task is cancelled and not shielded, else `Ok`.\n\n## struct AsyncLeaf\n\n```ceesafe\npublic struct AsyncLeaf\n```\n\nThe library's own steps for its waiting operations: not for programs (type-system.md §40.4).\n\n### AsyncLeaf.status\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn status() -> Result<void, AsyncError>\n```\n\nErr(Cancelled) if the current task is cancelled, Err(TimedOut) if its shield's time is up (type-system.md §40.7).\nSafety: ceesafe_async_status only reads the executor's state.\n\n## struct Raced\n\n```ceesafe\npublic struct Raced<T> {\n    public winner: u32;\n    public value: T;\n    public other: T?;\n}\n```\n\n`Async.race`'s result (type-system.md §40.4): the child that returned `Ok` first (0 or 1), its value, and the\nother's value if it also returned `Ok` before it saw its cancellation.\n\n## struct TaskSet\n\n```ceesafe\n@noncopy // the one handle of its record\npublic struct TaskSet<E: Copy>\n```\n\nThe set `Async.tasks`' body is given (ADR 0053 §3.1). The body, and the functions it passes the set to, start\nchildren with `await set.spawn(async move () => …)`, which waits while the set's limit of children run, and\n`set.trySpawn(async move () => …)`, which is false instead. A child owns what it captures: no loans, so it cannot\ncapture the set (N0349). The calls are the checker's (src/check/async.cee): these are the steps it joins.\n\n## struct AsyncTasks\n\n```ceesafe\npublic struct AsyncTasks<E: Copy>\n```\n\n`await Async.tasks(limit, oom, async (set) => …)` (ADR 0053): `Async.tasksIn` with the system heap.\n\n### AsyncTasks.run\n\n```ceesafe\n@effectsOf(body)\npublic async fn run(\n    limit: u32,\n    oom: E,\n    body: borrow mut fn(borrow TaskSet<E>) -> Result<void, E>,\n) -> Result<void, E>\n```\n\n## struct AsyncTasksIn\n\n```ceesafe\npublic struct AsyncTasksIn<E: Copy, A>\n```\n\n`await Async.tasksIn(alloc, limit, oom, async (set) => …)` (ADR 0053): the body runs in the awaiting task, as the\nset's owner, with the set; then every child it started is waited for. The children's tasks and frames come from\n`alloc`'s allocator type. The result is the body's error, else the first child's, else `oom` if a child's frame\ncould not be allocated, else Ok. The body's error cancels the children; a child's error cancels no sibling.\n\n### AsyncTasksIn.run\n\n```ceesafe\n@effectsOf(body)\npublic async fn run(\n    alloc: A,\n    limit: u32,\n    oom: E,\n    body: borrow mut fn(borrow TaskSet<E>) -> Result<void, E>,\n) -> Result<void, E>\n```\n\nSafety: the record is this frame's, which outlives every child: the set waits for them before it returns.\n\n## struct AsyncSignal\n\n```ceesafe\n@noncopy // one signal, shared: a copy would never be raised\npublic struct AsyncSignal\n```\n\nA one-shot wake-up from anywhere, to a task (ADR 0039): `await signal.wait()` parks the task until `signal.raise()`.\n`raise` has no effects, so an interrupt handler (`@context(irq)`) or anoth",
    79 =>
        "er thread may call it, the way a driver\ntells the task waiting for its device. A raise before the wait is kept: the wait then returns at once. One task\nwaits on a signal at a time.\n\n### AsyncSignal.create\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn create() -> AsyncSignal\n```\n\nA signal not raised.\n\n### AsyncSignal.constant\n\n```ceesafe\n@constant\npublic fn constant() -> AsyncSignal\n```\n\nThe same as create, in a static's initialiser (type-system.md §39.4): `static READY: AsyncSignal =\nAsyncSignal.constant();`, which an interrupt handler can raise.\n\n### AsyncSignal.raise\n\n```ceesafe\n@effects()\npublic fn raise(borrow this)\n```\n\nRaises the signal: the task waiting on it, if any, runs again.\nSafety: the runtime changes the record atomically, and only wakes the task it names.\n\n### AsyncSignal.wait\n\n```ceesafe\npublic async fn wait(borrow this) -> Result<void, AsyncError>\n```\n\nWaits until the signal is raised, then lowers it. A cancelled task returns `Err(AsyncError.Cancelled)` and no\nlonger waits (type-system.md §40.7).\nSafety: the record names this task only while it waits here; ceesafe_async_signal_forget takes it out.\n\n## enum LoadOrdering\n\n```ceesafe\npublic enum LoadOrdering {\n    Relaxed,\n    Acquire,\n    SequentiallyConsistent,\n}\n```\n\nThe ordering of an atomic load (ADR 0021): C11's memory_order_relaxed, _acquire and _seq_cst.\n\n## enum StoreOrdering\n\n```ceesafe\npublic enum StoreOrdering {\n    Relaxed,\n    Release,\n    SequentiallyConsistent,\n}\n```\n\nThe ordering of an atomic store: C11's memory_order_relaxed, _release and _seq_cst.\n\n## enum Ordering\n\n```ceesafe\npublic enum Ordering {\n    Relaxed,\n    Acquire,\n    Release,\n    AcquireRelease,\n    SequentiallyConsistent,\n}\n```\n\nThe ordering of an atomic read-modify-write (swap, compare-exchange, add, subtract): C11's memory_order_relaxed,\n_acquire, _release, _acq_rel and _seq_cst. A compare-exchange that fails orders as a load: AcquireRelease as\nAcquire, Release as Relaxed, the others as themselves.\n\n## struct AtomicU64\n\n```ceesafe\n@threadSafe\n@noncopy\npublic struct AtomicU64\n```\n\nA u64 that threads may read and change at once: every operation is atomic and sequentially consistent.\n\n### AtomicU64.create\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn create(value: u64) -> AtomicU64\n```\n\nA new atomic holding `value`.\n\n### AtomicU64.constant\n\n```ceesafe\n@constant\npublic fn constant(value: u64) -> AtomicU64\n```\n\nThe same as create, in a static's initialiser (type-system.md §39.4): `static N: AtomicU64 = AtomicU64.constant(0);`.\n\n### AtomicU64.load\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn load(borrow this) -> u64\n```\n\nSafety: the runtime accesses `value` only atomically, and safe code reaches it only through these methods.\n\n### AtomicU64.store\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn store(borrow this, value: u64)\n```\n\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU64.swap\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn swap(borrow this, value: u64) -> u64\n```\n\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU64.compareExchange\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn compareExchange(borrow this, expected: u64, desired: u64) -> Result<u64, u64>\n```\n\nOk(the old value) if it was `expected` and is now `desired`; else Err(the value it has).\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU64.fetchAdd\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchAdd(borrow this, value: u64) -> u64\n```\n\nAdds, wrapping; returns the old value.\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU64.fetchSub\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchSub(borrow this, value: u64) -> u64\n```\n\nSubtracts, wrapping; returns the old value.\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU64.fetchOr\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchOr(borrow this, value: u64) -> u64\n```\n\nSets the bits of `value`; the value before (ADR 0041).\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU64.fetchAnd\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchAnd(borrow this, value: u64) -> u64\n```\n\nKeeps only the bits of `value`; the value before (ADR 0041).\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU64.loadWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn loadWith(borrow this, order: LoadOrdering) -> u64\n```\n\nload, with an ordering (ADR 0021).\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU64.storeWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn storeWith(borrow this, value: u64, order: StoreOrdering)\n```\n\nstore, with an ord",
    80 =>
        "ering.\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU64.swapWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn swapWith(borrow this, value: u64, order: Ordering) -> u64\n```\n\nswap, with an ordering.\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU64.compareExchangeWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn compareExchangeWith(borrow this, expected: u64, desired: u64, order: Ordering) -> Result<u64, u64>\n```\n\ncompareExchange, with an ordering; a failure orders as Ordering says.\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU64.fetchAddWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchAddWith(borrow this, value: u64, order: Ordering) -> u64\n```\n\nfetchAdd, with an ordering.\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU64.fetchSubWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchSubWith(borrow this, value: u64, order: Ordering) -> u64\n```\n\nfetchSub, with an ordering.\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU64.fetchOrWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchOrWith(borrow this, value: u64, order: Ordering) -> u64\n```\n\nfetchOr, with an ordering.\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU64.fetchAndWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchAndWith(borrow this, value: u64, order: Ordering) -> u64\n```\n\nfetchAnd, with an ordering.\nSafety: as load.\n\n## struct AtomicU32\n\n```ceesafe\n@threadSafe\n@noncopy\npublic struct AtomicU32\n```\n\nA u32 that threads may read and change at once, as AtomicU64.\n\n### AtomicU32.create\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn create(value: u32) -> AtomicU32\n```\n\nA new atomic holding `value`.\n\n### AtomicU32.constant\n\n```ceesafe\n@constant\npublic fn constant(value: u32) -> AtomicU32\n```\n\nThe same as create, in a static's initialiser (type-system.md §39.4): `static N: AtomicU32 = AtomicU32.constant(0);`.\n\n### AtomicU32.load\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn load(borrow this) -> u32\n```\n\nSafety: as AtomicU64.load.\n\n### AtomicU32.store\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn store(borrow this, value: u32)\n```\n\nSafety: as AtomicU64.load.\n\n### AtomicU32.swap\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn swap(borrow this, value: u32) -> u32\n```\n\nSafety: as AtomicU64.load.\n\n### AtomicU32.compareExchange\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn compareExchange(borrow this, expected: u32, desired: u32) -> Result<u32, u32>\n```\n\nSafety: as AtomicU64.load.\n\n### AtomicU32.fetchAdd\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchAdd(borrow this, value: u32) -> u32\n```\n\nSafety: as AtomicU64.load.\n\n### AtomicU32.fetchSub\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchSub(borrow this, value: u32) -> u32\n```\n\nSafety: as AtomicU64.load.\n\n### AtomicU32.fetchOr\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchOr(borrow this, value: u32) -> u32\n```\n\nSets the bits of `value`; the value before (ADR 0041).\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU32.fetchAnd\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchAnd(borrow this, value: u32) -> u32\n```\n\nKeeps only the bits of `value`; the value before (ADR 0041).\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU32.loadWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn loadWith(borrow this, order: LoadOrdering) -> u32\n```\n\nload, with an ordering (ADR 0021).\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU32.storeWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn storeWith(borrow this, value: u32, order: StoreOrdering)\n```\n\nstore, with an ordering.\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU32.swapWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn swapWith(borrow this, value: u32, order: Ordering) -> u32\n```\n\nswap, with an ordering.\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU32.compareExchangeWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn compareExchangeWith(borrow this, expected: u32, desired: u32, order: Ordering) -> Result<u32, u32>\n```\n\ncompareExchange, with an ordering; a failure orders as Ordering says.\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU32.fetchAddWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchAddWith(borrow this, value: u32, order: Ordering) -> u32\n```\n\nfetchAdd, with an ordering.\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU32.fetchSubWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchSubWith(borrow this, value: u32, order: Ordering) -> u32\n```\n\nfetchSub, with an ordering.\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU32.fetchOrWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchOrWith(borrow this, value: u32, order: Ordering) -> u32\n```\n\nfetchOr, with an ordering.\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU32.fetchAndWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchAndWith(borrow this, value: u32, order: Ordering) -> u32\n```\n\nfetchAnd, with an ordering.\nSafety: as load.\n\n## struct AtomicU8\n\n```ceesafe\n@threadSafe\n@noncopy\npublic struct ",
    81 =>
        "AtomicU8\n```\n\nA u8 that threads may read and change at once, as AtomicU32: for a byte member that C reads and writes atomically\n(ADR 0080 §3.5), such as a process's flag.\n\n### AtomicU8.create\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn create(value: u8) -> AtomicU8\n```\n\nA new atomic holding `value`.\n\n### AtomicU8.constant\n\n```ceesafe\n@constant\npublic fn constant(value: u8) -> AtomicU8\n```\n\nThe same as create, in a static's initialiser (type-system.md §39.4).\n\n### AtomicU8.load\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn load(borrow this) -> u8\n```\n\nSafety: as AtomicU64.load.\n\n### AtomicU8.store\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn store(borrow this, value: u8)\n```\n\nSafety: as AtomicU64.load.\n\n### AtomicU8.swap\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn swap(borrow this, value: u8) -> u8\n```\n\nSafety: as AtomicU64.load.\n\n### AtomicU8.compareExchange\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn compareExchange(borrow this, expected: u8, desired: u8) -> Result<u8, u8>\n```\n\nSafety: as AtomicU64.load.\n\n### AtomicU8.loadWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn loadWith(borrow this, order: LoadOrdering) -> u8\n```\n\nload, with an ordering (ADR 0021).\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU8.storeWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn storeWith(borrow this, value: u8, order: StoreOrdering)\n```\n\nstore, with an ordering.\nSafety: as load.\n\n## struct AtomicUSize\n\n```ceesafe\n@threadSafe\n@noncopy\npublic struct AtomicUSize\n```\n\nA usize that threads may read and change at once, as AtomicU64 (usize is 64 bits on every target, §29.3).\n\n### AtomicUSize.create\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn create(value: usize) -> AtomicUSize\n```\n\nA new atomic holding `value`.\n\n### AtomicUSize.constant\n\n```ceesafe\n@constant\npublic fn constant(value: usize) -> AtomicUSize\n```\n\nThe same as create, in a static's initialiser (type-system.md §39.4).\n\n### AtomicUSize.load\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn load(borrow this) -> usize\n```\n\nThe value, sequentially consistent.\n\n### AtomicUSize.store\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn store(borrow this, value: usize)\n```\n\nStores `value`, sequentially consistent.\n\n### AtomicUSize.swap\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn swap(borrow this, value: usize) -> usize\n```\n\nStores `value` and returns the old value, sequentially consistent.\n\n### AtomicUSize.compareExchange\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn compareExchange(borrow this, expected: usize, desired: usize) -> Result<usize, usize>\n```\n\nOk(the old value) if it was `expected` and is now `desired`; else Err(the value it has).\n\n### AtomicUSize.fetchAdd\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchAdd(borrow this, value: usize) -> usize\n```\n\nAdds, wrapping; returns the old value.\n\n### AtomicUSize.fetchSub\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchSub(borrow this, value: usize) -> usize\n```\n\nSubtracts, wrapping; returns the old value.\n\n### AtomicUSize.fetchOr\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchOr(borrow this, value: usize) -> usize\n```\n\nAs fetchOr.\n\n### AtomicUSize.fetchAnd\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchAnd(borrow this, value: usize) -> usize\n```\n\nAs fetchAnd.\n\n### AtomicUSize.loadWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn loadWith(borrow this, order: LoadOrdering) -> usize\n```\n\nAs load, with `order`.\n\n### AtomicUSize.storeWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn storeWith(borrow this, value: usize, order: StoreOrdering)\n```\n\nAs store, with `order`.\n\n### AtomicUSize.swapWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn swapWith(borrow this, value: usize, order: Ordering) -> usize\n```\n\nAs swap, with `order`.\n\n### AtomicUSize.compareExchangeWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn compareExchangeWith(\n    borrow this,\n    expected: usize,\n    desired: usize,\n    order: Ordering,\n) -> Result<usize, usize>\n```\n\nAs compareExchange, with `order` (a failure orders as a load, §38.3).\n\n### AtomicUSize.fetchAddWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchAddWith(borrow this, value: usize, order: Ordering) -> usize\n```\n\nAs fetchAdd, with `order`.\n\n### AtomicUSize.fetchSubWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchSubWith(borrow this, value: usize, order: Ordering) -> usize\n```\n\nAs fetchSub, with `order`.\n\n### AtomicUSize.fetchOrWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchOrWith(borrow this, value: usize, order: Ordering) -> usize\n```\n\nAs fetchOrWith.\n\n### AtomicUSize.fetchAndWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchAndWith(borrow this, va",
    82 =>
        "lue: usize, order: Ordering) -> usize\n```\n\nAs fetchAndWith.\n\n## struct AtomicBool\n\n```ceesafe\n@threadSafe\n@noncopy\npublic struct AtomicBool\n```\n\nA bool that threads may read and change at once.\n\n### AtomicBool.create\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn create(value: bool) -> AtomicBool\n```\n\nA new atomic holding `value`.\n\n### AtomicBool.constant\n\n```ceesafe\n@constant\npublic fn constant(value: bool) -> AtomicBool\n```\n\nThe same as create, in a static's initialiser (type-system.md §39.4).\n\n### AtomicBool.load\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn load(borrow this) -> bool\n```\n\nThe value, sequentially consistent.\n\n### AtomicBool.store\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn store(borrow this, value: bool)\n```\n\nStores `value`, sequentially consistent.\n\n### AtomicBool.swap\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn swap(borrow this, value: bool) -> bool\n```\n\nStores `value` and returns the old value, sequentially consistent.\n\n### AtomicBool.compareExchange\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn compareExchange(borrow this, expected: bool, desired: bool) -> Result<bool, bool>\n```\n\nOk(the old value) if it was `expected` and is now `desired`; else Err(the value it has).\n\n### AtomicBool.loadWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn loadWith(borrow this, order: LoadOrdering) -> bool\n```\n\nAs load, with `order`.\n\n### AtomicBool.storeWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn storeWith(borrow this, value: bool, order: StoreOrdering)\n```\n\nAs store, with `order`.\n\n### AtomicBool.swapWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn swapWith(borrow this, value: bool, order: Ordering) -> bool\n```\n\nAs swap, with `order`.\n\n### AtomicBool.compareExchangeWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn compareExchangeWith(borrow this, expected: bool, desired: bool, order: Ordering) -> Result<bool, bool>\n```\n\nAs compareExchange, with `order` (a failure orders as a load, §38.3).\n\n## struct ByteOrder\n\n```ceesafe\npublic struct ByteOrder\n```\n\nReading and writing integers in a byte order: static functions; the struct has no fields and is never made.\n\n### ByteOrder.readU8BE\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn readU8BE(s: Slice<u8>, at: usize) -> u8\n```\n\nThe `u8` in the 1 byte at `at`, big-endian.\n\n### ByteOrder.readI8BE\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn readI8BE(s: Slice<u8>, at: usize) -> i8\n```\n\nThe `i8` in the 1 byte at `at`, big-endian, in two's complement.\n\n### ByteOrder.writeU8BE\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn writeU8BE(s: SliceMut<u8>, at: usize, x: u8)\n```\n\nWrites `x` to the 1 byte at `at`, big-endian.\n\n### ByteOrder.writeI8BE\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn writeI8BE(s: SliceMut<u8>, at: usize, x: i8)\n```\n\nWrites `x` to the 1 byte at `at`, big-endian, in two's complement.\n\n### ByteOrder.readU8LE\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn readU8LE(s: Slice<u8>, at: usize) -> u8\n```\n\nThe `u8` in the 1 byte at `at`, little-endian.\n\n### ByteOrder.readI8LE\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn readI8LE(s: Slice<u8>, at: usize) -> i8\n```\n\nThe `i8` in the 1 byte at `at`, little-endian, in two's complement.\n\n### ByteOrder.writeU8LE\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn writeU8LE(s: SliceMut<u8>, at: usize, x: u8)\n```\n\nWrites `x` to the 1 byte at `at`, little-endian.\n\n### ByteOrder.writeI8LE\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn writeI8LE(s: SliceMut<u8>, at: usize, x: i8)\n```\n\nWrites `x` to the 1 byte at `at`, little-endian, in two's complement.\n\n### ByteOrder.readU16BE\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn readU16BE(s: Slice<u8>, at: usize) -> u16\n```\n\nThe `u16` in the 2 bytes at `at`, big-endian.\n\n### ByteOrder.readI16BE\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn readI16BE(s: Slice<u8>, at: usize) -> i16\n```\n\nThe `i16` in the 2 bytes at `at`, big-endian, in two's complement.\n\n### ByteOrder.writeU16BE\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn writeU16BE(s: SliceMut<u8>, at: usize, x: u16)\n```\n\nWrites `x` to the 2 bytes at `at`, big-endian.\n\n### ByteOrder.writeI16BE\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn writeI16BE(s: SliceMut<u8>, at: usize, x: i16)\n```\n\nWrites `x` to the 2 bytes at `at`, big-endian, in two's complement.\n\n### ByteOrder.readU16LE\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn readU16LE(s: Slice<u8>, at: usize) -> u16\n```\n\nThe `u16` in the 2 bytes at `at`, little-endian.\n\n### ByteOrder.readI16LE\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn readI16LE(s: Slice<u8>, at: usize) -> i16\n```\n\nThe `i16` in the 2 bytes at `at`, little-en",
