        "; uint64_t len; uint64_t cap; }` (24 bytes, asserted), element code `V<U<T> or Z><A>` (`Vector<i32>` → `ceeV_Ui32S10SystemHeap`). An empty vector points to the runtime's `ceesafe_vec_empty` (a `max_align_t` object, never dereferenced) with `cap == 0`, so slices of it never do arithmetic on NULL. Growth is `ceesafe_vec_grow` (doubling, at least 4; a first buffer is allocated, a later one resized with `ceesafe_realloc`, which keeps the old bytes; NULL with nothing changed on overflow or allocation failure). Push and append call it only when the buffer is full, and pass the capacity through a local, so the vector's address does not escape the helper and the C compiler can keep its length in a register. Per type: `ceeVP_` push (destroys the value on failure), `ceeVO_` pop, `ceeVC_` clear, `ceeVA_` append (strings; it copies a text of up to 16 bytes with a byte loop and a longer one with `ceesafe_mem_copy`, since a `memcpy` of a length unknown to the C compiler is a library call for gcc, also for one byte), `ceeVW_` withCapacity; drop glue `ceeD_V…` destroys the elements in index order, then frees the buffer if `cap != 0`. The elements place `*v` is the vector itself, so elements are `v.ptr[i]` and views are rebuilt from `ptr` and `len` |\n| `SlotMap<T, A>`, `Handle<T>` (M6e) | `ceeK_<T><A>` = `struct { ceeKS_… *ptr; uint64_t len, cap, count; uint32_t free; }` (40 bytes) with slots `ceeKS_…` = `{ uint32_t gen; uint32_t next; T value; }`; element code `K<T><A>`. `Handle<T>` is `cee_handle` = `{ uint32_t index; uint32_t gen; }` from `ceesafe/rt.h` (code `H<T>`), compared with `ceesafe_handle_eq`. Generations are odd while a slot is live; a removal whose generation reaches `UINT32_MAX - 1` retires the slot (never put back on the free list), so generations never wrap. Helpers `ceeKI_` insert (grows the slots with `ceesafe_vec_grow`, at most `UINT32_MAX - 1` slots), `ceeKV_` validity, `ceeKG_`/`ceeKM_` get (an optional pointer), `ceeKR_` remove; drop glue destroys values in odd-generation slots |\n| `StableVector<T, A>` (M6e) | `ceeSV_<T><A>` = `struct { void *chunks[CEE_SVEC_CHUNKS]; uint64_t len; }` (264 bytes); element code `W<T><A>`. Chunk `k` holds `8 << k` elements and is allocated by `ceesafe_svec_slot` when its first element is pushed; chunks never move or shrink. Element `i` is `(*ceeSVE_…(&sv, i))` (`ceesafe_svec_at`: chunk `60 - clz(i + 8)`). A shared reference to a stable vector is a non-const pointer, since `push` mutates through it. Drop glue destroys the elements, then `ceesafe_svec_free` |\n| `File`, `TcpListener`, `TcpStream` (C3) | `int32_t` descriptors; drop glue is the runtime's `ceesafe_fd_drop` (close). I/O built-ins call `ceesafe_*` functions declared in `ceesafe/rt.h` and implemented in `runtime/hosted/ceesafe_rt_io.c`; each returns `cee_io_result { value, error }`, and a per-type helper `ceeIOR_<Result>` turns it into `Result<X, IoError>` (written only for an `X` an I/O built-in returns: `void`, an integer or a descriptor) (the runtime maps errno to the variant; `Other` keeps the number). The generated `main` is `int main(int argc, char **argv)` — the one place plain `char` appears — and passes argv to `ceesafe_init` (§6) |\n| `StringView` (M6c) | `cee_str` = `struct { const uint8_t *ptr; uint64_t len; }` from `ceesafe/rt.h` (16 bytes); element code `PZ` (`Z` is the unsized `str`). A literal is `((cee_str){ .ptr = (const uint8_t *)\"…\", .len = n })`, pointing to static storage. A literal longer than 4095 bytes, the most C99 and C11 require a compiler to accept (§5.2.4.1; clang and GCC warn beyond it under `-Wpedantic`, and MSVC rejects much longer ones), is instead a file-scope `static const uint8_t ceeLS_<k>[] = { 0x…, … };`, emitted once per distinct text, and `.ptr = ceeLS_<k>` (0.15.2). The same goes for a static's initialiser and `Console.write` of a literal. A file's bytes from `embed` or `embedText` (type-system.md §10.1, ADR 0087 §2.8) are such an array at any length but zero, once per",
