        "# ADR 0003 — M3 heap types are built in, ahead of general generics\n\n> **Inherited.** ADR 0003 was accepted before CeeSafe's fork (Decision 0006), when the language had its\n> earlier name. In this record, \"the reference\" is that language and project and \"the reference compiler\" its compiler:\n> read them as CeeSafe and `ceesafec`. Links to documents CeeSafe did not keep are given as text.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-22, when starting M3: \"Built-in types\")\n- Scope: how M3 provides `Result`, `Box` and allocators before generics (M6), interfaces\n  (M6) and raw pointers / `unsafe` (M8) exist.\n\n## Context\n\nThe M3 acceptance examples (`language-spec.md` Ex4, Ex5, Ex11, Ex14) use `Result<T, E>`,\n`Box<T, A>`, `T?`, a generic function with an interface bound (`createUser<A: Allocator>`),\n`StringView` and integer methods such as `checkedMul`. The `Allocator` interface in\n`memory-model.md` §3.2 uses `unsafe fn` and `*mut u8`. Generics and interfaces are scheduled\nfor M6 and raw pointers for M8 (`roadmap.md`).\n\n## Decision\n\n1. `Result<T, E>` and `Box<T, A>` are **compiler-known generic types**, like `Slice<T>` in M2.\n   Each instantiation is monomorphised in C. User-declared generic types and functions remain\n   M6.\n2. Allocators are **built-in, free-standing types**: `SystemHeap` and `FailingAllocator`. Their\n   values are zero-sized. `AllocError` is a built-in enum.\n3. The `Allocator` interface stays as specified in `memory-model.md` §3.2. User-defined\n   allocators, and generic functions over allocators (Ex5 as written), come with interfaces in\n   M6. M3 runs Ex5 with a concrete allocator type.\n4. Optionals (`T?`, `null`, `?` on optionals) stay in M6 with the other library types. Ex11 is\n   run in an adapted form without `StringView`, `checkedMul` and `okOr`.\n5. When M6 adds generics, `Result` and `Box` should become library types with the same surface\n   syntax and the same C layout, so programs written for M3 keep compiling.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- M3 stays small: no generic type checking and no interface resolution.\n- The acceptance criteria are met with Ex4, Ex14 and adapted Ex5/Ex11 (`milestone-3.md`); the\n  deferred parts of Ex5 and Ex11 are tracked for M6.\n- The built-in surface (`Ok`, `Err`, `Box.create`, `Box.createIn`, `?`, `!`) is the one M6 must\n  preserve.\n",
    140 =>
        "# ADR 0004 — M4 regions: built-in region types, scoped results, LIFO scratch frames\n\n> **Inherited.** ADR 0004 was accepted before CeeSafe's fork (Decision 0006), when the language had its\n> earlier name. In this record, \"the reference\" is that language and project and \"the reference compiler\" its compiler:\n> read them as CeeSafe and `ceesafec`. Links to documents CeeSafe did not keep are given as text.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (2026-10-22, by the agent under the owner's instruction \"start M4\", following\n  the pattern the owner chose in ADR 0003; open to revision by the owner)\n- Scope: how M4 provides `Arena`, `FixedBufferAllocator`, `Scratch` and `Pool` before generics,\n  interfaces and `unsafe` exist (M6, M8).\n\n## Context\n\n`memory-model.md` §3.3–3.5 and `ownership.md` R1–R4 describe region allocators whose allocations\nare **loans of the region**. The examples (`language-spec.md` Ex15, Ex16) use generic methods\n(`arena.allocArray<u32>(256)`), named arguments and allocations returned inside `Result`. v0 has\nnone of: generic methods, named arguments, references inside `Result` (§17.1 forbids them).\n\n## Decision\n\n1. **Built-in region types**, as ADR 0003 did for `Box`. `Arena`, `FixedBufferAllocator`,\n   `ScratchFrame`, `Pool<T>` and `PoolBox<T>` are compiler-known, and their operations are\n   built-in methods. M6 may turn them into library types with the same surface.\n2. **Scoped results.** A `Result<T, E>` whose `T` or `E` is scoped (a reference, a slice, or\n   another scoped value) is itself scoped. It follows the reference rules (§13.1): only in `let`\n   locals, parameters and return types. The borrow checker treats every local of a scoped type as\n   holding loans, not only reference locals (`lifetimes.md` §3).\n3. **R1 as specified.** Allocation methods take `borrow this` and return values that carry a shared\n   loan of the region. An allocation may still be a `borrow mut T`: its memory is fresh and\n   disjoint from every other allocation, so exclusive access to it does not conflict with the\n   shared loan of the region.\n4. **No generic methods.** `allocArray(n, fill)` takes the fill value (like `[v; N]`), and `T`\n   comes from `fill`. `Pool<T>`'s `T` comes from the expected type, as for `Ok`/`Err`.\n5. **`Arena.create` cannot fail.** Chunks are allocated on demand, so it returns an `Arena`, not a\n   `Result`.\n6. **Scratch frames are LIFO by borrowing (corrects `memory-model.md` §3.4).**\n   - The claim that `using` alone keeps frames LIFO is wrong. With frames `a`, then `b`, an\n     allocation from `a` while `b` is live lands above `b`'s watermark, and `b`'s release then\n     frees memory that `a`'s allocation still uses.\n   - Fixed by: a nested frame is made with `parent.enter()`, which holds a **mutable loan** of the\n     parent. The parent cannot allocate while the child lives (B1).\n   - A root frame is made with `Scratch.enter()`, and only one root frame per thread may be live:\n     entering a second one panics (\"a scratch frame is already active\").\n   - Scratch memory is a runtime-owned stack of heap chunks, not the native stack.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- R1 needs no special case in the borrow checker beyond decision 2: loans flow from the region\n  into the allocation through the method's receiver, like any `borrow(this)` result.\n- Ex15 and Ex16 run in adapted forms (`milestone-4.md`).\n- `memory-model.md` §3.4 is amended to point here.\n",
    141 =>
        "# ADR 0005 — M6: user generics and interfaces; built-in collections; floats deferred\n\n> **Inherited.** ADR 0005 was accepted before CeeSafe's fork (Decision 0006), when the language had its\n> earlier name. In this record, \"the reference\" is that language and project and \"the reference compiler\" its compiler:\n> read them as CeeSafe and `ceesafec`. Links to documents CeeSafe did not keep are given as text.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-22, when starting M6: \"Built-in types\" for collections,\n  \"Adapt Ex13\" for floats)\n- Scope: what M6 implements in the language, and what stays built in.\n\n## Decision\n\n1. **User generics and interfaces are real language features in M6.** This covers generic\n   functions, structs and enums, monomorphisation, interfaces, `impl` blocks and bounds\n   (`language-spec.md` Ex12, Ex13, OD-10: static dispatch only).\n2. **Generic bodies are checked once, generically** (`type-system.md` §20):\n   - a type parameter is an opaque type: a move type that may need drop, supporting only what its\n     bounds allow;\n   - move and borrow checking also run on the generic version;\n   - each instantiation is then monomorphised for C. Errors are reported at the definition, not\n     per instantiation.\n3. **Collections are built-in generic types**, like `Box` (ADR 0003) and the regions (ADR 0004):\n   `Vector<T, A>`, `String<A>`, `StringView`, `SlotMap<T>` with `Handle<T>`, and\n   `StableVector<T>`. Their memory is managed by the C runtime. The user-visible API is the one\n   `memory-model.md` §4 describes. A reference core library implementation waits for `unsafe` (M8).\n4. **Library conversion is deferred to M8.** `Result`, `Box`, the regions and the collections\n   become CeeSafe library types once raw pointers and `unsafe` exist. The surface syntax stays.\n5. **Floats stay out.** Ex13 runs with integer geometry. Floats (`f32`/`f64` arithmetic,\n   formatting, the kernel-profile ban) are an open item to schedule separately (OD-23 in `open-decisions.md`).\n\n## Consequences\n\n- M6 adds the generic machinery (type parameters, instantiation, inference, interfaces) that M8's\n  library will need. The collections' semantics (invalidation through borrows, H2) are checked\n  by the same borrow checker.\n- Ex13 and Ex12 run in adapted forms. Ex12's `return if (…) a else b` is written as an `if`\n  statement, since `if` expressions do not exist in v0.\n",
    142 =>
        "# ADR 0006 — OD-3: region parameters on aggregates and functions\n\n> **Inherited.** ADR 0006 was accepted before CeeSafe's fork (Decision 0006), when the language had its\n> earlier name. In this record, \"the reference\" is that language and project and \"the reference compiler\" its compiler:\n> read them as CeeSafe and `ceesafec`. Links to documents CeeSafe did not keep are given as text.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-23: \"implement OD-3 first\", before C3). The two\n  remaining questions of `od3-design.md` §10 were not answered explicitly; this ADR takes the\n  design's defaults, which the owner may revisit.\n- Scope: references and other scoped values inside user structs and enums, generic instances and\n  built-in collections. Implemented as milestone **M6x** (`milestone-6x.md`).\n\n## Decision\n\n1. **Implement OD-3 now**, as designed in `od3-design.md`, before C3.\n2. **Syntax (default of §10 question 2):** region parameters `<region r>` on structs, enums and\n   functions; region arguments first in a type's argument list (`Token<src>`, `StringView<r>`,\n   `Slice<r, T>`); `borrow(r) T` for references; `static` is the built-in region of static data.\n3. **Precision (default of §10 question 3):** one loan set per value, the union over its regions.\n4. **Regions are erased after checking.** Two types that differ only in region arguments are the\n   same type. Regions only determine, in signatures, which arguments' loans flow into the result\n   and into `borrow mut` arguments (`type-system.md` §25.6).\n5. **Type parameters act as regions** in signatures, and values whose type mentions a type\n   parameter carry loans in generic bodies, so a generic checked once stays sound when\n   instantiated with a scoped type.\n6. Destroying a value that both holds loans and needs drop is a use of its loans (drop check). It\n   reports the existing N0243 rather than a new code; N0271 stays unassigned.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- E3 and E4 are relaxed exactly as `type-system.md` §25 says. `Box`, statics, constants, arrays,\n  slices and pools still reject scoped values (N0246).\n- v0 programs keep their meaning: a v0 signature has no region names, and its flows equal its v0\n  return sources.\n- New diagnostics: N0270, N0272, N0273, N0274.\n",
    143 =>
        "# ADR 0007 — M8: hybrid core library, raw pointers, unsafe contexts\n\n> **Inherited.** ADR 0007 was accepted before CeeSafe's fork (Decision 0006), when the language had its\n> earlier name. In this record, \"the reference\" is that language and project and \"the reference compiler\" its compiler:\n> read them as CeeSafe and `ceesafec`. Links to documents CeeSafe did not keep are given as text.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-24, when starting M8: \"Hybrid\" for the core\n  library). Decisions 2–5 are the agent's defaults under that instruction, following the pattern of\n  ADR 0004; the owner may revise them.\n- Scope: how M8 provides the core library, raw pointers and unsafe code (`milestone-8.md`). FFI,\n  profiles and MMIO details are specified with their increments (8b–8d).\n\n## Context\n\nMaster prompt Milestone 8 asks for a core library, freestanding compilation, a C FFI, raw-pointer\nunsafe operations, custom entry points and target capabilities. Every library type today (Option,\nResult, Box, Vector, Console, File, …) is built into the compiler and the C runtime (ADR 0003,\n0005); the reference has no library source files. The grammar reserves `unsafe` and `extern`, and\n`ownership.md` U1–U3 sketch the rules, but nothing is specified or implemented.\n\n## Decision\n\n1. **Core library: hybrid** (owner). The existing built-in types stay built in. The \"split\" is\n   a per-profile capability table (hosted-only names such as `Console`, `File` and `SystemHeap` are\n   rejected in freestanding code) plus the runtime split into a freestanding core and a hosted part.\n   New systems types that unsafe the reference can express (`Volatile<T>`, `MmioPtr<T>`, `PhysAddr`, memory\n   helpers) are written in the reference, in a `lib/core` bundled with the compiler (8c, 8d).\n   *Rejected:* everything built in (`lib/core` would stay empty, and safe wrappers over unsafe code\n   would be exercised only by tests); porting the M3–M6 built-ins to the reference (a large rewrite with\n   regression risk, not needed for C4).\n2. **Raw pointers are `*T` and `*mut T`**, Copy, with no loans (U3). Making and converting them is\n   safe; using them is unsafe. Conversions use `as`, so no new operator is needed; the operations\n   are methods (`p.read()`, `p.write(v)`, `p.add(n)`) and `Ptr.*` / `Slice.fromRaw` built-ins.\n   There is no `*p` dereference operator, since the reference has no prefix dereference at all (references\n   and boxes auto-dereference). *Rejected:* a `*p` place operator (new syntax, and `*` is already\n   multiplication); `null` as a pointer literal (`null` is the optional's empty value, §22.1); the null pointer is\n   `0 as *T`.\n3. **Pointer reads and writes need a Copy pointee** in v0. Moving a value out of or into raw memory\n   would need the drop elaborator to track raw places. It can come later, with the allocator\n   interface.\n4. **Unsafe contexts** are `unsafe { }` blocks, `unsafe { e }` expressions (Ex17: `let n =\n   unsafe { write(…) };`, since the reference has no block expressions) and `unsafe fn` bodies. The `Safety:`\n   doc comment (U2) is an **error** when missing, not a warning: the reference has no warnings, and the rule\n   is cheap to satisfy.\n5. **Generated C has no undefined pointer arithmetic**: raw pointers are plain `T *` (no `const`),\n   and arithmetic and casts go through `uintptr_t` (`unsafe.md` §4).\n\n6. **FFI (8b)**:\n   - Extern declarations name C types exactly, through the mapping of `type-system.md` §28.3\n     (`usize` is `size_t`, `*T` is `const T *`, and the pointee-only `CChar` is `char`), and calls go\n     through generated wrappers. Calling a C function through an incompatible prototype is undefined\n     in C11, and clang and GCC reject redeclared library functions with other types.\n   - `@export` alone makes a function C-callable. Ex17's `public extern \"C\" fn` spelling is not\n     used: the attribute already says everything, and an `extern` definition would read like a\n     declaration.\n   - `@re",
    144 =>
        "pr(C)` structs keep their CeeSafe names in C, so C code using `ceesafe_exports.h` has the same\n     struct type. Arrays and other non-FFI-safe types are not allowed in them yet.\n   - The interpreter models a few C library functions and faults on any other C call.\n   - Function pointers move to 8d: a C callback needs a C-typed wrapper, which exports provide.\n7. **Systems capabilities (8d)**:\n   - `lib/core` is CeeSafe source embedded in `ceesafec` and part of every package; unused parts are\n     not emitted, so programs that do not use it compile to the same C as before.\n   - `Volatile<T>` is a cell written through `borrow mut this`, not an interior-mutable cell: writing a\n     register needs exclusive access to the register block, which `MmioPtr<T>.getMut()` gives.\n   - Only C function pointers exist (`extern \"C\" fn(…)`); their values are extern and exported\n     functions, whose C addresses already exist. Calling one is unsafe.\n   - `Target.isX86_64()` and friends are decided by the C compiler, because one generated C file\n     serves every LP64 target; there is no conditional compilation in the reference yet.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `ownership.md` U3's `borrowFromRaw(p)` is spelled `Ptr.toBorrow(p)` / `Ptr.toBorrowMut(p)` (`borrow` is a\n  keyword, so `Ptr.borrow` would not parse).\n- The interpreter becomes the dynamic oracle for unsafe code too (null, dead, out-of-bounds,\n  misaligned, uninitialised accesses), but it does not track loans.\n- M5's rule 1c (address exposed to unsafe) is met conservatively: a function that turns references\n  into pointers keeps its boxes on the heap.\n- Pointee types excluded type parameters in 8a. 8d lifts this (`lib/core`'s `Volatile<T>` needs\n  `*T`); a scoped type argument for such a `T` is N0274, as for boxes.\n",
    145 =>
        "# ADR 0008 — C struct types are imported from the codebase's headers\n\n> **Inherited.** ADR 0008 was accepted before CeeSafe's fork (Decision 0006), when the language had its\n> earlier name. In this record, \"the reference\" is that language and project and \"the reference compiler\" its compiler:\n> read them as CeeSafe and `ceesafec`. Links to documents CeeSafe did not keep are given as text.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-24: \"take the freedom of modifying [profiles.md §3.2] to\n  suit a better implementation. The reference in fact is multi-platform; it should work with the Linux codebase\n  also\"). The design below is the agent's under that instruction; the owner may revise it.\n- Scope: how CeeSafe code uses struct types that a C codebase defines: CosmoOS kernel and boot-protocol\n  structs, Linux kernel structs, C library structs. It replaces the rule of `profiles.md` §3.2\n  that kernel structs are \"never re-declared\" and are seen only as opaque types \"with size and alignment\n  queried at build time\".\n\n## Context\n\nThe old rule protected against a real hazard: a struct's layout in a kernel depends on the kernel's\nconfiguration (`CONFIG_*` adds and removes fields, e.g. lockdep's `class` in `spinlock_t`), on the\narchitecture, and on the ABI version (CosmoOS module ABI 11). A layout written down in the reference would be\nright for one build and silently wrong for another. But the rule also left the reference unable to *use* such a\nstruct: an opaque type can be passed along, not read. C6's survey found that three of its five\ncandidates (the framebuffer validator, the module ELF validator, GPT) are blocked for exactly this\nreason, and every driver or subsystem the reference will touch later takes kernel structs. The reference is also meant to\nwork with other C codebases than CosmoOS, the Linux kernel above all, where the same hazard holds at a\nmuch larger scale (layouts change with every `.config`).\n\n## Decision\n\n1. **Imported C types.** Inside an `extern \"C\"` block, `struct name { field: Type; … }` declares a view of\n   a C type that a header defines, and `@header(\"path.h\")` on the block names the headers that define\n   it. The C spelling is `struct name`, or the `@ctype(\"name_t\")` attribute's for a typedef. A\n   declaration without fields (`struct name;`) imports the type opaquely.\n2. **The layout is the header's, never the reference's.** the reference knows no size, alignment or offset of an imported\n   type. Generated C includes the headers and uses the real type, so every field access is the C\n   compiler's member access, compiled under the codebase's own flags. One CeeSafe source works for every\n   configuration and architecture that has the fields it names.\n3. **Only the fields the reference uses are declared, with their C names, and each is checked.** Generated C\n   asserts at compile time that every declared field exists and has exactly the declared type\n   (`_Static_assert` on `_Generic` of the member and on its size). A configuration that removes or changes\n   a field fails to compile, naming the field; it never miscompiles.\n4. **Imported types live behind pointers and references.** Since the reference does not know their size, values of\n   them are not CeeSafe values: no locals, parameters or fields of the type itself, no copies; `*T`,\n   `*mut T`, `borrow T` and `borrow mut T` only. Field places through them are read and written like any\n   field (embedded imported structs are places too). Ownership of such objects stays with the C API that\n   provides them (kernel objects, boot data), which is what the kernel-memory design wants anyway.\n5. **Nothing is platform-specific.** The same mechanism imports CosmoOS's `struct cosmoboot_info`, Linux's\n   `struct file`, or the C library's `struct stat`, each from that platform's headers under that\n   platform's flags. A profile only decides which headers and flags a build uses.\n6. **`@repr(C)` mirrors stay** for types the reference owns and exports (type-system",
    146 =>
        " §28.5), where the reference *defines*\n   the layout and C code includes `ceesafe_exports.h`.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `profiles.md` §3.2, `cosmoos/abi.md` and `risks.md` P-3 change accordingly; the research audit\n  (`research/cosmoos-kernel-audit.md`, implication 26) is left as written, since this satisfies it: types\n  still come from the headers of the exact ABI.\n- The interpreter cannot run code that touches an imported type's fields (it has no layout); such code is\n  an interpreter fault, as C calls are (type-system §28.6).\n- Field types must be types C and the reference both spell (integers, `bool`, raw pointers, imported types, and\n  `@repr(C)` structs); C arrays, bit-fields, unions and anonymous members need later additions.\n- It is specified in `type-system.md` §31 and implemented with the first component that needs it (C6b,\n  2026-10-24). The first implementation leaves out embedded imported structs (a field whose type is\n  an imported type itself): fields are integers, `bool` and raw pointers. Embedded members are a later\n  addition, with arrays and the rest above.\n",
    147 =>
        "# ADR 0009 — C7: context effects, and C state embedded by value\n\n> **Inherited.** ADR 0009 was accepted before CeeSafe's fork (Decision 0006), when the language had its\n> earlier name. In this record, \"the reference\" is that language and project and \"the reference compiler\" its compiler:\n> read them as CeeSafe and `ceesafec`. Links to documents CeeSafe did not keep are given as text.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-24, choosing \"embed by value\" for kernel locks and objects\n  over out-of-line C state). The rest is the agent's design under that choice, following\n  `cosmoos/kernel-memory-and-lifetimes.md` §4–§7; the owner may revise it.\n- Scope: C7 (`cosmoos/c7-report.md`). It amends ADR 0008 point 4 (imported types only behind pointers).\n\n## Context\n\nC7 wraps CosmoOS's locks, kernel objects and quiescence (audit in `cosmoos/c7-report.md`). Three facts from\nthe code decide the design:\n\n1. The kernel's synchronisation objects are C state that lives **inside** the data it protects. `spinlock_t` is 24\n   bytes (`kernel/include/kernel/spinlock.h:31-37`). `struct mutex` embeds a spinlock, a wait queue and list\n   links (`mutex.h:20-35`). A `struct kobject` is embedded in its owner and found again with `container_of`.\n2. Once initialised, some of this state **points into itself**. `mutex_init` runs `list_init`, which sets\n   `head->next = head` (`kernel/scheduler/mutex.c:21-22`, `list.h:24-28`). Moving such a value bitwise\n   corrupts it.\n3. Atomic versus sleepable is a runtime property: `might_sleep()` (`lockdep.h:239`) panics on sleeping with\n   preemption disabled. Nothing checks it statically, and nothing checks at all that a final `kobject_put`,\n   whose release may block, is not made under a spinlock (`object.c:74-81` has no `might_sleep`).\n\n## Decision\n\n### 1. Context effects (C7a)\n- Two effects: **`sleep`** (may block) and **`alloc`** (may allocate). A function's effects are those of\n  everything it calls and every value it drops, inferred over the whole package. Built-in operations\n  declare theirs (heap allocation is `alloc`; hosted I/O is `sleep`).\n- `@effects(sleep, alloc)` (any subset; `@effects()` for none) **declares** them: on an `extern` function it\n  is what the reference assumes, and on a CeeSafe function it is an upper bound that the inferred set must fit. An extern\n  function without `@effects` is assumed to have both, the most conservative choice. A call through a C\n  function pointer has both.\n- `@context(atomic)` (no `sleep`) and `@context(irq)` (no `sleep`, no `alloc`; KQ-1's the reference default) on a\n  function forbid effects in its body. On a **struct**, `@context(atomic)` makes every point where a value\n  of it is live (maybe initialised and not yet dropped) atomic: holding a spin-lock guard is an atomic\n  context, with no annotation at the use.\n- Effects are checked in **every profile**. They create obligations only where a context or an `@effects`\n  bound is declared, so hosted programs without them are unaffected.\n- The checker proves only that CeeSafe code respects the declared effects of what it calls. CosmoOS's runtime\n  checks (`might_sleep`, lockdep) stay the backstop. The reference generates no `might_sleep()` calls: the debug half of\n  the macro calls `lockdep_might_sleep`, which modules cannot link to (it is not exported), and every sleeping\n  primitive the reference calls has its own.\n\n### 2. C state embedded by value (C7b)\n- A CeeSafe struct may hold an imported C type by value, and so may an imported struct (an embedded member).\n  Such a type, and every type that contains one by value, is **C-laid-out**. Like an imported type, it has no\n  The reference layout. Generated C defines it with the real C member types, so the C compiler lays it out, and it\n  gets no layout assertions. The interpreter faults on it.\n- C-laid-out values are **immovable**. They are made **in place** and never moved:\n  - the only way to make one is a struct literal given directly to `Box.createIn` (",
    148 =>
        "or nested in such a\n    literal);\n  - imported fields are left out of the literal and **zero-filled** (C's `{0}`), which is the only imported\n    value the reference can make;\n  - the contents of such a box are never moved out;\n  - a C-laid-out type cannot be a local, parameter, result, array element, enum payload, optional or\n    type argument (a reference to one can be).\n  `static` items come later (§4).\n- **`@placed` methods** (`fn m(borrow mut this)`) run C's initialisers at the final address. After\n  `Box.createIn` places the value, the compiler calls the `@placed` methods of its fields' types, innermost\n  first in field order, then the type's own. They run before anything else can borrow the value, so code\n  never sees uninitialised C state. A lock's `@placed` method calls `spinlock_init` with its name.\n- `Ptr.containerOf` (unsafe) recovers a struct from a pointer to one of its fields, using C's `offsetof`.\n  Kernel callbacks need it, since they receive the embedded `struct kobject *` or `struct quiesce_head *`.\n  (Implementation note, C7b: it moved to C7d, whose release and quiescence callbacks are generated by the\n  compiler and are its only users so far.)\n\n### 3. CosmoOS bindings (C7c–C7e, `integrations/cosmoos/c7/`)\n- `SpinLock<T>`, `IrqSpinLock<T>` and `Mutex<T>` hold their C lock and their data. `lock()` returns a guard\n  that borrows the lock and gives `borrow mut T` access. Spin guards are `@context(atomic)`. `Mutex.lock` is\n  `sleep`. Dropping a guard unlocks. Data is reachable only through a guard.\n- `KRef<T>`, a counted reference to a reference-defined kernel object (it embeds `kobject`). Its drop is\n  `kobject_put`, with the `sleep` effect unless the type is `@nonblockingRelease`. A final put under a spin\n  guard is then a compile error, the hole of fact 3.\n- `Quiesce.synchronize()` (`sleep`). `Quiesce.deferDrop(box)` uses `call_quiesce` with an embedded\n  `quiesce_head`. It is `unsafe` in modules, because module unload does not wait for pending callbacks\n  (`module.c:627-631`; KQ-3). Its contract is that the module stays loaded until the callback has run.\n- Not in C7: `QuiesceGuard` read sections in modules (`quiesce_read_unlock` inlines the unexported\n  `preempt_enable`, `quiesce.h`; KQ-2; spin guards are read sections already), `PerCpu<T>` (CosmoOS exports\n  no general per-CPU allocation), and statics (§4).\n\n### 4. Deferred\n`static` items (locks and caches with a lifetime of the module), and with them `ObjectCache<T>` (C5). Lock-order\nchecking stays lockdep's job.\n\n## Consequences\n- ADR 0008 point 4 now reads: imported types appear by value only in C-laid-out types, which are immovable and\n  made in place. Everywhere else they appear behind pointers and references.\n- `type-system.md` gains §32 (effects) and §33 (embedded C state). `profiles.md` §1 changes the effects row to\n  checked everywhere. `kernel-memory-and-lifetimes.md` §5–§6 point to them.\n- The proposals of `kernel-memory-and-lifetimes.md` that C7 leaves out stay proposals: `PerCpu`, statics,\n  `Unlinked<T>`, `@scopedOnly`, and the irq-state ordering of nested `IrqSpinGuard`s beyond `using`'s LIFO.\n",
    149 =>
        "# ADR 0010 — M9c: writes through pointers made from references end with the loan\n\n> **Inherited.** ADR 0010 was accepted before CeeSafe's fork (Decision 0006), when the language had its\n> earlier name. In this record, \"the reference\" is that language and project and \"the reference compiler\" its compiler:\n> read them as CeeSafe and `ceesafec`. Links to documents CeeSafe did not keep are given as text.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-25, choosing to add the rule so that bounds checks on vectors can be\n  eliminated, over limiting M9c to arrays and slices). The wording is the agent's; the owner may revise it.\n- Scope: M9c (`milestone-9.md`, `optimisations.md` §2). It amends `unsafe.md` §2 (the U2 contract).\n\n## Context\n\nM9c removes bounds checks that an earlier check or a loop's condition already proves (`optimisations.md` §2). The\nproof is about values held in a function's locals: an index, a slice's length, a vector's length. It stays true\nuntil the local changes, and the analysis has to see every change.\n\nA local changes through an assignment, which the analysis sees, or through a reference to it. A slice or scalar\nlocal whose address is never taken cannot change any other way, and needs no new rule. A vector is different:\n`push`, `pop` and `clear` take `borrow mut` of it, so every vector has its address taken. The analysis can still\nfollow it, because a mutable loan is the only way to change the vector's length, and while that loan is live the\nborrow checker rejects every other use of the vector (B1–B2). So a length read directly from the vector stays\nvalid until the next `borrow mut` of it.\n\nThat argument fails if the vector can be changed with no live loan. Today's U2 contract allows that: a pointer made\nfrom a reference (`(borrow mut v) as *mut Vector<u8>`; casts are safe, `type-system.md` §27.2) can be kept, by this\nfunction or another, and used later through `Ptr.toBorrowMut(p)` in an `unsafe` block, after the loan it came from\nhas ended. The write is valid under §2 as written: the storage is live and nothing borrows it at that moment.\n\n## Decision\n\n1. Add a rule to U2 (`unsafe.md` §2): a pointer made from a reference may be used to **write** its target, directly\n   or through a reference made from it, only while the reference's loan could still be live. That is, only before the\n   target is next used by anything other than that pointer: read, written, borrowed, moved, or its storage ended.\n   Pointers that did not come from a reference are not affected: those from the C side, `Slice.fromRaw` of foreign\n   memory, MMIO addresses.\n2. This is the rule of Rust's raw pointers derived from `&mut` (inferred: Stacked Borrows makes such a later write\n   undefined), reduced to writes, which is all M9c relies on.\n3. The interpreter does not track loans (`unsafe.md` §5), so the rule is not checked, like the rest of U2's\n   \"as observed by safe code\".\n4. Unsafe code in this repository was audited for writes that the rule forbids (`optimisations.md` §2.6).\n\n## Consequences\n\n- M9c may treat a local's value, and a vector local's length, as fixed between two mutable loans of it. So it removes\n  `v[i]` checks in loops, and keeps facts about an index that is also passed as `mut i` to a helper (LZ4's\n  decompressor).\n- Unsafe code that kept a pointer to a local's storage and wrote through it after using the local directly becomes\n  invalid. The safe pattern is unchanged: use the pointer within the loan, or keep the data behind the pointer (a\n  raw buffer, a box made into a raw pointer) rather than in a local.\n",
    150 =>
        "# ADR 0011 — M11 G7: floating-point types\n\n> **Inherited.** ADR 0011 was accepted before CeeSafe's fork (Decision 0006), when the language had its\n> earlier name. In this record, \"the reference\" is that language and project and \"the reference compiler\" its compiler:\n> read them as CeeSafe and `ceesafec`. Links to documents CeeSafe did not keep are given as text.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-25, taking every recommendation in §5). The wording is the agent's;\n  the owner may revise it. Not yet implemented: the decisions are written into `type-system.md` (a new §35),\n  `grammar.md`, `c-backend.md` and `profiles.md` before any code (§6).\n- Scope: M11 G7 (`milestone-11.md`), closing OD-23 (`open-decisions.md`). It amends `type-system.md` §1 (N0216 for\n  floats), §5 (flexible literals) and §7 (conversions).\n\n## 1. Context\n\nFacts this design rests on, from the code and the docs as they are today:\n\n- **What already exists.** The lexer reads float literals with `f32`/`f64` suffixes (`Syntax/Lexer.cs:236-239`,\n  `grammar.md` §1: `1.` and `.5` are not floats). The checker rejects them: \"floating-point numbers are not supported\n  in M1\" (N0216, `TypeChecker.cs` `Synth`, `FloatLiteralExpr`), and `f64` as a type is unknown (N0204). `language-spec.md` §2 and Ex2/Ex13 already use\n  `f64` (IEEE 754, C `float`/`double`), and `c-backend.md`'s type table maps `f32`/`f64` to `float`/`double`.\n- **Profiles.** `profiles.md` §1: floats are *yes* in hosted and CosmoOS user programs, **forbidden** in the\n  CosmoOS kernel, and *target-defined* in the generic freestanding profile. CosmoOS builds its kernel and loader with\n  `-mgeneral-regs-only` on both arches (CosmoOS `build/arch/x86_64.mk:6,14`, `aarch64.mk:19,28`), and\n  `scripts/check-fpregs.sh` rejects objects that touch FP registers. The reference runs that script in `c4.py` and `c5.py`\n  (`c4.py:204-206`, `c5.py:104`); `c6.py` and `c7.py` reuse C4's other checks but not this one.\n- **No libm, no exact libc formatting.** CosmoOS's libc has no `<math.h>` (`libc/include/`), and its `printf`\n  float conversions are \"deliberately small … not exactly rounded in the last digit for every input\"\n  (`libc/src/printf.c:9-14`). Formatting therefore cannot go through libc if output is to be deterministic and equal\n  to the interpreter's.\n- **No compiler-rt** (`c-backend.md` §1). On the two 64-bit targets the reference supports, float arithmetic and\n  `int64 ↔ double` conversions compile to instructions. `fmod`, `sqrt` and the like are libm calls unless they become\n  builtins that lower to an instruction.\n- **The interpreter is an oracle** (M7c). Every program must print the same output compiled and interpreted, and the\n  fuzzer compares them (`tests/Reference.Fuzz`). .NET's `double` and `float` are IEEE binary64/binary32 with\n  round-to-nearest-even. The JIT does not fuse `a * b + c` into an FMA unless asked (`Math.FusedMultiplyAdd`)\n  (inferred, to verify in G7).\n- **C compilers may contract.** clang contracts `a * b + c` into an FMA within an expression by default\n  (`-ffp-contract=on`). gcc does in GNU modes (`-ffp-contract=fast`), but not under `-std=c11`. An FMA rounds once\n  instead of twice, so the C result could differ from the interpreter's and between compilers (inferred from\n  compiler documentation, to verify in G7).\n- **Undefined behaviour.** In C, converting a float to an integer type that cannot hold its truncated value is\n  undefined (C11 6.3.1.4). Generated C must have none (`c-backend.md`).\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. IEEE floats, deterministic (proposed) | B. Floats as in C | C. No floats; fixed-point library |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Semantics | IEEE 754 binary32/64, round-to-nearest-even, no contraction, no traps; same result compiled and interpreted | whatever the C compiler does (contraction, `-ffast-math` allowed) | integers only |\n| Float → int | checked: panics on NaN and out of range | C's cast (UB out of range) | — |\n| Printing | The reference's own sho",
    151 =>
        "rtest round-trip formatter | libc `printf(\"%g\")` | — |\n| Cost | a formatter in the runtime (~1–2k lines with tables, inferred) | small | a library |\n| Safety | no UB; deterministic | UB on conversion; results vary by compiler | — |\n\nB breaks two rules (no UB; the interpreter as oracle). C leaves Ex2/Ex13 and every numeric program out. **A is\nproposed.**\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n1. **Types.** `f32` and `f64` are IEEE 754 binary32 and binary64, laid out per `TargetInfo` (4/4 and 8/8 on both\n   targets). They are Copy, need no drop, hold no loans, and are FFI-safe (C `float`/`double`). The FP environment is\n   the default one, never changed by CeeSafe code: round to nearest even, no traps, and no flags read.\n2. **Literals.** `1.5`, `1e9`, `2.5e-3`, with an optional `f32`/`f64` suffix. An unsuffixed float literal is\n   *flexible* like an integer one (§5): it takes an expected `f32` or `f64`, and defaults to `f64`. The literal's\n   value is the decimal rounded to nearest (even) in its type, exactly once: `0.1f32` is the `f32` nearest 0.1, not the\n   `f64` nearest 0.1 rounded again. An integer literal is not a float (`let x: f64 = 1;` is N0205; write `1.0`), as\n   The reference has no implicit conversions. A literal that rounds to infinity is an error (N0206).\n3. **Operators.** `+ - * /` and unary `-` on two operands of the same float type give that type. They **never\n   panic**: overflow gives ±infinity, `0.0 / 0.0` gives NaN, as IEEE says. This is the one exception to \"arithmetic\n   panics on failure\" (OD-6), and the spec states it. `%` is not defined on floats in G7 (N0207), since C's `fmod`\n   is a libm call. Comparisons `== != < <= > >=` are IEEE's: NaN is unequal to everything, itself included, and\n   `-0.0 == 0.0`. Bitwise and shift operators are N0207.\n4. **No contraction, no excess precision.** Each operation rounds to its type. The generated C enforces this:\n   - `rt.h` checks `FLT_EVAL_METHOD == 0` (`<float.h>` is a freestanding header) and emits\n     `#pragma STDC FP_CONTRACT OFF`;\n   - The reference compiler's builds pass `-ffp-contract=off`, and `c-backend.md`'s checklist requires it of integrations, since\n     gcc ignores the pragma;\n   - `-ffast-math` and its parts are unsupported, as `-fwrapv` already is not relied on.\n\n   The interpreter computes each operation in .NET's `float` or `double`. Constant folding in the compiler uses the\n   same .NET arithmetic.\n5. **Conversions** (`type-system.md` §7).\n   - `as` stays lossless only: `f32 as f64`; `i8`, `i16`, `u8` and `u16 as f32`; and `i8`–`i32`, `u8`–`u32 as f64`.\n     Every value of the source type is exactly representable in the target type.\n   - `round<T>(e)` (new, free-standing like `truncate`): any integer or float to a float `T`, **rounded to nearest\n     even**. It is always defined: `round<f64>(x)` for `x: i64`, `round<f32>(d)` for `d: f64` (overflow gives\n     infinity).\n   - `truncate<T>(e)` extended: a float to an integer `T`, rounding **toward zero**. It **panics** if `e` is NaN or the\n     truncated value does not fit in `T` (\"float out of range\", a new panic kind). The C code compares `e` against the\n     exactly representable bounds before converting, so there is no UB.\n   - `checkedTruncate<T>(e)` returns `T?` instead of panicking. `saturatingTruncate<T>(e)` clamps, with NaN giving 0,\n     as Rust's `as` does.\n   - `f.bits()` and `f64.fromBits(u)` (`f32` likewise) reinterpret the representation, for hashing and serialising.\n     Literals are emitted to C by their bits, so their values never depend on the C compiler's decimal parsing.\n6. **Printing.** `Console.write`, `writeLine` and `String.appendNumber` accept `f32` and `f64`. The text is the\n   **shortest decimal that rounds back to the same value**, laid out as ECMAScript's `Number::toString` (ECMA-262\n   §6.1.6.1.20):\n   - plain digits for 1e-6 ≤ |x| < 1e21 (`0.1`, `1.5`, `100`, `123456.789`);\n   - an exponent otherwise (`1e21`, `1.5e-7`);\n   - `NaN`, `Infinity` and `-Infinity`, a",
    152 =>
        "nd `0` for both zeros.\n\n   ECMAScript's rule is fully specified and widely implemented, so test vectors exist. The C runtime implements\n   it with its own shortest-digits algorithm (Ryu or Schubfach, §5 Q4), using 64×64→128-bit multiplication in\n   portable 32-bit halves (no `__int128`, which MSVC lacks). The interpreter gets the shortest digits from .NET's\n   `ToString(\"R\")` (shortest round-trip since .NET Core 3.0) and lays them out by the same rule.\n7. **Profiles.** Floats are allowed in hosted programs and CosmoOS user programs. In the freestanding profile, a\n   build option `--no-float` (CosmoOS kernel builds pass it, from `c4.py`–`c8.py`) makes any float type, literal or\n   operation an error, with a new code. `check-fpregs.sh` stays the backstop, and G7 adds it to `c6.py`–`c8.py`. Without the option, freestanding code\n   may use floats, as `profiles.md` §1's \"target-defined\" says. Printing floats is hosted-only (it uses the console).\n8. **Not in G7.** `%`, a math library (`sqrt`, `floor`, `sin`, …), parsing floats from text, `f16`/`f128`,\n   SIMD, rounding modes and FP exceptions, floats as `match` patterns, and floats in `const` expressions beyond\n   literals and the four operators. The first two come next (§5 Q5).\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- Ex2 and Ex13 of `language-spec.md` become runnable, and numeric code can be written. The README's \"What the reference does\n  not have yet\" loses \"floating-point types\".\n- A float result is the same compiled with clang, gcc or MSVC and interpreted, so the fuzzer can generate float code\n  and compare outputs. The cost is the forbidden contraction: FMA-heavy kernels are slower than C compiled with\n  contraction on. A future explicit `f.mulAdd(a, b)` (an FMA builtin) would restore it where wanted. Any performance\n  claim needs a benchmark first (a float workload in `benchmarks/suite`, e.g. n-body).\n- The C runtime grows by a formatter and its tables (inferred: about 10 KiB of tables for Ryu's `double` path; less\n  for Schubfach). It is linked only into programs that print floats.\n- `truncate` on floats can panic, unlike `truncate` on integers. The name says what happens to the fraction, and the\n  panic is the out-of-range case. Q3 offers a different name.\n- The CosmoOS kernel profile is unchanged in effect: floats were forbidden, and stay so through `--no-float`.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner (decided 2026-10-25: each as recommended)\n\n1. **Approach A** (IEEE, deterministic, no contraction)? Recommended: yes.\n2. **Never-panicking float arithmetic** (infinity and NaN) as an explicit exception to OD-6? Recommended: yes. The\n   alternative, panicking on non-finite results, is expensive (a check after every operation) and unlike every\n   other language.\n3. **Conversion names.** `round<T>` for to-float, and `truncate<T>` (panicking), `checkedTruncate<T>` and\n   `saturatingTruncate<T>` for to-integer? Alternative: Rust's saturating `as` for float → int. Recommended: the\n   proposal, since an unexpected NaN or huge value then fails loudly.\n4. **Formatter.** A port of Ryu (Apache-2.0 or Boost licence; its licence would sit in `runtime/`), or a new\n   implementation of Schubfach from its paper (no licence question; more of our own code to test)? Recommended:\n   Schubfach, tested against .NET's shortest output on several million random and boundary values.\n5. **Math library scope after G7.** Recommended: next, `sqrt`, `abs`, `floor`, `ceil`, `min` and `max`, which lower\n   to single instructions on both targets (`__builtin_sqrt` with `-fno-math-errno`, `__builtin_floor`, …). Without\n   libm, everything else would be a reference implementation with its own accuracy tests.\n6. **`--no-float`** as an option rather than a separate profile? Recommended: an option. Profiles stay as\n   `profiles.md` defines them, and a Linux kernel build would use the same option.\n\n## 5a. Amendments during implementation (2026-10-25)\n\n- **G7a.** `rt.h`'s IEEE checks, contraction pragmas and float helpers app",
    153 =>
        "ly only in programs that use floats\n  (`CEE_FLOATS`).\n  - Without that guard, the check `FLT_EVAL_METHOD == 0` broke every CosmoOS kernel object on x86-64.\n  - CosmoOS builds those objects with `-mgeneral-regs-only`, which leaves no SSE there (`c4.py`).\n- **G7b.**\n  - **Exponent sign.** §3.6's examples wrote `1e21`. ECMAScript writes `1e+21`, and the reference follows ECMAScript exactly,\n    so its test vectors apply (type-system.md §35.7).\n  - **The interpreter's digits** come from an exact search over rationals, not from .NET's `ToString(\"R\")`. .NET 10\n    prints 2^-25 as `2.980232238769531E-08`, which does not read back as 2^-25.\n  - **One-digit results.** Schubfach is used without the Java code's two-digit minimum, so `5e-324` prints as\n    `5e-324`.\n\n## 6. Implementation plan (once accepted)\n\nSpec first (`type-system.md` §35, `grammar.md`, `c-backend.md`, `profiles.md`), then:\n\n- **G7a.** Types, literals, operators, comparisons, `as`, `round`, `truncate` and its variants, `bits`/`fromBits`, C\n  emission and the interpreter.\n- **G7b.** The formatter and printing, in the C runtime and the interpreter.\n- **G7c.** `--no-float`, the fuzzer generating float expressions, an MSVC run, and a float benchmark.\n\nTests to add (none run yet):\n\n- run-pass for every operator at ±0, ±infinity, NaN, subnormals and the boundaries of each conversion;\n- run-fail for `truncate` of NaN and out-of-range values;\n- check-fail for mixed types, `%`, bitwise operators and integer literals where a float is expected;\n- a formatter test against .NET on random bits and on ECMAScript's test cases;\n- C output compiled with clang, gcc and MSVC, and compared with the interpreter.\n",
    154 =>
        "# ADR 0012 — M11 G8: function values and scoped closures\n\n> **Inherited.** ADR 0012 was accepted before CeeSafe's fork (Decision 0006), when the language had its\n> earlier name. In this record, \"the reference\" is that language and project and \"the reference compiler\" its compiler:\n> read them as CeeSafe and `ceesafec`. Links to documents CeeSafe did not keep are given as text.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-25, taking every recommendation in §5; OD-9a and OD-9b record the\n  deferred options). The wording is the agent's; the owner may revise it. Not yet implemented: the decisions are\n  written into `type-system.md` (a new §36), `grammar.md`, `lifetimes.md`, `intermediate-representation.md` and\n  `c-backend.md` before any code.\n- Scope: M11 G8 (`milestone-11.md`), closing OD-9 (`open-decisions.md`: \"Non-capturing `fn` pointers only. Scoped\n  capturing closures in M6+\"). It amends `type-system.md` §8 (\"Functions are not values in M1\") and §29.5.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n- **No CeeSafe function values today.** A callee must be a package function or a built-in (`type-system.md` §8). The\n  only function-typed values are C function pointers, `extern \"C\" fn(…) -> R` (§29.5). Calls through them are\n  unsafe and limited to FFI-safe types. `@callback` gives a CeeSafe function a C address, with the same limits (§34.1).\n  Probing `fn` in an argument position gives `expected ')', found ':'` (N0100) (`milestone-11.md`, G8).\n- **No dynamic dispatch.** Interfaces are bounds, not types (§21; `dyn` is OD-10). Generics are monomorphised and\n  \"checked once, generically\" (§20).\n- **Loans already flow through values.** A scoped value carries the loans it was made from: references, views,\n  slices, and since M6x aggregates with region parameters (§25, ADR 0006). The NIR borrow checker propagates them\n  through moves, copies and calls, and a signature summarises a callee completely (OD-3 goal 3).\n- **Kernel constraints.** The kernel profile has no implicit allocation and a 2 KiB frame limit\n  (`profiles.md` §1, §3). CosmoOS kernel APIs take callbacks as a C function plus a `void *` argument, for\n  example the thread and timer bindings in `integrations/cosmoos/c8/`.\n- **Effects.** A call through a C function pointer is assumed to have both effects, `sleep` and `alloc` (§32.1).\n\nWhat programs need, from M9's suite and the README tour: comparators for sorting, `forEach`/`map`/`filter`-style\nhelpers over slices and vectors, retain-if on vectors, visitors over trees, and callbacks that capture a little local\nstate (a counter, a threshold). None of these needs the closure to outlive the function that made it.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. Scoped closures with one type per signature (proposed) | B. Monomorphised closures with an `Fn` interface | C. Owning, heap-allocated closures |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A closure value is | `{ code, env }`, with `env` pointing at the captures in the maker's frame | an anonymous struct, one type per literal | a box holding the captures, with a vtable |\n| Captures | by reference; the value carries their loans | by reference or by move | by move |\n| Can outlive its maker | no (scoped, like a reference) | by move, through generics only | yes |\n| Call | indirect | direct, inlinable | indirect |\n| Needs | function types, a literal syntax | anonymous types, an `Fn` bound with function-type syntax, inference of closure types in generics | `dyn` (OD-10), allocation (not in the kernel) |\n| Fits the kernel | yes (no allocation, a small env) | yes | no |\n\nA covers every use in §1 with the least new machinery: the closure is a scoped value, and scoped values are already\nchecked. B is faster at a call (inlining), but every function that takes a closure becomes generic, and a field\nholding one needs a type parameter. C needs `dyn` and the heap. **A is proposed now. B and C stay open (§5 Q5)**,\nand A does not block them.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n1. **Function types.** `fn(T1, …, Tn) -> R` is the type of a **plain funct",
    155 =>
        "ion value**: one code address, Copy, no\n   loans, and not FFI-safe. The name of any package function (generic ones with type arguments) is a value of that\n   type where one is expected, and so is a closure literal that captures nothing. `f(args)` calls it, safely. It is\n   distinct from `extern \"C\" fn(…)` (§29.5), which stays the FFI type.\n2. **Closure types.** `borrow fn(T…) -> R` and `borrow mut fn(T…) -> R` are **closures**. The value is a code address\n   and a reference to an environment. As with references (§13), the shared form is Copy and is called through a shared\n   loan of the environment. The `mut` form is unique: each call reborrows it, and it may change what it captured. A\n   plain `fn(…) -> R` converts implicitly to either closure type. Closure types are scoped (§19.1): they live within\n   the loans of their captures, follow the reference rules for where they may appear (§13.1: parameters, `let`\n   locals, results through a signature), and may be fields of aggregates with region parameters (§25).\n   `borrow(r) fn(…) -> R` names the region.\n3. **Literals.** TypeScript-style arrow functions:\n\n   ```ceesafe\n   (x: i32, y: i32) => x + y\n   (x) => x > limit\n   (item) => { total += item; }\n   ```\n\n   - The parameter types may be left out where a function or closure type is expected, and are taken from it.\n   - The body is an expression, or a block with `return`. It may not use `break` or `continue` out of the closure,\n     nor `?` and `return` for the enclosing function: `return` returns from the closure.\n   - The parser reads `(` … `)` `=>` as a closure when the parentheses hold only names, each with an optional `: T`.\n     A parenthesised expression cannot be followed by `=>`, so the two cannot be confused.\n4. **Captures.** A literal captures the enclosing locals (and parameters) that it names. Each is captured **by\n   reference**: shared if the body only reads it, mutable if the body assigns to it or borrows it mutably. Any\n   mutable capture makes the literal a `borrow mut fn`, so assigning it to a `borrow fn` is an error (a new code).\n   Copy captures that are only read may be copied into the environment instead, as an optimisation. The borrow checker\n   cannot tell the difference, since nothing may write them while the closure lives. A closure value carries the\n   loans of all its captures, so the existing rules (B1–B5) forbid, for example, changing `total` while a closure that\n   reads it is live. Moving a capture out (`take(x)` inside the body) is an error (a new code). Owning captures are\n   option B or C.\n5. **Environment.** The captures live in a hidden local of the block that holds the literal, not in a statement\n   temporary, so `let f = (x) => x + k;` works. The environment is a C struct of pointers (and copied values) in the\n   maker's frame, so there is no allocation, and its size counts against the kernel profile's frame limit.\n6. **C representation.**\n   - A plain function value is a function pointer, `R (*)(A…)`.\n   - A closure is `struct { R (*code)(void *env, A…); void *env; }`, with one `static` function per literal.\n   - A plain function converted to a closure gets a small generated adapter, which ignores `env`.\n   - Calls are indirect calls, which a C compiler may inline when it sees the target (inferred).\n   - No casts between function pointer types are needed, so there is no UB from calling through a mismatched type.\n7. **Effects** (§32). A call through a function or closure value has the effects of its type. A function or closure\n   type may carry an effect list, `@effects() fn(i32) -> bool`, which every value assigned to it must satisfy (the\n   checker knows a literal's effects, and a named function's). Without the list, a call is assumed to `sleep` and\n   `alloc`, as for C function pointers. Code in `@context(atomic)` therefore calls only closures whose type declares\n   no `sleep`.\n8. **Interpreter and NIR.** NIR gains a function-address operand (it exists for C poin",
    156 =>
        "ters), a closure aggregate\n   `{ code, env }`, and an indirect call. Captures lower to references to the captured places, so moves, drops and\n   loans need no new analysis, only the closure value's loan set. The interpreter executes closure bodies as NIR\n   functions with an environment argument.\n9. **Library.** With closures in place, these may follow in a separate increment, each spec-first:\n   - `Slice.sortBy(cmp: borrow fn(borrow T, borrow T) -> i32)`, implemented in the reference over the slice;\n   - `forEach`, `any` and `all` on slices and vectors;\n   - `Vector.retain(keep: borrow mut fn(borrow T) -> bool)`.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- Callbacks, comparators and small visitors are written directly, with checked captures, and without `unsafe` or\n  `@callback` plumbing. The C-callback pattern (`fn` plus `void *`) also gets a safe the reference form: a later binding may\n  pass a closure's `code` and `env` to C APIs that take exactly that pair (to be designed with the kernel bindings).\n- A closure cannot be returned to a caller unless its captures come from the caller (named regions), nor stored past\n  its captures, nor sent to another thread. Those need owning captures (B or C).\n- Calls through closures are indirect. Hot loops may still prefer a generic function with an interface bound, which\n  is monomorphised. No performance claim is made without a benchmark; a comparator sort against C's `qsort` would\n  be the first.\n- The parser gains one lookahead (arrow functions), and type syntax gains `fn(…) -> R`, also inside `borrow`.\n  `borrow fn(…)` contains no `(` right after `borrow`, so it does not touch G6's source-list rule.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner (decided 2026-10-25: each as recommended)\n\n1. **Approach A**, scoped closures with one type per signature, now? Recommended: yes.\n2. **Literal syntax:** TypeScript/C# arrows `(x) => x + 1`, or a keyword form `fn(x) => x + 1`? Recommended: arrows,\n   matching the language's TypeScript/C# style. `=>` already marks expression bodies in the reference (`fn f() => e`) and in\n   match arms.\n3. **Capture mode inference:** shared or mutable per variable, inferred from the body, as Swift and Rust do? Or\n   explicit capture lists (`mut total => …`, C++-style)? Recommended: inferred. The borrow checker reports any\n   conflict at the use that causes it, and both kinds of closure type are visible in signatures.\n4. **Effects on function types** (§3.7) in G8, or calls through closures always assumed to `sleep` and `alloc` until\n   later? Recommended: in G8. Without it, kernel code in atomic contexts cannot call closures at all.\n5. **Owning closures later:** B (monomorphised, `F: Fn(i32) -> i32` bounds) when performance needs it, and C only\n   with `dyn` (OD-10)? Recommended: record both as open decisions (OD-9a, OD-9b) and decide when a use appears.\n\n## 5a. Amendments during implementation (2026-10-26)\n\n- **One pointer, not two** (§3.6). A closure's value points at its environment, whose first member is the code\n  pointer (C11 6.7.2.1p15). So `borrow fn(…)` is an ordinary reference type, whose pointee is the callable object, and\n  every reference rule applies unchanged (type-system.md §36.2). A reference to a plain function value is a different\n  type, so the two representations cannot be confused.\n- **Closures never leave their block** (§4 said a closure could be returned when its captures come from the caller).\n  The environment is a local of the block that makes the closure, so returning it is N0244 even then. Returning\n  closures needs owning captures: OD-9a or OD-9b.\n- **A moved capture** is N0251 (a move out of a reference), not a new code.\n- **Effect lists** (§3.7) come in their own step after closures (G8b′, type-system.md §36.3), with these choices:\n  - the list goes before `fn`, also inside `borrow` (`borrow @effects() fn(i32)`), since it describes the callable;\n  - inference does not narrow a value's type: a named function or literal gets the type without a list unle",
    157 =>
        "ss one\n    is expected;\n  - a value whose type's list is too wide is N0299 (the wrong kind of value), not a new code. A named function's own\n    effects, known only after inference, are N0295 where it is named.\n- **The library** (§3.9, G8c, type-system.md §36.4):\n  - every method's closure is a `borrow mut fn`, `sortBy`'s too (§3.9 proposed `borrow fn`): a shared closure converts\n    to it, and a comparator may count its calls;\n  - `sortBy` is an unstable introsort, which needs no allocation and runs on move-type elements through a new\n    `swap`;\n  - `retain` is lib/core code over the elements followed by a new `truncate`, since generic code cannot name a\n    `Vector<T, A>` over an unknown allocator;\n  - the methods have the effects of a closure without an effect list. Taking a closure argument's effects instead is\n    OD-9c.\n\n## 6. Implementation plan (once accepted)\n\nSpec first (`type-system.md` §36, `grammar.md`, `lifetimes.md` for closure loans, `intermediate-representation.md`,\n`c-backend.md`), then:\n\n- **G8a.** Plain function values: types, named functions as values, calls, C emission and the interpreter.\n- **G8b.** Closure literals, captures and loans, environments, `borrow fn`/`borrow mut fn`, and effects on function\n  types.\n- **G8c.** `sortBy`, `forEach`/`any`/`all` and `retain`, a sort benchmark against `qsort`, and the fuzzer generating\n  closures.\n\nTests to add (none run yet):\n\n- run-pass: captures read and written, a closure passed down two calls, a generic higher-order function, a closure\n  in a struct with a region parameter, effects, compiled and interpreted;\n- check-fail:\n  - a write to a captured variable while a closure that reads it is live;\n  - a `mut` closure where a shared one is expected;\n  - a closure escaping its captures (returned, stored in a box);\n  - a moved capture;\n  - `break` out of a closure;\n  - an atomic context calling a closure without `@effects()`;\n- M2's mutation suite extended with closure loans.\n",
    158 =>
        "# ADR 0013 — M10 T5–T6: modules and packages\n\n> **Inherited.** ADR 0013 was accepted before CeeSafe's fork (Decision 0006), when the language had its\n> earlier name. In this record, \"the reference\" is that language and project and \"the reference compiler\" its compiler:\n> read them as CeeSafe and `ceesafec`. Links to documents CeeSafe did not keep are given as text.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-26). The owner took every recommendation of §4: package-level names\n  (option A) with `namespace` retired, `src/` as the default sources, git `rev`, `tag` or `branch` pinned in the lock,\n  and dependencies' C names prefixed with their package's name. The wording is the agent's; the owner may revise it.\n  Not yet implemented: the decisions go into `language-spec.md` §5, `type-system.md` §1 (scopes), `grammar.md`,\n  `c-backend.md` §2.1 and `tooling.md` (a new §4) before any code.\n- Scope: M10 T5 (modules) and T6 (packages and building), `milestone-10.md`. It settles OD-11 (`open-decisions.md`:\n  \"`ceesafe.toml` with name, version, profile, dependencies (path-only in v0)\"). The owner has already chosen git\n  dependencies as well as path ones, with a lock file (2026-10-26).\n\n## 1. Context\n\n- **One compilation is one package.** All its files share one item scope; names are simple. `namespace X;` is parsed\n  and recorded but qualifies nothing, and two items of the same name are N0201 even in different namespaces\n  (`type-system.md` §1). `import` is a reserved keyword, but the grammar has no import declaration.\n- **Visibility today:** `public`, `internal` (the default) and `private` are parsed. Only `private` on struct fields\n  has an effect, and it means visible within the file (§12, C7c).\n- **`lib/core`** is part of every package: its items are in scope unqualified, with no import (§29.2).\n- **The compiler is whole-program.** Generics are monomorphised (§20), and the ownership analyses, placement and the\n  optimisations (`optimisations.md`) run over the whole NIR. The C backend writes one `main.c` per program, with\n  names such as `ceeF_area` and `ceeT_Point` (`c-backend.md` §2.1), unique only within one package.\n- **Programs that would use packages:** CosmoOS integration code that today shares bindings by copying files\n  (`integrations/cosmoos/c5/kernel.cee` into c7 and c8), the benchmark programs, `nwc`, and code outside this\n  repository (ADR 0008: the reference also serves Linux and other C codebases, not only CosmoOS).\n- **The kernel profile** needs nothing to run at build time that is not CeeSafe source or C, and builds must be\n  reproducible.\n\n## 2. Decisions proposed\n\n### D1. A package is a directory with a manifest\n\n`ceesafe.toml` at the package's root; its sources are every `.cee` file under the directory `src/` (or the\ndirectories the manifest's `sources` lists), in ordinal path order. The manifest is a small subset of TOML: tables,\nstrings, booleans, arrays of strings and inline tables. `ceesafec` parses it itself, with no new dependency.\n\n```toml\n[package]\nname = \"geometry\"            # an identifier: what dependents call the package\nversion = \"0.3.1\"            # recorded; not used to choose versions (D6)\nkind = \"library\"             # \"executable\" (has main), \"library\", or \"object\" (one .o, profiles.md §4.3)\nprofile = \"hosted\"           # or \"freestanding\"\nfloat = true                 # false is --no-float\nc = [\"src/shim.c\"]           # C sources built with it (type-system.md §28.6)\n\n[dependencies]\nshapes = { path = \"../shapes\" }\nlz4 = { git = \"https://example.org/lz4-cee.git\", rev = \"3f2a9c0d…\" }    # a full commit id\nutil = { git = \"https://example.org/util.git\", tag = \"v1.2.0\" }          # resolved to a commit in ceesafe.lock\n```\n\n### D2. Names: one scope per package, qualified across packages\n\nRecommended (option **A** in §3):\n- Within a package nothing changes: its files share one item scope, and names are simple.\n- A dependency's **public** items are reached through the dependency's n",
    159 =>
        "ame as the manifest writes it:\n  `geometry.Point`, `geometry.area(p)`, as types and in expressions.\n- `import geometry;` brings every public name of `geometry` into the file's scope; `import geometry.Point;` brings\n  one. An imported name never hides a name of the package itself or of `lib/core`. Two imports giving the same name\n  are an error where the name is used, not where it is imported.\n- `namespace` declarations are retired: N0106 for any `namespace` after a grace period in which it is accepted and\n  ignored. (Alternatively, kept as documentation with no effect.)\n- `lib/core` stays unqualified, in every package.\n\n### D3. Visibility between packages\n\n- Only `public` items, and a public struct's `public` fields and methods, are visible to dependents. `internal`, the\n  default, stays within the package; `private` stays within the file (fields: §12).\n- A public item's signature must not name a type its dependents cannot see (a public function returning an internal\n  struct): a new error. Implementations of an interface (`impl I for T`) are visible wherever both `I` and `T` are.\n\n### D4. Building stays whole-program\n\n`ceesafec build` (no file arguments, in a package directory) checks and compiles the package with all its\ndependencies, as one program, as today. Generated C names of items from a package other than the one being built\ngain the package's name (`ceeF_geometry_area`, `ceeT_geometry_Point`), so two packages may declare the same name. No\nseparate compilation and no cache in this ADR: they would need generic code checked once per package and a stable\ninterface format, and would be decided after measuring build times (the M10 plan's \"cache of generated C per\npackage\" is deferred until then).\n\n### D5. Sources: path and git dependencies\n\n- `path` is relative to the manifest. `git` takes `rev` (a full commit id), or `tag` or `branch`, which `ceesafec fetch`\n  resolves to a commit and records.\n- Git sources are fetched with the `git` executable into a cache (`$CEESAFE_HOME/git`, default `the reference's install directory/git`), one\n  checkout per commit.\n- **Nothing runs at fetch or build time** but `git` and the C compiler: a package has no build scripts.\n\n### D6. The lock file\n\n- `ceesafe.lock`, written by `ceesafec fetch` and committed with the package. Each dependency, transitively: its name,\n  source, commit for git, and a SHA-256 of its manifest and sources.\n- `ceesafec build` uses the lock and fails if a package's sources no longer match their hash, or if `ceesafe.toml` names a\n  dependency the lock lacks (run `ceesafec fetch`). `--offline` never runs `git`.\n- **No version solving.** Two packages that depend on a package of the same name must agree on its source and commit;\n  otherwise it is an error that names both. `version` is recorded but not compared. *Amended by\n  [ADR 0017](0017-m13-package-registry.md) (2026-10-28): registry dependencies are version requirements solved\n  against an index; path and git dependencies keep this rule.*\n\n### D7. Tools follow the package\n\n`check`, `build`, `run`, `doc` and `fmt` without file arguments act on the package of the current directory. The\nlanguage server checks a document with its package when there is one (`tooling.md` §2.1). `ceesafec new <name>`\nwrites a manifest and `src/main.cee`.\n\n## 3. Options for names (D2)\n\n| | A. Package-level names (proposed) | B. Hierarchical namespaces | C. File modules |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Inside a package | one scope, as today | `namespace X.Y;` qualifies every item | each file its own module |\n| Across packages | `geometry.Point`, `import geometry;` | `import geometry.shapes;` | `import geometry/shapes;` |\n| Existing code | unchanged | every file needs a namespace or moves | every multi-file package needs imports |\n| Compiler work | qualified names through dependencies, imports, visibility | the above, plus namespaces within a package | the above, plus per-file scopes |\n\nA keeps everything written so far valid, and qualifies only what",
    160 =>
        " crosses a package boundary, where names can collide.\nB and C give finer structure inside a large package; nothing in this repository needs it yet, and A does not prevent\nadding namespaces later.\n\n## 4. Questions for the owner\n\n1. Names: A (recommended), B or C (§3)? And under A, retire `namespace` (recommended) or keep it as a comment?\n2. Sources: `src/` by default (recommended), or every `.cee` file under the package directory?\n3. Git references: `rev`, `tag` and `branch`, each resolved to a commit in the lock (recommended), or `rev` only?\n4. C names: prefix items of dependencies with the package name (recommended, D4)?\n5. `ceesafec new`: wanted now, or later?\n\n## 5. Consequences\n\n- New: a manifest parser, a dependency resolver and fetcher (`git`), the lock file, qualified names and imports in\n  the type checker, visibility errors, package-prefixed C names, and package-aware commands and language server.\n- New diagnostics (codes assigned when specified): manifest errors, an unknown dependency, an unresolved or\n  ambiguous imported name, a non-public item used from another package, a public signature naming a non-public\n  type, a hash mismatch, a dependency conflict.\n- Tests: packages in `tests/packages/` (path dependencies, a diamond, a conflict, visibility, imports); git sources\n  from a local bare repository made by the test, so the suite needs no network.\n- Risks: R-12 (new) for dependencies: a fetched package is code the build trusts. Mitigated by commits pinned in the\n  lock, verified hashes, and no build scripts; not by review, which stays the user's.\n",
    161 =>
        "# ADR 0014 — C arrays in imported structs, C member names, and exported constants\n\n> **Inherited.** ADR 0014 was accepted before CeeSafe's fork (Decision 0006), when the language had its\n> earlier name. In this record, \"the reference\" is that language and project and \"the reference compiler\" its compiler:\n> read them as CeeSafe and `ceesafec`. Links to documents CeeSafe did not keep are given as text.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-26: \"start C9 with the GPT parser\", then \"You can go ahead with any\n  integration you need\"). The design below is the agent's under that instruction; the owner may revise it.\n- Scope: the additions to C interoperability that C9 needs (`docs/cosmoos/c9-report.md`): CosmoOS's GPT\n  parser (`kernel/block/gpt.c`) cannot be written in the reference with the API of `kernel/include/kernel/gpt.h`\n  without them. ADR 0008 named C arrays as a \"later addition\"; this is it.\n\n## Context\n\n`gpt.h` is an ordinary C header, and its shapes are common in kernel and library headers:\n\n- `struct gpt_table` holds `uint8_t disk_guid[16]` and `struct gpt_part parts[32]`; `struct gpt_part` and\n  `struct gpt_build_part` hold `uint8_t type[16], uuid[16]`. An imported struct's fields could only be\n  integers, `bool`, raw pointers, function pointers and embedded imported structs (type-system §31.1, §33.1).\n- One member is named `type`, a CeeSafe keyword. A field had to be declared with its C name.\n- `gpt_build` takes `const struct gpt_build_part *parts, unsigned nparts`: an array of imported structs behind\n  a pointer. `Ptr.add` needs the pointee's size, which the reference does not know for an imported type (§31.2).\n- `gpt.h` declares data: `extern const uint8_t gpt_type_esp[16]` and `gpt_type_cosmo_root[16]`. The reference could\n  export functions only, and had no constant arrays at all (`const T: [u8; 2]` was N0216).\n\n## Decision\n\n1. **Array fields of imported structs.** A field of an imported struct may have type `[E; N]`, where `E` is an\n   integer, `bool`, or an imported type. Its C member must be `CE name[N]`: the generated C asserts\n   `_Generic(&member, CE (*)[N]: 1, default: 0)`, which is exact, and that the member has the size of the reference's\n   array. Inside the reference the field is an ordinary array place: index it (bounds-checked against `N`), slice it,\n   borrow it, and, when `E` is Copy, read and assign it whole.\n2. **Arrays of imported types are C-laid-out.** `[T; N]` with `T` imported (or C-laid-out) is itself C-laid-out\n   (§33.1): allowed where an imported type by value is (a field, an embedded member, a box's contents), never a\n   value. Indexing it gives a place of `T`, used through its fields or borrowed.\n3. **Lowering by the same representation.** the reference's `[E; N]` is `struct { E e[N]; }` in C (`c-backend.md` §2),\n   with the size and alignment of `E[N]`, and an access through the struct type is an access through an\n   aggregate that contains the member's type (C11 6.5p7). An array field place is `(*(ceeA… *)&p->member)`: no\n   copy, and every operation on arrays works unchanged. The generated C asserts the sizes are equal.\n4. **`@cname(\"name\")` on a field of an imported struct** gives its C member name when that name is not a usable\n   CeeSafe identifier (a keyword: `type`, `match`, `in`). CeeSafe code uses the CeeSafe name.\n5. **Pointer arithmetic on pointers to imported types.** `add`, `sub` and `offset` on `*T` / `*mut T` with `T`\n   imported are allowed (still unsafe, §27.3): C scales by its own `sizeof`. `read`, `write` and `fromRaw` stay\n   N0294, since they would copy a value or make a slice of one.\n6. **Constant arrays.** `const NAME: [E; N] = [ … ];` (or `[v; N]`), with `E` an integer or `bool` and every\n   element a constant expression (§10). A use is the array's value, as for any constant.\n7. **Exported constants.** `@export(\"symbol\")` (or `@export`) on a constant of integer, `bool` or such array type\n   defines C data: `const E symbol[N] = { … };` (",
    162 =>
        "or `const T symbol = v;`), declared in `ceesafe_exports.h` as\n   `extern const E symbol[N];`. It goes in read-only data, which C4's object checks allow.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- type-system §10 (constant arrays), §28.1 and §28.4 (exported constants, `@cname`), §31.1 to §31.3 (array\n  fields, `@cname`, pointer arithmetic) and §33.1 (arrays of imported types) change; `c-backend.md` §2.3\n  describes the lowering.\n- The interpreter has no layout for imported types: accessing an array field, or `add` on a pointer to an\n  imported type, is an interpreter fault, as other imported-field accesses are (§31.4). Constant arrays and\n  exported constants work in the interpreter.\n- A header's `char name[N]` (plain `char`) cannot be declared: `CChar` is pointee-only (§28.3). Bit-fields,\n  unions, anonymous members and flexible array members remain later additions.\n- An exported function whose C prototype in the header uses an `enum` type (`enum gpt_result gpt_parse(…)`)\n  is defined by the reference with the enum's underlying integer type. GCC and Clang make an enum without negative\n  values `unsigned int`, so the two declarations are compatible and the header can be included; a compiler\n  that chose another type would reject the build (it could not miscompile). This is recorded in the C9 report\n  as a property of those compilers, not a reference rule.\n",
    163 =>
        "# ADR 0015 — M13 G2–G3: dynamic dispatch and owning closures\n\n> **Inherited.** ADR 0015 was accepted before CeeSafe's fork (Decision 0006), when the language had its\n> earlier name. In this record, \"the reference\" is that language and project and \"the reference compiler\" its compiler:\n> read them as CeeSafe and `ceesafec`. Links to documents CeeSafe did not keep are given as text.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-28, taking every recommendation in §5). The wording is the agent's;\n  the owner may revise it. Not yet implemented: the decisions go into `type-system.md` (§21, §36, and a new §37),\n  `grammar.md`, `lifetimes.md`, `intermediate-representation.md` and `c-backend.md` before any code.\n- Scope: M13 G2 and G3 (`milestone-13.md`). It closes OD-10 (`dyn Interface`) and OD-9b (owning, heap-allocated\n  closures), which ADR 0012 deferred \"only together with `dyn`\". It amends `type-system.md` §21.1 (\"Interfaces are not\n  types\").\n\n## 1. Context\n\n- **Interfaces are bounds only.** `type-system.md` §21.1: \"there is no `dyn Shape` value in v0 (OD-10)\". Generics are\n  monomorphised (§21.2). A list of shapes of different types cannot be written. Probe at `b9fa451`:\n  `Vector<Box<dyn Shape>>` is `expected '>', found identifier` (N0100).\n- **Interface methods are dispatchable.** Every method has a receiver: `this`, `borrow this` or `borrow mut this`.\n  Methods take no type parameters, and v0 has no `Self` type (§21.1). So every method that takes a reference receiver\n  could be called through a table of function pointers. A method that takes `this` by value could not, since the\n  caller does not know the value's size.\n- **Closures are scoped.** A capturing closure is a reference to an environment in its maker's block. So it cannot be\n  returned, stored past its block, or sent to another thread (§36.2; ADR 0012 §5a). Probe: returning\n  `(x: u64) => x + n` as `fn(u64) -> u64` is N0299 (\"this closure captures 'n', so it is a 'borrow fn'\").\n- **Allocation is explicit and fallible.** `Box.create` returns `Result<Box<T, A>, AllocError>` (§18). The kernel\n  profile allows allocation only through an explicit allocator (`KernelHeap`, C5). So an owning closure must not\n  allocate behind the programmer's back.\n- **No C-level UB from function pointers.** ADR 0012 §3.6 generates an adapter instead of casting function pointer\n  types. The same rule applies to tables of methods.\n- **Threads (G4) will need both features.** A thread's body is an owning closure, sent to another thread. That ADR\n  comes after this one. This one should not rule out marking a `dyn` type as safe to send (§3.8).\n\n## 2. Options\n\n### Dynamic dispatch\n\n| | A. Fat pointers (proposed) | B. A vtable in the object | C. Enums only |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A `dyn` value is | `{ data, vtable }` behind a reference or box | a thin pointer to an object whose first field is its vtable | no `dyn`: users write a closed `enum` and `match` |\n| Works for existing types | yes (any `T` that implements `I`) | only types declared to carry a header | yes, but the set of types is closed |\n| Cost | two words per pointer; one indirect call | one word; a header in every object | a branch per call |\n| Precedent | Rust, Go's interface values | C++ | — (what the reference has today) |\n\nA works with today's types and values, needs no layout change, and keeps `borrow dyn I` free (no allocation). B\nchanges object layouts. C is the status quo. **A is proposed.**\n\n### Owning closures\n\n| | D. `move` literals boxed as `Box<dyn fn(…) -> R, A>` (proposed) | E. Monomorphised closures with `Fn` bounds (OD-9a) | F. Garbage-collected or reference-counted closures |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Storage | one explicit, fallible `Box` allocation | none: the closure is a value of an anonymous type | implicit heap |\n| Can be returned or stored in a field | yes | only through generics | yes |\n| Call | indirect | direct, inlinable | indirect |\n| Needs | A (`dyn`), a `move` literal | anonymous types, `Fn",
    164 =>
        "` interfaces, bound syntax | a runtime the reference does not have |\n\nD follows from A with little new: a `move` literal's environment is an ordinary value, and the box holds it behind a\n`dyn` function type. E stays open as OD-9a, the faster path for generic code. F conflicts with the reference's memory model.\n**D is proposed.**\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n1. **`dyn I` types.** For an interface instantiation `I` (`Shape`, `Comparable<i32>`), `dyn I` is a type whose values\n   are \"some type that implements `I`\". It has no size known to the compiler, so it appears **only behind a pointer**:\n   - `borrow dyn I` and `borrow mut dyn I`: references, scoped like every reference (§13);\n   - `Box<dyn I, A>`: an owning box, a move type that needs drop.\n\n   A `dyn I` anywhere else is a new error: a local, a field, a parameter, an array or `Vector` element, or a type\n   argument. `Vector<Box<dyn I>>` is allowed, since its elements are boxes.\n2. **Dyn-compatible interfaces.** `dyn I` is allowed when every method of `I` takes `borrow this` or\n   `borrow mut this`. A method that takes `this` by value makes `I` incompatible: `dyn I` is a new error, which names\n   that method. A generic interface is compatible once its type arguments are given.\n3. **Coercions** happen only where the expected type asks for them, never through inference:\n   - `borrow T` → `borrow dyn I` and `borrow mut T` → `borrow mut dyn I`, when `T` implements `I`;\n   - `Box<T, A>` → `Box<dyn I, A>` (a move of the box, with no new allocation);\n   - a `T` that does not implement `I` is N0268, as for an unsatisfied bound.\n\n   There is no way back: v1 has no downcasting and no `is`/`as` from `dyn I` to `T`.\n4. **Calls.** `x.area()` on a `borrow dyn Shape`, a `borrow mut dyn Shape` or a `Box<dyn Shape>` calls through the\n   table. The receiver rules are those of a reference to `T`: a `borrow mut this` method needs mutable access. A\n   call's effects are those declared on the interface method. Without a declaration, it may `sleep` and `alloc`, as\n   for C function pointers and closures without a list (§32.1, §36.3). An `impl` method's inferred effects must fit\n   what its interface declares (N0295).\n5. **What `T` may be.** In v1, `T` must not be scoped: no references, no closures and no region parameters, so the\n   `dyn` value carries no loans other than the pointer's own. That keeps `lifetimes.md` unchanged: `borrow dyn I`\n   carries the loan of the reference it came from, and `Box<dyn I>` carries none. A `dyn(r) I` holding scoped values\n   is future work.\n6. **Owning closures** (OD-9b):\n   - **Types.** `dyn fn(T…) -> R` and `dyn mut fn(T…) -> R` are the `dyn` forms of the closure types. Behind a\n     `Box`, they own their environment: `Box<dyn fn(u64) -> u64>` is an owning closure. The existing\n     `borrow fn(…) -> R` keeps its meaning and representation (§36.2). It is not rewritten as `borrow dyn fn`.\n   - **Literals.** `move (x) => x + n` is a **move literal**. It captures each named local **by move**, or by copy\n     for a Copy type. Its value may appear only as the argument of `Box.create` or `Box<…, A>.createIn` where the\n     expected result is a `Box<dyn fn…, A>` or `Box<dyn mut fn…, A>`. Anywhere else, it is a new error, until OD-9a\n     gives move literals their own types.\n   - **Mutation.** A body that changes a captured value makes the literal a `dyn mut fn`, called through a\n     `borrow mut` of the box. A `dyn fn` is called through a shared one.\n   - **Allocation.** `Box.create(move (x) => x + n)` returns `Result<Box<dyn fn(u64) -> u64>, AllocError>`.\n     Allocation stays explicit and fallible, and the kernel profile can use it with `KernelHeap`.\n   - **Captures.** v1 allows only non-scoped captures, by the same rule as 5. Capturing a reference is a new error.\n7. **C representation.**\n   - `borrow dyn I` is `struct { void *data; const ceeT_<T,I> *vt; }`, two words.\n   - `Box<dyn I, A>` is the same pair with an owning `data`.\n   - The table is a",
    165 =>
        " `static const` struct per (`T`, `I`). It holds the drop glue, the size and alignment (for a user\n     allocator's `deallocate` layout), then one entry per method in declaration order.\n   - Each entry is a generated adapter `R ceeTA_…(void *self, A…)`, which converts `self` to `T *` and calls `T`'s\n     method. So no function pointer is ever called through a different type.\n   - A call is `x.vt->m(x.data, args…)`. Dropping a `Box<dyn I, A>` calls `vt->drop(data)`, then frees `data` with `A`\n     and the table's size and alignment.\n   - A boxed move literal's environment is a struct of its captures. Its table holds the body as the call entry and\n     the environment's drop glue.\n8. **Room for threads (G4).** The thread ADR may add marker bounds to `dyn` types (`dyn fn() + Send`). Nothing here\n   depends on that, and nothing here prevents it.\n9. **NIR and the interpreter.** NIR gains a `dyn` pointer aggregate `{ data, vtable }`, a vtable constant per\n   (`T`, `I`), and a call through a table entry. Coercion is building the pair. The borrow checker treats\n   `borrow dyn I` as a reference, so it needs no new rule. The interpreter keeps a table of the implementing method\n   per (`T`, `I`) and calls it.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- Open sets of types become possible: `Vector<Box<dyn Shape>>`, plugin-style registries, callbacks stored in\n  structs, and functions that return closures (`fn adder(n: u64) -> Box<dyn fn(u64) -> u64>`).\n- A `dyn` call is indirect and not inlined. Hot code can still use generics. No performance claim without a\n  benchmark: a `dyn` against generic dispatch comparison joins `benchmarks/`.\n- The language gains one keyword in types (`dyn`), one literal prefix (`move`) and one generated table per\n  (`T`, `I`) actually coerced, so code size grows only with use.\n- Scoped `dyn` values (`dyn(r) I`), downcasting, and move literals outside a box remain open: OD-10a, OD-10b and\n  OD-9a.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner (decided 2026-10-28: each as recommended)\n\n1. **Fat pointers (A) for `dyn I`, behind references and `Box` only?** Recommended: yes.\n2. **Owning closures as boxed `move` literals (D), `Box<dyn fn(…) -> R>`?** Or wait for monomorphised closures\n   (OD-9a)? Recommended: D now. It is what returning or storing a closure needs. OD-9a can follow for speed.\n3. **Syntax.** `dyn I` and `move (x) => …`, as in Rust? Or a reference-specific spelling, such as `any Shape` (Swift)\n   or `interface Shape`? Recommended: `dyn` and `move`. They are short, widely known, and say what they mean.\n4. **v1 limits:** no scoped `T` behind `dyn`, and no scoped captures in move literals (§3.5, §3.6). Recommended: yes.\n   They keep the borrow checker unchanged, and `dyn(r) I` can follow once a use needs it.\n5. **No downcasting in v1.** Recommended: yes. Closed sets of types are better served by enums and `match`.\n\n## 6. Implementation plan (once accepted)\n\nSpec first (type-system §21 amended, §37 `dyn`, §36.5 move literals; grammar; IR; c-backend), then:\n\n- **G2a.** `dyn I` types, compatibility, coercions, calls through `borrow dyn I`: checker, NIR, C, interpreter.\n- **G2b.** `Box<dyn I, A>`: coercion from `Box<T, A>`, drop through the table, user allocators.\n- **G3.** Move literals, `dyn fn`/`dyn mut fn`, boxing, calls; the fuzzer generating `dyn` calls and boxed closures.\n\nTests to add (none run yet):\n\n- run-pass, compiled and interpreted:\n  - a heterogeneous `Vector<Box<dyn Shape>>`;\n  - `borrow mut dyn` mutation;\n  - a generic interface;\n  - drop order through `Box<dyn I>`;\n  - a returned adder closure;\n  - a counter closure (`dyn mut fn`);\n  - a closure stored in a struct field;\n  - fault injection on `Box.create(move …)`.\n- check-fail:\n  - `dyn I` by value;\n  - an incompatible interface;\n  - a `T` that does not implement `I`;\n  - scoped `T`;\n  - a move literal outside a box;\n  - a scoped capture;\n  - a `dyn mut fn` called through a shared reference;\n  - a `dyn` call in an atomic context without `@effects()`.\n- The M2 mu",
    166 => "tation suite extended with `dyn` references.\n",
    167 =>
        "# ADR 0016 — M13 G4: userland threads and data-race freedom\n\n> **Inherited.** ADR 0016 was accepted before CeeSafe's fork (Decision 0006), when the language had its\n> earlier name. In this record, \"the reference\" is that language and project and \"the reference compiler\" its compiler:\n> read them as CeeSafe and `ceesafec`. Links to documents CeeSafe did not keep are given as text.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-28, taking every recommendation in §5; OD-24 and OD-25 record the\n  deferred options). The wording is the agent's; the owner may revise it. Not yet implemented: the decisions go into\n  `type-system.md` (a new §38), `ownership.md` (the data-race invariants), `c-backend.md`, `profiles.md` and\n  the runtime before any code.\n- Scope: M13 G4 (`milestone-13.md`): threads in user programs (hosted and CosmoOS userland), with a compile-time\n  guarantee that safe code has no data races. The kernel keeps C7's model (ADR 0009: effects, lock guards, `KRef`).\n\n## 1. Context\n\n- **No threads.** Probe at `b9fa451`: `Thread.spawn(…)` is `cannot find 'Thread' in this scope` (N0200). The reference has\n  no mutable globals: the only top-level data are `const` items (grammar.md §2, `ConstDecl`). So today a program's\n  state is reachable only from its one thread, apart from C state reached through `unsafe` code.\n- **Owning closures exist** (G3, type-system §36.5). A `move` literal owns its captures, which is what a thread body\n  needs: it must not borrow its spawner's stack, which may be gone.\n- **The platforms have what is needed.** CosmoOS userland libc has `cosmo_thread_start`/`join`, `cosmo_mutex_*` and\n  `cosmo_cond_*` over futexes (`libc/include/cosmo/thread.h`). POSIX hosts have pthreads, and Windows has\n  `CreateThread` and SRW locks. C11 `<stdatomic.h>` gives atomics on every compiler the reference supports except MSVC\n  before 17.5. There, the `Interlocked*` intrinsics are used (inferred; to check on the Windows VM).\n- **The kernel already has a model.** C7's lock guards, `KRef<T>` and context effects are kernel-only (§32–§34). A\n  userland design should look the same where it can: a lock returns a guard that lends the data.\n- **What a guarantee needs.** A data race needs two threads reaching the same memory, at least one writing, without\n  synchronisation. Safe the reference already gives one writer or many readers per value, within a thread (B1–B5). Across\n  threads, what remains is controlling **what may cross** to another thread, and **what may be shared** between\n  threads.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. Marker properties, owned transfer, `Arc`/`Mutex`/atomics (proposed) | B. Message passing only (channels; no shared memory) | C. Unchecked threads (`unsafe` spawn) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Model | Rust's `Send`/`Sync`, simplified | Erlang/Go-style | C's |\n| Shared state | through `Arc<Mutex<T>>`, atomics | none: values are moved through channels | anything |\n| Data-race freedom | checked | checked (by construction) | none |\n| Cost | two inferred properties per type; a few library types | copies through channels; no shared caches | none |\n| Covers | workers, shared counters, caches, pools | pipelines | everything, unsafely |\n\n**A is proposed**, with channels as a later library type over it. B alone cannot express a shared cache or counter\nwithout copying. C contradicts the master prompt's safety rules.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n1. **Two inferred properties**, never written by users in v1 (no `unsafe impl`), and usable as bounds like `Copy`\n   (`<T: Send>`):\n   - **`Send`**: a value may be moved to another thread. It holds for integers, floats, `bool`, and owned types made\n     only of `Send` types: structs, enums, arrays, `Box<T, A>`, `Vector`, `String`, `SlotMap`, `StableVector` and\n     resources. It also holds for `Arc<T>` when `T: Share`, `Mutex<T>` when `T: Send`, and the atomics.\n     - It fails for: references and every scoped type; raw pointers; imported C types; closures (`borrow fn`); a box\n       or ",
    168 =>
        "vector with a user allocator; regions; and `Thread<T>`, whose join must happen on the thread that spawned\n       it.\n     - `Box<dyn I>` and `Box<dyn fn…>` are `Send` only when written `dyn I + Send` or `dyn fn(…) + Send`. Coercing to\n       one requires `T: Send`.\n   - **`Share`**: shared references to it may be used from several threads at once. It holds for every `Send` type\n     with no interior mutability (all of them in v1: the reference has no `Cell`), and for `Mutex<T>` and the atomics.\n2. **Spawning.** `Thread.spawn(move () => body)` starts a thread running the move literal's body. It returns\n   `Result<Thread<T>, SpawnError>`, where `T` is the body's result type.\n   - The literal's captures must be `Send` (new code). Since they are moved in, the spawner keeps nothing it could\n     race with.\n   - `T` must be `Send` (the same code).\n   - Spawning allocates the closure and the thread's stack. It has the `sleep` and `alloc` effects.\n   - `Thread.spawn` takes only a move literal, or a `Box<dyn fn() -> T + Send>`. This is the one place besides\n     `Box.create` where a move literal may appear (amending §36.5).\n3. **Joining.** `t.join()` consumes the handle, waits, and returns the body's `T`. Dropping an unjoined `Thread<T>`\n   joins it and destroys the result. There are no detached threads in v1, so a program ends only after every thread\n   it started. A panic in any thread aborts the process (OD-5).\n4. **Shared state** (library types, safe to use; their implementation is `unsafe` over the runtime):\n   - `Arc<T>` (`T: Share`): shared ownership with an atomic count. `Arc.create(v)` returns a `Result`. `a.clone()`\n     gives another owner. `borrow` of the contents is shared only. The last drop destroys `T`.\n   - `Mutex<T>` (`T: Send`): `m.lock()` returns a guard lending `borrow mut T`, scoped like C7's guards (type-system\n     §32.4), and the unlock is the guard's drop. `Arc<Mutex<T>>` is the usual shared, mutable state.\n   - `AtomicU32`, `AtomicU64`, `AtomicBool`, `AtomicUSize`: `load`, `store`, `swap`, `compareExchange`, `fetchAdd`,\n     `fetchSub`, all sequentially consistent in v1. Weaker orderings stay open (OD-24).\n   - `Condvar` and channels follow as a separate increment, each spec-first.\n5. **Why this is enough.** Every value is reachable from one thread (moved in) or through an `Arc` of a `Share`\n   type. A `Share` type without a `Mutex` or an atomic has no way to be written through a shared reference. So two\n   threads can write the same memory only through a `Mutex` or an atomic. `unsafe` code may break this, as it may\n   break B1–B5. `ownership.md` gains invariants T1–T3 with this argument, and the mutation suite gains thread cases.\n6. **Profiles.** Hosted (pthreads, or Win32 on Windows) and CosmoOS userland (`cosmo_thread_*`, `cosmo_mutex_*`). The\n   kernel and freestanding profiles reject `Thread`, `Arc` and `Mutex` as hosted-only names (N0293). The kernel keeps C7.\n7. **Effects** (§32). `spawn`, `join`, `lock` and a `Thread` drop have `sleep`. Atomic operations have none.\n8. **The interpreter** runs threads deterministically: one at a time, switching only when a thread blocks (`join`,\n   a contended `lock`) or ends, in spawn order. A deadlock, where every thread is blocked, is an interpreter fault.\n   This keeps `ceesafec run --interp` reproducible. It means the interpreter cannot find races, but safe code has none.\n9. **C.**\n   - The runtime gains `ceesafe_thread_start`, `ceesafe_thread_join`, `ceesafe_mutex_*` and atomics over `<stdatomic.h>`, in\n     `runtime/hosted` and as platform hooks for CosmoOS.\n   - A thread's body is the boxed closure, called through its table as for `dyn fn` (§37.4).\n   - `Arc<T>` is `struct { atomic count; T value; } *`. `Mutex<T>` embeds the platform mutex and `T`.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- Programs can use several cores safely, with the same ownership rules plus two inferred properties. Nothing changes\n  for single-threaded code: a type's `Send` and `Share` matter onl",
    169 =>
        "y at `spawn`, `Arc` and `Mutex`.\n- **Not in v1:**\n  - scoped threads that borrow the spawner's locals (`Thread.scope`; OD-25);\n  - user `unsafe impl Send`;\n  - weaker atomic orderings (OD-24);\n  - thread-locals;\n  - detached threads.\n- Benchmarks: a parallel workload (for example `numeric`'s sieve over several threads) joins `benchmarks/` before any\n  performance claim, on the Ryzen box (6 cores).\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner (decided 2026-10-28: each as recommended)\n\n1. **Marker properties with `Arc`/`Mutex`/atomics (A)**, rather than message passing only (B)? Recommended: A.\n2. **Names.** Should they be `Send` and `Share` (Rust's `Sync` renamed to say what it means), or `Send` and `Sync`?\n   Recommended: `Send` and `Share`.\n3. **Joining on drop**, with no detached threads in v1? Recommended: yes. Nothing outlives `main`, and no handle\n   leaks a thread.\n4. **Sequentially consistent atomics only in v1?** Recommended: yes. Weaker orderings would wait for a benchmark that\n   needs them (OD-24).\n5. **Scoped threads later** (OD-25)? Recommended: yes. They need a rule for borrows that outlive a closure's call,\n   which the other parts do not.\n6. **CosmoOS userland in v1, with a test in QEMU** (`integrations/cosmoos/`, as for C3)? Recommended: yes.\n\n## 6. Implementation plan (once accepted)\n\nSpec first (type-system §38, ownership.md T1–T3, c-backend, profiles), then:\n\n- **G4a.** `Send` and `Share` inference and bounds, with check-fail tests over every kind of type.\n- **G4b.** `Thread.spawn`/`join`, the hosted runtime, the interpreter's scheduler; run-pass and fault-injection tests.\n- **G4c.** `AtomicU32`/`U64`/`Bool`/`USize`, `Arc<T>`, `Mutex<T>` with guards, run under ThreadSanitizer as an extra\n  C configuration.\n- **G4d.** CosmoOS userland: the platform hooks, and a QEMU test on both arches.\n- A parallel benchmark on the Ryzen box.\n",
    170 =>
        "# ADR 0017 — M13 G6: a package registry and version solving\n\n> **Inherited.** ADR 0017 was accepted before CeeSafe's fork (Decision 0006), when the language had its\n> earlier name. In this record, \"the reference\" is that language and project and \"the reference compiler\" its compiler:\n> read them as CeeSafe and `ceesafec`. Links to documents CeeSafe did not keep are given as text.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-28, taking every recommendation in §5; OD-26 records the deferred\n  option). The wording is the agent's; the owner may revise it. Not yet implemented: the decisions go into\n  `tooling.md` §4 and `diagnostics.md`, with an amendment to ADR 0013 D6, before any code.\n- Scope: M13 G6 (`milestone-13.md`): finding packages by name and version, and choosing compatible versions, on top\n  of ADR 0013's path and git dependencies, lock file and hashes.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n- **Today** (ADR 0013, `tooling.md` §4): a dependency is a path or a git repository pinned to a commit by `ceesafe.lock`\n  with a SHA-256 of its sources. `version` in `ceesafe.toml` \"is recorded but not compared\" (D6). Two packages that need\n  the same dependency must name the same source and commit, or it is N0326. There is no way to find a package by name,\n  and no way to say \"any 1.x\".\n- **What G6 must not lose:** reproducible builds (the lock and its hashes), `--offline`, nothing executed at build\n  time except `git` (D5), and the kernel profile's rule that a build needs only CeeSafe source and C.\n- **Hosting.** A registry needs somewhere to live. The reference has a GitHub organisation and a documentation site on GitHub\n  Pages. It has no server, and the project should not have to run one.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. A git index (proposed) | B. A registry server with an HTTP API | C. No registry: a curated list in the docs |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| What it is | a git repository (`ceesafe-lang/index`) with one small TOML file per package, listing each version's git URL, commit and source hash | a service storing package archives, with publish and download endpoints | links |\n| Hosting | GitHub; anyone can mirror it with `git clone` | a server, storage, accounts, uptime | none |\n| Publishing | a pull request adding a version line, checked by CI (the commit exists, the hash matches, the manifest's name and version agree) | `ceesafec publish` with a token | edit a page |\n| Integrity | the index records each version's hash, and the lock checks it as today | the server's archive hashes | none |\n| Offline and mirrors | the index is a git repository like any dependency | needs a mirror protocol | — |\n| Precedent | crates.io-index, Homebrew taps, Go's sum database (in part) | npm, PyPI | — |\n\n**A is proposed.** It adds names and versions without a service to run. The code still comes from each package's\nown git repository, pinned to a commit and a hash exactly as today. B can come later behind the same manifest\nsyntax, if the index outgrows git.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n1. **The index** is a git repository: by default `https://github.com/CeeSafe/CeeSafe-index` (`vkuttyp/CeeSafe-index`\n   until the repository moved to the `CeeSafe` organisation, 2026-11-03; question 2), or `$CEESAFE_INDEX`.\n   - One file per package, `packages/<name>.toml`, with one entry per published version: `version`, `git` (a URL),\n     `commit` (a full id), `hash` (the SHA-256 of `tooling.md` §4.3), and `dependencies` (the version requirements of\n     that version's `ceesafe.toml`, so solving needs no checkouts).\n   - Entries are only appended. A version can be marked `yanked = true`, which keeps old locks working and stops new\n     resolutions from choosing it.\n   - The index's CI checks each pull request: the commit exists, its manifest's name and version match the entry,\n     the hash matches, and the version is new.\n2. **Registry dependencies** in `ceesafe.toml`:\n   ```toml\n   [dependencies]\n   geometry = \"1.4\"           # a version requirement: >=1.4.0, <2.0.0\n   lz4 = \"=0.3.1\"",
    171 =>
        "             # exactly\n   json = { version = \"0.9\", index = \"https://example.org/my-index.git\" }   # another index\n   ```\n   - Requirements follow Semantic Versioning's caret rule: `\"1.4\"` means `>=1.4.0, <2.0.0`, and `\"0.3\"` means\n     `>=0.3.0, <0.4.0`.\n   - Also `=`, `>=`, `<`, and comma-separated combinations.\n   - Path and git dependencies keep their meaning, and override the index for their name.\n3. **Version solving** (`ceesafec fetch`): the newest version of each package that satisfies every requirement on it,\n   found by a PubGrub-style solver.\n   - A **failure is explained**, as a chain of requirements (`app needs geometry ^1.4; geometry 1.4 needs shapes ^2;\n     plot needs shapes ^1`), as a new code.\n   - **One version per package name** in a build, as today. Allowing several would need distinct C names per version\n     (`c-backend.md` §2.1) and is left open (OD-26).\n   - The **result goes into `ceesafe.lock`**: the source is `registry+<index URL>`, with the version, commit and hash.\n     From then on a build is exactly as reproducible as with git dependencies today. `ceesafec fetch --update [name]`\n     re-solves; otherwise a lock that still satisfies the manifest is kept.\n4. **Publishing** is a pull request to the index.\n   - `ceesafec publish` (no network) checks the package: it builds, the manifest's version is new, and the working tree\n     is a clean, pushed commit.\n   - It then prints the entry to add.\n   - No tokens and no accounts. The index's maintainers review the first version of each new package name, and later\n     versions from the same repository merge automatically after CI.\n5. **Unchanged:**\n   - D5: nothing runs at fetch or build time but `git`.\n   - `--offline`.\n   - The hashes.\n   - N0326, for a name reached through a path or git dependency and the registry at once.\n   - The kernel profile, which needs nothing new.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- Packages can be found by name and depended on by a range, and builds stay pinned and hashed.\n- The index is a public repository that someone must review: the first version of each package name, by hand.\n- Solving adds about one module to `Packages/` and new tests:\n  - unit tests of the solver over synthetic indexes, including the failure explanations;\n  - an end-to-end test against a local index repository made in the test, so it needs no network.\n- Not decided here: several versions of one package in a build (OD-26), private-index authentication (plain git\n  credentials work already), and a search command.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner (decided 2026-10-28: each as recommended)\n\n1. **A git index (A)** rather than a registry server? Recommended: A.\n2. **Where it lives:** a new public repository, `vkuttyp/CeeSafe-index`? Or an organisation, which would need creating?\n   Recommended: `vkuttyp/CeeSafe-index` now, moved later if an organisation is made. Creating it needs your account.\n3. **Caret requirements** (`\"1.4\"` means `^1.4`)? Recommended: yes, as Cargo does.\n4. **One version per package name in a build** (OD-26 for more)? Recommended: yes.\n5. **Review:** should the first version of each package name be reviewed by hand, and later versions merge after CI?\n   Recommended: yes.\n\n## 6. Implementation plan (once accepted)\n\nSpec first (`tooling.md` §4.6 the index, §4.7 solving, §4.8 publishing; diagnostics), then:\n\n- **G6a.** Version requirements and the index format, with a local test index; the solver and its failure\n  explanations.\n- **G6b.** `ceesafec fetch` with registry dependencies, and the lock entries.\n- **G6c.** `ceesafec publish`'s checks.\n- **G6d.** The index repository and its CI, once the owner has created it.\n",
    172 =>
        "# ADR 0018 — OD-25: scoped threads by fork-join\n\n> **Inherited.** ADR 0018 was accepted before CeeSafe's fork (Decision 0006), when the language had its\n> earlier name. In this record, \"the reference\" is that language and project and \"the reference compiler\" its compiler:\n> read them as CeeSafe and `ceesafec`. Links to documents CeeSafe did not keep are given as text.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-28, taking every recommendation in §5). The wording is the agent's. The\n  decisions are in `type-system.md` §38.6 and `ownership.md` T4. §38.6 makes one point precise: the arguments must be\n  closure literals or `fn()` values, since the capture rule is about captures the checker can see.\n- Scope: OD-25 (`open-decisions.md`), deferred by ADR 0016 §5 question 5: threads that borrow their spawner's data.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n- **What v1 threads cannot do.** `Thread.spawn` takes only a move literal, or a `Box<dyn fn() -> T + Send>`, and\n  every capture must be `Send` (type-system §38.2). References, slices and views are never `Send` (§38.1). So a\n  program cannot hand two threads the two halves of a vector it owns. It must move the data into an `Arc` (an\n  allocation, and the vector is no longer its own), copy it, or, as `benchmarks/parallel` does, give each thread\n  only integers to compute from.\n- **Why references are not `Send` today.** A spawned thread can outlive the block that made it: the `Thread<T>` is a\n  value that can be stored, returned or dropped later. A reference it carried could then outlive its referent.\n- **What the borrow checker already does.** A closure is a reference to an environment in its maker's block, and it\n  carries its captures' loans (§36.2). Two closures passed to one call are both live for the whole call, so their\n  captures must not conflict. Probes, run 2026-10-28 with the reference compiler 0.2.0-dev:\n  - two closure arguments that capture the same local mutably are refused (**N0241**, \"cannot borrow 'x' as mutable\n    while 'x' is mutably borrowed\");\n  - one that captures it shared and one mutably are refused too (**N0241**).\n- **Where the gap shows.** Divide-and-conquer over data the caller owns: sort the halves of a slice in parallel,\n  sum the chunks of a vector, process the rows of an image. `splitAtMut` (M13 G5) already gives two disjoint\n  `SliceMut` halves; only the thread is missing.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. Fork-join: `Thread.both(a, b)` (proposed) | B. Scopes with handles: `Thread.scope((s) => { s.spawn(…) … })` | C. Library-only: `parallelChunks` over slices |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Model | Rayon's `join` | Rust's `std::thread::scope` | a fixed data-parallel helper |\n| What a thread borrows | anything its closure captures, for the call | anything, for the scope | one chunk of one slice |\n| New borrow rule | **none**: two scoped closures used only during one call | closures that leave their maker's block with borrowed captures (heap environments holding references; a move literal with scoped captures, which ADR 0015 §3.6 forbids) | none |\n| New `Send` rule | references are `Send` inside the call, under conditions (§3.2) | the same | the same, for one element type |\n| Number of threads | two per call; more by recursion | any, in a loop | any |\n| Size | small | large | small, narrow |\n\n**A is proposed.** It adds parallelism without adding a borrow rule: everything it needs about lifetimes is the\nexisting rule for closure arguments. B is the more familiar API, but it needs closures that outlive their maker's\nblock while holding references, which is a new kind of value (OD-10a's territory). C covers one pattern and can be\nwritten over A in the library later.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n1. **`Thread.both(a, b)`**, in `lib/std` (hosted and CosmoOS userland, as `Thread`): `a` and `b` are closures with\n   no parameters and a `void` result, `borrow fn()` or `borrow mut fn()` (§36.2). It runs `a` on a new thread and\n   `b` on the calling thread, waits for both, and returns. Resul",
    173 =>
        "ts travel through the closures' captures:\n\n   ```ceesafe\n   fn sum(values: Slice<u64>) -> u64 { ... }\n\n   fn parallelSum(values: Slice<u64>) -> u64 {\n       let halves = values.splitAt(values.length / 2);\n       var low: u64 = 0;\n       var high: u64 = 0;\n       Thread.both(() => { low = sum(halves.left); }, () => { high = sum(halves.right); });\n       return low + high;\n   }\n   ```\n\n   The reference has no tuples, so a pair-returning form would need a library struct; it is left out of v1 (question 3).\n   The example checks and runs today (2026-10-28) with a plain two-closure function in place of `Thread.both`: the\n   two closures' captures (`halves` shared by both, `low` and `high` each changed by one) are already accepted.\n2. **What may be captured.** Inside the call, `a` runs on another thread while `b` runs here, so each capture of\n   `a` and of `b` must be safe to use from another thread at the same time:\n   - a capture used shared needs its type to be **`Share`**, where, for this rule only, `borrow T`, `Slice<T>` and\n     `StringView` are `Share` when `T` is;\n   - a capture used mutably needs its type to be **`Send`**, where, for this rule only, `borrow mut T` and\n     `SliceMut<T>` are `Send` when `T` is;\n   - a captured closure is refused in v1 (question 4);\n   - otherwise the error is **N0334**, as for `Thread.spawn`, naming the capture and the type.\n   The conflicts between `a`'s and `b`'s captures are the borrow checker's, unchanged (§1): two closures cannot both\n   change a value, or one read what the other changes.\n3. **Why this is sound.** Both closures and every loan they carry are live for the whole call (§36.2), and the call\n   returns only after the thread running `a` has ended. So nothing `a` reaches can be ended, moved or changed by\n   the caller while `a` runs, and the two closures cannot reach one value in conflicting ways. With the capture\n   rule, a value reachable from both threads is either read by both (and `Share`) or reached by one only (and\n   `Send`). This is T1–T3 of `ownership.md` with \"moved in\" widened to \"borrowed for a `Thread.both` call\".\n4. **If a thread cannot be started** (the runtime's record cannot be allocated, or the platform refuses), `both`\n   runs `a` and then `b` on the calling thread. The result is the same, since each closure's effects are its own, so\n   `both` cannot fail and returns `void`. A program cannot tell from its output whether `a` ran in parallel.\n5. **Panics.** A panic in either closure aborts the process (OD-5), as in any thread.\n6. **Effects** (§32): `sleep` and `alloc`, those of `spawn` and `join` together.\n7. **The interpreter** runs `a` as a thread under its baton (`Interpreter/Threads.cs`), then `b`, then waits, so\n   `ceesafec run --interp` stays deterministic.\n8. **C.** A scoped closure is a pointer to an environment whose first member is its code (c-backend §2). `both`\n   starts a thread on a runtime helper that calls `a`'s code with `a`'s environment (`ceesafe_thread_start`), calls `b`\n   directly, then `ceesafe_thread_join`s. No allocation beyond the runtime's thread record.\n9. **More than two.** Recursion: split, `both`, split again. A data-parallel helper (option C) may follow in\n   `lib/std` over `both`, spec first.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- Programs can process data they own on several cores without `Arc`, copies or `unsafe`.\n- The `Send` and `Share` rules gain a scoped extension used only by `Thread.both`. Outside it, references stay\n  neither, and `Thread.spawn` is unchanged.\n- One OS thread per call: `both` suits coarse splits (a few per core). A thread pool, so fine-grained recursion is\n  cheap, is not in this decision.\n- **Not in this decision:** handles and loops of spawns (option B); results returned as a pair; captured closures;\n  a thread pool.\n- Benchmarks: `benchmarks/parallel` gains a variant that sums a vector the program owns, split by `both`, against\n  C with pthreads over the same split, on the Ryzen box (6 cores), before any perfo",
    174 =>
        "rmance claim.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. **Fork-join (A)**, rather than Rust-style scopes (B) or a slice helper only (C)? Recommended: A.\n2. **The name.** `Thread.both(a, b)`, or `Thread.join(a, b)` (Rayon's name, but `t.join()` already means joining one\n   thread), or `Thread.parallel(a, b)`? Recommended: `both`.\n3. **Results through captures only in v1**, with no pair-returning form until the reference has tuples or a library pair?\n   Recommended: yes.\n4. **No captured closures in v1** (a closure captured by `a` or `b`)? Recommended: yes. Allowing them needs the\n   capture rule applied through a closure's own captures, which can follow when a use needs it.\n5. **Sequential fallback when a thread cannot start**, so `both` cannot fail? Recommended: yes.\n\n## 6. Implementation plan (once accepted)\n\nSpec first (type-system §38.6, ownership.md T1–T3, c-backend §2, the runtime), then:\n\n- the capture rule (N0334 cases) with check-fail tests: each kind of capture, shared and mutable, accepted and refused;\n- `Thread.both` in the checker, C and the interpreter; run-pass tests, including the sequential fallback under fault\n  injection;\n- the guide's threads page (`guide/threads.md`) and `ceesafec explain` text;\n- the fuzzer generating `both` calls;\n- the `benchmarks/parallel` variant on the Ryzen box;\n- a CosmoOS userland run in QEMU on both arches (`c2.py`, as for M13 G4).\n\n## 7. Implementation and results (2026-10-28)\n\n- Spec: type-system §38.6, ownership T4, c-backend (`ceeTF_`, `ceeBOTH_`), N0334's row (`4e4576d`).\n- Code (`ab9c95c`): `CheckThreadBoth` and `TypeTable.IsForkSafe` in the checker; `BuiltinFunction.ThreadBoth` through NIR\n  and the effect checker (`sleep`, `alloc`); the C helpers; the interpreter's `ThreadBoth` under its baton, mirroring\n  `ceesafe_thread_start`'s one allocation, so the fault-injection sweep exercises the sequential fallback. No new borrow\n  rule: the probes of §1 hold for `Thread.both` too (`check-fail/thread-both-borrows.cee`).\n- Found on the way, and fixed first (`e6b1a67`): a `SliceMut` field (`halves.left` of `splitAtMut`) was moved by a\n  use instead of reborrowed (type-system §13.3), so a closure could not pass it on (N0251), and a second use outside\n  one was N0250.\n- Tests: `run-pass/thread-both.cee` (halves read and changed in parallel, recursion to four threads, a `Mutex`, an\n  `fn()` value), `check-fail/thread-both.cee` (each refused capture, a closure variable, a move literal) and\n  `thread-both-borrows.cee`; the guide's threads page; the fuzzer (26 of the first 200 seeds). Run 2026-10-28: the\n  full suite (3,424) passes; the fuzzer's seeds 1–300 give no finding; `c2.py` runs the corpus, `thread-both`\n  included, in CosmoOS on x86-64 (188 programs, the fault sweep 43 programs and 381 runs) and AArch64 (the same: 188, and 43 and 381).\n- Benchmark (`benchmarks/parallel`, the `both` pair; the Ryzen box, a Ryzen 5 PRO 3600 with 6 cores, clang 18.1.8 -O2, 7 runs\n  interleaved, at normal priority, load average\n  about 1.2 on the shared box): 16 million values in a vector the program owns, split in halves.\n\n  | Threads | The reference `Thread.both` | C, recursive pthreads | The reference/C | Speed-up the reference / C |\n  |---|---|---|---|---|\n  | 1 | 277.5 ms | 268.8 ms | 1.03 | 1.00 / 1.00 |\n  | 2 | 155.1 ms | 149.2 ms | 1.04 | 1.79 / 1.80 |\n  | 4 | 89.4 ms | 82.6 ms | 1.08 | 3.10 / 3.26 |\n\n  `Thread.both` scales as the same split does in C. Both stay below linear by the same margin; filling the vector,\n  which is serial, is inside the times (inferred).\n",
    175 =>
        "# ADR 0019 — OD-9a: direct closure calls by specialisation\n\n> **Inherited.** ADR 0019 was accepted before CeeSafe's fork (Decision 0006), when the language had its\n> earlier name. In this record, \"the reference\" is that language and project and \"the reference compiler\" its compiler:\n> read them as CeeSafe and `ceesafec`. Links to documents CeeSafe did not keep are given as text.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-28, taking every recommendation in §5). The wording is the agent's. The\n  decision is in `optimisations.md` §5, `intermediate-representation.md` §3 and `c-backend.md` §2.1. A remains open\n  (OD-9a), as a language question.\n- Scope: OD-9a (`open-decisions.md`), deferred by ADR 0012 §5 question 5 \"until a performance need appears, with a\n  benchmark\": monomorphised closures, so a call through a closure can be a direct call the C compiler inlines.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n- **How a closure call compiles today.** A closure is a pointer to an environment whose first member is its code\n  (type-system §36.2). A call through one is `(*c)(c, a, b)` in C (c-backend §2). A library method such as `sortBy`\n  takes `borrow mut fn(borrow T, borrow T) -> i32`, one type for every comparator of that signature, so its body cannot\n  know which function it calls. The C compiler does not specialise lib/core's introsort for the one comparator either.\n- **The need, measured** (2026-10-28, the Ryzen box: Ryzen 5 PRO 3600, Debian 12; `benchmarks/sort`, 10 rounds of\n  sorting 1,000,000 values; sort time with the data generation subtracted; median of 7 runs, interleaved; load average\n  1.0–2.1 on the shared box). \"Direct\" is the reference compiler's C for the benchmark with the seven calls through the comparator\n  replaced by direct calls to its function, `ceeCB_92(t, a, b)`, by hand: what a specialisation would produce. Every\n  program printed the same checksum.\n\n  | Compiler | The reference today | The reference, direct calls | C `qsort` | the same introsort in C | the same, comparator opaque |\n  |---|---|---|---|---|---|\n  | clang 18.1.8 -O2 | 1.062 s | **0.671 s** | 1.171 s | 0.617 s | 0.907 s |\n  | gcc 12.2.0 -O2 | 1.076 s | **0.797 s** | 1.195 s | 0.659 s | 0.941 s |\n\n  Direct calls take 37% (clang) and 26% (gcc) off the sort, bringing the reference from 1.72× the C introsort to 1.09× under\n  clang. The rest is the reference's checks (M11 G8c found the same split).\n- **Where the closures come from.** Every call of lib/core's closure-taking methods (`sortBy`, `retain`, `forEach`,\n  `any`, `all`) in the corpus, the benchmarks and the guide passes a closure literal written at the call, or a named\n  function adapted to a closure (§36.2: `sortBy(compare)` in `closure-methods.cee`), and the methods pass it on\n  unchanged to their helpers (checked 2026-10-28 by searching for the calls). So at those calls the code that will\n  run is known. User functions with closure parameters were not surveyed.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. Closure types and `Fn` bounds (OD-9a as written) | B. Specialisation in the compiler (proposed) | C. Nothing |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Model | Rust's: each literal its own type; `fn sortBy<F: Fn(borrow T, borrow T) -> i32>(…, cmp: borrow mut F)` | the call graph: a callee cloned per closure code it is called with | — |\n| Language change | yes: closure types, an `Fn` bound family, inference of `F`, its effects and loans in bounds; lib/core's signatures change | **none**: `sortBy` and every user function keep their signatures | none |\n| Which calls become direct | only through functions written with an `F` bound | every call through a parameter whose closure is known at the call, in any function | none |\n| Cost | a large language feature | a NIR-to-NIR pass, like M9's; code size: one clone per (function, closure) pair | the measured 26–37% stays |\n\n**B is proposed.** It gives the measured gain to code as it is written, lib/core's included, without adding to the\nlanguage. A remains open as a language question (expre",
    176 =>
        "ssiveness: owning closures in generic code without a box),\nnot as a performance one.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n1. **Closure-call specialisation**, an optimisation pass on NIR before C (`optimisations.md` §5), on by default with\n   `--no-closure-specialisation`, as M9's are. It changes no program's behaviour, only which C function is called.\n2. **What is known.** At a call `f(…, c, …)`, an argument `c` is *known* when it is a closure made in the caller from a\n   literal, or an `fn` value's adapter, or a known parameter of a function being specialised, or a reborrow or copy\n   of a known value. Its *code* is that literal's or adapter's function.\n3. **What is cloned.** When `f`'s parameter `p` has a closure type and the argument is known with code `k`, the call\n   goes to `f[p := k]`: a copy of `f` in which every call through `p` (or a reborrow or copy of `p`) is a direct call\n   to `k`, and every call that passes `p` on is specialised the same way. Clones are made once per (function,\n   parameter, code), so recursion (`introsort` calling itself) calls its own clone. A function with several closure\n   parameters is specialised for each known one.\n4. **What is not.** A closure stored in a field, a `dyn fn` (§37), a `Box<dyn fn…>`, or one whose code depends on a\n   branch is called as today. Generic functions are specialised after monomorphisation, as one more instance.\n5. **Equivalence.** The clone is the same NIR with a call target fixed, so its checks, effects and loans are the\n   original's; borrow and effect checking run before the pass and are not repeated. The interpreter runs the program\n   unspecialised, and the corpus already compares it with native runs. The pass is checked by: the run-pass corpus with\n   and without it (outputs equal), the fuzzer's configurations (one more: `--no-closure-specialisation`), a test that\n   counts direct and indirect closure calls in the C of `benchmarks/sort`, and `--explain-closure-calls`, which lists\n   each call through a closure and whether it became direct.\n6. **Kernel and freestanding profiles** get it too: it adds no allocation and changes no frame size (a clone's frame\n   is the original's).\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- `sortBy`, `retain`, the closure-taking slice methods (§36.4) and user functions with closure parameters call\n  their closures directly, so the C compiler can inline them. No source changes.\n- Code size grows by one function per (function, closure) pair actually reached; `--explain-closure-calls` reports\n  the clones.\n- A remains open, as a language question.\n- **Not in this decision:** closure types or `Fn` bounds (A); specialising `dyn` calls (devirtualisation); inlining\n  in the reference compiler itself (the C compiler does it once the call is direct).\n- Benchmarks: `benchmarks/sort` is measured again on the Ryzen box after the pass, with clang and gcc, before any\n  claim, and `benchmarks/suite`'s closure workload (`nbody`) with it.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. **Specialisation in the compiler (B)**, rather than closure types and `Fn` bounds (A) now? Recommended: B, with\n   A left open as a language question.\n2. **On by default**, with `--no-closure-specialisation` and `--explain-closure-calls`? Recommended: yes, as for M9's\n   bounds-check and overflow-check elimination.\n3. **No limit on clones** in v1, the count reported by `--explain-closure-calls`? Recommended: yes; the count is\n   bounded by the closure literals a program writes times the functions they reach.\n\n## 6. Implementation plan (once accepted)\n\nSpec first (`optimisations.md` §5, `intermediate-representation.md`, `c-backend.md`), then:\n\n- the pass (`Optimisation/ClosureSpecialisation.cs`): known closures, the clone worklist, the switch and the\n  explanation;\n- tests: the corpus with and without it, a C-shape test on `benchmarks/sort` (no indirect comparator calls remain),\n  explanation snapshots, the fuzzer configuration;\n- `benchmarks/sort` and `benchmarks/suite` re-measured on the Ryzen b",
    177 =>
        "ox.\n\n## 7. Implementation and results (2026-10-28)\n\n- Spec: `optimisations.md` §5, `intermediate-representation.md` §3, `c-backend.md` §2.1 (`5bae3d6`).\n- Code (`9ec6aa1`): `Nir/ClosureSpecialisation.cs`; `NirFunction.Variant` and `NirCall.Variant`; the C names\n  `ceeXS<n>_<name>`; the emitter's reachability now follows variants, so a library function that only its variants\n  replace is not emitted; the interpreter runs the specialised NIR; `--no-closure-specialisation` and\n  `--explain-closure-calls`.\n- In `benchmarks/sort` every one of the seven calls through the comparator is direct, through six variants of lib/core's\n  introsort (`sortBy`, `introsort`, `insertion`, `heapsort`, `partition`, `siftDown`).\n- Found on the way: `ceesafec run` gave the interpreter the C backend's NIR, so with the pass on it was specialised twice,\n  which replaced originals by variants (an interpreter fault on the first variant call). The pass now leaves a\n  specialised program unchanged, and `run` passes its switch to the backend.\n- Tests, run 2026-10-28: the full suite passes (3,431), with the run corpus built in one more C configuration\n  (`clang -O2 no-closure-specialisation`); `ClosureSpecialisationTests` (the `--explain-closure-calls` snapshot of\n  `closure-calls/known.cee`, whose closure chosen by a branch stays indirect; its output with and without the pass;\n  no indirect comparator call in `benchmarks/sort`'s C, seven without the pass); the fuzzer's seeds 1–300 give no\n  finding in six configurations and the interpreter; `c2.py` runs the corpus in CosmoOS on x86-64 and AArch64 (188 programs each; the fault sweep 43 programs and 381 runs each).\n- Measured on the Ryzen box (clang 18.1.8 and gcc 12.2.0, -O2, 7 runs, the same session; load average 1.3–2.8 on the\n  shared box, normal priority). `benchmarks/sort`, sort time:\n\n  | Compiler | The reference, pass on | The reference, `--no-closure-specialisation` | the same introsort in C | C `qsort` |\n  |---|---|---|---|---|\n  | clang | **0.691 s** | 1.048 s | 0.610 s | 1.162 s |\n  | gcc | **0.791 s** | 1.100 s | 0.657 s | 1.188 s |\n\n  The pass takes 34% (clang) and 28% (gcc) off the sort, which is what §1's hand edit showed (0.671 s and 0.797 s).\n  The reference goes from 1.72× and 1.68× the C introsort to 1.13× and 1.20×.\n- `benchmarks/suite` with the pass on and off: `nbody`, `parsing` and `data-oriented` move within ±8% in both\n  directions, which is noise on that box. None of them calls through a closure: `nbody` has no closure literal any\n  more, so §4's reference to it as the suite's closure workload was out of date. The sort is the measured case.\n",
    178 =>
        "# ADR 0020 — OD-9c: functions whose effects are their closure arguments'\n\n> **Inherited.** ADR 0020 was accepted before CeeSafe's fork (Decision 0006), when the language had its\n> earlier name. In this record, \"the reference\" is that language and project and \"the reference compiler\" its compiler:\n> read them as CeeSafe and `ceesafec`. Links to documents CeeSafe did not keep are given as text.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-29, taking every recommendation in §5). The wording is the agent's.\n- Scope: OD-9c (`open-decisions.md`), deferred by M11 G8c (type-system §36.4): a function whose effects are its own plus\n  those of a closure argument, so that `sortBy` with an `@effects()` comparator may run in an atomic context.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n- **Effects today** (type-system §32, §36.3). Each function's effects (`sleep`, `alloc`) are inferred from its body. A\n  call through a closure or function value has its *type's* effect list, and a type without one has both effects.\n  `@context(atomic)` forbids `sleep`, and `@context(irq)` forbids both.\n- **The gap.** lib/core's closure-taking methods (`sortBy`, `forEach`, `any`, `all`, `retain`; §36.4) take\n  `borrow mut fn(…)` with no list, so each of them has both effects, whatever closure it is given. None of them can be\n  used in atomic or interrupt code, even with a comparator that does nothing but compare. Giving the parameter a list\n  (`borrow mut @effects() fn(…)`) would forbid every comparator that allocates, everywhere. What is wanted is Swift's\n  `rethrows`: the method has the effects of what it is given.\n- **Who needs it.** CosmoOS kernel code holds spinlocks (atomic context) and runs interrupt handlers. Sorting or\n  scanning a small table under a lock, with `sortBy` or `any`, is today impossible in safe the reference. It must be written out by\n  hand (inferred: no CosmoOS port does this yet; `kernel/block/gpt.cee` and the A5 ports have no closures).\n- **What compiles the same either way.** Nothing in C or the interpreter changes. Effects are only checked.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. `@effectsOf(p)` on a function (proposed) | B. Effect variables (`fn sortBy<effects E>(…, cmp: … @effects(E) fn …) @effects(E)`) | C. Check effects after closure specialisation (ADR 0019) | D. Nothing |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Model | Swift's `rethrows`, for two effects | Koka's effect polymorphism | the variant `sortBy` for a known comparator has that comparator's effects | — |\n| Syntax | one attribute naming parameters | effect parameters, bounds and arguments | none | none |\n| Inference at calls | the argument's own effects (a literal's body, a named function's inference) | unification of effect variables | precise, per variant | — |\n| Soundness argument | short (§3.3) | the general one | ties acceptance to an optimisation (`--no-closure-specialisation` would change which programs compile) | — |\n| Cost | the checker, one attribute | a new kind of generic parameter | none in the checker, but wrong in principle | the gap stays |\n\nC is ruled out because a program's acceptance must not depend on an optimisation. B can say more, for example a\nfunction whose effects are the union of two closures stored in a struct. But nothing needs that, and it is a new kind\nof generics. A covers the library methods and any user function of the same shape.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision: A\n\n### 3.1 The attribute\n\n```ceesafe\n@effectsOf(cmp)\npublic fn sortBy(s: SliceMut<T>, cmp: borrow mut fn(borrow T, borrow T) -> i32) { … }\n\n@context(atomic)\nfn sortUnderLock(lock: *mut spinlock_t, table: SliceMut<i32>) {\n    unsafe { spin_lock(lock); }\n    table.sortBy((a, b) => a - b);   // allowed: sortBy's own effects are none, and the comparator's are none\n    unsafe { spin_unlock(lock); }\n}\n```\n\n- `@effectsOf(p, …)` on a function or method names one or more of its parameters. Each must be a function or closure\n  type: `fn(…)`, `borrow fn(…)` or `borrow mut fn(…)`, with or without an effect list. Oth",
    179 =>
        "erwise it is a new error code\n  (N0336). It may be combined with `@effects(…)`, which then bounds the function's *own* effects (§3.2).\n- It changes no type. The function's type and value are as before (§3.4).\n\n### 3.2 Inside the function\n\n- A call whose callee is the parameter `p` itself is not one of the function's own effects: the caller accounts for it.\n- Every other use is checked as today:\n  - a call through a copy or a reborrow of `p` (`let c = p; c(x)`) has its type's list;\n  - `p` passed to a function that is not `@effectsOf` for it: that function's inferred effects, which include\n    calls through its parameter by type;\n  - `p` stored in a field or captured by a closure: the type's list wherever it is called.\n- `p` passed directly as the argument for another function's `@effectsOf` parameter contributes nothing either:\n  `sortBy` may hand `cmp` to an `@effectsOf` helper.\n- The function's own effects are inferred from everything else. They are bounded by `@effects(…)` if given, and checked\n  against `@context` as today.\n\n### 3.3 At a call\n\nThe effects of a call to an `@effectsOf` function are its own effects, plus, for each named parameter, the effects of\nthe argument:\n\n| Argument | Its effects |\n|---|---|\n| a closure literal | its body's inferred effects |\n| a named function (adapted as in §36.1–§36.2) | that function's inferred effects |\n| the caller's own `@effectsOf` parameter, passed on directly | none (the caller's caller accounts for it) |\n| anything else | its type's effect list (both effects without one) |\n\nA diagnostic for such a call names the argument as the witness, for example \"calls sortBy with a closure that does\nconsole I/O (line 12)\", following the chain as N0295 does today.\n\n**Why it is sound.** A parameter is immutable (N0211), and closures and function values cannot be `var` (N0246). So\nduring the call, every call through `p` itself runs exactly the argument the caller passed, and that argument's effects\nare counted at the call. Calls through anything else made from `p` are counted by their types, as before. Effects are a\nproperty of a call's dynamic extent: a closure stored by the function and called after it returns is counted where it\nis called, by its type.\n\n### 3.4 As a value\n\nNaming an `@effectsOf` function as a value (`let f: @effects() fn(…) = sortBy`) gives it no special treatment. Its\neffects as a value are its own effects plus each named parameter's *type* list. The check of §36.3 then applies.\n\n### 3.5 lib/core\n\n- `sortBy`, `forEach`, `any` and `all` become `@effectsOf` their closure parameter, and so does `retain`. `retain` still\n  has the effects of destroying a `T` (its own, inferred per instantiation).\n- The spec's sentence \"these methods cannot be used in `@context(atomic)` … code\" (§36.4) changes accordingly.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- **Kernel code** can use the library's algorithms under a lock with a comparator that neither sleeps nor allocates.\n- **One new attribute** and one diagnostic code (N0336 for a misplaced or misnamed parameter). N0295 gains a witness\n  kind (the argument of a call).\n- **Nothing changes** for code that does not use the attribute, or in C, the interpreter, or the fuzzer's programs.\n- **Tests (planned):**\n  - check-pass: `sortBy`/`any` under `@context(atomic)` with literal and named comparators, and an `@effectsOf` helper\n    that passes its parameter on;\n  - check-fail: a sleeping comparator in atomic code (N0295 naming the argument), a copy of `p` called inside (counted\n    by type), a misnamed parameter or one that is not a closure (N0336), and `@effectsOf` with an `@effects()` bound\n    violated by the function's own body.\n- **Not decided here:** effect variables (B), which stay open in OD-9c if a need appears.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. Option A, `@effectsOf(p)`, as above? (Recommended.)\n2. The name: `@effectsOf(p)` (proposed), or `@effects(…, of p)` inside the existing attribute?\n3. lib/core's five methods change in the same s",
    180 =>
        "tep (§3.5)? (Recommended.)\n\n## 6. Implementation (2026-10-29)\n\nThe decision is in type-system §32.5, with §36.2 and §36.4 changed to match. Three points refine §3 without changing\nwhat it decides. Each follows from the same argument (§3.3: a parameter is never assigned):\n\n- **Copies of `p`.** §3.2 counted a copy of `p` by its type. A local that is assigned nothing but copies, casts or\n  reborrows of `p` holds the same argument, so a call through it is exempt as well. Anything else still counts by type.\n- **Arguments.** Besides a literal written in the call, a local *known* to hold a literal counts as that literal. That\n  is closure specialisation's analysis (optimisations.md §5.1), which the effect checker runs whatever the\n  optimisation switches are, so which programs compile does not depend on them. So does a local holding the caller's\n  own `@effectsOf` parameter.\n- **Named functions.** A named function adapted to a closure (§36.2) used to be held in the adapter's environment and\n  called through. Its effects were then the closure type's, both effects without a list, whatever the function did.\n  The adapter now calls the function directly. Its effects are the function's own, and the C has one indirect call\n  fewer.\n\nThe diagnostic code is N0336: N0335 was already `ceesafec publish`'s. `EffectChecker.CallEffects` computes a call's\neffects, and `OriginsOf` finds what each local holds. Tests: `check-pass/effects-of.cee`, `check-fail/effects-of.cee`\n(N0295 for a sleeping literal, a sleeping named function, an argument without an effect list, a parameter handed to a\nfunction without the attribute, and the function as a value), `check-fail/effects-of-attribute.cee` (N0336), and\n`check-fail/closure-method-effects.cee` (lib/core's methods with a comparator that does I/O).\n",
    181 =>
        "# ADR 0021 — OD-24: weaker orderings for lib/std's atomics\n\n> **Inherited.** ADR 0021 was accepted before CeeSafe's fork (Decision 0006), when the language had its\n> earlier name. In this record, \"the reference\" is that language and project and \"the reference compiler\" its compiler:\n> read them as CeeSafe and `ceesafec`. Links to documents CeeSafe did not keep are given as text.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-29, taking every recommendation in §5). The wording is the agent's.\n- Scope: OD-24 (`open-decisions.md`), deferred by ADR 0016 \"sequentially consistent only in v1, until a benchmark needs\n  more\": acquire, release and relaxed orderings for `AtomicU64`, `AtomicU32`, `AtomicUSize` and `AtomicBool`.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n- **Today** (type-system §38.3, `lib/std/sync.cee`): every atomic operation is sequentially consistent. Each is a call\n  into `runtime/hosted/ceesafe_rt_thread.c`, which uses `__atomic_*_n(…, __ATOMIC_SEQ_CST)`, or `Interlocked*` under\n  MSVC, where even a load is a compare-exchange.\n- **The benchmark.** `benchmarks/atomics` passes 50 million values through a single-producer, single-consumer ring of\n  1,024 slots between the two threads of `Thread.both`. It is the shape of a work queue or a log buffer. Four C builds\n  of the same ring separate the cost of the ordering from the cost of the call:\n\n  | Ring (median of 9, interleaved) | clang 18 | gcc 12 |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | The reference today (seq_cst, runtime calls) | 2.683 s | 2.629 s |\n  | C, seq_cst inline | 2.761 s | 2.170 s |\n  | C, seq_cst through calls | 1.607 s | 1.529 s |\n  | **C, acquire/release (slots relaxed) through calls** | **0.289 s** | **0.291 s** |\n  | C, acquire/release inline | 0.158 s | 0.157 s |\n\n  Ryzen 5 PRO 3600 (x86-64), Debian 12, 2026-10-29, threads pinned to two cores sharing an L3 (CPUs 1 and 2), under\n  `nice`, load average 1.0–2.3 on the shared box. Every program printed the same checksum. A seq_cst run varies by up\n  to 40% between runs: it is dominated by the ring's two cache lines moving between cores. The acquire/release runs\n  vary by 1–2%.\n\n  On the Mac (Apple M-series, clang 21, 20 million values, 5 runs, not pinned), the orderings make no difference: 0.718\n  s and 0.712 s for acquire/release, against 0.573 s for seq_cst through calls. arm64's seq_cst loads and stores are\n  `ldar`/`stlr`, which acquire/release also uses. x86's seq_cst store is an `xchg`, a full barrier, which is what the\n  orderings remove.\n- **The need, then:** on x86-64, the commonest server target, the orderings alone make this ring 5–9 times faster,\n  without changing how atomics are called.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. An ordering argument on new methods (proposed) | B. Inline atomics in generated C | C. Both | D. Nothing |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Change | `loadWith(Acquire)` and friends in lib/std; the runtime gains ordered entry points | atomics become built-in operations the emitter writes as `__atomic_*` | — | — |\n| Gain measured | 5–9× on the ring (x86) | none alone: inline seq_cst was no faster than calls (table) | a further 1.8× over A (0.158 s against 0.289 s) | — |\n| Cost | library and runtime; no compiler change | the emitter, the interpreter, MSVC's intrinsics, `Send`/`Share` reasoning unchanged | both | — |\n\nB gains nothing without A. C's extra step is a compiler change with its own decision, left open (§4).\n\n## 3. Proposed decision: A\n\n### 3.1 The library\n\n```ceesafe\npublic enum LoadOrdering { Relaxed, Acquire, SequentiallyConsistent }\npublic enum StoreOrdering { Relaxed, Release, SequentiallyConsistent }\npublic enum Ordering { Relaxed, Acquire, Release, AcquireRelease, SequentiallyConsistent }\n\n// On each of AtomicU64, AtomicU32, AtomicUSize, AtomicBool (where the operation exists today):\npublic fn loadWith(borrow this, order: LoadOrdering) -> u64;\npublic fn storeWith(borrow this, value: u64, order: StoreOrdering);\npublic fn swapWith(borrow this, value: u64, order: Ordering) -> u64;\npublic fn compareExcha",
    182 =>
        "ngeWith(borrow this, expected: u64, desired: u64, order: Ordering) -> …;   // as compareExchange\npublic fn fetchAddWith(borrow this, value: u64, order: Ordering) -> u64;\npublic fn fetchSubWith(borrow this, value: u64, order: Ordering) -> u64;\n```\n\n- The existing methods are unchanged and stay sequentially consistent, so no program changes meaning.\n- **No undefined orderings.** C11 leaves a store with acquire ordering, and a load with release ordering, undefined.\n  Separate enums for loads and stores make those impossible to write. A compare-exchange takes one ordering, and its\n  failure ordering is derived as C11 requires: `AcquireRelease` fails with `Acquire`, `Release` fails with `Relaxed`,\n  and the others fail with themselves.\n- **The meaning** is C11's (the C standard §7.17.3), which the documentation states for each enum value. The reference adds no\n  memory model of its own.\n\n### 3.2 The runtime and the C\n\n- `ceesafe_rt_thread.c` gains `ceesafe_atomic_<op>_<width>_ord` entry points taking the ordering as an `int`. Each switches on\n  it, with constant orderings in the `__atomic_*` builtins, so the C compiler emits each case's own instructions.\n- Under MSVC, every `Interlocked*` function is a full barrier, which is a correct (stronger) implementation of every\n  ordering. So MSVC changes nothing.\n\n### 3.3 Safety\n\n- **Memory safety and data-race freedom are unchanged.** Every access to an atomic is still atomic, and `Send`/`Share`\n  and T1–T3 (`ownership.md`) are untouched. A weaker ordering changes which values a load may return, never whether an\n  access is a race.\n- **What it does allow is logic bugs.** A program that publishes data with `Relaxed` instead of `Release` may read\n  stale values. That was OD-24's stated safety impact, and the API keeps it opt-in.\n- **Testing limits.** The interpreter runs threads one at a time, in sequentially consistent order, which is one legal\n  execution of a weaker program. So it cannot find an ordering bug, and neither can ThreadSanitizer, which sees no race.\n  The guide says so.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- lib/std gains three enums and six methods per atomic type. The runtime gains the ordered entry points. Neither the\n  compiler nor the language changes.\n- `benchmarks/atomics` gains a reference build with acquire/release, expected near the \"through calls\" row.\n- **Not decided here:**\n  - inline atomics (B), whose further 1.8× is its own question;\n  - `fence(order)`;\n  - weaker orderings inside `Arc`'s count, which are a change to `unsafe` library code with its own argument.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. Option A as above: ordered `…With` methods, existing methods unchanged? (Recommended.)\n2. Separate `LoadOrdering`/`StoreOrdering` enums, so undefined orderings cannot be written? (Recommended; the alternative\n   is one enum and a panic on an invalid ordering.)\n3. Record inline atomics (B) as a new open decision, OD-27? (Recommended.)\n\n## 6. Implementation and results (2026-10-29)\n\n- lib/std (`sync.cee`) has the three enums and the `…With` methods. The runtime has `ceesafe_atomic_<op>_ord_<width>`\n  (`ceesafe_rt_thread.c`, declared in `ceesafe/rt.h`), which switch to constant orderings. The interpreter treats each as\n  sequentially consistent (`CLibrary.cs`). Type-system §38.3 and the threads guide describe them.\n- Tests: `run-pass/atomic-orderings.cee` checks each method's result and a release/acquire message between the two\n  threads of `Thread.both`, in every C configuration, ThreadSanitizer included, and in the interpreter.\n- **Results.** `benchmarks/atomics` gained `spsc-ordered.cee`, the same ring with acquire/release indices and relaxed\n  slots. On the Ryzen box (as in §1: pinned to CPUs 1 and 2, median of 9, interleaved, load average 1.1–2.9):\n\n  | | clang 18 | gcc 12 |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | The reference, sequentially consistent | 1.663 s | 2.634 s |\n  | **The reference, acquire/release** | **0.366 s** | **0.442 s** |\n  | C, acquire/release through calls | 0.302 s ",
    183 =>
        "| 0.293 s |\n  | C, acquire/release inline | 0.161 s | 0.156 s |\n\n  The reference's ring is 4.5× (clang) and 6.0× (gcc) faster with orderings, within 1.2–1.5× of C's through calls. The rest of\n  the gap to inline C is OD-27.\n- **The interpreter and spinning.** The interpreter switches threads only when one blocks or ends (ADR 0016 §3.8). So a\n  thread that spins on an atomic, waiting for a thread that has not yet run, spins forever under `ceesafec run --interp`.\n  This was already true with sequentially consistent atomics. The test puts its spinning receiver on the new thread\n  for that reason.\n",
    184 =>
        "# ADR 0022 — M14 K2a: generated C in several translation units\n\n> **Inherited.** ADR 0022 was accepted before CeeSafe's fork (Decision 0006), when the language had its\n> earlier name. In this record, \"the reference\" is that language and project and \"the reference compiler\" its compiler:\n> read them as CeeSafe and `ceesafec`. Links to documents CeeSafe did not keep are given as text.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-29, taking every recommendation in §5). The wording is the agent's.\n- Scope: M14 K2a (`milestone-14.md`), the first step of OD-29: the reference compiler stays whole-program, but its C is split so that\n  a C compiler can build it in parallel, and rebuild only what changed.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n- **Today** (c-backend §2.1): `ceesafec build --emit c` writes one `main.c` holding every function of the program,\n  lib/core's included, each with internal linkage (`static`), plus the runtime files (`ceesafe_rt_core.c`, …) and, with\n  `@export`, `ceesafe_exports.h`. Internal linkage is what lets CosmoOS link several separately generated programs (its\n  four ports) into one kernel: each has its own copy of the lib/core functions it uses, and the copies never collide.\n- **The cost at kernel scale** (M14, measured 2026-10-29, Mac M-series): a synthetic 202,004-line package became one\n  1,076,433-line `main.c`. The reference compiler took 13.1 s, and `clang -O2 -c` took 33.8 s and 1.25 GB on one core. A kernel\n  written in the reference would pay both on every change. CosmoOS's C kernel compiles each file separately, in parallel, and\n  only what changed.\n- **What does not change here.** Checking stays whole-program (ADR 0013 D4), and so does the reference compiler's own time. Separate\n  compilation of CeeSafe packages is OD-29's second step, decided later with K2a's measurements.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. Split by source file (proposed) | B. Split into N equal parts | C. One file, as today |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Unit of rebuild | the CeeSafe file that changed, if no other unit's declarations changed | any change can move functions between parts | everything |\n| Determinism | each function's unit is a function of its source file | needs a stable partition | — |\n| Readable | `kernel/net/tcp.cee` → `tcp.c` | no | — |\n\n## 3. Proposed decision: A, behind a switch\n\n### 3.1 The layout\n\n`ceesafec build --emit c --split -o DIR` writes:\n\n- `ceesafe_program.h`, the program's declarations: every type, every function prototype, the runtime's header, and the\n  `@export` prototypes (the content of `ceesafe_exports.h`, which is still written as before);\n- one `<name>.c` per CeeSafe source file, holding the functions declared in it, the closures written in them, and their\n  string literals and source locations. Each includes `ceesafe_program.h`;\n- `ceesafe_instances.c`, holding the instantiations of generic code, lib/core's functions and the drop glue: everything\n  that belongs to no single user file. One file, so that two units never define the same instance;\n- the runtime files, as today.\n\nA unit's name is its source file's name, with the shortest distinguishing directory prefix when two files share a\nname (`net_tcp.c`, `vfs_tcp.c`), so the layout is a pure function of the package's file list.\n\n### 3.2 Linkage and names\n\n- A function called from another unit has external linkage. Every other function stays `static`, as today.\n- **External names carry the program's prefix**, so two programs linked into one image cannot collide: `ceeF_` becomes\n  `ceeF_<program>_`. The program's name is the package's (`ceesafe.toml`), or `--program NAME` for a file build. The\n  prefix also applies to the instances in `ceesafe_instances.c`, which every program has a copy of.\n- Small helpers that the emitter generates per use (`ceeVP_`, `ceeVA_`, the check helpers) become `static inline` in\n  `ceesafe_program.h`, so every unit can inline them. A call across units to a CeeSafe function cannot be inlined without\n  link-time optimisation. §4 says w",
    185 =>
        "hat that may cost.\n- `@export` names are unchanged: they are the C names the user chose.\n\n### 3.3 Determinism\n\nThe output stays a pure function of the inputs: the units, their order, each function's placement and the prefix are\nderived from the sources and the switch, never from hash order or time. A test builds each `emit-c` corpus program\nwith `--split` twice and compares, and builds the result in every C configuration.\n\n### 3.4 Without `--split`\n\nNothing changes: one `main.c`, internal linkage, today's names. CosmoOS's four ports, and every snapshot, stay\nbyte-identical. CosmoOS opts in per program when its build moves to the reference (its roadmap 2.0's SA1).\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- **Parallel and incremental C builds.** The acceptance measures them on the 202,004-line program: `make -j` over\n  the units, and a rebuild after a one-file change. A change to a function's signature changes `ceesafe_program.h`, which\n  every unit includes, so it rebuilds them all. A body-only change rebuilds one unit, plus `ceesafe_instances.c` if it\n  changes an instance.\n- **Performance.** Calls across units are no longer inlinable at `-O2` without LTO. The ports' benchmarks\n  (`benchmarks/lz4`, `fbvalid`, `gpt`) are run with and without `--split` to measure it. Kernel code built with LTO\n  regains it.\n- **Tools.** The debugger helpers (`editors/debug/ceesafe_names.py`) learn the prefix. Source maps (`#line`) are\n  unchanged, because each unit's `#line` directives name its own CeeSafe file.\n- **Not decided here:** separate checking of packages, the rest of OD-29.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. Split by source file, behind `--split`, with one-file output unchanged by default? (Recommended.)\n2. External names prefixed with the program's name (`ceeF_<program>_`)? (Recommended: the alternative, hidden\n   visibility, does not prevent duplicate symbols in a static kernel link.)\n3. Generic instances and lib/core in one `ceesafe_instances.c`? (Recommended; the alternative is one unit per instance.)\n\n## 6. Implementation and results (2026-10-29)\n\n- `ceesafec build --split [--program NAME]` (`CEmitOptions.Split`, `CEmitter.EmitSplit`), specified in c-backend §2.4.\n  The run corpus builds every program split as a C configuration of its own (`clang -O2 split`), with the strict\n  warnings. The debugger helpers read the renamed functions when `CEESAFE_PROGRAM` is set (tooling §3.3).\n- **As §3.2 says, the per-type helpers belong in the header.** A first version put them in `ceesafe_instances.c` with\n  external linkage. Every vector push and drop then became a call across units, and `vector-growth` and\n  `string-building` ran 1.7–1.9× slower. In the header, `static inline`, both run as fast as one file.\n- **Measurements** (Mac M-series, Apple clang 21, `-O2`; the synthetic program of `milestone-14.md` spread over 21\n  CeeSafe files): the reference compiler 14.2 s. The C:\n\n  | Build | Wall | CPU |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | one file, as before | 33.8 s | 33.8 s |\n  | `--split`, `make -j10` | 14.0 s | 80.3 s |\n  | `--split`, one unit changed | 2.4 s | 2.3 s |\n\n  The program printed the same value either way. CPU time grows because every unit parses the 99,484-line header. A\n  smaller header per unit, holding only what that unit uses, would cut it, and is left for when kernel builds show the\n  need.\n- Two `--split` builds of the same sources are byte-identical.\n\n",
    186 =>
        "# ADR 0023 — M14 K4: building a reference kernel where the reference compiler does not run\n\n> **Inherited.** ADR 0023 was accepted before CeeSafe's fork (Decision 0006), when the language had its\n> earlier name. In this record, \"the reference\" is that language and project and \"the reference compiler\" its compiler:\n> read them as CeeSafe and `ceesafec`. Links to documents CeeSafe did not keep are given as text.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-29, taking every recommendation in §5). The wording is the agent's.\n- Scope: M14 K4, OD-31. CosmoOS's roadmap 2.0 makes two choices that conflict once its kernel is the reference. Its rule 7\n  stops committing generated C, so every build runs the reference compiler. Its SW0 makes CosmoOS build itself. The reference compiler does not run on\n  CosmoOS.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n- **The reference compiler** is a .NET program, distributed as NativeAOT binaries for Linux, macOS and Windows on x64 and arm64\n  (`release.md`). NativeAOT needs a .NET runtime port for each operating system, and CosmoOS has none. It runs Linux\n  programs through its personality, but not the .NET runtime's full use of Linux: threads, `mmap` and signal-based GC\n  suspension (inferred; untested).\n- **Today** CosmoOS commits the generated C beside each `.cee` file, so any machine with a C compiler builds the\n  kernel. That is what makes a self-hosted build possible now.\n- **Generated C is a pure function** of the sources and the reference compiler's version (c-backend §1; CosmoOS checks it in CI).\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. Source releases carry the generated C (proposed) | B. The reference compiler on CosmoOS | C. A reference compiler in the reference | D. Keep committing generated C |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Self-hosted build | from a release tarball, no the reference compiler | yes | yes | yes |\n| Changing a `.cee` file on CosmoOS | needs a host with the reference compiler | yes | yes | needs a host with the reference compiler |\n| Cost | a `make dist` target and a CI check | a .NET runtime port, or the Linux personality running .NET | years | the tree doubles as the kernel becomes the reference |\n\n## 3. Proposed decision: A\n\n- The repository holds `.cee` sources only, as roadmap 2.0's rule 7 says. `make dist` (CosmoOS) writes a source\n  tarball with the generated C beside each `.cee` file, made by the pinned the reference compiler. A build from the tarball runs no\n  The reference compiler.\n- CI checks that the tarball builds without the reference compiler on both architectures, and that its C matches a fresh generation\n  (the reproducibility check rule 7 already plans).\n- A self-hosted CosmoOS builds its kernel from a release tarball. Developing the kernel's CeeSafe code on CosmoOS itself\n  needs a machine with the reference compiler until B or C exists. Both stay open as later decisions; C is the long-term direction for a\n  self-hosting the reference.\n- The reference needs nothing new for A: `ceesafec build --emit c` already writes the files, with ADR 0022's `--split` layout when\n  the kernel uses it.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- SW0's self-hosting acceptance builds the kernel from the release tarball.\n- CosmoOS's roadmap 2.0 and `docs/build/ceesafe.md` record the choice (a CosmoOS PR once this is accepted).\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. Option A? (Recommended.)\n2. Record B (the reference compiler on CosmoOS) and C (a reference compiler written in the reference) as open decisions for later? (Recommended.)\n",
    187 =>
        "# ADR 0024 — M14 K1: static items (global state)\n\n> **Inherited.** ADR 0024 was accepted before CeeSafe's fork (Decision 0006), when the language had its\n> earlier name. In this record, \"the reference\" is that language and project and \"the reference compiler\" its compiler:\n> read them as CeeSafe and `ceesafec`. Links to documents CeeSafe did not keep are given as text.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-29), with `static mut` added at the owner's request (§3.7). The\n  wording is the agent's.\n- Scope: M14 K1, OD-28. Global state for a kernel written in the reference (CosmoOS roadmap 2.0's SA2 and SA3), and for hosted\n  programs.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n- **Today** the reference has only compile-time `const` items (type-system §10). `static var count: u64 = 0;` and\n  `var count: u64 = 0;` at top level are N0101. CosmoOS's four CeeSafe ports keep all their state in C, and C7's kernel\n  demonstrations (`integrations/cosmoos/c7`) get every lock from a function argument or a `KernelHeap` box made at run\n  time.\n- **A kernel needs** tables and singletons protected by locks (the handle table, the module list, lockdep's graph),\n  counters, and per-CPU data. CosmoOS keeps per-CPU data in arrays indexed by CPU id (`[CONFIG_MAX_CPUS]`, beside\n  `struct percpu`).\n- **Kernel locks are C state** (R-11, type-system §33). A spinlock may not be zero-initialised: a zeroed\n  `spinlock_t`'s owner is CPU 0. CosmoOS writes a static lock with a C initialiser macro,\n  `SPINLOCK_INIT(name)`, which sets the owner to `SPINLOCK_NO_OWNER`. C7's `SpinLock<T>` instead initialises its lock at\n  run time, in place (`@placed fn init`).\n- **Thread-safety already has a vocabulary.** `Share` (§38.1) says a value may be used from several threads at once.\n  Lock and atomic types are `Share` by the library's `@threadSafe` declaration.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. Immutable statics of `Share` types, built by compile-time constructors (proposed) | B. `static mut` with `unsafe` access (Rust's) | C. Lazily initialised statics (a run-time constructor on first use) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Safe access | `borrow NAME`, like any shared reference; mutation only through the type's own synchronisation | every access is `unsafe` | safe, but needs a once-guard on every access |\n| Data races | impossible: the type is `Share` | the user's argument | impossible |\n| Initialisation | at compile time, as C static initialisers: no constructor runs, no order to get wrong | the same | at run time, in an order the program must not depend on |\n| Kernel fit | yes: a `SpinLock<Table>` in a static, built with `SPINLOCK_INIT` | yes, unsafely | no: a once-guard in interrupt context needs a lock of its own |\n\nA is Rust's `static` with interior mutability, and Linux's `DEFINE_SPINLOCK`. The owner chose A and B together: B\nas `static mut`, every use of which is `unsafe` (§3.7), for the floor and for state whose synchronisation the reference cannot\nexpress. C is not taken.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision: A\n\n### 3.1 The item\n\n```ceesafe\nstatic TABLE: SpinLock<HandleTable> = SpinLock.constant(\"handles\", HandleTable.empty());\nstatic OPENED: AtomicU64 = AtomicU64.constant(0);\n\nfn openOne() -> u64 => OPENED.fetchAdd(1) + 1;\n```\n\n- `static NAME: T = e;` at package level, `public` or not. `T` must be `Share` (§38.1), or it is a new error,\n  N0337: \"a static is shared by every thread\". This also covers a static used only from one thread, because the\n  checker cannot know that.\n- A static is **never assigned or moved** (N0337), and `NAME` in an expression is a place of type `T` that only shared\n  borrows and copies (for a `Copy` `T`) may use. So all mutation goes through `T`'s own methods taking `borrow this`:\n  a lock's guard or an atomic's operation.\n- **Region.** `borrow NAME` has the static region, which outlives every other region. A reference to a static may be\n  stored and returned anywhere a reference may (E1–E4 hold trivially), and passed to another thread when `T` is\n  `Share`.\n- A static is never ",
    188 =>
        "dropped. A `T` with a `drop` is allowed, and its drop never runs: a kernel never exits, and a\n  hosted program's statics end with the process, as in C.\n- Generic statics, and statics inside functions, are not in v1 (N0337).\n\n### 3.2 Compile-time constructors\n\n`e` must be a **static initialiser**, evaluated by the compiler to a C constant initialiser. It may be:\n\n- a constant expression (§10), a string literal (as `*CChar`), or a struct, enum or array literal of static\n  initialisers (fields private to `T` are allowed only inside `T`'s own `@constant` constructor);\n- a call of a `@constant` function: one whose body is a single expression that is a static initialiser of its\n  parameters, the parameters being static initialisers at each call. `@constant` functions are checked as ordinary\n  functions too, so they can be called at run time;\n- a call of a **C constant macro**: an `extern \"C\"` function declared `@constant`, whose name is a C macro that expands\n  to a constant initialiser (`@constant fn SPINLOCK_INIT(name: *CChar) -> spinlock_t;`). The reference trusts the declaration,\n  as it trusts `@effects` on an extern (R-2).\n\nA type whose values are made at their final address (`@placed init`, §33.3) may be in a static only through a\n`@constant` constructor, whose initialiser must leave it as `init` would. For CosmoOS's locks that is the C macro's\njob, so `SpinLock.constant` uses `SPINLOCK_INIT` and does not call `init`.\n\n### 3.3 Per-CPU data is a library type\n\nThere is no `@percpu` in the language. The kernel library (K3) provides `PerCpu<T>`, an array of `T` per CPU behind a\n`Share` type. `PerCpu<T>.local(guard)` lends the calling CPU's element, for as long as a guard that disables\npreemption (or interrupts, for data an interrupt handler shares) is borrowed. Its `unsafe` core argues the rest: the\nguard pins the CPU, so only this CPU's code, and not its interrupts when the guard masks them, uses the element. So:\n\n```ceesafe\nstatic TICKS: PerCpu<u64> = PerCpu.constant(0);\n\n@context(atomic)\nfn tick(preempt: borrow PreemptGuard) { *TICKS.local(preempt) += 1; }\n```\n\nThat keeps the language change to statics, and puts the CPU model where CosmoOS's own API is (OD-30).\n\n### 3.4 C\n\nA static is a C object definition with internal linkage, or external with `@export` (or across ADR 0022's units),\nnamed `ceeS_<name>`. Its initialiser is the evaluated C initialiser: the `@constant` calls are expanded, and the C macros\nare written as calls. It is `const` in C only when `T` contains no C state, no atomic and no lock, so that its methods\ncan write through the pointers they derive, in their own `unsafe` code.\n\n### 3.5 Unsafe access at the floor\n\n`unsafe` code may still take `borrow NAME as *T as *mut T`, as for any shared reference (`unsafe.md`). That is how\nthe library types implement their synchronisation. It is not a `static mut`.\n\n### 3.7 `static mut`\n\n```ceesafe\nstatic mut BOOT_INFO: BootInfo = BootInfo.empty();\n\n/// Safety: runs once on the boot CPU, before any other CPU or interrupt can read BOOT_INFO.\nunsafe fn recordBoot(info: BootInfo) { BOOT_INFO = info; }\n```\n\n- `static mut NAME: T = e;` takes the same static initialisers (§3.2). `T` need not be `Share`.\n- **Every use is an `unsafe` operation**: reading, writing, assigning to a field, borrowing, or taking its address. U1\n  (`unsafe.md`) gains \"use a `static mut`\", so outside `unsafe` code each use is N0280, and a function using one needs\n  its `/// Safety:` comment (N0283).\n- A borrow of a `static mut` has the static region, as for a `static`. Nothing checks that two such borrows do not\n  alias, or that two threads do not race: that is the `/// Safety:` argument (U3's rule for references made from\n  pointers).\n- In C it is a non-`const` object (`ceeS_<name>`), like a static whose type holds C state.\n- The interpreter checks accesses through it as it checks raw pointers: an access out of bounds or after the end of a\n  value is a fault (`UnsafeTests`).\n\n### 3.6 Hosted programs\n\nThe",
    189 =>
        " same `static` works in hosted programs for atomics (`AtomicU64.constant`, in lib/std) and any `@threadSafe` type\nwith a `@constant` constructor. lib/std's `Mutex<T>` allocates its platform lock and so has none in v1. A static mutex\nis a later library change: `PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER` and `SRWLOCK_INIT` are C constant macros.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- **The language:** `static` and `static mut` items, the static region, `@constant` functions and C constant macros.\n  Diagnostics: N0337 for a static that is not `Share`, is assigned or moved, or is generic or local; N0338 for an\n  initialiser that is not a static initialiser; N0280 for a use of a `static mut` outside `unsafe`.\n- **The interpreter** evaluates static initialisers once, before `main`, into its memory. C macros it cannot evaluate\n  (it has no C) fault, so kernel statics are tested natively.\n- **Tests (planned):**\n  - run-pass: atomics and a lock in statics, from two threads (`Thread.both`) under TSan;\n  - check-fail: each N0337/N0338 case;\n  - a CosmoOS kernel object table in a static `SpinLock`, as M14 K1's acceptance says.\n- **`@threadSafe` outside the reference's libraries.** A static's type must be `Share`, and kernel lock types are `Share` only\n  by `@threadSafe`, which today is accepted only in the reference's own library files (N0290). The kernel library (K3, OD-30)\n  needs the same permission. ADR 0025 decides it with the library's home: for example, a package that declares itself\n  a trusted library in its `ceesafe.toml`.\n- **Not decided here:** lazily initialised statics, generic statics.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. Option A: immutable statics of `Share` types, built at compile time? (Recommended.)\n2. `@constant` functions and `@constant` C macros as the way to build them? (Recommended. The alternative is to allow\n   only literals and constants, which cannot build a private-field lock or call `SPINLOCK_INIT`.)\n3. Per-CPU data as the kernel library's `PerCpu<T>`, not a language attribute? (Recommended.)\n\n## 6. Implementation (2026-10-29)\n\nSpecified in type-system §39, grammar `StaticDecl`, diagnostics N0337 and N0338.\n\n- **Uses** need no new borrow rule. A function that uses a static gets a hidden local holding its address, a\n  `borrow(static) T` (`borrow(static) mut T` for a `static mut`) made by `HirStaticAddress`/`NirStaticAddress`, which\n  holds no loans. `NAME` is `*local`. The existing rules then apply: through a shared reference nothing is changed\n  (N0211, N0248) or moved out (N0251), and returned references need a return type that names `static` (N0245\n  otherwise). Diagnostics call the place \"static 'NAME'\". So assigning or moving a static is N0211 or N0251, not N0337\n  as §4 planned. N0337 is for a static whose type is not `Share`, and N0338 for an initialiser.\n- **One exception to `Share`:** a `StringView`, the only reference a static may hold, viewing static string data.\n- **Initialisers** are type-checked before the function bodies, so the instances their `@constant` calls create are\n  checked with them. They are folded after the bodies, by substituting arguments into `@constant` bodies\n  (`TypeChecker.StaticFold`). A `@constant` body is checked once with its parameters symbolic.\n- **C** (`CEmitter.EmitStatics`): `static CEE_UNUSED T ceeS_NAME = { … };`. With `--split`, it is `extern` in the\n  header, defined in `ceesafe_instances.c` and renamed like the functions. A C constant macro gets no prototype and no\n  wrapper. A run-time call of one, in a `@constant` function called at run time, is a compound literal\n  `((T)MACRO(args))`.\n- **Interpreter**: each static is a writable allocation, filled before `main` and not counted as a heap allocation; a\n  C macro in an initialiser is a fault.\n- lib/std's atomics have `@constant` constructors (`AtomicU64.constant`, …).\n- **Tests:**\n  - `run-pass/statics.cee`, in every C configuration (split and TSan included) and the interpreter: atomics changed\n    from two threads, constants, struct, arra",
    190 =>
        "y and `StringView` statics, a `@constant` constructor setting private\n    fields, `static mut`, and references returned as `borrow(static)`;\n  - check-fail `statics.cee`, `static-moves.cee` and `static-initializers.cee`;\n  - `parse-pass/statics.cee`.\n- **Not yet:** `@export` statics, enum values in initialisers, and a C constant macro compiled natively. The macro path\n  is checked and emitted, but its C needs a header defining the macro: M14 K3's kernel library, with CosmoOS's\n  `SPINLOCK_INIT`, is its first real use and test. Per-CPU data (`PerCpu<T>`) is K3 too.\n",
    191 =>
        "# ADR 0025 — M14 K3: the kernel library\n\n> **Inherited.** ADR 0025 was accepted before CeeSafe's fork (Decision 0006), when the language had its\n> earlier name. In this record, \"the reference\" is that language and project and \"the reference compiler\" its compiler:\n> read them as CeeSafe and `ceesafec`. Links to documents CeeSafe did not keep are given as text.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-29), with option **B**, the library in the reference's repository, instead of the\n  recommended A. `trusted` packages (§3.2) and the v1 API (§3.3) were accepted as recommended. §6 records what B\n  changes. The wording is the agent's.\n- Scope: M14 K3, OD-30. The types CosmoOS's roadmap 2.0 needs for SA2 (allocation and object lifetimes) and SA3 (locks,\n  waits and quiescence): where they live, who may declare them thread-safe, and their API.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n- **What exists.** These were written as demonstrations, each with its own C glue and tests:\n\n  | Type | Where | Status |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | `KernelHeap` | C5, `integrations/cosmoos/c5/kernel.cee` | an `Allocator` over `kmalloc`/`kfree` |\n  | `KRef<T>` | C7, `c7/kobject.cee` | a counted kobject whose drop is the release |\n  | `SpinLock<T>`, `IrqSpinLock<T>`, `Mutex<T>` and their guards | C7, `c7/sync.cee` | the data only through the guard; a spin guard is an atomic context |\n  | `Quiesce` | C7, `c7/quiesce.cee` | quiescence guards |\n  | `ObjectCache<T>` | — | not built |\n  | `PerCpu<T>`, `PreemptGuard` | — | not built (ADR 0024 §3.3) |\n\n  Each lock is made at run time, in a `KernelHeap` box (`@placed init`). None can be a static yet.\n- **Statics** (ADR 0024) now let a lock live in a static, built by a `@constant` constructor and a C constant macro.\n  CosmoOS has `SPINLOCK_INIT(name)`. It has no static initialiser for `struct mutex`, only `mutex_init`.\n- **`@threadSafe`**, which makes a type `Send` and `Share` by declaration, is accepted only in the reference's own library files\n  (N0290). A static's type must be `Share`, and a kernel lock is `Share` only by that declaration.\n- **How CosmoOS builds the reference today:** each `.cee` file is compiled alone (`scripts/cee-gen.py`), so files share no the reference\n  types.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. A CeeSafe package in CosmoOS's tree (proposed) | B. A library in the reference's repository (`lib/kernel`) | C. Keep demonstrations |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Owner | CosmoOS: it binds CosmoOS's C API | The reference, but it binds one kernel's API | — |\n| Changes with the kernel | in the same PR as the C it binds | needs a reference release for each change | — |\n| `@threadSafe` | needs a way to trust a package | already allowed | — |\n\n## 3. Proposed decision: A\n\n### 3.1 Home and build\n\n- `kernel/ceesafe/core/` in CosmoOS is a CeeSafe package (`ceesafe.toml`: `name = \"kcore\"`, `kind = \"library\"`,\n  `profile = \"freestanding\"`, `float = false`).\n- CosmoOS's kernel code written in the reference becomes packages that depend on it by path. `cee-gen.py` builds each package\n  with `ceesafec build --emit c --split --program <package>` (ADR 0022), so the kernel's CeeSafe packages share CeeSafe types\n  through `kcore`. Packages replace today's one-file ports as each is touched; the four existing ports keep working as\n  they are.\n\n### 3.2 Trusted packages\n\n- A package may declare in its manifest that it holds `unsafe` synchronisation code: `trusted = true`. In such a\n  package `@threadSafe` is accepted, as in the reference's libraries. Elsewhere it stays N0290. A dependency that is trusted is\n  listed in `ceesafec build`'s output, so a reviewer sees which packages carry the argument.\n- `trusted` permits nothing else: `unsafe` already works in any package, with its `/// Safety:` comments (U2). What it\n  adds is that a type's thread-safety becomes the package's claim.\n\n### 3.3 The API (v1)\n\n- **`KernelHeap`**, from C5, unchanged.\n- **`ObjectCache<T>`**, a typed slab cache over `kmem_cache`. A static or boxed cache made by `ObjectCache<T>",
    192 =>
        ".create(name)`\n  (`kmem_cache_create` with `T`'s size and alignment). `cache.alloc(value)` returns `Result<CacheBox<T>, AllocError>`.\n  A `CacheBox<T>` is one pointer whose drop drops the value and returns the memory to the cache. Because `Box<T, A>`\n  needs a stateless allocator (C5), `CacheBox<T>` is its own type, scoped to the cache it came from.\n- **`SpinLock<T>`, `IrqSpinLock<T>`, `Mutex<T>` and their guards**, from C7, plus `@constant` constructors:\n  `SpinLock.constant(name, data)` with `SPINLOCK_INIT`, and `Mutex.constant(name, data)` with a new `MUTEX_INIT(name)`\n  macro, which CosmoOS adds to `mutex.h` in the same change.\n- **`PreemptGuard`** (preemption off until dropped; an atomic context) and **`IrqGuard`** (interrupts off; also\n  atomic), and **`PerCpu<T>`**: `CONFIG_MAX_CPUS` values, `PerCpu.constant(v)`, and `p.local(borrow guard)`, which\n  lends this CPU's value while the guard is borrowed. Its `unsafe` core is argued once: a guard pins the CPU, and an\n  `IrqGuard` also keeps this CPU's interrupt handlers out.\n- **`KRef<T>`** and **`Quiesce`**, from C7.\n\n### 3.4 Tests\n\nThe C5 and C7 self-tests and stress tests move into CosmoOS as kernel self-tests of `kcore`. They run in every debug\nboot, on both architectures, under lockdep. The reference's `c5.py` and `c7.py` stay as they are, against their own copies, until\n`kcore` replaces them.\n\n### 3.5 What the reference changes\n\n- `trusted = true` in the manifest, and `@threadSafe` in trusted packages (tooling §4, type-system §38.1).\n- Nothing else. The API is CeeSafe code in CosmoOS.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- **Order:**\n  1. The reference: `trusted`, then the 0.2.0 release with statics and `--split`.\n  2. CosmoOS: the pin moves to 0.2.0, `kcore` lands with its self-tests, and `MUTEX_INIT` is added.\n  3. M14 K1's acceptance, a kernel object table in a static `SpinLock`, is then CosmoOS's SA2 proving conversion.\n- `kcore`'s `unsafe` code is a new place unsoundness can hide (R-2, R-13). Its `/// Safety:` arguments go in the\n  safety-audit pack (`safety-audit.md` §3) for the outside audit (K6).\n- **Not decided here:** a stateful `Box` allocator, which would let `ObjectCache` be an `Allocator`, and wait queues and\n  completions (SA3's own design, on top of these types).\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. The library as a CeeSafe package in CosmoOS's tree, `kernel/ceesafe/core/` (\"kcore\")? (Recommended.)\n2. `trusted = true` in `ceesafe.toml` to allow `@threadSafe` there? (Recommended; the alternative is to keep the kernel\n   library's thread-safe types in the reference's repository.)\n3. The v1 API in §3.3, including the new `MUTEX_INIT` macro in CosmoOS? (Recommended.)\n\n## 6. The decision: B, and what it changes\n\nThe owner chose B: the kernel library is part of the reference's repository and ships inside `ceesafec`, like lib/std.\n\n- **`lib/kernel`**, a library for a kernel with CosmoOS's C API. It is not compiled into every freestanding build, as\n  lib/core is, because it names CosmoOS's headers. A build selects it with `kernel = \"cosmoos\"` in `ceesafe.toml`'s\n  `[package]`, or `ceesafec --kernel cosmoos` for a build of files. That is valid only with the freestanding profile\n  (N0320 otherwise). Its names are global in the build, as lib/std's are in a hosted one (§38.5).\n- **`@threadSafe`** is accepted in lib/kernel as in the reference's other libraries. `trusted = true` (§3.2) is still accepted,\n  for packages outside the reference that hold synchronisation code of their own, such as a CosmoOS driver's.\n- **A change to the library is a reference change**, released and then picked up by CosmoOS's pin. §3.1's \"in the same PR as\n  the C it binds\" becomes two changes, the reference's first. `MUTEX_INIT` was to be added to CosmoOS's `mutex.h` (not\n  done; §7).\n- **Testing.**\n  - The reference's suite type-checks programs that use lib/kernel (`--kernel cosmoos`, freestanding) and the misuses it\n    refuses. It does not compile their C, because CI has no CosmoOS headers.\n  -",
    193 =>
        " `integrations/cosmoos/k3.py` builds and runs the library's tests natively against a CosmoOS checkout, as `c7.py`\n    does (its host harness and shims).\n  - Once the pin moves, CosmoOS's own self-tests, in debug boots on both architectures, are the library's acceptance.\n\n## 7. Implementation (2026-10-29)\n\n- **`lib/kernel`** (`heap.cee`, `sync.cee`, `kobject.cee`, `quiesce.cee`), embedded in the reference compiler. It is selected by\n  `--kernel cosmoos`, `kernel = \"cosmoos\"` in `ceesafe.toml`, or `// option: kernel cosmoos` in a corpus file.\n  - From C5 and C7: `KernelHeap`, `SpinLock`/`IrqSpinLock`/`Mutex` and their guards, now `@threadSafe` with `T: Send`;\n    `KRef<T>`; `Quiesce`, `Deferred<T>`, `deferDrop`.\n  - New: the `@constant` constructors (`SpinLock<T>.constant` and `IrqSpinLock<T>.constant`, with `SPINLOCK_INIT`);\n    `PreemptGuard`; `IrqGuard`; and `PerCpu<T: Copy + Send>`, whose `local(IrqGuard)` returns a\n    `PerCpuRef` that owns the guard and lends the value, a second loan on one CPU panicking.\n- **What this needed in the compiler** (type-system §39.4, \"C state at compile time\"):\n  - C state allowed in a static's type, a C constant macro's result and a `@constant` constructor's literal;\n  - `@constant` functions whose result holds C state compile-time only;\n  - each static C string emitted as a `char` array of its own, because CosmoOS builds with `-Wwrite-strings`;\n  - the emitter now counts a box's construction (allocator, `@placed` methods) and a taken function address as\n    reaching library code, which lib/kernel's boxed locks and `KRef` need;\n  - lib/kernel's own composite types are emitted only when used, as lib/std's are.\n- **Trusted packages:** `trusted = true` in `ceesafe.toml` (`PackageInfo.Trusted`) admits `@threadSafe`.\n- **Found in the implementation:**\n  - CosmoOS exports to modules neither `arch_irq_save`/`restore` (they inline to `arch_irq_*_hw`) nor\n    `preempt_enable`. So `IrqGuard`, `PreemptGuard` and `PerCpu` are for code built into the kernel. Locks, kernel\n    objects and the heap work in modules too.\n  - `ObjectCache<T>` is **not built**. Moving a value that is not `Copy` into raw memory has no primitive (`p.write`\n    needs `Copy`, §27.3), so a cache box cannot be filled. It needs a language addition (a move into raw memory, or a\n    stateful `Box` allocator) and is left open. **Resolved by [ADR 0027](0027-m14-static-mutex-and-object-cache.md)**, with a static `Mutex` below.\n  - **No static `Mutex`.** A CosmoOS mutex holds list heads that point to themselves. CosmoOS's static initialisers\n    take the variable's own name for that (`WAITQUEUE_INIT(name)`, `LIST_HEAD_INIT(name)`), and a C constant macro\n    is not given it. So §3.3's `MUTEX_INIT(name)` could not initialise one correctly, and it was not added to\n    CosmoOS. A static mutex needs initialisers that know their own address (a §39 extension), or a lock made at\n    start-up. It is left open. Static spinlocks are not affected: `SPINLOCK_INIT` has no self-pointers.\n- **Tests:**\n  - The reference's suite: `check-pass/kernel-library.cee`, `check-fail/kernel-library.cee` (sleeping under a static spin\n    guard, with interrupts off), `check-fail/kernel-library-constant.cee` (N0338), and `KernelLibraryTests` (trusted\n    packages, the manifest keys, the library absent unless selected).\n  - `integrations/cosmoos/k3.py` (2026-10-29, both architectures, one file and split): the module subset\n    (`k3/module.cee`) meets C4's module checks against CosmoOS's headers and exports. The whole library compiles as\n    kernel code; its only kernel-internal references are `arch_irq_save_hw`/`arch_irq_restore_hw`.\n  - **In a booted kernel** (2026-10-29, CosmoOS #489): the self-test `cee-kcore` (CosmoOS\n    `kernel/ceesafe/kcore_test.cee`, built with the reference compiler 0.2.0) uses every type but `ObjectCache` as code built into the\n    kernel: a table under a static `SpinLock`, a static `IrqSpinLock`, a `PerCpu` counter, boxed locks, a `KRef`'s",
    194 =>
        "\n    clone and release, a grace period and a deferred free. It passes in debug boots (lockdep on) on AArch64 and\n    x86-64. It needed CosmoOS's lockdep class table raised from 512 to 640: main was at about 500, and the test's\n    classes overflowed it later in an x86-64 boot.\n  - **Static analysis.** CosmoOS's analyzer gate (`make analyze`, clang `--analyze`) reports \"Potential memory leak\"\n    for each `Box.create` in a `KernelHeap`. They are false: each box frees its block on drop. CosmoOS #489 baselines\n    the four the test makes. The cause, reduced in plain C (2026-10-29, Apple clang 21): `KernelHeap.allocate`\n    returns `Ok(p)`, which the emitter writes as a compound literal with a union member (`(R){ .tag = 0,\n    .u.v_Ok = { .f__0 = p } }`), and the analyzer loses the pointer read back from that union. The same value built\n    by member stores (`r.u.v_Ok.f__0 = p; return r;`) is tracked, and so is a plain struct.\n  - **Fixed in the emitter** (2026-10-29, after 0.2.0; c-backend §2): a variant whose payload may hold a pointer is\n    built by a `static inline` constructor of member stores (`ceeMk_<E>_v_<variant>`); scalar payloads keep the\n    compound literal. CosmoOS's `kcore_test.cee` analyzes clean on both architectures with it (4 reports with\n    0.2.0). A first version used constructors for every payload: gcc then ran `string-building` 1.8× slower, with\n    a constructor only on its cold growth path (inferred: inlining). With constructors for pointer payloads only,\n    the suite on the Ryzen box (the reference, both compilers, interleaved, two rounds) moves within its noise (±6% where the\n    C did not change at all), except memory-copy under clang, 1.10×, whose only change runs once outside the loop\n    and which is 1.03× with `-falign-loops=32` (code placement, inferred). CosmoOS's baseline entries can go once\n    its pin moves past 0.2.0.\n\n",
    195 =>
        "# ADR 0026 — M14 K5: inline atomics, and atomics in every profile\n\n> **Inherited.** ADR 0026 was accepted before CeeSafe's fork (Decision 0006), when the language had its\n> earlier name. In this record, \"the reference\" is that language and project and \"the reference compiler\" its compiler:\n> read them as CeeSafe and `ceesafec`. Links to documents CeeSafe did not keep are given as text.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-29), both questions as recommended (§5). The wording is the agent's.\n- Scope: OD-27 (M14 K5's first item). How the atomic types' operations reach the machine, and which profiles have\n  them.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n- **Today** (ADR 0016, ADR 0021): `AtomicU64`, `AtomicU32`, `AtomicUSize`, `AtomicBool` and `Ordering` are in\n  `lib/std`, a hosted-only library. Each operation calls a runtime function in `runtime/hosted/ceesafe_rt_thread.c`\n  (`ceesafe_atomic_add_u64`, `ceesafe_atomic_load_ord_u32`, …). The ordered ones take the ordering as an `int` and switch\n  on it.\n- **The cost of the call** (ADR 0021 §1, `benchmarks/atomics`, Ryzen box, x86-64): the ring with acquire/release takes\n  0.289 s through the runtime calls and 0.158 s written in C with inline `__atomic_*`, a further 1.8×. Sequentially\n  consistent inline C was no faster than the calls (2.761 s against 2.170 s), because the fence dominates.\n- **The kernel has no the reference atomics.** `lib/std` is not in a freestanding build, and `lib/kernel` (ADR 0025) has none.\n  Roadmap 2.0's SA track converts CosmoOS's C, which uses the GCC builtins directly: 1,233 `__atomic_*` calls in 98\n  files (`__atomic_load_n` 593, `__atomic_store_n` 393, `__atomic_fetch_add` 141, …; counted 2026-10-29 in\n  `kernel/` and `drivers/`). A converted subsystem needs atomics of its own.\n- **No library calls.** CosmoOS builds AArch64 code with `-mno-outline-atomics`, and so does the reference's freestanding\n  profile (`profiles.md` §4.3). With it, gcc and clang expand every 8-, 16-, 32- and 64-bit `__atomic_*` on\n  both architectures inline, with no `libatomic` or `__aarch64_*` helper (inferred from CosmoOS's own use; checked\n  per architecture by the implementation, below). MSVC has the `_Interlocked*` intrinsics, already used by the\n  runtime.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. Keep the calls | B. Inline in the runtime header | C. Built-in operations in the emitter |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| How | as today | the `ceesafe_atomic_*` functions become `static inline` in `rt.h`; the ordering switch folds away where the ordering is a constant | the emitter writes `__atomic_*` with the ordering constant at each call site |\n| Compiler change | none | none: the runtime and its header only | a new intrinsic kind in NIR and the emitter |\n| An ordering not known at compile time | works | works: the switch stays | needs a fallback call |\n| `-O0` | a call | a call to the inline function, with its switch | inline |\n| Gain at `-O2` | — | as C (expected; to be measured) | as C |\n\nB gives what C gives at `-O2`, where the ordering at a call site is a constant (it is in every `…With` call written\nwith an enum constant, after inlining), with no change to the compiler.\n\n## 3. Decision\n\n1. **B: inline atomics in the runtime header.** `rt.h` defines the `ceesafe_atomic_*` functions as `static inline`, with\n   `__atomic_*` (gcc and clang) or `_Interlocked*` (MSVC), and `ceesafe_rt_thread.c` loses them. Nothing else in the\n   generated C changes.\n2. **Atomics in every profile.** `AtomicU64`, `AtomicU32`, `AtomicUSize`, `AtomicBool` and `Ordering` move from\n   `lib/std` to `lib/core`, so freestanding and kernel code has them, with the same API (type-system §38.3). Their\n   `Send`/`Share` argument (`@threadSafe`) is unchanged. `Arc`, `Mutex` and `Thread` stay in `lib/std`: they need\n   the platform's threads and allocator.\n3. **The freestanding profile's rule is checked.** A test compiles a program using every atomic operation with the\n   freestanding profile's flags for both CosmoOS architectures and ",
    196 =>
        "requires no undefined symbol (as C4's checks do,\n   `c4.py`).\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- `lib/core`'s names are reserved in every package: a freestanding package that declares its own `AtomicU64` stops\n  compiling (none of CosmoOS's four ports does).\n- The kernel's CeeSafe code uses the same atomics as userland CeeSafe code, compiled as CosmoOS's C compiles its own.\n  `lib/kernel`'s future reference counts and flags use them.\n- `benchmarks/atomics` is rerun on the Ryzen box after the change: the reference acquire/release row against the C inline\n  row, and the sequentially consistent rows, under clang and gcc. The result goes into §6 and `docs/benchmarks.md`.\n  If the gap to C does not close, C (built-in operations) is reconsidered.\n- **Not decided here:** atomics on pointers and on 8- and 16-bit integers (add as converted code needs them), and\n  fences as a library function (`atomicFence(order)`), which CosmoOS's C uses 22 times.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. Inline atomics through the runtime header (B)? (Recommended.)\n2. Move the atomic types and `Ordering` to `lib/core`, so kernel code has them? (Recommended.)\n\n## 6. Implementation and results (2026-10-29)\n\n- `ceesafe/rt.h` defines the `ceesafe_atomic_*` functions as `static inline`: `__atomic_*` with constant orderings in a\n  switch (gcc, clang), `_Interlocked*` intrinsics (MSVC). `ceesafe_rt_thread.c` no longer has them, and a program that\n  uses only atomics no longer links it. The interpreter is unchanged (`CLibrary.cs`).\n- `lib/core/atomics.cee` has `AtomicU64`, `AtomicU32`, `AtomicUSize`, `AtomicBool`, `LoadOrdering`, `StoreOrdering`\n  and `Ordering`, moved from `lib/std/sync.cee` unchanged (doc comments added where lib/core requires them).\n  Type-system §38.3 and §38.5 say so.\n- The atomics' `Result` types would have been emitted in every program, because a generic instance made by lib/core's\n  own code counted as the program's. Now an instance made only by a library's code (lib/core, as lib/std and\n  lib/kernel already were) is emitted only where the program uses it. The `emit-c` snapshots did not change.\n- **Freestanding** (§3.3): `integrations/cosmoos/k3/atomics.cee` calls every operation of every type, with every\n  ordering kind, from statics. `k3.py` compiles it with CosmoOS's module flags on both architectures, one file and\n  split: the only undefined symbol is the kernel's `panic`, so no operation calls a helper. On AArch64 the object\n  holds the expected instructions: `ldaxr`/`stlxr` for acquire-release and sequentially consistent read-modify-writes,\n  `ldxr`/`stxr` for relaxed ones, `ldar` and `stlr` for loads and stores.\n- **Results.** `benchmarks/atomics` on the Ryzen box (as ADR 0021 §6: pinned to CPUs 1 and 2, median of 9,\n  interleaved; load average 0.7–2.7):\n\n  | | clang 18 | gcc 12 |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | The reference, acquire/release, through calls (ADR 0021 §6) | 0.366 s | 0.442 s |\n  | **The reference, acquire/release, inline** | **0.215 s** | **0.195 s** |\n  | C, acquire/release through calls | 0.296 s | 0.297 s |\n  | C, acquire/release inline | 0.160 s | 0.157 s |\n  | The reference, sequentially consistent | 2.508 s | 2.584 s |\n  | C, sequentially consistent inline | 2.816 s | 2.116 s |\n\n  The ordered ring is 1.7× (clang) and 2.3× (gcc) faster than through calls, and within 1.34× and 1.24× of inline C.\n  The reference's ring is now faster than C's through calls. Sequentially consistent rings are dominated by their fences, as\n  before, and vary by tens of percent between runs. Option C (built-in operations) is not needed for this gap: what\n  remains is the ring's own code (inferred: the indexing and its checks around the atomics), not the atomics.\n\n",
    197 =>
        "# ADR 0027 — M14: a static `Mutex`, and `ObjectCache<T>`\n\n> **Inherited.** ADR 0027 was accepted before CeeSafe's fork (Decision 0006), when the language had its\n> earlier name. In this record, \"the reference\" is that language and project and \"the reference compiler\" its compiler:\n> read them as CeeSafe and `ceesafec`. Links to documents CeeSafe did not keep are given as text.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-29), both questions as recommended (§5). The wording is the agent's.\n- Scope: the two parts of ADR 0025's v1 API (§3.3) that §7 left open: a `Mutex<T>` in a `static` item, and\n  `ObjectCache<T>`, a typed slab cache. Both need a language addition. CosmoOS's roadmap 2.0 needs both for SA2\n  (object caches) and SA3 (locks and waits in statics).\n\n## 1. Context\n\n### 1.1 A static mutex\n\n- **The C.** `struct mutex` (CosmoOS `kernel/include/kernel/mutex.h:20`) holds a spinlock, an owner, a\n  `struct waitqueue`, a name, a lockdep class, `pi_active`, a `struct list_node pi_link` and `handoff`. `mutex_init`\n  (`kernel/scheduler/mutex.c:13`) makes `pi_link` and the wait queue's two lists point to themselves\n  (`list_init`, `waitqueue_init`).\n- **CosmoOS's own static initialisers** for such state take the object's own name: `LIST_HEAD_INIT(name)` is\n  `{ &(name), &(name) }` and `WAITQUEUE_INIT(name)` uses `(name).waiters` (`list.h:21`, `wait.h:58`). There is no\n  `MUTEX_INIT`.\n- **The reference's statics** (type-system §39.4) build C state only through a C constant macro whose arguments are static\n  initialisers. None of those can name the static being defined, so a self-pointing list cannot be written. Static\n  spin locks work because `SPINLOCK_INIT` has no self-pointers (ADR 0025 §7).\n- **SA3 needs more of the same:** wait queues, completions and list heads in statics all point to themselves.\n\n### 1.2 An object cache\n\n- **The C.** `kmem_cache_create(name, object_size, align)` allocates and returns a cache, or NULL;\n  `kmem_cache_alloc(cache, flags)` and `kmem_cache_free(cache, obj)` (`kernel/include/kernel/kmalloc.h:47-53`).\n  CosmoOS creates its caches at start-up into globals (`kernel-services/network/mbuf.c:26-27`, `kmalloc.c:43`).\n- **What the reference lacks:**\n  - **Moving a value into raw memory.** `p.write(v)` and `p.read()` are defined only for `Copy` types (§27.3, N0282).\n    A cache's objects are generally not `Copy` (they own boxes, or have a `drop`), so a cache box cannot be filled\n    or emptied.\n  - **A type's size and alignment as a value.** `kmem_cache_create` needs them. The compiler knows them\n    (`TargetInfo`), and `Box` passes them to an allocator as a `Layout`, but CeeSafe code cannot ask for one.\n- **Why not `Box<T, A>`:** a box's allocator is stateless (C5, §30), and a cache is state. A stateful allocator would\n  make every box carry its allocator: two pointers instead of one, through placement, niche optionals and drop glue.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n### 2.1 Static mutex\n\n| | A. `here`: a constant initialiser may take the address it initialises | B. Lazy: a `StaticMutex<T>` that runs `mutex_init` at its first lock | C. None: mutexes made at start-up |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Language | one new static-initialiser form | none | none |\n| CosmoOS | a `MUTEX_INIT_AT(p, name)` macro in `mutex.h` | none | none |\n| Run-time cost | none | an acquire load per lock, and a spinlock for the first | an allocation and a pointer per lock |\n| Also serves | wait queues, completions, list heads (SA3) | mutexes only | — |\n\n### 2.2 Object cache\n\n| | A. Moves through raw pointers, `Layout.of<T>()`, and a library cache | B. Stateful `Box` allocators | C. Wait |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Language | lift `Copy` from `read`/`write` (unsafe); one built-in | `Box<T, A>` carries `A`'s state | — |\n| Size of a cache box | two pointers (object, cache) | two pointers for every box with a stateful allocator | — |\n| Reach | any unsafe container code (rings, intrusive lists) | caches and other stateful allocators | — |\n\n## 3. Deci",
    198 =>
        "sion\n\n### 3.1 `here` (option A for the mutex)\n\n- **`here`** is a static initialiser (§39.4) valid only as an argument of a C constant macro whose result\n  initialises a place. Its value is that place's address, and its type is `*mut T` for the place's type `T`. In\n  ```ceesafe\n  @constant\n  public fn constant(name: *CChar, data: T) -> Mutex<T> => Mutex { raw: MUTEX_INIT_AT(here, name), data: data };\n  ```\n  `here` is the address of the field `raw` in whichever static the constructor builds. Through nested `@constant`\n  constructors the place is the full path (`&ceeS_TABLE.f_lock.f_raw`).\n- **C.** It lowers to an address constant (C11 §6.6p9: `&`, `.`, `->` and `*` may form one, and no object is read),\n  so the macro may build self-pointers from it: `LIST_HEAD_INIT((p)->wq.waiters)` expands to\n  `{ &((p)->wq.waiters), &((p)->wq.waiters) }`.\n- **Rules.** `here` elsewhere is N0338. A `@constant` function that uses it is compile-time only (§39.4, as for C\n  state), so it is never called at run time, where there is no fixed place. The interpreter cannot evaluate C\n  constant macros already (§39.5), so nothing changes there.\n- **lib/kernel.** `Mutex<T>.constant(name, data)` with CosmoOS's new macro:\n  ```c\n  /* A mutex in static storage, as mutex_init leaves one; p is its own address. */\n  #define MUTEX_INIT_AT(p, n) { .lock = SPINLOCK_INIT(n), .owner = NULL, \\\n      .wq = { .lock = SPINLOCK_INIT(n), .waiters = LIST_HEAD_INIT((p)->wq.waiters), \\\n              .callbacks = LIST_HEAD_INIT((p)->wq.callbacks) }, \\\n      .name = (n), .class = 0, .pi_active = 0, .pi_link = LIST_HEAD_INIT((p)->pi_link), .handoff = NULL }\n  ```\n  A CosmoOS self-test compares a static mutex made by it with one made by `mutex_init`, member by member, and locks\n  it under lockdep. A change to `struct mutex` must keep the two equal, as for `SPINLOCK_INIT`.\n\n### 3.2 Moves through raw pointers, `Layout.of<T>()`, `ObjectCache<T>` (option A for the cache)\n\n- **`p.write(v)` and `p.read()` for any `T`** (§27.3; unsafe, as now):\n  - `p.write(v)` moves `v` to `p`, without reading or destroying what was there, for `p: *mut T`. A `Copy` `v` is\n    copied, as now. For a type with a `@placed init` (§33.3) the store is followed by `init` at `p`, as\n    `Box.create` does.\n  - `p.read()` moves the `T` at `p` out. The memory then holds no value: the unsafe code must not read it again,\n    drop it or let anything else do so before writing it (U-rules, `unsafe.md`). For `Copy` `T`, as now. For a type\n    with C state (§33), `read` is N0282: C state never moves.\n- **`Layout.of<T>()`**, a `@constant` built-in: `T`'s size and alignment from `TargetInfo` (in C,\n  `sizeof`/`_Alignof` of its C type). It is a static initialiser too.\n- **lib/kernel** (ADR 0025's API, §3.3 there):\n  - `ObjectCache<T: Send>`, `@threadSafe`: `constant()` (empty, for a static), `setUp(name) -> Result<void,\n    AllocError>` (once, at start-up: `kmem_cache_create(name, Layout.of<T>().size, Layout.of<T>().align)`, stored\n    atomically), and `alloc(value) -> Result<CacheBox<T>, AllocError>` (`kmem_cache_alloc`, then `p.write(value)`;\n    on failure the value is dropped, as `Box.create` does). `alloc` before `setUp` is `Err(OutOfMemory)`.\n  - `CacheBox<T>`, `@noncopy`, two pointers: `get`, `getMut`, and a `drop` that reads the value out, drops it and\n    returns the memory (`kmem_cache_free`). It is `Send` when `T` is.\n  - A cache in a box, not a static, would let its boxes outlive it. So v1 has static caches only, the form CosmoOS\n    uses, and a cache is never destroyed.\n\n### 3.3 What changes elsewhere\n\n- Type-system §27.3 (`read`/`write`), §30.1 (`Layout.of<T>()` beside `Layout`), §39.4 (`here`, and `Layout.of` as\n  a static initialiser), `unsafe.md` (the moved-out state) and ownership.md's U-rules.\n- Diagnostics: no new codes. `here` misused is N0338, and `read` of C state is N0282.\n- Tests: corpus check-fail for `here` outside a macro argument, a run-time call of a function using it, and `read`\n",
    199 =>
        "  of C state; run-pass for `read`/`write` of a type with a drop (each dropped once, under ASan and the interpreter);\n  `k3.py` for a static `Mutex` and an `ObjectCache`; CosmoOS self-tests `cee-kcore` extended (in a later pin bump).\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- Static mutexes, and later static wait queues and completions, cost nothing at run time and are made as CosmoOS's\n  own C makes them, with lockdep's classes from their names.\n- `read` and `write` of non-`Copy` values are new ways for unsafe code to duplicate or leak ownership. They are\n  unsafe, and each use needs its `/// Safety:` argument (U2). The safety-audit pack (`safety-audit.md`) gains them\n  for K6.\n- `ObjectCache<T>` objects are not `Box`es: code that takes a `Box<T, A>` does not take a `CacheBox<T>`.\n- **Not decided here:** destroying a cache (needs boxes scoped to their cache), stateful `Box` allocators, and\n  per-CPU object caches.\n\n## 6. Implementation (2026-10-29)\n\n- **Moves through raw pointers** (type-system §27.3, `unsafe.md` U2): `p.read()` and `p.write(v)` take any `T`; the\n  volatile forms still copy. The NIR move checker lets a value leave `*p` for a raw `p` (`NirPlace.IsRawDeref`), the\n  drop elaborator does not drop the old contents of `*p`, and a write of a type with `@placed` methods is followed\n  by `PtrPlace`, emitted as its `ceePL_` helper (the in-place checker accepts the literal there, as for `Box.create`).\n  `p.read()` of a CeeSafe struct holding C state is N0282.\n- **`layoutOf<T>()`** (§30.1), not `Layout.of<T>()`: the reference has no type arguments on a member call, so it is a function,\n  like `truncate<T>`. Folded from `TargetInfo` to a `Layout` literal; for a C-laid-out `T`, C's `sizeof`/`_Alignof`.\n- **`here`** (§39.4–§39.5): typed by the macro parameter it is given to, checked against the macro's result type,\n  emitted as the address of the place being initialised. lib/kernel's `Mutex<T>.constant` uses CosmoOS's\n  `MUTEX_INIT_AT`, which `integrations/cosmoos/k3/mutex_init_at.h` supplies to `k3.py` until CosmoOS's `mutex.h`\n  defines it.\n- **`ObjectCache<T>`** and **`CacheBox<T>`** (`lib/kernel/cache.cee`) as §3.2 says, with two differences found in\n  the implementation:\n  - `T` holds no C state in v1: a cache box moves its value out to drop it, and C state never moves. A drop in place\n    would lift this.\n  - `CacheBox<T>` is not `Send`, like a `KernelHeap` box: raw pointers are not `Send` (§38.1), and a `@threadSafe`\n    declaration would claim `Share` for any `T`.\n- **Tests:**\n  - run-pass `raw-moves.cee` and `layout-of.cee`, every C configuration and the interpreter;\n  - check-fail `raw-moves-c-state.cee`, `layout-of.cee` and `static-here.cee`; the changed\n    `raw-pointer-types.cee`, whose non-`Copy` `read`/`write` are now valid;\n  - emit-c `static-here.cee`: `MUTEX_INIT_AT((&ceeS_TABLE.f_raw), …)`;\n  - `k3.py` (`k3/statics.cee`: a static `Mutex` and an `ObjectCache`), both architectures, one file and split.\n- **Found in a boot** (2026-10-29, the reference compiler 0.3.0, CosmoOS branch `ceesafec-0.3.0`): `MUTEX_INIT_AT` matches `mutex_init`\n  member by member, but `ObjectCache.setUp` failed for `T` aligned below 16: CosmoOS's `kmem_cache_create` refuses an\n  alignment below `KMALLOC_MIN_ALIGN` (`slab.c`, `cache_setup`), and asks for that minimum with 0. lib/kernel now\n  passes 0 for alignments up to 16; `cee-kcore` then passes. `k3.py` only compiles, so it could not see this.\n- **Not yet run (superseded by the above):** a static mutex compared with `mutex_init`'s, and the cache, in a booted kernel. That is CosmoOS's\n  side, once a the reference compiler release carries this: `MUTEX_INIT_AT` in `mutex.h` and `cee-kcore` extended.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. A static mutex through `here` and a CosmoOS `MUTEX_INIT_AT` macro (A)? (Recommended; B needs no language change but\n   costs a check per lock and covers mutexes only.)\n2. Lift `Copy` from unsafe `p.read()`/`p.write(v)` and add `Layout.of<T>()`, for a lib/kernel `Ob",
    200 => "jectCache<T>` with\n   static caches only (A)? (Recommended.)\n",
    201 =>
        "# ADR 0028 — M15 S7: the host services a compiler needs\n\n> **Inherited.** ADR 0028 was accepted before CeeSafe's fork (Decision 0006), when the language had its\n> earlier name. In this record, \"the reference\" is that language and project and \"the reference compiler\" its compiler:\n> read them as CeeSafe and `ceesafec`. Links to documents CeeSafe did not keep are given as text.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-30), every question as recommended (§5). The wording is the agent's. Implemented the same day (§6).\n- Scope: M15 S7 (OD-35). These are the operating-system services `ceesafec build` and its package commands use, which\n  The reference's hosted library (type-system §26) lacks: a compiler written in the reference (OD-33) cannot do without them.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nThe services the reference compiler itself uses (an audit of `src/`, 2026-10-30):\n\n| Service | Where the reference compiler uses it | The reference today (§26) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Run a program and capture its stdout and stderr, optionally with stdin | the C compiler and linker (`Backend.C/CBackend.cs:252-290`); tool discovery (`CCompilers.cs:165`) | `Process.run`: inherited stdio, exit status only |\n| Read an environment variable | `CC`, `PATH`, `CEESAFE_HOME`, `CEESAFE_INDEX`, `INCLUDE` | none |\n| Create and list directories | `-o DIR` for `--emit c` (`Program.cs:586`, `:603`, `CBackend.cs:42`); `src/` of a package (`Manifest.cs:271`) | none |\n| Whether a path exists, and what it is | manifests, the cache, `fmt` on a directory | none (`File.open` and look at the error) |\n| A fresh temporary directory, removed afterwards | every `build` (`Program.cs:614`) | none |\n| The working directory | package lookup (`Manifest.FindDirectory`), source paths (`SourceText.SourcePath`) | none |\n\nThe CeeSafe port (P1, `host-process` probe) confirmed the rest works: writing a C file, running `cc` with an argument\nvector, its exit status, and reading the object back.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. Built-in library API in `lib/std` and §26 (proposed) | B. C adapters in each program | C. A generic `extern \"C\"` POSIX binding |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Safety | safe API; the runtime owns the unsafe parts | every program writes unsafe FFI | unsafe everywhere, and POSIX only |\n| Portability | the runtime selects the platform (macOS, Linux, Windows, CosmoOS), as `Process.run` does (§26.6) | per program | none on Windows or CosmoOS |\n| Interpreter | modelled, and refused in the sandbox | runs no C (§28.6) | runs no C |\n\n## 3. Proposed decision: A\n\nHosted-only functions (N0293 in freestanding code), blocking, with errors as `IoError`:\n\n- `Process.capture(program, arguments, input) -> Result<ProcessOutput, IoError>`.\n  - `program: StringView` is found on `PATH`; `arguments: borrow Vector<StringView>`; `input: Slice<u8>?` is written to\n    the child's stdin, which is then closed (`null`: an empty stdin).\n  - `ProcessOutput` is a built-in struct with `status: i32`, as `Process.run` reports it, plus `stdout: Vector<u8>`\n    and `stderr: Vector<u8>`.\n  - The runtime reads both pipes and writes stdin concurrently (`poll` on POSIX, overlapped pipes on Windows), so a\n    child that fills one pipe cannot deadlock it.\n  - Failures: allocation is `OutOfMemory`; the program not found is `NotFound`; arguments as for `Process.run`.\n- `Environment.variable(name: StringView) -> StringView?`: the value as static text, like `Process.argument` (§26.1).\n  It is `null` if absent or not valid UTF-8. The reference has no way to set a variable, so the text never changes under the\n  program.\n- `Directory.create(path) -> Result<void, IoError>` (one level; `AlreadyExists` if it exists) and\n  `Directory.remove(path)` (an empty directory).\n- `Directory.list(path) -> Result<Vector<String>, IoError>`: the entries' names, without `.` and `..`, **sorted by\n  byte value**, so programs stay deterministic (c-backend §1's rule applied to I/O).\n- `Path.kind(path) -> Result<PathKind?, IoError>`: `File`, `Directory` or `Othe",
    202 =>
        "r`, or `null` if nothing is there. It\n  follows symbolic links.\n- `Directory.temporary() -> Result<String, IoError>`: a new, empty, private directory, created atomically (`mkdtemp`)\n  under the platform's temporary directory. The caller removes it.\n- `Directory.current() -> Result<String, IoError>`.\n\nPaths are `StringView`s, under the same rules as `File.open`: zero bytes and paths over 4,095 bytes are `InvalidInput`.\nThe interpreter models these with .NET's APIs on macOS and Linux, and the sandbox (`ceesafec mcp`, `ceesafe_run`) refuses\nthem all, as it refuses processes today.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- Spec: type-system §26.7–§26.10, diagnostics (none new expected), and the runtime in `runtime/hosted/ceesafe_rt_io.c`\n  (POSIX and Windows) with CosmoOS's variants selected at compile time.\n- Tests:\n  - run-pass and run-fail programs for each function (a child writing more than a pipe's buffer to both streams;\n    absent variables; sorted listing; temporary-directory cleanup);\n  - `InterpreterTests` and `McpTests` for the sandbox;\n  - the MSVC configuration for Windows;\n  - `c3.py` in a CosmoOS guest for the spawn path.\n- A compiler written in the reference (the CeeSafe port) can then implement `build` and package mode without C adapters.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. Option A, with this API? (Recommended.)\n2. `Environment.variable` as a `StringView?` of static text, rather than a copied `String`? (Recommended: it matches\n   `Process.argument`, and nothing can change the environment.)\n3. `Directory.list`: should a name that is not valid UTF-8 make the call fail with `InvalidInput`, or be skipped?\n   (Recommended: fail. Skipping could make a compiler silently miss a source file.)\n4. CosmoOS: implement `Process.capture` there in 0.4.0 (its spawn API needs pipe redirection; to be audited in\n   CosmoOS's code), or report `Other` until a later release? (Recommended: audit first, and decide with the evidence.)\n5. Windows: full parity in the same release? (Recommended: yes. The reference compiler's own Windows builds depend on these services.)\n\n## 6. Implementation (2026-10-30)\n\n- **Q4 (CosmoOS), from the audit.** CosmoOS's libc (`libc/include/spawn.h`, `unistd.h`, `dirent.h`, `sys/stat.h`,\n  `stdlib.h`) has `spawnvp` with `spawn_handle` mappings, plus `pipe`, `getenv`, `getcwd`, `opendir`/`readdir`, `stat`,\n  `mkdir` and `rmdir`, but no `poll` and no `mkdtemp`. So `Process.capture` is implemented there in this release.\n  On every platform it routes the child's streams through files in a private temporary directory, not pipes, so\n  nothing needs `poll` or threads to avoid a deadlock. The temporary directory is made by trying names until `mkdir`\n  succeeds, which is atomic on every platform.\n- **One name changed.** `Path.kind` is `File.kind`. A lib/std `Path` would break programs that declare their own:\n  lib/std names cannot be redeclared in a hosted package (§38.5), and the corpus's `run-pass/struct-nested.cee` has\n  one. `Environment`, `Directory`, `PathKind`, `ProcessOutput` and `HostServices` matched nothing in the repository,\n  CeeSafe or the site's guide.\n- **Where it lives:**\n  - `lib/std/host.cee`, with its unsafe code behind documented contracts;\n  - ten `ceesafe_host_*` functions in `runtime/hosted/ceesafe_rt_io.c`, for POSIX, CosmoOS and Windows, declared in `rt.h`;\n  - two checker hooks, so `Process.capture` and `File.kind` call lib/std's `HostServices`;\n  - the interpreter's models in `Interpreter/HostServices.cs`.\n- **Tests:**\n  - `run-pass/host-services.cee`, run in every C configuration and in the interpreter;\n  - `check-fail/host-services.cee`;\n  - `HostServicesTests`, where capture runs natively and in the interpreter with a child writing 300,000 bytes to\n    each stream, and the sandbox refuses.\n- **CosmoOS, in a guest.** `c3.py --ssh` builds `integrations/cosmoos/c3/c3-host.cee` for CosmoOS and runs it.\n  The first run found that CosmoOS's `S_ISDIR`/`S_ISREG` test the stat record's `st_type` (a `DT_*` value),",
    203 =>
        " not\n  `st_mode` (its `sys/stat.h`). The runtime selects `st_type` there.\n- **Not yet run:** the Windows `CreateProcess` path. The CI's Windows job builds and runs the run corpus with MSVC,\n  which covers everything but capture; `HostServicesTests` covers capture, but not on Windows.\n\n",
    204 =>
        "# ADR 0029 — M14: exported structs that hold C state\n\n> **Inherited.** ADR 0029 was accepted before CeeSafe's fork (Decision 0006), when the language had its\n> earlier name. In this record, \"the reference\" is that language and project and \"the reference compiler\" its compiler:\n> read them as CeeSafe and `ceesafec`. Links to documents CeeSafe did not keep are given as text.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-30), both questions as recommended (§5): option A, with arrays in this ADR. The wording is the agent's.\n- Scope: a struct defined in the reference and used by C by value, whose fields include C state (a `spinlock_t`) and fixed\n  arrays. CosmoOS's roadmap 2.0 SA2 needs it for the handle table; the owner chose the reference-owned types for SA2\n  (2026-10-30), and SA3 and later conversions need it for every C struct that embeds a lock.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n### 1.1 The handle table\n\n- **The C.** `struct handle_table` (CosmoOS `kernel/include/kernel/handle.h:86`) is a `spinlock_t`, an array of 64\n  `struct handle_entry { struct kobject *obj; unsigned rights; unsigned flags; }`, two counts and a `bool`.\n  `handle.c` (266 lines) is its only code apart from three writes of `limit` in `process.c` (`:965`, `:1211`,\n  `:2415`).\n- **C makes the values.** The table is embedded by value in `struct process` (`process.h:180`), and self-tests put\n  it in statics and on the stack (`epolltest.c:112`, `:447`, `:530`, `:627`, `:660`; `memtest.c:994`). It is\n  initialised in place by `handle_table_init(t)` and torn down by `handle_table_destroy(t)`. CeeSafe code would only\n  ever see it through a `*mut handle_table` from C.\n- **SA2 so far.** The kobject layer is the reference over C's `struct kobject` (CosmoOS #493): an *imported* struct, which\n  works today. The owner's choice for SA2 was that the types themselves become the reference's, so the definition\n  `struct handle_table { … }` must come from the reference's generated header, not from `handle.h`.\n\n### 1.2 What the reference allows today\n\n- **`@repr(C)` structs** (type-system §28.5) cross to C and are defined in `ceesafe_exports.h`, but their fields may only\n  be integers, `bool`, raw pointers and other `@repr(C)` structs. A `spinlock_t` field is N0291 (checked with the reference compiler\n  0.4.0, 2026-10-30: \"a @repr(C) struct cannot hold a 'spinlock_t'\"), and so is an array field (§28.3: arrays do not\n  cross).\n- **C state by value** (§33, C7b) is allowed in a *the reference* struct, which then becomes C-laid-out (§33.1): the C\n  compiler lays it out, it is never moved (§33.2), and it is made only by `Box.create` with a `@placed`\n  initialiser (§33.3). Such a struct is `struct ceeT_S` in `main.c` only: C code cannot name it.\n- **Arrays in imported structs** are allowed (ADR 0014, §31): `f: [E; N]` is seen as the reference's array struct over the C\n  member, with no copy.\n\nSo each half exists: C-laid-out structs inside the reference, and C-visible structs without C state. Nothing joins them.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. `@repr(C)` may hold C state and arrays | B. A new `@cstruct` kind | C. Keep the type C's |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Language | lift two N0291 cases of §28.5; §33's rules apply as they stand | a third struct kind with its own rules | none |\n| Who defines `struct handle_table` | The reference (`ceesafe_exports.h`) | The reference | C (`handle.h`), imported by the reference |\n| SA2's \"the reference-owned types\" | yes | yes | no: the code is the reference, the type C's |\n| New rules to learn | none: a `@repr(C)` struct with C state is C-laid-out, as any struct with C state is | a kind that is both | none |\n| Later conversions (SA3: `struct mutex`, wait queues, `struct process`) | the same rule | the same | each type stays C's |\n\n## 3. Decision: A\n\n### 3.1 Fields\n\nA `@repr(C)` struct's fields may also be:\n\n- **an imported C type by value** (§31), such as `spinlock_t`. The struct is then C-laid-out (§33.1);\n- **an array `[E; N]`** whose element is an integer, `bool",
    205 =>
        "`, a raw pointer as in §28.3, a `@repr(C)` struct, or an\n  imported type. Inside the reference the field is the reference's array; in C it is the member `CE f[N]`.\n\nEvery other N0291 case stays: references, slices, boxes, vectors, optionals, results, non-`@repr` structs and\nenums, and `@repr` enums as fields. None of the allowed field types needs drop; in addition, a `@repr(C)` struct\nwith C state may not declare a `drop` method (N0291, new): C makes, embeds and discards these values without calling\nThe reference, so a drop would silently not run.\n\n### 3.2 A `@repr(C)` struct with C state\n\nIt is C-laid-out (§33.1), with every rule of §33 unchanged:\n\n- it is never moved, copied or assigned in the reference (N0294), and crosses to C only behind a pointer (`*T`, `*mut T`;\n  §28.3's by-value crossing is for `@repr(C)` structs without C state);\n- The reference makes one only through `Box.create` with its `@placed` initialiser (§33.2–§33.3), or in a static (§39.4);\n- C makes one wherever C makes a struct (embedded, static, on the stack). CeeSafe code that receives a pointer to\n  one from C relies on C having initialised it, as with any pointer from C: the exported function that takes it\n  says so in its `/// Safety:` comment (§27.4).\n\n### 3.3 C\n\n- **`ceesafe_exports.h`** defines the struct as `struct S { … }` with members named as in the reference, after the `@header`\n  includes that declare its imported member types (it includes them already, c-backend §2.3), and after the\n  `@repr(C)` structs it contains. There is no layout assertion, as for every C-laid-out type: the C compiler lays\n  it out, and `layoutOf<T>()` gives C's `sizeof`/`_Alignof` (ADR 0027).\n- **Array members** are `CE f[N];` with `N` written as a number. A C header that also names the length (CosmoOS's\n  `HANDLE_TABLE_SIZE`) checks it with a `_Static_assert` beside its `#include`. In `main.c` a field place is the\n  member seen as the reference's array struct, `(*(ceeA<N>_<E> *)(uintptr_t)&p->f)`, as for ADR 0014's imported arrays, after\n  an assertion that the member and the array struct have one size and alignment (the member's type needs no\n  `_Generic` check: `main.c` defines it).\n- **`main.c`** uses `struct S` for it, as for every `@repr(C)` struct, so both translation units name one type\n  (C11 6.2.7).\n\n### 3.4 The interpreter\n\nC-laid-out types are already outside the interpreter (§33.4), and a `@repr(C)` struct with C state is one of\nthem. Arrays of `@repr(C)` elements in a `@repr(C)` struct without C state run in the interpreter as CeeSafe arrays.\n\n### 3.5 For the handle table (CosmoOS, after a release)\n\n```ceesafe\n@header(\"kernel/spinlock.h\", \"kernel/object.h\")\nextern \"C\" {\n    @ctype(\"spinlock_t\")\n    struct spinlock_t;\n    struct kobject;\n}\n\nconst HANDLE_TABLE_SIZE: usize = 64;\n\n@repr(C)\nstruct handle_entry {\n    obj: *mut kobject; // null: free\n    rights: u32;\n    flags: u32;\n}\n\n@repr(C)\nstruct handle_table {\n    lock: spinlock_t;\n    entries: [handle_entry; 64];\n    count: u32;\n    limit: u32;\n    exiting: bool;\n}\n\n/// Safety: `t` points to storage for a table that nothing uses yet.\n@export(\"handle_table_init\")\nfn tableInit(t: *mut handle_table) { … }\n```\n\n`make cee` writes the generated header (`kernel/include/kernel/handle_types.h`, say), and `handle.h` includes it\nin place of its own definitions, with `_Static_assert(HANDLE_TABLE_SIZE == 64)`. `process.c` and the self-tests are\nunchanged: `unsigned` and `uint32_t` are the same type on both of CosmoOS's targets, and the C sees the same\nmembers. The entries hold `*mut kobject` and count their references by hand, in unsafe code, as the C does: a\n`KRef<kobject>` field would need drop glue, which C-made values never run (§3.1).\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- SA2's handle table, and later SA3's locks and wait queues and `struct process` itself, can be the reference's types\n  without changing how C embeds them.\n- C code may write a reference-owned struct's fields (`process.c` writes `limit`). Such a",
    206 =>
        " struct is plain data with C\n  state: the reference keeps no invariant in it that C could break without `unsafe` on the reference side noticing, which is\n  already the rule for anything behind a pointer from C (unsafe.md U2).\n- No layout is checked for these structs in the reference; the C compiler is the authority, as for every C-laid-out type.\n  The member types are checked in both translation units by the shared definition.\n- **Tests.**\n  - The run corpus gets a C file that embeds a C-state `@repr(C)` struct by value in a C struct, initialises it\n    through an export, and uses its array.\n  - Compile-fail tests cover a move of one (N0294), a by-value parameter (N0294), a `drop` method on one (N0291),\n    and an array of a non-FFI type (N0291).\n  - `k3.py` compiles CosmoOS's header against the generated one.\n- **Release.** It needs a the reference compiler release (0.5.0) and a CosmoOS pin move before SA2's third PR.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. **Which option?** A (recommended), B or C (§2).\n2. **Arrays in the same ADR?** Recommended: yes, since the handle table needs both. The alternative is a separate\n   ADR for array fields of `@repr(C)` structs.\n\n## 6. Implementation (2026-10-30)\n\n- **Checker** (`TypeChecker.IsFfiSafe`, the `@repr(C)` field loop): a field may be an imported type or an array of an\n  allowed element; a `drop` on a C-laid-out `@repr(C)` struct is N0291.\n- **C** (`CEmitter`): a `@repr(C)` struct's array field is the C array member in `main.c` and `ceesafe_exports.h`, with\n  an assertion that it has the layout of the reference's array struct; places use it through the cast that ADR 0014's imported\n  arrays use (`IsCArrayMember`); a struct literal assigns its arrays after the compound literal\n  (`EmitConstruction`); a static's initialiser writes them as plain braces (`CArrayInitializer`). The header names an\n  imported member by its C spelling and defines no imported struct.\n- **Tests.**\n  - `ffi/exported-c-state.cee` with `.c` and `.types.h`, under every C configuration: C embeds `xs_table` (a lock, a\n    self-pointing list head, an array of `@repr(C)` entries) in its own struct, a static and a stack frame, writes a\n    field, and uses it through the reference's exports. `Grid`, a plain `@repr(C)` struct with nested arrays, is a value and a\n    static in the reference. The reviewed header is `ffi/exported-c-state.h`.\n  - `check-fail/c-state-types.cee`: a `drop` (N0291) and a by-value parameter (N0294).\n    `check-fail/ffi-declarations.cee`: array fields are allowed, and arrays of a non-`@repr` struct or a `@repr`\n    enum are N0291.\n",
    207 =>
        "# ADR 0030 — M14: what SA3 needs from the reference (one definition, `offsetOf`, the caller's address)\n\n> **Inherited.** ADR 0030 was accepted before CeeSafe's fork (Decision 0006), when the language had its\n> earlier name. In this record, \"the reference\" is that language and project and \"the reference compiler\" its compiler:\n> read them as CeeSafe and `ceesafec`. Links to documents CeeSafe did not keep are given as text.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-30), every question as recommended (§5): A, A, A. The wording is the agent's.\n- Scope: what CosmoOS's roadmap 2.0 SA3 needs to rewrite `wait.c`, `completion.c`, `semaphore.c`, `mutex.c` and\n  `quiesce.c` in the reference with the reference-owned types (the owner's choice, 2026-10-30). SA3's host test, `tests/host/test_sync.c`\n  (CosmoOS #496), records the behaviour the reference versions must reproduce.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nWhat the C uses, and where the reference stands. Each item was checked against the reference compiler 0.5.0 on 2026-10-30.\n\n### 1.1 The same struct, defined twice in one translation unit (blocks SA3)\n\n- **The include graph.** `thread.h` includes `completion.h`, which includes `wait.h`; `mutex.h`, `completion.h` and\n  `semaphore.h` include `wait.h`. Once `struct waitqueue` is the reference's (ADR 0029), `wait.h` gets it by including the\n  generated header, as `handle.h` does since SA2 (CosmoOS #494).\n- **The collision.** A CeeSafe port that reads a thread's fields (`wait.cee` sets `thread->waiting_on`; the mutex reads\n  priorities) names `kernel/thread.h` in its `@header`. Its `main.c` then sees `struct waitqueue` twice: once through\n  `thread.h` → `completion.h` → `wait.h` → the generated header, and once in its own definition (c-backend §2.3:\n  `main.c` defines every `@repr(C)` struct). A second definition of a struct in one translation unit is an error\n  (C11 §6.7.2.3p1).\n- **Why SA2 did not meet it.** `handle.cee` names `spinlock.h` and `object.h`, and neither includes `handle.h`.\n- **The guard.** CosmoOS's `cee-gen.py` renames each generated header's guard `CEESAFE_EXPORTS_H` after the file's path,\n  so that two generated headers in one file do not hide each other. The guard is not the problem; the second\n  definition in `main.c` is.\n\n### 1.2 A struct from a pointer to its member (`container_of`)\n\n- **The C.** `list_for_each_entry(e, head, link)` and `container_of` recover a struct from its embedded `list_node`:\n  a wait entry from its `link`, and a mutex from its `pi_link` in the owner's `pi_held` list. The second member is not\n  at offset 0.\n- **The reference.** ADR 0009 §2 proposed `Ptr.containerOf` (unsafe). Its implementation note moved it to C7d, and C7d's\n  generated callbacks never needed it, so **it does not exist** (no match in `src/` or `lib/`). Nothing gives a field's\n  offset as a value either. `layoutOf<T>()` (ADR 0027) gives only size and alignment.\n\n### 1.3 The caller's address (lockdep)\n\n- **The C.** `mutex_lock`, `mutex_trylock`, `mutex_unlock`, `complete` and the completion waits pass\n  `(uintptr_t)__builtin_return_address(0)` to lockdep, which names the acquiring and releasing call sites in its\n  reports (`docs/kernel/lockdep/`, CosmoOS L21).\n- **The reference.** Nothing gives it. A CeeSafe function's C is `static`, and the C compiler may inline it into its exported\n  wrapper, so even a raw `__builtin_return_address` in its body would name different frames depending on the\n  optimiser.\n\n### 1.4 What is not a gap (checked)\n\n- **Static inline functions of a header.** An `extern \"C\"` declaration of `list_init`, which `list.h` defines\n  `static inline`, compiles warning-free under CosmoOS's flags. The generated prototype `void list_init(struct\n  list_node *);` follows the inline definition, has no storage class, and so takes the definition's internal linkage\n  (C11 §6.2.2p4–5). `raw_this_cpu`, `list_*`, `wait_entry_init` and `thread_current` are all callable already. The\n  spec does not say so; §3.4 adds it.\n- *",
    208 =>
        "*Function pointers in a `@repr(C)` struct** (`wait_entry.fn`) and calls through them: type-system §29.5. The\n  generated typedef and C's `wait_callback_fn` name compatible types.\n- **Macros that are code** (`might_sleep`, `list_for_each_entry`, `wait_event`): `might_sleep` is three lines over\n  `raw_this_cpu()` and `lockdep_might_sleep`, which a port writes in the reference. A list walk is a loop over `next` once\n  §1.2 is solved. `wait_event` stays a C macro for C callers, over the exported functions.\n- **`panic`'s format** stays in a floor, as in SA2.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n### 2.1 One definition (§1.1)\n\n| | A. `main.c` includes its exports header | B. `@cdefined` structs | C. No the reference-owned types where the graph loops |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| What changes | a `@repr(C)` struct is defined only in the exports header, which `main.c` includes; `--exports-header <path>` names the header (the `#include` and the guard) | an attribute marks a struct whose definition a `@header` brings, and `main.c` asserts its members instead of defining it | the type stays C's, imported (§31) |\n| The definition | one, generated, used by both | The reference's, but written in C's header by hand or by a script | C's |\n| `cee-gen.py` | passes `--exports-header kernel/include/kernel/wait_types.h`; no guard renaming | unchanged | unchanged |\n| SA3's choice of the reference-owned types | kept | kept, with two copies to keep in step | given up for waitqueue, completion and mutex |\n\n### 2.2 `container_of` (§1.2)\n\n| | A. `offsetOf(T, field)` | B. `Ptr.containerOf(p, T.field)` only | C. Keep list walks in a C floor |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Form | a built-in, `usize`, C's `offsetof` | an unsafe built-in for the one use | none |\n| Reach | `container_of`, and any other offset (an embedded member's address from its struct's) | `container_of` | — |\n\n### 2.3 The caller's address (§1.3)\n\n| | A. `callerAddress()` in an export | B. Floor wrappers | C. Report no address |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Form | a built-in, valid only in an `@export` function: the exported symbol's return address | each exported lock function is C that captures the address and calls a CeeSafe function with it | lockdep gets 0 |\n| Lockdep reports | as today | as today | lose the call sites |\n| C per primitive | none | one wrapper per entry point (about a dozen) | none |\n\n## 3. Decision: A, A, A\n\n### 3.1 One definition\n\n- `ceesafec build --exports-header <path>` names the exports header: `<path>` is the name C code includes, and the\n  guard is derived from it (`kernel/include/kernel/wait_types.h` → `KERNEL_INCLUDE_KERNEL_WAIT_TYPES_H`). Without it\n  the header is `ceesafe_exports.h`, as today.\n- `main.c` (and, with `--split`, every unit) includes the exports header, after the `@header`s, and does not define\n  the `@repr(C)` structs itself. Their layout assertions follow the include, in `main.c`. A `@repr(C)` struct is then\n  defined once in every translation unit that sees it, however many headers bring it in, so a kernel header may\n  include the generated header and a CeeSafe port may include that kernel header.\n- The header's `@header` includes come before its own definitions, as today (c-backend §2.3).\n\n### 3.2 `offsetOf(T, field)`\n\n- `offsetOf(T, f)` is a built-in whose value is the offset in bytes of field `f` of the struct type `T`, as `usize`.\n  `T` is a struct with a field `f` (a non-struct `T` or a missing field is N0339, new). For a C-laid-out `T`, or an\n  imported one, it is C's `offsetof`, which the C compiler computes (§33.1); otherwise it is the layout's offset, a\n  constant.\n- It is a static initialiser (§39.4), like `layoutOf<T>()`.\n- It is not `unsafe`: it reads no memory. Using it to recover a struct, as `(p as *mut u8).sub(offsetOf(T, f)) as\n  *mut T`, is raw-pointer arithmetic, which is (`unsafe.md` U2). lib/kernel adds `containerOf` and a list walk over\n  `list_node` on it (§3.5).\n\n### 3.3 `callerAddress()`\n\n- `callerAddress()` is a built-in of type `usize`, valid only ",
    209 =>
        "in the body of an `@export` function (N0340 elsewhere,\n  new). Its value is the address the exported symbol's call returns to: C's `__builtin_return_address(0)` taken in\n  the export's wrapper, which passes it to the function's body as a hidden argument. So it does not depend on\n  inlining. The reference calls of the same function get 0, since no C frame called them.\n- MSVC: `_ReturnAddress()` (c-backend §1.1); the interpreter gives 0.\n- It is not `unsafe`: it is a number, not a pointer one may follow.\n\n### 3.4 Static inline functions (spec only)\n\ntype-system §28.2 says what §1.4 found: an `extern \"C\"` function that a `@header` defines `static inline` may be\ndeclared and called. The generated prototype takes the definition's internal linkage (C11 §6.2.2p4).\n\n### 3.5 lib/kernel\n\n`lib/kernel/list.cee` binds CosmoOS's `struct list_node` and its inline helpers (`list_init`, `list_push_back`,\n`list_remove`, `list_empty`) and adds `containerOf` over `offsetOf`, with unsafe iteration that may remove the\ncurrent entry, as `list_for_each_entry_safe` does. `integrations/cosmoos/k3.py` checks it against CosmoOS's headers\nwith the rest.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- SA3's ports can be written with the reference-owned types and only the format of `panic` in C. Each primitive's exported\n  functions keep their C names and types, so the 669 call sites and the `*_INIT` macros are unchanged.\n- **`main.c` no longer defines `@repr(C)` structs.** A build with `--emit c` must keep the exports header beside\n  `main.c`, as it already writes it. FFI tests check that every C configuration still compiles.\n- **Tests.**\n  - The run corpus gets a program whose `@header` includes its own exports header, which used to be a redefinition\n    error.\n  - `offsetOf` gets tests on a CeeSafe struct, a C-laid-out one and an imported one, against C's `offsetof`.\n  - `callerAddress()` gets a test in an export called from C, comparing against the C caller's own\n    `__builtin_return_address`.\n  - Check-fail tests cover N0339 and N0340.\n- **Release.** It needs a the reference compiler release (0.6.0) before SA3's conversion PRs. The host test (#496) does not wait.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. **One definition (§2.1):** A (recommended), B or C?\n2. **Offsets (§2.2):** A, `offsetOf(T, field)` (recommended), B or C?\n3. **The caller's address (§2.3):** A, `callerAddress()` (recommended), B or C?\n\n## 6. Implementation (2026-10-30)\n\nTwo forms differ from §3, for the reason `layoutOf<T>()` differs from ADR 0027's `Layout.of<T>()`:\n- **`offsetOf<T>(field)`**, not `offsetOf(T, field)`: a type is no expression in the reference, so `T` is a type argument.\n- **`containerOf(node, offset)` returns `*mut void`**, which the caller casts, and is not generic: the struct that\n  embeds a node is usually C-laid-out, and no type argument may be (type-system §33.1). It is a top-level function of\n  lib/kernel, since the reference has no type arguments on a member call.\n\nWhat changed:\n- **One definition** (`CEmitter`): `HeaderStructs` is the set the exports header defines (the `@repr(C)` structs and the\n  CeeSafe structs they hold); `main.c` includes the header after the `@header`s and skips their definitions, keeping the\n  layout assertions; `--exports-header` (`CEmitOptions.ExportsHeader`) names the header and its guard.\n- **`offsetOf`**: `TypeChecker.CheckOffsetOf`, a literal for a reference layout, `BuiltinFunction.OffsetOf` (C's\n  `offsetof`) for a C-laid-out or imported type, in static initialisers too; an interpreter fault for the latter.\n- **`callerAddress()`**: `TypeChecker.CheckCallerAddress` marks the export (`HirFunction.UsesCallerAddress`); its C\n  gets a hidden last parameter `cee_caller`, which the export's wrapper fills with `cee_return_address()` (rt.h) and\n  The reference's calls and callback wrappers with 0. The interpreter gives 0.\n- **lib/kernel/list.cee**: `list_node`, `List.init`/`isEmpty`/`pushBack`/`pushFront`/`remove`/`first`/`next` over\n  list.h's inline helper",
    210 =>
        "s, and `containerOf`.\n- **Tests:**\n  - `ffi/includes-exports.cee` (a `@header` that includes the program's own exports header; `callerAddress()` from\n    two C call sites at `-O0` and `-O2`, and 0 from the reference);\n  - `ffi/exported-c-state.cee` (`offsetOf` on a reference layout, a C-laid-out struct, an imported one and in a static,\n    each equal to C's `offsetof`);\n  - `check-fail/offset-of.cee` and `check-fail/caller-address.cee`.\n\n  A walk over a list with removals was checked against CosmoOS's `list.h` on the host (2026-10-30: sum 15, the odd\n  nodes unlinked).\n",
    211 =>
        "# ADR 0031 — L1: async/await (stackless frames, structured scopes, cancellation as a flag)\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-10-31 (\"I accept it as written.\"): every question as recommended (§5).\n  **Amended** the same day while writing `type-system.md` §40 (§6): a scope's allocation failure is an error the caller\n  names (\"Caller names the OOM error\", the owner's choice), and the changes that follow from `?` needing one error\n  type (§17.4).\n- Scope: `async fn`, async closures, `await`, the `suspend` effect, `Async.run`, structured scopes, cancellation, and the\n  frame ABI between ceesafec, the runtime and `lib/std`. The first CeeSafe language decision after the fork (Decisions\n  0006, 0007). The design and its rationale are `docs/async-await-proposal.md` (revised after a cross-check against the tree,\n  2026-10-31); this record fixes the choices the implementation depends on.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nCeeSafe has no concurrency other than threads (§38: `Thread.spawn`, `Thread.both`, `Arc`, `Mutex`, the atomics).\n`async`, `await` and `yield` are reserved (`language-spec.md` §1) and lexed as keywords\n(`src/syntax/token.cee`) but used nowhere.\n\nRust's async shows ten failure modes (the proposal, \"What goes wrong in Rust and others\"). Most come from three\nchoices: futures that move (hence `Pin`), tasks that outlive their spawner (hence `'static + Send`), and cancellation\nby dropping a suspended future. CeeSafe can avoid all three because of rules it already has:\n\n- a panic aborts (OD-5), so a suspended frame is never unwound;\n- context effects exist (§32), so blocking in async and a spin guard across `await` can be refused;\n- fork-join borrowing exists (§38.6, ADR 0018);\n- scoped collections of closures exist (§25, §36.2);\n- allocators are explicit (§18, §30).\n\nFacts from the tree that constrain the design:\n\n- NIR has no call terminator: a call is an rvalue (`intermediate-representation.md`).\n- The C structurer falls back to flat form on an irreducible graph (`c-backend.md` §3).\n- The interpreter runs NIR on an explicit activation stack.\n- Every console, file and socket operation has `sleep` (§32.1).\n- There are no channels yet (ADR 0016 left them for a later increment).\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| Model | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Stackless frames that never move, structured scopes, cancellation as a flag** | Proposed |\n| B. Stackful coroutines or green threads | Needs stack switching per architecture, which C11 cannot express; kernel stacks are small |\n| C. Effect handlers (OCaml 5) | The same stack switching, and a large new language feature |\n| D. Rust's model (`poll`, `Pin`, cancel by drop, detached tasks) | The source of the problems A avoids |\n\n## 3. Decision: A\n\n### 3.1 The language\n\n1. **`async fn f(…) -> T`.** A call to it is a *task*. In v1 a task must be awaited at once (`await f(x)`), passed\n   to `Async.run`, or spawned into a scope (§3.3). Anything else is **N0342**. There is no first-class task value.\n2. **`await e`** is a prefix operator. It is allowed only in an async fn or async closure body (**N0341**: a sync\n   function, a `drop` block or a sync closure). Its operand is a primary followed by calls, member accesses and\n   indexes; the first `?` or `!` ends the operand and applies to the awaited value, so `await f(x)?` is\n   `(await f(x))?` and `await f(x)?.len` is `((await f(x))?).len` (a task is never a `Result`). Its type is the\n   task's result. (Wording corrected 2026-10-31 while writing `grammar.md`: the accepted text said \"binds looser than\n   postfix operators\", which would group `await (f(x)?)`, contrary to its own example; the example is the decision.)\n3. **Async closures**, `async (…) => …`, are closure literals whose call is a task (§36.2's capture rules apply\n   unchanged). They are accepted where a task-producing closure is expected: `spawn`, `race`, `all`, `shield`.\n4. **Recursion through `await`** would make a frame infinitely large, so it is **N0343**. Its sure fix,\n   `await Asyn",
    212 =>
        "c.boxed(alloc, f(…))`, comes with L1 A3.\n\n### 3.2 Effects (amending §32 and §36.3)\n\n1. **A third effect, `suspend`:** the code may await. `@effects(…)` and effect lists on function types accept it\n   (N0290 as before for other names). `suspend` is not in the default effect list of a function type written\n   without one, because sync code cannot await.\n2. **New contexts and restrictions:**\n\n   | Context | Forbids | Diagnostic |\n   |---|---|---|\n   | An async fn or async closure body | `sleep` | N0295, with the call chain; `Async.blocking` is the bridge |\n   | A live `@context(atomic)` value (§32.3's liveness rule) | `suspend` | N0295 |\n   | `@context(irq)` | `suspend`, in addition to `sleep` and `alloc` | N0295 |\n\n3. **Calls through closures.** `Async.scope`, `spawn`, `race`, `all` and `shield` are `@effectsOf` their closure\n   arguments (§32.5).\n\n### 3.3 Structured concurrency\n\n1. **Scopes.** `Async.scopeIn(alloc, oom, (s) => …)` runs its body, which may call `s.spawn(async () => …)`. It\n   returns only after every child has returned. Child frames are placed with `alloc`. `Async.scope(oom, body)` uses the\n   system heap and has `alloc`. `spawn` returns `void`; a child whose frame cannot be allocated is not started, the\n   others are cancelled, and the scope returns `Err(oom)` (amended, §6).\n2. **The scope-loan rule.** `s` is typed as a scoped collection of closures whose region is the body's environment,\n   and `s.spawn(c)` checks like a `push(c)`. Hence:\n   - captures that conflict across spawns are **N0241**, by the existing borrow rules;\n   - nothing a child captures can be changed, moved or ended by the body before the scope ends;\n   - a child that captures one of the body's own locals, which end before the children do, is **N0344**.\n3. **No `Send` or `Share` check** on children's captures, because children run on the scope's own thread.\n   `Async.blocking(closure)` runs its closure on a worker thread, so its captures follow §38.6's rule (**N0334**).\n4. **The result.** The scope's result is the first error a child returns. The other children are then cancelled\n   (§3.4), and the scope still waits for them.\n5. **Combinators** (amended, §6).\n   - `Async.race(a, b)` returns the winner and hands back the loser's value if it also finished (`Raced<T>`).\n   - `Async.all(a, b)` runs both and returns the first error, with results through captures.\n   - Both take two async closures and embed their frames in the caller's, so they never allocate.\n   - There is no detached spawn in v1.\n\n### 3.4 Cancellation\n\n1. **Cancelling a task sets a flag.** It never destroys a frame. A frame is freed only after its task returns.\n2. **Who sees the flag.** Only the library's operations that would block observe it: timers, I/O, channel receives,\n   plus `Async.checkpoint(cancelled)` and `Async.isCancelled()`. The library's operations return\n   `Err(AsyncError.Cancelled)`, where `AsyncError` (`lib/std`) has `Cancelled`, `TimedOut` and `Io(IoError)`.\n   `checkpoint(cancelled: E)` returns `Result<void, E>` (amended, §6).\n3. **Cleanup** is ordinary code on the `?` path, and destructors run in the normal order.\n4. **Shields.** `Async.shield(deadline, body)` makes those operations ignore the flag. Once the deadline passes they\n   return `Err(TimedOut)`, never `Cancelled`.\n5. **`drop` blocks stay synchronous** (§3.1, N0341).\n\n### 3.5 Threads, executors and the library\n\n1. **One executor per thread; a task never moves between threads.** Tasks need not be `Send`. Several cores are used by\n   several threads, each with `Async.run`, connected by channels (ADR 0032, L1 A2).\n2. **Built in and library.** The compiler builds in only what needs frame layout or control flow:\n   - `async fn`, async closures, `await`;\n   - `Async.run`, `scope`/`scopeIn`, `spawn`, `race`, `all`, `shield`, `checkpoint`, `blocking`;\n   - two primitives, `Async.park()` (suspend the current task until woken) and `Async.waker()` (its wake handle).\n     They are accepted onl",
    213 =>
        "y in library files (**N0345**).\n\n   Timers, console, files, sockets, `Async.Mutex` and `Channel` are library code in `lib/std` over runtime functions\n   with honest `@effects` (Decision 0008).\n3. **Console output from async code** waits for the descriptor to be writable (`poll`) and writes at most `PIPE_BUF`\n   bytes per step. POSIX guarantees such a write does not block, so the runtime function declares `@effects()`.\n4. **Profiles.** Async is in the hosted profile until L1 A4. The frame ABI is in every profile.\n\n### 3.6 Lowering (amending `intermediate-representation.md` and `c-backend.md`)\n\n1. **NIR** (amended, §6.5).\n   - An awaited call is an ordinary `Call`, marked as awaited; `Async.run`'s task call is marked too; `Async.park()` is\n     a builtin. So the borrow, move and drop analyses see calls and need no rule of their own.\n   - A coroutine pass after the effect checker builds the await graph (N0343).\n2. **Frames.** Async fn `F` becomes four pieces of C (`c-backend.md` §3a):\n   - `struct ceeAF_<F>` holds the parameters, every local, one child-frame field per awaited call, the result and the\n     state;\n   - `ceeAI_<F>` fills in the arguments;\n   - `ceeAR_<F>(frame)` returns false while the task is pending, and `ceeAT_<F>` is it as the runtime calls it.\n\n   A frame is placed once (on `Async.run`'s caller's stack, in its parent's frame, or from a scope's allocator) and\n   never moves, so a borrow of a local across `await` stays valid. In v1 every local lives in the frame; L1 A3 shrinks\n   frames by liveness and adds `--explain-frames`.\n3. **Resume functions are always emitted in flat form.** The state `switch` makes the graph irreducible for an `await`\n   inside a loop.\n4. **The runtime.** `ceesafe_rt_async.c` holds the executor, the timers and the reactor (`poll` in A1, kqueue and epoll\n   in A2), plus wakers by id. `ceesafe/rt.h` declares the task header: the resume function, root frame, cancel flag,\n   shield depth, scheduler links and waker.\n5. **Determinism.** The interpreter runs async code on a virtual clock with a seeded run order. A\n   `CEESAFE_CANCEL_AT=k` sweep cancels at every suspension point, modelled on `CEESAFE_FAIL_ALLOC_AT`.\n\n### 3.7 Diagnostics\n\n| Code | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| N0341 | `await` outside an async fn or async closure |\n| N0342 | A call of an async fn that is not awaited, run or spawned |\n| N0343 | Recursion through `await` (a frame that contains itself) |\n| N0344 | A spawned child that captures the scope body's own locals |\n| N0345 | `Async.park` or `Async.waker` outside the library |\n\nN0290 and N0295 extend to `suspend`.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- Eight of the ten Rust failure modes become compile errors or cannot arise. Colouring is kept on purpose, and the\n  debugger's task tree is a tooling task (L1 A3).\n- The compiler itself does not use async, so the fixed point is unaffected until a later decision makes it do so.\n- `lib/std` gains `async.cee` (A1) and `channel.cee` (A2, with ADR 0032).\n- The interpreter gains a task scheduler (A2). Scopes and their bodies must run as activations, never through the\n  interpreter's nested calls.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\nEach has a recommended answer, which §3 assumes.\n\n1. First-class `Task<T>` values in v1? **No** (§3.1.1).\n2. How `Cancelled` is typed: **a case of every async I/O error enum**, with `checkpoint()` returning\n   `Result<void, Cancelled>`. Amended (§6): one library enum, `AsyncError`, and `checkpoint(cancelled: E)` (§3.4.2).\n3. `race` and a loser that also finished: **hand back its value** (§3.3.5).\n4. `await` and `?`: **`await f(x)?` is `(await f(x))?`** (§3.1.2).\n5. Awaiting in a `defer` or `drop` inside an implicit shield: **not in v1** (§3.4.5).\n6. Resume functions: **flat C in v1** (§3.6.3).\n7. Scope storage: **an explicit allocator**, `scopeIn`, with `scope` on the system heap (§3.3.1).\n\n## 6. Amendments (2026-10-31)\n\n`?` propagates only an error of the same type (`type-system.md` §17.4: there are no `From` conversions).",
    214 =>
        " Three\naccepted choices did not respect that, and were changed while §40 was written:\n\n1. **A scope's allocation failure.** As accepted, `spawn` returned `Result<void, AllocError>`, which cannot become the\n   scope's `Result<void, E>`. Asked, the owner chose the caller-named error: `Async.scope(oom, body)` and\n   `scopeIn(alloc, oom, body)`, with `spawn` returning `void` (§3.3.1).\n2. **`checkpoint`.** For the same reason, `checkpoint()` returning `Result<void, Cancelled>` could not be propagated\n   with `?` from a function with its own error type. It takes the error to return: `checkpoint(cancelled: E)`.\n   `isCancelled()` is added for code that decides for itself (§3.4.2).\n3. **The I/O error type.** Adding `Cancelled` to the built-in `IoError` would break every exhaustive `match` on it.\n   The library's async operations return `AsyncError` instead, which wraps `IoError` (§3.4.2).\n4. **`race` and `all`** are `Async.race(a, b)` and `Async.all(a, b)` rather than scope methods, with two children whose\n   frames are embedded. They then need no allocator and no out-of-memory error (§3.3.5). Implemented as specified in\n   L1 A2b (the owner's choice, \"option 1\"): lib/std's `AsyncRaceJoin<T, E>.settle` and `AsyncAllJoin<E>.settle` make\n   the value from the two results in the order the children returned.\n5. **Lowering, found while implementing A1.** New NIR terminators (`StartTask`, `Await`, `Park`) would have needed an\n   arm in every analysis and in the interpreter for what is, to all of them, a call. Instead an awaited call stays a\n   `Call` marked in a side table, and the C backend alone turns marked calls into suspension points (§3.6.1). The C\n   names follow the C function name, `ceeAF_<F>` and so on, and the runtime's entry is `ceesafe_async_run(frame,\n   resume)`; the task header arrives with A2's scheduler.\n6. **The scope body is a block, not a closure** (2026-10-31, the owner's choice \"Inline block\"). Checked as a closure,\n   a spawned child's environment would live in the body's block, which ends while the child still runs. So\n   `Async.scope(oom, (s) => { … })`'s body is checked as a block of the awaiting function; `s` names the scope only in\n   `s.spawn(…)`. The scope-loan rule is then checked on the children's capture lists (`src/check/async.cee`),\n   conservatively, per local:\n   - two children capturing one local where either changes it are N0241;\n   - a body statement that uses a local a child has captured is N0241, since the child may run until the scope\n     returns (the awaiting function itself is suspended until then);\n   - a child that captures one of the body's own locals outlives it, which is N0344.\n\n   `return`, `?`, `break` and `continue` cannot leave the body, the same limit a closure body has (§3.3.2).\n\n## 7. Implementation\n\nNone yet. `ROADMAP.md` (language track L1) records each phase as it is run.\n",
    215 =>
        "# ADR 0032 — L1: channels between threads and between tasks\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-10-31 (\"Accept as written\"): every question as recommended (§5).\n- Scope: `Channel<T>` in `lib/std/channel.cee`. It is the increment ADR 0016 §3.4 deferred (\"`Condvar` and channels\n  follow as a separate increment, each spec-first\"), and the transport ADR 0031 §3.5 needs between executors.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nThreads share state today only through `Arc<Mutex<T>>` and the atomics (§38.3). ADR 0031 runs one executor per\nthread and never moves a task between threads, so tasks on different threads need a way to pass values that wakes a\nwaiting task without blocking its thread.\n\nConstraints from the tree:\n\n- **No tuples** (`AGENTS.md`), so `let (tx, rx) = channel()` cannot be written.\n- **`Mutex.lock` has `sleep`** (§38.3), so async code cannot use one (N0295, type-system.md §40.1).\n- **Allocation failure is a value** (OD-6 and `ownership.md`), so a send must not allocate behind the caller's back.\n- **The interpreter runs one thread at a time** (ADR 0016 §3.8).\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. One cloneable handle, bounded buffer (proposed) | B. Separate `Sender<T>`/`Receiver<T>` | C. Unbounded |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Creation | `Channel<T>.create(n)` | needs a pair: no tuples, so an out-parameter or a struct | as A or B |\n| Who may send and receive | every handle | senders and receivers | as A or B |\n| Allocation | once, at `create` | once | on every send that grows the buffer |\n| Back-pressure | a full channel waits | as A | none |\n\n**A is proposed.** B adds two types and a pair struct for what close() and documentation can say. C breaks the rule\nthat allocation failure is a value at the place it happens.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n1. **`Channel<T: Send>`** is a library type, `@threadSafe`: a handle to a shared, reference-counted buffer of\n   `capacity` values. It is `Send` and `Share`.\n   - `Channel<T>.create(capacity: usize) -> Result<Channel<T>, AllocError>` allocates the buffer once. A capacity of 0\n     panics: a rendezvous channel is not in v1.\n   - `c.clone()` returns another handle to the same channel. Dropping the last handle drops the values still\n     buffered, in order, and frees the buffer.\n   - `c.close()` makes every later send fail. Receivers still take what is buffered, then see the end.\n2. **Blocking operations**, for threads, with the `sleep` effect:\n   - `c.send(v: T) -> Result<void, T>` waits while the channel is full, and returns `Err(v)` if it is closed.\n   - `c.recv() -> T?` waits while it is empty, and returns `null` once it is closed and empty.\n3. **Async operations**, for tasks (type-system.md §40), with the `suspend` effect. While they wait, the task is parked\n   and its thread runs other tasks:\n   - `await c.sendAsync(v: T) -> Result<void, SendError<T>>`. `SendError<T>` is `Closed(T)` or `Cancelled(T)`, so a\n     value is never lost: a cancelled or refused send hands it back.\n   - `await c.recvAsync() -> Result<T?, AsyncError>`. It returns `Err(AsyncError.Cancelled)` only while it has taken\n     nothing, so no value is lost to cancellation (ADR 0031 §3.4).\n4. **Waking.** A parked task registers its waker (`Async.waker()`) with the channel. A send wakes the oldest waiting\n   receiver, and a receive wakes the oldest waiting sender. Waking a task of another thread goes through that\n   thread's executor (`ceesafe_async_wake`, safe from any thread), so a channel connects executors as well as tasks\n   on one executor. Waiters are served first come, first served.\n5. **The lock.** The channel's state is guarded by a spin lock over `lib/core`'s atomics. Its critical sections are a\n   constant number of steps and never wait, allocate or call user code. Taking it therefore has no effect, and async\n   code may use the channel. The blocking operations wait outside the lock, on a runtime condition variable\n   (`ceesafe_cond_*`, `@effects(sleep)`).\n6. **Drops run outside the lock.** A value is moved out of the buffer und",
    216 =>
        "er the lock and dropped by the caller, so a\n   `drop` block never runs while the lock is held.\n7. **The interpreter.** Threads run one at a time and tasks on the virtual clock. A thread or task waiting on a\n   channel that no one else can serve is a deadlock, reported as an interpreter fault (as ADR 0016 §3.8).\n8. **Profiles.** Hosted only, with threads (§38.5): `Channel` is N0293 elsewhere.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- Three runtime additions: `ceesafe_cond_*` (a condition variable beside `ceesafe_mutex_*`), thread-safe wakers, and\n  each executor's wake descriptor (a self-pipe, or eventfd on Linux) that its `poll` waits on with its other\n  registrations.\n- The spin lock's `unsafe` code keeps `ownership.md` T1–T3. Only `Send` values cross (T1), and the buffer is reached\n  only under the lock (T2).\n- The tests to add:\n  - run-pass: one thread to another, task to task on one executor, and a task on one thread to a task on another;\n  - check-fail: a value that is not `Send` (N0334), and a blocking `recv` in async code (N0295);\n  - the fault sweep reaches `create`, the only allocation.\n\n## 5. Implementation (2026-10-31, L1 A2c)\n\n- **The runtime** (`runtime/hosted/ceesafe_rt_async.c`) keeps the channel element-blind: a reference count, a\n  ring of `capacity` slots of the element's stride, a spin lock, an epoch that every send, receive and close advances,\n  a list of task wait nodes, and a mutex and condition variable for blocked threads.\n  - `begin_send` and `begin_recv` return a slot under the lock, and `end_send` and `end_recv` release it.\n  - `wait` blocks a thread until the epoch moves; it is the one call with `sleep`.\n  - `park` queues a node in the task's frame unless the epoch has moved (then the task retries at once), and every\n    change wakes every queued task through its executor.\n- **The library** (`lib/std/channel.cee`) moves values with `p.write(v)` and `p.read()` between begin and end, and\n  drops them after. A cancelled task leaves the queue before it returns. A capacity of 0 panics through an unwrapped\n  `null`, until the language has a panic with a message.\n- **The interpreter** models it in its memory. A blocking wait is a thread wait, a task's wait parks it until the next\n  change, and when every task waits, a thread that has not started runs. Its threads run one at a time, so a thread\n  that blocks on a full channel cannot be resumed: values that cross threads in the interpreter need a channel large\n  enough for them (ADR 0016 §3.8).\n- **Tests:** run-pass/async-channel, check-fail/async-channel and check-fail/async-effects. The channel test passed 20\n  runs under ThreadSanitizer with small capacities, after one race it found in the cancellation sweep's counter.\n\n## 6. Questions for the owner\n\n1. One handle type rather than a sender/receiver pair? **Yes** (§3.1).\n2. Bounded only, with a capacity of at least 1? **Yes**: unbounded and rendezvous channels can come later.\n3. A spin lock inside the channel, so async code can use it? **Yes** (§3.5).\n",
    217 =>
        "# ADR 0033 — L1 A4d: a scope child per loop iteration, with its own values\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-01 (\"A now, B later\"); implemented: §3.1 first, then §3.2–3.3 (the\n  owner's \"Go with ADR 0033 §3.2–3.3\").\n- Scope: what a child spawned in an `Async.scope` body may capture (type-system.md §40.5; ADR 0031 §3.3 and §6.6).\n  Found by the reactor benchmark (L1 A4b, ROADMAP §10).\n\n## 1. Context\n\nA scope's body is a block of the awaiting function (ADR 0031 §6.6). A child captures by reference, and its\nenvironment lives in the body's block, so a child that captures one of the body's own locals is **N0344**: the local\nends with the body while the child may still run. That rule is sound, but it leaves a common program with no spelling:\n\n```ceesafe\nawait Async.scope(AsyncError.Cancelled, (s) => {\n    for (i in 0..workers) {\n        s.spawn(async () => await work(i));          // N0344: i is the body's\n    }\n    for (c in streams.view()) {\n        s.spawn(async () => await hold(c));          // N0344: c is the body's\n    }\n})?;\n```\n\nChecked 2026-11-01 with ceesafec at `844e755`: both loops are N0344, even for a `u32`. The only workaround is\nrecursion through `Async.all` and `Async.boxed`, one boxed frame per value (`benchmarks/async-reactor/reactor.cee`).\n\nWhat a child sees through a by-reference capture of a `Copy` local cannot differ from a copy taken at the spawn:\n- the body may not use a local after a child captured it (N0241);\n- the awaiting function is suspended from the body's end until the scope returns;\n- a child that changes a capture captures it mutably, not as a copy.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. Copies by value, automatically | B. `async move` children | C. A spawn argument |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Spelling | `s.spawn(async () => await work(i))` as written | `s.spawn(async move () => await work(i))` | `s.spawn(i, async (i) => …)` |\n| `Copy` values (`u32`, `borrow T`) | yes | yes | yes |\n| Owned values (a `Vector` built in the body) | no (still N0344) | yes: moved into the child | yes |\n| New syntax | none | `async move` (as `move` literals, §36.5) | a second `spawn` form |\n| Change to programs that compile today | none (they never capture a body local) | none | none |\n\n## 3. Proposed decision: A, then B\n\n1. **A child's `Copy` captures are held by value in its own frame.** `spawn` copies them when it starts the child,\n   whoever owns the captured local, so a `Copy` local of the body is no longer N0344. A child keeps capturing by\n   reference what it changes, and what is not `Copy`.\n2. **A copied borrow keeps its loan until the scope returns.** A captured `borrow T` (or a `Copy` struct holding one)\n   is a value whose loan the scope-loan rule now follows: its referent must outlive the scope. A borrow of a body\n   local is N0344 still, with the referent named. A borrow of the awaiting function's locals, or of anything they own,\n   is allowed. That is the `for (c in streams.view())` loop.\n3. **`async move () => …` children** (B) move non-`Copy` captures into the child's frame, as a `move` literal moves\n   into its environment (§36.5). The body cannot use what it moved. The child drops what it owns when it returns or\n   is cancelled, and a frame that cannot be allocated drops the moved values with the error.\n4. Race and all children (§40.4) follow the same rules. Their frames are the caller's, so a copy lives as long as\n   the race.\n5. The interpreter already gives each child its own copy of its environment, and keeps doing so.\n\nA first, because it makes the reported program compile as written with no new syntax. B second, as its own step,\nbecause it adds syntax and drop obligations to frames.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- type-system.md §40.5: the scope-loan rule distinguishes copied captures (by value; only their loans are followed)\n  from referenced ones (as today). diagnostics.md: N0344's text names the referent of a copied borrow.\n- The C backend: a child closure's environment gains the copied valu",
    218 =>
        "es as fields, which `ceeAI_` copies into its\n  frame. The frame-size report (`--explain-frames`) counts them.\n- Tests:\n  - run-pass: a loop of children over indices, and over a vector's elements by borrow;\n  - check-fail: a copied borrow of a body local (N0344), and a changed capture in a loop (N0344 still);\n  - `benchmarks/async-reactor/reactor.cee` written with the loop, not the recursion.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. A, copies by value without new syntax? **Yes.**\n2. B, `async move` for owned values, as a later step? **Yes, after A.**\n\n## 6. Implementation (2026-11-01)\n\n- **§3.1, done.** While a `spawn` child is checked (`Checker.copyCaptures`), a capture it only reads whose type is\n  `Copy` and not scoped becomes a copy (`CaptureMode.Copy`, `src/check/closures.cee`). `checkSpawn` already exempted\n  copies from N0344 and N0241. Every async closure's frame holds `cee_env`, its own copy of the environment, which\n  `ceeAI_` copies from the closure value (`src/backend/c_async.cee`; the frame report counts it). The closure value is\n  one temporary per literal in the awaiting frame, made again by each iteration of a loop, so without the copy every\n  child of a loop would read the last iteration's environment. N0344's message now says what a child can copy.\n- **§3.2, done (2026-11-01).** A copied capture may be scoped (`borrow T`). The borrow checker\n  (`src/nir/borrow.cee`, `collectHolders`) makes each spawn's environment a holder: the scope's wait uses it, and\n  making it again does not end it (its points-to set already has every iteration's loans), so its loans live from\n  each spawn until the wait. A new `Builtin(AsyncScopeStart, [site])` (1007), evaluated where the body starts, ends\n  them there: without it, a scope inside a loop held the previous run's loans across the loop's back edge\n  (run-pass/async-scope's `rounds` found it). It does nothing in C or the interpreter.\n- **§3.3, done (2026-11-01).** `async move () => …` parses as an async closure with `isMove`; `s.spawn` checks it as\n  a move literal whose result is the scope's (`dynFunctionOf`, mutable). Its environment is the closure's value, moved\n  into the spawn; `ceeAI_` copies it into the child's frame, the child thunk drops the copy when the child returns\n  (`ownsEnvironment`), and a failed spawn drops the original. An `async move` child may move a borrow in, unlike a\n  boxed move literal (§36.5): its environment is then scoped (`capturesLoans`), so the holders follow its loans. The\n  interpreter does the same (`environmentDrop`). Race and all children may not be `async move` (N0209).\n- **Tests:** run-pass/async-spawn-loop (the loop variable, a body local and a copied borrow), run-pass/async-spawn-move\n  (vectors moved into children, a borrow moved in; live=0 at every injected allocation failure), in C, the\n  interpreter and ASan+UBSan; check-fail/async-spawn-copies (N0344), async-spawn-loans (N0240, N0241, N0243 for\n  copied and moved borrows), async-spawn-moved (N0250), async-spawn-moves (N0209); parse-pass/async-syntax\n  (`async move`); `benchmarks/async-reactor/reactor.cee` spawns its holders in a loop.\n",
    219 =>
        "# ADR 0034 — C `char` arrays in imported structs\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-01 (\"`[u8; N]` matches `char[N]`\").\n- Scope: the field types of imported C structs (type-system.md §31.1) and §28.3's `CChar`. Reported by the owner from\n  CosmoOS work (`cosmofs_internal.h:199`, `char pool_name[CFS_LABEL_MAX + 1]`).\n\n## 1. Context\n\nAn imported C struct's field must be declared with exactly its member's C type, and the generated C checks it with\n`_Generic` (§31.1, §31.3). `CChar` is C's `char`, allowed only as a pointee (`*CChar`), because `char`'s signedness\ndiffers between targets (§28.3, N0281). So a member `char name[N]` has no declaration. §31.1 says so: \"A plain `char`\narray cannot be declared\".\n\nChecked 2026-11-01 with ceesafec at `844e755`:\n- `name: [CChar; 16]` is N0281;\n- `name: [u8; 16]` compiles in CeeSafe, then the C fails: `static assertion failed: ceesafec import: struct\n  pool.name is not uint8_t[16]`.\n\nSo a C struct with a `char` array member cannot be used from CeeSafe, even when CeeSafe never touches that member.\n\nC itself allows reading and writing any object through an lvalue of character type (C11 6.5p7). `unsigned char`,\nand `uint8_t` where it exists, has no padding bits and represents every byte. A `char` array read as bytes is\ntherefore the same on every target, whatever `char`'s signedness.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. `[u8; N]` also matches `char[N]` | B. `[CChar; N]` as a field type only |\n|---|---|---|\n| Declaration | `name: [u8; 16]` | `name: [CChar; 16]` |\n| Reading a byte | `p.name[0]`, a `u8` (0–255) | not a value: through a new `.bytes()` view |\n| Passing to C as `char *` | `(borrow p.name as *u8) as *CChar` | `borrow p.name as *CChar` |\n| New language surface | none: one more accepted C type in the import check | a new array element rule and a view API |\n| Matches the C declaration exactly | as bytes (the documented meaning) | yes |\n\n## 3. Proposed decision: A\n\n1. **For an imported struct's member, `[u8; N]` matches `uint8_t[N]`, `char[N]`, `signed char[N]` and\n   `unsigned char[N]`.** The import check accepts any of them. Its size assertion is unchanged and still holds, since\n   the character types are one byte. (An imported member is never an array of arrays: §31.1.)\n2. **A scalar `char` member** is declared `u8`, on the same grounds: C converts the member to `uint8_t` modulo 256\n   when CeeSafe reads it, and back when it writes. Its place may be borrowed: the generated C converts its address to\n   `uint8_t *`, a character-type access (C11 6.5p7). ceesafec cannot tell a `char` member from a `uint8_t` one (only\n   the C compiler sees the header), so it converts the address of every `u8` member of an imported struct.\n3. `CChar` stays a pointee only (§28.3). Function prototypes keep matching the C library exactly.\n4. Elsewhere, outside imported structs (exports, `@repr(C)` structs CeeSafe defines, prototypes), `char` arrays\n   still do not cross: a CeeSafe-defined struct says `uint8_t` in its C.\n\nA because the bytes are the meaning C guarantees, and it adds nothing to learn. B would give `CChar` values that\ncannot be read and need their own API, for the same bytes.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- type-system.md §31.1: the sentence \"A plain `char` array cannot be declared\" is replaced by rules 1 and 2.\n- The C backend (`src/backend/c_skeleton.cee`, the import assertions): `_Generic` gains the character-type cases for\n  a `[u8; N]` or `u8` field. The emitter already reads array members through a cast to `ceeA<N>_u8 *`, which is a\n  character-type access.\n- Tests:\n  - run-pass: a header struct with `char name[16]` and a `char tag` member, read, written and passed to a C function\n    as `char *`, on every host (`--cc cl` too);\n  - the existing `[u8; N]`-is-`uint8_t[N]` import test unchanged.\n\n## 6. Implementation (2026-11-01)\n\n- The import assertions (`src/backend/c_skeleton.cee`) accept the character types for a `[u8; N]` or `u8` field in\n  a nested `_Generic`: `uint8_t` is usually `unsig",
    220 =>
        "ned char`, and one `_Generic` may not name compatible types twice.\n- A borrow of a `u8` member of an imported struct is `(uint8_t *)(uintptr_t)&…` (`isImportedByteMember`,\n  `src/backend/c_body.cee`). Amended from the accepted text, which made such a borrow N0294: ceesafec cannot know the\n  member's C type, so that rule would have forbidden borrowing `uint8_t` members too, which compiled before.\n- Test: `ffi/c-chars.cee` (`char name[16]`, `char tag`, `signed char level[4]`, `unsigned char key[2]`; read,\n  written in place, borrowed, and the name passed to C's `strlen` as `*CChar`), with `-Werror` and under ASan+UBSan.\n\n## 5. Question for the owner\n\n1. A, `[u8; N]` for `char[N]` members? **Yes.**\n",
    221 =>
        "# ADR 0035 — L1 A4b: completion-based I/O (IOCP, io_uring)\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-01 (every question as proposed). Phase 1 of what is left of L1 A4 (ROADMAP,\n  the owner's \"in a multiphased way\").\n- Scope: how lib/std's async socket operations use completion-based kernel interfaces, IOCP on Windows and io_uring\n  on Linux, beside the readiness reactors of L1 A4b (kqueue, epoll, `poll()`, `WSAPoll`); and which backend each host\n  uses. The language does not change.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nEach socket operation in `lib/std/async.cee` is a readiness loop: try the call, and on `CEE_ASYNC_WOULD_BLOCK`\nregister a wait (`ceesafe_async_wait_readable`/`_writable`) and park. A cancelled task leaves at the top of the loop\n(`AsyncLeaf.status()`). Nothing is in flight while the task waits, so leaving is always safe.\n\nMeasured 2026-11-01 (`benchmarks/async-reactor`, ROADMAP §10): epoll and kqueue stay flat as idle connections grow,\nbut Windows does not: `WSAPoll` sees every waiting socket, 28 µs per round trip at 10 idle connections and 3200 µs\nat 1000.\n\nWith IOCP or io_uring the kernel is given the operation and its buffer, and completes it later. That is sound only\nif the buffer outlives the kernel's use of it. ADR 0031 provides the basis: a frame never moves and is never freed\nwhile its task waits. type-system.md §40.7 states the rule: a cancelled completion-based read first cancels the\nkernel's operation and waits for it. What is missing is the protocol between lib/std and the runtime, and the choice\nof backend.\n\n## 2. Options for the protocol\n\n| | A. An operation record in the frame (proposed) | B. Completion behind the readiness calls |\n|---|---|---|\n| Library | one loop per operation over `ceesafe_async_op_*`, the record (`AsyncOp`) a local of the async fn | unchanged |\n| In flight | the record: OVERLAPPED or io_uring's user data, the result | hidden runtime buffers, copied |\n| Buffers | the caller's, lent to the kernel | the runtime's own, then a copy |\n| Cancellation | explicit: cancel in the kernel, wait, then return | the runtime would own buffers that outlive the task |\n\n**A is proposed.** B gives up the reason to use completion I/O (no copy) and needs buffers the runtime allocates\nbehind the program's back, against \"allocation failure is a value\" (ADR 0032 §1).\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n1. **An operation record.** lib/std's `AsyncOp` is opaque runtime storage in the awaiting frame, like `ChannelWait`\n   (ADR 0032 §5). It holds what the backend keeps in flight (an `OVERLAPPED` and its `WSABUF`, or the io_uring\n   submission's user data), the state and the result.\n2. **One call per step.** `ceesafe_async_op_read(op, fd, buffer, length)`, `_send`, `_accept` and `_connect` return\n   the result (a value ≥ 0 or a negated errno) once the operation is complete, or `CEE_ASYNC_PENDING` when the task\n   must park and call again. On a readiness backend a step is today's attempt (try; if it would block, register the\n   wait and say pending). On a completion backend the first step submits and later steps read the completion. The\n   library's loop is the same on every host.\n3. **Cancellation waits for the kernel.** When the task is cancelled with an operation in flight,\n   `ceesafe_async_op_cancel(op)` asks the kernel to cancel it (`CancelIoEx`, `IORING_OP_ASYNC_CANCEL`) and returns\n   whether it is still in flight. The task then parks without being cancellable until the completion arrives. Its\n   frame, and so the buffer, outlives the kernel's use. The test is a buffer-lifetime program: cancel a read while\n   the kernel holds the buffer, under ASan.\n4. **No value is lost** (§40.7, as `recvAsync`): a read that completed before the cancellation reached the kernel\n   returns its bytes (`Ok(n)`), and the next operation sees the cancellation. A send that completed reports what it\n   sent. Only an operation that did nothing returns `Err(Cancelled)`.\n5. **Backends.**\n   - **Windows: IOCP**, the executor's port, with each s",
    222 =>
        "ocket associated once. `WSARecv`, `WSASend`, `AcceptEx` and\n     `ConnectEx` replace `WSAPoll` for sockets. Consoles and files are unchanged.\n   - **Linux: io_uring**, with its rings mapped through the raw system calls (no liburing). It adds `IORING_OP_RECV`,\n     `_SEND`, `_ACCEPT`, `_CONNECT` and `_ASYNC_CANCEL`, and the wait pipe and deadlines through the ring.\n   - epoll, kqueue, `poll()` and `WSAPoll` remain, as readiness backends of the same protocol.\n6. **The interpreter** implements `ceesafe_async_op_*` as readiness steps over its real sockets, as today.\n7. Every executor keeps its structures of L1 A4b. An operation in flight is another reason for a task to wait.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- `runtime/hosted/ceesafe_rt_async.c` and `ceesafe_rt_io.c` gain the op calls and the two backends; `rt.h` declares\n  `ceesafe_async_op` (size asserted against lib/std's `AsyncOp`) and `CEE_ASYNC_PENDING`. c-backend.md §6 describes\n  them. lib/std's accept, connect, read and write become op loops.\n- `benchmarks/async-reactor/run.py` gains a column per backend. Tests: run-pass async-cancel-read (the\n  buffer-lifetime program), the existing async socket programs on every backend, and the cancel sweep, which cancels\n  at every suspension, so also with operations in flight.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. The protocol, A? **Yes.**\n2. **Linux's default:** io_uring where the kernel allows it, or epoll by default with io_uring opt-in\n   (`CEESAFE_REACTOR=uring`) until the benchmark shows it faster? (Proposed: opt-in first. epoll is already flat at\n   18 µs per round trip, and io_uring is often disabled in containers.) **epoll by default, io_uring opt-in.**\n3. **Windows' default:** IOCP, with `WSAPoll` kept behind `CEESAFE_REACTOR_POLL=1`? (Proposed: yes; `WSAPoll` is\n   linear.) **Yes.**\n\n## 6. Implementation (2026-11-01)\n\n- **The protocol** (§3.1–3.4): `AsyncOp` in `lib/std/async.cee` (the runtime's 192-byte record, then the reason the\n  task is leaving). `check()` before each step leaves at once if nothing is in flight, and otherwise asks for the\n  cancellation and steps on. `result()` keeps a completed value and makes an error the cancellation's. accept,\n  connect, read and write are op loops. The runtime's record (`cee_op`) is one layout for every backend, so a\n  cancellation knows what it holds.\n- **IOCP**, Windows' default (§3.5): as c-backend.md §6 describes. Found while measuring:\n  - a task waiting on an operation was first a wake waiter, and every wait scanned them all, so the cost stayed\n    linear; such tasks now have their own state, `on_op`;\n  - `ConnectEx`'s socket was tied to the port before it had an id, so the table did not know, and its next\n    association failed, which sent it to readiness. `ceesafe_socket_adopt` now records the port.\n- **io_uring**, opt-in (§3.5): the variable is `CEESAFE_REACTOR_URING=1`, numeric like `CEESAFE_REACTOR_POLL`, not\n  the `CEESAFE_REACTOR=uring` written above. Found while measuring: at the end of the benchmark, cancelling 2000\n  readers at once overflowed the 512-entry completion queue. The kernel then refused the cancellations' submissions\n  (EBUSY) and kept completions back, so readers waited for ever. Now the queue has 16384 entries, a refused\n  submission is retried after a drain, and an overflow is flushed.\n- The async runtime defines `_GNU_SOURCE` on Linux, for `syscall()`; it still gives `clock_gettime`.\n- **Tests:** run-pass/async-cancel-read (the buffer-lifetime program: a child's read cancelled in flight, its frame\n  freed, the peer writing afterwards; the next read gets every byte); a contract step, \"reactor uring\", running the\n  async programs on io_uring under ASan+UBSan on Linux; `benchmarks/async-reactor/run.py`, a column per backend.\n",
    223 =>
        "# ADR 0036 — L1 A4: async methods through `dyn`\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-01 (every question as proposed). Phase 2 of what is left of L1 A4\n  (ROADMAP, the owner's \"in a multiphased way\").\n- Scope: making an interface with async methods dyn-compatible, and what `await x.m(…)` on a `dyn I` means, checks\n  and lowers to. Lifts N0331's async case (type-system.md §40.1, `src/check/dyn.cee:73-91`).\n\n## 1. Context\n\nToday `dyn I` is N0331 when `I` has an async method: \"a call through a table does not know its frame's size yet\".\nThrough a type parameter (`T: I`) each instance awaits the concrete method, whose frame is known, and that frame is\nembedded in the awaiter's (type-system.md §40.6: a frame is placed once and never moves; c-backend.md §3a: waiting\nstorage is the union `cee_u`).\n\nThrough a table, the awaiter's frame is laid out without knowing which `T` it will meet. What makes this tractable in\nCeeSafe:\n\n- **The program is closed.** ceesafec compiles a program and all its packages at once (generics are instantiated,\n  tables are emitted per pair (`T`, `I`) that a coercion names, type-system.md §37.4). `--split` divides the C into\n  several translation units, but the compiler still sees every coercion. So the set of types that can stand behind a\n  `dyn I` is known at compile time.\n- **Frame sizes are computed by ceesafec** (`frameReport`, `src/backend/c_async.cee`) and asserted in the C.\n- **`Async.boxed(alloc, …)` already exists** (§40.1): a frame from an allocator, the awaiter holding a pointer.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. Room for the largest (proposed) | B. A frame from an allocator per call | C. Keep N0331 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `await x.m(…)` | `T`, as through `T: I` | `Result<T, AllocError>`, or only inside `Async.boxed` | not allowed |\n| Storage | the awaiter's frame, sized for the largest frame of `I.m` among the coerced types | the heap or a given allocator, each call | — |\n| Allocation | none | one per call | — |\n| Cost | an awaiter as large as the largest impl | an allocation and a failure path per call | users write enum dispatch |\n| Open world | needs the whole program (which CeeSafe has) | works with separate compilation | — |\n\n**A is proposed.** It keeps \"allocation failure is a value\" honest without adding a failure to every call (ADR 0032\n§1). It keeps `await x.m()` the same type as through `T: I`, and it is the frame model of §40.6 with a union in\nplace of one member. B is still available, explicitly, through `Async.boxed` (§3.4). It caps the awaiter's size, or\nbreaks a cycle.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n1. **Dyn-compatibility.** An async method no longer makes an interface incompatible. N0331's other rule stands: a method\n   taking `this` by value.\n2. **Checking.** `await x.m(args)` with `x` a `borrow dyn I`, `borrow mut dyn I` or `Box<dyn I, A>` is checked as\n   §37.3's call and §40.2's `await`. The receiver rules are §37.3's. The receiver and the arguments are lent into the\n   callee's frame until it returns (§40.1), so `x` stays borrowed across the `await`. A bare `x.m(args)` is N0342, as\n   for any task.\n3. **Effects.** As §37.3: a call through `dyn I` has the effects of `I.m`'s list. Without a list, it has `suspend`\n   and `alloc` (an async body cannot `sleep`, §40.1). An `impl` method whose effects exceed the list is N0295, at the\n   `impl`.\n4. **Storage.** For each async method `m` of each dyn-used `I`, ceesafec forms the set `S(I, m)` of the impls\n   `T.m` for every `T` coerced to `dyn I` anywhere in the program. An await site through the table gets a member of\n   the waiting union `cee_u`: a union of `S(I, m)`'s frames (a byte if the set is empty: no value can reach the call).\n   `--explain-frames` names the member and its largest impl.\n   - **`await Async.boxed(alloc, x.m(args))`** is allowed: the frame comes from `alloc`, its size and alignment read\n     from the table, and the awaiter holds a pointer, as for a direct call.\n5. **Recursion.** If an impl in `S",
    224 =>
        "(I, m)` awaits, directly or not, a call through `dyn I.m` (or anything whose frame\n   contains `S(I, m)`'s union), the frames would contain themselves: **N0343**. The cycle is named through the table\n   (\"… awaits `dyn Walker.walk`, which may be `Tree.walk`, …\"). Its sure fix is the same: `Async.boxed(SystemHeap\n   {}, x.m(…))!`.\n6. **The table** (§37.4) gains, per async method, the frame's size and alignment and two adapters taking `void *`:\n   `init(frame, receiver, args…)` and `resume(frame)`. The frame is the callee's own `ceeAF_<T.m>`. An adapter is\n   called through a pointer of its own type, so no function is called through a pointer of another type\n   (c-backend.md §2). Cancellation, drops and the debugger's `tasks` command see the callee's frame as for a direct\n   await: the resume chain is the same, one indirect call deeper.\n7. **Interpreter.** The awaited call pushes the concrete method's activation, as a direct await does.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- `src/check/dyn.cee` drops the async rejection. The checker's call through a table and `await` handle a `dyn`\n  receiver. `src/nir/coroutines.cee` builds `S(I, m)` and checks recursion through it. `src/backend/c_dyn.cee`\n  emits the async entries, and `c_async.cee` emits the union members and the indirect await.\n- Spec: type-system.md §37.1, §37.3, §37.4 and §40.1; c-backend.md §2 and §3a; diagnostics.md N0331 and N0343.\n- Tests:\n  - run-pass: two impls of very different frame sizes behind `Box<dyn I>` and `borrow dyn I` in a `Vector`; a\n    borrowed receiver across the `await`; cancellation inside a dyn-awaited method; `Async.boxed` on a dyn call;\n    drops in the callee's frame;\n  - check-fail: N0343 through the table; N0342 for a bare dyn call; N0295 for an impl exceeding the list; N0248 for\n    a `borrow mut this` method through `borrow dyn I`;\n  - frames: a `.frames` snapshot showing the union;\n  - the cancel sweep and `--check-leaks` over the run-pass programs, C and interpreter agreeing.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. Storage, A (room for the largest frame, no allocation)? **Yes.**\n2. `Async.boxed` on a call through `dyn`: allowed, sizes from the table? (Proposed: yes.) **Yes.**\n3. Effects of an async method through `dyn` with no list: `suspend, alloc`? (Proposed: yes.) **Yes.**\n\n## 6. Implementation (2026-11-01)\n\n- **Checking** (§3.1–3.3): `src/check/dyn.cee` no longer rejects async methods. The call and `await` needed no\n  change: a dyn method is declared from the interface's signature, so it is async exactly when the interface's is.\n  `effectsOf` (`src/nir/effect_check.cee`) gives an async dyn method without a list `suspend` and `alloc`.\n- **The frame** (§3.4, §3.6): `struct ceeAF_dyn_<D…>_m<k>` as c-backend.md §3a describes, with `ceeAI_`, `ceeAR_`,\n  `ceeAT_` and `ceeAN_` like any async fn's, so the await, `Async.run` and `Async.boxed` paths are unchanged except\n  for the boxed frame's size. Its members are the methods in slot `k` of every table of the dyn type\n  (`dynAsyncImpls`), so of every type a coercion names. The frame is emitted once every member's is, and its size\n  and the union's offset are asserted against ceesafec's arithmetic (`dynFrameParts`). `--explain-frames` reports it\n  at each await (`explainDynAwaits`).\n- **The table** (§3.6): an async slot's entry is `void (*m<k>)(void *frame, void *self, args…)` with `uint64_t\n  s<k>`, the frame's size for that type, beside it. The entry stores the resume adapter `…_<k>_r` and the size in\n  the frame, then starts the method in its member.\n- **Recursion** (§3.5): the coroutine pass follows a call through `dyn` to every impl it may reach. N0343 says so:\n  \"contain itself through 'dyn Node.sum', which may be its own\". Only coerced types count, so a type that\n  is never made a `dyn Node` cannot close a cycle.\n- **The interpreter** needed no change: `prepareCall` already resolves a dyn call to the receiver's method.\n- **Tests:**\n  - run-pass/async-dyn: boxes in a `Vector`, refe",
    225 =>
        "rences, a void method, drops in the callee's frame, `Async.run` of\n    a call through the table, a cancelled callee, `Async.boxed` with a counting allocator, and a tree of\n    `Box<dyn Node>` summed recursively through `Async.boxed`;\n  - check-fail/async-dyn (N0342, N0248) and check-fail/async-dyn-frames (N0343 through the table, N0295 for an impl\n    exceeding the list);\n  - frames/async-dyn (the `.frames` snapshot with the dyn line);\n  - check-fail/async-interface no longer expects N0331.\n",
    226 =>
        "# ADR 0037 — L1 A4: higher-order functions that suspend when their closure does\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-01 (every question as proposed). Phase 3 of what is left of L1 A4\n  (ROADMAP, the owner's \"in a multiphased way\").\n- Scope: letting a function that takes a closure (`forEach`, `any`, `retain`, a user's `retry(op)`) be called with an\n  async closure, so that the call is a task that suspends where the closure does. It extends `@effectsOf` (§32.5,\n  ADR 0020) and async closures (§40.3).\n\n## 1. Context\n\nToday a closure type never has `suspend` (§36.3): \"only an async closure's call suspends, and that is a task\". So\n`values.forEach(async (v) => await send(v))` is rejected: `forEach` is a sync function and cannot await its argument.\nEach higher-order function would need an async twin (`forEachAsync`, `anyAsync`, …). Rust has the same split, and its\nkeyword generics are not settled.\n\nCeeSafe already has the two pieces a third way needs:\n\n- **`@effectsOf(p)`** (§32.5) makes a function's effects depend on its closure argument's: a call has the callee's own\n  effects plus the argument's. `suspend` is an effect (§32.1), so \"the call suspends when the argument does\" is the\n  same rule, one effect further.\n- **The program is compiled whole**, and calls of closures that are known are already rewritten into a variant of the\n  callee bound to the literal's body (optimisations.md §5). An async argument needs that variant always, since the\n  callee's frame must embed the literal's.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. Async instances of `@effectsOf` functions (proposed) | B. An `async?` marker on functions | C. Async twins in the library |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Written | `await values.forEach(async (v) => …)` | `async? fn forEach(…)`, then as A | `await values.forEachAsync(async (v) => …)` |\n| New syntax | none | a marker, its rules for the body | none |\n| Which functions | every `@effectsOf` function | those marked | those written twice |\n| Library | `forEach`, `any`, `all`, `retain` work as they are | each marked | each duplicated |\n| Body checked | per instance, as an async body | per instance | once each |\n\n**A is proposed.** `@effectsOf` already says \"my effects are my argument's\", and `suspend` is an effect. B adds\nsyntax for what the attribute already states. C doubles the library and every user's higher-order code.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n1. **An async argument.** An `@effectsOf` parameter `p` of type `fn(A…) -> R` (by reference or not) accepts an\n   async closure literal whose task's result is `R`, or a named async fn of that signature (§36.1), unless its type\n   has an effect list without `suspend` (N0295 then, as for any argument exceeding its list). In such a parameter's\n   type alone, an effect list may name `suspend` (N0290 elsewhere, as today).\n2. **The call is a task.** A call that passes an async argument for any `@effectsOf` parameter is a task (§40.1):\n   `await f(…)`, `Async.run(f(…))` or a spawn's body, N0342 elsewhere. Its value is `f`'s result.\n3. **Its async instance.** The task runs `f`'s *async instance* for those arguments. It is a copy of `f` in which\n   each call through `p`, or through a local that holds `p` (§32.5's rule), awaits the argument, and passing `p` on\n   to another function's `@effectsOf` parameter awaits that function's async instance. There is one instance per\n   (`f`, the literals or functions bound to its parameters), as for closure-call variants (optimisations.md §5.1). It\n   is made whether or not that optimisation is on, because it is semantics, not an optimisation.\n4. **The instance is checked as an async body** (§40.1) after effect inference, with its chain naming the call that\n   made it (\"in the async instance of 'forEach' for the closure at main.cee:12\"):\n   - a context that forbids `sleep`: a blocking call in `f` is N0295 at the caller's call, naming it;\n   - `suspend` while a `@context(atomic)` value may be live: N0295;\n   - recursion through the instan",
    227 =>
        "ce (`f` calling itself with `p`): N0343, at the caller's call. There is no sure fix\n     there, since the recursion is in `f`.\n   Borrow checking is not repeated: an await is checked as a call (§40.2), and `f`'s calls through `p` were checked\n   as calls.\n5. **Effects** of the call: `f`'s own, the argument's (with `suspend`), and the instance's (§32.5's table, unchanged\n   otherwise).\n6. **Cancellation** is the argument's (§40.7). `f` sees only the values its awaits return. A cancelled\n   `forEach` keeps calling its closure, whose leaf operations return `Err` at once. A function that should stop\n   early must look at the results, as for any closure.\n7. **lib/core.** `forEach`, `any`, `all` and `retain` work with async closures as they are. `sortBy`'s introsort is\n   recursive, so its async instance would be N0343. It becomes iterative (an explicit stack of at most\n   `2 · log2(length)` ranges, in the frame), so that `await s.sortBy(async (a, b) => …)` works too (question 3).\n8. **The interpreter** runs an instance like any async fn. **The C backend** emits it like any async fn, named\n   `ceeAS<n>_<f>`, with its frame embedding the literal's.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- The checker: an `@effectsOf` parameter accepts async arguments; such a call is a task. NIR: a pass after\n  `buildNir`, before effect checking, makes the async instances (from a worklist, deterministic as §5.1's) and turns\n  calls through bound parameters into awaits. The effect checker and the coroutine pass treat instances as async\n  fns with their chain. The backend and the interpreter take instances where they take async fns.\n- lib/core's introsort becomes iterative; `benchmarks/sort` must not regress (guide/performance.md).\n- Spec: type-system.md §32.5, §36.3, §40.1, §40.3; optimisations.md §5.1 (instances are not variants);\n  c-backend.md §3a; diagnostics.md N0295, N0342 and N0343.\n- Tests:\n  - run-pass: `forEach`, `any`, `retain` and `sortBy` with async closures; a user's `retry(op)`; a named async fn\n    as the argument; `p` passed on to another `@effectsOf` function; an instance cancelled midway; C and the\n    interpreter agreeing;\n  - check-fail: N0342 for an unawaited call; N0295 for a blocking call in the instance and for a list without\n    `suspend`; N0343 for a recursive instance; N0290 for `suspend` in a non-`@effectsOf` type;\n  - frames: an instance's frame;\n  - the cancel sweep over the run-pass programs.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. The approach, A (async instances of every `@effectsOf` function, no new syntax)? **Yes.**\n2. Async arguments: closure literals and named async fns only (there is no type of async closure values to store or\n   pass otherwise)? **Yes.**\n3. `sortBy`: make introsort iterative, so it accepts an async comparator? Or leave it recursive, with N0343 at an\n   async call? **Iterative.**\n\n## 6. Implementation (2026-11-01)\n\n- **Checking** (§3.1–3.2): `synthCall` (`src/check/expr.cee`) checks an async closure literal for a parameter that\n  `@effectsOf` names (`namesEffectsOf`, before `finishFunctionFacts` resolves the attribute), and a named async fn\n  becomes an async adapter there (`adapterClosure`, whose body awaits it). Such a call is a task\n  (`checkSuspendingCall`). `v.retain(async …)`, a builtin, does the same, and marks its call of lib/core's retain as\n  awaited. An async fn is a function or closure value nowhere else: N0298. This also closes a hole that predates\n  this ADR: `let g: fn(u32) -> u32 = asyncFn;` was accepted, and its address was null in C.\n- **Instances** (§3.3): `src/nir/async_instances.cee`, after drop elaboration and before the effect checker. An\n  instance is a HIR function copied from `f` with `asyncInstanceOf` set (so `isAsyncFunction` holds), its call site in\n  `siteFile`/`siteStart`/`siteEnd`, and the bound parameters dropped from its `@effectsOf` list. Its NIR is `f`'s with\n  new statements where calls change. The known-closure analysis is closure specialisation's (`specialisedClo",
    228 =>
        "sures`).\n  `Async.boxed(alloc, f(…))` takes the instance too.\n- **Errors** (§3.4) are reported at the making call: the effect checker reports an instance's own operations there\n  and not again in its caller, and the coroutine pass reports a recursive instance there.\n- **Effect lists:** §36.3 already lets a type's list name `suspend`, so there is no new N0290. §3.1's \"N0290\n  elsewhere, as today\" was wrong; an `@effects(suspend)` type outside `@effectsOf` simply has that effect.\n- **`--explain-frames`** reports an instance at its call: \"the async instance of 'twice' for this call\".\n- **sortBy** (§3.7): introsort keeps its pending ranges in three arrays of 64 in its frame, the shorter side first, so\n  the comparator is called in the order the recursion called it. Measured 2026-11-01 on macOS (Apple clang, `-O2`, a\n  `sortBy` of 2 000 000 `u32`, 15 interleaved runs of each build): 126.4 ms recursive, 128.0 ms iterative (+1.3%; two\n  copies of one build differ by 0.1%). One array of a range struct measured 128.8 ms, so it is not used.\n- **Tests:** run-pass/async-effects-of (lib/core's forEach, any, all, retain and sortBy; a user's functions, one\n  passing its closure on; a named async fn; Async.boxed; an instance in a spawned child, cancelled midway);\n  check-fail/async-effects-of (N0342, N0298) and async-effects-of-instances (N0295 twice, N0343, N0342);\n  frames/async-effects-of.\n",
    229 =>
        "# ADR 0038 — L1 A4e: an opt-in work-stealing executor\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-01 (every question as proposed). Phase 4 of what is left of L1 A4\n  (ROADMAP, the owner's \"in a multiphased way\").\n- Scope: letting one `Async.run` use several threads, by moving tasks from a busy thread to an idle one. It covers\n  which tasks may move, what the compiler checks, and the runtime's shape.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nADR 0031 §3.7 chose one executor per thread, where a task never moves. So tasks need not be `Send`, and children\nborrow from their parent's frame with no thread rule (§40.5). To use several cores today, a program starts threads,\neach with its own `Async.run`, and connects them with channels (ADR 0032).\n\nThat leaves a common case awkward: a scope that fans out CPU-heavy children (parse, hash, compress per item). They all\nrun on one thread, and splitting them across threads by hand means channels and owned copies of the input.\n\nCeeSafe already has the rule that makes borrowing across threads safe: **`Thread.both`'s capture rule** (§38.6,\nADR 0018). A capture used shared needs `Share`, a capture used mutably needs `Send`, with references counting as\n`Share`/`Send` when their target is. Both closures end before `both` returns, so their loans are scoped. A scope's\nchildren end before the scope's wait returns (§40.5), the same structure.\n\nThe proposal (docs/async-await-proposal.md §6) noted that \"a later opt-in work-stealing executor can require frames to\nbe `Send`; the compiler knows which locals live across which `await`\".\n\n## 2. Options\n\n### 2.1 What may move\n\n| | A. Marked children, before they start (proposed) | B. Every task, at any suspension | C. Marked children, at any suspension |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Opt-in | `s.spawnSend(child)` | `Async.runWorkers(n, task)` makes every task movable | `s.spawnSend(child)` |\n| Checked | the child's captures, by §38.6's rule, and its result | every frame of every task: each local live across an `await` must be `Send` | the child's captures and every frame it awaits |\n| Diagnostic | N0334 naming the capture (as `Thread.both`) | N0334 naming the local and the await | as B, for the child's tree |\n| Runtime | a shared queue of unstarted children; each worker is today's executor | tasks, timers and I/O registrations move between reactors | as B |\n| I/O, timers | the worker that started the task owns them, as today | a registration must move with the task | as B |\n\n**A is proposed.** A started task stays on the worker that first ran it, so its frame never crosses threads and need not\nbe `Send`. Only what the child is given crosses: its captures, as `Thread.both`'s rule checks them, and its result. Each\nworker is the executor that exists today, with its own reactor, timers and wake list. The new runtime piece is a queue\nof unstarted children that idle workers take from.\n\nThe cost is balance. A long child cannot be split once it has started. Fan-out workloads have many children, and those\nspread well. B and C would also need every reactor to hand over registrations, which none of the four backends does.\n\n### 2.2 Where the workers come from\n\n| | D. `Async.runWorkers(n, task)` (proposed) | E. A process-wide pool, sized to the cores |\n|---|---|---|\n| Threads | `n − 1` started for this run and joined when it returns; the caller is the n-th | started on the first run that needs one, never joined |\n| Without it | `spawnSend` children run on the scope's thread, as `spawn`'s | — |\n| Failure | a worker that cannot start leaves fewer workers; the result is the same | as D |\n\n**D is proposed.** It is explicit, scoped like everything else in the model, and needs no global state. A library that\ncalls `spawnSend` works unchanged under plain `Async.run`.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n1. **`s.spawnSend(child)`** in an `Async.scope` body, alongside `spawn` (§40.5). It takes an async closure literal and\n   returns `Result<void, AllocError>`, like `spawn`. Its captures follow §38.6's rule: a sh",
    230 =>
        "ared capture needs `Share`\n   and a mutable one `Send`, references counting by their target, and a captured closure is refused (**N0334**,\n   naming the capture). Its result's error type must be `Send` (**N0334**). The scope-loan rule (§40.5, ADR 0033)\n   applies unchanged, so two children may not both change one value (N0241).\n2. **A child may move only before it starts.** An idle worker of the same run may take it from the scope's thread.\n   Once started, a task runs only on the worker that started it, and every task it awaits or spawns starts there.\n   Nothing about frames is checked, because frames do not move.\n3. **`Async.runWorkers(n, task)`** runs `task` like `Async.run` (§40.4), with `n − 1` more worker threads for the run\n   (`n ≥ 1`). It returns when the task has returned and every worker is idle. The workers are joined then.\n   `Async.run(task)` is `Async.runWorkers(1, task)`. A worker that cannot be started is skipped: the result is the\n   same, and a program cannot tell how many ran (as `Thread.both`).\n4. **Cancellation and the scope** work across threads. A child's error cancels its siblings wherever they run: the\n   flag is atomic, and a worker's task is woken through that worker's wake pipe (ADR 0032 §5). The scope's count of\n   live children is atomic. The child that finishes last wakes the parent across threads.\n5. **Runtime.** One run shares a queue of unstarted `spawnSend` children (a mutex-protected deque in v1, which the\n   benchmark may later replace). Each worker is a `ceesafe_executor` as today. A worker takes from the queue only when\n   its ready queue is empty and nothing it waits for is due. The `tasks` command of the debugger (L1 A3) lists each\n   worker's tasks.\n6. **The interpreter** has one thread: `spawnSend` is `spawn`, and `runWorkers(n, …)` is `run`. A program's output must\n   not depend on which worker ran what (§40.8), which the corpus checks by comparing C with the interpreter.\n7. **Profiles.** Hosted only, like `Thread` (§38.5). The freestanding executor (phase 5) has one worker.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- Checker: `spawnSend` beside `spawn` (`src/check/async.cee`), with §38.6's capture rule (`checkThreadBoth`'s,\n  `src/check/threads.cee`); `runWorkers` beside `run`. NIR and backend: `spawnSend` lowers like `spawn` with a flag,\n  `runWorkers` like `run` with a worker count. Runtime: the run's shared queue, atomic scope counts and cancellation,\n  cross-thread wakes of a parent, workers started and joined by `ceesafe_async_run_workers`.\n- Spec: type-system.md §40.4, §40.5 and §40.8; c-backend.md §3a and §6; diagnostics.md N0334; profiles.md.\n- Tests:\n  - run-pass: a scope fanning out `spawnSend` children over a borrowed `Vector` (the same output under `run` and\n    `runWorkers(4, …)`, and in the interpreter); cancellation across workers; children doing I/O and timers on\n    their own worker;\n  - check-fail: N0334 for a non-`Share` shared capture, a non-`Send` mutable capture, a captured closure, and a\n    non-`Send` error type;\n  - TSan on Linux for the runtime (a new contract step if clang has it), plus the cancel sweep;\n  - `benchmarks/async-parallel`: a CPU-bound fan-out with its speed-up at 1, 2 and 4 workers on Debian and macOS.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. What may move: **A**, marked children before they start (no frame checks), rather than B or C (every task, or\n   marked trees, at any suspension, with `Send` frames)? **A.**\n2. Workers: **D**, `Async.runWorkers(n, task)` per run, rather than a process-wide pool? **D.**\n3. The name `spawnSend`? (Alternatives: `spawnAnywhere`, `spawnParallel`.) **`spawnSend`.**\n\n## 6. Implementation (2026-11-01)\n\n- **Checking** (§3.1, §3.3): `checkSpawn` (`src/check/async.cee`) takes `send`. `checkSendChild` applies §38.6's rule\n  through `isForkSafe`, refuses captured closures, and requires a `Send` error type and the system heap. These are\n  the messages of `Thread.both`, naming `spawnSend`. A send child's HIR `Spawn` is lis",
    231 =>
        "ted in `c.sendSpawns`, and NIR\n  marks its `Builtin(AsyncSpawn)` with the role `taskSend`. `Async.runWorkers(n, task)` is\n  `BuiltinCall(AsyncRunWorkers, [n, RunTask(task)])`, lowered to `Builtin(AsyncWorkers, [n])` then the run.\n  `spawn` returns `void` (a failed spawn is recorded in the scope, §40.5), so `spawnSend` does too, not the\n  `Result<void, AllocError>` that §3.1 says.\n- **Runtime** (§3.4–3.5), `runtime/hosted/ceesafe_rt_async.c`:\n  - The loop of `ceesafe_async_run` is shared by the caller and `cee_worker` threads (`cee_exec_begin`,\n    `cee_exec_loop`, `cee_exec_end`).\n  - `cee_run` holds a mutex, the queue of published children with an atomic count, an atomic `done`, a\n    `cancel_epoch`, and the workers' executors.\n  - A child is queued only by `ceesafe_async_publish`, after its frame is initialised.\n  - A shared scope's `live`, `failed` and `waiter` change under the mutex. `cancelled` is atomic, and so is a task's.\n  - The waiting parent parks `on_wake`, which cancellation does not wake. The child that finishes last wakes it\n    through its executor.\n  - Wake pipes are non-blocking: a busy worker drains its pipe only when it waits.\n  - `ceesafe_scope` grew from 24 to 32 bytes. A task grew from 128 to 144 (its executor `home`, an atomic\n    `cancelled`, `unpublished`), and `ceesafe_task_slot` with it. The frame layouts follow (`scopeLayout`,\n    `taskSlotLayout`), and so does the interpreter's model of a child's block.\n- **Found by ThreadSanitizer:** each `spawnSend` rewrote the scope's `shared` flag while workers read it. Now only the\n  first does, before any child is published.\n- **Not done:** §3.5 said the debugger's `tasks` command lists every worker's tasks. It lists the stopped thread's\n  executor, as before.\n- **Tests:**\n  - run-pass/async-workers: a fan-out with 1 and 4 workers, cancellation across workers, children with scopes of\n    their own;\n  - check-fail/async-workers: a capture read that is not `Share`, one changed that is not `Send`, a captured closure,\n    an error type that is not `Send`, `Async.scopeIn` with a user allocator;\n  - a contract step \"run tsan\": the async programs under ThreadSanitizer, on macOS and Linux;\n  - the cancel and fault sweeps reach the new program (47 and 63 points on macOS, none failing);\n  - `benchmarks/async-parallel` (macOS, 8 cores, 32 children): 412 ms with 1 worker, 194 with 2 (2.12x), 111 with 4\n    (3.71x), 70 with 8 (5.89x).\n",
    232 =>
        "# ADR 0039 — L1 A4c: an executor for freestanding code, and a CosmoOS boot test\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-01 (every question as proposed). Phase 5, the last of what is left of\n  L1 A4 (ROADMAP, the owner's \"in a multiphased way\").\n- Scope: running async tasks where there is no operating system below, as in a kernel, through hooks the environment\n  provides. It covers what the freestanding profile allows of §40, and a CosmoOS self-test that runs tasks at boot.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nIn the freestanding profile (profiles.md §4) async fns compile, but nothing can run them: `Async` is N0293 (\"needs\nthe hosted runtime\"). The hosted executor (`runtime/hosted/ceesafe_rt_async.c`) mixes two kinds of code:\n- portable bookkeeping: the ready queue, the deadline heap, the wake list, scopes, cancellation, shields, joins;\n- platform services: `poll`, kqueue, epoll, IOCP, io_uring, pipes, `clock_gettime`, threads, `malloc`.\n\nCosmoOS, surveyed read-only on 2026-11-01:\n- **CeeSafe is already in its build.** `scripts/cee-gen.py:183` runs `ceesafec build --profile freestanding\n  --no-float --kernel cosmoos`, and CeeSafe 0.2.0 is pinned at `:64`.\n- **A boot self-test exists.** `kernel/ceesafe/kcore_test.cee` is registered as `cee-kcore` in\n  `kernel/core/selftest.c:583`. `make test` (Makefile:243) runs every self-test under QEMU\n  (`tests/boot/run_boot_test.py`), keyed on `SELFTEST: <name> ... ok`.\n- **The kernel has what an executor needs:**\n  - a monotonic clock, `clock_now_ns` (`kernel/include/kernel/timer.h:63`, lock-free);\n  - wait queues: `wait_event_timeout` (`wait.h:182`), with wakers safe in IRQ context (`wait.h:10-11`);\n  - `complete()`, callable from IRQ (`completion.h:4`);\n  - timers whose callbacks run in IRQ context (`timer.h:255-271`).\n- No `cosmoos-kernel` or `cosmoos-user` profile exists yet in CeeSafe, so the kernel builds with `freestanding`.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. The executor in the core runtime, with environment hooks (proposed) | B. Frames only: the environment drives `ceeAR_` itself | C. Keep N0293 until a cosmoos profile |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Runtime | the portable part of the executor moves to `runtime/core/ceesafe_rt_exec.c`, compiled in every profile; the hosted file keeps the reactors | none | — |\n| The environment gives | `now`, `idle(deadline)`, `wake()`; and memory, through `Async.scopeIn`'s allocator | a loop calling resume functions, with its own timers and scopes | — |\n| Language in freestanding | `Async.run`, `scopeIn`, `spawn`, `race`, `all`, `shield`, cancellation, `Timer.sleep`, an IRQ-safe `AsyncSignal` | `async fn` and `await` only: no scopes, no timers | async fns compile; nothing runs them |\n| Cost | splitting the executor, a hook ABI to keep stable | each environment rewrites scheduling, cancellation, scopes | — |\n\n**A is proposed.** Scheduling, scopes and cancellation are the hard, tested part (cancel sweep, TSan), and an\nenvironment should not rewrite them. The hooks are the three things only the environment knows: the time, how to idle,\nand how an interrupt or another thread ends the idling.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n1. **The split.** The executor's portable part moves to `runtime/core/ceesafe_rt_exec.c`: tasks, the ready queue, the\n   deadline heap, wakes, scopes, joins, shields and cancellation. It compiles in every profile. In the hosted profile\n   `runtime/hosted/ceesafe_rt_async.c` implements the hooks with its reactors, and adds descriptors, operations\n   (ADR 0035), workers (ADR 0038) and `Async.blocking`. Nothing hosted changes behaviour, and the hosted contract suite\n   is the check.\n2. **The environment's hooks**, declared in `rt.h`, defined by a freestanding environment:\n   - `uint64_t ceesafe_env_now_ms(void)`: a monotonic clock in milliseconds;\n   - `void ceesafe_env_idle(void *executor, uint64_t deadline_ms)`: block the calling thread until the deadline\n     (`UINT64_MAX`: none) or a wake;\n   - `void ceesafe_env_wake(void *executor)`: end that idling. It must be s",
    233 =>
        "afe from an interrupt handler and from\n     another thread, and a wake before the idle must not be lost.\n   A missing hook is a link error, as for `ceesafe_panic` (profiles.md §4.2).\n3. **What freestanding code may use of §40:**\n   - `Async.run`, `await`, `Async.scopeIn(alloc, …)` (the system heap does not exist, so `Async.scope` stays N0293),\n     `spawn`, `race`, `all`, `shield`, `checkpoint`, `isCancelled`, and `Async.boxed` with a user allocator;\n   - `Timer.sleep` and `Timer.now`, moving from lib/std to lib/core and implemented over the hooks;\n   - **`AsyncSignal`**, a new lib/core type. `await signal.wait()` parks the task until `signal.raise()`, which is\n     `@effects()`, so an `@context(irq)` handler may call it. It is a flag plus the waiting task, and the hook wakes\n     the executor. It is how a driver's interrupt resumes a task.\n   - Still hosted only (N0293): sockets, console and file I/O, `Async.blocking`, `spawnSend` and `runWorkers`,\n     channels.\n4. **Contexts.** An async fn's body forbids `sleep` (§40.1), so a kernel task cannot call a blocking kernel function:\n   the kernel's own blocking primitives are `sleep` (CosmoOS's bindings say so), and the task waits through\n   `AsyncSignal` instead.\n5. **The CosmoOS boot test.** A self-test `cee-async`:\n   - `kernel/ceesafe/async_test.cee` runs a scope of tasks that sleep, race, are cancelled, and wait on an\n     `AsyncSignal` that a kernel timer callback (IRQ context) raises;\n   - C glue implements the three hooks with `clock_now_ns`, a wait queue and `wait_event_timeout`;\n   - it is registered in `kernel/core/selftest.c` beside `cee-kcore`.\n   Gate: `make test` prints `SELFTEST: cee-async ... ok` and `SELFTEST: PASS`. Where the change is made in CosmoOS is\n   question 3.\n6. **Tests in CeeSafe:**\n   - a freestanding harness in `tools/freestanding_check.py`: a host C file implements the hooks with\n     `nanosleep`/`clock_gettime` and a thread raising a signal, and links a freestanding object, so the freestanding\n     executor runs on every contract host;\n   - the cancel sweep over it;\n   - check-fail for N0293 on what stays hosted (`Async.scope`, a socket, `spawnSend`) and for `AsyncSignal.wait` in a\n     sync fn.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- Runtime: the split, the hooks, `AsyncSignal`'s runtime part. lib/core gains `Timer` and `AsyncSignal`, and lib/std\n  keeps the rest of `async.cee`.\n- The checker's N0293 list (profiles.md §4.1) gets shorter by exactly §3.3's list. The C backend links\n  `ceesafe_rt_exec.c` whenever the program uses `ceesafe_async_`.\n- Spec: profiles.md §1 and §4.1–4.2, type-system.md §40.4 and §40.8, c-backend.md §6.\n- CosmoOS needs a `ceesafec` that has this, so its pin (`scripts/cee-gen.py:64`, 0.2.0) moves: question 4.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. The approach, A (the executor in the core runtime, with three environment hooks)? **Yes.**\n2. Freestanding's share of §40, as §3.3 lists it, with `AsyncSignal` as the interrupt-to-task bridge? **Yes.**\n3. **Where the CosmoOS change is made.**\n   - (a) On a new branch in a separate git worktree of `~/dev/CosmoOS`, leaving your working tree and its untracked\n     files alone; you review and merge. (Proposed.)\n   - (b) As a patch kept in CeeSafe (`tests/cosmoos/`), which you apply.\n   - (c) Not in this phase: the CeeSafe-side harness only.\n\n   **(a).**\n4. CosmoOS pins ceesafec 0.2.0. On that branch, should the pin move to this tree's ceesafec (0.3.0-dev, built from\n   the commit that adds this)? Or do you want a release first? **Move it to this tree.**\n\n## 6. Implementation (2026-11-01)\n\n- **The split** (§3.1) is by `CEE_FREESTANDING` within `runtime/hosted/ceesafe_rt_async.c`, not a file of its own in\n  `runtime/core`. The file was already one executor with platform branches (POSIX, Windows, kqueue, epoll,\n  io_uring), and freestanding is one more. The portable part is what every branch keeps: the queue, scopes, joins,\n  shields, cancellation and `AsyncSignal`. The backend embeds the",
    234 =>
        " file, with the define, in a freestanding program\n  that uses async. Built that way, its object has no `.data`, `.bss` or TLS. Its undefined symbols are the hooks,\n  `ceesafe_panic` and `memset`.\n- **Hooks** (§3.2): there are **four**, not three. `void **ceesafe_env_slot(void)` names the calling thread's\n  executor, since the profile has no thread-local storage and no mutable globals (profiles.md §4.4). `Async.run`\n  keeps the executor in its own frame and puts it in the slot. The wake order and the root the sweep cancels became\n  the executor's fields. With no heap for its arrays, the freestanding executor finds deadlines by walking its parked\n  tasks. A stuck executor, or one asked for a system-heap child, panics with the new `CEE_PANIC_ASYNC`.\n- **Library** (§3.3): lib/core's `async.cee` holds `AsyncError`, `Timer`, `AsyncControl`, `AsyncLeaf.status`,\n  `Raced` and the joins, moved from lib/std, and `AsyncSignal` (`@noncopy`, four words: raised, the waiting task, its\n  executor, a raise in progress). lib/std keeps the console, sockets and files, its own steps as `AsyncIo`. The\n  `IoError` type is allowed in freestanding code, since `AsyncError.Io` names it, but making one stays N0293.\n  `Async.scope`, `Async.blocking`, `Async.runWorkers` and `spawnSend` are N0293 there.\n- **AsyncSignal's protocol:** `raise` marks itself `busy`, sets `raised`, takes the waiting task by swapping the\n  `task` word to 0, wakes it, and clears `busy`. A take that loses that race waits until `busy` clears, then keeps\n  the raise for its next take. So no raise touches a task that may have returned. A raise only sets the task's woken\n  flag and pokes its executor, never its queues, as it may be an interrupt's. `AsyncSignal` works in the hosted\n  profile too (another thread, `Async.blocking`'s worker) and in the interpreter.\n- **Found by the CosmoOS boot test:** the executor asked for a child's block with `sizeof(cee_max_align_t)` as the\n  alignment, and rounded the task head the same way. On x86-64 that is 32, but the type's alignment is 16, which\n  CosmoOS's `kmalloc` gives and a host's `posix_memalign` hid. It now uses `_Alignof`, on every profile.\n- **The freestanding tool:** `tools/freestanding_check.py` allows the four hooks. It allows `bzero` on a Darwin host,\n  where LLVM makes a zeroing `memset` a call of `bzero` whatever the flags. It now implements profiles.md §4.4's\n  exception for a program's own static items, so `main.c` may have `.bss`, while the runtime may not.\n- **CosmoOS** (§3.5, question 3: a worktree branch): branch `ceesafe-async-boot` of `~/dev/CosmoOS`, in\n  `~/dev/CosmoOS-ceesafe-async`. It adds:\n  - `kernel/ceesafe/async_test.cee` and `async_selftest.c`, the hooks over `clock_now_ns`, a wait queue with a\n    pending flag, and `waitqueue_wake_all`. A kernel timer's callback raises the test's `AsyncSignal` in interrupt\n    context.\n  - the self-test `cee-async`, registered after `cee-kcore`;\n  - the async runtime file in `scripts/cee-gen.py` (emitted only by ports that use async) and in `kernel/kernel.mk`;\n  - the pin moved to `0.3.0-dev` (question 4).\n  The glue keeps one slot for the kernel, enough for the self-test. It panics if a second thread runs tasks while one\n  does; a per-thread slot comes with the first driver that runs tasks.\n- **Tests:**\n  - freestanding/async with its harness `async.c`: the hooks over a mutex and a condition variable, and a thread\n    raising the signal as an interrupt would. It runs on the host, and compiles for both CosmoOS triples with the\n    strict warnings and with `ld.lld`;\n  - run-pass/async-signal, in C, in the interpreter and under ThreadSanitizer;\n  - check-fail/freestanding-hosted-names: N0293 for `Async.scope`, `Async.blocking` and `Async.runWorkers`, and the\n    `IoError` type now accepted;\n  - check-pass/effects-of.hir: closure numbers +8 by a reviewed edit, because lib/core gained closures;\n  - CosmoOS `gmake test` on x86_64: `SELFTEST: cee-async ... ok (28 ms)`, `SELFTEST: P",
    235 => "ASS (525 tests)`, `boot-test:\n    PASS`.\n",
    236 =>
        "# ADR 0040 — The hosted executor in CosmoOS userland\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-01 (\"tackle the open works\", of which this was one); after review, the\n  three choices of §5 accepted as proposed (\"I accept ADR 0040's choices as proposed\").\n- Scope: running async programs (type-system.md §40) as CosmoOS user programs, the target `integrations/cosmoos/c1.py`\n  builds for (`CEE_COSMOOS`; profiles.md §2).\n\n## 1. Context\n\nA CeeSafe program built for CosmoOS userland compiles the hosted runtime with `-DCEE_COSMOOS` against CosmoOS's own\nlibc. Since the async work (ADRs 0031–0038) the hosted executor, `runtime/hosted/ceesafe_rt_async.c`, needs:\n\n- `poll()`, or kqueue, epoll or io_uring, to wait for descriptors and deadlines;\n- a pipe made non-blocking with `fcntl`, which another thread writes to end that wait;\n- `pthread` mutexes and condition variables for channels (ADR 0032) and `sched_yield` while spinning.\n\nCosmoOS's libc, surveyed read-only at CosmoOS `25742b40`, has none of these: no `poll.h`, `pthread.h`, `sched.h` or\n`fcntl()`. A descriptor there waits or not per call (`MSG_DONTWAIT`) or through CosmoOS's own completion ring\n(`SYS_aio_*`), never by a flag set on it. It does have, as public libc functions:\n\n- threads, `cosmo_thread_start`/`join` (`libc/include/cosmo/thread.h`), which `ceesafe_rt_thread.c` already uses;\n- `cosmo_mutex_t` and `cosmo_cond_t`, with `cosmo_cond_timedwait(c, m, timeout_ns)`;\n- `clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC)` and `nanosleep`.\n\nSo no async program builds for CosmoOS userland (`c1.py --corpus` skips all 22), and since commit `ea582b7` opening\nan async socket there is `ENOSYS` (`ceesafe_rt_io.c`).\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. A CosmoOS backend in the hosted executor (proposed) | B. The freestanding executor (ADR 0039) with userland hooks | C. A backend on CosmoOS's completion ring |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| What runs | everything hosted async does but sockets: timers, scopes, cancellation, shields, channels, `Async.blocking`, `AsyncFiles`, `runWorkers`, `spawnSend`, `AsyncConsole`, `AsyncSignal` | only what freestanding allows: no `blocking`, `runWorkers`, `spawnSend` or channels between threads (§40.5, N0293) | as A, and async sockets |\n| Waiting | the executor's own condition variable, timed to the earliest deadline; another thread's wake signals it | the environment's `idle` and `wake` hooks | the ring: completions, timeouts, and a posted wake |\n| Descriptors | count as ready, as consoles and files do on Windows (ADR 0035): the task retries at once | none | completions |\n| Cost | one `#if` per platform service in one file | a second set of hooks for userland, and programs lose the hosted API | the ring's ABI is CosmoOS's, not libc's: raw syscalls, which L1 (OD-15) forbids until the CosmoOS owner rules |\n\n**A is proposed.** It gives CosmoOS programs the whole hosted API but sockets, through public libc only, and keeps\nthe executor's tested bookkeeping unchanged. C is the way to sockets later, once OD-15 allows the ring.\n\n## 3. Decision\n\n1. **`CEE_COSMOOS` has no reactor.** The executor's descriptor table, kqueue, epoll, io_uring and `poll()` paths are\n   not compiled there.\n2. **Waiting and waking.** With no task ready, the executor waits on its own `cosmo_cond_t` under its own\n   `cosmo_mutex_t`, timed to the earliest deadline (or untimed if there is none), until a wake: another thread, a\n   finished `Async.blocking` call, a channel, a `runWorkers` peer or an `AsyncSignal` raise, signals it after\n   setting a pending flag, so a wake that comes before the wait is not lost. This replaces the wake pipe.\n3. **Descriptors count as ready.** A task parked on a descriptor (console output, `AsyncConsole`) is readied by the\n   next wait, as Windows does for consoles and files; its operation then runs, and may block the worker for as long\n   as a console write or read takes. `ceesafe_async_poll` reports every descriptor ready in the directions asked,\n   and sleeps for the timeout when there is n",
    237 =>
        "one.\n4. **Channels** use `cosmo_mutex_t` and `cosmo_cond_t` where other hosts use pthreads, and spin with plain retries\n   where they call `sched_yield`.\n5. **Async sockets stay `ENOSYS`** (ADR text in `ceesafe_rt_io.c`; profiles.md §2.2).\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- Async programs build and run as CosmoOS user programs, and the c1 corpus run covers them, but for the three that use\n  async sockets.\n- A console read through `AsyncConsole` blocks its worker until a line comes, where a host with readiness would run\n  other tasks meanwhile. Programs that need both wait in `Async.blocking`.\n- Linux, macOS, Windows and freestanding builds are unchanged.\n\n## 5. Choices the owner reviewed (accepted as proposed)\n\n1. A rather than C: sockets wait for OD-15's ruling on raw syscalls (the ring has no libc wrapper).\n2. Descriptors count as ready, as on Windows, rather than being refused.\n3. A condition variable per executor, rather than a futex word (`cosmo_futex_*` are raw syscalls under OD-15).\n",
    238 =>
        "# ADR 0041 — C interop for porting a stateful subsystem: shared atomics, imported unions, variadic calls\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-01: every question of §5 as recommended (A; members restricted to\n  any-bit-pattern types; variadic calls unsafe, with a safe formatter and log line in the libraries; ceesafec 0.4.0).\n  Asked for by the owner (\"Start with the ADR\") after a survey of CosmoOS's network stack, which roadmap 2.0 SA6\n  converts to CeeSafe.\n- Scope: three gaps in C interoperability (type-system.md §28–§33) that stop CeeSafe code from taking over C state\n  which C code still shares: integers C reads and writes atomically, unions, and C's variadic functions.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nCosmoOS's network stack (`kernel-services/network/`, 18 files, about 11,900 lines of C besides its self-tests) is\nstateful code whose structs the rest of the kernel and its drivers share. The checksum, its pure first module, is\nported. A survey of the rest (2026-11-01, CosmoOS `ed33a047`) found what CeeSafe's interop cannot yet express\nsafely; everything else it uses is covered (C function pointers §29.5, imported and exported structs §31 and ADRs\n0029–0030, statics §39, the kernel library's locks and objects, and floors for C-only state).\n\n**Shared atomics.** About 290 operations: `__atomic_load_n` 121, `__atomic_store_n` 99, `__atomic_fetch_add` 48,\n`__atomic_compare_exchange_n` 11, `__atomic_fetch_sub` 5, `__atomic_exchange_n` 4, `__atomic_fetch_or` 2,\n`__atomic_fetch_and` 1, on plain `uint32_t`/`uint64_t` members (interface statistics, reference counts, flags, the\nTCP connection's state) that C code on other CPUs also reads and writes atomically. CeeSafe's atomics (§38.3, ADR\n0026) are its own types, `AtomicU32`/`AtomicU64`/`AtomicUSize`/`AtomicBool`; an imported field must be declared\nwith exactly its C type (§31.1), so a CeeSafe port could only reach such a member non-atomically, which races with C.\n`AtomicU64` lowers to a struct whose one member is a `uint64_t` at offset 0, with its size and alignment (checked by\n`_Static_assert` in every unit), so it is already laid out as the integer.\n\n**Unions.** `struct netaddr` (`kernel/net/inet.h`), the address every socket, protocol control block, firewall rule\nand NAT entry holds, keeps its IPv4 and IPv6 addresses in an anonymous union:\n`struct netaddr { uint16_t family; uint16_t port; union { uint32_t v4; struct in6_addr v6; }; }`. CeeSafe imports no\nunion. Declaring `v4` and `v6` as two fields would let safe code borrow both at once while they share storage.\n\n**Variadic calls.** 41 calls in 9 files: the kernel log (`klog`, through `kinfo`/`kwarn`/`kerror`), `ksnprintf`,\n`panic` and `kprintf`, all `__printf`-checked variadic functions. CeeSafe cannot declare or call a C variadic\nfunction, so ports so far moved each message into a small C floor (`wait_floor_fatal(const char *what)`), which adds\nC for every message, and SA6 would add dozens.\n\nNot in scope: `goto` (19 in `tcp.c`, 3 in `tap.c`) is rewritten as structured control flow; an anonymous struct\nmember (`netif.h`) is named on CosmoOS's side, as `struct mbuf_pkt` was; why clang vectorises the C checksum's pair\nloop with `ld2` on arm64 and not the C ceesafec emits for one is a separate investigation (the checksum was accepted\nat 1.06–1.09x on arm64).\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. CeeSafe expresses the C (proposed) | B. CosmoOS changes its C | C. Floors |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Atomics | an imported or exported field may have an atomic type whose C member is the integer | wrap each shared member in a CeeSafe-owned struct | a C accessor per member |\n| Unions | imported union members, one place for the borrow checker | split `netaddr`'s union into two fields (+4 bytes each, and every `memcmp`/hash over it changes) | — |\n| Variadics | declare and call variadic C functions (unsafe), and a safe, allocation-free formatter in the libraries on top | a non-variadic log entry, `klog_write(level, text, len)`, in CosmoOS | one C wrapper",
    239 =>
        " per message |\n| Cost | three interop features, specified and tested in CeeSafe | changes to shared kernel headers and C written for the port | C grows with every port: against the CeeSafe rule's point 1 |\n\n**A is proposed.** The C is the contract other code relies on; CeeSafe should be able to state it exactly, as §31\nalready does for structs. B changes shared kernel code for the convenience of a port, and C adds the C the roadmap\nexists to remove.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n### 3.1 Atomic fields shared with C\n\n1. In an `extern \"C\"` struct (§31.1), a field may be declared `AtomicU32`, `AtomicU64` or `AtomicUSize` when its C\n   member's type is `uint32_t`, `uint64_t` or `size_t`. (Implementation note: `AtomicBool` is excluded. It is laid\n   out as a `u32` and C's `bool` is one byte, so it cannot be a `bool` member; the survey found no shared `bool`.) The `_Generic` member check compares with\n   that integer type. The field is a place of the atomic type: safe code reaches it only through the type's methods,\n   which lower to `__atomic_*` on the member's address, the same operations C's code uses on it.\n2. In a `@repr(C)` struct that a C header shares (§28.3, ADR 0029's exports header), a field of an atomic type is\n   written as the integer type in the header, so C code goes on using `__atomic_*` on it. The atomic types are\n   specified as laid out exactly as their integer, which the existing layout assertions already check.\n3. The atomic types gain `fetchSub`, `fetchOr` and `fetchAnd` (with `…With(order)` forms, as `add` and `swap`\n   have), which CosmoOS's code uses; `fetchAdd`, `swap` and `compareExchange` exist.\n4. Soundness: CeeSafe accesses such a member only atomically. That C does too is the C side's obligation, as for any\n   state C shares (§31); a non-atomic C access would be a data race in C already.\n\n### 3.2 Unions in imported structs\n\n1. Inside an `extern \"C\"` struct, `union { a: T; b: U; }` declares the members of a C11 anonymous union member, reached\n   as `r.a` and `r.b` (C's `p->a`). `union name { … }` declares an imported union type, used as a struct type is\n   (by pointer, by reference, embedded as a field), whose members are reached the same way.\n2. The members share storage, so the borrow checker treats every member of one union as **one place**: borrowing or\n   assigning any member conflicts with every other member while the loan lives (B1–B2).\n3. A member's type must be one for which every bit pattern is a value: integers, raw pointers, `CChar`, arrays of\n   these, and imported types whose declared fields obey the same rule. `bool`, enums, references, slices, closures and\n   CeeSafe structs are refused with a new diagnostic, so reading one member after writing another reinterprets bytes,\n   as C11 allows, and can never produce an invalid value.\n4. Lowering: an anonymous member's member is `p->a`; a union type's is `u.a`. Each declared member gets the same\n   `_Generic` check as a struct field.\n\n### 3.3 Variadic C functions, and formatting without allocation\n\n1. `fn klog(level: u32, fmt: *CChar, ...);` in an `extern \"C\"` block declares a variadic C function. A call passes\n   its fixed parameters as usual and any number of further arguments of the types C's default argument promotions\n   leave unchanged: `i32`, `u32`, `i64`, `u64`, `usize`, `isize`, `f64`, raw pointers and `*CChar`. A narrower\n   integer is refused (with the fix: widen it with `as`), as is anything else.\n2. A call to a variadic function is **unsafe**: whether the arguments agree with a format string is not something\n   CeeSafe checks (C's `__printf` checks only a literal format, and the generated C passes a pointer).\n3. Safe formatting on top, with no allocation, for every profile: `lib/core` gains a writer over a caller's\n   `SliceMut<u8>` that appends text, decimal and hexadecimal integers and a byte, truncating as `snprintf` does and\n   keeping the result zero-terminated, and reports the length it wanted, as `snprintf`'s result d",
    240 =>
        "oes. Network code\n   formats addresses with it (`netaddr_str`, `procnet`).\n4. `lib/kernel` gains a log line built with that writer in a fixed buffer of `KLOG_LINE_MAX` (256) bytes and sent with\n   one call, `klog(level, \"%s\", line)`: the only variadic call, inside the library, with a fixed format, so a port's\n   messages are safe code. A `panic` built the same way ends in `panic(\"%s\", line)`.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- A port can take over a C struct whose members C also changes atomically, and `struct netaddr`, without a floor.\n  Every message a port logs is safe CeeSafe; the one variadic call is in `lib/kernel`.\n- Imported unions are restricted to members of any-bit-pattern types; a union holding a `bool` or a pointer to a\n  CeeSafe struct stays opaque or behind a floor.\n- New diagnostics: a union member of a type with invalid bit patterns, a variadic argument of a type C would promote\n  or cannot pass, and an atomic field whose C member is not the matching integer.\n- CosmoOS can use these after a ceesafec release that has them and a pin bump; SA6's ports wait for it.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner (answered 2026-11-01: each as recommended)\n\n1. A (CeeSafe expresses the C) rather than B (change CosmoOS's headers) or C (floors)? Recommended: A.\n2. Union members restricted to any-bit-pattern types (§3.2.3)? Recommended: yes; it is what makes reading a union safe.\n3. Variadic calls unsafe, with a safe formatter and log line in the libraries (§3.3)? Recommended: yes. The\n   alternative, a CosmoOS `klog_write(level, text, len)`, would need no language change but adds C to CosmoOS.\n4. The release: these as ceesafec 0.4.0 (main is 0.4.0-dev), with CosmoOS's pin moved in its own commit? Recommended:\n   yes.\n\n## 6. Implementation plan (once accepted)\n\nSpec first: §31.1 and §31.2 (atomic fields; union members and union types), §28 (variadic declarations and calls),\n§38.3 (the atomic types' layout and new operations), lib/core's and lib/kernel's API pages, the diagnostics table.\nThen, each with corpus tests (check-fail for every new diagnostic, run-pass and emit-c for the lowering, ffi for a C\nharness that shares an atomic field, a union and a variadic function with CeeSafe), under ASan, UBSan and TSan:\n\n1. Atomic fields in imported and exported structs; `fetchSub`, `fetchOr`, `fetchAnd`.\n2. Imported unions, the one-place borrow rule and the member-type restriction.\n3. Variadic declarations and calls.\n4. The formatter in `lib/core`, the log line and `panic` in `lib/kernel`, with a `--kernel cosmoos` test (K3) and a\n   CosmoOS self-test that logs from CeeSafe.\n\nThen the contract suite, a release, and CosmoOS's pin.\n\n## 7. Implementation notes (2026-11-01)\n\n- **Atomic fields.** `AtomicBool` is excluded (§3.1.1). In `ceesafe_exports.h` an `AtomicUSize` member is written\n  `uint64_t`, as a `usize` member of a `@repr(C)` struct is (§28.5); in an imported struct it is checked against\n  `size_t`.\n- **Unions.** `union` is a word only in an extern block, before a name or, inside an imported struct, before `{`;\n  elsewhere it stays an identifier. Every member of one union shares a union group in the type table, which the borrow\n  checker's overlap test compares (N0240–N0242). Member types: N0346. An empty union, or a union inside an anonymous\n  union: N0107.\n- **Variadic calls.** `...` is lexed as `..` and `.` with nothing between, so no token changes. A variadic function\n  has no wrapper: each call converts its arguments in place, so it has no function pointer (N0291) and cannot return\n  a `@repr` enum (N0291). A unit that calls one disables `-Wformat-security` and `-Wformat-nonliteral`: the format is a\n  pointer, and agreeing with it is the caller's `unsafe` obligation. Further arguments: N0347.\n",
    241 =>
        "# ADR 0042 — Statics that are C state: imported structs, ops tables and function addresses\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-01 (\"I accept ADR 0042 as proposed\"): every question of §5 as\n  recommended. Found porting CosmoOS's `loopback.c` (roadmap 2.0 SA6),\n  after ADR 0041.\n- Scope: static items (type-system.md §39) whose type is an imported C type, and the static initialisers they need:\n  a struct literal of an imported type, a C function's address, another static's address, a null C pointer.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nKernel C defines objects that C code then owns, registers and mutates: a driver's or protocol's **ops table**\n(`static const struct netif_ops lo_ops = { .transmit = lo_transmit, .release = netif_release_static };`) and the\n**object** that points to it (`static struct netif g_lo = { .name = \"lo\", .mtu = 65535, .flags = …, .ops = &lo_ops };`),\nwhich an init function finishes and registers (`netif_register(&g_lo)`). CosmoOS has 64 ops tables in 26 files\n(drivers, block, the network stack); every SA6 and SA9 port that defines a device or a protocol meets this.\n\nceesafec 0.4.1 refuses each part of it (checked 2026-11-01 on a reduced copy of `loopback.c`):\n\n| What C writes | CeeSafe today |\n|---|---|\n| a static of an imported struct, initialised by a literal | N0294: CeeSafe cannot make an imported type, only point to one (§31.2); only a C constant macro can initialise one (§39.4) |\n| that static at all | N0337: an imported struct is not `Share`, and a `static` must be (§39.2) |\n| `.release = netif_release_static`, an extern function's address | N0298 in a static initialiser, though §29.5 makes it a value of an `extern \"C\" fn` field elsewhere |\n| `.ops = &lo_ops`, another static's address | N0338: not a static initialiser (§39.4) |\n| `static lo_filter_fn g_filter;`, a null C function pointer set later | `0 as extern \"C\" fn(…)` is not a static initialiser |\n\nThe constant-macro route (§39.4) would move each initialiser into a C header macro, and the ops table with it: C that\nthe conversion exists to remove.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. Static initialisers for C objects (proposed) | B. C floors | C. Allocate at init |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| What changes | §39.4 accepts a literal of an imported struct, function and static addresses, and null; such a static is `static mut` | the object and its ops table stay in a `*_floor.c`, CeeSafe implements the functions | CosmoOS gains allocation APIs (`netif_alloc`), and ports allocate their object |\n| C left | none | a floor per device or protocol: 26 files' worth | none, but C APIs change for the ports' sake |\n| Cost | the checker and the static-initialiser lowering | grows with every port | changes shared kernel interfaces; static objects (loopback, boot devices) become heap objects |\n\n**A is proposed**, as for ADR 0041: CeeSafe should state the C exactly.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n1. **A `static mut` may have an imported C type** (§31), or an array of one. It is C state from the start: C code\n   reaches it through the pointers CeeSafe gives it (`netif_register(borrow mut LO as *mut netif)`), so CeeSafe\n   cannot assume it unchanged, and every use is unsafe, as §39.3 already makes every use of a `static mut`. A plain\n   `static` of an imported type stays N0337: CeeSafe would read it as immutable while C writes it.\n2. **A struct literal of an imported type is a static initialiser** in such a static, or in a field of one: it names\n   any of the declared fields, each with a static initialiser, and every C member it does not name is zero, as C's\n   designated initialisers leave it (C11 §6.7.9p21). It lowers to a C designated initialiser over the members' C names\n   (`@cname`). Outside a static initialiser it stays N0294: no value of an imported type exists at run time.\n3. **More static initialisers** (§39.4), where an `extern \"C\" fn` or raw pointer type is expected:\n   - the name of an `extern \"C\"`, `@export` or `@callback` function: its address (§29.5's values, now in i",
    242 =>
        "nitialisers\n     too);\n   - `borrow S as *T` / `borrow S as *mut T` and the same of a field or element of `S`, for a static `S`: its address,\n     an address constant (C11 §6.6p9); a `static mut`'s address is not an unsafe use, only what is done through it;\n   - `0 as extern \"C\" fn(…) -> R` and `0 as *T`: null.\n4. A `[u8; N]` field (a C `char` array, ADR 0034) takes a byte string literal's bytes in an initialiser, zero-padded\n   to `N` (`name: \"lo\"`), so names read as they do in C. A literal longer than `N` is N0338.\n5. **Lowering**: the static's C object is named by its C spelling's type and the static's own C name; ops tables are\n   not `const` in C (§39.5), which C accepts wherever `const struct …_ops *` is expected.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- Loopback becomes: `static mut LO_OPS: netif_ops = netif_ops { transmit: transmit, release: netif_release_static }`,\n  `static mut LO: netif = netif { name: \"lo\", mtu: 65535, flags: …, caps: …, ops: borrow LO_OPS as *netif_ops }`, the\n  transmit function as `@callback`, and `loopback_init` writing the addresses before `netif_register`.\n- Drivers (SA9) and the block layer (SA7) define their ops tables and static devices the same way, with no floor.\n- An ops table that C only reads is a `static mut` though it is never written: CeeSafe has no `const` C object in v1.\n  A later decision may add one; nothing here prevents it.\n- New diagnostics: none; N0294, N0337 and N0338 narrow.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. A (static initialisers for C objects) rather than B (floors) or C (allocation APIs)? Recommended: A.\n2. Imported-type statics only as `static mut` (every use unsafe), rather than a `Share` rule for C state? Recommended:\n   yes: C writes them, so CeeSafe's shared-reference reading of a `static` would be wrong.\n3. Byte string literals for `[u8; N]` name fields (§3.4)? Recommended: yes; otherwise `name: [108, 111, 0, …]`.\n4. Release as ceesafec 0.5.0, then CosmoOS's pin in its own commit, as for 0.4.1? Recommended: yes.\n\n## 6. Implementation plan (once accepted)\n\nSpec first (§39.3–§39.5, §31.2, §29.5), then the checker and the static-initialiser lowering, with corpus tests: an ffi\ntest where C calls through a CeeSafe ops table and registers a CeeSafe static object; check-fail for a plain `static`\nof an imported type, a literal outside a static, an oversized name; emit-c for the designated initialisers. Then the\ncontract suite, K3 against CosmoOS, a release, CosmoOS's pin, and the loopback port.\n\n## 7. Implementation notes (2026-11-01)\n\n- An imported type's literal is accepted while a static initialiser is checked, so a plain `static` of an imported\n  type gets N0337 for its type (with a hint to declare it `static mut`), not N0294 for its literal.\n- In an initialiser a static's name is its place, `*StaticAddress`: StaticFold accepts the place only under a borrow\n  cast to a raw pointer (`IsStaticAddress`), and reports its value as \"another static's value\" (N0338).\n- `emittedCallbacks` reads the statics' initialisers too, so a `@callback` named only in an ops table gets its C\n  function (`ceeW_…`) and its prototype before the statics. Every static is declared by a tentative definition first\n  when one initialiser holds an address, in one unit or `--split`'s `ceesafe_instances.c`.\n- A string for a `[u8; N]` member is checked against `N` when the literal is checked (N0338), and lowers to a C string\n  literal, which C zero-pads.\n",
    243 =>
        "# ADR 0043 — Exports that exist only in some builds, and C function pointers from addresses\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-02 (\"Yes, I accept your recommendations\"): every question of §5 as\n  recommended. Found porting CosmoOS's `netif.c` (roadmap 2.0 SA6),\n  after ADRs 0041 and 0042.\n- Scope: `@export` functions whose C symbol a build's configuration includes or leaves out; and the conversion of an\n  address back to a C function pointer, which the compiler already accepts and the specification does not state.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nKernel C compiles parts of a file only in some builds. `netif.c` has two kinds:\n\n- **Exported functions that exist in some builds only.** `netif_tx_probe_set` and `netif_tx_probe_read` are defined\n  `#if CONFIG_SELFTEST`; in other builds `netif.h` defines them as `static inline` stubs, so a definition of the same\n  name there would not compile. `netif_test_worker_hooks_set` is defined, and exported to modules, only\n  `#if CONFIG_DEBUG`; `netif.h` declares it only then.\n- **Code inside a function that exists in some builds only**: the self-test's timing of a transmit, the debug hooks'\n  calls. These need no feature: a C constant macro read in a static initialiser (`COSMO_CONFIG_DEBUG()`, as CosmoOS's\n  `mbuf.cee` has it since 2026-11-02) gives a `static` that the C compiler folds, so `if (SELFTEST) { … }` compiles away\n  where the C's `#if` did.\n\nCeeSafe has no conditional items: every function is compiled in every build, and an exported one always defines its C\nsymbol. So a port of `netif.c` cannot define `netif_tx_probe_set` in a release build, where `netif.h` already does.\n\nSeparately, the hooks (`g_rx_hook`, the test hooks) are C function pointers published with atomic stores. CeeSafe keeps\nthem in an `AtomicUSize` (`f as usize`, §29.5) and needs the address back as a function pointer. `ceesafec` accepts\n`n as extern \"C\" fn(…) -> R` for any `n: usize` and lowers it to a cast (checked 2026-11-02), as `n as *T` is for raw\npointers (§27.2); the specification states only `0 as extern \"C\" fn(…)`, the null pointer.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. `@cfg` on exports (proposed) | B. Conditional items in general | C. Floors |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| What | an `@export` function's C symbol is emitted inside `#if NAME`; the CeeSafe function exists in every build | any item may be conditional, as Rust's `cfg` | the configured exports stay C, calling CeeSafe |\n| Checking | every item is checked in every build | an item is checked only in builds that have it, or the checker evaluates conditions | as A |\n| Cost | the attribute and the lowering | the checker must know the build's configuration, or check every combination | C grows with each port that has a configured export |\n\n**A is proposed.** It is what C interop needs, the configured symbols, while CeeSafe code stays one program checked\nonce. Code inside functions already has the constant-macro route, which also checks both branches.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n1. **`@cfg(\"NAME\")`** on an `@export` function: its C definition (the exported function, its prototype, and its line\n   in `ceesafe_exports.h`) is emitted inside `#if NAME` … `#endif`, so the symbol exists in the builds where the C\n   macro `NAME` is non-zero, exactly where the C's `#if NAME` had it. `NAME` must be a valid C identifier (N0292). The\n   CeeSafe function itself is compiled and checked in every build, and CeeSafe code may call it in any build.\n2. `@cfg` anywhere else is N0290: a non-exported function, a static or a type has no C symbol to leave out.\n3. **The run-time switch** stays the C constant macro (§39.4): the guide and §39.4 say how, with\n   `static SELFTEST: bool = COSMO_CONFIG_SELFTEST();`, which the C compiler folds.\n4. **C function pointers from addresses**: `n as extern \"C\" fn(…) -> R` for `n: usize` is a C function pointer to\n   that address, as `n as *T` is a raw pointer (§27.2): making it is safe, calling through it is unsafe (§29.5), and\n   a wrong address is th",
    244 =>
        "e caller's `unsafe` obligation at the call. §29.5 states it.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- `netif.c` ports with no floor beyond its module exports (which stay C, `#if CONFIG_DEBUG` included).\n- A program's exports header differs between builds only by the `#if` lines, which C evaluates.\n- New diagnostics: none; N0290 and N0292 cover a misplaced `@cfg` and a bad name.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. A (`@cfg` on exports only) rather than B (conditional items in general) or C (floors)? Recommended: A.\n2. State the existing `n as extern \"C\" fn` conversion in the specification as it behaves? Recommended: yes.\n3. Release as ceesafec 0.6.0, then CosmoOS's pin in its own commit? Recommended: yes.\n\n## 6. Implementation plan (once accepted)\n\nSpec first (§28.1's attribute table, §28.4, §27.2 and §29.5), then the attribute's checking and the `#if` lowering of\nthe export and of its exports-header line, with corpus tests: an ffi test whose C harness is built twice, with the\nmacro set and unset; check-fail for `@cfg` elsewhere and a bad name. Then the contract suite, K3, a release, CosmoOS's\npin, and the netif port.\n\n## 7. Implementation notes (2026-11-02)\n\n- The HIR function carries the macro's name (`cfgName`); the backend wraps the export's prototype, its definition (in\n  one unit or `--split`'s instances), and its exports-header line in `#if NAME` / `#endif`. Nothing else changes: the\n  CeeSafe function, its checks and its callers are the same in every build.\n- `@cfg` on a struct or other item is N0290 through the attribute table, as any attribute in the wrong place; on a\n  function without `@export`, N0290 with a message saying so; a name that is not a C identifier, N0292.\n- `n as extern \"C\" fn(…)` needed no change: §29.5 now states what ceesafec already did.\n",
    245 =>
        "# ADR 0044 — Socket addresses, IPv6, socket options and network error kinds\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-02 (\"Yes, I accept the ADR each with your recommendation\"): every\n  question of §5 as recommended. Asked for by the owner for CeeSafe.Web, a web framework built on CeeSafe\n  (`~/dev/CeeSafe.Web/docs/ceesafe-upstream-work.md`, items U1 and U2). This is the first of that document's ADRs: this\n  one is one API surface (addresses, options and the errors they report); vectored sends and the operation record's\n  size (U3), `Async.timeout` (U4), DNS (U5) and library links in the manifest (U8) follow in their own ADRs.\n- Scope: `lib/std`'s async sockets, the built-in blocking `TcpListener` and `TcpStream` (type-system.md §26.5), the\n  built-in `IoError` (§26.2), the hosted runtime's socket calls on every reactor, and the interpreter's models of them.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nCeeSafe's sockets speak IPv4 only, with no options:\n\n- An address is dotted-quad text and a port; the runtime parses it into a `sockaddr_in` (`ceesafe_rt_io.c`,\n  `ceesafe_ipv4`; `ceesafe_rt_async.c`'s listen, connect and accept). AcceptEx's address buffer has room for IPv4.\n- The listen backlog is 16 (`ceesafe_rt_io.c`, both `listen` calls). A server under a burst of connections refuses\n  them while the C library's own limit is far larger.\n- `SO_REUSEADDR` (listeners) and `SO_NOSIGPIPE` (macOS) are set inside the runtime. Nothing else can be: no\n  `TCP_NODELAY`, keep-alive, buffer sizes, linger, `SO_REUSEPORT` or `IPV6_V6ONLY`.\n- Neither end's address can be read except the listener's port (`port()`), and only the write side can be shut down.\n\nErrors: `IoError` is a built-in enum of eleven variants (§26.2). A connection reset, an abort, a timeout or an\nunreachable host is `Other(errno)`, whose number differs between Linux, macOS and Windows (the runtime already\ntranslates Winsock's codes to errno values, `cee_wsa_errno`, but folds `WSAEHOSTUNREACH` into `ENETUNREACH`). A server\nmust tell a peer that reset the connection from a bug of its own, portably.\n\nA library cannot add any of this: the operations the async types are built from are the runtime's, and the async\nprimitives are library-only (§40.4, N0345). So it is CeeSafe's work, designed here.\n\nTwo facts constrain the design:\n\n- **`TcpListener`, `TcpStream` and `IoError` are compiler built-ins** (`src/check/io.cee`, `src/check/declare.cee`,\n  `src/check/structs.cee`), with interpreter models (`src/interpreter/io.cee`). The async types are lib/std.\n- **The bootstrap.** The tree is built by stage 0, ceesafec 0.3.2 (Decision 0009), whose `IoError` has eleven\n  variants. The compiler's own sources match on `IoError` (the interpreter's `errnoOf`). Both 0.3.2 and 0.6.0 accept a\n  wildcard arm after a match that is already exhaustive (checked 2026-11-02), so those matches gain a `_` arm first, and\n  the tree keeps building with stage 0 after the variants exist.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n**Where the address type lives.**\n\n| | A. lib/std enum (proposed) | B. Built-in enum | C. Text only |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| What | `SocketAddress` in `lib/std/net.cee`, used by the async types | a built-in like `IoError`, usable by the built-in blocking types too | every API keeps taking and returning text |\n| Cost | the library and the runtime's C record | the checker, both backends, the interpreter, for a type a library can define | parsing on every call; no typed peer address |\n\n**A is proposed.** The address is plain data, which is what lib/std is for (Decision 0008). The built-in blocking types\ntake IPv6 text where they take IPv4 text today and get the options, but not the typed addresses (§3.6, question 1).\n\n**Where the new error kinds go.** A new variant appended after `Other` keeps every existing tag value (the runtime's\n`CEE_IO_*` numbers, a C program reading an exported `IoError`); a variant inserted before `Other` reads better in the\ndeclaration but moves `Other`'s tag. **Appending is proposed**; the specification li",
    246 =>
        "sts them in a sensible order\nanyway.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n### 3.1 `SocketAddress` (lib/std)\n\n```\npublic enum SocketAddress {\n    V4([u8; 4], u16),            // address bytes in network order, port\n    V6([u8; 16], u16, u32),      // address bytes, port, scope id (0: none)\n}\n```\n\n- `SocketAddress.parse(text: StringView) -> SocketAddress?`: `a.b.c.d:port`, `[v6]:port`, `[v6%zone]:port`, where\n  `v6` is any RFC 4291 text form (`::`, embedded IPv4 `::ffff:1.2.3.4`) and `zone` a number or an interface name.\n  A name is resolved by the runtime (`if_nametoindex`; `ConvertInterfaceNameToLuidA` + `ConvertInterfaceLuidToIndex`\n  on Windows), the only part of parsing that is not pure. Null if the text is not an address.\n- `SocketAddress.parseIp(text, port) -> SocketAddress?`: an address without a port (`127.0.0.1`, `::1`, `fe80::1%2`).\n- `a.write(w: borrow mut TextWriter)` (lib/core's writer): `127.0.0.1:8080`, `[::1]:8080`, `[fe80::1%2]:8080`, IPv6\n  in RFC 5952's canonical form (lowercase, the longest run of zeros as `::`, embedded IPv4 for `::ffff:0:0/96`). The\n  scope is written as its number.\n- `a.port()`, `a.withPort(p)`, `a.isLoopback()`, `a.isUnspecified()`; `SocketAddress.v4Any(port)`,\n  `v6Any(port)`, `v4Loopback(port)`, `v6Loopback(port)`.\n\nIt crosses into the runtime as a C record, `struct ceesafe_socket_address { uint32_t family; uint16_t port; uint32_t\nscope; uint8_t bytes[16]; }` (family 4 or 6), which the runtime converts to and from `sockaddr_storage`. CeeSafe\ncode never sees `sockaddr`.\n\n### 3.2 Listeners\n\n```\npublic struct ListenOptions {\n    public backlog: u32;         // 0: the platform's maximum (SOMAXCONN)\n    public reuseAddress: bool;   // SO_REUSEADDR (default true, as today on POSIX)\n    public reusePort: bool;      // SO_REUSEPORT (default false)\n    public v6Only: bool;         // IPV6_V6ONLY for a V6 address (default false: dual stack)\n}\n```\n\n- `ListenOptions.defaults()`; `AsyncTcpListener.bindWith(address: SocketAddress, options: ListenOptions) ->\n  Result<AsyncTcpListener, IoError>`. `bind(address: StringView, port: u16)` stays, now taking IPv6 text too (`::`,\n  `::1`) and the defaults, so its backlog becomes SOMAXCONN.\n- `listener.localAddress() -> Result<SocketAddress, IoError>`; `port()` stays.\n- On Windows `reuseAddress` is ignored, as the runtime does today: Winsock's `SO_REUSEADDR` lets another socket\n  take over a bound port, which is not what the POSIX option means (a listener there may rebind past `TIME_WAIT`\n  without it). `reusePort` where the platform has no load-balancing `SO_REUSEPORT` (Windows) is refused with\n  `Unsupported` (§3.4), not silently ignored: a server that asks for it expects the kernel to spread connections.\n- `v6Only` is always set explicitly on a V6 socket, so a program behaves the same on Linux (default off) and Windows\n  (default on). With dual stack, an IPv4 peer's address is reported as `V4`, not as `::ffff:a.b.c.d` (question 2).\n\n### 3.3 Streams\n\n`AsyncTcpStream.connectTo(address: SocketAddress)`; `connect(address: StringView, port)` stays, taking IPv6 text too.\nEvery one of these is synchronous and returns `Result<…, IoError>`:\n\n| Method | Option |\n|---|---|\n| `setNoDelay(on: bool)` | `TCP_NODELAY` |\n| `setKeepAlive(idleSeconds: u32?)` | `SO_KEEPALIVE`, and with a value the idle time (`TCP_KEEPIDLE`; macOS `TCP_KEEPALIVE`; Windows `SIO_KEEPALIVE_VALS`); null turns it off |\n| `setSendBuffer(bytes: u32)`, `setRecvBuffer(bytes: u32)` | `SO_SNDBUF`, `SO_RCVBUF` (the kernel may round; Linux doubles) |\n| `setLinger(seconds: u32?)` | `SO_LINGER`; `0` makes the close send a reset; null restores the default |\n| `peerAddress()`, `localAddress()` | `getpeername`, `getsockname`, as `SocketAddress` |\n| `shutdownRead()` | `shutdown(SHUT_RD)`; `shutdownWrite()` stays |\n\n`AsyncSockets`, for code with its own wait loop, gains the same calls on\ndescriptors.\n\n### 3.4 `IoError` kinds\n\nAppended after `Other`, in this order: `ConnectionReset` (`ECONNRESET`, `WSAECONNRESET`), `Conne",
    247 =>
        "ctionAborted`\n(`ECONNABORTED`), `NotConnected` (`ENOTCONN`), `TimedOut` (`ETIMEDOUT`), `HostUnreachable` (`EHOSTUNREACH`; Winsock's\nno longer folded into `ENETUNREACH`), `NetworkUnreachable` (`ENETUNREACH`), `AddressNotAvailable` (`EADDRNOTAVAIL`),\nand one not in the request, **`Unsupported`** (`EOPNOTSUPP`, `ENOTSUP`, `ENOPROTOOPT`, `EAFNOSUPPORT`,\n`EPROTONOSUPPORT`): what a platform without an option or an address family reports, so §3.2's refusal and an IPv6\naddress on a host without IPv6 are portable (question 3).\n\nEach has a `message()`, the C library's wording (\"Connection reset by peer\", \"Operation timed out\" is macOS's and\n\"Connection timed out\" Linux's: the specification fixes one, glibc's). `CEE_IO_*` gains 11–18; `ceesafe_io_kind`,\nlib/std's `hostError`, and the interpreter's `errnoOf`/`ioKindOf` map them.\n\n`IoError.TimedOut` is not `AsyncError.TimedOut`: the first is the kernel's (a connect or keep-alive gave up), the\nsecond a deadline of the program's (U4).\n\n**Compatibility.** A `match` on `IoError` that names every variant and has no `_` arm stops compiling (N0256). The\ncorpus and the guide are updated; `src/` gains wildcard arms first (§1). CeeSafe is before 1.0 and has no written\nversioning rule; this ADR takes the usual one, that a change breaking source is a minor release: **0.7.0**, whose\nnotes say so (question 5).\n\n### 3.5 The runtime, on every reactor\n\n- `sockaddr_storage` everywhere; one conversion pair (`cee_address_to_storage`, `cee_address_from_storage`); CosmoOS's\n  own layout (`COSMO_AF_INET`, already 16 address bytes) keeps its branch.\n- Readiness reactors (epoll, kqueue, `poll()`, `WSAPoll`): the same non-blocking calls with the family's size.\n- IOCP: the AcceptEx buffer becomes `2 * (sizeof(struct sockaddr_in6) + 16)`, read with `GetAcceptExSockaddrs`;\n  ConnectEx binds the socket first to `INADDR_ANY` or `in6addr_any` by family.\n- io_uring: `IORING_OP_ACCEPT` keeps a null address; `peerAddress` is `getpeername` on demand.\n- Options are plain `setsockopt` calls on the descriptor (or `WSAIoctl` for Windows keep-alive), outside any operation\n  record, so `ceesafe_async_op`'s size is unchanged (the record's layout is decided once, in U3's ADR).\n- The blocking `ceesafe_tcp_*` calls share the conversion and the options.\n\n### 3.6 The blocking built-ins\n\n`TcpListener.bind` and `TcpStream.connect` accept IPv6 text and use SOMAXCONN; `TcpStream` gains `setNoDelay`,\n`setKeepAlive`, `setSendBuffer`, `setRecvBuffer`, `setLinger` and `shutdownRead`, built-in methods like its\nothers. The typed forms (`bindWith`, `connectTo`, `peerAddress`, `localAddress`) are the async types' only: a built-in\ncannot name a lib/std type, and making `SocketAddress` a built-in (option B) costs the checker, both backends and the\ninterpreter for what a library type does. A blocking program that needs them uses `Async.run` with the async types,\nwhich is what a server does anyway (question 1).\n\n### 3.7 The interpreter\n\nThe interpreter runs no C (ADR 0035 §3.6): each new runtime call has a model in `src/interpreter/io.cee` that performs\nit through the interpreter's own host (`AsyncSockets`, `TcpStream`), as the existing socket models do. Interface-name\nzones are resolved by the host's runtime call; the models are what the interpreter's `run --interp` row in the\ncontract suite exercises.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- CeeSafe.Web's transport (its M1) can listen on IPv6 and dual stack, tune its sockets and classify network errors\n  portably.\n- A program matching `IoError` exhaustively must add the new arms (0.7.0's notes).\n- New diagnostics: none. A bad option value (a buffer size above `i32`'s range) is `InvalidInput` at run time.\n- `ceesafe_async_op` is unchanged; U3 decides its layout.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. The blocking `TcpListener`/`TcpStream` get IPv6 text, SOMAXCONN and the setters, but not `SocketAddress`\n   (§3.6)? Recommended: yes. (The alternative, a built-in `SocketAddress`, is option B.)\n2. Dual-stack listeners by",
    248 =>
        " default (`v6Only: false`), and IPv4-mapped peers reported as `V4`? Recommended: yes.\n3. Add `Unsupported` to the seven requested kinds, and refuse `reusePort` on Windows with it? Recommended: yes.\n4. Append the new variants after `Other`, keeping every existing tag? Recommended: yes.\n5. Release this, with U3 and U4, as ceesafec **0.7.0** (the request said 0.6.0, which shipped on 2026-11-02 with ADR\n   0043)? Recommended: yes.\n\n## 6. Implementation plan (once accepted)\n\n1. Spec: §26.2 (the kinds and messages), §26.5 (the blocking types' additions), §40.4 (the async table and\n   `SocketAddress`), `c-backend.md` §6 (the runtime calls and the address record), the API pages.\n2. `src/`: wildcard arms on every `IoError` match first; then the variants (`declare.cee`, `structs.cee`), the\n   interpreter's mappings and models, and `TcpStream`'s new built-in methods (`check/io.cee`, both backends).\n3. Runtime: the conversions, `ceesafe_io_kind`, the options, IPv6 on each reactor; `tools/gen_runtime.py`.\n4. lib/std: `net.cee` (`SocketAddress`, `ListenOptions`), the async types' methods, `AsyncSockets`;\n   `tools/gen_library.py`.\n5. Tests, with expected values from oracles (Python's `ipaddress` for parsing and RFC 5952 text; a C harness reading\n   each option back with `getsockopt`):\n   - corpus run-pass: IPv6 loopback accept and connect; dual stack with an IPv4 peer reported as `V4`; peer address\n     equal to the connector's local address; every option round-trip; a backlog of 1000 simultaneous connects, all\n     accepted; a reset (`setLinger(0)` then drop) read as `ConnectionReset`, and written as `ConnectionReset` or\n     `BrokenPipe` as the platform reports (the specification says which);\n   - check-fail: an `IoError` match naming only the eleven old variants (N0256);\n   - every reactor: the contract suite's runs, and its \"reactor uring\" step with `CEESAFE_REACTOR_URING=1`; Windows\n     (IOCP and `WSAPoll`) on the Windows 11 ARM64 test host;\n   - ASan/UBSan, the allocation-failure sweep with `--check-leaks`, and `tools/cancel_sweep.py` over the new tests.\n6. The guide (`guide/async.md`, \"Waiting for the outside world\", and the sockets section), ROADMAP §10, a release.\n\n## 7. Implementation notes (2026-11-02)\n\n- **Errors (U2).** The eight kinds follow `Other` (`CEE_IO_*` 11–18). The interpreter's `errnoOf` cannot name them\n  while stage 0 (0.3.2) builds it, so its `_` arm maps a kind by its message, each kind's being fixed (§26.2).\n- **Constructors.** An enum has no functions of its own (type-system.md §15.1), so `SocketAddress`'s instance\n  methods come through the interface `SocketEndpoint`, and the constructors and parsers are `SocketAddresses`'\n  (`SocketAddresses.parse`, `parseIp`, `v4Any`, …) rather than `SocketAddress.parse` as §3.1 wrote them.\n- **Names.** `setRecvBuffer`, as requested (§3.3 had briefly proposed `setReceiveBuffer`); CeeSafe already says\n  `recv` for channels.\n- **Parsing.** IPv4 parts may not have leading zeros (`01.2.3.4` is refused), as Python's `ipaddress`, the tests'\n  oracle; the runtime's own text parser for `bind(text, port)` keeps accepting them, as before. A zone given by an\n  interface's name is resolved with `if_nametoindex` on POSIX; Windows resolves no names (numeric zones only), since\n  that would link iphlpapi into every program.\n- **The record.** On Windows the AcceptEx address buffer, now sized for IPv6, shares a union with the WSABUF and\n  flags of a read or send, so `ceesafe_async_op` stays 192 bytes; elsewhere the record had room.\n- **CosmoOS** has no `setsockopt` or `getpeername`: the options and `peerAddress` are `Unsupported` there.\n- **The bootstrap chain.** `f7a7d57` (runtime and lib/std) is built by 0.3.2; `93c433f` (the compiler's built-ins\n  and the interpreter's models of the async calls) by that; the next commit (the interpreter's models of the blocking\n  setters, which call the host's built-ins) by that. Recorded in `tools/bootstrap_release.sh` and\n  `bootstrap/refere",
    249 =>
        "nce.md`.\n- **The backlog test** connects 100 clients before any accept, not 1000: each connection is two descriptors, and\n  macOS's default limit is 256.\n",
    250 =>
        "# ADR 0045 — Vectored send, and the message operation record\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-02 (\"Yes, I accept each and every ADR's questions\"): every question of\n  §5 as recommended. Asked for by the owner for CeeSafe.Web\n  (`~/dev/CeeSafe.Web/docs/ceesafe-upstream-work.md`, item U3, and U6's question about the operation record's size).\n  The second of that document's ADRs, after ADR 0044. It decides the record that vectored sends and, later, datagram\n  messages (U6) use. The UDP API itself is a later ADR.\n- Scope: `lib/std`'s `AsyncTcpStream` and `AsyncSockets`, a new `lib/std` type for a list of byte parts, the hosted\n  runtime's operation records and steps on every reactor, and the interpreter's model.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nAn HTTP response is a head and a body in two buffers. Today `AsyncTcpStream.write` takes one slice, so a server\neither sends twice (two system calls, and a small first segment unless `TCP_NODELAY` is set) or copies both into one\nbuffer. The kernels have the answer in one call: `writev`/`sendmsg` (POSIX), `WSASend` with an array of `WSABUF`\n(Windows), `IORING_OP_SENDMSG` (io_uring).\n\nThree facts constrain the design:\n\n- **The operation record.** A socket operation is steps over a record in the awaiting task's frame (ADR 0035):\n  `ceesafe_async_op`, 24 words (`rt.h`), lib/std's `AsyncOp`. It never moves, so what the kernel uses outlives that\n  use. On a completion backend the kernel reads a vectored send's array of parts after the first step returns, so the\n  array must be in the record too, or somewhere else that never moves. U6's datagram messages need more: a\n  `msghdr`/`WSAMSG`, the peer's address and a control buffer for ancillary data (packet info, ECN, GSO).\n- **Every waiting operation has a record in its frame.** A server's idle keep-alive connection is a task parked in\n  `read`, so the record's size is paid once per open connection. CeeSafe.Web's worker frame is 920 bytes today, and\n  the record is 192 of them.\n- **CeeSafe has no slices of slices** (N0246: a slice is never an array or slice element, type-system.md §25.4).\n  `writeVectored(parts: Slice<Slice<u8>>)`, as the request wrote it, cannot be declared. A struct with region\n  parameters may hold slices in fields, and CeeSafe.Web's `IoParts<region r>` already does that with four named slots\n  (`packages/web_core/src/transport.cee`).\n\n## 2. Options\n\n**Where the parts live while a send is in flight.**\n\n| | A. A second, larger record (proposed) | B. One larger record | C. The caller's frame |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| What | `ceesafe_async_msg_op` (64 words) for vectored and datagram operations; `ceesafe_async_op` stays 24 words | `ceesafe_async_op` grows to hold the parts, the message header and the control buffer | the record points at an array in the calling task's frame |\n| Cost | two record types in the runtime; one shared header, so cancellation is unchanged | about 320 more bytes in every waiting read's frame, idle connections included | a pointer into another frame; correct only while that frame outlives the step, which ADR 0035 §3.3 proves only for the record itself |\n\n**A is proposed.** Only a task in a vectored or datagram operation pays for the larger record, and the larger record is\nstill in a frame that never moves. C would be correct with today's frames, but it adds a second thing that must\noutlive the kernel's use, which nothing checks.\n\n**How a list of parts is written.**\n\n| | D. A lib/std type with fixed slots (proposed) | E. Allow arrays of slices |\n|---|---|---|\n| What | `IoParts<region r>`: up to eight parts, built by value: `IoParts.of(head).then(body)` | lift N0246 for array elements, so `[Slice<r, u8>; N]` and a borrow of it as a list exist |\n| Cost | lib/std only; eight private slot fields | a language change: element loans in the borrow checker, the flows of §25.6, both backends, the interpreter |\n\n**D is proposed** for 0.7.0. It has the same shape as CeeSafe.Web's `IoParts`, so CeeSafe.Web maps onto it or ado",
    251 =>
        "pts\nit. E is a better language. It is not needed for this, and it is worth its own ADR if more code wants lists of\nborrowed slices.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n### 3.1 `IoParts<region r>` (lib/std, `net.cee`)\n\n```ceesafe\npublic struct IoParts<region r> {\n    // eight private Slice<r, u8> slots and a count\n    public fn of(part: Slice<r, u8>) -> IoParts<r>;\n    public fn then(borrow this, part: Slice<r, u8>) -> IoParts<r>?;   // null when it holds eight already\n    public fn count(borrow this) -> u32;\n    public fn part(borrow this, i: u32) -> Slice<r, u8>;              // empty past the last part\n    public fn totalLength(borrow this) -> usize;\n    public fn after(borrow this, n: usize) -> IoParts<r>;             // the bytes after the first n, as parts\n}\n```\n\n- A value, built by copying: a scoped type cannot be a `var` local (N0246), so `then` returns a new value instead of\n  appending.\n- Empty parts are allowed and cost nothing: they are skipped when the record is filled.\n- `after(n)` drops whole parts and cuts into the part where byte `n` falls. It is what a write loop needs after a\n  partial send. Past `totalLength()` it is empty.\n- The capacity is **eight**. It is at most `IOV_MAX`'s POSIX minimum (`_XOPEN_IOV_MAX`, 16) on every host, and an HTTP\n  response needs two (head, body) or three per chunk (size line, data, CRLF).\n\n### 3.2 The streams\n\n```ceesafe\n// AsyncTcpStream\npublic async fn writeVectored(borrow this, parts: borrow IoParts) -> Result<usize, AsyncError>;  // one step\npublic async fn writeAllVectored(borrow this, parts: borrow IoParts) -> Result<void, AsyncError>; // every byte\n// AsyncSockets (raw descriptors)\npublic async fn sendVectored(fd: i32, parts: borrow IoParts) -> Result<usize, AsyncError>;\n```\n\n- `writeVectored` waits until the socket takes at least one byte, then returns how many it took, which may stop in\n  the middle of any part. A partial count is normal, not an error. Empty `parts` (total 0) returns `Ok(0)` without a\n  system call.\n- `writeAllVectored` loops `writeVectored` over `parts.after(sent)` until every byte is sent, as `write` does for one\n  slice.\n- Errors and cancellation are `write`'s:\n  - `BrokenPipe` and `ConnectionReset` are `Io(…)`. No signal is raised: the runtime already sends with\n    `MSG_NOSIGNAL` and `SO_NOSIGPIPE`.\n  - A cancellation with a send in flight on a completion backend follows ADR 0035 §3.4. The task asks the kernel to\n    cancel and waits for the completion. A send the kernel completed anyway reports its count to `writeVectored`.\n    `writeAllVectored` returns `Err(Cancelled)` at its next step, as `write` does.\n- The blocking `TcpStream` gets no vectored write: it is a compiler built-in (type-system.md §26.5), and CeeSafe.Web\n  does not need one.\n\n### 3.3 The message record\n\n- **Size.** `rt.h` adds `typedef struct ceesafe_async_msg_op { uint64_t words[64]; } ceesafe_async_msg_op;`, with a\n  `_Static_assert` that each backend's record fits. lib/std adds `AsyncMsgOp` (`words: [u64; 64]`, plus `leaving`, as\n  `AsyncOp` has), with the same `check` and `result`.\n- **Layout.** The runtime's record starts with today's `cee_op`, so `ceesafe_async_op_cancel` and the completion paths\n  take either record. After that header comes the message area:\n  - the parts as the kernel reads them: `struct iovec[8]` (POSIX, io_uring) or `WSABUF[8]` (Windows);\n  - a `struct msghdr` (POSIX, io_uring) or `WSAMSG` (Windows);\n  - a peer address (`struct sockaddr_in6`, which also holds IPv4);\n  - a control buffer of 128 bytes for U6's ancillary data: packet info, traffic class and segment size each fit with\n    room to spare.\n\n  U3 uses the parts and, on io_uring, the `msghdr`. The rest is reserved for U6, so the UDP ADR adds operations and\n  leaves the layout alone.\n- **Filling it.** lib/std cannot build an array of C pointers. It hands the parts to the record one at a time, before\n  the first step: `ceesafe_async_msg_part(op, index, bytes, length)`. The runtime reads them only during th",
    252 =>
        "at call.\n  The step is `int64_t ceesafe_async_op_sendv(void *op, int32_t fd, uint32_t count)`, with the usual result: a count\n  ≥ 0, a negated errno, or `CEE_ASYNC_PENDING`.\n\n### 3.4 The runtime, on every reactor\n\n- **Readiness (epoll, kqueue, `WSAPoll`):**\n  - Linux: `sendmsg(fd, &msg, MSG_NOSIGNAL)`.\n  - macOS and the BSDs: `writev`. The socket already has `SO_NOSIGPIPE`.\n  - `WSAPoll`: a non-overlapped `WSASend` with the `WSABUF` array.\n  - On `EAGAIN` the step registers for writability, as `ceesafe_async_op_send` does.\n- **IOCP:** an overlapped `WSASend` with the record's `WSABUF` array and `OVERLAPPED`, completed like `send`.\n- **io_uring:** `IORING_OP_SENDMSG` with `MSG_NOSIGNAL` and the record's `msghdr`.\n- **CosmoOS userland** (`CEE_COSMOOS`): one `send` of the first non-empty part, a legal partial write. The runtime's\n  CosmoOS socket calls take one buffer today; a vectored call there can come later without an API change.\n- **More parts than the host's `IOV_MAX`** cannot happen: the record holds eight.\n\n### 3.5 The interpreter\n\nThe interpreter calls its host's lib/std (ADR 0044 §3.7), and stage 0 (0.3.2) has no vectored send. A vectored step\nin the interpreter therefore sends the first non-empty part with the host's `send` and returns its count. That is a\nlegal partial write, so programs see the same bytes in the same order, and the bootstrap needs no chain step.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- CeeSafe.Web sends a response's head and body in one system call, without a copy.\n- A task in a vectored send has a 512-byte record in its frame, instead of 192 bytes. Every other operation is\n  unchanged.\n- U6 (UDP) gets its record now; its ADR designs only the API.\n- New diagnostics: none.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. A second record, `ceesafe_async_msg_op` (64 words), for vectored and datagram operations, leaving the plain record\n   at 24 words (option A)? Recommended: yes.\n2. Write the list of parts as lib/std's `IoParts<region r>`, eight fixed slots built by value (option D), and leave\n   arrays of slices (option E) to a later language ADR? Recommended: yes.\n3. A capacity of eight parts? Recommended: yes. (Sixteen is the most every host allows in one call. Eight keeps\n   `IoParts` at 136 bytes.)\n4. No vectored write on the blocking built-in `TcpStream`? Recommended: yes, none.\n\n## 6. Implementation plan (once accepted)\n\n1. Spec: §40.4 (the stream table and `IoParts`), `c-backend.md` §6 (the message record and its calls), the API pages.\n2. Runtime:\n   - `rt.h`: `ceesafe_async_msg_op`, `ceesafe_async_msg_part` and `ceesafe_async_op_sendv`;\n   - `ceesafe_rt_async.c`: the record on each backend, its static asserts, the steps;\n   - then `tools/gen_runtime.py`.\n3. lib/std: `IoParts` (`net.cee`); `AsyncMsgOp`, `writeVectored`, `writeAllVectored` and `AsyncSockets.sendVectored`\n   (`async.cee`); then `tools/gen_library.py`.\n4. The interpreter's model of `ceesafe_async_msg_part` and `ceesafe_async_op_sendv` (§3.5).\n5. Tests. Expected bytes come from a Python oracle that generates the parts and the concatenation each side must see:\n   - corpus run-pass: parts of 0, 1 and more than the socket's send buffer; eight parts; `after` at every boundary;\n   - a slow reader (a small `SO_RCVBUF` and a reader that sleeps), forcing partial sends across part boundaries;\n   - a peer that resets mid-send gives `ConnectionReset` or `BrokenPipe`;\n   - cancellation in flight under ASan, where the frame is freed only after the completion; `tools/cancel_sweep.py`\n     over the new tests;\n   - every reactor: the contract suite, its \"reactor uring\" step, and Windows (IOCP and `WSAPoll`) on the Windows 11\n     ARM64 test host;\n   - the allocation-failure sweep with `--check-leaks`.\n6. The guide (`guide/async.md`, the sockets section), ROADMAP §10.\n\n## 7. Implementation notes (2026-11-02)\n\n- **Record sizes** (each checked by a `_Static_assert` in `ceesafe_rt_async.c`, and on every host the tests ran on):\n  - the header, then the count, the eight parts an",
    253 =>
        "d the message header;\n  - a 32-byte peer address;\n  - a control buffer of 128 bytes, or 96 on Windows, whose header is larger (an `OVERLAPPED` and three sockets). 96\n    bytes still holds Windows' packet info, ECN and segment-size messages.\n- **`IoParts`' receivers.** `then`, `part` and `after` take `this` by value. With `borrow this`, a result of type\n  `IoParts<r>` was taken to borrow the receiver's own local (N0244), since regions are erased (ADR 0006), so a helper\n  could not return a list it had built. `IoParts` holds only slices, so the copy is a few words.\n- **The interpreter.** It keeps each part's address and length in the record's words 8 to 23. A step sends at most\n  64 KiB of the first non-empty part, which is a legal partial send.\n- **Windows' interpreter errors.** The interpreter's errno mapping (ADR 0044) had Linux's and macOS's numbers only.\n  On Windows the runtime gives MSVC's `errno.h` values, read with `cl` on the test host: ECONNRESET 108, ENOTCONN 126,\n  ETIMEDOUT 138, EOPNOTSUPP 130, and the rest. So a reset read as `Other`, and both reset tests failed there in the\n  interpreter until the mapping gained a third column.\n- **T1, done with this ADR.** `tools/cancel_sweep.py` and `tools/fault_sweep.py` take `--package DIR`\n  (spec/testing.md §1).\n",
    254 =>
        "# ADR 0046 — `Async.timeout` and `Async.deadline`\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-02 (\"Yes, I accept each and every ADR's questions\"): every question of\n  §5 as recommended. Asked for by the owner for CeeSafe.Web\n  (`~/dev/CeeSafe.Web/docs/ceesafe-upstream-work.md`, item U4). It is the third of that document's ADRs, after ADR 0044\n  (U1, U2) and ADR 0045 (U3).\n- Scope: two new built-in `Async` operations (type-system.md §40.4). This covers the checker, the HIR and NIR forms, the\n  C backend and the interpreter, plus a lib/core join.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nA deadline on an async operation is written today as a race:\n\n```ceesafe\nlet r = await Async.race(\n    async () => await readRequest(mut stream),\n    async () => { await Timer.sleep(30000)?; return Err(WebError.TimedOut); },\n);\n```\n\nThat has three costs:\n\n- **It is verbose, and the result is a `Raced<T>`** to unwrap.\n- **The deadline child must return the body's error type.** So that type needs a `TimedOut` variant of its own, and\n  the child must turn `Timer.sleep`'s own `Cancelled` into it. When the body wins, the deadline child is cancelled and\n  its error is dropped. That is correct, but it is easy to get backwards.\n- **It cannot be wrapped in a function.** A helper `withDeadline(ms, body)` takes the body as a closure parameter. A\n  closure parameter cannot be awaited inside an `Async.race` child of an `@effectsOf` function (N0342 when awaited,\n  N0295 when called, §40.3).\n  - The other route, an `@effectsOf` *async* fn called with an async closure, is checked but miscompiles: it is bug\n    B1 in the request, a null function call. That bug is fixed separately, not by this ADR.\n\nSo a deadline must be one of `Async`'s built-in operations, as `race` and `shield` are.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. Built-in, lowered as a two-child join (proposed) | B. Built-in, a cancel deadline on one child | C. A library function |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| What | the body and a deadline child run as `race`'s two children; whichever returns first decides | the body runs as one child whose scope the executor cancels when a deadline passes | `@effectsOf(body) async fn timeout(…)` in lib/core |\n| Cost | a new HIR form and checker rule. The frames, cancellation, the C join, the interpreter and the cancel sweep are `race`'s | a new executor mechanism: a deadline that cancels a scope, on every executor (hosted, freestanding, interpreter) | cannot be written today (§1); needs B1 fixed and a language change for closure parameters in race children |\n\n**A is proposed.** It reuses `race`'s tested machinery: frames embedded in the caller's, so no allocation;\ncancel-and-await of the loser; the interpreter's join. The deadline child is a `Timer.sleep`, whose frame is small. B\nwould save that frame, but it adds a second way for a task to be cancelled, on every executor.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n### 3.1 The operations\n\n| Operation | Result | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Async.timeout(ms, timedOut, body)` | `Result<T, E>` | Runs `body`, an async closure of result `Result<T, E>`. If it has not returned `ms: u64` milliseconds after the call, it is cancelled and awaited, and the result is `Err(timedOut)` (`timedOut: E`) |\n| `Async.deadline(at, timedOut, body)` | `Result<T, E>` | The same, with an absolute deadline `at: u64` on `Timer.now()`'s clock. A deadline already past cancels the body at its first waiting operation |\n\nBoth are written after `await`, as every task-producing `Async` operation is. The body's type comes from an expected\n`Result<T, E>`, else from the body's expression, as for `shield`. `timedOut` is evaluated before the body starts. If\nit is not used, it is dropped when the operation returns.\n\n### 3.2 Who decides\n\nThe first of the two to return decides, as in `race`:\n\n- **The body returns first** with `Ok(v)` or `Err(e)`: that is the result. The deadline child is cancelled and\n  awaited.\n- **The deadline passes first:** the body is cancelled and awaited (§40.7: its next waiting operation return",
    255 =>
        "s\n  `Cancelled`, and an operation in the kernel is cancelled and waited for).\n  - If the body still returns `Ok(v)`, because it finished before it saw the cancellation, the result is `Ok(v)`. No\n    value is lost, as for `race`'s `other`.\n  - If it returns any `Err`, the result is `Err(timedOut)` and the body's error is dropped. After the deadline, the\n    body's error is almost always its view of the cancellation.\n- **The caller is cancelled from outside:** the body is cancelled with it (the flag is inherited, §40.7). The deadline\n  child's `Timer.sleep` returns `Err(Cancelled)`, and the deadline child never decides with an error. So the result is\n  the body's own, typically its `Cancelled`, never `timedOut`. A timeout reports only its own deadline.\n- **A tie:** if the body and the deadline finish at the same moment, whichever task runs first decides, as in `race`.\n  Natively that is timing. In the interpreter, the virtual clock and the run-order seed fix it, so a given seed always\n  gives the same result.\n- **`ms = 0`:** the body starts and runs until its first waiting operation, which returns `Cancelled`. A body that\n  never waits returns its value.\n\n### 3.3 Shields and nesting\n\n- A timeout inside `Async.shield` still cancels its own body when its deadline passes. The body is the timeout's\n  child task, with its own flag, as a `race` child inside a shield is. This is checked in the implementation, not\n  assumed.\n- Timeouts nest. An inner deadline later than the outer one never fires: the outer one cancels the inner body first,\n  and the inner timeout returns the body's error.\n\n### 3.4 Lowering\n\n- **Checker** (`src/check/async.cee`, beside `race` and `shield`): it checks the arguments and the body's result\n  type. It builds the deadline child as a closure over the computed deadline that awaits lib/core's `Timer.sleep`.\n  Its result is `Result<void, AsyncError>`, which is never the user's `E`.\n- **HIR:** a new form `AsyncTimeout(site, body, deadline child, timedOut, settle)`.\n- **NIR and the C backend:** lowered as `race`'s join: both frames are embedded in the caller's, and the first to\n  return cancels the other.\n- **Settle:** lib/core's `AsyncTimeoutJoin<T, E>.settle(first, body, deadline, timedOut)` gives §3.2's result.\n- **Interpreter:** the same join, with `isRace` and a timeout flag.\n\nThe compiler's own sources do not use the operations, so the bootstrap from 0.3.2 needs no chain step.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- CeeSafe.Web's connection deadlines (header read, idle keep-alive, body read) are one expression each, with\n  CeeSafe.Web's own error type and no `TimedOut` plumbing.\n- A generic `withDeadline` helper is still not writable as a function (§1). `Async.timeout` takes its place.\n- New diagnostics: none. A `timedOut` that is not `E` is the usual type mismatch, and a body that is not an async\n  closure is N0342, as for `race`.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. A built-in operation lowered through `race`'s two-child join (option A), rather than a new executor deadline\n   (option B)? Recommended: yes.\n2. Add `Async.deadline(at, timedOut, body)` beside `Async.timeout`? Recommended: yes. It is the same lowering with a\n   different first argument.\n3. After the deadline, keep a body's late `Ok`, and turn any `Err` into `timedOut` (§3.2)? Recommended: yes.\n4. A cancellation from outside gives the body's error, never `timedOut`? Recommended: yes.\n5. Ties decided by run order, as in `race`, rather than a \"body wins\" rule? Recommended: yes. Such a rule would need\n   the executor to compare the two tasks' deadlines, which no other operation does.\n\n## 6. Implementation plan (once accepted)\n\n1. Spec: §40.4 (the table), §40.7 (cancellation: the timeout's own deadline), `intermediate-representation.md` and\n   `c-backend.md` (the join), the API page.\n2. `src/`:\n   - checker: argument types and inference, the deadline child;\n   - HIR: `AsyncTimeout` and its printer form;\n   - NIR: the join;\n   - the C backend: `c_async.cee`'s join;",
    256 =>
        "\n   - the interpreter's join;\n   - `effect_check` (the operation has `suspend`, as `race` has).\n3. lib/core: `AsyncTimeoutJoin<T, E>`; then `tools/gen_library.py`.\n4. Tests:\n   - corpus run-pass, each in the interpreter (virtual clock) and natively. The timeout fires during a socket read, a\n     sleep, a channel receive and a nested scope. A body that finishes first. A late `Ok` that is kept. An outer\n     cancellation that is not reported as `timedOut`. A timeout inside a shield. Nested timeouts. `ms = 0`.\n     `Async.deadline` with a past deadline;\n   - check-fail: a `timedOut` of the wrong type; a body that is not an async closure;\n   - `.hir` snapshot of the new form;\n   - `tools/cancel_sweep.py` over the new tests, under ASan with `--check-leaks`. The contract suite on macOS, on\n     Debian (with \"reactor uring\") and on Windows.\n5. The guide (`guide/async.md`, cancellation and deadlines), ROADMAP §10.\n\n## 7. Implementation notes (2026-11-02)\n\n- **The join.** The HIR form is `AsyncTimeout(site, body, until, at, timedOut, settle)`. It lowers to the join `race`\n  uses, with kind 2: `Builtin(AsyncJoin, [site, 2, body, its closure, until, at])`. Two things about a join changed,\n  each for every kind:\n  - a child that is a function rather than a closure, with one parameter, is given the operand as its argument (in\n    C, to its initialiser; in the interpreter, as its first value);\n  - the second result has its own child's type.\n- **Library walk.** The C backend's walk of the library functions a program uses now follows a join's children.\n  `AsyncTimeoutJoin<T, E>.until` is reached only that way.\n- **Shields.** A timeout inside a shield cancels its body: a join's children are tasks of their own, with no shield\n  (corpus `async-timeout`, case i).\n- **Bug B3, fixed here.** `async-timeout`'s socket case found it again: a closure that slices a captured array as\n  `mut a[..]` captured it shared, so its environment held a `const` pointer, which `-Werror` rejects. `synthSlice`\n  now notes the mutable use, as `borrow mut` does (corpus `async-race-mut-capture`, the CeeSafe.Web probe).\n- **Bug B6, fixed later (2026-11-02).** `settle` bound the body's `Ok` value, which is invalid when `T` is `void`. The\n  compiler instantiates `AsyncTimeoutJoin<void, E>` for a body of type `Result<void, E>`, and the instance's error was\n  reported in its library file, whose source the diagnostic printers did not have: `check` panicked. `settle` now\n  binds no payload. Every printer shows a diagnostic whose file it lacks against a blank `<library>` source, rather\n  than crash. Test: run-pass/async-timeout-void.\n",
    257 =>
        "# ADR 0047 — System libraries in the manifest\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-02 (\"Yes, I accept both ADRs with your recommendations\"): every\n  question of §5 as recommended. Asked for by the owner for CeeSafe.Web\n  (`~/dev/CeeSafe.Web/docs/ceesafe-upstream-work.md`, item U8): CeeSafe.Web links the system's OpenSSL for TLS (its\n  M4).\n- Scope: `ceesafe.toml` (`src/packages/manifest.cee`, tooling.md §4.1), the build's compile and link commands\n  (`src/host/build.cee`, `src/host/toolchain.cee`, `src/host/compilers.cee`), `--emit c` and `--emit obj`.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nA package can list C files (`c = [\"src/shim.c\"]`), and every package's are compiled into the program. But nothing in a\nmanifest can name a library to link, or where its headers and archives are. Only `--cflag` on the command line can,\nand a dependency cannot pass that on to the programs that use it. A package that wraps OpenSSL therefore cannot be used\nby a plain `ceesafec build`.\n\nThree facts constrain the design:\n\n- **Nothing runs at build time** (ADR 0013): no pkg-config and no build scripts. Whatever the manifest names is\n  literal.\n- **One compile command.** The build compiles every C file, generated and listed, in one invocation of the C compiler\n  with one set of flags (`compileExecutable`); `--emit obj` compiles each file with `-c`, then joins them with `-r`.\n- **The package hash already covers the manifest's text** (`packageHash`, tooling.md §4.3), so any field added there\n  changes the hash with it.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n**Where a package's include directories apply.**\n\n| | A. To its own C files (proposed) | B. To every C file of the build |\n|---|---|---|\n| What | a package with `include-dirs` has its C files compiled apart (`-c`, with its directories), and the objects go into the build's command | the directories are added to the one command |\n| Cost | one more compiler run per such package | a header in one package's directory can shadow another package's, or the runtime's, of the same name |\n\n**A is proposed**: it is what the request asks for, and it keeps one package's headers from changing another's C. A\npackage without `include-dirs` is compiled as today.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n### 3.1 The fields\n\n```toml\n[package]\nlinks = [\"ssl\", \"crypto\"]                 # libraries to link: -lssl -lcrypto; ssl.lib crypto.lib with cl\nlib-dirs = [\"/opt/homebrew/opt/openssl@3/lib\"]\ninclude-dirs = [\"$OPENSSL_DIR/include\"]   # for this package's own C files\n\n[target.windows]                          # also [target.macos] and [target.linux]\nlinks = [\"libssl\", \"libcrypto\"]\nlib-dirs = [\"C:/vcpkg/installed/x64-windows/lib\"]\ninclude-dirs = [\"C:/vcpkg/installed/x64-windows/include\"]\n```\n\n- **Values.**\n  - Each field is an array of strings.\n  - A name in `links` is letters, digits, `_`, `.`, `+` and `-`, and does not start with `-`. So no entry can become a\n    compiler flag.\n  - A directory is absolute, or relative to the package's directory. It does not start with `-`.\n- **Targets.** A `[target.OS]` table's fields are added after `[package]`'s when the build is for that system (`linux`,\n  `macos`, `windows`; the host's, as for every hosted build). Other names are rejected.\n- **Environment variables.** A directory may start with `$NAME` or `${NAME}`, which is replaced by that variable's value\n  when ceesafec reads the manifest. An unset variable is an error naming it (N0321). This reads the environment and\n  runs nothing (ADR 0013). The hash is of the manifest's text, so the variable's value is not part of it.\n- **Errors.** A bad value, an unknown field in a target table, or an unknown target is N0321, as for every manifest\n  field.\n\n### 3.2 The link line\n\n- `links` and `lib-dirs` from every package in the build reach the executable's link, whatever its kind: a library's\n  are its users'.\n- **Order.** Packages are taken dependents before their dependencies, which is what static archives need. In a\n  package, `[package]`'s entries come before its target's.\n- **D",
    258 =>
        "uplicates.** A library named twice keeps its last place, so it follows everything that uses it. A directory named\n  twice keeps its first.\n- **Flags.**\n  - GCC and clang: `-L dir` for each directory, `-l name` for each library, after the C files.\n  - MSVC (`cl`): `/I dir` for headers; after `/link`, `/LIBPATH:dir` and `name.lib`.\n  - clang on Windows: GCC's flags, which its driver maps to `name.lib`.\n\n### 3.3 Include directories\n\nA package with `include-dirs` has its own C files compiled apart:\n- with `-c` (or `cl /c`), the build's flags and its directories (`-I`, `/I`), into the build's work directory;\n- the objects then go into the build's command in place of those C files.\n\nThe generated C, and other packages' C, never see those directories.\n\n### 3.4 Building by hand\n\n- `--emit c` writes the link line next to the C: `ceesafe_link.txt` in the output directory. It holds one argument per\n  line, in the C compiler's spelling (GCC/clang's unless `--cc` names `cl`), and it includes the include directories\n  each package's C files need.\n- `--emit obj` writes the same as `<output>.link.txt` beside the object. An object's libraries are linked by whoever\n  links the object.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- CeeSafe.Web's TLS package names OpenSSL once, and every program that depends on it links it.\n- **A dependency can now add libraries and library directories to your link.** That trusts it as far as its C files\n  already are: a dependency's C runs in your program anyway.\n- New diagnostics: none. Bad fields are N0321.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. The fields `links`, `lib-dirs` and `include-dirs`, in `[package]` and in `[target.linux|macos|windows]` tables added\n   after it? Recommended: yes.\n2. A package's `include-dirs` apply only to its own C files, compiled apart (option A)? Recommended: yes.\n3. Directories may start with `$NAME` or `${NAME}`, read from the environment, with an unset variable an error?\n   Recommended: yes.\n4. Link order dependents first, a repeated library keeping its last place? Recommended: yes.\n5. `--emit c` writes `ceesafe_link.txt`, and `--emit obj` writes `<output>.link.txt`? Recommended: yes.\n\n## 6. Implementation plan (once accepted)\n\n1. Spec: tooling.md §4.1 (the fields), §4.3 (the hash already covers them), §6 and §8 (the link line, the files for\n   building by hand).\n2. `src/packages/manifest.cee`: the fields and target tables, their checks, the variables.\n3. `src/main.cee` and `src/host/`:\n   - the workspace's link list in dependency order;\n   - the separate compile of packages with `include-dirs`;\n   - the flags for GCC/clang and for `cl`;\n   - the link files.\n4. Tests:\n   - a package test that links a small C static library, built by the test, from `lib-dirs`, with its header from\n     `include-dirs`;\n   - the same through a two-level dependency, where the library's user is the dependency's dependency;\n   - an include directory whose header shadows a name the root package also uses, so it must stay its package's;\n   - N0321 for each bad value, an unknown target, and an unset variable;\n   - the lock: the package hash changes when `links` changes;\n   - `--emit c`'s link file;\n   - Windows with `cl` and with clang, on the Windows 11 ARM64 test host.\n5. The guide (`guide/packages.md`, C libraries), ROADMAP §10.\n\n## 7. Implementation notes (2026-11-02)\n\n- **Link files.** They are written only when the manifests name a library, a library directory or an include\n  directory, so existing `--emit c` outputs do not change.\n- **Separate C files.** A package with `include-dirs` has its C files compiled from where they are, not copied into\n  the build's directory as other C files are. So a `#include \"local.h\"` beside the file also works.\n- **The hash.** A path dependency has no hash (tooling.md §4.3). `tools/package_tests.py` checks the hash through a git\n  dependency instead: its locked hash changes with `links`.\n- **Windows file names.** `addCFile` took a C file's name after the last `/` only, so a Windows pat",
    259 =>
        "h (`C:\\…\\x.c`)\n  became its whole name, and writing it into the build directory failed (\"cannot write the C: Invalid argument\"; the\n  ROADMAP's open Windows item). It now also stops at `\\`.\n",
    260 =>
        "# ADR 0048 — DNS resolution for tasks\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-02 (\"Yes, I accept both ADRs with your recommendations\"): every\n  question of §5 as recommended. Asked for by the owner for CeeSafe.Web\n  (`~/dev/CeeSafe.Web/docs/ceesafe-upstream-work.md`, item U5, phase A). CeeSafe.Web's HTTP client (its M5) connects to\n  host names. Phase B, a resolver over UDP, is CeeSafe.Web's own work later, on U6.\n- Scope: lib/std (`AsyncDns`), the hosted runtime (a resolver on worker threads), the interpreter's model.\n- Amended by ADR 0060 (2026-11-03): \"no such name\" and \"no address\" are `IoError.HostNotFound`, not `NotFound`, and\n  the interpreter's model answers it for names under `.invalid`.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n`AsyncTcpStream.connect` takes an address, not a name. A task that has a name has nowhere to turn it into addresses.\n\nThe system's resolver is `getaddrinfo`, which blocks and cannot be cancelled. `Async.blocking` runs blocking work on a\nworker thread, but a cancelled task still waits for its worker (§40.4). A lookup that hangs for 30 seconds would hold\na cancelled request, and a deadline (`Async.timeout`) could not cut it short. A lookup needs its own path: the task can\nleave, and the worker finishes alone.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n**How errors are reported.**\n\n| | A. `IoError` kinds that exist (proposed) | B. A new `DnsError` |\n|---|---|---|\n| What | `AsyncError.Io(NotFound)` for an unknown name, `TimedOut` for a temporary failure, and so on | `Result<…, DnsError>`, with its own variants |\n| Cost | the resolver's exact code is lost beyond those kinds | a second error type beside `AsyncError`: every caller converts, since `?` does not |\n\n**A is proposed.** A caller wants to know \"no such name\" and \"try again\", and both are there. `IoError` gains nothing,\nso no `match` breaks.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n### 3.1 The operation (lib/std, `net.cee`)\n\n```ceesafe\npublic struct AsyncDns {\n    public async fn resolve(host: StringView, port: u16) -> Result<Vector<SocketAddress>, AsyncError>;\n}\n```\n\n- **Result.** The addresses of both families, each with `port`, in the resolver's order, duplicates removed. The\n  system's settings (`/etc/hosts`, `gai.conf`, Windows' prefix policy) decide that order. It is never empty: no\n  addresses is `NotFound`.\n- **Literals.** A host that is an IPv4 or IPv6 literal (`SocketAddresses.parseIp`, zones included) is returned at once\n  as its one address, with no thread.\n- **Calls.**\n  - POSIX: `getaddrinfo(host, NULL, {AF_UNSPEC, SOCK_STREAM, flags 0})`. `AI_ADDRCONFIG` is off, so `localhost`\n    resolves on a host without IPv6.\n  - Windows: `GetAddrInfoW`, the name converted from UTF-8 to UTF-16.\n  - A name is passed as it is given: an internationalised name must already be in its ASCII form (punycode).\n- **Errors** (`AsyncError.Io(kind)`):\n\n  | Resolver result | Kind |\n  |---|---|\n  | `EAI_NONAME`, `EAI_NODATA` (`WSAHOST_NOT_FOUND`, `WSANO_DATA`), or no addresses | `NotFound` |\n  | `EAI_AGAIN` (`WSATRY_AGAIN`) | `TimedOut` (temporary: try again) |\n  | `EAI_MEMORY` | `OutOfMemory` |\n  | `EAI_FAMILY`, `EAI_SERVICE`, `EAI_BADFLAGS`; a name that is empty, has a NUL, or is longer than 253 bytes | `InvalidInput` |\n  | `EAI_SYSTEM` | the kind of its `errno` |\n  | anything else (`EAI_FAIL`, `WSANO_RECOVERY`) | `Other(EIO)` |\n\n- **Profiles.** CosmoOS userland has no resolver yet: its lookups of a name return `Unsupported` (literals still work).\n  The operation is hosted only, like the sockets.\n\n### 3.2 Workers and cancellation\n\n- **The pool.** Lookups run on at most **4** resolver threads per process, started when first needed and kept until it\n  exits. Further lookups wait in first-come order. A flood of lookups costs a queue entry each, not a thread each.\n- **Waiting.** The task parks until its lookup is done; the worker wakes it through its executor, as `Async.blocking`'s\n  worker does.\n- **Cancellation.**\n  - A cancelled task returns `Err(Cancelled)` at once, whether its lookup is queued (taken out) ",
    261 =>
        "or running (abandoned).\n  - The lookup's record is allocated by the runtime, not in the task's frame. An abandoned one is freed by its worker\n    when `getaddrinfo` returns, so nothing refers to the frame after the task leaves.\n  - So `Async.timeout(2000, …, async () => await AsyncDns.resolve(…))` returns after 2 seconds, whatever the resolver\n    does.\n- **Allocation.**\n  - If the record cannot be allocated: `OutOfMemory`.\n  - If no thread can start: the lookup runs on the calling thread, as `Async.blocking` does when it cannot start one.\n    The result is the same, but it is not cancellable then.\n\n### 3.3 The runtime (rt.h)\n\n```c\nvoid *ceesafe_dns_start(const uint8_t *host, uint64_t length, uint16_t port); /* NULL: out of memory */\nint32_t ceesafe_dns_poll(void *lookup);   /* 0 not done (parks), 1 done, else a negated errno (its kind) */\nuint32_t ceesafe_dns_count(void *lookup);\nvoid ceesafe_dns_address(void *lookup, uint32_t index, uint8_t *bytes, uint32_t *family, uint32_t *scope);\nvoid ceesafe_dns_end(void *lookup);       /* done: frees it; still queued: unqueued and freed; running: abandoned */\n```\n\nlib/std reads the addresses into a `Vector<SocketAddress>`, then ends the lookup, in a `drop` too, so every path frees it\nonce.\n\n### 3.4 The interpreter\n\nThe interpreter runs on stage 0's library (0.3.2), which has no resolver. Its model therefore resolves:\n- literals, as natively;\n- `localhost`, as `127.0.0.1` then `::1`.\n\nAny other name is `Err(Io(Unsupported))`. A test can rely only on literals and `localhost`, and gets the same answer\neverywhere: the interpreter's `localhost` order is fixed, so tests should not depend on it natively.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- CeeSafe.Web's client resolves, connects to the addresses in order, and can bound the whole lookup with a deadline.\n- A cancelled lookup's thread stays busy until the resolver returns. With 4 threads, at most 4 hung lookups can delay\n  the queue. That is the price of `getaddrinfo`, until phase B.\n- New diagnostics: none.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. Report errors as existing `IoError` kinds through `AsyncError.Io` (option A), not a new `DnsError`? Recommended: yes.\n2. At most 4 resolver threads per process, queueing beyond? Recommended: yes.\n3. A cancelled lookup returns at once and is abandoned to its worker, which frees it? Recommended: yes.\n4. The interpreter resolves literals and `localhost` only, other names `Unsupported`? Recommended: yes.\n5. Release U8 (ADR 0047) and U5 together as **0.8.0**? Recommended: yes. They add to the manifest and the library, so\n   a minor version, not a patch.\n\n## 6. Implementation plan (once accepted)\n\n1. Spec: §40.4 (`AsyncDns`), c-backend.md §6 (the resolver), the API page.\n2. Runtime: the lookup record, the pool and its queue, POSIX and Windows calls, the error mapping; then\n   `tools/gen_runtime.py`.\n3. lib/std: `AsyncDns.resolve`, a record type with a `drop`; then `tools/gen_library.py`.\n4. The interpreter's model (§3.4).\n5. Tests:\n   - corpus run-pass, interpreter and native:\n     - literals, IPv4 and IPv6 with a zone, each its one address;\n     - `localhost`, compared as a set: it contains `127.0.0.1` or `::1`;\n     - an invalid name (`NotFound`; the reserved `.invalid` top-level domain, RFC 6761);\n     - an empty or over-long name (`InvalidInput`);\n   - native only:\n     - a lookup cancelled while running, under a resolver made slow by a test hook (`CEESAFE_DNS_DELAY_MS`); ASan shows\n       the abandoned record freed with `live=0`;\n     - 100 lookups at once, which never use more than 4 threads;\n   - `tools/cancel_sweep.py` over the new tests; every reactor; Windows on the Windows 11 ARM64 test host.\n6. The guide (`guide/async.md`, sockets), ROADMAP §10, the 0.8.0 release.\n\n## 7. Implementation notes (2026-11-02)\n\n- **Threads.** A resolver thread ends when the queue is empty, rather than staying until the process exits: the next\n  lookup starts one again. Threads, records and the lock are the platform's (detached t",
    262 =>
        "hreads, `SRWLOCK` or a static\n  `pthread_mutex_t`, `ceesafe_platform_alloc`). So a lookup a cancelled task left behind is not the program's memory:\n  `CEESAFE_ALLOC_REPORT` shows `live=0`, and the process does not wait for the resolver to exit.\n- **Where it is.** The resolver is in `ceesafe_rt_async.c`. A first cut put it in `ceesafe_rt_io.c`, which every\n  hosted program links, calling into the async runtime, which only async programs link. On Windows a program without\n  async then failed to link (the package tests found it).\n- **Waking.** A task waits as a channel's waiter does: cancellable, woken from another thread through its executor.\n  The waiter is registered and unregistered under the lock, so no thread wakes a task after it has ended its lookup.\n- **Tests.**\n  - The corpus case (run-pass/async-dns) runs in the interpreter and natively with one expected output. An unknown\n    name is `NotFound` natively and `Unsupported` in the interpreter; both print \"not resolved\".\n  - The timed and sanitizer checks are `tools/dns_check.py` (in the contract suite). The thread bound is checked by\n    time: eight lookups with a 300 ms resolver take two rounds.\n",
    263 =>
        "# ADR 0049 — `ceesafec test`: declared tests, a generated harness, a runner\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-02 (\"Yes, I accept ADR 0049 with your recommendations\"): every\n  question of §6 as recommended. Asked for by the owner, from \"CeeSafe Testing Framework — Research, Architecture,\n  and Roadmap\" (2026-11-02), whose direction this follows: tests discovered by the compiler, a generated harness, a\n  small assertion library, a runner. CeeSafe.Web's upstream document lists `ceesafec test` as its optional item T2.\n- Scope: the core only. A test attribute, the `test` command and its runner, `lib/std`'s checks, per-test leak checks\n  and sweeps. Parameterised cases, fixtures, property tests, JUnit output, benchmarks and a freestanding runner are a\n  later ADR (§4).\n\n## 1. Context\n\nCeeSafe has no test command (`guide/ceesafec.md`). Its own tests are the corpus, whose files carry their expectations\n(`// expect-stdout:`, `// error[N0100]`, snapshots), and Python tools around it. A package author has neither:\nCeeSafe.Web wrote its own runner (`tools/run_tests.py`), one program per test directory, comparing printed output.\n\nWhat CeeSafe already has, and a test command should use rather than rebuild:\n\n- **Runtime hooks, per run:**\n  - `CEESAFE_ALLOC_REPORT` counts live allocations at exit;\n  - `CEESAFE_FAIL_ALLOC_AT=k` fails the k-th allocation;\n  - `CEESAFE_CANCEL_AT=k` cancels the root task at the k-th suspension.\n\n  `tools/fault_sweep.py` and `tools/cancel_sweep.py` drive them, on packages too (T1).\n- **The interpreter:** one thread, a virtual clock, a run order fixed by a seed (type-system.md §40.8). An async test\n  in it is deterministic, and a panic there is an interpreter stop, not the end of the process.\n- **Annotations** (`@effects`, `@export`, `@cfg`, …) and an attribute checker that rejects unknown ones (N0290).\n\nThree facts about the language decide the shape:\n\n- **A panic ends the process natively**: a failed `!`, an index out of bounds, a `Process.exit`. A native runner cannot\n  continue in the same process after one, so each test runs in a process of its own.\n- **There is no `==` on a type parameter, and no equality interface in lib/core** (generic impls are future work,\n  U10). A generic `equal<T>` cannot be written yet, so the checks are typed.\n- **`Async.run` is sync only.** An async test is run by the harness, not by the test.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n**How a check fails.**\n\n| | A. Checks return `Result` (proposed) | B. Checks panic |\n|---|---|---|\n| What | `Check.equal…(…)?` returns `Err(TestFailure)` from the test | a failed check aborts, as `assert` does in C |\n| Cost | `?` after each check, as for any error | every failure is a crash: no message beyond the panic's, and in the interpreter, whose stops are recoverable, still one test per process to be uniform |\n\n**A is proposed.** It is how CeeSafe code fails already. A test's cleanup runs (its locals are dropped as it returns),\nso a leak check after a failing test still means something. A panic is still caught, as a crash of its own process.\n\n**Where the runner is.** In `ceesafec` (proposed), or a library a package depends on. The runner starts processes,\nmeasures time, and reads the runtime's reports; `ceesafec` does all three for `build` and `run` already, and every\npackage gets it with no dependency.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n### 3.1 Declaring tests\n\n```ceesafe\n@test\nfn parses_a_request_line() -> Result<void, TestFailure> {\n    let parsed = parseLine(\"GET / HTTP/1.1\".bytes());   // its own error type: checked, not `?`-ed into TestFailure\n    Check.isOk(borrow parsed, \"the request line parses\")?;\n    let line = parsed!;\n    Check.equalText(\"GET\", line.method)?;\n    Check.isTrue(line.version == 11, \"HTTP/1.1\")?;\n    return Ok();\n}\n\n@test\nasync fn times_out_an_idle_read() -> Result<void, AsyncError> {\n    // ...\n    return Ok();\n}\n\n@test\nfn plain() {\n    // a void test fails only by panicking\n}\n```\n\n- `@test` goes on a free function with no parame",
    264 =>
        "ters and no type parameters, sync or async. It returns `void` or\n  `Result<void, E>` for any `E`. Anything else is **N0348**: on a method, with parameters, generic, or returning\n  another type.\n- **Where tests are.**\n  - In the package's sources, beside the code they test, and in its **test directories**: the manifest's\n    `tests = [\"tests\"]`, by default `tests` when that directory exists.\n  - Test directories are part of the same package, so a test sees the package's private items. Tests limited to the\n    public API, as a dependent package sees it, are a later addition.\n  - A test function's name is its identity: `module.function` for one in a module, the function's name otherwise.\n- **Build and check.**\n  - `check` checks the test directories too, so tests do not rot unseen.\n  - `build` leaves them out, and emits no `@test` function. A test cannot change a release build.\n\n### 3.2 The checks (lib/std, `testing.cee`)\n\n```ceesafe\npublic struct TestFailure {           // what a failed check returns; its message is shown with the test's name\n    public message: Vector<u8>;\n    public fn create(message: StringView) -> TestFailure;\n}\n\npublic struct Check {\n    public fn isTrue(condition: bool, what: StringView) -> Result<void, TestFailure>;\n    public fn isFalse(condition: bool, what: StringView) -> Result<void, TestFailure>;\n    public fn equalU64(expected: u64, actual: u64) -> Result<void, TestFailure>;    // \"expected 3, got 4\"\n    public fn equalI64(expected: i64, actual: i64) -> Result<void, TestFailure>;\n    public fn equalText(expected: StringView, actual: StringView) -> Result<void, TestFailure>;\n    public fn equalBytes(expected: Slice<u8>, actual: Slice<u8>) -> Result<void, TestFailure>;  // the first difference\n    public fn isOk<T, E>(r: borrow Result<T, E>, what: StringView) -> Result<void, TestFailure>;\n    public fn isErr<T, E>(r: borrow Result<T, E>, what: StringView) -> Result<void, TestFailure>;\n    public fn fail(message: StringView) -> Result<void, TestFailure>;\n}\n```\n\n- A message is built into an owned `Vector<u8>`, so a failure borrows nothing from the test.\n- **Other error types.** A test that returns `Result<void, E>` for another `E` (`AsyncError`, `IoError`, its own) fails\n  with that error. The runner shows `IoError`'s and `AsyncError`'s message, and for any other type the variant's name\n  where the generated code knows it.\n- Generic `equal`, and messages for any type, wait for an equality and a text interface (U10).\n\n### 3.3 The command\n\n```text\nceesafec test [--filter TEXT] [--list] [--interp] [--jobs N] [--timeout SECONDS]\n              [--sweep alloc|cancel] [--format text|json]\n```\n\n- **Building.** It builds the package's test harness once: the sources, the test directories and a generated entry\n  point. Then it runs each test, filtered by `--filter` (a substring of the name), in the name's order.\n- **Results.**\n  - Text: one line per test (`ok`, or `FAILED` with the failure), a summary, and exit status 1 if any failed.\n  - `--format json`: one document, as `check --diagnostics-format=json` does. Each test gives its name, its outcome,\n    its time, its failure message, and its captured output.\n- **Isolation.** Each test runs as its own process: the harness started with `--ceesafe-test <name>`. Its standard\n  output and error are captured and shown only when it fails. `--jobs N` runs N at once (default 1).\n- **Timeouts.** A test that runs past `--timeout` (default 60 s) is ended and counts as timed out. That needs the host\n  library to wait for a process with a limit, and end it: a small addition to `Process` (§5).\n- **Outcomes,** each reported as such:\n\n  | Outcome | When |\n  |---|---|\n  | passed | the test returned `Ok` or `void` |\n  | failed | it returned `Err` |\n  | panicked | the runtime's panic report, with its location |\n  | crashed | a signal or exception |\n  | timed out | it ran past `--timeout` |\n  | leaked | it passed, but allocations were still live at exit |\n\n- **Leaks.** `CEESAFE_ALLOC",
    265 =>
        "_REPORT` is on for every test, so a leak fails it: CeeSafe has no garbage collector, and a\n  leak is a bug.\n- **`--interp`** runs each test in the interpreter, in the `ceesafec` process, one after another: a panic is a stop the\n  runner catches. The clock is virtual and the run order fixed by the seed, so an async test's timing is deterministic.\n  Tests that call C (`extern \"C\"`, a package's C files) are reported as skipped there, as `run --interp` cannot run C.\n- **`--sweep alloc` and `--sweep cancel`** run each passing test again under the existing hooks:\n  - `alloc`: once per allocation, with `CEESAFE_FAIL_ALLOC_AT=k`;\n  - `cancel`: once per suspension point, with `CEESAFE_CANCEL_AT=k` (sampled past 200, as the sweep tools do).\n\n  Each run must end without a panic or a leak. A failing run is reported with its k, so it can be reproduced.\n\n### 3.4 The harness\n\n- `ceesafec` generates the entry point, in CeeSafe's NIR, before C exists: no reflection, nothing at run time beyond a\n  table.\n- **The generated program:**\n  - `--ceesafe-list` prints the tests' names;\n  - `--ceesafe-test <name>` runs that test and exits: 0 passed, 1 failed with the failure on standard error between\n    marker lines the runner reads, the runtime's own status for a panic;\n  - an async test runs under `Async.run`.\n- An executable package's own `main` is not run by the harness. It is still checked and compiled.\n- The protocol between harness and runner is these two arguments and the marker lines. It is versioned\n  (`ceesafe-test 1`) so a runner can refuse a harness it does not understand.\n\n## 4. Later, in another ADR\n\n- parameterised cases;\n- fixtures and setup;\n- tags and skips;\n- property tests with shrinking;\n- fuzzing with a kept corpus;\n- snapshots;\n- JUnit XML;\n- benchmarks;\n- a freestanding runner over a serial sink (CosmoOS);\n- public-API-only integration tests;\n- a generic `equal` once an equality interface exists.\n\nThey build on this one's harness and runner. Each needs its own design: a case table needs data the language can\nwrite without tuples.\n\n## 5. Consequences\n\n- A package's tests are `@test` functions and `ceesafec test`, with no runner of its own. CeeSafe.Web can move from\n  `tools/run_tests.py` to it.\n- Every test is leak-checked, and can be swept for allocation failures and cancellations: the checks CeeSafe's own\n  corpus has, for every package.\n- **New diagnostic:** N0348 (a `@test` function's shape).\n- **Host library:** `Process` gains a wait with a limit that ends the process when the limit passes. On POSIX that is\n  `kill`; on Windows, `TerminateProcess`. `test` uses it, and it is public for others.\n- **The compiler's own corpus is unchanged.** It tests the compiler: diagnostics, snapshots, the C. `ceesafec test` is\n  for packages.\n\n## 6. Questions for the owner\n\n1. `@test` on parameterless free functions, sync or async, returning `void` or `Result<void, E>`, in the sources and\n   in test directories (`tests = [...]`, default `tests`) that share the package's private items? Recommended: yes.\n2. Checks return `Result<void, TestFailure>` and are used with `?` (option A), with typed comparisons until an equality\n   interface exists? Recommended: yes.\n3. Each test in its own process natively; `--interp` in-process in the interpreter, deterministic; `--jobs` default\n   1? Recommended: yes.\n4. A leak fails a test by default? Recommended: yes.\n5. `--sweep alloc|cancel` per test, reusing the runtime's hooks? Recommended: yes.\n6. Leave cases, fixtures, property tests, JUnit, benchmarks and the freestanding runner to a later ADR (§4)?\n   Recommended: yes.\n\n## 7. Implementation plan (once accepted)\n\n1. Spec:\n   - tooling.md: the `test` command, the `tests` field, the harness protocol;\n   - type-system.md: `@test`;\n   - diagnostics.md: N0348;\n   - the API page for `testing.cee`;\n   - the guide (a new page, \"Testing\").\n2. `src/`:\n   - the attribute and its checks;\n   - the test directories in the workspace;\n   - `build` leaving them out;\n   -",
    266 =>
        " the generated entry point;\n   - the runner: processes, timeouts, the outcomes, JSON, the sweeps, `--interp`.\n3. lib/std: `testing.cee`; `Process`'s wait with a limit (runtime: `ceesafe_process_*`); then `tools/gen_library.py`\n   and `tools/gen_runtime.py`.\n4. Tests:\n   - a fixture package with a test of every outcome: pass, fail, panic, crash (a C file that raises a signal), time\n     out, leak, and an async test. `ceesafec test` must report each as itself, natively and with `--interp`;\n   - `--filter`, `--list`, `--jobs 4`, and the JSON document against an expected file reviewed by hand;\n   - `--sweep alloc` and `--sweep cancel` against `tools/fault_sweep.py` and `tools/cancel_sweep.py` on the same\n     programs, which must agree on the number of points;\n   - N0348 for each wrong shape; `build` emits no test;\n   - the contract suite step, on macOS, Debian and Windows.\n5. ROADMAP §10, and CeeSafe.Web's runner moved onto it as the first user.\n\n## 8. Implementation notes (2026-11-02)\n\n- **The timeout is the test process's own.** The ADR (§5) planned a `Process` wait with a limit that ends the child.\n  The compiler's own sources must build with stage 0 (ceesafec 0.3.2), whose library has none, so the runner would\n  have needed another bootstrap chain step. Instead the harness arms a watchdog thread in the runtime\n  (`ceesafe_thread_watchdog`), which prints `ceesafe-test 1 timeout` and exits with 124. The interpreter checks the\n  same deadline on the wall clock every 4096 steps. `Process` is unchanged.\n- **The harness is generated CeeSafe source**, `out/ceesafe-test/harness.cee`, checked and lowered with the package\n  like any file. That is still before C, with no reflection. The checker's entry is a setting (`entryName`): `main`,\n  or `ceesafeTestMain` for the harness, whose `main` is then an ordinary function.\n- **`isOk` and `isErr`** are `CheckResult<T, E>`'s, not `Check`'s: a method declares no type parameters of its own in\n  v0.\n- **A test's name is its function's name.** A package's free functions share one namespace (N0201), so the name is\n  unique.\n- **`--interp`** runs each test as `ceesafec run --interp --ceesafe-harness <harness> -- --ceesafe-test <name> …`, a\n  process each, so its output is captured as natively. Leaks and sweeps are native only. A test that calls C is\n  reported as skipped.\n- **`--jobs N`** splits the tests between two threads (`Thread.both`) until each has one job; the results keep the\n  tests' order.\n- **Hooks without the environment.** `ceesafe_test_begin` sets the allocation that fails, the suspension that cancels,\n  and both reports, overriding `CEESAFE_FAIL_ALLOC_AT` and `CEESAFE_CANCEL_AT`: the runner cannot set a child's\n  environment.\n- **Tests:**\n  - check-fail/test-shapes (N0348, each shape and a call from `main`);\n  - `tools/test_command_check.py` in the contract suite, on `tests/packages/test-command`, whose 14 tests have every\n    outcome, against `expected.tsv`.\n- **After CeeSafe.Web's report (2026-11-02).**\n  - `--interp` finds leaks too: the interpreter's model of `ceesafe_test_begin` turns on its allocation report, which\n    counts as the runtime's does. The sweeps stay native, and `--interp --sweep` is now a usage error (it ran no sweep\n    and passed).\n  - A `--filter` that matches nothing is exit status 1.\n  - Tests run in the names' order, as §3.3 says (they ran in declaration order).\n  - A test failing with its own enum names the variant (`returned Err(Mine.Broke)`), as §3.2 says, when the harness\n    can name the type unqualified and it is not generic; else `returned Err`.\n  - A new outcome, **exited**: a test that ends the process itself with a status (`Process.exit(3)`) was reported as\n    crashed, which §3.3 keeps for a signal or exception (129 to 192 on POSIX, an exception's code on Windows).\n  - `ceesafec test --help`.\n  - The fixture gains `leaksSafely` (leaked natively and under `--interp`) and `exits`; `tools/test_command_check.py`\n    checks the empty filter, t",
