    268 =>
        "# ADR 0050 — UDP sockets for tasks\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-02 (\"Yes, I accept all five ADRs with your recommendations\"): every\n  question of §5 as recommended. Asked for by the owner for CeeSafe.Web\n  (`~/dev/CeeSafe.Web/docs/ceesafe-upstream-work.md`, item U6). Its HTTP/3 (QUIC) and its own DNS client (phase B of\n  U5) need UDP.\n- Scope: lib/std's `AsyncUdpSocket`, the hosted runtime's datagram operations on every reactor, the interpreter's\n  model. It uses ADR 0045's message record, which was sized for this.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nCeeSafe has TCP only. QUIC needs four things beyond a plain datagram send and receive:\n\n- **The local address of a received datagram:** on a server bound to `[::]` with several addresses, a reply must leave\n  from the address the client used (`IP_PKTINFO`, `IPV6_RECVPKTINFO`).\n- **ECN bits:** both received (`IP_RECVTOS`, `IPV6_RECVTCLASS`) and sent.\n- **Truncation:** a datagram larger than the buffer must be reported, not silently cut.\n- **Sending many datagrams at once:** generic segmentation offload (`UDP_SEGMENT` on Linux, USO on Windows). This is\n  an optimisation, with a plain loop elsewhere.\n\nThese are `recvmsg`/`sendmsg` with control messages on POSIX and io_uring, and `WSARecvMsg`/`WSASendMsg` on Windows.\nADR 0045 already gave the message record room for the message header, a peer address and a control buffer (128\nbytes; 96 on Windows), so the record is settled.\n\nOne fact constrains the interpreter. Its socket models call the *host's* lib/std (ADR 0044 §3.7), and stage 0 (ceesafec\n0.3.2) has no UDP. The models therefore need a bootstrap chain step: one commit adds the library functions, the next\nuses them. That is the same pattern ADR 0044 used (`f7a7d57`, `93c433f`).\n\n## 2. Options\n\n**One call with every option, or simple calls plus a message call.**\n\n| | A. `sendTo`/`recvFrom`, plus `sendMsg`/`recvMsg` (proposed) | B. Messages only |\n|---|---|---|\n| What | the common case is two plain calls; QUIC uses the message calls with their fields | every send and receive is a message with optional fields |\n| Cost | four operations | every caller fills or ignores fields it does not use |\n\n**A is proposed.** DNS needs only `sendTo` and `recvFrom`. QUIC needs the message calls.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n### 3.1 The API (lib/std, `net.cee` and `async.cee`)\n\n```ceesafe\npublic struct UdpOptions {\n    public reuseAddress: bool;     // SO_REUSEADDR (ignored on Windows, as for TCP)\n    public reusePort: bool;        // SO_REUSEPORT; Unsupported on Windows\n    public v6Only: bool;           // IPV6_V6ONLY, set explicitly on an IPv6 socket\n    public sendBuffer: u32;        // SO_SNDBUF, 0: the system's\n    public recvBuffer: u32;        // SO_RCVBUF, 0: the system's\n    public receiveEcn: bool;       // IP_RECVTOS / IPV6_RECVTCLASS: recvMsg reports ECN\n    public receiveLocal: bool;     // IP_PKTINFO / IPV6_RECVPKTINFO: recvMsg reports the local address\n    public fn defaults() -> UdpOptions;   // everything off, buffers the system's\n}\n\n/// What recvFrom gives.\npublic struct Datagram { public length: usize; public from: SocketAddress; public truncated: bool; }\n\n/// What recvMsg gives.\npublic struct UdpReceived {\n    public length: usize;            // bytes in the buffer\n    public truncated: bool;          // the datagram was longer (MSG_TRUNC); the rest is gone\n    public from: SocketAddress;\n    public to: SocketAddress?;       // the local address it arrived at (with receiveLocal)\n    public ecn: u8;                  // the ECN bits, 0 to 3 (with receiveEcn), else 0\n}\n\n@noncopy\npublic struct AsyncUdpSocket {\n    public fn bind(address: SocketAddress, options: UdpOptions) -> Result<AsyncUdpSocket, IoError>;\n    public fn localAddress(borrow this) -> Result<SocketAddress, IoError>;\n    public async fn sendTo(borrow this, bytes: Slice<u8>, to: SocketAddress) -> Result<void, AsyncError>;\n    public async fn recvFrom(borrow this, buffer: SliceMut<u8>) -> Result<Datagram, AsyncError>;\n    pu",
    269 =>
        "blic async fn sendMsg(borrow this, message: borrow UdpMessage) -> Result<usize, AsyncError>;\n    public async fn recvMsg(borrow this, buffer: SliceMut<u8>) -> Result<UdpReceived, AsyncError>;\n}\n\n/// A datagram to send with sendMsg: the parts (ADR 0045's IoParts), where to, and optionally from where, its ECN\n/// bits, and a segment size.\npublic struct UdpMessage<region r> {\n    public parts: IoParts<r>;\n    public to: SocketAddress;\n    public from: SocketAddress?;   // the source address (IP_PKTINFO); null: the system's choice\n    public ecn: u8;                // 0: not set\n    public segmentSize: u16;       // 0: one datagram; else the parts are sent as datagrams of this size (GSO)\n}\n```\n\n- **Sending.** `sendTo` sends one datagram whole, or fails: a datagram is never partly sent. `EMSGSIZE` is\n  `InvalidInput`.\n- **Segments.** `sendMsg` with `segmentSize > 0` sends `ceil(total / segmentSize)` datagrams and returns how many\n  bytes it sent:\n  - Linux: one call with `UDP_SEGMENT`;\n  - Windows: `UDP_SEND_MSG_SIZE` where the system has it;\n  - elsewhere: one send per segment.\n\n  Under backpressure it may stop between datagrams: the count says how many segments went, and never a part of one.\n- **Receiving.** `recvFrom` and `recvMsg` wait for one datagram. A datagram longer than the buffer fills it and is\n  reported `truncated`.\n- **Addresses.** An IPv4-mapped address received on a dual-stack socket is reported as `V4`, as for TCP (ADR 0044).\n- **Errors** are ADR 0044's `IoError` kinds. A port unreachable that the system reports on a later receive\n  (`ECONNREFUSED`, `WSAECONNRESET` on Windows) is `ConnectionRefused` on that receive. Windows' reporting of it is\n  turned off (`SIO_UDP_CONNRESET`), as other runtimes do, so a server's receive loop is not broken by one bad peer.\n\n### 3.2 The runtime, on every reactor\n\n- **Readiness (epoll, kqueue, `WSAPoll`):** non-blocking `recvmsg`/`sendmsg` (`WSARecvMsg`/`WSASendMsg`), registering\n  for readability or writability on `EAGAIN`.\n- **IOCP:** overlapped `WSARecvMsg` (found with `WSAIoctl`, `WSAID_WSARECVMSG`) and `WSASendMsg`. The message header,\n  the peer address and the control buffer are in the message record (ADR 0045).\n- **io_uring:** `IORING_OP_RECVMSG` and `IORING_OP_SENDMSG`, with the record's `msghdr`.\n- **Control messages** are parsed and built in the runtime:\n  - packet info (`IP_PKTINFO`, `IPV6_PKTINFO`; macOS's `IP_RECVDSTADDR` for IPv4 where `IP_PKTINFO` is absent);\n  - traffic class (`IP_TOS`, `IPV6_TCLASS`; Windows `IP_ECN`, `IPV6_ECN`);\n  - segment size.\n\n  A sent ECN value is set per message where the system takes it as a control message (Linux, Windows). Elsewhere it\n  is set on the socket before the send, which is correct while one task sends on the socket at a time, as the type's\n  `borrow this` operations allow.\n- **CosmoOS userland** has no UDP in its socket layer yet: `bind` is `Unsupported`.\n\n### 3.3 The interpreter\n\nThe interpreter models the operations with the host lib/std's raw `AsyncSockets` UDP calls (ADR 0044 §3.7's pattern),\nwhich this ADR adds. So the bootstrap gains one chain step: the library commit, then the interpreter commit that uses\nit. Control messages are modelled for packet info and truncation; ECN and segments are reported as 0 and sent as\nplain datagrams there.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- CeeSafe.Web can write QUIC and a DNS client over UDP.\n- The bootstrap chain grows by one step, until stage 0 moves to a release with UDP (Decision 0009).\n- New diagnostics: none.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. `sendTo`/`recvFrom` for the common case, plus `sendMsg`/`recvMsg` with the local address, ECN, truncation and\n   segments (option A)? Recommended: yes.\n2. A datagram is sent whole or not at all, and a segmented send reports whole segments only? Recommended: yes.\n3. Windows' port-unreachable reports on receive turned off (`SIO_UDP_CONNRESET`)? Recommended: yes.\n4. A batched receive (`recvmmsg`) and connected UDP sockets left for later, when QUIC measur",
    270 =>
        "es the need? Recommended:\n   yes.\n\n## 6. Implementation plan (once accepted)\n\n1. Spec: §40.4 (the UDP types), c-backend.md §6 (the operations and the control messages), the guide.\n2. Runtime: the datagram socket, the options, the operations on each backend, control messages; `tools/gen_runtime.py`.\n3. lib/std: the types and operations, and `AsyncSockets`' raw UDP calls; `tools/gen_library.py`. One commit, which\n   becomes a chain step.\n4. The interpreter's models (the next commit); `tools/bootstrap_release.sh`'s chain and `bootstrap/reference.md`.\n5. Tests:\n   - corpus run-pass: a round trip on IPv4 and on IPv6 loopback; a truncated datagram flagged; `from` and `to` on\n     loopback v4 and v6 (the local address reported); ECN bits sent and seen; a segmented send received as the\n     expected datagrams; an empty datagram;\n   - a receive cancelled in flight under ASan, and `tools/cancel_sweep.py`;\n   - expected datagram contents from a Python oracle (the bytes and their split into segments);\n   - every reactor, the contract suite on macOS, Debian (io_uring) and Windows 11 ARM64 (IOCP, `WSAPoll`).\n6. ROADMAP §10.\n\n## 7. Implementation notes (2026-11-02)\n\n- **Two commits, one chain step** (§3.3): `71d5c13` adds the library and the runtime, with `AsyncSockets.udpBind`,\n  `udpSend` and `udpRecv`; the next commit's interpreter models the datagram calls with them on the host.\n  `tools/bootstrap_release.sh` builds `71d5c13` after `93c433f`.\n- **The record.** The send's destination is written into the record's name (IPv4-mapped for an IPv6 socket), and its\n  control messages are built once before the first step; five 32-bit fields after the control buffer hold the socket's\n  family, the ECN bits, the segment size and the two lengths. Windows' control buffer is 72 bytes (a packet info and\n  an ECN value), so the record still fits ADR 0045's 512.\n- **ECN on send** is a control message on Linux and Windows (`IP_ECN`/`IPV6_ECN` where the headers have them). On\n  macOS and the BSDs the socket's `IP_TOS`/`IPV6_TCLASS` is set for the send and cleared after it, which no other task\n  can see, since the step is one synchronous call on the executor's thread. **Windows refuses a sender's CE (3)**:\n  `InvalidInput`, which `tools/udp_check.py` expects there.\n- **Segments.** Linux sends up to 64 segments in one call (`UDP_SEGMENT`); more, and every other system, return\n  `CEE_ASYNC_NO_SEGMENTS`, and lib/std sends them one by one (`IoParts.upTo` cuts each). Windows' `UDP_SEND_MSG_SIZE`\n  is left for later, when QUIC measures the need (as §5.4 left batched receives).\n- **The local address** comes from the packet info: `IP_PKTINFO`'s `ipi_addr` (macOS sets it with `IP_RECVPKTINFO`),\n  `IPV6_PKTINFO` (IPv4-mapped as V4; a link-local address with its interface as the scope), `IP_RECVDSTADDR` where\n  the others are missing. Its port is the socket's, which lib/std keeps from the bind. macOS needs\n  `__APPLE_USE_RFC_3542` for the IPv6 options.\n- **Windows:** `WSAEMSGSIZE` is a truncated datagram, also from an overlapped `WSARecvMsg`'s completion; the\n  control-message arithmetic is the runtime's own (ws2def.h's `TYPE_ALIGNMENT` makes clang warn).\n- **An empty datagram** is sent as one empty part: macOS refuses `sendmsg` without any.\n- **The interpreter** reports the socket's bound address as the local one when it asked for it and is bound to a\n  specified address (what the packet info says on loopback), no ECN bits, and sends segments one by one.\n- **Tests:** run-pass/async-udp from `tools/oracles/gen_udp_cases.py` (IPv4 and IPv6 round trips, an empty datagram,\n  a truncated one, the peer and the local address, three segments across two parts), natively and in the interpreter;\n  `tools/udp_check.py` in the contract suite. A dual-stack socket's IPv4-mapped traffic with packet info and ECN is\n  not tested yet.\n\n",
    271 =>
        "# ADR 0051 — Sending a file on a stream without copying it\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-02 (\"Yes, I accept all five ADRs with your recommendations\"): every\n  question of §5 as recommended. Asked for by the owner for CeeSafe.Web\n  (`~/dev/CeeSafe.Web/docs/ceesafe-upstream-work.md`, item U7). Its static files (M9) are sent this way.\n- Scope: lib/std's `AsyncTcpStream`, the hosted runtime on every reactor, the interpreter's model.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nA static file is sent today by reading it into a buffer (`AsyncFiles.read`, on a worker thread) and writing the buffer\nto the socket. Every byte crosses into the process and back out. The kernels can send a file's pages to a socket\ndirectly:\n- Linux `sendfile`;\n- macOS and the BSDs `sendfile`, with a different signature;\n- Windows `TransmitFile`.\n\nThree facts shape the design:\n\n- **`File` is a compiler built-in, and its C form is its descriptor**, an `int32_t` (rt.h's `ceesafe_fd_drop(int32_t\n  *)`). lib/std can pass the runtime a pointer to it, under the same contract. The borrow of the `File` keeps it open\n  for as long as the operation is in flight: the frame that holds the borrow outlives the operation (ADR 0035).\n- **A file read can block on the disk.** `sendfile` on a non-blocking socket waits for the socket without blocking,\n  but it still reads pages the cache does not hold. nginx's default accepts that; its `aio threads` avoid it.\n- **Windows' `TransmitFile` sends at most 2³¹ − 1 bytes a call,** and takes the file's offset in the `OVERLAPPED`.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n**io_uring.** `IORING_OP_SPLICE` sends a file without blocking on the disk, but through a pipe: a pipe per stream, and\ntwo linked submissions per step. **Proposed:** on io_uring, the readiness path's `sendfile`, until a measurement shows\nthe disk wait matters. The splice path can come later without an API change.\n\n**Where a step reads the disk.** On the executor's thread (proposed, as nginx's default), or always on a worker thread.\nA worker per step adds a thread hand-off to every chunk, the common case being a cached file.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n### 3.1 The API (lib/std)\n\n```ceesafe\n// AsyncTcpStream\npublic async fn sendFile(borrow this, file: borrow File, offset: u64, length: u64) -> Result<u64, AsyncError>;\npublic async fn sendFileAll(borrow this, file: borrow File, offset: u64, length: u64) -> Result<void, AsyncError>;\n```\n\n- **`sendFile` is one step.** The task waits until the socket takes at least one byte, and the result is how many the\n  kernel sent, from `offset`. Fewer than `length` is normal; 0 means the file ends at `offset`. A `length` of 0 is\n  `Ok(0)` without a system call.\n- **`sendFileAll`** loops until `length` bytes are sent. If the file ends first, the result is `Err(Io(InvalidInput))`,\n  with the bytes before it sent.\n- **Offsets and lengths are 64-bit.** The runtime splits a step to the platform's limit, so files over 4 GiB work.\n- **The file's own position is not used or moved:** the offset is explicit. So one file may be sent on many streams at\n  once.\n- **Cancellation** follows ADR 0035: on IOCP an operation in flight is cancelled and waited for, and a step completed\n  meanwhile reports its count.\n\n### 3.2 The runtime, on every reactor\n\n- **Linux** (epoll, and io_uring per §2): `sendfile(socket, file, &offset, count)`. On `EAGAIN` the step registers for\n  writability.\n- **macOS and the BSDs:** `sendfile(file, socket, offset, &len, NULL, 0)`. On `EAGAIN`, `len` holds what was sent:\n  a partial count, or the wait.\n- **Windows, IOCP:** an overlapped `TransmitFile` with the offset in the `OVERLAPPED`, at most 2³¹ − 1 bytes. The\n  handle is the CRT descriptor's (`_get_osfhandle`).\n- **Windows `WSAPoll`, CosmoOS userland, and any system call that refuses** (`EINVAL` or `ENOSYS` from `sendfile`, a\n  file that is not a regular file): the runtime returns `Unsupported`. lib/std then falls back to a bounded copy: up to\n  64 KiB read with `pread` semantics on a worker thread (`As",
    272 =>
        "ync.blocking`), and sent with `write`. Same results, with a\n  copy.\n- **The record** is the plain operation record (`AsyncOp`, ADR 0035): no buffers are lent to the kernel.\n\n### 3.3 The interpreter\n\nIt models a step with the host's file read at an offset and the host's socket send: at most 64 KiB, a legal partial\nsend. The host lib/std has both, so no chain step is needed.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- CeeSafe.Web's static files go to the socket without passing through the process, where the system can.\n- A step may block the executor's thread on a disk read (§2). A server for files that are rarely cached should know it.\n  A later option can move steps to a worker.\n- New diagnostics: none.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. `sendFile` (one step, a partial count) and `sendFileAll`, with an explicit 64-bit offset and the file's position\n   untouched? Recommended: yes.\n2. `sendfile` on the executor's thread, as nginx's default, rather than a worker per step? Recommended: yes.\n3. io_uring uses the readiness path's `sendfile` for now, not splice through a pipe? Recommended: yes.\n4. Where the system cannot, a bounded 64 KiB copy in lib/std, with the same results? Recommended: yes.\n\n## 6. Implementation plan (once accepted)\n\n1. Spec: §40.4, c-backend.md §6, the guide's sockets section.\n2. Runtime: `ceesafe_async_op_sendfile(op, socket, file, offset, length)` on each backend; `tools/gen_runtime.py`.\n3. lib/std: `sendFile`, `sendFileAll` and the fallback; `tools/gen_library.py`.\n4. The interpreter's model.\n5. Tests:\n   - corpus run-pass: files of 0 and 1 bytes, a file larger than the socket buffers to a slow reader (partial steps),\n     an offset in the middle, a length past the end (`sendFileAll`'s `InvalidInput`), the same file on two streams at\n     once. The received bytes are checked against the file's (Python writes the file and its digest);\n   - a file larger than 4 GiB, sparse, so it takes no disk space: native only, in a tool beside the contract suite,\n     since the corpus runs in the interpreter too;\n   - a step cancelled in flight under ASan, and `tools/cancel_sweep.py`;\n   - every reactor, Debian, and Windows 11 ARM64 (IOCP's `TransmitFile`; `WSAPoll`'s fallback).\n6. ROADMAP §10.\n\n## 7. Implementation notes (2026-11-02)\n\n- **Windows.** `TransmitFile` refuses a count that runs past the file's end, so the runtime clamps the step to the\n  file's size (`GetFileSizeEx`), and a step at the end returns 0 as `sendfile` does. On a synchronous handle both\n  `TransmitFile` and a `ReadFile` at an offset move the file pointer, so each runs on a handle of its own from\n  `ReOpenFile`, closed at the completion (or at once if the submission fails): the `File`'s position is never moved.\n- **The interpreter** has no file send: `ceesafe_async_op_sendfile` returns `CEE_ASYNC_NO_SENDFILE`, and its\n  `ceesafe_file_read_at` reopens the file's path (each open file's path is kept beside it) and reads past `offset`,\n  since stage 0's `File` cannot seek. No chain step was needed.\n- **Linux** needs `_GNU_SOURCE` in `ceesafe_rt_io.c` for `pread` under glibc's strict C11 headers, as\n  `ceesafe_rt_thread.c` has for `nanosleep` (ADR 0049).\n- **Tests** as §6 planned: the 4 GiB case is `tools/sendfile_check.py`, in the contract suite. Writing it found B7, a\n  closure capture named `code` (ROADMAP §10).\n\n",
