        "# ADR 0052 — `HashMap` in lib/std\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-02 (\"Yes, I accept all five ADRs with your recommendations\"): every\n  question of §5 as recommended. Asked for by the owner for CeeSafe.Web\n  (`~/dev/CeeSafe.Web/docs/ceesafe-upstream-work.md`, item U9): header and query maps keyed by untrusted text.\n- Scope: lib/std (a key interface, a hasher, the map), one checker change (methods of `String` and `Vector`), a runtime\n  source of random seeds.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nlib/std's collections are `Vector`, `StableVector` and `SlotMap`. The compiler has its own map (`src/support/map.cee`):\n- insertion-ordered key and value vectors, and an open-addressing table of `u32` slots;\n- linear probing, load 3/4, doubling;\n- no removal;\n- keys `K: Key<K>`, an interface with `hashCode` and `sameAs`;\n- failure handled by docs/limits.md §2: a failed `put` leaves the map as it was.\n\nA library map can start from it. Four facts decide the shape:\n\n- **Interfaces have no `Self`.** A key interface is generic over the key, `HashKey<K>`, used as the bound\n  `K: HashKey<K>`, as the compiler's `Key<K>` is. Bounds are monomorphised, so a call through one is direct\n  (type-system.md §20).\n- **There is no equality or hash interface in lib/core or lib/std,** and `==` exists only on integers, floats, bools,\n  handles, pointers and `StringView`s.\n- **`impl I for String` is accepted, but its methods cannot be called.** A method call on a `Vector` or `String`\n  reaches only the built-in methods (`src/check/members.cee`'s `checkVectorMethod` reports N0200 for any other name).\n  A map keyed by `String` needs that fixed.\n- **Untrusted keys need a keyed hash.** With a fixed hash, a client can send header names that all collide (HashDoS).\n  A hasher keyed by a per-process random seed (SipHash-1-3) avoids it. Because iteration follows insertion, not the\n  hash, the seed changes no output, only timing. Programs stay deterministic, in the interpreter too.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n**Order after a removal.**\n\n| | A. The last entry fills the gap (proposed) | B. Strict insertion order | C. Unordered |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| What | iteration is insertion order until a removal, which moves the last entry into the removed one's place, as `Vector.swapRemove` | removed entries leave tombstones, compacted when the map grows | the table's order |\n| Cost | O(1) removal; the order is deterministic and simple to state | iteration skips tombstones; removal still O(1) | output depends on the seed: nondeterministic |\n\n**A is proposed.** It is deterministic, cheap, and simple to model in an oracle.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n### 3.1 Keys and the hasher (lib/std, `collections.cee`)\n\n```ceesafe\npublic interface HashKey<K> {\n    fn hash(borrow this, h: borrow mut Hasher);\n    fn sameAs(borrow this, other: borrow K) -> bool;\n}\n\npublic struct Hasher {                         // SipHash-1-3, keyed\n    public fn bytes(borrow mut this, data: Slice<u8>);\n    public fn u64(borrow mut this, value: u64);\n    public fn finish(borrow this) -> u64;\n}\n```\n\n- lib/std implements `HashKey` for:\n  - every integer type and `bool`;\n  - `String`, by bytes;\n  - `Vector<u8>`, by bytes.\n- A program implements it for its own types: a struct of fields hashes each field.\n- `StringView` keys are not offered: a scoped type cannot implement an interface (N0267). Use `String`, which a map\n  owns.\n\n### 3.2 The map\n\n```ceesafe\npublic struct HashMap<K: HashKey<K>, V> {\n    public fn create() -> HashMap<K, V>;\n    public fn withSeed(k0: u64, k1: u64) -> HashMap<K, V>;           // a fixed key, for tests and for maps of trusted keys\n    public fn put(borrow mut this, key: K, value: V) -> Result<V?, AllocError>;   // the old value, if the key was there\n    public fn get(borrow this, key: borrow K) -> borrow V?;\n    public fn getMut(borrow mut this, key: borrow K) -> borrow mut V?;\n    public fn contains(borrow this, key: borrow K) -> bool;\n    public fn remove(borrow mut this, key: borrow K) -> V?;\n    public fn ",
    274 =>
        "length(borrow this) -> usize;\n    public fn keyAt(borrow this, i: usize) -> borrow K;              // iteration: 0..length, in the order of §2\n    public fn valueAt(borrow this, i: usize) -> borrow V;\n    public fn clear(borrow mut this);\n}\n```\n\n- **Allocation.** Only `put` allocates. A failure leaves the map as it was (docs/limits.md §2): the table grows before\n  the entry is added, and a half-added key is taken back. `remove` and `clear` never allocate.\n- **The seed.** `create` uses a per-process key, read once from the system's random source:\n  - `getrandom` (Linux), `arc4random_buf` (macOS, the BSDs), `BCryptGenRandom` (Windows);\n  - a fixed key in the freestanding profile and in the interpreter.\n\n  `withSeed` fixes it.\n- **The allocator.** The system heap, as the compiler's map uses. A map from a user allocator follows when lib/std's\n  generic structs can take an allocator parameter.\n\n### 3.3 The checker: methods of `String` and `Vector`\n\nA method call on a `Vector` or `String` that names no built-in method looks for an impl method before reporting N0200,\nas calls on other types do. A built-in method keeps its name: an impl method with one is already N0228.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- CeeSafe.Web keeps header and query maps in a `HashMap<String, …>`, safe from colliding keys.\n- `impl` on `String` and `Vector` becomes usable, for any interface.\n- New diagnostics: none.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. `HashKey<K>` (hash into a keyed `Hasher`, and `sameAs`), with lib/std's impls for integers, `bool`, `String` and\n   `Vector<u8>`? Recommended: yes.\n2. Iteration in insertion order, a removal moving the last entry into the gap (option A)? Recommended: yes.\n3. SipHash-1-3 keyed per process from the system's random source, with `withSeed` to fix it? Recommended: yes.\n4. Method calls on `String` and `Vector` reach impl methods (§3.3)? Recommended: yes.\n5. The system heap only for now, a user allocator later? Recommended: yes.\n\n## 6. Implementation plan (once accepted)\n\n1. Spec: a collections section beside §23–24, and §21 for `HashKey`; the guide's collections page.\n2. lib/std: the interface, the impls, the hasher, the map; `tools/gen_library.py`.\n3. Runtime: `ceesafe_random_key` for the seed; `tools/gen_runtime.py`.\n4. The checker change of §3.3, and the interpreter's model of the seed (a fixed key).\n5. Tests, with the support library's approach:\n   - a Python oracle drives random `put`, `get`, `remove` and iteration against a `dict` whose order is kept as §2\n     says (`tools/oracles/`);\n   - SipHash-1-3 against the reference vectors;\n   - the allocation-failure sweep with `--check-leaks`, each failed `put` leaving the map as it was;\n   - many keys that collide under a fixed hash, which a keyed one spreads;\n   - check-fail: a key type without `HashKey` (N0268).\n6. ROADMAP §10.\n\n## 7. Implementation notes (2026-11-02)\n\n- **Removal** is backward-shift deletion in the open-addressing table, so there are no tombstones. The moved last\n  entry's slot is renumbered.\n- **Replacing a value goes through the end.** CeeSafe vectors have no in-place replace, and a popped value keeps a\n  loan on its vector. So the new value is pushed, swapped into place, and the old one popped. That push may allocate:\n  a replacement can also fail, and leaves the map as it was.\n- **Each entry keeps its hash,** so growing the table hashes nothing again.\n- **C names.** An impl method on a built-in type whose name is not an identifier (`Vector<u8>`) now takes its C name\n  from the type's mangled form. Before, the C was invalid.\n- **Tests:**\n  - run-pass/hash-map, generated by `tools/oracles/gen_hashmap_cases.py`: SipHash-1-3 under the key 00..0f from the\n    generator's own SipHash, itself checked against SipHash-2-4's published vectors; 3000 random operations against a\n    model of the order; `String` keys under the random key;\n  - `tests/support/fault/hash-map-sweep.cee`, in the contract suite (\"hashmap sweep\"): 79 failure points, each\n    leaving th",
    275 =>
        "e map as it was;\n  - check-fail/hash-map-key (N0268).\n  - Natively on macOS, on Debian (gcc `-Werror`, `getrandom`) and on Windows 11 ARM64 (clang and cl, `rand_s`), and\n    in the interpreter.\n\n## 8. Amendment (2026-11-03): the table's slots, and the hasher's word paths\n\nFrom a reviewer's prototypes (`/tmp/ceesafe-bench/hashmap-prototypes`, shared by the owner), after the independent\nbenchmark's `hashmap` workload. No promise of §3 changes: the API, the order of §2, allocation only in `put`, and a\nfailed `put` leaving the map as it was all stay.\n\n- **Slots carry 32 bits of hash.** A slot is a `u64`: the hash's low 32 bits above the entry's index plus one (0:\n  empty). The low 32 bits are the slot's home (`& mask`) and a fingerprint. So a probe rejects a slot of another hash\n  without reading any entry, and growing and backward-shift deletion read nothing but slots. The `hashes` vector of §7\n  is gone. The table is the same size per slot as `slots` plus `hashes` was per entry.\n- **A new key goes into the empty slot its lookup stopped at,** unless the table grew, so an insert probes once.\n- **Moving the last entry into a removed one's place** finds its slot from its key's hash, computed again. The search\n  for its entry number wraps, so it ends even if the key's hash were not stable.\n- **At most 2^32 − 1 entries.** A slot's entry field is 32 bits. One more `put` is `Err(OutOfMemory)`, where the u32\n  slots of §7 wrapped silently.\n- **`Hasher.u64`** compresses the word directly when nothing is pending, and **`Hasher.bytes`** compresses whole\n  eight-byte words while nothing is pending. Both give the same output as byte by byte.\n- **Tests:** run-pass/hash-map gains SipHash-1-3 of mixed feeds (p bytes, a word, p + 9 bytes, p = 0..8) from the\n  generator's own SipHash. They pass with the old byte-by-byte hasher and the new one. The map's tests, the oracle of\n  the order (3000 operations, `String` keys) and the \"hashmap sweep\" (now 64 failure points, from 79, since there is\n  no `hashes` push) pass natively, under the leak check and in the interpreter.\n- **Measured** with the reviewer's per-phase driver (4 M `u64` keys, the library's map before and after, best of a few\n  runs):\n\n  | | insert | hit | miss | remove |\n  |---|---|---|---|---|\n  | Apple M1, clang 21 `-O2`: before | 320–366 ms | 227–236 | 249–280 | 204–205 |\n  | the same, after | 264–266 | 160–171 | 216–218 | 210–212 |\n  | Rust `HashMap` (rustc 1.99, `-O3`) | 285 | 212 | 68 | 113 |\n  | Ryzen 5 3600, gcc 12 `-O2` (loaded): before | 832–1206 | 696–823 | 717–877 | 445–551 |\n  | the same, after | 550–608 | 418–464 | 432–475 | 441–519 |\n\n  Misses stay about 3× Rust's on the M1. Rust's map scans a group of tag bytes per probe. The reviewer's tag-byte\n  prototype (variants 6–7) cut big maps' misses by 40% but slowed removals 15–25%, so it is left for later.\n",
    276 =>
        "# ADR 0053 — Task sets: spawning while a task runs\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-02 (\"Yes, I accept all five ADRs with your recommendations\"): every\n  question of §5 as recommended. Asked for by the owner for CeeSafe.Web\n  (`~/dev/CeeSafe.Web/docs/ceesafe-upstream-work.md`, item U11). A server starts a task per accepted connection, and\n  an HTTP/2 connection starts a task per stream as streams open.\n- Scope: a new `Async` operation and a lib/std type, the checker and the borrow checker's view of it, both backends,\n  the runtime's scope record, the interpreter.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nA scope's children are spawned only in its synchronous body (type-system.md §40.5: \"`s` is not a local, and names the\nscope only in `s.spawn(…)`\"). Everything is built on that:\n\n- **The checker** resolves `s` only while the body is checked, in the same function (`src/check/async.cee`,\n  `scopeNamed`). A child closure is another function, so `s` is unknown there.\n- **The borrow checker** keeps each child's captured loans until the scope's wait, in the same NIR function\n  (`src/nir/borrow.cee`, the spawn holders).\n- **The C** names the scope as a field of the awaiting frame (`cee_frame->cee_s<site>`), which only that function's\n  code can reach.\n- **The runtime** sets the scope's owner at each spawn (`cee_spawn`: `scope->owner = cee_exec.current`). A child\n  spawning into its parent's scope would make the owner chain, which cancellation walks, wrong.\n\nSo a server today runs a fixed set of worker children, each looping accept-then-serve on a shared listener\n(CeeSafe.Web's `packages/web_transport/tests/workers.cee`). The worker count is the connection limit, and every\nworker's frame exists from the start.\n\nThere are no detached tasks, and this ADR keeps it so (§40.5: \"There is no detached task\"). A task set is a scope\nwhose children are started while it runs, and it waits for all of them before it returns.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n**What dynamically started children may capture.**\n\n| | A. Owned values only (proposed) | B. Loans, as a scope's children |\n|---|---|---|\n| What | `async move` closures whose captures are owned or `Copy` and hold no loan; shared state through `Arc` | children borrow from the task that started them, as a scope's do |\n| Cost | a shared listener or configuration goes in an `Arc`, or is passed down by value | a child may outlive the task that started it, so its loans would have to reach past that task's frame: a borrow-checker rule across functions and tasks that does not exist |\n\n**A is proposed.** It is sound with the rules that exist: a child holds nothing that can end before it does. A\nconnection's task owns its stream, and what it shares it shares through `Arc`.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n### 3.1 The operation\n\n```ceesafe\nawait Async.tasks(limit, oom, async (set) => {\n    loop {\n        let stream = await listener.accept()?;\n        await set.spawn(async move () => await serve(stream))?;   // waits while `limit` children run\n    }\n})\n```\n\n(Note, ADR 0074 §2.3: until 0.18 a child could not move a capture out, and this example was N0251. A child's body\nruns once, so it now may.)\n\n- **The operation.** `await Async.tasks(limit, oom, body)` is `Result<void, E>`:\n  - `limit: u32` is the most children live at once;\n  - `oom: E` is the result if a child's frame cannot be allocated;\n  - `body` is an async closure taking `set: borrow TaskSet<E>` and returning `Result<void, E>`.\n- **The body** runs as the set's owner, like any awaited call. When it returns, the set waits for every child.\n- **`Async.tasksIn(alloc, limit, oom, body)`** takes the children's frames from `alloc`, as `Async.scopeIn` does.\n- **The handle.** `set` is an ordinary `borrow` parameter. The body may pass it to the functions it calls and awaits,\n  so an accept loop can live in its own function. A spawned child cannot capture it: by §3.2, it holds no loan.\n\n### 3.2 Spawning\n\n- **`await set.spawn(child)`** starts `child`, an `async move` closure literal of res",
    277 =>
        "ult `Result<void, E>`, and is\n  `Result<void, E>`:\n  - while `limit` children are live, the spawning task waits for one to return (admission, as backpressure);\n  - once the set is cancelled, it returns the cancellation's error and starts nothing;\n  - if the frame cannot be allocated, it returns `Err(oom)`, and the set goes on.\n- **`set.trySpawn(child)`** is `bool`: false when `limit` children are live or the set is cancelled, without waiting.\n  It is not a task, so it needs no `await`.\n- **Captures** (option A): each capture of `child` is moved or copied into it, and its type holds no loan: no\n  reference, slice or `StringView`, and no struct with region parameters. Otherwise **N0349**, naming the capture.\n  `spawnSend`'s thread rule (§40.5, ADR 0038) applies to a `set.spawnSend`, which may run on another worker.\n\n### 3.3 Errors and cancellation\n\n- **A child's `Err` does not cancel its siblings.** One failed connection must not end the others. The set keeps the\n  first child error.\n- **The set's result,** once the body and every child have returned:\n  - the body's `Err` if it returned one;\n  - else the first child error, or `oom` if a frame could not be allocated;\n  - else `Ok`.\n- **The body's `Err`, or a cancellation of the set's owner, cancels every child** (§40.7's flag), and the set waits for\n  them.\n- **The owner is fixed when the set starts.** A child spawned by any task of the set has the set's owner in its chain,\n  so cancellation reaches it.\n\n### 3.4 Lowering and the runtime\n\n- **The set's record** is a `ceesafe_scope` with a limit and a count, in the frame of the task that awaits\n  `Async.tasks`, as a scope's is. `TaskSet<E>` is lib/std's handle to it: a reference to the record, which the body\n  takes by `borrow` and so cannot outlive. The C reaches it through the handle, from any function.\n- **`ceesafe_async_spawn` learns about sets:**\n  - the owner is set once, at the set's start;\n  - a full set parks the spawning task until a child returns (a wait list on the record);\n  - a returning child wakes one waiting spawner.\n- **The interpreter** looks a set up by its handle, not by the current task and site as it does a scope today. So a\n  spawn from any task of the set reaches the set.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- CeeSafe.Web's server starts a task per connection, up to a limit, with no worker frames allocated in advance, and\n  HTTP/2 a task per stream.\n- **Sharing goes through `Arc`** (or values passed down). That is the price of children that may outlive the task that\n  started them.\n- **A scope's `s.spawn` is unchanged.** Children that borrow from their caller stay the static form.\n- **New diagnostic:** N0349, a set child's capture that holds a loan.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. `await Async.tasks(limit, oom, async (set) => …)` and `Async.tasksIn(alloc, …)`, the handle a `borrow` parameter\n   the body may pass down but no child may capture? Recommended: yes.\n2. Children capture owned or `Copy` values only, with no loans, sharing through `Arc` (option A)? Recommended: yes.\n3. `await set.spawn` waits while `limit` children are live; `set.trySpawn` returns false instead? Recommended: yes.\n4. A child's `Err` does not cancel its siblings; the set reports the body's error, else the first child's? Recommended:\n   yes.\n\n## 6. Implementation plan (once accepted)\n\n1. Spec:\n   - §40.5 (task sets beside scopes, the capture rule and N0349);\n   - §40.7 (cancellation through the set's owner);\n   - c-backend.md §3a;\n   - intermediate-representation.md;\n   - diagnostics.md;\n   - the guide's async page.\n2. `src/`:\n   - the checker (the operation, the handle's type, `spawn`/`trySpawn` on it, N0349);\n   - HIR and NIR forms;\n   - the C backend (the record in the frame, spawn through the handle);\n   - the interpreter's sets.\n3. Runtime: the record's limit, count, waiting spawners and fixed owner; `tools/gen_runtime.py`.\n4. lib/std: `TaskSet<E>`; `tools/gen_library.py`.\n5. Tests:\n   - corpus run-pass: an accept loop starting a task",
    278 =>
        " per connection, with more clients than the limit, all served; a\n     full set making the spawner wait; `trySpawn` refused when full; a child's error not cancelling siblings; the\n     body's error and an outer cancellation cancelling every child; `oom` from `tasksIn` with a failing allocator; the\n     handle passed to a function that spawns;\n   - check-fail: N0349 for a borrowed capture, a slice, a `StringView`; the handle captured by a child;\n   - `tools/cancel_sweep.py`, the allocation-failure sweep with `--check-leaks`, TSan for `spawnSend` (ADR 0038);\n   - every reactor and Windows.\n6. ROADMAP §10.\n\n## 7. Implementation notes (2026-11-02)\n\n- **The body through ADR 0037.** `Async.tasks(limit, oom, body)` is checked as a call of lib/core's\n  `AsyncTasks<E>.run`, an `@effectsOf(body)` async fn whose async instance awaits the body in the awaiting task: the\n  set's owner, as §3.1 says. Its frame holds the record (`TaskSetRecord<E>`: the runtime's `ceesafe_task_set`, then\n  the first child error), and after the body it waits for the children with `ceesafe_async_set_done` and\n  `Async.park`. So the compiler needs no form for the body or the wait, and the borrow checker no rule: only the\n  start is the compiler's.\n- **The start.** `await set.spawn(child)` is checked as `await TaskSet.spawned(set, SetSpawn(set.recordOf(), await\n  set.room(), child))`, and `trySpawn` with `tryRoom` and `started`. `SetSpawn` is a HIR form and the NIR builtin\n  `AsyncSetSpawn`, emitted in any function (a sync one may call `trySpawn`). `set` must be a local or parameter of\n  type `TaskSet<E>` or a reference to one, since it is read once per step.\n- **Readings of §3.2 and §3.4.**\n  - `spawn`'s `Err(oom)` may come many times, so `E` must be `Copy` (N0268 at `Async.tasks`).\n  - A spawn on a cancelled set has no error of `E` that means cancellation, so it starts nothing and is `Err(oom)`;\n    its task is being cancelled, and so is its set's result.\n  - Only the owner can spawn (a child cannot capture the set), so the runtime keeps one task waiting for room, and\n    the owner never changes.\n  - A `limit` of 0 is 1.\n- **`Async.tasksIn` (the second commit).** A spawn in another function does not know the allocator's type, so the set\n  is given its adapters when it starts: lib/core's `AsyncTasksIn<E, A>.run` calls `Async.setAllocator(record, alloc)`,\n  a library-only primitive (N0345), whose builtin `AsyncSetAllocator` carries `A` and emits\n  `ceesafe_async_set_allocator(record, ceeAA_<A>, ceeAD_<A>)`; the runtime's set spawns take their blocks from them.\n  `Async.tasks` is `AsyncTasksIn<E, SystemHeap>` (its frames are the system heap's either way). `tasksIn` works in\n  the freestanding profile; `tasks` is hosted only.\n- **`set.spawnSend`** checks ADR 0038's rule on the child's captures (each moved in, so each must be `Send`) and on\n  `E`, and calls `ceesafe_async_set_spawn_send` and `ceesafe_async_publish`. A set with `spawnSend` children is shared:\n  admission and its wait for room take the run's lock, as `scope_done` does. With a user allocator (tasksIn) the\n  child runs on this worker, since a frame from it is freed where the child returns.\n- **The interpreter** takes a user allocator's blocks for a set's children as for `scopeIn`, and models\n  FailingAllocator's attempts (`failAfter`).\n- **The interpreter** models the record (its InterpScope, flagged as a set), the room and done waits (parked until a\n  child returns, through `asyncWoken`), the body's cancellation, and the start.\n- **Tests:** run-pass/async-task-set (ten children under a limit of three, started from a function the set is passed\n  to; a child's error that cancels no sibling; the body's error cancelling every child; `trySpawn` refused while full;\n  an outer timeout reaching every child), natively, in the interpreter, under the cancellation sweep; check-fail/\n  async-task-set (N0349 for a reference, a slice, a `StringView` and the set itself; N0209; N0342; N0268); the guide's\n  example.\n\n",
    279 =>
        "# ADR 0054 — Generic impls, and impls for types with region parameters\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-02 (\"Yes, I accept all five ADRs with your recommendations\"): every\n  question of §5 as recommended. Asked for by the owner for CeeSafe.Web\n  (`~/dev/CeeSafe.Web/docs/ceesafe-upstream-work.md`, item U10). CeeSafe.Web's M0 designed around the gap: `Box<dyn\n  Transport>` at the connection edge, concrete body types instead of an interface, hook-style middleware instead of\n  generic wrappers.\n- Scope: the parser, the checker's impls, bounds and `dyn` tables, the backends' view of instantiated impl methods.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nAn impl names one concrete, unscoped type (type-system.md §21; `src/check/interfaces.cee`, `processImpl`):\n\n- `impl<T: Transport> Transport for Wrap<T>` does not parse: N0105, since `parseImpl` reads a type right after `impl`.\n- `impl Body for RequestBody`, where `RequestBody<region r>`, is N0267: \"v0 implements interfaces only for concrete,\n  unscoped types\". The reason is §37: a `dyn I` value carries no loans of its own.\n\nWhat exists to build on:\n\n- **Monomorphisation.** A generic function or struct is instantiated per type argument (`instantiateFunction`,\n  `instantiateStruct`), and calls through a bound resolve to the concrete type's impl method (`findMethod`, then\n  `implMethodFor`).\n- **Impls are found by exact type id** (`implements`, `implMethodFor`), so an impl for `Pair<u8>` exists and one for\n  `Pair<T>` cannot be written.\n- **`dyn I` tables** are recorded per (implementing type, interface type) when a value is coerced (`coerceToDyn`), and\n  the backend emits one table per pair.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n**Overlap.** A generic impl `impl<T> I for Wrap<T>` and a concrete `impl I for Wrap<u8>` would both apply to\n`Wrap<u8>`. Choosing between them is specialisation, which needs rules for \"more specific\". **Proposed:** no overlap,\nat most one impl per (interface, type constructor). A later ADR may add specialisation.\n\n**Where an impl may be written.** With generic impls, two packages could each write `impl I for Wrap<T>` and both be\nlinked. **Proposed:** the orphan rule. An impl is written in the package that declares the interface or the type\nconstructor, so two packages never both provide one.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n### 3.1 Generic impls\n\n```ceesafe\nimpl<T: Transport> Transport for Logged<T> {\n    async fn read(borrow mut this, buffer: SliceMut<u8>) -> Result<usize, NetError> => await this.inner.read(buffer);\n    // ...\n}\n```\n\n- **Syntax:** `impl<T: B, U> I<…> for C<…T…, …U…> { … }`. The impl's type parameters must each appear in the target\n  type; their bounds are as a generic function's (§20).\n- **Matching.** A type `C<A>` implements `I` when an impl's target unifies with it and the bounds hold for the\n  arguments found. `implements` and `implMethodFor` unify against impl templates as well as concrete impls.\n- **Instantiation.** An impl's methods are instantiated per type argument, as a generic struct's are, and cached. A\n  call through a bound, a call on the concrete type, and a `dyn I` table all use the instance. The backend sees\n  ordinary functions and needs no change.\n- **Overlap** (§2): two impls of one interface whose targets have the same type constructor are N0267, generic or not.\n  So `impl I for Wrap<u8>` beside `impl<T> I for Wrap<T>` is refused.\n- **Orphan rule** (§2): an impl whose package declares neither the interface nor the target's type constructor is\n  N0267. Built-in types (integers, `String`, `Vector`) count as lib/core's, so a program implements its own interfaces\n  for them, as today.\n\n### 3.2 Impls for types with region parameters\n\n```ceesafe\nimpl<region r> Body for RequestBody<r> { … }\n```\n\n- A struct or enum with region parameters may implement an interface, with the regions named in the impl header. The\n  methods' signatures use them as the struct's own methods do (§25).\n- **Such a type is used through bounds, statically.** Coercing it to `dyn ",
    280 =>
        "I` stays N0268, since a `dyn` value carries no\n  loans (§37). Scoped `dyn` is OD-10a, separate.\n- An interface method that declares its own region parameter (`async fn handle<region r>(…)`, N0216 today) stays\n  refused: methods declare no type or region parameters of their own in v0 (`src/check/declare.cee`, N0216).\n\n### 3.3 Compile time\n\nEach instance of a generic impl is checked and compiled once per distinct type argument list, as generic functions\nare. The depth limit for instantiation (N0266, 64) applies to impls too.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- CeeSafe.Web can wrap a transport generically (`Logged<T: Transport>`), and implement `Body` for its scoped request\n  body.\n- **The orphan rule may refuse an impl that compiles today:** one written in a package that declares neither the\n  interface nor the type. The implementation searches the corpus, the tests and CeeSafe.Web for such impls and reports\n  each before the rule lands. The check is N0267, with a message saying where the impl belongs.\n- New diagnostics: none. N0267 and N0268 gain the cases above.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. `impl<T: B> I for C<T>`, monomorphised per type argument, with at most one impl per (interface, type constructor)\n   and no specialisation? Recommended: yes.\n2. The orphan rule: an impl lives in the package of its interface or of its type constructor? Recommended: yes.\n3. Impls for types with region parameters, usable through bounds but not as `dyn I`? Recommended: yes.\n\n## 6. Implementation plan (once accepted)\n\n1. Spec: §21 (impls, matching, overlap, the orphan rule), §25 (scoped impls), §37 (`dyn` unchanged), diagnostics.md\n   (N0267, N0268 cases), the guide's generics page.\n2. `src/`:\n   - the parser (`impl<…>`);\n   - the checker:\n     - impl templates;\n     - unification in `implements` and `implMethodFor`;\n     - instantiation and caching of impl methods;\n     - the overlap and orphan checks;\n     - scoped impls and the `dyn` refusal;\n   - the HIR printer.\n3. Tests:\n   - check-pass with `.hir` snapshots: a generic impl used through a bound, on a concrete instance and as `dyn I`; an\n     impl with two parameters; a scoped impl through a bound;\n   - run-pass: the same, natively and in the interpreter, with expected output from the program's specification (a\n     wrapper that counts calls);\n   - check-fail: overlap; an orphan impl; a scoped type coerced to `dyn I`; a parameter that does not appear in the\n     target; an unmet bound;\n   - the contract suite, and CeeSafe.Web's three probes (`tests/probes/impl-generic-fail.cee`,\n     `impl-scoped-fail.cee`) turned into passing tests.\n4. ROADMAP §10.\n\n## 7. Implementation notes (2026-11-02)\n\n- **Parsing.** `ItemKind.Impl` carries the impl's type and region parameters; the formatter and the AST printer\n  print them, and the documentation generator lists a generic impl under its struct's name, as any impl.\n- **Templates.** `processImpl` declares the impl's type parameters (with their bounds) and region names, resolves the\n  target and the interface with them in scope, and checks the impl once for its target, `C<T…>` with the parameters\n  opaque, as a generic struct's methods are checked. The template (`ImplTemplate`) is then implemented for each\n  instance of `C` that the target matches (unification, then substitution equal to the instance) and whose arguments\n  meet the bounds (`checkBounds`, quietly): its methods are declared again for the instance with the parameters'\n  environment (`attachGenericImpls`). Instances made while the declarations are read are handled once every impl is\n  known (`attachToInstances`, twice, so a bound met through another instance's impl does not depend on their order);\n  later ones as they are made (`instantiateStruct`, `instantiateEnum`). So `implements` and `implMethodFor` keep\n  looking up exact types, and the backends see ordinary methods.\n- **Overlap** is checked as §3.1 says: with a generic impl on either side, two impls of one interface (with argument",
    281 =>
        "s\n  that could be equal) whose targets are the same struct or enum are N0267.\n- **The orphan rule** compares packages: the impl's file's, the interface's and the target struct's or enum's. The\n  libraries are one package for it (the built-in types, lib/core and lib/std). The search §4 asked for found nothing it\n  breaks: the corpus, the support and package tests, and every package of CeeSafe.Web check as before.\n- **Regions.** A method of `impl<region r> …` has the impl's region names in scope (`regionScope`, through\n  `implRegionsOf`); regions are erased, so nothing else changes. A scoped type that implements an interface coerced to\n  `dyn I` is N0268 (`coerceToDyn`).\n- **Tests:** run-pass/impl-generic (a counting wrapper used directly, through a bound, nested and as `dyn`; two\n  parameters; a generic interface implemented generically; a scoped impl through a bound) and\n  run-pass/impl-generic-async (CeeSafe.Web's two probes as passing programs: an async transport wrapper, an async body\n  over borrowed bytes); check-fail/impl-generic (overlap, a parameter missing from the target, a generic impl for\n  `u32`, the orphan rule with lib/std's `HashKey` for `f64`, an unmet bound, a scoped type as `dyn`); the guide's\n  generics page. No `.hir` snapshot was added: the run-pass programs' output is specified by the programs.\n\n",
    282 =>
        "# ADR 0055 — Wall-clock time\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-02 (\"Yes, I accept ADR 0055 with your recommendations\"): every question\n  of §5 as recommended. Asked for by CeeSafe.Web (`~/dev/CeeSafe.Web/docs/ceesafe-upstream-work.md`, item\n  U12): an HTTP server with a clock must send `Date` (RFC 9110 §6.6.1), and cookies' `Expires`, logs and certificate\n  checks need the real time. CeeSafe.Web's `HttpDate.format(unixSeconds, …)` is written and waits for this.\n- Scope: lib/std, the hosted runtime, the interpreter.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nCeeSafe has one clock, the monotonic one: `Timer.now()` (lib/core, `ceesafe_async_now`: `CLOCK_MONOTONIC`,\n`GetTickCount64`, or the freestanding environment's `ceesafe_env_now_ms`), in milliseconds from an arbitrary start. It\norders events and measures time, and it never jumps, but it says nothing of the date. Nothing in `runtime/` or `lib/`\nreads the system's real time.\n\nThe two clocks answer different questions and must stay apart: the wall clock jumps (NTP, the user), so a deadline or\na timeout on it would fire early or never. `Timer` keeps the monotonic clock for every wait.\n\nThe interpreter runs tasks on a virtual clock (type-system.md §40.8): its `Timer.now()` starts at 0 and moves only\nwhen no task can run, so its runs are reproducible.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n**What the interpreter answers.**\n\n| | A. A fixed epoch on the virtual clock (proposed) | B. The host's real time |\n|---|---|---|\n| What | 2000-01-01T00:00:00Z plus the virtual clock's milliseconds | what the native program would read |\n| Cost | the interpreter's date is not today's | a program's output under `run --interp` and the corpus differs from run to run |\n\n**A is proposed**: the interpreter is the reference implementation and the corpus's second runner, and its runs are\nreproducible now. A program that prints today's date runs natively.\n\n**Precision.** Seconds and nanoseconds (as `timespec`) cost nothing more than milliseconds and lose nothing; the\nmilliseconds are one call on top.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n```ceesafe\n// lib/std, hosted\npublic struct UnixTime {\n    public seconds: i64;     // since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z, leap seconds not counted (POSIX time); negative before\n    public nanoseconds: u32; // 0 to 999 999 999\n\n    public fn millis(borrow this) -> i64;  // seconds * 1000 + nanoseconds / 1 000 000, rounded toward minus infinity\n}\n\npublic struct Clock {\n    /// The system's real time now. It may jump backward or forward (NTP, the user): measure and wait with Timer.\n    public fn now() -> UnixTime;\n    public fn unixMillis() -> i64;   // Clock.now().millis()\n}\n```\n\n- **The runtime:** `ceesafe_clock_realtime(int64_t *seconds, uint32_t *nanoseconds)`:\n  - POSIX (Linux, macOS, the BSDs): `clock_gettime(CLOCK_REALTIME)`;\n  - Windows: `GetSystemTimePreciseAsFileTime`, from 1601 in 100 ns steps, converted;\n  - CosmoOS userland: its `clock_gettime(CLOCK_REALTIME)` if its libc has one, else 0 (1970), which the guide states.\n- **Hosted only:** `Clock` is lib/std's (N0293 in the freestanding profile, as for files). A freestanding environment\n  hook for the real time is left for when a kernel asks.\n- **The interpreter** answers 2000-01-01T00:00:00Z (946 684 800 s) plus its virtual clock (option A).\n- **Infallible:** every hosted system above has the call; a failure (none is documented for these clocks) reads as 0.\n- **Not in this ADR:** calendar dates, time zones and formatting. CeeSafe.Web formats `Date` itself from Unix seconds,\n  and a calendar library is a separate design.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- CeeSafe.Web turns `Date` on; cookies and logs get times.\n- Two clocks, named for what they answer: `Timer` (monotonic, for waits and measurements) and `Clock` (the date).\n- New diagnostics: none.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. `Clock.now() -> UnixTime { seconds: i64, nanoseconds: u32 }` and `Clock.unixMillis() -> i64`, in lib/std, hosted\n   only, infallible? Recommended: yes.\n2. The interpreter answers a fixed epoch (200",
    283 =>
        "0-01-01T00:00:00Z) plus its virtual clock, so its runs stay reproducible\n   (option A)? Recommended: yes.\n3. Calendar dates, time zones and formatting left out, for a later design? Recommended: yes.\n\n## 6. Implementation plan (once accepted)\n\n1. Spec: §26 (host services) or §40.4 beside `Timer`; tooling.md's interpreter section (the epoch); the guide.\n2. Runtime: `ceesafe_clock_realtime` in `ceesafe_rt_io.c` (every hosted program links it); `tools/gen_runtime.py`.\n3. lib/std: `UnixTime`, `Clock`; `tools/gen_library.py`.\n4. The interpreter's model: the epoch plus the virtual clock.\n5. Tests:\n   - corpus run-pass: under the interpreter the epoch, and `millis()` of negative and positive times against values\n     worked out by hand (the rounding toward minus infinity);\n   - a native-only check beside the contract suite: `Clock.now()` within a second of Python's `time.time()` on macOS,\n     Debian and Windows, and nanoseconds below 10⁹;\n   - check-fail: `Clock` in the freestanding profile (N0293).\n6. ROADMAP §10.\n\n## 7. Implementation notes (2026-11-02)\n\n- **Freestanding:** lib/std is not loaded in the freestanding profile, so `Clock` there is an unknown name (N0200), as\n  `Directory` and every lib/std type are, not N0293 as §3 said; check-fail/freestanding-hosted-names shows it.\n- **The runtime** is `ceesafe_clock_realtime` in `ceesafe_rt_io.c`; on Windows it counts from 1601 in 100 ns steps\n  and gives 1970 for an earlier time, which no system reports.\n- **The interpreter** writes 946684800 plus its virtual milliseconds' seconds, and their remainder as nanoseconds.\n- **Tests:** run-pass/clock (`millis()` rounding toward minus infinity, worked out by hand; `Clock.now()` after 2000,\n  natively and in the interpreter); `tools/clock_check.py` in the contract suite (the native clock within two seconds\n  of Python's `time.time()`, the interpreter's epoch exactly, and after a 1500 ms virtual sleep).\n\n",
    284 =>
        "# ADR 0056 — Recursion through `await`: frames from a per-task stack\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-02 (\"I accept ADR 0056 with your recommendations\"): every question of §5\n  as recommended. Asked for by the owner after CeeSafe.Web's notes on middleware (its v0 limit 3):\n  a `Logged` handler holding the next handler as `Box<dyn Handler<S>>` is N0343 once one `Logged` can wrap another,\n  and the only way out today is `Async.boxed`, one allocation per middleware layer per request.\n- Scope: the coroutine pass's recursion rule (N0343), the C backend's await of a frame held by pointer, the hosted\n  runtime's executor, the interpreter (unchanged), the freestanding profile.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nA frame has a size known at compile time and is placed once (type-system.md §40.6): an awaited call's frame is\nembedded in its awaiter's. ADR 0036 made a call through `dyn I` take room for the largest frame among the types\ncoerced to `dyn I` (`S(I, m)`). So if an impl in `S(I, m)` awaits, directly or not, a call that may reach itself, its\nframe would contain itself: **N0343** (ADR 0036 §3.5; the same rule for direct recursion, §40.1).\n\nThe sure fix is `await Async.boxed(alloc, x.m(…))`: the frame from an allocator, the awaiter holding a pointer, the\nvalue `Result<T, AllocError>`. For middleware that is an allocation and a failure path per layer per request, written\nat every call that might recurse.\n\nThree facts make something cheaper possible:\n\n- **Within a task, frames are strictly last in, first out.** An awaited call's frame lives exactly while its awaiter\n  waits for it: the awaiter cannot resume, return, or start another await of its own until that call returns, and a\n  cancelled call still returns (§40.7: cancellation never destroys a frame). Children (spawned, race, all, sets) are\n  other tasks, with frames of their own. So a task's frames that are not embedded form a stack.\n- **A task stays on one worker once it has started** (ADR 0038), so its stack is touched by one thread.\n- **ceesafec sees every cycle** (ADR 0036 §1, the closed program): the coroutine pass already finds each await edge\n  that closes one, to report N0343.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n**Where a recursive await's frame comes from.**\n\n| | A. A per-task frame stack (proposed) | B. `Async.boxed` everywhere, as today | C. A fixed reserve per task |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| What | an await edge on a cycle takes its frame from its task's stack; the frame is popped when the call returns | each recursive call allocates and frees, and returns `Result` | each task gets a fixed block, as a thread gets a stack |\n| Steady cost | none: an executor reuses the stacks' chunks across tasks | an allocation and a free per call | the reserve per task, used or not |\n| Source | unchanged: the program that was N0343 compiles | `Async.boxed(…)!` at each such call | unchanged |\n| When memory runs out | §3.3 | `Err(OutOfMemory)` at the call | at a depth fixed in advance |\n\n**A is proposed.** It removes the per-call allocation and the source change. Like a thread's call stack, it ties memory\nto the depth actually reached, but it grows on demand and gives memory back to the executor, not to the system.\n`Async.boxed` stays for code that wants the failure as a value, or a frame from its own allocator.\n\n**Which cycles.** The mechanism is the same for a cycle through `dyn` (ADR 0036) and for direct recursion\n(`async fn walk` awaiting `walk`). Proposed: both, so N0343 is gone in the hosted profile, and recursion through\n`await` behaves like recursion in sync code (§3.1).\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n### 3.1 Which awaits are stacked\n\n- The coroutine pass finds the **strongly connected components** of the await graph: functions, and through a `dyn I`\n  call every impl in `S(I, m)`, as N0343 does today. An await edge **inside** a component is **stacked**: its awaiter\n  holds a pointer to the callee's frame, not the frame. Every other await embeds its callee's frame as before, so a\n  program without re",
    285 =>
        "cursion through `await` compiles exactly as today.\n- Within one component every edge is stacked, so no frame of the component contains another of it.\n- `--explain-frames` marks a stacked await (`stacked: the frame comes from the task's stack, N bytes`).\n- **N0343 stays only** where there is no task stack (§3.4), and for an `@effectsOf` function's async instance calling\n  itself with its closure (ADR 0037), whose frame is laid out with the closure's.\n\n### 3.2 The task's frame stack\n\n- Each task has a **frame stack**: a list of chunks, empty at first. A stacked await pushes its callee's frame on it\n  (aligned for the frame, its size from ceesafec or, through `dyn`, from the table, ADR 0036 §3.6) before the call\n  starts, and pops it when the call has returned.\n- **Chunks** are 16 KiB, or the frame's size if larger. A chunk the stack no longer uses goes back to its\n  **executor's pool** (one per worker thread), which keeps up to 64 for the next tasks; a task that returns gives all\n  of its back. So a server whose requests recurse to the same depth allocates in its first requests and then not at\n  all.\n- A frame on the stack is still placed once and never moves (§40.6): chunks are never moved or resized.\n- Drops, cancellation and the debugger's `tasks` command see a stacked frame as an embedded one: the resume chain is\n  the same, one pointer deeper.\n\n### 3.3 When memory runs out\n\nA stacked await's value is the callee's, `T`, as for any await. If no chunk can be had:\n\n| | a. Panic (proposed) | b. Cancel the task |\n|---|---|---|\n| What | `async frame stack: out of memory`, as a sync program whose call stack overflows stops | the task is cancelled, and the failing await returns the cancellation's error |\n| Cost | the process stops | only an `Async.*` or `Result<T, AsyncError>` call can carry it; a `T` cannot |\n\n**a is proposed**, and stated where recursion is: memory for recursion through `await` is the call stack's kind, not a\nvalue. Code that wants the failure as a value keeps `Async.boxed`. The fault-injection sweep reaches the chunk\nallocation (it is the runtime's `ceesafe_alloc`), so a test sees the panic where memory runs out.\n\n### 3.4 Profiles and threads\n\n- **Hosted:** chunks from the system heap, pooled per executor.\n- **Freestanding:** no heap, so no stack: N0343 stays, and `Async.boxed(alloc, …)` remains the way (ADR 0039). An\n  environment hook for chunks can come later, when a kernel asks.\n- **Threads** (`spawnSend`, `Async.runWorkers`): a task's stack is used only by the worker running it, and its pool is\n  that worker's.\n\n### 3.5 The interpreter\n\nIts activations are already dynamic: it runs the program that was N0343 as it is, with no stack to model. Its frames do\nnot count toward `CEESAFE_ALLOC_REPORT` today and still do not.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- CeeSafe.Web's middleware stacks without `Async.boxed` and without an allocation per request in the steady state:\n  `await this.inner.handle(…)` compiles as written.\n- Recursive async code (a tree walk, a recursive descent parser) is written as sync code is.\n- **The diagnostic changes meaning:** N0343 is the freestanding profile's and ADR 0037's case only. Programs that use\n  `Async.boxed` to break a cycle keep working, with their own allocation.\n- **Memory is no longer fully known at compile time** for a program with recursion through `await`: its depth decides\n  it, as for sync recursion. `--explain-frames` says which awaits are stacked.\n- New diagnostics: none.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. Frames of recursive awaits from a per-task stack of pooled chunks (option A), with no source change? Recommended:\n   yes.\n2. Both kinds of cycle: through `dyn` and direct recursion, so N0343 remains only in the freestanding profile and for\n   ADR 0037's case? Recommended: yes.\n3. Running out of memory for the stack panics (3.3 a), with `Async.boxed` kept for failure as a value? Recommended:\n   yes.\n4. 16 KiB chunks and a pool of 64 per executor, as constants in the runtime",
    286 =>
        " for now? Recommended: yes.\n\n## 6. Implementation plan (once accepted)\n\n1. Spec: §40.1 and §40.6 (stacked awaits; N0343's remaining cases), §40.8 (the interpreter), c-backend.md §3a (the\n   stacked await, the stack and the pool), diagnostics.md N0343, the guide's async page.\n2. `src/nir/coroutines.cee`: the components of the await graph; stacked edges instead of N0343, where the profile has a\n   stack.\n3. C backend: a stacked await site holds a pointer in the awaiter's frame, pushes the frame (`ceesafe_async_stack_push(\n   size, align)`), starts and resumes it through the pointer, and pops it (`ceesafe_async_stack_pop(frame)`) when it\n   returns; `--explain-frames`.\n4. Runtime: the task's chunk list, push and pop, the executor's pool, the panic; `tools/gen_runtime.py`.\n5. Tests:\n   - run-pass: middleware stacked three deep through `Box<dyn Handler>` (CeeSafe.Web's shape), a recursive tree walk\n     through `dyn`, direct recursion (a recursive `async fn` 10 000 deep, past one chunk), a cancellation deep in a\n     recursion, natively and in the interpreter;\n   - `CEESAFE_ALLOC_REPORT`: after a warm-up, a thousand requests through stacked middleware allocate no chunk (a\n     native tool beside the contract suite);\n   - the fault sweep on the recursive programs (the panic where the chunk fails), the cancellation sweep, ASan, TSan\n     with `Async.runWorkers`;\n   - frames: a `.frames` snapshot showing stacked awaits;\n   - check-fail: N0343 in the freestanding profile and for ADR 0037's case.\n6. ROADMAP §10.\n\n## 7. Implementation notes (2026-11-02)\n\n- **The role.** The coroutine pass gives each await on a cycle the NIR task role `taskStacked` (it finds them as it\n  found N0343's: an await whose callee reaches its caller, through `dyn` tables too), so every await of a component is\n  stacked. The C backend gives a stacked await a pointer member, pushes and pops the frame around it, and does not wait\n  for the callee's struct before the awaiter's (a pointer needs only its name). The dyn frame of a stacked call through\n  `dyn` is emitted after every impl's, as one only run or boxed already was.\n- **The size comes from the C struct,** not the table: the program is closed, so `sizeof(struct ceeAF_dyn_…)` is the\n  union of every impl's frame, as ADR 0036 lays it out.\n- **The task record** gains the top chunk (`cee_task.stack`), so it and `ceesafe_task_slot` are 160 bytes (were 144):\n  the backend's `taskSlotLayout`, and the interpreter's model of a task's head under `scopeIn` and `tasksIn`.\n- **The task record is aligned to 16** (`_Alignas(16)` on its first member): at 152 bytes, a spawned child's frame,\n  which follows its record rounded to `max_align_t`'s alignment, was 8-aligned where that alignment is 8 (macOS arm64,\n  MSVC), and UBSan reported a frame struct requiring 16 (run-pass/async-wake-waiters). A static assertion keeps it.\n- **The fault sweep** (`tools/fault_injection.py`) counts `ceesafe_async_stack_push` as an allocation and accepts its\n  panic, which §3.3 makes the outcome of a failed chunk.\n- **N0343 stays** for a cycle through a `race` or `all` child (no single call holds the frame) and for ADR 0037's\n  async instance, as §3.1 said, and in the freestanding profile.\n- **Effects:** the frame stack's allocation is not an `alloc` effect of the awaiting function, as a sync call's stack\n  is not: the coroutine pass runs after the effect checker, and §3.3 makes the failure a panic of the call stack's\n  kind.\n- **Tests:** run-pass/async-recursion-stacked (direct and mutual recursion, recursion 5 000 deep, a tree of\n  `Box<dyn Node>`, middleware three deep, a timeout cancelling a recursion 500 deep), natively and in the interpreter;\n  check-fail/async-recursion now freestanding (N0343 there), check-fail/async-dyn-frames keeps its N0295;\n  `tools/frame_stack_check.py` in the contract suite (a thousand requests through stacked middleware allocate no chunk\n  after the first; a recursion 20 000 deep leaves nothing live; a fai",
    287 => "led chunk panics, under ASan).\n\n",
    288 =>
        "# ADR 0057 — A faster `sortBy`: branchless partitioning, equal keys, sorted runs\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-02 (\"Yes to all ADRs. I accept.\"): every question of §5 as\n  recommended. Asked for by the owner (\"draft an ADR for a faster sortBy\") after the independent benchmark\n  (`benchmarks/independent/`, imported in `b9ec7ed`): its `sort` workload is CeeSafe's slowest, 4.3× Rust on an\n  Apple M1 at every C optimisation level (`results-macos-0.9.3.json`) and 4.2× on the author's Linux VM\n  (`results-linux-0.9.0.json`).\n- Scope: lib/core's `SliceAlgorithms<T>.sortBy` and its helpers (`lib/core/slices.cee`), type-system.md §36.4, the API\n  page, the guide. No language, compiler, runtime or interpreter change.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n`sortBy` (type-system.md §36.4; `lib/core/slices.cee:46`) is an introsort: a Lomuto partition around a median of three,\ninsertion sort by adjacent swaps below 17 elements, heapsort past a depth of 2·log₂ n. It is generic over any `T`,\ntakes a `borrow mut fn(borrow T, borrow T) -> i32`, allocates nothing, and uses only safe CeeSafe and `swap`.\n\n**The comparator is not the cost.** `--explain-closure-calls` on the benchmark shows every call of `cmp` in\n`partition`, `insertion` and `siftDown` made direct to the closure (ADR 0019), so the C compiler sees and inlines it.\n\n**The algorithm is.** The benchmark's `sort-same` workload, the same hand-written Hoare quicksort in both languages, is\n1.00× Rust; Rust's own `sort-same` is about four times slower than Rust's `sort_unstable_by`. Rust's library sort\n(ipnsort) wins by what it does, not by how it is compiled:\n\n- On random keys, a partition whose loop branches on the comparison mispredicts about half the time. ipnsort's\n  partition has no data-dependent branch: it moves every element and advances its boundary by the comparison's\n  outcome.\n- With many equal keys, it partitions the elements equal to the pivot off and does not sort them again. Our Lomuto\n  partition sends every equal key to one side, so all-equal input runs to the depth limit and finishes in heapsort.\n- A slice that is already one ascending or descending run is detected in one pass and finished (reversed if\n  descending).\n\n### 1.1 The prototype (run 2026-11-02, Apple M1, ceesafec 0.9.3 at its default `-O2`, best of 3)\n\nUser-level CeeSafe code with the same signature as the library, through the same comparator closure:\n`Branchless<T>.sortBy` = the iterative quicksort of today with (a) a branchless Lomuto partition, (b) pdqsort's\nequal-key partition, (c) a run check at the start. Rust is `sort_unstable_by` (rustc 1.99.1, `-C opt-level=3`). Each\ntime is the whole process: generating the keys, sorting them, and a checksum pass. All three sorts printed equal\nchecksums.\n\n| 10 M `u64` keys | Rust | today | prototype |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| random | 0.19 s | 0.75 s | **0.31 s** |\n| ascending | 0.04 s | 0.15 s | **0.04 s** |\n| descending | 0.04 s | 0.39 s | **0.04 s** |\n| all equal | 0.04 s | 0.25 s | **0.04 s** |\n| 16 distinct values | 0.07 s | 0.35 s | **0.08 s** |\n\n| Larger elements, random keys | today | prototype |\n|---|---|---|\n| 32-byte struct, 10 M | 1.57 s | **0.95 s** |\n| 128-byte struct, 4 M | 1.06 s | **0.73 s** |\n\nThe branchless partition alone (without (b) and (c)) took random keys from 0.75 s to 0.28 s, but went quadratic on\nall-equal and 16-value input (no result within 120 s): (b) is not optional. A Hoare partition that still branches\ngained only 4% (0.73 s), and an insertion sort that shifts instead of swapping (possible only for `T: Copy`) gained\nnothing measurable over it.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. Branchless partition, equal keys, runs (proposed) | B. A port of ipnsort / driftsort | C. Keep the introsort, tune it |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| What | §3: three changes to the introsort we have, all safe CeeSafe | block partition, sorting networks for small slices, a merge of runs, scratch memory | Hoare partition, a better insertion sort |\n| Random keys (prototype) | 2.4× fa",
    289 =>
        "ster; 1.6× Rust | close to 1× Rust | 1.03× faster |\n| Patterns | sorted, reversed, equal keys at Rust's speed | the same | unchanged |\n| Cost | about 60 lines changed in `slices.cee` | thousands of lines; scratch memory breaks \"allocates nothing\", or needs `unsafe` copies | small |\n| Guarantees of §36.4 | all kept (§3.4) | at risk | kept |\n\n**A is proposed.** It removes most of the gap with what the language already has: no `unsafe`, no allocation, no new\nAPI. B's remaining step from 1.6× to about 1× (small-sort networks, a cyclic permutation that halves the writes) can\ncome later, measured on top of A.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n### 3.1 The partition\n\n`partition(s, lo, hi, cmp)` keeps the median-of-three pivot at `s[hi - 1]`, and partitions without a branch on the\ncomparison:\n\n```ceesafe\nvar store = lo;\nfor (i in lo..last) {\n    let below: usize = if (cmp(borrow s[i], borrow s[last]) < 0) 1 else 0;\n    s.swap(i, store);\n    store += below;\n}\ns.swap(store, last);\n```\n\nThe swap is unconditional: an element not below the pivot is swapped with itself or with one not below it, so\n`[lo, store)` is below the pivot and `[store, i]` is not. The C compilers turn the comparison into a conditional set\n(`cset`, `setcc`); the implementation checks this on gcc and clang (§6).\n\n### 3.2 Equal keys\n\nEvery range the sort works on, except one starting at 0, starts just after an earlier pivot, and that pivot is at\nmost every element of the range. So, after choosing a pivot for `s[lo..hi]` with `lo > 0`: if `cmp(s[lo - 1],\npivot) >= 0`, the pivot equals that lower bound, and the elements not above it are all equal to it. They are\npartitioned to the left (the same branchless loop with `cmp(pivot, s[i]) >= 0`), and the sort continues with the\nelements above the pivot only (pdqsort's `partition_left`). Each such step finishes at least the pivot, so a range of\nk distinct values takes O(n·k) comparisons at most, and the depth limit still bounds the rest.\n\n### 3.3 Runs\n\nBefore partitioning, `sortBy` scans from the start while the slice is ascending (`cmp(s[i], s[i - 1]) >= 0`) or\nstrictly descending. A slice that is all one ascending run is done; all one strictly descending run is reversed\n(strictly, so no two of its elements are equal, and reversing it is its sort). The scan stops at\nthe first element out of order, so it costs at most n − 1 comparisons, wasted only when the slice is nearly sorted\nbut not quite.\n\n### 3.4 What §36.4 promises, kept\n\n- **Not stable; deterministic.** The order chosen is still a function of the input and of `cmp`'s results: no\n  randomness, the same in the interpreter and natively. It changes from 0.9.3's for equal keys (§4).\n- **O(n log n) comparisons in the worst case, O(log n) stack, no allocation.** The heapsort fallback past a depth of\n  2·log₂ n stays, and so does the explicit stack of at most 64 waiting ranges (ADR 0037 §3.7: an async instance's frame\n  has a size).\n- **An inconsistent `cmp` leaves a permutation, and nothing panics.** Every index the new code touches is inside\n  `[lo, hi)` whatever `cmp` returns: `store ≤ i < last`, and the equal-key step makes `lo` grow by at least one. A\n  `cmp` that lies may leave the slice unsorted but cannot make the loop run past its range or forever.\n- **`@effectsOf(cmp)`, the async instance, `@context(atomic)` and `irq`:** unchanged; the new helpers are\n  `@effectsOf(cmp)` as the old ones are.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- The benchmark's `sort` should go from 4.3× Rust to about 1.6× (§1.1); programs sorting sorted, reversed or\n  duplicate-heavy data gain most.\n- **Observable changes.** Equal elements may end in a different order than with 0.9.3, and `cmp` is called a\n  different number of times and in a different sequence. §36.4 never promised either, but a program that printed\n  them sees the difference. The corpus's `run-pass/closure-methods` counts comparisons only against a bound\n  (`comparisons < 2000 * 40`).\n- Heapsort is reached more rarely: all-equal ke",
    290 =>
        "ys no longer reach it (§3.2), so the corpus needs an input that\n  exhausts the depth limit on purpose (§6).\n- New diagnostics: none.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. The three changes of option A, keeping §36.4's guarantees, with B's further work left for a later ADR?\n   Recommended: yes.\n2. The run check of §3.3, which costs up to n − 1 comparisons on input that is nearly but not quite sorted?\n   Recommended: yes; Rust's sort does the same.\n3. Equal elements may end in a different order than 0.9.3's (unstable both ways): a note in the release, no\n   compatibility switch? Recommended: yes.\n\n## 6. Implementation plan (once accepted)\n\n1. Spec: type-system.md §36.4's `sortBy` paragraph (the algorithm named: introsort with a branchless partition,\n   pdqsort's equal-key partition, a run check), the API page through `tools/gen_docs.py`, guide/closures.md.\n2. `lib/core/slices.cee`: `partition` as §3.1, a `partitionEqual`, the run check in `sortBy`, the equal-key step in\n   `introsort`; `tools/gen_library.py`.\n3. Check the generated C: the partition loop has no conditional branch on the comparison under gcc and clang at `-O2`\n   (macOS, the Debian box's gcc 12, MSVC on the Windows VM).\n4. Tests:\n   - run-pass/closure-methods: its comment no longer says all-equal keys reach heapsort; add an input that reaches it\n     (a median-of-three killer sequence), and patterns of every kind (runs, reversed, all equal, few distinct values,\n     a run followed by one out-of-order element), each checked against Python's `sorted` as an oracle in\n     `tests/support` (the expected values from outside the code under test);\n   - an inconsistent comparator (random results from a seeded generator) on 10 000 elements: a permutation, no\n     panic, natively and in the interpreter;\n   - the async instance (run-pass/async-effects-of) unchanged;\n   - the fault and cancellation sweeps, ASan, the collection properties.\n5. Benchmarks: `benchmarks/independent/` `sort` before and after, recorded in its README; `benchmarks/run.py` unchanged.\n6. ROADMAP §10.\n\n## 7. Implementation notes (2026-11-02)\n\n- **The code** is §3 as written: `sortBy` checks the run at the start, `introsort` sets equal keys aside when\n  `low > 0` and the pivot is not above `s[low - 1]`, and the new helpers `pivot` and `partitionEqual` sit beside\n  `partition`, all `@effectsOf(cmp)` (`lib/core/slices.cee`).\n- **No branch on `cmp`'s result** in either partition loop, checked in the assembly of the benchmark's `sort`: clang\n  21 on arm64 (`cinc`), gcc 12 on x86-64 (`setb`, `setnb`, then `add`), MSVC on arm64 (`csetlo`, `cseths`, then `add`).\n  The loop's remaining branches are its index checks, never taken.\n- **Every call of `cmp` stays direct** (ADR 0019): `closure-calls/known.calls` gains the run check's calls in `sortBy`\n  and the new helpers' lines, and the library's line numbers moved. The user code's lines are unchanged. This is a\n  reviewed edit of the snapshot.\n- **Heapsort** is reached on purpose by McIlroy's adversary in run-pass/closure-methods. Its comparator fixes values\n  lazily, and the first two values are fixed in descending order so that the run check does not finish the sort\n  first. With the depth limit, n = 2000 takes 74 752 comparisons; the prototype without one took 1 001 938 (n²/4),\n  and 256 M for n = 32 000.\n- **The oracle test** is run-pass/sort-patterns, from `tools/oracles/gen_sort_cases.py`. It covers 10 patterns at 11\n  lengths, ascending and descending, (key, index) entries checked as a permutation, and an inconsistent comparator\n  followed by a proper sort, all against Python's `sorted`. Its lengths stop at 700 so that the interpreter runs it in\n  about 6 s; at 5000 it took 52 s.\n- **Measured** with the benchmark's `sort` (25 M random `u64`, Apple M1, `-O2`, best of 3): 1.96 s before, 0.77 s\n  after; Rust takes 0.46 s.\n",
    291 =>
        "# ADR 0058 — The generated C compiled at `-O3`\n\n- Status: **Withdrawn** by the owner, 2026-11-03, at the gate of §3.2 (\"Lets stop it there. I don't want to land\n  slow.\"): `-O2` stays the default. It had been accepted earlier the same day (\"I accept both ADRs with your\n  recommendations\"), subject to that gate, and §7 is what the gate found. `--cflag -O3` remains available to anyone\n  who wants it. From the independent benchmark's analysis (pasted by the owner): the optimisation\n  level of the generated C is the largest gap between CeeSafe's default build and Rust's release build.\n- Scope: `ceesafec build` (src/host/build.cee, src/host/toolchain.cee), tooling.md §8, the guide's performance page,\n  the fixed point's record. No language, runtime or library change.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n`ceesafec build` compiles its C at `-O2` with gcc and clang (`src/host/build.cee:158`, `src/host/toolchain.cee:145`)\nand at `/O2` with MSVC, which has nothing above it. `-g` builds use `-O0`. A user's `--cflag -O3` comes after ours, so\nit wins. Rust's release profile is `opt-level=3`, LLVM's `-O3`. The benchmark (`benchmarks/independent/`) built\nevery workload both ways:\n\n| Workload | M1, Apple clang 21: `-O2` → `-O3` | Linux VM, clang 18: `-O2` → `-O3` |\n|---|---|---|\n| matmul | 1158 → 333 ms (0.29) | 1512 → 688 ms (0.46) |\n| nbody | 1057 → 565 ms (0.53) | 1233 → 1198 ms (0.97) |\n| spectral-norm | 827 → 627 ms (0.76) | 3231 → 1322 ms (0.41) |\n| text-parse | 715 → 617 ms (0.86) | 1152 → 996 ms (0.86) |\n| hashmap-same | 682 → 595 ms (0.87) | 1071 → 1045 ms (0.98) |\n| **dyn-dispatch** | **417 → 726 ms (1.74)** | 1109 → 1082 ms (0.98) |\n| the other seven | 0.97–1.03 | 0.90–1.03 |\n| **geometric mean, × `rust-checked`** | **1.43 → 1.24** | **1.42 → 1.20** |\n\nWhat `-O3` adds is chiefly vectorisation (`matmul`, `nbody` on arm64) and more inlining: at `-O2`, clang 18 leaves\n`spectral-norm`'s small helper as a call in the hot loop.\n\n**One regression.** `dyn-dispatch` (200 M calls through `Box<dyn Shape>`) is 1.74× slower at `-O3` with Apple clang 21.\nThat was re-measured on 2026-11-03: 0.41 s against 0.74 s, three runs each. It does not happen with clang 18 on Linux.\nThe calls go through the table's thunks as at `-O2`, and the cause is not yet known.\n\n**Costs, measured 2026-11-03 on the M1:**\n\n- ceesafec builds itself in 44.4 s at `-O3` against 42.1 s at `-O2` (+5%), and its binary is 5793 KiB against\n  5497 KiB (+5%). The thirteen benchmark programs compile in 5.0 s against 4.9 s, with binaries of 53 KiB either way.\n- ceesafec at `-O3` is no faster at its own work: it writes its own C in 4.8 s at either level.\n- Floating point is unchanged: `-ffp-contract=off` stays, and neither level relaxes IEEE semantics. Every\n  checksum of the benchmark agreed across all five configurations.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. `-O3` by default (proposed) | B. Keep `-O2`, document `--cflag -O3` | C. A `--release` flag for `-O3` |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Default build | 1.24× / 1.20× Rust (geometric mean) | 1.43× / 1.42× | 1.43× unless asked |\n| Cost | +5% C compile time and binary size for large programs | none | a flag to learn; two kinds of build to test |\n| Risk | the `dyn-dispatch` regression on Apple clang (§3.2) | none | as A, when asked |\n\n**A is proposed**, with the regression gated (§3.2). `ceesafec build` is CeeSafe's release build: there is already\n`-g` for debugging, and a user who wants `-O2` passes `--cflag -O2`.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n### 3.1 The level\n\n- gcc and clang: `-O3` in place of `-O2` for `ceesafec build`, `--emit exe` and `--emit obj`, hosted and freestanding.\n  `-g` keeps `-O0`. `--cflag` still comes last, so `--cflag -O2` restores today's build.\n- MSVC: `/O2` stays, as its highest level.\n- The release workflow builds ceesafec itself at `-O3` too (`.github/workflows/release.yml` seed builds), so the\n  compiler is built the way it builds programs. The fixed point's record (`docs/p6-fixed-point.md`) states the flag",
    292 =>
        "s\n  and is re-recorded. Its C is unchanged, since the level does not change what ceesafec writes.\n\n### 3.2 The gate\n\nBefore the switch, the `dyn-dispatch` regression is explained, and either removed in the C ceesafec writes or bounded\non the toolchains CeeSafe releases for. The acceptance check is the benchmark at `-O2` and `-O3` on macOS arm64, Linux\nx86-64 (gcc and clang) and Windows (MSVC unchanged). No workload may be slower at `-O3` than its interquartile spread\nallows. If the cause is Apple clang's alone and cannot be steered from the C, this ADR comes back to the owner with the\nmeasurement before anything changes.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- The default build gains about 15% in the geometric mean, and much more on loop-heavy numeric code.\n- Builds of large programs take about 5% longer in the C compiler, and their binaries grow about 5%.\n- Performance numbers in `guide/performance.md` and `benchmarks/README.md` (`benchmarks/run.py` builds at `-O2`\n  through `--cc clang`) are re-measured and restated.\n- New diagnostics: none.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. `-O3` as `ceesafec build`'s default with gcc and clang, `/O2` with MSVC, `-O0` with `-g`? Recommended: yes.\n2. The gate of §3.2: the switch waits until the `dyn-dispatch` regression is explained and no workload regresses?\n   Recommended: yes.\n3. ceesafec itself built at `-O3` in the release workflow, with the fixed point re-recorded? Recommended: yes.\n\n## 6. Implementation plan (once accepted)\n\n1. Find the `dyn-dispatch` regression's cause (the loop's assembly at both levels with Apple clang 21 and clang 18),\n   and change the emitted C if that removes it.\n2. `src/host/build.cee`, `src/host/toolchain.cee`: `-O3`; tooling.md §8; the guide.\n3. The release workflow's seed builds; `docs/p6-fixed-point.md` re-recorded; `bootstrap/reference.md` if it names a\n   level.\n4. The benchmark at both levels on the three platforms (§3.2), recorded in `benchmarks/independent/README.md`; the\n   contract suite.\n5. ROADMAP §10.\n\n## 7. The gate's finding (2026-11-03)\n\n`dyn-dispatch`'s slowdown is not caused by `-O3`. It comes from where the code lands in memory, and `-O2` builds land\njust as badly.\n\n- **The same code at both levels.** The generated C compiled with Apple clang 21 at `-O2` and at `-O3` gives the same\n  hot loop, instruction for instruction (`ldp`, `ldr` of the table entry, `blr`, `fadd`), and the same thunks and\n  `area` methods. Only `main`'s cold paths differ, by 12 lines of assembly, which moves the code after them.\n- **The time follows the layout, not the level.** The same C was built several ways and timed, best of 3:\n\n  | Build | Hot loop's `ldr` (address mod 64) | Time |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | `ceesafec build` (`-O2`) | 12 | 0.44 s |\n  | `ceesafec build --cflag -O3` | 36 | 0.70 s |\n  | clang `-O2`, all files on one command line | 4 | 0.96 s |\n  | clang `-O3`, the same | 44 | 0.92 s |\n  | clang `-O2 -falign-loops=64` | 4 | 0.45 s |\n  | clang `-O3 -falign-loops=64` | 4 | 0.74 s |\n  | clang `-O2 -falign-functions=64` | 36 | 1.04 s |\n  | clang `-O3 -falign-functions=64` | 60 | 1.03 s |\n\n  The same `-O2` code takes anything from 0.44 s to 1.04 s. Two builds whose loop starts at the same offset (4) take\n  0.45 s and 0.96 s, so the loop's alignment alone does not decide it. The loop makes one indirect call per element to\n  one of three thunks, and the M1's indirect-branch prediction is sensitive to where the call and its targets sit.\n- **Not on Linux.** The benchmark author's clang 18 on x86-64 showed nothing similar: 1109 ms at `-O2` against 1082\n  ms at `-O3`.\n- **Not steerable from the C.** Nothing in the emitted C decides these addresses. An alignment flag moves the swing\n  around but does not remove it: `-falign-loops=64` helps one build and not the other.\n\nSo the regression in the table of §1 is one unlucky layout, not something `-O3` does, and `-O2` has unlucky layouts of\nits own. §3.2 asked for the cause to be explained and the regression removed or bounded. It is",
    293 =>
        " explained, but it\ncannot be removed from the C, and it is no worse than what `-O2` already does. As §3.2 requires, the owner decides\nwhether `-O3` becomes the default with this known, or waits. Nothing has changed in `src/host` yet.\n\n## 8. Outcome (2026-11-03)\n\nThe owner kept `-O2` after §7's finding: a default that can land 1.7× slower on a common workload is not taken for a\n15% gain elsewhere. Nothing in `src/host`, the release workflow or the fixed point changed. If the C compilers' layout\nsensitivity is ever bounded (for example by a layout that does not depend on unrelated code), this can be proposed\nagain with new measurements.\n",
    294 =>
        "# ADR 0059 — Allocation counters only when something reads them\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-03 (\"I accept both ADRs with your recommendations\"): every question of\n  §5 as recommended. From the independent benchmark's analysis (pasted by the owner): the runtime's allocation\n  counters are the whole of `binary-trees`' gap to Rust on x86-64.\n- Scope: the C runtime (`runtime/core/ceesafe_rt_core.c`, `tools/gen_runtime.py`), c-backend.md's runtime section,\n  testing.md (the hooks). No language, compiler or library change.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nEvery allocation through `ceesafe_alloc` and `ceesafe_alloc_zeroed` makes two atomic additions. One is\n`alloc_attempts` in `alloc_refused`; the other is `alloc_live` on success. A reallocation makes one, for the\nattempt, and every free makes one, subtracting from `alloc_live` (`runtime/core/ceesafe_rt_core.c:250–303`). They are atomic because a threaded program allocates from\nevery thread (type-system.md §38).\n\nThey have three readers, and every one is switched on when the program starts:\n\n- `CEESAFE_ALLOC_REPORT`, read by `ceesafe_platform_config` at the start of `main`, prints both counters at the end:\n  the corpus's `--check-leaks` and `tools/frame_stack_check.py` use it;\n- `CEESAFE_FAIL_ALLOC_AT` fails the n-th attempt: the fault sweep (`tools/fault_injection.py`) and the collection\n  properties use it;\n- `ceesafe_test_begin` (ADR 0049) turns on the report, the n-th failure and cancellation, for `ceesafec test`.\n\n`FailingAllocator`'s arming (`failing_armed`, `failing_left`) is separate, test code that is single-threaded by its\ncontract.\n\nSo a release build that sets none of them pays three atomic read-modify-writes per allocation and free, for\ncounts nothing reads. On x86-64 each is a `lock`-prefixed instruction.\n\n**Measured** with the benchmark's `binary-trees` (depth 18, millions of small boxes). The runtime was edited by hand\nfor this, in a copy of the generated C, with `CEE_COUNT` as a plain addition:\n\n| Machine | atomic counters | plain counters | Rust |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Ryzen 5 3600, Debian, gcc 12 `-O2`, pinned, the box loaded (≈7), median of 5 | 2.13 s | 1.72 s | — |\n| Apple M1, clang 21 `-O2`, best of 3 | 1.29 s | 1.28 s | 1.16 s |\n| Xeon VM (the benchmark's author) | 3.2 s | 1.9 s | 2.0 s |\n\nOn arm64 with LSE, uncontended atomics cost almost nothing. On x86-64 they cost a fifth to two fifths of an\nallocation-heavy program.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. Count only when switched on (proposed) | B. Per-thread counters, summed at exit | C. As today |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| What | one flag, set before the first allocation, decides whether to count at all | each thread counts in its own counters, which are added to the totals when it ends | atomic counters always |\n| Release cost | one load and a predictable branch per allocation and free | a thread-local access per allocation | three atomics |\n| Fault injection | unchanged: when counting is on, it counts atomically as today | the n-th attempt needs one order across threads, which per-thread counts do not give | unchanged |\n| Complexity | a few lines | thread exit hooks on every platform, and freestanding | none |\n\n**A is proposed.** It removes the cost where nothing reads the counters, and keeps today's exact behaviour where\nsomething does.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n- The runtime gets one flag, `alloc_counting`. It is true when `CEESAFE_ALLOC_REPORT` or `CEESAFE_FAIL_ALLOC_AT` is\n  set, or when `ceesafe_test_begin` runs. It is decided before the program's first allocation and never changes\n  afterwards. So a free never subtracts from a count its allocation did not add.\n- When the flag is set, everything is as today: atomic counters, the n-th attempt failing, the report at the end.\n- When it is clear, `ceesafe_alloc`, `ceesafe_alloc_zeroed`, `ceesafe_realloc` and `ceesafe_free` touch no counter. `FailingAllocator` still fails when armed,\n  because its check does not depend on the counters.\n- The interpreter writes its o",
    295 =>
        "wn report line (`src/interpreter/runtime.cee`) and is unchanged.\n- If an allocation could ever happen before the configuration is read, for example from a constructor of a linked C\n  library, it is simply not counted. The implementation checks that ceesafec's generated `main` reads the\n  configuration first.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- An allocation-heavy release program on x86-64 runs a fifth to two fifths faster: on the benchmark's `binary-trees`,\n  at or below Rust's time.\n- Every leak check and fault sweep behaves as before, because each one sets a flag that turns counting on.\n- New diagnostics: none.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. Counting only when a report, a fault-injection setting or `ceesafec test` asks for it (option A)? Recommended: yes.\n2. Leave per-thread counters (B) unless a threaded test shows the atomic counters' contention matters under a\n   report? Recommended: yes.\n\n## 6. Implementation plan (once accepted)\n\n1. `runtime/core/ceesafe_rt_core.c`: the flag, set in the configuration step and in `ceesafe_test_begin`; the counters\n   behind it; `tools/gen_runtime.py`. c-backend.md's runtime section and testing.md.\n2. Check that the generated `main` and the test driver read the configuration before their first allocation, hosted\n   and freestanding.\n3. Tests: the corpus with `--check-leaks`, the fault and cancellation sweeps, `frame_stack_check`,\n   `test_command_check`, all unchanged in outcome. A run-pass program with no environment variable set, whose C is\n   checked to make no atomic call in `ceesafe_alloc`'s path (`tools/` beside the contract suite).\n4. `binary-trees` on Debian (gcc, pinned) and the M1, recorded in `benchmarks/independent/README.md`.\n5. ROADMAP §10.\n\n## 7. Implementation notes (2026-11-03)\n\n- **The flag** is `alloc_counting` in `runtime/core/ceesafe_rt_core.c`. `ceesafe_rt_start` sets it from the two\n  settings, and `ceesafe_test_begin` sets it too. Each `CEE_COUNT` (the attempt in `alloc_refused`, `alloc_live` on a\n  successful allocation, the subtraction in `ceesafe_free`) is behind it.\n- **Before the first allocation:** the generated `main` calls `ceesafe_rt_start` first (`src/backend/c_interop.cee`,\n  `emitMainWrapper`). The generated test harness reads only argument views and compares strings before\n  `TestRun.begin` (`src/host/test_runner.cee`).\n- **The check** is `tools/alloc_counting_check.py`, a contract-suite step. It runs a program that allocates 1000 boxes\n  with no setting, with the report and with a failure at the 500th attempt. It also checks that every `CEE_COUNT` in\n  the emitted runtime is guarded. That source check stands in for §6's look at the assembly, which would depend on each\n  compiler's output. It fails against 0.9.3's runtime, at the three unguarded counters.\n- **Measured:** `binary-trees` at depth 18 on the Debian box (Ryzen 5 3600, gcc 12 `-O2`, pinned to one CPU, the box\n  loaded at about 7.5, 7 runs): median 1.87 s before, 1.59 s after.\n",
    296 =>
        "# ADR 0060 — Random bytes, `Channel.tryRecv`, C allocations, `IoError.HostNotFound`\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-03 (\"Lets go back to CeeSafe once again to implement the following\n  request by CeeSafe.Web\"), with the choices of §3 as recommended; §5 lists them for the owner to revisit. Asked for by\n  CeeSafe.Web (`~/dev/CeeSafe.Web/docs/ceesafe-upstream-work.md`, items U16, U13, U14 and U15), as one release.\n- Scope: lib/std, the hosted and core runtime, the built-in `IoError`, the interpreter, the build's C headers.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nFour small gaps, each found while CeeSafe.Web designed a milestone:\n\n- **U16, random bytes** (blocks the WebSocket client, Web ADR 0010 §3.6). lib/std gives a program no random bytes. The\n  hosted runtime reads the system's source (`cee_random_fill`, `runtime/hosted/ceesafe_rt_io.c`) only to seed\n  `HashMap`'s keys (`ceesafe_random_key`, ADR 0052). A WebSocket client needs 16 random bytes per connection\n  (`Sec-WebSocket-Key`, RFC 6455 §4.1) and a masking key per frame \"derived from a strong source of entropy\" (§5.3);\n  QUIC connection ids, session ids and CSRF tokens follow.\n- **U13, a receive that does not wait** (Web ADR 0008 §3.3–3.4). `Channel<T>` (ADR 0032) has `trySend`, but every\n  receive waits. `web_sse` wants to drop the oldest event of a full queue, and to write every event already queued\n  before one flush.\n- **U14, C allocations** (Web ADR 0002 §3.5, §6.1). OpenSSL can allocate through `CRYPTO_set_mem_functions`. Through\n  `ceesafe_alloc` its allocations would be counted and fault-injected, so the allocation-failure sweep would reach\n  OpenSSL's out-of-memory paths, but its global state (error strings, providers, the default context) lives until\n  the process exits, and every test would report it as a leak. A package's C file has no declared header for the\n  allocator either.\n- **U15, a name that does not resolve** (Web M5). `AsyncDns.resolve` maps `EAI_NONAME`, `EAI_NODATA` and\n  `EAI_ADDRFAMILY` to `-ENOENT` (ADR 0048 §3.1), so an unknown host is `IoError.NotFound`, whose message is \"No such\n  file or directory\".\n\n## 2. Options\n\n**U16: where the bytes come from on each call.**\n\n| | A. The system's source on every call (proposed) | B. A user-space ChaCha20 seeded from it |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cost per call | macOS and the BSDs: `arc4random_buf`, a user-space ChaCha20 already; Linux: one `getrandom` system call (the vDSO's `getrandom` where glibc has it); Windows: `BCryptGenRandom`, a user-mode per-processor generator | a few nanoseconds |\n| Risks | none of its own | a generator state to reseed after `fork`, to keep per thread or under a lock, and to get right: what the system already does |\n\nA per-frame call costs about as much as the frame's own socket write at worst (§7 measures it). B would duplicate the\nsystem's generator and its fork handling; it can come later behind the same API if a measurement asks for it.\n\n**U16: fallible or not.** `getrandom` can fail (`ENOSYS` before Linux 3.17, then `/dev/urandom` may be missing in a\ncontainer), and CosmoOS userland has no source at all. HashMap can keep a fixed key, since its order is its only\nuse of the bytes, but a masking key or a session token must never silently be predictable: **fallible**,\n`Result<void, IoError>`.\n\n**U13: how \"closed\" is told apart.** A `Result<T?, …>` makes every caller match three cases where the uses above\nneed two; `isClosed()` answers the rare question separately. **`tryRecv() -> T?` and `isClosed() -> bool`.**\n\n**U14: an allocator number or a separate API.** The request names an allocator number; the core already routes\nevery allocation by one (`CEE_ALLOC_SYSTEM`, `CEE_ALLOC_FAILING`). A number keeps one counter of attempts, so\n`CEESAFE_FAIL_ALLOC_AT` numbers CeeSafe's and C's allocations in one sequence, which is what the sweep needs.\n\n**U15: a new variant or a better message.** A message for `NotFound` that fits both a file and a host cannot be glibc's\nwording, and a program c",
    297 =>
        "ould still not tell the two apart. **A new variant**, after `Unsupported` so every earlier\nvariant keeps its tag (as ADR 0044 did).\n\n## 3. Decision\n\n**U16.**\n\n```ceesafe\n// lib/std, hosted\npublic struct Random {\n    /// Fills `into` with bytes from the system's cryptographic random source.\n    public fn fill(into: SliceMut<u8>) -> Result<void, IoError>;\n}\n```\n\n- The runtime's `int64_t ceesafe_random_fill(uint8_t *out, uint64_t length)`, 0 or a negated errno:\n  - Linux and other POSIX systems: `getrandom(out, n, 0)`, retried on `EINTR` and for short reads; on `ENOSYS`, or\n    where the C library has no `getrandom`, `/dev/urandom`. `getrandom` without flags waits only until the kernel's\n    pool is first initialised, early in boot.\n  - macOS and the BSDs: `arc4random_buf`, which cannot fail.\n  - Windows: `BCryptGenRandom(NULL, …, BCRYPT_USE_SYSTEM_PREFERRED_RNG)`, in pieces of at most `ULONG_MAX` bytes;\n    a failure is `EIO`.\n  - CosmoOS userland: `EOPNOTSUPP` (`IoError.Unsupported`).\n- No effects (`@effects()`): it does not allocate, and after boot it does not wait, so async code and `drop` blocks\n  may call it, once per WebSocket frame.\n- `HashMap`'s seed reads through the same function; where it fails, the seed keeps its fixed key, as today.\n- **The interpreter** answers a fixed sequence (SplitMix64 from a fixed seed, continuing from call to call), so a run\n  stays reproducible (§40.8). Its bytes are not secret; the guide says so.\n- Hosted only: in freestanding code `Random` is not defined (N0200, as every lib/std type).\n\n**U13.**\n\n```ceesafe\n// lib/std, Channel<T>\npublic fn tryRecv(borrow this) -> T?;   // the next buffered value, or null at once (empty, or closed and empty)\npublic fn isClosed(borrow this) -> bool; // whether close() has been called; values may still be buffered\n```\n\n- Both have no effects, like `trySend`. A drained channel is `isClosed()` then `tryRecv() == null`, in that order:\n  no value is sent after `close`, so the order rules out a value sent between the two calls.\n- The runtime's `bool ceesafe_chan_closed(void *channel)` reads the flag under the channel's lock.\n\n**U14.**\n\n- `#define CEE_ALLOC_FOREIGN 2u`: an allocator number for C code. Its attempts are counted and fault-injected like\n  every other (one sequence for `CEESAFE_FAIL_ALLOC_AT`); its live blocks are counted apart and are not a leak.\n- With it, `ceesafe_alloc`, `ceesafe_realloc` and `ceesafe_free` follow C's `malloc`, `realloc` and `free` where C\n  differs: `ceesafe_free(NULL, …)` does nothing, and `ceesafe_realloc(NULL, n, …)` allocates.\n- A new header, `ceesafe/foreign.h`, declares the three functions and the number. ceesafec writes it beside\n  `ceesafe/rt.h` in every build, whose include directory every C file of the build already has (`-I` on the work\n  directory, ADR 0047 §3.3), so a package's C files include it as `#include \"ceesafe/foreign.h\"`. `rt.h` includes\n  it, so the declarations exist once.\n- The report (`CEESAFE_ALLOC_REPORT`, `ceesafec test`) keeps its line, `ceesafe alloc: attempts=<n> live=<m>`, where\n  `attempts` now includes C's and `live` does not; when C allocated at all, a second line follows:\n  `ceesafe alloc: foreign held=<k>`, the blocks C still holds at exit, for information.\n\n**U15.**\n\n- `IoError.HostNotFound`, variant 19, after `Unsupported`; its message is \"Host not found\".\n- The resolver's \"no such name\" (`EAI_NONAME`, `EAI_NODATA`, `EAI_ADDRFAMILY`; Windows `WSAHOST_NOT_FOUND`,\n  `WSANO_DATA`) and an answer without addresses are `HostNotFound`, through a runtime-only error number\n  (`CEE_ERROR_HOST_NOT_FOUND`, 65536, which no system's errno uses) that `ceesafe_io_kind` maps to it.\n- The interpreter's resolver model answers `HostNotFound` for the reserved `.invalid` top-level domain (RFC 6761\n  §6.4), and `Unsupported` for other names besides `localhost`, as before.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- CeeSafe.Web's WebSocket client gets its keys and masks; `web_sse` gets `DropOldest` and batched flushes; OpenSSL'",
    298 =>
        "s\n  allocations join the sweep in test builds; a DNS failure says so.\n- **A source change:** a `match` over `IoError` that names every variant and has no `_` arm no longer covers it\n  (N0256), as with ADR 0044 in 0.7.0. ceesafec's own sources have none.\n- New diagnostics: none.\n\n## 5. Choices for the owner to revisit\n\n1. `Random.fill` reads the system's source on every call, with no user-space generator (§2, §7's measurements).\n2. `Random.fill` is fallible, `Result<void, IoError>`; CosmoOS userland is `Unsupported`.\n3. The interpreter's random bytes are a fixed, reproducible sequence.\n4. `Channel.tryRecv() -> T?` with a separate `isClosed()`.\n5. `CEE_ALLOC_FOREIGN`'s live blocks are reported on their own line and never fail a test.\n6. `IoError.HostNotFound`, message \"Host not found\", also for a name with no addresses.\n\n## 6. Implementation plan\n\n1. Runtime: `ceesafe_random_fill`, `ceesafe_chan_closed`, `CEE_ALLOC_FOREIGN`, `CEE_IO_HOST_NOT_FOUND` and the\n   resolver's mapping; `ceesafe/foreign.h`; `tools/gen_runtime.py`.\n2. The built-in `IoError` (`src/check/structs.cee`) and its message (core runtime, interpreter).\n3. lib/std: `Random`, `Channel.tryRecv` and `isClosed`, `HostServices.hostError`; `tools/gen_library.py`.\n4. The interpreter's models: `ceesafe_random_fill`, `ceesafe_chan_closed`, the `.invalid` names, error 65536.\n5. Tests: run-pass for each (natively and in the interpreter), a check-fail for the freestanding name, and a\n   contract-suite tool for the native random bytes and for a C file allocating through `CEE_ALLOC_FOREIGN` under\n   the sweep.\n6. Spec (type-system.md §26.2, §26.12, §39 channels, §28.6; tooling.md §4.1), the guide, ROADMAP §10.\n\n## 7. Implementation notes (2026-11-03)\n\n- **The cost of `Random.fill`**, a 4-byte fill as the mean of 100 000 calls (`tools/random_check.py`): 0.04–0.05 µs on\n  macOS (`arc4random_buf`), 0.57 µs on Debian 12 (Linux 6.1, glibc 2.36: a `getrandom` system call each time), and\n  below the 15.6 ms tick of Windows' millisecond clock over the 100 000 calls, so under 0.16 µs, on Windows 11 ARM64.\n  No user-space generator is needed (§2).\n- **`rt.h` includes `\"foreign.h\"`**, its neighbour, not `\"ceesafe/foreign.h\"`: the first form compiled only where the\n  build's directory was on the include path, and C written by `--emit c` and compiled by hand has no such flag (found\n  on Debian).\n- **Package C files already had the runtime's headers**: `-I` on the build directory reaches every C file, those\n  compiled apart with `include-dirs` too (`src/host/toolchain.cee`, `pushCommonFlags`). The new header gives C code a\n  small contract instead of all of `rt.h`.\n- **`ceesafe_free(NULL, …)` does nothing** for every allocator, and `ceesafe_realloc(NULL, n, …)` allocates for every\n  one; generated code never does either (its frees are guarded by a capacity or a null check), so only C sees the\n  difference.\n- **The interpreter's sources cannot name `HostNotFound`**, since stage 0 (ceesafec 0.3.2, Decision 0009) does not\n  have it: `src/interpreter/io.cee` recognises the variant by its message, as it does ADR 0044's.\n- **check-pass snapshots**: `async-timeout.hir` and `effects-of.hir` differ in closure ids only (lib/std's new items\n  move them), checked normalised and updated.\n",
    299 =>
        "# ADR 0061 — C allocations that are counted but never failed, for a library's one-time initialisation\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-03 (\"You can take over CeeSafe session and tackle U17 then release as per\n  the worfklow v* tag\"), with §3 as below. Asked for by CeeSafe.Web\n  (`~/dev/CeeSafe.Web/docs/ceesafe-upstream-work.md`, item U17).\n- Scope: the core runtime's allocator, `ceesafe/foreign.h`, the allocation report.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nADR 0060 gave C code `CEE_ALLOC_FOREIGN`: its allocations are counted and fault-injected in one sequence with\nCeeSafe's, and its blocks held at exit are reported apart, never as a leak. CeeSafe.Web routed OpenSSL's allocations\nthrough it (`CRYPTO_set_mem_functions`), and its allocation sweep then failed at the first points of every TLS test.\n\nThose points are OpenSSL's one-time initialisation, which cannot survive a failed allocation. In OpenSSL 3.6.4,\n`CRYPTO_malloc`'s failure path raises an error, and raising an error calls `OPENSSL_init_crypto` again inside the\n`pthread_once` that is running it:\n\n```text\n_os_once_gate_recursive_abort ← pthread_once ← OPENSSL_init_crypto ← ossl_err_get_state_int ← ERR_new\n← CRYPTO_malloc ← CRYPTO_THREAD_lock_new ← ossl_init_base ← pthread_once ← OPENSSL_init_crypto ← SSL_CTX_new_ex\n```\n\nmacOS aborts the recursion; on Linux a recursive `pthread_once` deadlocks. Every later allocation fails cleanly.\nOther C libraries have the same shape: global setup run once, with no way back from a failure in it. A sweep that\nfails those allocations measures the library's initialisation, which the program cannot handle, instead of the\nprogram's own out-of-memory paths.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. A second allocator number (proposed) | B. Pause fault injection (`ceesafe_faults_hold()` / `release()`) |\n|---|---|---|\n| What C writes | its allocation functions choose the number while its initialisation runs | two calls around the initialisation |\n| Threads | per call: nothing global changes | a process-wide switch: another thread's allocations would also escape the sweep while it is on |\n| Sweep numbering | the initialisation's allocations are not attempts, so the sequence is the program's own | the same, if paused allocations are not counted |\n\nA keeps the allocator's contract per call, as `CEE_ALLOC_FOREIGN` is, with no state to get wrong.\n\n## 3. Decision\n\n- `#define CEE_ALLOC_FOREIGN_INIT 3u` in `ceesafe/foreign.h`: an allocator number for a C library's one-time\n  initialisation.\n  - Its allocations are never failed, by `CEESAFE_FAIL_ALLOC_AT` or by `FailingAllocator`, and are not counted as\n    attempts. So a sweep's numbering, and `attempts` in the report, are the program's own.\n  - Its live blocks count with `CEE_ALLOC_FOREIGN`'s, in `foreign held=<k>`, never as a leak. A block may be\n    reallocated or freed under either foreign number, since a library cannot tell which made it.\n  - `ceesafe_alloc`, `ceesafe_realloc` and `ceesafe_free` follow `malloc`, `realloc` and `free` with it, as with\n    `CEE_ALLOC_FOREIGN`.\n- It is for initialisation only. A program that sends every allocation through it has opted its C library out of the\n  sweep; the guide says so.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- CeeSafe.Web's shim runs `OPENSSL_init_ssl` once under `CEE_ALLOC_FOREIGN_INIT` (its allocation functions check a\n  flag set only during that call), then routes every other allocation through `CEE_ALLOC_FOREIGN`.\n- No source change. No new diagnostics.\n\n## 5. Implementation notes (2026-11-03)\n\n- `alloc_refused` returns false at once for `CEE_ALLOC_FOREIGN_INIT`, before counting the attempt;\n  `alloc_count_live` counts its blocks in `alloc_foreign`.\n- `tools/foreign_alloc_check.py` covers the number:\n  - the package's C file makes and keeps one block under `CEE_ALLOC_FOREIGN_INIT`, reallocates it under\n    `CEE_ALLOC_FOREIGN`, and keeps it (`foreign held=2`);\n  - over every `CEESAFE_FAIL_ALLOC_AT` point, the initialisation never fails;\n  - `attempts` is the same with an",
    300 => "d without it.\n",
    301 =>
        "# ADR 0062 — A cancellation visits only the cancelled scope's tasks\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-03 (\"I accept ADR 0062 with your recommendations\"): every question of §5\n  as recommended. Asked for by the owner (\"Merge and release 0.10.2, then draft the B17 ADR\") after CeeSafe.Web's item\n  B17 (`~/dev/CeeSafe.Web/docs/ceesafe-upstream-work.md`).\n- Scope: the hosted runtime's cancellation (`cee_cancel_scope`, `cee_wake_waiters`), the task and scope records in\n  `ceesafe/rt.h`, the backend's layouts for them (`taskSlotLayout`, `scopeLayout` in `src/backend/c_async.cee`), and\n  the interpreter's model of a task's head. No language or library change.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nA cancellation must reach every task it cancels, wherever that task waits (type-system.md §40.7). Today that is\n`cee_wake_waiters` (`runtime/hosted/ceesafe_rt_async.c`), which walks **every task parked on the executor**. For each\ntask it climbs the task's scopes in `cee_cancelled` and unparks the ones that are cancelled. B5 (CeeSafe.Web, fixed in\n0.8.0) made the walk wake only those tasks. The walk itself still visits all of them.\n\nIt runs:\n\n- on each scope cancellation: `cee_cancel_scope`, called when a race is won, when a scope's child fails, or when a\n  spawn cannot be allocated;\n- on the cancellation sweep's cancellation of the root;\n- on another worker's cancellation of a shared scope (`cancel_epoch`, ADR 0038).\n\nThe first is the common case. Every `Async.race` that a child wins cancels the others, so one race costs O(parked\ntasks on the executor), whatever the race's own size. A server keeps a task parked per connection, per request and per\nwait, and races to bound waits (a read against a deadline, a handler against a reset). So the cost grows with the\nsquare of its concurrency.\n\nCeeSafe.Web measured it with HTTP/2. There, a task per stream and a race per connection wake and per stream (handler\nagainst reset) make it plain. `examples/server` with `h2load -n 20000`, built by ceesafec with B16's fix (the code\nreleased as 0.10.2), macOS arm64:\n\n| connections × streams | req/s | mean request |\n|---|---|---|\n| 10 × 100 | 21 944 | 45 ms |\n| 50 × 10 | 22 308 | 22 ms |\n| 50 × 30 | 15 860 | 93 ms |\n| 20 × 100 | 13 871 | 144 ms |\n| 50 × 50 | 11 782 | 212 ms |\n| 50 × 100 (`-n 100000`) | 3 562 | 1.40 s |\n\n`sample` of the server at 50 × 100 puts 895 of about 2 400 samples in `cee_cancel_scope` and 1 393 in\n`cee_exec_loop`, into which the walk's helpers are inlined. HTTP/1.1 on the same server, with one task per connection,\ndoes not show it at 100 connections (42 076 req/s).\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. Each scope lists its children (proposed) | B. A parked list per scope | C. Keep the walk; make it cheaper |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| What a cancellation visits | the cancelled scope's live tasks, and the scopes they own, recursively | the parked tasks of the cancelled scope and its descendants | every parked task |\n| Cost per race | O(the race's children and what they own) | the same | O(parked tasks), with a smaller constant |\n| Record size | two links and a list head per task, a list head and a link per scope | the same, and a second membership to keep when a task parks and unparks | none |\n| Other workers' tasks | a shared scope's children may run on other workers: those keep today's epoch walk (§3.3) | the same | the same |\n\n**A is proposed.** It visits exactly what the cancellation reaches, its links change only when a task starts or\nreturns and when a scope starts or ends, never on each park. B would also keep a membership on every park and unpark.\nThat is the hot path, so it costs more and gains nothing. C leaves the quadratic cost in place.\n\n## 3. Decision\n\n### 3.1 The records\n\n- A task (`cee_task`) gains `sibling_next` and `sibling_prev`, linking it into its scope's list of live children, and\n  `owned`: the innermost scope it owns that has not ended. A task owns several scopes at once when, for example, a\n  task set's body awaits a race. Those scopes",
    302 =>
        " nest, last in first out, as frames do (ADR 0056 §1).\n- A scope (`ceesafe_scope`) gains `children`, the head of its live children's list, and `outer`: the scope its owner\n  owned before this one, so that `owned` is a stack.\n- A child joins its scope's list when it is spawned, started (`ceesafe_async_start`), or embedded (race, all). It\n  leaves when it returns, before its slot or block is released. A scope is pushed on its owner's `owned` when its first\n  child is added, and popped when its last child has returned. Both happen where `live` already changes.\n\n### 3.2 A cancellation\n\n`cee_cancel_scope(scope)` sets `scope->cancelled` (as now), then visits:\n\n- **each child in `scope->children`:**\n  - If it is parked, waiting on a deadline, a descriptor, or a cancellable wake (the condition `cee_wake_waiters` uses\n    today), it is unparked to see the cancellation.\n  - A task parked `on_scope` stays parked: its own children are visited, and the last to return readies it, as now.\n- **each scope the child owns** (its `owned` stack), with that scope's children visited the same way, depth first.\n\nA task already ready or running is passed over: it sees the cancellation at its next check (§40.7), as now. The visit\nuses an explicit stack in the executor, sized by the deepest nesting met, so a deep tree does not recurse in C.\n\n### 3.3 What stays as it is\n\n- **Shared scopes** (`spawnSend` children, ADR 0038). Their children may be parked on other workers, whose lists this\n  thread cannot walk. A visit that reaches a shared scope does today's: it increments the run's `cancel_epoch` and\n  pokes the workers, which walk their parked tasks (`cee_wait`). Only programs that use `spawnSend` with several\n  workers pay for it, and only when a shared scope is cancelled.\n- **The cancellation sweep** (`CEESAFE_CANCEL_AT`) cancels the root, which reaches every task. It keeps the full walk.\n  It is a test, and its cost is the walk's.\n- `cee_cancelled`, shields, and what a cancelled wait returns do not change.\n\n### 3.4 Layout\n\n- `cee_task` grows by 24 bytes. `ceesafe_task_slot` goes from 160 to 192 bytes (kept a multiple of 16). The\n  backend's `taskSlotLayout` and the interpreter's model of a task's head follow, as in ADR 0056 §5.\n- `ceesafe_scope` grows by 16 bytes: `scopeLayout` goes from 32 to 48. Generated code zero-initialises scopes, and a\n  zero list is an empty one.\n- A frame with a race or a scope grows accordingly. The C size checks (`_Static_assert`) catch any disagreement.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- A race's cost no longer depends on how many tasks wait elsewhere on the executor. For CeeSafe.Web's HTTP/2 server,\n  the 50 × 100 run should match the 10 × 100 run per stream (to be measured, §6).\n- Each spawn and return does two more pointer writes. Each scope's first child and its end do one more.\n- B5's check (`tools/wake_check.py`: 200 races beside 100 idle sockets, tasks woken) still holds. It gains a count of\n  tasks *visited*, which must not grow with the idle tasks.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. Option A, each scope listing its children, with the visit of §3.2? Recommended: yes.\n2. Shared scopes and the sweep keep the full walk (§3.3), for now? Recommended: yes. A per-worker list for shared\n   scopes can come when a program needs it.\n3. `ceesafe_task_slot` at 192 bytes and `ceesafe_scope` at 48 (§3.4)? Recommended: yes.\n4. A patch release (0.10.3) when it lands, since only performance changes? Recommended: yes.\n\n## 6. Plan and tests (once accepted)\n\n1. Runtime: the links and their upkeep (spawn, start, embed, return, a scope's first child and end), the visit, and\n   the fallbacks of §3.3; `tools/gen_runtime.py`.\n2. Backend and interpreter: the two layouts.\n3. Tests:\n   - `tools/wake_check.py` counts tasks visited per cancellation. With 1 000 idle tasks parked beside 1 000 races,\n     each race must visit only its own children. Today each visits about 1 000 (to be confirmed by the check before\n     the change).\n   - The corpus's ca",
    303 =>
        "ncellation programs, the cancel and fault sweeps, ASan and TSan, `run interp`, and\n     `reactor poll`: unchanged results.\n   - Nested cancellation: a race inside a task set inside a race, cancelled from the outside. Every level's waiting\n     tasks must see it, checked by their results in the corpus.\n   - A shared scope with several workers is cancelled while its children are parked on other workers\n     (ADR 0038's tests, extended).\n   - CeeSafe.Web's `h2load` table of §1 again, before and after.\n\n## 7. Implementation notes (2026-11-03)\n\n- **The records:**\n  - `cee_task` gains `listed`, `sibling_next`, `sibling_prev` and `owned`, and is 192 bytes.\n  - `ceesafe_scope` gains `listed`, `children` and `outer`, and is 48 bytes; `TaskSetRecord`'s 96 bytes still hold a\n    set (88).\n  - `taskSlotLayout`, `scopeLayout` and the interpreter's `CEE_TASK_HEAD` (`src/interpreter/io.cee`) follow.\n- **The upkeep:**\n  - `cee_spawn` and `ceesafe_async_start` list the child, unless it is a published `spawnSend` child, and list the\n    scope on its owner.\n  - `cee_child_done` unlists the child before its block is released.\n  - `ceesafe_async_scope_done` unlists the scope when it returns true. That is every scope's end, a task set's\n    included: a set's live count may reach zero and rise again while a race nested in its body is still listed.\n  - A scope that another task spawns into moves to that task's list, as `scope->owner` does.\n- **The visit** (`cee_cancel_visit`) walks down, across and up through the records' own links (owner, scope, sibling,\n  `outer`), so it keeps no stack. A shared scope met on the way ends it with `cee_wake_waiters`.\n- **`CEESAFE_CANCEL_REPORT`** adds `visited=V`, the tasks cancellations looked at. The prefix parsers\n  (`cancel_sweep.py`, `ceesafec test`) are unchanged.\n- **The wake list, found on the way (§1's server):**\n  - With the visit in place, a profile of the HTTP/2 server built without inlining put about 40 % of the time in\n    `cee_wait`: it found woken tasks one at a time, walking the whole wake list for each. That is quadratic, since\n    every HTTP/2 connection and stream waits on a channel.\n  - `cee_ready_woken` now takes them in one pass into the executor's `woken` buffer and heap-sorts them by wake\n    sequence, so they still run earliest wake first. If the buffer cannot grow, the old walk remains.\n  - No layout or behaviour changes, so it ships with this ADR.\n- **Measured (2026-11-03, macOS arm64):**\n  - `tools/wake_check.py`: 200 cancellations looked at 400 tasks, on the reactor and on poll. The check now requires\n    at most 400.\n  - A benchmark of 20 000 races whose losers park, beside idle tasks parked on a channel:\n\n    | idle tasks | 0.10.2 | this ADR |\n    |---|---|---|\n    | 0 | 2 ms | 3 ms |\n    | 1 000 | 144 ms | 7 ms |\n    | 2 000 | 287 ms | 16 ms |\n    | 4 000 | 594 ms | 56 ms |\n\n  - CeeSafe.Web's `examples/server`, `h2load -n 20000` (`-n 100000` for 50 × 100), req/s:\n\n    | connections × streams | 0.10.2 | the visit | the visit and the wake list |\n    |---|---|---|---|\n    | 10 × 100 | 21 944 | 41 061 | 75 812 |\n    | 50 × 10 | 22 308 | 43 242 | 73 456 |\n    | 20 × 100 | 13 871 | 29 708 | 73 829 |\n    | 50 × 50 | 11 782 | 23 833 | 68 880 |\n    | 50 × 100 | 3 562 (mean 1.40 s) | 7 880 | 70 956 (mean 70 ms) |\n    | 100 × 1 | 10 698 | 30 380 | 37 297 |\n\n- **Tests:**\n  - corpus `run-pass/async-cancel-nested.cee`: an outer race's loser owns a task set. The set's 8 children and its\n    body each wait in a race against a 100 s sleep. All 9 see the cancellation, and it ends in 0.24 s natively and in\n    the interpreter. It also passes on 0.10.2, whose full walk was correct, only slow.\n",
    304 =>
        "# ADR 0063 — Index ranges, integer `min`/`max`, and reborrowing into `dyn`\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-03 (\"I accept the ADR\", then \"Both 0063 and 0064\"): §5 questions 1 and 3\n  as recommended (the narrow index rule; no `min`/`max` on `char` or `bool`). Question 2: \"Both\", so §2.5 adds a\n  panicking `abs` and a never-panicking `unsignedAbs`. Implemented on the working tree; not released. Raised by the ClayWind\n  port (`~/dev/CeeSafeApps/CEESAFE-FINDINGS.md`, items A1, A4 and B), a ~25k-line pure CeeSafe program: a Clay layout\n  engine, a software rasterizer matched to ThorVG, a TrueType reader, controls and three hosts.\n- Scope: the checker (`src/check/body.cee`, `src/check/expr.cee`, `src/check/io.cee`, `src/check/members.cee`, the new\n  `src/check/range_index.cee`), the built-in list (`src/hir/builtins.cee`), the interpreter\n  (`src/interpreter/runtime.cee`), the C backend (`src/backend/c_body.cee`) and `rt.h`. Diagnostics: N0246's message\n  and fix, N0295's explanation. Spec: type-system.md §6, §16.4, §37.2.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nThree frictions came up over and over in a real program, each with a cheap, local fix.\n\n1. **`for (i in 0..16) { a[i] = … }` does not compile.** Both bounds are flexible literals, so the range is `i32`,\n   and an index is `usize` (N0205). The port wrote `0usize..16` in about forty places. The guide's \"common mistakes\"\n   lists it. A range of literals that indexes is the commonest loop there is.\n2. **`i32` and `u32` have no `min` or `max`.** Floats do (§35.5). Integer code wrote `if (a < b) a else b`, or small\n   `imin`/`imax` helpers per package.\n3. **A `borrow mut T` passed where a `borrow mut dyn I` is expected is moved, not reborrowed.** §13.3 says every use of\n   a `borrow mut` reborrows, and it does at the same type. The coercion's operand was the variable itself, so the\n   move checker saw a move (N0250 on the second use). The port wrapped a platform in a struct to lend a field each\n   frame. A `borrow mut` *field* could not be passed this way at all (N0251).\n\n## 2. Decision\n\n### 2.1 Index ranges (§16.4)\n\nWhen both bounds of a `for` range are flexible literals, the range is `usize` if the body **indexes with** the\nvariable, and `i32` (the literals' default) otherwise. The body indexes with `i` where `i` is an index (`a[i]`) or a\nslice bound (`a[lo..i]`), or an operand of `+ - * / %` that is one (`a[i * w + x]`), anywhere in the body: nested\nblocks and closures included. Inside a call, cast, comparison or condition within an index it does not count.\n\n- **Syntactic, before the body is checked** (`indexesWith`, `src/check/range_index.cee`). Locals cannot be shadowed\n  (N0203), so the name means the variable throughout, and the decision needs no type information. The first use does\n  not decide it, so the order of statements never changes a type.\n- **Narrow on purpose.** `a[truncate<usize>(i)]` and `a[f(i)]` keep `i32`: there the program already converts, and\n  changing the variable's type would break the conversion or the call. Only the pattern the program cannot write\n  today becomes valid.\n- **Compatibility.** A program that compiles today does not index with an `i32` loop variable, since that is N0205.\n  It could still use `i` both inside an index's arithmetic and as an `i32` elsewhere, but only if the index were also\n  converted, which the arithmetic rule excludes. The compiler itself (about 170k lines), `lib/`, the corpus and the\n  ClayWind and CosmoStack packages check unchanged. The one false positive found while building (a variable inside\n  an `if` condition within an index, `nir_builder.cee:2340`) is what fixed the rule to arithmetic operands only.\n\n### 2.2 Integer `min` and `max` (§6)\n\nEvery integer type gets `x.min(y)` and `x.max(y)`, of result `T`. They take one argument of the receiver's type (a\nflexible literal adapts) and never overflow; with equal operands the result is `x`. They are built-ins like\n`wrappingAdd` (`bfIntMin`, `bfIntMax`): `cee_min_<S>`/`c",
    305 =>
        "ee_max_<S>` in `rt.h`, and an `intCompare` in the\ninterpreter. Their names join the reserved integer method names (N0228).\n\n`abs` is §2.5.\n\n### 2.5 `abs` and `unsignedAbs` (accepted §5 question 2: \"Both\")\n\nEvery signed integer type gets:\n- `x.abs()`, of type `T`. It panics at `T`'s minimum, whose magnitude `T` cannot hold, exactly as `-x` does (§6), and\n  the panic is reported at the call.\n- `x.unsignedAbs()`, of the unsigned type of the same width. It never panics: the minimum's magnitude fits.\n\nOn unsigned types both are N0234, since a non-negative value has no sign to drop. Both names join the reserved integer\nmethod names (N0228).\n\n### 2.3 Reborrowing into `dyn` (§37.2)\n\nA `borrow mut T` variable or field that converts to `borrow mut dyn I` is first reborrowed, as at the same type\n(`coerce`, `src/check/expr.cee`): the coercion's operand is the `Reborrow`, so the variable stays usable after the\ncall and is frozen while the reborrow lives (B4). This is a fix, not a new rule: §13.3 already says it. It also lets a\n`borrow mut` field be passed through `dyn`, which was N0251. The aliasing checks are unchanged:\n`check-fail/dyn-reborrow.cee` shows a write and a read of the original while a `dyn` reborrow is live, still N0240 and\nN0242.\n\n### 2.4 Diagnostics\n\n- **N0246** (`var k = len;` with `len: borrow mut T` and `T` Copy) adds: to copy the value, declare the variable's\n  type. It carries a fix that inserts `: T` (`ceesafec fix` applies it), after which the declaration reads through\n  the reference.\n- **N0295 and the other effect explanations**: a chain that reaches an interface method without `@effects` now says\n  so, and says that the list belongs on the interface (`@effects() fn …`), as it already did for a C function.\n  Before, the explanation stopped at \"which calls 'dyn I.m'\".\n\n## 3. Evidence (run 2026-11-03, macOS arm64)\n\n- **New corpus tests:**\n  - `run-pass/dyn-reborrow.cee`, `check-fail/dyn-reborrow.cee`, `run-pass/int-min-max.cee`,\n    `run-pass/for-range-index.cee` and `check-fail/for-range-index.cee`. The run-pass expectations come from Python\n    (`max`, `min`, `sum`).\n  - With the released ceesafec 0.10.3 the dyn-reborrow pair fails, as the bug predicts. With this change all five\n    pass, compiled and with `--interp`.\n- **Full corpus:** `tools/run_corpus.py --corpus tests/reference/tests/corpus` passes 580 tests with 0 failures. The 4\n  freestanding cases are partial, as before.\n- **Fixed point:** the changed compiler builds itself, and that build emits byte-identical C (`--emit c`,\n  `diff -rq`).\n- **Contract suite:** `tools/contract_tests.py` (result recorded in ROADMAP §10).\n- **Downstream:** ClayWind's 72 tests pass, and CosmoStack checks.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- `0usize..N` stays valid, so nothing has to change. The guide's \"common mistakes\" entry can drop its index advice.\n- The interpreter and the C backend each gain two small built-ins. `rt.h` gains `CEE_MINMAX`.\n- The range rule makes a loop variable's type depend on its body. The rule is syntactic and narrow, and the error\n  for a mixed use (`let s: i32 = i` beside `a[i]`) is the ordinary N0205 at that use.\n\n## 5. Open questions for the owner\n\n1. Accept §2.1's rule as specified, or the wider one (any mention inside an index)? The wider rule reports the\n   compiler's own `nir_builder.cee:2340` loops, which use `k` in an `if` inside an index and as an `i64`.\n2. `abs`: a panicking built-in (the operators' panic, at the call), or `unsignedAbs() -> uN`, which cannot overflow?\n3. Should `min`/`max` also be on `char`/`bool`? Proposed: no.\n",
    306 =>
        "# ADR 0064 — Generic methods, arrays and slices of views, `dyn` as a bound and scoped `dyn` (from the ClayWind port)\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-03 (\"I accept the ADR\", then \"Both 0063 and 0064\"), with every question\n  as proposed: §1 questions 1 and 2 (explicit or inferred type arguments; inherent `impl` in the struct's own\n  package), §2 questions 1 and 2 (the collections' rule; `const` tables of static views), §3 question 1 (an inferred\n  \"behind references only\" condition). For §3 question 2 the order is part 1, then OD-10a. Implementation follows in\n  the sections' order. The smaller items from the same port are ADR 0063.\n- Implemented on the working tree (2026-11-04), not released; §5 records how each part was built and checked.\n- Source: `~/dev/CeeSafeApps/CEESAFE-FINDINGS.md` (A1–A3), from a ~25k-line pure CeeSafe port of ClayWind (layout,\n  rasterizer, fonts, controls, PNG, hosts). Each section says what the port had to do instead, so the cost of\n  leaving things as they are is concrete.\n\n## 1. Methods with their own type parameters, and inherent `impl` blocks (finding A2)\n\n**Today.**\n- A method cannot declare type parameters (N0216, \"use the struct's, or a top-level generic function\"; §21.1).\n- Methods live only in the struct body.\n\n**Cost in the port.** `Controls.button(...)` is a method, but `textInputEx(ui, metrics, ...)` is a free function,\nonly because it is generic over the text metrics. The same goes for `endLayout<M>`, `endFrame<M>`, `renderInto<B>` and\n`renderTree<B>`, which belong on `LayoutTree` and `Ui`. Users must learn which half of the API is which.\n\n**Proposal.**\n- **Generic methods.** `fn m<T: B>(borrow this, x: T)` is the top-level generic function\n  `m<S-params…, T: B>(this: borrow S, x: T)`, instantiated the same way (P3 generics). The receiver adds no new rule.\n  Type arguments come from the arguments, as for free functions, or explicitly: `x.m<u32>(…)`.\n- **Inherent `impl S { … }` blocks.** Sugar for methods in the body, so a type's methods can be grouped or split\n  across files of the package. They have the same visibility, the same \"a method cannot have a field's name\" rule,\n  and no second definition of a name.\n- **Not proposed: generic methods in interfaces.** A table cannot hold an unbounded family of instances, so interface\n  methods stay monomorphic (§37).\n\n**Questions.**\n1. Explicit type arguments on a method call: `x.m<T>(…)` or only inference? (Proposed: both, as for functions.)\n2. Inherent `impl` blocks only in the struct's own package (proposed), or anywhere?\n\n## 2. Arrays and slices of scoped values (finding A3)\n\n**Today.** §25.4 rejects array and slice elements of scoped type (N0246), so `Slice<StringView>` and\n`[StringView; 3]` are errors. Yet `Vector`, `StableVector` and `SlotMap` elements may be scoped.\n\n**Cost in the port.**\n- The controls take item lists as one `\"Low|Medium|High\"` view, split with `itemCount`/`itemAt`. This is a public-API\n  wart that will be hard to remove later.\n- The browser page's HTML is 30 separate calls, since a table of lines cannot be written either. Multi-line string\n  literals would also cover this case.\n\n**Proposal.** Allow scoped element types in arrays and slices with the collections' rule.\n- All elements of one array share its element type's regions. `[StringView<r>; N]` and `Slice<a, StringView<r>>` have\n  `r` for every element; elements are not tracked separately. Arrays are already one place for the borrow checker\n  (§16.3), so no per-element loans are needed.\n- A slice of views has two regions, the slice's and the elements'. As for `Vector<StringView<r>>`, the elements' region\n  must outlive the slice's (`r: a`), checked where the slice is made.\n- Array literals of string literals (`[\"Low\", \"Medium\", \"High\"]`) have `static` elements, so a constant table of\n  views is possible. That is the common case, and it fits `const` too (§2, question 2).\n\n**Questions.**\n1. Is the collections' machinery (ADR for §",
    307 =>
        "25.4's Vector rule) reusable for arrays, or does `[T; N]` by value need\n   its own place rule for moves out of elements of scoped type?\n2. `const ITEMS: [StringView; 3] = [...]`: allowed under this proposal (static elements)? Proposed: yes, which also\n   answers finding B's \"a `const` cannot hold a `StringView`\".\n\n## 3. `dyn I` satisfying `T: I`, and loans in `dyn` (finding A1)\n\n**Today.**\n- `dyn I` does not implement `I` for bounds (N0268, \"'dyn Sink' does not implement 'Sink', which 'S' requires\").\n- A type that holds loans cannot be `dyn` (N0268; §37.2, OD-10a).\n\n**Cost in the port.**\n- The layout engine needs text metrics both from a font engine (which borrows its font pool, so it can only be used\n  through a bound) and from simple test doubles (natural as `dyn`). With neither composition available, every\n  function on the path became generic and free (`endLayout<M>`, `endFrame<M>`, `renderInto<B>`, `textInputEx<M>`).\n- A host whose platform borrows its script copies it instead (`ScriptPlatform`, 4 KB per platform).\n\n**Proposal (two independent parts).**\n1. **`dyn I` implements `I`.** For an interface without generic methods (all of them, §1), `dyn I` gets the obvious\n   impl: each method calls through the table. Instantiating a generic with `T = dyn I` is then allowed where the\n   generic uses `T` only behind references (`borrow T`, `borrow mut T`), since `dyn I` is unsized (§37.2). A generic\n   that needs `T` by value (moves it, makes an array of it, boxes it) is N0274 at the instantiation, as for scoped\n   arguments. Instances are made per use, as now.\n2. **OD-10a, scoped `dyn(r) I`.** A `dyn` value that carries the loans of region `r`, so a struct with region\n   parameters can be one. The port's use is the common one: a borrowed engine passed to code that wants dynamic\n   dispatch. This is the larger change (region erasure, §25.5, must keep `r` on the `dyn` type). Part 1 alone already\n   removes most of the port's workarounds, because the generic route then accepts both kinds of caller.\n\n**Questions.**\n1. Is \"uses `T` only behind references\" checkable from the generic's body as P3 has it (the same analysis as\n   N0274's \"needs an unscoped type\"), or does it need a declared bound such as `T: ?Sized`? Proposed: inferred, like\n   N0274; no new syntax.\n2. Take up OD-10a now, or after part 1?\n\n## 4. Not proposed\n\n- **Blocks as values** (`let y = { …; v };`). §15 G5 leaves this out deliberately, and the port's few cases read fine\n  as statements.\n- **`abs` on integers.** That is ADR 0063 §5, question 2.\n- **The interpreter's speed** (the port's suite takes 18 minutes interpreted, seconds compiled). This is worth a\n  benchmark item of its own, not a language change.\n\n## 5. Implementation (2026-11-04)\n\n### 5.1 Generic methods and inherent impls (§1)\n\n- **Generic versions.** A struct method with type parameters is declared once, with those parameters opaque, as a\n  `GenericFunctionEntry`. The entry gains `owner` and `ownerEnvironment`, and the generic version records it in\n  `HirFunction.methodTemplate` (`src/check/structs.cee`). Interface and `impl I for T` methods keep N0216, with a\n  message that says why.\n- **Calls.** `x.m(args)` and `S.f(args)` infer from the arguments after the receiver, or take written type\n  arguments (`instantiateMethod`, `src/check/generics.cee`). `instantiateFunction` declares the instance with its\n  owner and the owner's environment.\n- **Written type arguments.** `x.m<T>(…)` parses into `Ast.memberTypeArguments`, keyed by the member's identifier,\n  so that `ExprNode.Member` and its many matches stay as they are. The formatter walks them. Type arguments on a\n  function without type parameters are N0209.\n- **C names.** An instance is `ceeMG_<owner>_<length><name><mangled arguments>` (`src/backend/c_names.cee`).\n  `editors/debug/ceesafe_names.py` demangles it, and `tools/debugger_check.py` checks a real name.\n- **Inherent impls.** `ItemKind.InherentImpl`. `mergeInherentImpls` (`src/check/inh",
    308 =>
        "erent.cee`) appends a block's\n  functions to its struct's list before anything is declared. The block must be in the struct's own package and\n  name the struct with its own parameters (N0267).\n\n### 5.2 Arrays and slices of views; constant views (§2)\n\n- **Elements.** `isScoped` of an array is its element's (`src/types/types.cee`). `checkElement` allows views and\n  values holding loans, and rejects bare `borrow T` (`src/check/arrays.cee`). The Copy rule for elements is\n  unchanged, so an array of `SliceMut` is still rejected: two mutable views cannot share an array.\n- **Constants.** `StringView` and `[StringView; N]` (`isConstantView`, `constElementOk`). `fold` and the constant's\n  use carry `HirExpr.StringConst`, which is static data in both backends.\n- **Adversarial checks.** Each of these is rejected:\n  - an element outliving its data (N0243);\n  - an element's loan stored through a parameter (N0272);\n  - writing data an array still views (N0241);\n  - returning an array of a local's views (N0245).\n  The signature that N0272 suggests, `fn keep<region r>(items: Slice<StringView<r>>, into: borrow mut\n  Vector<StringView<r>>)`, compiles and runs.\n\n### 5.3 `dyn I` implements `I` (§3.1)\n\n- **Bounds.** `implements` accepts `dyn I` for `I`, with the same arguments (`src/check/interfaces.cee`).\n- **Function instances.** An instance whose parameters, locals or result hold a `dyn` by value is N0274 inside the\n  instance, which `endInstance` gives as N0222 at the use (`checkDynInstance`). The check came from a test:\n  `fn copyOf<M: Metrics + Copy>(m: borrow M) -> M` with `M = dyn Metrics` compiled before it and made a `dyn` local.\n- **User types.** Their type arguments may be `dyn` where the instance holds it only behind references\n  (`ByRef<dyn I>`); otherwise N0330 (`holdsBareDyn`). Instances made before the members are resolved are checked\n  afterwards (`pendingDynInstances`).\n\n### 5.4 Scoped `T` behind `borrow dyn I` (§3.2, OD-10a)\n\n- **The rule.** A type with region parameters coerces to `borrow dyn I` and `borrow mut dyn I`. `Box<dyn I>` still\n  refuses it (N0268), since a box keeps no region. Nothing new is tracked. A `borrow(a) View<r>` already requires\n  `r` to outlive `a`, and borrowing the value keeps its loans alive (B4), so the `dyn` reference may carry only its\n  own loan.\n- **Adversarial checks.** Each of these is rejected:\n  - changing data while a `dyn` view of it lives (N0240);\n  - a struct field holding a `dyn` past its data (N0243, for both the view and its data);\n  - a `Vector` of `dyn` references outliving their data (N0243);\n  - overwriting a borrowed field (N0240);\n  - returning a `dyn` of a local (N0244);\n  - storing a parameter's view into a longer-lived holder (N0272).\n  A references-to-references swap is not expressible at all (N0246).\n\n### 5.5 Evidence (macOS arm64)\n\n- **New corpus tests:**\n  - `run-pass/generic-methods`, `check-fail/generic-methods`;\n  - `run-pass/slices-of-views`, `check-fail/slices-of-views`, `check-fail/slices-of-views-loans`;\n  - `run-pass/dyn-as-bound`, `check-fail/dyn-as-bound`, `check-fail/dyn-as-bound-by-value`;\n  - `run-pass/dyn-scoped`, `check-fail/dyn-scoped`.\n  Expectations come from Python, and the run-pass tests pass compiled and with `--interp`.\n- **Existing expectations that encoded the old rules**, each changed in place with a note saying so:\n  - `check-fail/generics.cee:18` (a generic method);\n  - `check-fail/closure-methods.cee:17` (an array of views);\n  - `check-fail/region-instances.cee:15` (`[T; 2]` of views);\n  - `check-fail/scoped-types.cee:5–6` (arrays and slices of slices);\n  - `check-fail/impl-generic.cee:66` (a scoped value behind `borrow dyn`).\n- **Full corpus:** 596 tests, 0 failures. **Fixed point:** byte-identical C. **Downstream:** ClayWind (all seven\n  packages) and CosmoStack build. The contract suite is recorded in ROADMAP §10.\n",
    309 =>
        "# ADR 0065 — Agent-facing reference: the library API in llms-full.txt, `ceesafe_api`, and \"did you mean\"\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-04 (\"Go ahead implement 1-4\"), after the review of the agent-facing\n  documentation that followed the ClayWind port. Implemented on the working tree; not released.\n- Scope: documentation (`spec/api/std.md`, new; `guide/builtins.md`, new; `guide/for-agents.md`; `templates/AGENTS.md`;\n  the site's `llms.txt` and `llms-full.txt`), the MCP server (`ceesafe_api`), and diagnostics (N0100, N0200, N0230,\n  N0234 messages). No language change.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nAn agent porting ClayWind (~25k lines) to CeeSafe missed names that existed, and guessed names from other languages\nthat did not:\n\n- it looked for `TextWriter.unsigned`, but `TextWriter.decimal(u64)` existed (`spec/api/core.md`);\n- it wrote `Environment.args()` for `Process.argument(i)`, and `Timer.now().since(…)` for `Timer.now()`, a `u64`;\n- it learned the async socket API from another program's source.\n\nThe causes were in what an agent reads:\n\n- `llms-full.txt`, the one file `AGENTS.md` tells an agent to load, had the guide and the specification but not the\n  library reference;\n- lib/std had no API page at all;\n- the compiler's own types (`Console`, `File`, `Process`, `Vector`, `StringView`, the integer methods) are described\n  only in specification prose;\n- the MCP server had no lookup by name (`ceesafe_symbol` is a hover at a position);\n- \"has no method named 'unsigned'\" named nothing that does exist.\n\n## 2. Decision\n\n1. **The reference, complete and in one file.**\n   - `spec/api/std.md` is `ceesafec doc lib/std --title \"lib/std\"`, and `tools/spec_check.py` checks it as it checks\n     `core.md`.\n   - `guide/builtins.md` tabulates the built-in types and functions. Each section ends with a run example, and a\n     final type-checked block calls every row with the type the tables give, so `guide_check` fails when a table\n     drifts from the compiler. Writing that block found two wrong rows: `checkedTruncate` and `saturatingTruncate` take\n     only floats.\n   - `llms-full.txt` now holds the guide (with the new page), the specification, and both API pages. `llms.txt` links\n     lib/std, and tells agents to look a name up before concluding it is missing.\n2. **`ceesafe_api`**, the MCP server's tenth tool (`src/tooling/api_lookup.cee`, tooling.md §6.3).\n   - **Input:** a type (`TextWriter`), a member (`TextWriter.decimal`) or a bare member name (`decimal`).\n   - **Results:** the matching entries of the two API pages and the built-in types page: a type's section, a member's\n     entry, or the table rows that name it.\n   - **When nothing matches,** up to eight suggestions:\n     - the closest names by edit distance;\n     - for `Type.member`, the type's real members;\n     - the entries whose documentation mentions the member's name (`unsigned` finds `TextWriter.decimal`).\n   - `tools/mcp_api_check.py`, a new contract, holds 15 lookups against names the documents state.\n3. **\"Did you mean\"** (`src/check/suggest.cee`, diagnostics.md).\n   - **Unknown member** (N0200, N0230): a struct's close members, then those whose doc comment mentions the name,\n     at most three; a small type with no close member lists all its members.\n   - **Built-in types:** ranked by the longest shared start, then edits, from the same lookup as `ceesafe_api`, so\n     the error and the tool agree.\n   - **Numbers:** an unknown method on a number is \"has no method 'm'\", with its close methods. Before, it was\n     \"has no methods\", which became false with ADR 0063.\n   - **Reserved words:** N0100 says when the token is a reserved word.\n4. **The agent guide and template.**\n   - **New sections in `guide/for-agents.md`:** looking names up, reserved words that read like names, optionals\n     against results, `Console` against `AsyncConsole`, and `mut` at a call against `borrow mut` elsewhere. Each\n     section has a checked example with the compiler's exact output.\n   - **",
    310 =>
        "`templates/AGENTS.md`:** four new lines on the same.\n\n## 3. Evidence (macOS arm64)\n\n- **Checks:** `guide_check` passes 117 examples, `spec_check` reports 0 problems (including `std.md`), and\n  `mcp_api_check` reports 0 problems over 15 lookups. The corpus passes 596 tests with 0 failures; no expectation\n  needed a change.\n- **Messages the port actually hit**, before and after:\n  - `w.unsigned(42)` gives \"; did you mean 'signed', 'decimal' or 'hex'?\";\n  - `Process.args(1)` gives \"'argument', 'argumentCount' or 'run'\";\n  - `Console.writeLn` gives \"'writeLine', 'write' or 'writeBytes'\";\n  - `Environment.args()` gives \"it has 'variable'\";\n  - `n.mx(1)` gives \"'max'\".\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- The built-in types page is written by hand, but it can't drift silently: its final block is checked against the\n  compiler.\n- Suggestions only append to existing messages. The codes are unchanged, and tools that match on codes are\n  unaffected.\n- `ceesafe_api` reads the documents embedded in `ceesafec`, so it answers for the compiler that serves it, as\n  `ceesafe_spec_search` does.\n",
    311 =>
        "# ADR 0066 — Static tables of views\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-04 (\"fix the static view table finding in CeeSafe\"). Implemented on the\n  working tree; not released. Raised by the ClayWind cleanup on 0.11.0 (`~/dev/CeeSafeApps/CEESAFE-FINDINGS.md` §G).\n- Scope: the checker (`src/check/statics.cee`). Spec: type-system.md §10 and §39.2. No new diagnostic.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nADR 0064 §2 allowed constant tables of views, such as `const LEVELS: [StringView; 3] = [\"Low\", \"Medium\", \"High\"];`.\n\n- **A constant is a value, not a place** (§10). It can be indexed and iterated, but slicing it or taking its\n  `.length` is N0247 or N0216 until it is bound to a local.\n- **The ClayWind port passes tables to the controls as `Slice<StringView>`,** so every such call needed an extra\n  line: `let prioItems = PRIO_ITEMS;` and then `prioItems[..]`.\n- **A `static` is a place** (§39.2), which is what such a table wants. But `static LEVELS: [StringView; 3]` was\n  N0337: \"'[StringView; 3]' is not 'Share'\".\n- **The check makes an exception for a bare `StringView`.** `static NAME: StringView` is accepted, as the spec's own\n  example shows, because \"a view of static string data … is safe to share\". The exception did not extend to an\n  array of the same views.\n\n## 2. Decision\n\n- **What a `static` may hold.** In a `static` (not a `static mut`), `T` may also be `[StringView; N]`. This is the\n  same set of types a constant view may have, `isConstantView` (`src/check/declare.cee`), so constants and statics\n  agree.\n- **Its initialiser** is unchanged (§39.4): an array literal or repeat of string literals or constant views.\n- **What it allows.** The table is a place, so it is sliced, measured and borrowed where it is named:\n  - `f(LEVELS[..])` and `LEVELS.length`;\n  - `borrow LEVELS`, as `borrow(static) [StringView; 3]`.\n- **What stays rejected:**\n  - **Changing the table:** assigning an element (N0211) and borrowing it mutably (N0248), as for every static.\n  - **Arrays of arrays of views** (N0337), as for constants.\n  - **`static mut` tables of views** (N0337, unchanged). See §3.\n\n## 3. Not taken: `static mut` tables\n\n- **Release 0.11.0 does not check the regions of values written to a `static mut`.** Writing a view of a local\n  `String` into `static mut N: StringView` compiles, and reading `N` afterwards reads freed memory.\n- **This ADR does not extend `static mut` to arrays of views,** which would widen that hole. Only the immutable\n  form is added, and it cannot be written at all.\n- **The hole itself is a separate soundness bug,** fixed by ADR 0067: a write to a static may carry no loan\n  (N0272).\n\n## 4. Evidence (macOS arm64)\n\n- **New corpus tests:**\n  - `run-pass/static-view-tables` covers slicing, `.length`, iteration, a `borrow(static)` result, a repeat, and a\n    constant view as an element. Its expected output comes from a Python model of the same tables. It passes\n    compiled and with `--interp`.\n  - `check-fail/static-view-tables` covers `static mut`, nesting, a non-`@constant` initialiser, writing and a\n    mutable borrow.\n  - Both tests fail on 0.11.0, with N0337 on every immutable table: the run-pass test's tables, and the\n    check-fail test's `LEVELS`, which should be accepted.\n- **The full corpus, the fixed point and the contract suite** are recorded in ROADMAP §10.\n\n## 5. Consequences\n\n- ClayWind's demo tables can be `static`. Its `let x = TABLE;` lines then go, once a release carries this.\n- A `const` table still cannot be sliced where it is named. A table that is passed as a slice should be declared\n  `static`.\n",
    312 =>
        "# ADR 0067 — A write to a `static mut` carries no loan\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-04 (\"fix the static mut hole too\"). Implemented on the working tree;\n  not released. Found while testing ADR 0066.\n- Scope: the borrow checker (`src/nir/borrow.cee`, `checkStaticWrites`). Spec: type-system.md §39.3, diagnostics.md\n  (N0272). No new code.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n- **A `static mut` is the place `*S`, with `S: borrow(static) mut T`** (§39.3). Every use is unsafe, but the unsafe\n  block only makes the use unsafe (two uses may alias, two threads may race). It is not meant to switch off the\n  borrow checker.\n- **Release 0.11.0 accepts a dangling view in a static.** It compiles, and printing `LAST` afterwards reads freed\n  memory:\n\n  ```ceesafe\n  static mut LAST: StringView = \"none\";\n  /// Safety: …\n  fn keep(s: borrow String) { unsafe { LAST = s.view(); } }\n  ```\n\n- **Why the checker misses it.** The borrow checker records the write as a store through the reference `S`\n  (`collectWrites`, §25.7 rule 2). N0272 then checks such stores only against the parameters a reference was\n  borrowed from (`checkParameterWrites`). `S` comes from `StaticAddress`, so it points to no loan, and the store\n  was never checked.\n- **Only `StringView` is affected.** It is the one scoped type a `static mut` may hold: N0337 rejects every other\n  scoped type, and ADR 0066 keeps arrays of views out of `static mut`. References to references are not supported,\n  so a view in a static cannot be reached any other way.\n\n## 2. Decision\n\n- **The rule.** A value written to a static, through its address local `S` (`S = StaticAddress(s)`), may carry no\n  loan. Any loan in what it stores is N0272: \"this stores a value borrowed from 's' into static 'LAST', which\n  outlives every borrow; a static may hold only static data, such as a string literal\". When the loan is of a place,\n  a note points at the borrow.\n- **Still accepted: static data,** which carries no loan:\n  - a string literal and a constant view;\n  - a view read from another static or from a static table (`LEVELS[1]`);\n  - a call's result when its arguments are static data, or when its type names only `static` (ADR 0068).\n- **Rejected:** a view of a local or of a parameter, including through a call or a subview.\n- **`StringView<static>` parameters.** As first implemented, this ADR rejected them too, since a call did not check\n  that their argument is static data (`take(s.view())` with a local `s` compiled). ADR 0068 made `static` in a\n  parameter's type N0273, so the case no longer arises.\n- **Why N0272 and not a new code.** N0272 is \"storing loans where the signature does not let them flow\". A static\n  is the extreme case: nothing may flow there.\n\n## 3. Evidence (macOS arm64)\n\n- **`check-fail/static-mut-loans`:** four writes rejected (a local, a parameter, through a call, a subview). Its\n  fifth case, a `StringView<static>` parameter, moved to ADR 0068's `check-fail/static-parameters`.\n- **`run-pass/static-mut-views`:** six accepted writes of static data, and a `static mut` integer, alongside them.\n  Its expected output comes from a Python model. It passes compiled and with `--interp`.\n- **On 0.11.0, both tests fail:** the check-fail test because the hole compiles, and the run-pass test because it\n  uses ADR 0066's static table.\n- **The full corpus, the fixed point and the contract suite** are recorded in ROADMAP §10.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- **Programs this change breaks.** None of the repository's packages writes a `static mut` view. A program that\n  stored a non-static view this way was already unsound, and now gets N0272.\n- **`StringView<static>` parameters,** left open here, are settled by ADR 0068: N0273 on a parameter, and a result\n  that names only `static` is enforced and may be stored in a static.\n",
    313 =>
        "# ADR 0068 — `static` in signatures: rejected on parameters, enforced on results\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-04 (\"Tackle ADR 0067 §4 too\", then \"Reject on params, enforce on\n  results\"). Implemented on the working tree; not released.\n- Scope: the checker (`src/check/references.cee`: `writesStatic`, `staticParameterError`, `resolveFlows`;\n  `src/check/interfaces.cee`), the borrow checker's N0245 message (`src/nir/borrow.cee`). Spec: type-system.md\n  §25.2, §25.6, §39.3; diagnostics.md (N0273).\n\n## 1. Context\n\nBefore this ADR, release 0.11.0 accepted `static` written anywhere in a signature and then ignored it:\n\n- **Ignored where flows are computed.** `static` is never a flow name (§25.6), so `v: StringView<static>` had the\n  same flows as `v: StringView`.\n- **On a parameter, it promised nothing.** `take(s.view())` with a local `s` compiled for\n  `fn take(v: StringView<static>)`. This was sound only because the callee could not use the promise either:\n  storing `v` anywhere long-lived was N0272 (and since ADR 0067, also into a static).\n- **On a result, it promised nothing either.** `-> StringView<static>` fell into elision (§25.6) and borrowed from\n  the scoped parameter. So `fn launder(v: StringView) -> StringView<static> => v` compiled, and its result stayed\n  tied to the caller's loan.\n- **Without a scoped parameter, it was rejected outright.** `fn table() -> Slice<static, StringView>` was N0245:\n  \"a function without reference parameters cannot return a reference\".\n\nADR 0067 §4 left two ways out:\n- **(a)** check the argument at each call;\n- **(b)** reject `static` on parameters.\n\nOption (a) has to hold on every path to the callee. A function value has its regions erased (§25.5), and so do a\n`dyn` method and a generic bound. None of those calls sees the parameter's `static`, so (a) would need a check on\neach, or a stored view would dangle again.\n\n## 2. Decision\n\n- **Parameters: N0273.** `static` written in a parameter's type is N0273. That covers `StringView<static>`,\n  `Slice<static, T>`, `S<static>`, `borrow(static) T`, and an optional or array of one. It applies to functions,\n  methods, generic functions and interface methods. Without `static`, a string literal still passes.\n- **Results: enforced.** A result whose written regions are all `static` has no flow names and is not elided. It\n  borrows from no parameter, which has two sides:\n  - **In the body.** Returning a parameter's loan is N0245, which now adds \"a 'static' result may hold only static\n    data\". Returning a local's loan is N0244.\n  - **At a call.** The result carries no loan. It outlives the arguments, and it may be stored in a static (ADR\n    0067).\n- **Mixed results are unchanged.** In `borrow(static, a) T` or `Pair<static, a>`, the other regions give the flows,\n  as before.\n- **`borrow(static) T` results** already had no sources, through their source list. Only the `StringView<static>`,\n  `Slice<static, T>` and `S<static>` forms change.\n- **Codes:** none new. The parameter case is N0273, the region-misuse code, and the result cases are N0244 and\n  N0245, the return-source codes.\n\n## 3. Evidence (macOS arm64)\n\n- **New corpus tests:**\n  - `check-fail/static-parameters`: seven N0273 cases (functions, a slice, a reference, an optional, a generic, a\n    method, an interface method), with a plain parameter still taking a literal.\n  - `check-fail/static-results`: `launder` and a slice version are N0245, and a local's view is N0244.\n  - `run-pass/static-results`: a `static` result kept past its argument's scope, stored in a `static mut`, and a\n    parameterless `Slice<static, StringView>` result. Expected output comes from Python. It passes compiled and\n    with `--interp`.\n- **On the previous build (0.11.0 plus ADRs 0066–0067), all three fail:**\n  - `find(owned.view())`'s result was tied to `owned` (N0243);\n  - `table()` was N0245;\n  - the parameters compiled.\n- **Expectations that changed, each noted in place:**\n  - `tests/check/chec",
    314 =>
        "k-pass/arrays-and-slices.cee` (`staticView` wrote `static` on its parameters; it now writes it\n    on its result);\n  - `tests/check/check-fail/arrays-and-slices.cee` (lines 13 and 16 also give N0273);\n  - `check-fail/static-mut-loans` (its `StringView<static>` case moved here).\n- **Code that still builds:** the compiler's own sources, lib/core, lib/std and ClayWind's six packages.\n  CosmoStack was being edited during the check, and its two errors (an unknown field and an unknown variant) were\n  the same on 0.11.0. The full corpus, the fixed point and the contract suite are recorded in ROADMAP §10.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- **A program that wrote `static` on a parameter** gets N0273 and drops the word. Its callers are unaffected, since\n  the word was never checked.\n- **A function can now return static data from no parameter.** `fn table() -> Slice<static, StringView>` and\n  `-> StringView<static>` helpers are allowed, and their results can go into a `static mut`.\n- **Not covered:** `static` in a function type's parameters (`fn(StringView<static>)`) and in closure literals'\n  parameters is not rejected. Region erasure makes it as meaningless there, and nothing relies on it.\n",
    315 =>
        "# ADR 0069 — Native windows in lib/std\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-04. The owner asked for a native app demo of the ClayWind port, chose\n  \"Add windowing to CeeSafe first\", then accepted §5 as proposed: all three backends, `nextEvent`, the\n  `CEESAFE_WINDOW_SCRIPT` switch, premultiplied RGBA8 `u32`s, and release as 0.13.0 (\"Accept, implement it\").\n- Scope:\n  - `lib/std/window.cee` (new) and `runtime/hosted/ceesafe_rt_window.c` (new);\n  - the interpreter's model of the new runtime calls (`src/interpreter/io.cee`);\n  - the embedded library and runtime (`tools/gen_library.py`, `tools/gen_runtime.py`);\n  - spec: type-system.md (a new §26 subsection), `spec/api/std.md` (generated), guide.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n- **There is no way to open a window.** lib/std reaches the operating system through `extern \"C\"` calls into the C\n  runtime (`lib/std/host.cee` → `runtime/hosted/ceesafe_rt_io.c`): files, sockets, processes, clocks, threads. None\n  of those is a window, so a CeeSafe program that wants one needs `extern \"C\"` in its own code.\n- **The ClayWind port shows the cost.** It is a pure-CeeSafe UI toolkit: a layout engine, a software rasterizer and\n  controls, with a rule of no `extern \"C\"` in the port. It can show its frames only headless, in a terminal, or in a\n  browser fed over a WebSocket.\n- **What a window has to provide is small.** The rasterizer already produces RGBA pixels. A window needs to:\n  - show them;\n  - report the pointer, the keys, typed text, the wheel, a resize and a close request;\n  - set the cursor.\n\n## 2. Proposal\n\n### 2.1 The API (`lib/std/window.cee`, hosted only)\n\n```ceesafe\nlet window = Window.open(\"ClayWind\", 1024, 640)?;      // Result<Window, WindowError>\nloop {\n    while (true) {\n        match (window.nextEvent()) {                     // WindowEvent?, never blocks\n            Some(WindowEvent.Closed) => { return Ok(); }\n            Some(e) => { handle(e); }\n            null => { break; }\n        }\n    }\n    window.present(pixels, width, height)?;              // premultiplied RGBA8, rows top to bottom\n    window.waitFrame();                                  // until the display's next refresh, or an event\n}\n```\n\n- **`Window`** is a move type with a drop, which closes the window. It is neither `Send` nor `Share`: macOS lets\n  only the main thread use windows, so `Window.open` on another thread is `WindowError.NotMainThread` on every\n  system, which keeps programs portable.\n- **Opening and closing:**\n  - `Window.open(title, width, height)` takes a size in points.\n  - `WindowError` is `Unsupported` (no backend, including under the interpreter), `NoDisplay` (no window server,\n    such as `DISPLAY` unset or a CI runner), `NotMainThread` or `Resources`.\n- **Size:** `window.size()` gives `WindowSize { width, height }` in points, and `window.scale()` gives pixels per\n  point (2.0 on a Retina display).\n- **Drawing:** `window.present(pixels: Slice<u32>, width, height)` shows the image scaled to the window. Each\n  `u32` is one premultiplied RGBA8 pixel, bytes R, G, B, A in memory order: the format of ClayWind's `Raster`\n  frame, so it is passed without a copy. A program that wants sharp pixels renders at `size() * scale()`.\n- **Window settings:** `window.setCursor(WindowCursor)` takes `Default`, `Pointer`, `Text`, `Crosshair`, `Grab`,\n  `ResizeHorizontal` or `ResizeVertical`. `window.setTitle(StringView)` sets the title.\n- **Events:** `WindowEvent` is an enum, positioned in points:\n  - `Closed`, `Resized(WindowSize)` and `Focus(bool)`;\n  - `PointerMoved(x, y)`, `PointerButton(button, down, x, y)` and `Wheel(dx, dy)`;\n  - `Key(WindowKey, down, repeat, WindowModifiers)` and `Text(u32)`, a Unicode scalar.\n- **Keys:** `WindowKey` names the keys a UI needs: letters, digits, arrows, Tab, Enter, Escape, Backspace, Delete,\n  Home, End, PageUp, PageDown, Space, F1–F12, and `Other(u32)` for the rest.\n- **`waitFrame()`** sleeps until about the next 1/60 s, or until an event arrives, so an idle program does",
    316 =>
        " not\n  spin.\n\n### 2.2 The runtime (`runtime/hosted/ceesafe_rt_window.c`)\n\n- **The ABI.** A handful of C functions, in the style of `ceesafe_rt_io.c`: `ceesafe_window_open`, `_close`, `_poll`\n  (one event into a plain struct), `_present`, `_size`, `_scale`, `_set_cursor`, `_set_title`, `_wait`. Each call\n  returns a value or a negative error code.\n- **macOS.**\n  - **Loading.** AppKit, CoreGraphics, QuartzCore and the Objective-C runtime are opened with `dlopen` the first\n    time a window is opened, and called through `objc_msgSend`. So no program needs a link flag (the runtime has no\n    way to name a framework, ADR 0047), and a program that never opens a window does not load AppKit.\n  - **Events and drawing.** Events come from a manual pump (`nextEventMatchingMask:untilDate:inMode:dequeue:`), as\n    SDL and GLFW do, so the CeeSafe program keeps its own loop. A frame is shown by setting a layer-backed view's\n    `contents` to a `CGImage` of the pixels.\n- **Windows.** Win32: `RegisterClassW`, `CreateWindowExW`, `PeekMessageW`, and `StretchDIBits` for a frame. user32\n  and gdi32 are named with `#pragma comment(lib)`, as `ceesafe_rt_io.c` names ws2_32.\n- **Linux.** X11: `libX11.so.6` is opened with `dlopen`, and frames are shown with `XPutImage`. Without the library\n  or a `DISPLAY`, `open` is `NoDisplay`. Wayland is not in this proposal (§5, question 1).\n- **Elsewhere.** On any other system, and in the freestanding profile, `open` is `Unsupported`, and the runtime file\n  holds only that stub.\n\n### 2.3 Testing\n\n- **The release workflow cannot look at a window.** Its runners have no display, or none a test can see.\n- **Proposed.** A runtime switch, `CEESAFE_WINDOW_SCRIPT=<file>`, replaces the real system with a scripted one:\n  - the window exists only in memory;\n  - events come from the file, in the grammar of ClayWind's `ScriptPlatform`;\n  - `present` keeps the last frame, and `CEESAFE_WINDOW_DUMP=<file>` writes it out.\n\n  A new contract, `tools/window_check.py`, runs a corpus program through that, on every system, and compares the\n  frame and the event trace with expectations computed in Python.\n- **On macOS,** an opt-in check (`--display`) also opens a real window, presents frames, resizes it and closes it.\n  It is for a machine with a display, not CI.\n\n### 2.4 ClayWind (after a release)\n\n- **A new host, `claywind-hosts/desktop`.** It is the demo in a native window, 100% `.cee`: it implements ClayWind's\n  `Platform` on `WindowEvent`s, and presents the `Raster`'s pixels at the window's pixel size.\n- **Packaging.** A `Clay.app` bundle script for macOS.\n\n## 3. Alternatives considered\n\n- **A browser window** (`--app` mode over the existing browser host). It works today, but it is not native: the\n  pixels go through a WebSocket.\n- **`extern \"C\"` in the app.** It breaks the port's rule, and every CeeSafe program would repeat the work.\n- **Wrapping SDL.** It is a C dependency that each user would have to install, which goes against the runtime's\n  self-contained design.\n\n## 4. Risks\n\n- **macOS through `objc_msgSend`.** The ABI depends on the call's types: arguments and results that are structures\n  (`NSRect`, `CGSize`) need correctly cast calls, and on x86_64, `objc_msgSend_stret` for some of them. The two\n  macOS release targets, arm64 and x86_64, both need the check of §2.3.\n- **Size.** About 1,500 lines of C in three backends, plus the library and the interpreter's model. The release\n  then grows its runtime, though not its binaries' dependencies.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. **Backends:** all three now (macOS, Windows, Linux X11), or macOS first with the others `Unsupported`?\n   Proposed: all three, since the release builds six targets and an API that works on one of them invites\n   non-portable programs. Wayland stays later.\n2. **Events:** `nextEvent() -> WindowEvent?` (no allocation, proposed), or `poll(into: borrow mut\n   Vector<WindowEvent>)`?\n3. **Pixels:** premultiplied RGBA8 in `u32`s only (proposed; ClayWind's `Ras",
    317 =>
        "ter` frame as it is), or also\n   straight alpha and BGRA?\n4. **Testing:** the `CEESAFE_WINDOW_SCRIPT` switch of §2.3 (proposed), or a separate test-only API?\n5. **Release:** 0.13.0, a minor version for a new library API (proposed).\n\n\n## 6. Consequences\n\n- **The new names are taken.** `Window`, `WindowEvent`, `WindowKey`, `WindowModifiers`, `WindowButton`,\n  `WindowCursor`, `WindowSize` and `WindowError` become lib/std names, which a program may not redefine (N0201). The\n  repository's only clash was the corpus test `run-pass/region-near-misses.cee`, whose `struct Window<region r>` is\n  renamed `Pane`, with a note.\n- **The spec** is type-system.md §26.13.\n\n## 7. Implementation and evidence (2026-11-04)\n\n**Files:**\n- `lib/std/window.cee`: the API of §2.1, decoding the runtime's events.\n- `runtime/hosted/ceesafe_rt_window.c`: the scripted window and three backends, embedded as runtime file 8. The\n  backend adds it only to a program whose C calls `ceesafe_window_` (`src/backend/c_backend.cee`).\n- `src/interpreter/io.cee`: `ceesafe_window_open` answers `Unsupported`.\n- type-system.md §26.13 and `spec/api/std.md`.\n\n**Implementation notes:**\n- **macOS colour.** The macOS backend tags frames as sRGB, so they are colour-managed on wide-gamut displays like\n  the browser's canvas. Tagged as DeviceRGB, the same values looked oversaturated.\n- **Windows.** The Win32 backend uses wide resource names (`MAKEINTRESOURCEW`), since the runtime is compiled\n  without `UNICODE`. It records the first thread with a `.CRT$XCU` initializer, or a constructor outside MSVC.\n\n**Tests:**\n- **`tools/window_check.py`,** a new contract, makes 7 checks:\n  - a scripted trace of every event kind: across frames, a resize and a focus that change nothing, and non-ASCII\n    text. It and the last frame's bytes must equal what the script computes by §26.13's grammar;\n  - a malformed and a missing script, a zero size, another thread, a plain scripted open, and the interpreter.\n\n  `--display` adds a real window.\n- **`check-fail/window-not-send`:** a `Window` moved into `Thread.spawn` is N0334.\n- **The release workflow's smoke test** builds and runs a scripted window on all six targets.\n\n**Real windows**, each driven by injected input, with the frames checked in screenshots:\n- **macOS** (arm64, clang): the frames, the scale of 2, and the colours after sRGB tagging.\n- **Windows 11** (arm64, a Parallels VM):\n  - built with MSVC `/std:c11 /W3 /O2`, with no warnings;\n  - mouse, keys, text and a resize, through `SendInput`, `SendKeys` and `MoveWindow`;\n  - the x64 build compiles and runs too.\n- **Ubuntu 22.04** (arm64, gcc under the strict warnings, Xvfb): mouse, keys, text and a resize, through `xdotool`.\n  `WM_DELETE_WINDOW` gives `Closed`.\n- **The same scripted run** gives byte-identical output and frames on macOS and Windows.\n\n**Downstream: ClayWind's `claywind-hosts/desktop`,** the demo in a native window, runs on all three systems. The\nowner ran it on the Mac and on the Windows VM: \"Worked perfectly on both mac and windows vm\".\n\n**Corpus:**\n- The full corpus passes.\n- `check-pass/async-timeout.hir` and `effects-of.hir` were regenerated, since lib/std's new items shift closure\n  numbers. Only numbers differ, as when ADRs 0055, 0057 and 0060 grew lib/std.\n\n**Found on the way:** a library file's type errors are reported only by `check --library-diagnostics`, the\n`library` contract. A user program that uses the file gets misleading results instead, such as N0212 or an\n\"unreachable\" panic. Anyone changing lib/std should run that contract first.\n\n## 8. After the release: an sRGB window on macOS (0.13.1)\n\n- **What profiling found.** ClayWind's desktop host on a Retina display, profiled after it gained incremental\n  rendering, spent most of its time presenting frames. Core Animation converted every new sRGB `CGImage` to the\n  display's colour space on the CPU: vImage table lookups, about 500 samples against about 230 for all of the\n  rendering.\n- **The fix.** The window's co",
    318 =>
        "lour space is now sRGB (`setColorSpace:` with `NSColorSpace.sRGBColorSpace`). Its\n  backing store then matches the frames, and the window server matches the window to the display.\n- **Result:** the host's CPU use while idle went from 47% to 31%, and vImage left the profile. A screenshot's\n  colours are within 1 of the earlier ones.\n- **No API change.** The other backends are unchanged.\n\n",
    319 =>
        "# ADR 0070 — Presenting only the changed parts of a window's frame\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-04 (\"Add a present that takes the changed boxes\", then §5 as proposed:\n  `presentChanged`, an IOSurface on macOS, a whole-frame present for a box outside the image, and release as 0.14.0).\n  Raised by ClayWind's desktop host (ClayWind-PORT-PLAN.md §19).\n- Scope: `lib/std/window.cee`, `runtime/hosted/ceesafe_rt_window.c` (all backends), the interpreter's model, type-system.md §26.13,\n  `tools/window_check.py`.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n- **What ClayWind's desktop host costs now.** It redraws only what changed (ClayWind's `Damage`), and a typical Retina\n  frame takes 2–4 ms to draw. Its idle CPU is still 31%, because `Window.present` takes the whole frame every time.\n- **Why every frame changes.** The demo's rotating badge changes every frame. Each present then copies 10 MB\n  (2048 × 1280 × 4) into a new `CGImage`, and Core Animation uploads all of it.\n- **The cause.** The caller knows which pixels changed, but `present` has no way to say so.\n\n## 2. Proposal\n\n```ceesafe\npublic struct WindowBox { public x: u32; public y: u32; public width: u32; public height: u32; }\n\n/// Like `present`, but only the pixels inside `changed` differ from the last frame presented.\npublic fn presentChanged(borrow mut this, pixels: Slice<u32>, width: u32, height: u32,\n                         changed: Slice<WindowBox>) -> Result<void, WindowError>\n```\n\n### 2.1 The contract\n\n- **What the caller promises.** Outside the boxes, every pixel equals the last frame presented. The boxes are in the\n  image's pixels and may overlap.\n- **When it is a plain `present`.** The window shows everything when there is no previous frame, when the image's\n  size differs from the last one, or when a box is not inside the image (§5, question 3).\n- **Empty `changed`.** It shows nothing new.\n- **Pixels outside the boxes.** The runtime may show them from `pixels` or from its own copy of the last frame. A\n  program that breaks the promise sees either.\n- **Checks.** The same as `present`'s: `pixels.length ≥ width × height`, and `Resources` otherwise.\n\n### 2.2 The backends\n\n- **macOS.**\n  - **The frame's storage.** The layer's contents become an IOSurface (IOSurface.framework, loaded with dlopen like\n    the rest). It is tagged sRGB, with 32-bit BGRA pixels.\n  - **A partial present** writes only the boxes into the surface, converting RGBA to BGRA as it goes, and tells the\n    layer the contents changed. Core Animation then shares the surface with the window server, with no `CGImage`\n    and no copy of the frame.\n  - **A full present** writes the whole surface. A new size makes a new surface.\n- **Windows.** The runtime keeps the frame as BGRA for `WM_PAINT`.\n  - **At 1:1** (the image's size equals the client area's): only the boxes are converted, then drawn with\n    `SetDIBitsToDevice`.\n  - **Otherwise:** the boxes are converted, and the whole frame is drawn with `StretchDIBits`.\n- **Linux (X11).** The runtime keeps the frame.\n  - **At 1:1** (the image's size equals the window's): only the boxes are converted, then drawn with `XPutImage`.\n  - **Otherwise:** the whole frame is scaled, as now.\n- **Scripted windows** keep a copy of the frame and apply the boxes to it, so `CEESAFE_WINDOW_DUMP` is the frame\n  the window would show. A test of a program's boxes is then a comparison of dumps.\n\n### 2.3 ClayWind (after a release)\n\n- **`Damage`** returns the boxes its last `render` redrew, in pixels. A full redraw is one box over the whole frame.\n- **The desktop host** passes them to `presentChanged`, and presents nothing when nothing changed.\n\n## 3. Alternatives considered\n\n- **`present` with an optional box argument.** It is one call fewer to learn. But `present`'s promise is \"this is\n  the frame\"; this call promises something about pixels it is not given, so it should be named apart.\n- **The window works out what changed itself,** by comparing the frame with its copy. That nee",
    320 =>
        "ds no API, but it\n  reads all 10 MB of the frame every time, which is the cost this proposal removes.\n- **On macOS, a persistent `CGImage` over a shared buffer.** Core Animation treats a `CGImage` as immutable, and\n  would still upload all of it.\n\n## 4. Testing\n\n- **`tools/window_check.py`, scripted:**\n  - a full frame, then frames that change only some boxes, then frames that change nothing;\n  - then a frame with a new size, and a frame whose box is outside the image.\n\n  Each dump must equal the frame a plain `present` gives, which the script computes.\n- **`--display`:** `presentChanged` on a real window, at 1:1 and scaled.\n- **By hand:** on macOS, Windows (the Parallels VM) and Linux (Xvfb), a moving box drawn by `presentChanged`\n  alone, checked in screenshots.\n- **ClayWind's desktop host on a Retina display:** idle CPU and a profile, before and after.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. **Name:** `presentChanged` (proposed), `presentRegions`, or `presentDamaged`?\n2. **macOS:** an IOSurface (proposed), or a smaller change that still makes a `CGImage` per frame but converts and\n   copies only the boxes? The second avoids IOSurface but keeps the full upload.\n3. **A box outside the image:** present the whole frame (proposed, forgiving), or `WindowError.Resources`?\n4. **Release:** 0.14.0 (proposed), a minor version for a new library API.\n\n## 6. Implementation and evidence (2026-11-04)\n\n**Files:**\n- `lib/std/window.cee`: `WindowBox` and `presentChanged`. It passes up to 64 boxes to the runtime as four `u32`s each;\n  more are a plain `present`.\n- `runtime/hosted/ceesafe_rt_window.c`: `ceesafe_window_present_changed`.\n  - **Every backend:** the shared code remembers the last present's size, skips empty boxes, and falls back to\n    `present` when there is no frame of this size or a box is outside the image.\n  - **macOS:** two sRGB BGRA IOSurfaces are shown in turn. Each keeps the boxes it still lacks (`pending`, up to 16,\n    else all of it), so a surface is brought up to date only by what changed since it was last shown. Without\n    IOSurface.framework, frames are `CGImage`s as before.\n  - **Windows:** `StretchDIBits` of each box at 1:1. Its source rectangle's y counts from the bottom row, even for a\n    top-down DIB, as testing on Windows 11 found.\n  - **X11:** `XPutImage` of each box at 1:1.\n  - **Scaled windows,** on Windows and X11, draw the whole frame.\n  - **Scripted windows** keep the frame and apply the boxes to it.\n\n**Tests:**\n- **`tools/window_check.py` now has 15 checks.** The new ones run 1 to 8 steps of a program that paints the whole\n  image a new colour each step but declares only some boxes. Each last frame must equal what the script computes:\n  - no previous frame, a new size, or a box outside the image show the whole frame;\n  - an empty list shows nothing new;\n  - a zero-size box is skipped.\n- **A real window on each system:** a grid presented once, then a square moving 4 pixels a frame through\n  `presentChanged` of its old and new places only. Each screenshot shows the whole grid and exactly one square, with\n  no trail:\n  - **macOS** (Retina, through IOSurface): 6400 red pixels, one 80-pixel square;\n  - **Windows 11 ARM64** (MSVC): 1600 red pixels at 1:1; scaled after a resize, one square;\n  - **Ubuntu on Xvfb** (gcc, strict warnings): 1600 red pixels at 1:1, and 3600 scaled to 600 × 450.\n\n",
    321 =>
        "# ADR 0071 — Bytes and text: bulk appends, byte order, bit rotation, searching and number parsing\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-05 (\"Accept as proposed, implement and release 0.15.0\": §6 as proposed:\n  generic byte-order methods, the bit counts included, one release). Implemented with the changes listed in §7, and\n  released as 0.15.0. Raised by the work on a Postgres wire client, later SMTP/IMAP/MIME (notes from that session,\n  passed on by the owner).\n- Scope: type-system.md §6 (integer methods), §16.5 (slice bulk operations), §22.2 (`StringView`), §23.1\n  (`Vector<T, A>`), §35 (floats); `lib/core/` (new `bytes.cee`, `text.cee`, `numbers.cee`); `src/check/vectors.cee`\n  and `src/check/library.cee` (the method tables); the interpreter; `runtime/hosted/ceesafe_rt_float.c` (float\n  parsing); the corpus.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nA wire-protocol client spends its time turning values into bytes and bytes back into values. Today, every one of\nthose steps is hand-written:\n\n- **Appending bytes.** `Vector<T>` has `push` and nothing bulk. An encoder pushes byte by byte, or pushes zeros and\n  then `copyFrom`s over them. This is the hottest path in such a client.\n- **Byte order.** Every Postgres message has big-endian lengths and fields. Each read or write is four shifts,\n  four index checks and a `truncate`.\n- **Bit rotation.** SHA-256 and MD5 (for SCRAM and the md5 login) need `rotateLeft`/`rotateRight`. `ownership.md`\n  §0 says \"`wrappingShl` masks\", but no such method exists: the integer methods are the nine of §6 plus `min`, `max`,\n  `abs` and `unsignedAbs`.\n- **Searching.** `StringView` and `Slice<u8>` have no `indexOf`, `startsWith`, `endsWith`, `split` or `trim`. SCRAM\n  messages (`r=…,s=…,i=…`), command tags (`INSERT 0 1`), and all of SMTP, IMAP and MIME need them.\n- **Numbers from text.** Postgres sends results as text. Integers are easy to hand-roll, but parsing a float with\n  correct rounding is not. The runtime already formats floats exactly (§35.7); nothing reads them back.\n\nWhat stays out (§4): hashes, HMAC, PBKDF2, hex and base64 belong in packages, and TLS is a separate decision.\n\n## 2. Proposal\n\nEvery addition is a method on a built-in type, like `sortBy` and `splitAt` (§36.4, `lib/core/slices.cee`). The\ncompiler's method tables map each name to a `lib/core` function written in CeeSafe. Only float parsing is in the C\nruntime, linked only when used, next to the float formatter. The names become reserved on their types, as the\ninteger methods are (N0228).\n\n### 2.1 Bulk appends on `Vector<T, A>` (§23.1)\n\nFor a Copy `T` (N0259 otherwise):\n\n| Method | Result | What it does |\n|---|---|---|\n| `v.extend(s)` (`s: Slice<T>`) | `Result<void, AllocError>` | Appends all of `s`, with at most one growth. On failure `v` is unchanged |\n| `v.resize(n, fill)` | `Result<void, AllocError>` | Grows to `n` with copies of `fill`, or truncates to `n` |\n| `v.reserve(extra)` | `Result<void, AllocError>` | Makes room for `extra` more elements without changing the length |\n\n- They take `borrow mut this` and are two-phase (B5), like `push`.\n- `v.extend(v.view())` is rejected (N0240), as `s.append(s.view())` is for strings.\n- `String` keeps `append`. It gets no `extend`, because its bytes must stay UTF-8.\n\n### 2.2 Byte order\n\nThere are generic forms over the integer types, so the names don't multiply:\n\n| Method | On | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| `s.readBE<T>(at)`, `s.readLE<T>(at)` | `Slice<u8>`, `StringView` bytes, a vector | `T` |\n| `s.writeBE(at, x)`, `s.writeLE(at, x)` | `SliceMut<u8>`, a mutable vector | `void` |\n| `v.pushBE(x)`, `v.pushLE(x)` | `Vector<u8, A>` | `Result<void, AllocError>` |\n| `x.byteSwap()` | every integer type | `T` |\n\n- `T` is any integer type, and `x`'s type gives the width for writes and pushes. A flexible literal is N0260 (no\n  width), so the code writes `v.pushBE(5u32)` or `v.pushBE(length)`.\n- Each call checks bounds once (`at + size(T) ≤ length`), and panics \"index out of bounds\" at the call o",
    322 =>
        "therwise.\n- Signed types read and write two's complement.\n- The generated C is a `memcpy` and a byte swap where the host's order differs, with no undefined behaviour for\n  unaligned access.\n\nA Postgres message header is then:\n\n```ceesafe\nout.pushBE(0x51u8)?;                          // 'Q'\nlet at = out.length;\nout.pushBE(0u32)?;                            // length, patched below\nout.extend(sql.bytes())?;\nout.pushBE(0u8)?;\nlet length = truncate<u32>(out.length - at);\nout.viewMut().writeBE(at, length);\n```\n\n### 2.3 Bits (§6)\n\nOn every **unsigned** integer type (N0234 on signed, as with the bit operators):\n\n| Method | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `x.rotateLeft(n)`, `x.rotateRight(n)` | `T`; `n: u32`, taken modulo the bit width. Never panics |\n| `x.wrappingShl(n)`, `x.wrappingShr(n)` | `T`; `n` modulo the bit width. This makes `ownership.md` §0 true |\n| `x.leadingZeros()`, `x.trailingZeros()`, `x.countOnes()` | `u32` (the width for `0`) |\n\n### 2.4 Searching and trimming (§22.2, §16.5)\n\nOn `StringView`, with the same methods on `Slice<u8>` (taking and giving `Slice<u8>`):\n\n| Method | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `s.indexOf(needle)`, `s.lastIndexOf(needle)` | `usize?` (an empty needle is found at `0`, and at `length` by `lastIndexOf`) |\n| `s.indexOfByte(b)` | `usize?` |\n| `s.contains(needle)`, `s.startsWith(p)`, `s.endsWith(p)` | `bool` |\n| `s.equalsIgnoreAsciiCase(t)` | `bool`; only `A–Z`/`a–z` fold. SMTP, IMAP and HTTP headers need it |\n| `s.trim()`, `s.trimStart()`, `s.trimEnd()` | the view without ASCII whitespace (space, `\\t`, `\\n`, `\\v`, `\\f`, `\\r`) |\n| `s.splitOnce(sep)` | `TextHalves?`: the parts before and after the first `sep` |\n| `s.split(sep)` | `TextSplit`: `next(borrow mut this) -> StringView?`, the pieces in order |\n\n- `needle`, `p` and `sep` are `StringView` (or `Slice<u8>`). An empty `sep` is a panic (\"empty separator\").\n- **UTF-8.** A match of valid UTF-8 inside valid UTF-8 always starts and ends on character boundaries. So every\n  view these methods return is a valid `StringView`, without further checks.\n- **Loans.** A result borrows `s`, like `s[lo..hi]`. `TextSplit<r>` and `TextHalves<r>` are region structs, like\n  `SliceHalves` in `lib/core/slices.cee`.\n- **Speed.** `indexOfByte` is a word-at-a-time scan. `indexOf` uses `indexOfByte` on the needle's first byte, then\n  compares, which is enough for protocol text. A two-way search can come later, with the same results.\n\n```ceesafe\nvar fields = message.split(\",\");\nwhile (true) {\n    let field = match (fields.next()) { Some(f) => f, null => { break; } };\n    match (field.splitOnce(\"=\")) {\n        Some(kv) => { if (kv.left == \"i\") { iterations = kv.right.parseInt<u32>()?; } }\n        null => {}\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### 2.5 Numbers from text (§6, §35)\n\n| Method | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `s.parseInt<T>()` | `T?`, for any integer type `T` |\n| `s.parseIntRadix<T>(radix)` | `T?`; `radix: u32` from 2 to 36 (panics otherwise); digits `0–9`, `a–z`, `A–Z` |\n| `s.parseFloat<T>()` | `T?`, for `f32` or `f64` |\n\nThese are on `StringView` and on `Slice<u8>`.\n\n- **Integers.** An optional `-` (signed `T` only) or `+`, then one or more digits, with nothing else: no\n  whitespace, no `_`, no `0x`. Overflow is `null`.\n- **Floats.** The grammar is that of the formatter's output plus the common forms:\n  - `[+-]digits[.digits][(e|E)[+-]digits]`, with digits on at least one side of the `.`;\n  - `inf`, `infinity` and `nan` in any case, with an optional sign. Postgres writes `Infinity`, `-Infinity` and `NaN`.\n- **Rounding.** The result is the nearest `T` to the decimal value, with ties to even. Overflow gives ±∞, as IEEE\n  754 and every correct `strtod` do. Underflow gives the nearest subnormal or ±0.\n- **How floats parse.**\n  - A fast path (the Clinger/Eisel-Lemire method) handles nearly every input.\n  - A big-decimal fallback handles the rest, so every input is exact.\n  - It is in `ceesafe_rt_float.c`, independent of the locale (never `strtod`), and linked only if the program\n    parses a float.",
    323 =>
        "\n  - The interpreter does the same over rationals, as it does for formatting (`float_format.cee`).\n  - Under `--no-float`, `parseFloat<f64>` is rejected with N0297, like any other use of a float type (§35.9).\n- **Round-trip.** For every finite `x`, parsing what `Console.writeLine(x)` prints gives `x` back. The tests check\n  this.\n\n## 3. Alternatives considered\n\n- **Named byte-order methods** (`readU32BE`, `writeI16LE`, …): 8 types × 2 orders × read, write and push is 48\n  names, against 6 generic ones. A program that wants the explicit names can wrap the generic ones. **(Question 1.)**\n- **Free functions in a `Bytes` struct** (`Bytes.readBE<u32>(s, at)`) instead of methods: this needs no compiler\n  tables, but it reads worse, and it doesn't match `copyFrom` and `fill`.\n- **A general `Iterator` interface for `split`:** CeeSafe has none yet. `TextSplit.next()` has the shape a future\n  iterator protocol would adopt.\n- **`strtod` for floats:** it depends on the locale, varies by libc, and isn't in the freestanding profiles.\n\n## 4. Not in this ADR\n\n- **Hashes and HMAC, PBKDF2, MD5:** a package (PostgresClient first), in pure CeeSafe. 2.3 gives them what they need.\n- **Hex and base64:** a package. Encoding fits in a few lines on top of 2.1 and 2.2.\n- **TLS:** later, as its own ADR. The one runtime feature it will need is upgrading an existing `AsyncTcpStream` to\n  TLS in place, for STARTTLS. Without that, STARTTLS needs a memory-BIO shim.\n\n## 5. Testing\n\n- **The corpus:** run-pass programs for each method, in the compiled program and in the interpreter. Check-fail\n  programs for:\n  - the signed-type and flexible-literal errors;\n  - `v.extend(v.view())`;\n  - a `Slice<u8>` receiver for `writeBE`.\n- **Integer parsing:** every boundary (`MIN`, `MAX`, `MAX + 1`) for every width, and every malformed form.\n- **Float parsing:**\n  - the round-trip over a few million random `f64`s and every `f32` exponent;\n  - the known-hard inputs, such as halfway cases and the longest exact decimals (the `parse-number-fxx-test-data`\n    set);\n  - the special values.\n- **A compiler check:** reimplementing the compiler's own hand-rolled helpers on the new methods, where it already\n  has them (byte search in the lexer, decimal parsing), is a good check, but not part of this ADR.\n\n## 6. Questions for the owner\n\n1. **Byte order:** generic `readBE<T>` / `writeBE` / `pushBE` (proposed), or named `readU32BE`-style methods?\n2. **Bits:** include `leadingZeros`/`trailingZeros`/`countOnes` (cheap, and wanted by bitsets and allocators), or\n   only rotation and the masking shifts?\n3. **Float parsing in the runtime C:** in `ceesafe_rt_float.c` next to the formatter (proposed, and fastest), or in\n   lib/core CeeSafe (slower to write and run, but no C)?\n4. **Release:** all of it as 0.15.0, or 2.1–2.3 first (the encoder path) and 2.4–2.5 next?\n\n## 7. Implementation and evidence (2026-11-05)\n\n### 7.1 Where it is\n\n- **lib/core:**\n  - `bytes.cee`: `ByteOrder` and `VectorBytes`;\n  - `text.cee`: `TextSearch`, `ByteSearch`, the halves and splits, and a private `ByteScan`;\n  - `numbers.cee`: `NumberParsing` and a private `FloatDecimal`.\n- **The checker:**\n  - `src/check/bytes_text.cee` chooses the lib/core function by name and type;\n  - `vectors.cee` (`extend`, `resize`, `reserve`) and `io.cee` (the bit methods) handle the new methods;\n  - `members.cee` routes them.\n- **Built-ins:** eleven new ones, `bfIntRotateLeft` to `bfVectorReserve`, in the interpreter, the C backend and `rt.h`.\n- **NIR:**\n  - two-phase borrowing for `VectorBytes` calls;\n  - the call-site range checks of byte order (`checkByteRange`).\n\n### 7.2 Changes from the text above\n\n1. **Float parsing is in lib/core, in CeeSafe, not in the C runtime** (question 3's proposed answer).\n   - **Why.** The interpreter needed the same algorithm in CeeSafe anyway (§2.5). One implementation now serves\n     both, with no runtime file to link and no extern call.\n   - **The algorithm.** Clinger's fast path, then the simple",
    324 =>
        " decimal conversion of Go's strconv and Wuffs, both in\n     exact integer arithmetic.\n   - **Accuracy.** Correctly rounded on every test below.\n   - **Speed.** About 50 ns for a short input (`3.14159`) and about 210 ns for a 17-digit one\n     (`0.30000000000000004`), which takes the decimal path. Exponents far from zero cost a few µs. A faster path\n     (Eisel-Lemire) can come later without changing a result.\n2. **No panics in the parsers.** lib/core has no way to panic with a message of its own, so these give a defined\n   result instead:\n   - a radix outside 2–36 is `null`;\n   - an empty separator is found at the start by `splitOnce`, and gives the text whole from `split`.\n3. **Byte order is on fixed-width types only:** not `usize` or `isize` (N0207), since a wire format has a fixed width.\n   - **Receivers.** Byte slices and byte vectors; a `StringView` reads through `.bytes()`.\n   - **Where it panics.** Each read and write checks its range at the call (two NIR `BoundsCheck`s), so the panic is\n     \"index out of bounds\" on the program's line, as §2.2 says. A check inside the library would have named lib/core.\n4. **`VectorBytes` has no allocator parameter.** A vector's allocator is `SystemHeap` or `FailingAllocator` (§23.1),\n   and a generic `Vector<u8, A>` is not a type (N0263). So it has a function for each, and the checker picks one.\n   - **Two-phase.** Its calls take their argument before the vector's loan, as `push` does. The NIR builder\n     recognises the struct by its name, which only lib/core may use.\n5. **`--no-float` does not apply to library files** (type-system.md §35.9). Programs are unaffected:\n   - a program reaches the float parser only through `parseFloat<f32>` or `parseFloat<f64>`, which N0297 still\n     rejects;\n   - unused library functions are not emitted.\n6. **Additions:**\n   - a `String` has the search and parse methods, on its text;\n   - `Vector<u8>` has every byte-slice method, on its elements.\n\n### 7.3 Evidence\n\n- **Float parsing** against exact references:\n  - 3.1 million inputs, from six seeds, with no difference:\n    - random doubles in three formats;\n    - random mantissas of up to 30 digits with exponents from −340 to 320;\n    - exact halfway points and their neighbours;\n    - random `f32`s;\n    - the boundaries (largest subnormal, smallest normal, `f64` and `f32` overflow midpoints, `2^53 + 1`, 400-digit\n      inputs);\n    - malformed text.\n  - **The references:** Python's `float` for `f64`, and exact rational rounding for `f32`.\n  - **The interpreter:** gives the same results on a 3,000-input subset.\n- **Round-trip:** 2 million random `f64`s and 2 million `f32`s, each printed with `appendNumber`, parse back to the\n  same bits.\n- **The corpus:**\n  - **New tests:** five run-pass (`int-bits`, `vector-bulk`, `byte-order`, `text-search`, `number-parsing`), one\n    run-fail (`byte-order-past-end`) and six check-fail. The run-pass tests run compiled and interpreted.\n  - **Changed tests:** `string-views.cee` no longer uses `trim` as its missing method. Four snapshots moved:\n    - the HIR of `async-timeout` and `effects-of`, where only closure numbers changed;\n    - the C of `vectors` and `slot-maps`, which now include the new vector helpers.\n- **Speed** (Apple M-series, `-O2`):\n  - `readBE<u32>`: 52 million calls in a loop take about 40 ms in total, about 1 ns each;\n  - `indexOfByte` scans about 10 GB/s;\n  - `extend` is a `memcpy` after one capacity check.\n",
    325 =>
        "# ADR 0072 — Optionals and equality: `??`, `isSome`/`isNull`, `==` on fieldless enums, slices and arrays\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-05 (\"Accept ADRs\": §6 as proposed: `??` one token, no `valueOr`,\n  equality on integers, `bool` and fieldless enums, one release as 0.16.0). Implemented with the changes listed in\n  §7. Raised by the library work on 0.14–0.15 (sha256, tls, Postgres, mstore, mmime,\n  msmtp, mdns, msieve: `CeeSafeLibs/NOTES-ceesafe-0.14.md` §2, §4, §5, §6, §8, §9), passed on by the owner.\n- Scope: type-system.md §15.1 (enum values), §16.2 and §16.4 (arrays, `for`), §10 (constants), §22.1 (optionals),\n  §9 (`let`); grammar.md §1 (the `??` token) and §3 (its precedence); the checker, NIR lowering, the C backend and the\n  interpreter; guide/structs-and-enums.md, guide/basics.md, guide/unsafe-and-c.md; the corpus.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nThe notes from eight libraries written on 0.14 and 0.15 repeat a handful of points. Each one costs a few lines, but\nthey come up on almost every page:\n\n- **No fallback for an optional** (§2, §5, §6). `StringView.fromUtf8(b) ?? \"fallback\"` has to be a four-line\n  `match`. A generic helper doesn't fix it: `fn orElse<T: Copy>(v: T?, d: T) -> T` cannot take a `StringView`\n  (N0274: a scoped type is not a generic argument), so the libraries carry one helper per type (`textOr`,\n  `numberOr`).\n- **No test for presence as a method** (§6). `o == null` works (§22.1), but `o.isSome()` is N0234. Code coming\n  from C#, TypeScript or Rust writes the method first.\n- **No `==` on fieldless enums** (§5). `kind == RecipientKind.User` is N0207, so each enum gets an `isUser()` per\n  variant, written with `match`. These enums are just tags.\n- **No `==` on slices or arrays** (§7, §8). Tag parsers write `a.length == b.length && a.startsWith(b)`. A 16-byte\n  digest comparison is a loop. `StringView == StringView` already compares bytes (§22.2), so the gap is surprising.\n- **Three small checker gaps** (§8, §9):\n  - `for (i in 0..25) { appendNumber(mut key, i); }`, with `appendNumber(…, n: usize)`, is N0205. Both bounds are\n    flexible literals and the body doesn't index with `i`, so ADR 0063's rule types `i` as `i32`. The notes met\n    this twice in one slice. (`for (i in 0..list.count())`, the first §9 example, already works: the non-literal\n    bound fixes the type. Checked on 0.15.1, 2026-11-05.)\n  - `TABLE.length` for a `const TABLE: [u32; N]` is N0216. The fix is a second constant for the length, or a copy\n    of a 10,406-entry table into a local.\n  - `let _ = f();` is N0100 (\"expected identifier\"). A value that must be bound but isn't needed takes a name.\n- **Three things the guide doesn't say** (§3, §6, §8):\n  - an `extern \"C\"` function without `@effects` counts as possibly sleeping, so calling it from async code is\n    N0295;\n  - the reserved words (`move`, `own`, …) are listed in grammar.md §1.3, but the guide doesn't link to it;\n  - `Console.write` panics on bytes that aren't UTF-8, and `Console.writeBytes` is the answer.\n\n## 2. Proposal\n\n### 2.1 `a ?? b`, the fallback (§22.1)\n\n```ceesafe\nlet name = StringView.fromUtf8(bytes) ?? \"(not UTF-8)\";\nlet port = config.port ?? 5432u16;\nlet first = cache.find(key) ?? store.find(key) ?? fail(\"missing\");\n```\n\n- **Types.** `a` is a `T?`. `b` is checked against `T`, and the whole is a `T`. If `b` is itself a `T?`, the whole\n  is a `T?`, so a chain can end in an optional. A non-optional `a` is N0262, the code `?` and `!` already use for\n  that mistake.\n- **Evaluation.** `a` first. `b` is evaluated only when `a` is `null`. It short-circuits, as `||` does, so\n  `?? fail(\"…\")` (a call of type *never*) is a way to stop with a message.\n- **Ownership.** `a ?? b` consumes `a`, as `match (a)` does: for a non-Copy `T` its payload moves out. Optionals of\n  Copy types, scoped ones like `StringView?` included, are copied. This is the case in all the libraries'\n  examples.\n- **Precedence.** `??` is right-associative (`a ?? ",
    326 =>
        "b ?? c` is `a ?? (b ?? c)`). It binds less tightly than every\n  other binary operator, `||` included, as in C#. Writing `a ?? b || c` needs parentheses, so the parser asks for them\n  (N0100) whenever `??` and `||` or `&&` meet without them.\n- **Token.** `??` becomes one token. In a type, `T??` still means an optional of an optional: the type parser reads\n  the token as two `?`. In an expression, `x??` (propagate twice) must then be written `(x?)?`. No `.cee` file in\n  this repository, in `CeeSafeLibs` or in `CeeSafeApps` writes it (searched 2026-11-05).\n- **Lowering.** The checker lowers it to the `match` it replaces, so NIR, the C backend and the interpreter need\n  nothing new.\n\n### 2.2 `o.isSome()` and `o.isNull()` (§22.1)\n\nTwo methods on every `T?`, both of type `bool`. Like `o == null`, they only read `o`, so a non-Copy optional is\nnot consumed. `isNull` is `o == null`; `isSome` is `o != null`. A method of another name on an optional is still\nN0234, now with a hint naming these two, `??` and `match`.\n\nNo `valueOr(d)` (§3).\n\n### 2.3 `==` and `!=` on fieldless enums (§15.1)\n\n```ceesafe\nenum RecipientKind { User, Alias, List }\nif (kind == RecipientKind.User) { … }\n```\n\n- Defined when both operands have the same enum type and no variant of that enum has a payload. It compares the\n  variant. Each operand is only read.\n- An enum with any payload is still N0207, with a hint to use `match`. Its equality would have to compare payloads,\n  which is an equality interface's job (§4).\n- `<`, `>` and the other orderings stay undefined (N0207): the declaration order of variants isn't an ordering.\n- `Result` and optionals are enums with payloads, so nothing changes for them (`o == null` stays as it is).\n\n### 2.4 `==` and `!=` on slices and arrays (§16.2, §22.2)\n\n```ceesafe\nif (tag == \"BODY[]\".bytes()) { … }\nif (computed == expected) { … }       // [u8; 32] digests\n```\n\n- **Slices.** `a == b` with `a` and `b` both `Slice<T>` (a `SliceMut<T>` or a vector, lent as one, also counts) is\n  true when the lengths are equal and the elements are equal in order. `T` must be an integer type, `bool`, or a\n  fieldless enum (2.3). Floats are N0207: `NaN != NaN` would make a slice unequal to itself. Other element types are\n  N0207 too: their equality is the interface's (§4).\n- **Arrays.** `a == b` with `a` and `b` both `[T; N]`, same `T` and `N`, for the same element types. An array of\n  another length is N0205, as for any type mismatch.\n- **Other combinations.** `Slice<u8> == StringView` is N0205: write `s.bytes() == t` or `s == StringView.fromUtf8(t)`.\n  Each operand is only read.\n- **Cost and constant time.** The C backend compares integer, `bool` and fieldless-enum elements with\n  `ceesafe_mem_equal` (the length check first, then `memcmp` under hosted profiles and a loop otherwise), and the\n  interpreter compares in a loop. The result is not constant-time. The guide says so: a digest comparison against a\n  secret wants a constant-time function, which belongs in a crypto package, not in `==`.\n\n### 2.5 Three checker gaps\n\n1. **Index ranges follow `usize` arguments** (§16.4, extending ADR 0063). When both bounds are flexible literals,\n   the range is also `usize` if the body passes `i` directly as a call argument whose parameter is `usize`. The\n   callee must be known before the body is checked:\n   - a function or static function named directly (`appendNumber(mut key, i)`, `Table.at(i)`);\n   - a method on a receiver whose type is already known: `this`, a parameter, or a local declared before the `for`\n     (`list.host(i)`).\n\n   Like ADR 0063's rule, this is syntactic and decided before the body is checked. A call whose callee can't be\n   resolved then doesn't count, and `i` stays `i32` there, as today.\n2. **`.length` of a constant array** (§10, §16.2). `TABLE.length` is the constant `N` for a `const` array. §16.2\n   requires a place because evaluating the operand might have effects that the constant would skip. A constant has\n   no effec",
    327 =>
        "ts, so the rule doesn't apply to it. Slicing a constant still needs a local or a `static` (§10).\n3. **`let _ = e;`** (§9). `e` is evaluated and its value destroyed at the end of the statement. `_` binds nothing,\n   so it can appear any number of times. It is the explicit way to discard a value, and nothing else changes.\n   `var _` is N0100.\n\n### 2.6 Guide additions (documentation only)\n\n- **guide/unsafe-and-c.md**, beside the async rule: an `extern \"C\"` function without `@effects` is assumed to sleep;\n  `@effects()` or `@effects(alloc)` on the declaration lets async code call it.\n- **guide/basics.md**: a short \"Reserved words\" section that links to grammar.md §1.3 and calls out the ones that\n  look like ordinary names (`move`, `own`, `type`, `namespace`, `macro`, `yield`). It also lists the built-in type\n  names a program can't reuse (`Handle`, `Check`, and lib/core's names), pending the naming ADR (§4).\n- **guide/builtins.md**, at `Console.write`: text must be UTF-8 (it panics otherwise); `Console.writeBytes` writes\n  bytes as they are.\n\n## 3. Alternatives considered\n\n- **`o.valueOr(d)` instead of `??`.** A method evaluates `d` every time, so it can't be `?? fail(\"…\")`, and it costs\n  the default's construction on every call. Having both would be two ways to say the same thing. `??` is what C#\n  and TypeScript programmers already write. **(Question 2.)**\n- **A generic `orElse<T>` in lib/core.** This needs scoped generic arguments first (N0274, §4), and it still\n  evaluates the default eagerly.\n- **`==` through an equality interface now.** It's the general answer, covering structs, payload enums and any\n  element type, but it needs derived implementations and a design for `Self` in interfaces (§21). 2.3 and 2.4 cover\n  the cases the libraries hit. When the interface comes, it will give these types the same meaning, so nothing here\n  has to change.\n- **Defaulting a character literal to `u8` in `pushBE`.** This is already settled in 0.15.2: it is N0265, with a\n  hint (type-system.md §16.6).\n\n## 4. Not in this ADR\n\nEach of these needs its own ADR. These are the notes' remaining items (groups D and E of the triage on\n2026-11-05):\n\n- multi-line or raw string literals, or `embed(\"file\")` (§5; ADR 0064's finding A3);\n- `match` arms that are blocks with a value (§7);\n- temporaries that live to the end of their statement (`f(make().view())`, §8);\n- two-phase borrows for free-function arguments (§9; ADR 0071 has them for `borrow mut this` methods and\n  `VectorBytes`);\n- `async move` children that move their captures out, and a `move` form of `Async.timeout` (§7);\n- a method result that borrows only the struct's region, and region parameters on methods (§6);\n- generics over scoped types and over `void` (§5, §9);\n- built-in names (`Handle`, `Check`) moved into namespaces, and a local shadowing a dependency's name with a\n  warning instead of N0315 (§3, §5, §8);\n- an equality interface (§3 above).\n\n## 5. Testing\n\n- **The corpus:**\n  - run-pass programs for `??` (short-circuit, chains, a non-Copy payload moved out, a `never` fallback),\n    `isSome`/`isNull`, enum, slice and array equality, the new range rule, `const` `.length` and `let _`, each also\n    in the interpreter;\n  - check-fail programs for:\n    - `??` on a non-optional;\n    - `??` mixed with `||` without parentheses;\n    - `==` on an enum with payloads;\n    - `==` on float slices;\n    - `Slice<u8> == StringView`;\n    - arrays of different lengths;\n    - `var _`.\n- **Parsing:** parse-pass snapshots for `??` precedence and for `T??` in types; a parse-fail program for `x??` in an\n  expression, with the `(x?)?` hint.\n- **The notes' own examples:** each example in §1 compiles to what it says, as a corpus program.\n- **The libraries:** after the release, CeeSafeLibs' helpers (`textOr`, the `isUser` family, the digest loops) are\n  replaced and their tests rerun. This is a check of the design, not a gate for the release.\n\n## 6. Questions for the owner\n\n1. **`??` as on",
    328 =>
        "e token.** It makes the expression `x??` (propagate twice) a parse error that needs `(x?)?`. No code\n   in the three repositories writes it. **Proposed: yes.**\n2. **`valueOr(d)` as well as `??`?** **Proposed: no**, one way (§3).\n3. **Slice and array equality element types:** integers, `bool` and fieldless enums only, or `StringView` elements\n   too (comparing their bytes)? **Proposed: the first set.** `StringView` elements can come with the equality\n   interface.\n4. **Release:** everything here as 0.16.0, after 0.15.2 (the bug fixes). **Proposed: yes.**\n\n## 7. Implementation (2026-11-05)\n\n### 7.1 Where\n\n- **`??`:**\n  - `src/syntax/token.cee` and `lexer.cee`: the token `QuestionQuestion`;\n  - `src/syntax/parser.cee`: precedence 0, right-associative, the `(x?)?` hint and the parentheses rule (`startsOperand`,\n    `requireParenthesesAround`), and `T??` in types read as two optionals;\n  - `BinaryOp.Coalesce` in the AST, lowered by `synthCoalesce` (`src/check/options.cee`) to\n    `{ let coalesced = a; if (coalesced == null) b else coalesced! }`, so NIR, the C backend and the interpreter are\n    unchanged.\n- **`isSome` and `isNull`:** `checkOptionalMethod` (`src/check/members.cee`), as `IsNull`, the HIR of `o == null`.\n- **Equality:**\n  - `src/check/equality.cee` (`checkEquality`): a vector operand is lent as its elements;\n  - NIR compares fieldless enums' tags (`enumTag`, `src/nir/nir_builder.cee`);\n  - the C backend calls `cee_mem_eq` (arrays) and `cee_slice_eq` (slices) from `rt.h`;\n  - the interpreter compares the bytes.\n- **The checker gaps:**\n  - `passesAsUsize` (`src/check/range_index.cee`);\n  - `isConstantArray` (`src/check/arrays.cee`);\n  - `let _` parsed as a `let` named `_`, and checked as a discarded expression (`src/check/body.cee`).\n- **Spec and guide:**\n  - grammar.md §1.7, §2 and §2.1; type-system.md §3, §8, §10, §15.1, §16.2, §16.4 and §22.1; diagnostics.md\n    (N0207, N0234, N0262);\n  - guide/basics.md, guide/structs-and-enums.md, guide/builtins.md and guide/unsafe-and-c.md; templates/AGENTS.md.\n- **Tests:**\n  - run-pass `optional-coalesce`, `optional-methods`, `enum-slice-equality`, `checker-gaps-0072` and `coalesce-types`;\n  - check-fail `optionals-equality-errors`; parse-fail `coalesce-syntax`.\n\n  The expected outputs were checked by hand against the programs. The compiled programs and the interpreter agree.\n\n### 7.2 Changes from the accepted text\n\n1. **Arrays of different lengths** are N0207 (\"requires operands of the same type\"), the code every other operator\n   uses for mismatched operand types, not N0205.\n2. **`ceesafe_mem_equal` became `cee_mem_eq`,** a byte loop in `rt.h` like `ceesafe_str_eq`, not `memcmp`. It has no\n   profile split, and C compilers recognise the loop.\n3. **`let _: T = e`** is N0100, like `var _` (§2.5 only named `var _`).\n",
    329 =>
        "# ADR 0073 — Temporaries as receivers, two-phase `mut` arguments, block values in branches, and a first warning\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-05 (\"Accept the ADR as proposed\": §6 as proposed: temporaries for\n  `borrow this` and `borrow mut this` receivers, two-phase reservations allow reads only, `--deny-warnings` on\n  `check`, `build` and `test`, the B5 order fix in this release with a release note, one release as 0.17.0). Implemented with the changes listed in §7. Raised by the mail server port on 0.15 (`CeeSafeApps/mailserver/CEESAFE_FINDINGS.md`\n  §2.2, §3.2, §3.3, §3.6; the running log is `CeeSafeLibs/NOTES-ceesafe-0.14.md`), passed on by the owner (\"Draft the\n  ADR for §3.2, §3.3, §3.6 and §2.2\"). Each finding was reproduced with ceesafec 0.16.1 on 2026-11-05.\n- Scope: type-system.md §13.2 (creating references), §15.3 (`if` and `match` expressions), §16.2 (slicing),\n  §14.5 (destruction of temporaries); lifetimes.md §5 (B5) and §6 (temporaries); ownership.md B5; language-spec.md\n  (evaluation order); grammar.md §1.6 (escapes) and the `Branch` rule; diagnostics.md (a new warnings range);\n  tooling.md (warnings in the human, JSON, LSP and MCP output); the checker, NIR lowering, the interpreter, `fmt`;\n  guide/for-agents.md, templates/AGENTS.md; the corpus.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nThe mail server port is about 25,000 lines across PostgresClient, tls, the crypto packages and eight mail packages.\nAfter 0.16.0 and 0.16.1, four of its open findings come up most often while writing code. Three of them cost a line\nor a helper each time. The fourth produces a wrong program without a word.\n\n- **A temporary cannot be a receiver** (§3.2). Passing a temporary to a `borrow` parameter already works\n  (§13.2: \"a value becomes a temporary that lives until the end of the statement\"), so `useb(make())` compiles. But\n  `make().view()` is N0247 (\"only a variable, a field, or a place reached through a reference can be borrowed\"), and\n  so is `use(make().view())`. The receiver of a `borrow this` method has to be a place. The same rule refuses\n  `make()[0..1]` (§16.2) and `table().length` (N0216). The notes say it \"happens constantly in tests and string\n  building\", and every case is the same two lines: `let tmp = make(); use(tmp.view());`.\n- **No two-phase borrows for `mut` arguments** (§3.3). B5 (lifetimes.md §5) lets a `borrow mut this` method's\n  arguments read the receiver (`v.push(v.length)`), but a free function's `mut` argument gets no such allowance:\n  `fail(mut s, s.count + 1)` is N0242 (\"cannot use 's.count' while 's' is mutably borrowed\"), although `s.count` is\n  a `u32` copied before the call. msieve carries a separate `Problem` struct to work around it.\n- **A `match` or `if` branch can't be a block that gives a value** (§3.6). §15.3 allows a block branch only if it\n  cannot complete, and says \"a block that ends in a value, as in Rust, is not part of G5\". So\n  `Some(v) => { let w = v + 1; w }` is N0100. The workaround is a `var` assigned in every arm, or a helper function.\n- **`\"x = {x}\"` prints `x = {x}`** (§2.2). CeeSafe has no string interpolation. C# and TypeScript habits write\n  it anyway, and nothing says so. It is the only one of the four that produces a wrong program rather than an error.\n\nReproducing §3.3 turned up a bug in B5 as implemented. `nir_builder.cee` lowers the other arguments first and the\nreceiver's place last, so an argument that changes an index in the receiver's place changes which element is\nborrowed:\n\n```ceesafe\nvar ps = [P { v: 0 }, P { v: 0 }];\nvar i: usize = 0;\nps[i].set(bump(mut i));   // bump: i += 1, returns 9. Writes ps[1]: the receiver's index is read after the argument.\n```\n\nlanguage-spec.md requires \"strictly left to right, receiver before arguments\", so `ps[0]` is the right answer.\nlifetimes.md §5 justifies B5 with \"evaluating a place has no side effects, so creating its loan later does not change\nwhat the program computes\". That holds for the loan, but not fo",
    330 =>
        "r the place's index operands, which are evaluated\nwith it.\n\n## 2. Proposal\n\n### 2.1 A temporary as a receiver, a slice base or a `.length` operand (§13.2, §16.2)\n\nA **receiver** of a `borrow this` or `borrow mut this` method that is not a place is evaluated into a temporary, and\nthe receiver borrows the temporary. So is the **base of a slice** (`e[lo..hi]`), and the **operand of `.length`** on\nan array. It is the rule §13.2 already applies to a value passed to a `borrow T` parameter:\n\n```ceesafe\nuse(make().view());                          // the String lives until the end of the statement\nlet n = make().view().length;                // a usize, copied out: nothing borrows the temporary afterwards\nlet first = text.split(\",\").next();          // a borrow mut this method on a temporary TextSplit\nlet head = digest(data)[0..4];               // a slice of a temporary [u8; 32]\nlet count = table().length;                  // an array result: its length is N; the call still runs\n```\n\n- **Lifetime.** The temporary lives until the end of its statement (§14.5). In an `if` or `while` condition it lives\n  until the condition has been evaluated, and in a branch of an `if` or `match` expression until the branch ends\n  (§15.3), exactly as today's temporaries do. It is destroyed then, `drop` included.\n- **A result that borrows it.** A result derived from the temporary (`make().view()`) can be used inside the\n  statement. Kept in a `let` that is used after the statement, it is N0243 (\"temporary dropped while still\n  borrowed\"), the error `same(five())` already gives, with a note naming the temporary and suggesting a local.\n- **Field paths.** A field path on a temporary is a place of that temporary: `make().name.view()` borrows the\n  temporary's `name`.\n- **`borrow mut this` receivers** are included. The receiver has no marker at the call site, and the common case\n  is an iterator or a builder made for that one call (`text.split(\",\").next()`). **(Question 1.)**\n- **Unchanged:**\n  - An explicit `borrow e` or `mut e` of a non-place is still N0247 (`borrow 5`, `take(mut five())`). The `mut`\n    marker says the caller will see the change, and a temporary's change can't be seen. An explicit `borrow` of a\n    value adds nothing: pass the value, or bind it.\n  - N0244 is unchanged: a function cannot return a reference to its temporary.\n\n### 2.2 Two-phase `mut` arguments, and B5 evaluated left to right (lifetimes.md §5, ownership.md B5)\n\n**Every** argument written `mut p`, with `p` a place, and every `borrow mut` reference local passed on to a `borrow\nmut` parameter (a reborrow, B4), is **reserved** where it stands and **activated** at the call. Today that is true\nonly of a `borrow mut this` receiver (B5). The rule covers functions, static functions, methods' other arguments,\nclosure and `dyn fn` calls alike:\n\n```ceesafe\nfail(mut s, s.count + 1);           // was N0242\nrecord(mut log, log.entries.length); // was N0242\n```\n\n- **Evaluation stays left to right.** The place's own operands, the index expressions in `mut a[i].f`, are evaluated\n  where the argument stands. The bounds check happens there too, and the values go into temporaries. Only the\n  creation of the loan moves to the call. B5's receivers follow the same rule. This fixes `ps[i].set(bump(mut i))`\n  (§1), which now writes `ps[0]`, as language-spec.md requires.\n- **While reserved, the place can only be read.** Between its argument and the call, the reserved place behaves as\n  if it were borrowed shared:\n  - **Allowed:** a read of an overlapping place, a copy (`s.count`) or a shared borrow that ends before the call\n    (`s.name.view().length`).\n  - **N0241**, with a note \"reserved here for the `mut` argument (l:c)\":\n    - a shared borrow still live at the call, such as `f(mut s, s.name.view())` or `f(mut s, borrow s.inner)`;\n    - a `borrow mut` of an overlapping place, such as `f(mut s, g(mut s))`;\n    - a second `mut` argument for an overlapping place.\n  - **N0240:** a write to an over",
    331 =>
        "lapping place.\n  - **N0251:** a move out of one, as for any use of a borrowed place.\n- **Why it is sound.** No mutable loan exists until the call. Each read finishes, or its shared loan ends, before\n  the loan is created. The place denotes the same storage at the call as at the argument: its index values were\n  fixed at the argument, and while it is reserved, nothing can write to it or to any reference it passes through.\n- **Not covered.** A *mutable* use of the place in a later argument stays an error. That includes PostgresClient's\n  `run(mut connection, await connection.batch(sql))`, which the notes list beside §3.3. Write\n  `let rows = await connection.batch(sql); run(mut connection, rows);`. **(Question 2.)**\n\n### 2.3 A block branch may end in its value (§15.3)\n\nA block branch of an `if` or `match` **expression** may end with an expression written without `;`. That expression\nis the branch's value:\n\n```ceesafe\nlet x = match (y) {\n    Some(v) => {\n        let w = v + 1;\n        w\n    }\n    null => 0,\n};\nlet label = if (n == 1) \"one\" else {\n    let rest = describe(n);\n    rest.kind\n};\n```\n\n```text\nBranch     = Expr | Block | ValueBlock\nValueBlock = '{' Statement* Expr '}'       (* the final Expr has no ';' *)\n```\n\n- **Where.** Only in the branches of `if` and `match` *expressions* (§15.3). Function bodies, the statement forms of\n  `if` and `match`, and plain blocks keep their meaning. A function returns with `return` or `=>`, as now. In a\n  statement-form `match`, a final expression without `;` is still N0100. Its hint now says that a statement-form\n  `match` has no value, and that `let x = match …` makes it an expression.\n- **The final expression.**\n  - **Typing.** It is the branch's expression for typing: it is checked against the expected type, or it fixes the\n    type of the whole, exactly as an expression branch does today.\n  - **`if` or `match` at the end.** One that ends a value block, directly before `}`, is read as an expression.\n    `{ let w = f(); if (w > 0) 1 else 2 }` works. Today a branch block that ends in a statement-form `if` must\n    already be unable to complete, and read as an expression it still can't, so existing programs keep their\n    meaning.\n  - **A block that can complete with no final expression** is still N0235. Its hint now says \"end the block with its\n    value, written without `;`\".\n- **Ownership.**\n  - The value is moved (or copied) out of the block before the block's locals and temporaries are destroyed.\n  - A reference to a block local or temporary cannot be the value: N0243, as for an expression branch's temporary\n    (§15.3).\n  - A `return`, `break` or `continue` before the final expression is allowed, as in any block.\n- **Formatting.** `ceesafec fmt` writes a value block's statements and its value one per line, the value last, and\n  no comma after the block (§15.3: a block arm needs none).\n\n### 2.4 The first warning: `{name}` in a string literal (N0400)\n\n```text\nmain.cee:3:23: warning[N0400]: this string prints '{x}' as written: CeeSafe has no string interpolation\n  |\n3 |     Console.writeLine(\"x = {x}\");\n  |                       ^^^^^^^^^\n  = help: write the text and the value in two calls: Console.write(\"x = \"); Console.writeLine(x);\n  = help: to mean the braces, write \"x = \\{x}\"\n```\n\n- **When.** A string literal's source text contains `{` *name* (`.` *name*)* `}`, the first *name* is bound where the\n  literal stands (a local, a parameter, `this`, or a constant), and the `{` is not escaped.\n  - `\\u{…}` escapes are not candidates.\n  - A name that is not bound is not a candidate. `\"^{commit}\"` in `src/packages/workspace.cee`, a git revision\n    suffix, doesn't warn.\n  - A search on 2026-11-05 across `CeeSafe`, `CeeSafeLibs` and `CeeSafeApps` found 18 matches of `{name}` inside\n    double quotes:\n    - `\\u{…}` escapes;\n    - `${NAME}` and `{x}` in the embedded documentation (`src/library/documents.cee`);\n    - `\"^{commit}\"`;\n    - examples inside comments.\n\n    None of them n",
    332 =>
        "ames a binding where it stands, so no existing code should warn. The implementation confirms\n    this by running over the three repositories (§5).\n- **`\\{` and `\\}`, two new escapes** (grammar.md §1.6), mean the characters `{` and `}`. Writing `\\{` is how a\n  program says it means the braces, and it silences N0400. No other way to silence it is added.\n- **Warnings in general.** This is the first warning the compiler emits. diagnostics.md gains a range for them,\n  N0400–N0499 (\"Warnings\"), and these rules:\n  - **Exit status.** A warning doesn't stop `check`, `build`, `run`, `test` or `fix`, and doesn't change the exit\n    status. **(Question 3.)**\n  - **Output.**\n    - In the human format, a warning is written `warning[N0400]`.\n    - In the JSON document (tooling.md), it has `\"severity\": \"warning\"` and is counted in `\"warnings\"`; the field\n      already exists and is always 0 today.\n    - The language server sends it with severity Warning.\n    - MCP `ceesafe_check` lists it.\n  - **Which files.** Warnings are reported only for the root package's files, not for dependencies, lib/core or\n    lib/std, so a library's warnings never fall on its users.\n  - **Checklists.** templates/AGENTS.md and the guide's checklist say \"`ceesafec check` reports no errors and no\n    warnings\".\n- **Not an error.** Text with literal braces is legitimate: route templates, git revision syntax, documentation. An\n  error would break programs that are correct. A warning that fires only when the name is in scope catches the\n  mistake with no false alarms in the code we have.\n\n### 2.5 Guide and template changes\n\n- **guide/for-agents.md:**\n  - \"A temporary is not sliced or measured\" (which 0.16.0 rewrote to `table().length`) is removed. Its example now\n    compiles.\n  - A new pitfall shows `\"x = {x}\"` and N0400.\n  - The \"Arrays of structs\" example is unchanged.\n- **templates/AGENTS.md** (\"Common mistakes\"):\n  - \"Bind a constant or a temporary to a local before slicing it or asking its `.length`\" becomes \"A view of a\n    temporary lasts until the end of the statement: bind the value to a local to keep the view\".\n  - A line says that there is no string interpolation.\n- **guide/structs-and-enums.md**, at `if` and `match` expressions: value blocks.\n\n## 3. Alternatives considered\n\n- **Extend a temporary's life to the enclosing block when a `let` keeps a view of it**, as Rust does for `let r =\n  &make();`. This would make `let v = make().view(); use(v);` compile. Rust limits the rule to syntactic cases,\n  and it surprises people at the edges: `let r = make().view();` is extended, `let r = id(make().view());` is not.\n  CeeSafe's rule stays one sentence: a temporary ends with its statement. The notes' cases all finish in one\n  statement.\n- **Two-phase for every use that ends before the call, mutable ones included** (§2.2, Question 2). This would\n  accept `run(mut connection, await connection.batch(sql))`. It is sound once index values are fixed at the\n  argument, but `f(mut s, g(mut s))` then runs `g`'s change to `s` before `f` sees `s`. In a C#-like language,\n  readers expect the arguments to be independent there. Rust refuses it too. If the libraries keep meeting it, this\n  can be widened later without breaking anything.\n- **Block values everywhere** (a function body or any block ending in an expression, as in Rust). CeeSafe already\n  has `=> expr` bodies and explicit `return`. A body whose value is its last line would be a second way to return,\n  and a missing `;` would change what a function returns. Keeping it to branches, where §15.3 already makes blocks\n  special, gives the notes' case and nothing else.\n- **A `yield` or `=>` keyword for a block's value** (`{ let w = v + 1; yield w; }`). It's explicit, but `yield`\n  is reserved for generators (grammar.md §1.3), and `=> w` inside a block reads like an arm. The final-expression\n  form is what C#'s switch-expression users and Rust users already expect.\n- **String interpolation** (`$\"x = {x}\"`). It is the rea",
    333 =>
        "l answer to §2.2, but it needs a design for where the text\n  goes (a `TextWriter`, an allocating `String`), for formats (`{x:x8}`) and for scoped values. That is an ADR of its\n  own. N0400 is right with or without it: an interpolated string would carry a prefix, and a plain literal would\n  still not interpolate.\n- **Making N0400 an error.** See §2.4, \"Not an error\".\n\n## 4. Not in this ADR\n\nThese are the mail server's other open findings. Each needs its own ADR:\n\n- generics over `void` (§3.7);\n- async children and `Async.timeout` that move their captures out (§3.9);\n- region parameters on methods, a method result tied to the struct's region only, and `var` views (§3.10);\n- multi-line or raw string literals, `embed(\"file\")`, and constants in structs (§3.11);\n- an optional field passed as an optional borrow (§3.12);\n- `for` over a type with `next() -> T?` (§3.13);\n- `as` from `usize` to `i64` where the value range allows it (§3.14);\n- a local shadowing a dependency's name (§4);\n- a never-returning type for functions such as `die()` (§4.1);\n- string interpolation (§3 above).\n\n## 5. Testing\n\n- **Temporaries (§2.1):**\n  - run-pass programs for:\n    - a `borrow this` method on a call result, a field of one, and a chained call;\n    - a `borrow mut this` method on a temporary iterator;\n    - slicing a temporary vector or array;\n    - `.length` of an array result;\n    - a temporary with a `drop` block destroyed at the end of the statement, its output showing the order;\n    - the same inside a condition and inside a branch;\n\n    each also in the interpreter, and with `--check-leaks`.\n  - check-fail programs for:\n    - a view of a temporary kept in a `let` and used after the statement (N0243);\n    - `borrow five()` and `take(mut five())` (still N0247).\n  - `check-fail/arrays.cee` (`make()[0..1]`) and `check-fail/region-types.cee` (`Scratch.enter().alloc(1)`) expect\n    N0247 today. They become N0243, with a use after the statement, or move to run-pass.\n- **Two-phase (§2.2):**\n  - run-pass:\n    - the notes' `fail(mut s, s.n[0])`;\n    - a `mut` argument followed by a read of the same place through a field;\n    - a reborrowed `borrow mut` local;\n    - `ps[i].set(bump(mut i))` printing `9` then `0` (it prints `0` then `9` today); compiled and interpreted.\n  - check-fail:\n    - a shared borrow live at the call;\n    - a second `mut` of an overlapping place;\n    - a write, a move and a `borrow mut` during the reservation (N0241, N0240, N0251).\n  - The mutation suite and the fault and cancellation sweeps run unchanged, and the fixed point checks that the\n    compiler's own code (which uses B5 heavily) compiles to the same behaviour.\n- **Block values (§2.3):**\n  - run-pass: value blocks in `match` and `if` expressions; a value block whose last item is an `if` expression; a\n    non-Copy value moved out of a block; a `return` inside a value block. Each also in the interpreter.\n  - parse-fail: a final expression without `;` in a statement-form `match` (N0100, the new hint).\n  - check-fail: a reference to a block local as the value (N0243); a completing block with no final expression\n    (N0235, the new hint). `check-fail/branch-expressions.cee` keeps its N0235 line.\n  - `fmt`: snapshots of value blocks.\n- **N0400 (§2.4):**\n  - a check program with every candidate shape:\n    - bound and unbound names;\n    - `{a.b}`;\n    - `\\{x}` and `\\u{41}`;\n    - a constant;\n    - a literal outside `Console.write`;\n\n    with the expected warnings. `run_corpus.py` learns `// warning[N0400]` expectations, and a warning-only program\n    must still run.\n  - The JSON, LSP and MCP outputs: one test each, through `tools/test_command_check.py` and `mcp_api_check.py`.\n  - A dependency's warning isn't reported: a package test in `tools/package_tests.py`.\n  - The compiler, CeeSafeLibs and CeeSafeApps produce no N0400. This checks the binding rule against real code.\n- **The libraries:** after the release, msieve's `Problem` struct, the `let tmp = …` lines the ",
    334 =>
        "notes count, and the\n  `var`-per-arm matches are simplified, and their tests rerun. This is a check of the design, not a gate.\n\n## 6. Questions for the owner\n\n1. **Temporaries for `borrow mut this` receivers** (`text.split(\",\").next()`), or only for `borrow this`? The risk\n   is `make().append(\"x\")!`, a change made to a value that is then destroyed. **Proposed: both.** The receiver has\n   no `mut` marker that promises the caller will see a change, and the iterator case is common.\n2. **Two-phase: reads only, or any use that ends before the call?** Reads only means\n   `run(mut c, await c.batch(sql))` keeps needing a local. **Proposed: reads only** (§3), as Rust does; it can be\n   widened later.\n3. **A flag to make warnings errors**, `--deny-warnings` on `check`, `build` and `test`, for CI? **Proposed: yes**,\n   with the default as in §2.4: warnings never change the exit status unless asked.\n4. **The B5 order fix** (§1) changes what `ps[i].set(bump(mut i))` does. It is a bug against language-spec.md, but\n   a program could depend on it. **Proposed:** fix it in this release, and say so in the release notes. No program\n   in the three repositories passes a `mut` index variable to an argument of a call on the indexed receiver\n   (to be confirmed with a search before the release).\n5. **Release:** everything here as 0.17.0. **Proposed: yes.**\n\n## 7. Implementation (2026-11-05)\n\n### 7.1 Where\n\n- **Temporaries (§2.1):**\n  - `borrowReceiver` (`src/check/places.cee`) borrows a receiver that is not a place as a statement temporary:\n    `BorrowTemp` for a value, `BorrowPath` for a field or owned contents of one (NIR lowering spills the root);\n  - it is used for the built-in receivers (`src/check/vectors.cee`, `bytes_text.cee`, `containers.cee`, `io.cee`,\n    `library.cee`, `regions.cee`) and for `borrow mut this` user methods (`members.cee`); `borrow this` user methods\n    already took a temporary;\n  - slicing a temporary is the new `HirExpr.SliceTemp` (`src/check/arrays.cee`, lowered by `lowerSliceAt` in\n    `src/nir/nir_builder.cee`); `.length` of an array value is `SliceLength(SliceTemp(…))`, so the value is still\n    evaluated.\n- **Two-phase arguments (§2.2):**\n  - `lowerTwoPhaseOperands` (`src/nir/nir_builder.cee`) lowers the arguments of every call, method call, closure\n    call and function-value call;\n  - a `mut p` argument, or a reborrowed `borrow mut` local, has its place lowered where it stands (indexes copied and\n    bounds-checked there), and its `Ref` made after the last argument;\n  - a reservation is a shared `Ref` of the place, held in a temporary that is used just before the activations, so\n    the borrow checker enforces the reads-only rule with its existing errors;\n  - reads of a reserved variable, or reads through a reference, in later arguments are copied where they stand.\n- **Value blocks (§2.3):**\n  - `StmtNode.Value` (`src/syntax/ast.cee`) is made by `parseBlockOf(true)` for branch blocks\n    (`src/syntax/parser.cee`), which decides with a balanced token scan (`endsValueBlock`) whether an ending `if` or\n    `match` is the value;\n  - `checkValueBlock` (`src/check/patterns.cee`) checks the block in its scope, and the value becomes the\n    `HirStmt.Yield` that an expression branch already lowered to;\n  - the statement walkers (closures' captures, `range_index`, the formatter, the AST printer) were given the new\n    statement.\n- **N0400 (§2.4):**\n  - `warnStringBraces` (`src/check/string_braces.cee`) is a syntactic pass over the root package's files, run once\n    after the bodies;\n  - `Diagnostics.warning` and `denyWarnings` (`src/diagnostics/diagnostic.cee`);\n  - `--deny-warnings` and the warnings printed by `build`, `run` and `test` (`src/main.cee`);\n  - `\\{` and `\\}` (`src/syntax/lexer.cee`, the VS Code grammar);\n  - the \"Warnings\" section of `diagnostics.md`;\n  - `run_corpus.py` accepts `// warning[N0400]`, and `guide_check.py` a `warns` marker.\n- **Spec and guide:**\n  - type-system.md §13.2, §15.3, §16.2",
    335 =>
        "; grammar.md (`Branch`); lifetimes.md §5, §6; ownership.md B5; tooling.md §5.1;\n    diagnostics.md;\n  - guide/for-agents.md, guide/basics.md, templates/AGENTS.md.\n- **Tests:**\n  - run-pass `temporaries-as-receivers`, `two-phase-mut-arguments`, `value-blocks`;\n  - check-pass `string-braces-warning`;\n  - check-fail `temporary-view-kept`, `two-phase-errors`, `value-block-local-view`;\n  - parse-fail `value-block-statement`;\n  - `check-fail/arrays.cee` no longer expects N0216 and N0247 for `make().length` and `make()[0..1]`.\n\n  The run-pass programs pass compiled, in the interpreter and with `--check-leaks`. `ps[i].set(bump(mut i))` prints\n  `9` then `0`, where 0.16.1 printed `0` then `9`.\n\n### 7.2 Changes from the accepted text\n\n1. **Receivers keep B5's rule.** 0.16.1 accepts `v.push(v.pop()!)` and `p.set(bumpP(mut p))`, a mutable use of the\n   receiver in a later argument. So the reads-only reservation (§2.2) applies to `mut p` arguments and reborrows,\n   not to receivers. Receivers get only the left-to-right fix. Nothing that compiled before is refused.\n2. **A reservation is made only when a later argument does more than read** (a call, a borrow, a move, a closure).\n   Reads and arithmetic cannot change the place, and a read through a reference to it conflicts at the activation\n   anyway. This keeps the generated C of `step(mut c, i)` unchanged.\n3. **The reservation errors are the borrow checker's own:**\n   - N0241 for a mutable borrow and for a shared borrow live at the call, with the note \"borrowed here\" at the `mut`\n     argument, not \"reserved here\";\n   - a move in a later argument (`takes(mut s, s)`) is N0250, a use after the move, rather than N0251.\n4. **Slicing a temporary** covers array values and view or slice values (`s.view()[1..3]`, which was N0247 too). A\n   vector or `String` value is sliced through `.view()`. `for (x in make())` is unchanged: its view must outlive the\n   statement.\n5. **The §2.1 example `let head = digest(data)[0..4];`** is N0243 when `head` is used afterwards. That is the rule\n   working: a slice of an array temporary ends with its statement. A slice of a view (`s.view()[1..3]`) can be kept.\n6. **N0400's text.** Notes print as `= note: … (line:col)`, so the advice is in the message:\n   `this string prints '{x}' as written (there is no string interpolation): write the value in a call of its own, or\n   '\\{' to mean the brace`. There is no separate `help` line.\n7. **The search in §2.4 missed one.** `src/packages/workspace.cee` has `\"^{commit}\"` in `hasCommit`, whose parameter\n   is named `commit`, so it warns. The compiler's source must still build with stage 0 (0.3.2), which rejects `\\{`,\n   so it is now written as two appends, `\"^{\"` and `\"commit}\"`.\n8. **Frames** (`Scratch.enter().alloc(1)`) stay N0247: a frame must be a local (type-system.md §19.5), a rule of its\n   own.\n9. **`guide_check.py`** gains a `warns N0400` marker. It is `fail` for warnings, but the check must succeed.\n",
    336 =>
        "# ADR 0074 — The rest of the port's findings: unit payloads, `never`, run-once captures, method regions, optional borrows, raw literals, associated consts, iterator loops, integer conversions, shadowing a dependency\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-05 (\"Take over ~/dev/CeeSafe session and fix all those\"; §6 answered as\n  proposed: one release as 0.18.0, deep results with the new callee rule, C#-style raw `\"\"\"` literals, N0401 on an\n  ambiguous shadowed member). Raised by the mail server port (`CeeSafeApps/mailserver/CEESAFE_FINDINGS.md`\n  §3.7, §3.9, §3.10, §3.11, §3.12, §3.13, §3.14, §4, §4.1). The owner asked to fix every finding still open in\n  0.17.0. Each was reproduced with ceesafec 0.17.0 on 2026-11-05 (the findings' status table).\n- Scope: type-system.md §1, §7, §17, §20.4, §22.1, §25.1, §25.6, §36.5, §40.4–§40.5, the `for` statement; lifetimes.md\n  §7 (elision); grammar.md §1.6 (string literals) and the struct and impl bodies; diagnostics.md; the lexer, parser,\n  checker, NIR lowering, drop elaboration, C backend, interpreter, formatter, VS Code grammar and semantic index;\n  guide/for-agents.md, templates/AGENTS.md; the corpus.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n0.16 and 0.17 fixed 14 of the port's 24 findings. Nine are still open. Each costs the port a helper, a rename or a\nworkaround structure:\n\n| Finding | Today | The port's workaround |\n|---|---|---|\n| §3.7 a generic used with `void` | N0222: `Ok(v)` binds a payload that `Result<void, E>` lacks | a second, void-only adapter per generic (msmtp `oom`/`stored`, mqueue `grown`) |\n| §3.9 `async move` cannot consume a capture | N0251 for any non-Copy capture, ADR 0053's own `async move () => await serve(stream)` included | the value travels in a one-element `Vector` that the child pops; tls gained `acceptWithin` |\n| §3.10 method region parameters; a `borrow mut this` result | N0216; the result keeps the `this` loan (N0241 on the next call) | free functions, lexers that return offsets |\n| §3.11 multi-line literals; consts in structs | N0005; N0107 | SQL in `.sql` files and a generator; `OID_…` top-level names |\n| §3.12 an optional field as `borrow T?` | N0205 | always hold a configuration, pass a `useTls` flag |\n| §3.13 `for` over `split` | N0205 | `loop { match (parts.next()) { … } }` |\n| §3.14 `usize` to `i64` | N0208, and `checkedTruncate` is float-only | `truncate<i64>(n)`, which reads as lossy |\n| §4 a dependency's name on a local | N0315 for every local, parameter and binding | renamed the `files` library to `diskio`; parameter names other than `tls` |\n| §4.1 a function that never returns | `never` is not nameable (N0204); a `die()` wrapper in a value match is N0235 | a dead `return` after each call |\n\nMost of the machinery exists: `TypeKind.Never` and divergence (`src/types/types.cee:12`, `src/check/body.cee:684`),\ndrop flags and partial moves (`src/nir/drops.cee`, `src/nir/move_paths.cee`), method region scopes\n(`src/check/regions.cee:23`), header temporaries for `for` (`src/nir/nir_builder.cee:1109`).\n\n## 2. Proposal\n\n### 2.1 Unit payloads: `Ok(v)` with `T = void` (§20.4)\n\nWhere a variant's payload type is `void`, a pattern may bind one name to it and a construction may pass one argument\nof type `void`:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn conv<T>(r: Result<T, A>) -> Result<T, B> => match (r) { Ok(v) => Ok(v), Err(_) => Err(B.Y) };\nconv(act())   // act() -> Result<void, A>: now accepted\n```\n\n- A name bound to a unit payload is a **unit local**: it has type `void` and holds nothing. Reading it is a unit\n  value. `let v = r?;` with `r: Result<void, E>` declares one too.\n- A unit value may be passed where `void` is expected: a void payload, `return v` in a void function (it is\n  `return;`), an expression statement.\n- The checker erases unit values. A unit local becomes no HIR local, a unit read becomes no operand, and a\n  construction `Ok(e)` with a unit `e` keeps `e`'s effects and builds the payload-less variant. NIR, the C backend\n  and the interprete",
    337 =>
        "r are unchanged.\n- This holds in generic instances and in ordinary code, so a generic body means the same written out by hand.\n- Not included: user generic enums instantiated at `void` (N0226), `Some(v)` with `void`.\n\n### 2.2 `never`, a return type (§1, §15.3)\n\n`never` names the existing bottom type, as a function's return type only:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn die(message: StringView) -> never { Console.writeLine(message); Process.exit(1); }\nlet n = match (parse(text)) { Ok(v) => v, Err(_) => { die(\"bad number\"); } };   // accepted\n```\n\n- A `never` function's end must be unreachable (N0213), and `return` in it is an error.\n- A call of one diverges, as `Process.exit` does: the code after it is unreachable, and a branch ending in it gives no\n  value and needs none.\n- Anywhere else (a parameter, a field, a local, a type argument, a function type, an async function, a closure, an\n  interface method, an `extern` function) `never` is N0226: \"'never' can only be a function's return type\".\n- In C, the function is `_Noreturn void`. In NIR, a call of one ends its block with `Unreachable`.\n- `never` is a type name, not a keyword: values named `never` (lib/std's `channel.cee:60`) are unaffected.\n\n### 2.3 Run-once environments: a child body consumes its captures (§36.5, §40.5, ADR 0053)\n\nA move environment whose body runs at most once is **run-once**. Its body may move each capture out, once:\n\n```ceesafe\ns.spawn(async move () => await serve(stream));                       // stream: AsyncTcpStream, moved into serve\nlet r = await Async.timeout(ms, E.Timeout, async move () => await TlsStream.accept(stream, cfg));\n```\n\n- Run-once bodies:\n  - `s.spawn`;\n  - `set.spawn`, `spawnSend` and `trySpawn`;\n  - the bodies of `Async.timeout`, `deadline`, `race` and `all`, which may now be `async move`. Today they are\n    N0209.\n  - Not `Box<dyn fn>` move literals, which may be called again. They keep N0251.\n- The environment's captures become move paths under `*env` in a run-once function (`src/nir/move_paths.cee`), as a\n  box's contents are. A second move is N0250. A capture that holds a `drop` block cannot be partly moved (as today).\n- The body drops what it did not move, when it returns, through the drop elaboration that locals use. Captures that\n  may or may not have been moved get drop flags. The code that ran a child's whole environment drop after the body\n  (`src/backend/c_async.cee:120`, `src/interpreter/io.cee:2029`) no longer does. A child that never started still\n  drops its whole environment.\n- A cancelled body returns along a normal path (§40.6: cancellation never destroys a frame), so its remaining\n  captures are dropped there. A value moved into a timeout's body is gone after the deadline. That is the meaning of\n  `move`.\n- Field-precise captures stay references, and are never movable.\n\n### 2.4 Regions on methods, and results tied to the struct's region (§25.1, §25.6, lifetimes.md §7)\n\n**Method region parameters.** A struct's or an inherent impl's method may declare its own regions, as a free function\ndoes:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn first<region a>(borrow this, other: borrow(a) Vector<u8>) -> Slice<a, u8> => other[0..1];\n```\n\n- Interface methods and methods that implement an interface keep N0216, because conformance compares the\n  signatures and would have to match the region lists.\n\n**Deep results.** Every reference parameter, the receiver included, gets its own elided outer name. A result that\nnames only regions the parameter's pointee carries is a **deep** result of that parameter. It is tied to those\nregions, not to the parameter's loan:\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Lexer<region r> {\n    text: Slice<r, u8>;\n    at: usize;\n    fn token(borrow mut this) -> Slice<r, u8> { let s = this.at; this.at += 1; return this.text[s..this.at]; }\n}\nlet t = lexer.token();\nlexer.skipSpace();   // accepted: t points into the text, not into lexer\n```\n\n- **Callee rule.** A deep result may carry only loans the parameter's pointee already held: a copy of a view field,\n  or a slice o",
    338 =>
        "f one. It may not carry a loan of a place reached through the parameter. Today `fn f(borrow mut this)\n  -> Slice<r, u8> => this.buf[0..2]` is accepted, and is sound only because callers tie the result to the `this`\n  loan. Under this ADR, it is N0245.\n- Slicing a shared view place (`this.text[a..b]` with `text: Slice<r, u8>` or `StringView<r>`) copies the view's\n  loans instead of making a new loan of the place. Slicing a `SliceMut` field stays a reborrow of the place.\n- **Caller rule.** A deep source's argument `borrow p` contributes `p`'s own loans to the result, not the new loan of\n  `p`.\n- **What breaks.** Only a function returning a view of its parameter's own storage under a pointee region. It must\n  name the parameter's elided region (`-> Slice<u8>` from `borrow this`) instead. The three repositories are searched\n  before the release.\n\n### 2.5 An optional place as an optional borrow (§22.1, §13.2)\n\n- `o.asBorrow()` on a place of type `T?`, or on a `borrow (T?)`, is `borrow T?`: `Some` borrows the payload, and\n  `null` is `null`. `o.asBorrowMut()` on a mutable place is `(borrow mut T)?`. These are optionals' third and fourth\n  methods (ADR 0072 §2.2).\n- At an argument whose parameter is `borrow T?` (or `(borrow mut T)?` with `mut`), a place of type `T?` converts\n  implicitly, as a place of `T` converts to `borrow T` today (§13.2). A place of `T` converts to `Some(borrow t)`.\n  So `f(relay.tlsConfig)` is accepted.\n- The lowering is the hand-written `match`: a tag test, then the payload's address or the null niche. The borrow\n  checker sees a shared or mutable loan of the place.\n\n### 2.6 Raw multi-line string literals (grammar.md §1.6)\n\n```ceesafe\nlet query = \"\"\"\n    SELECT id, name\n    FROM users\n    WHERE id = $1\n    \"\"\";\n```\n\n- `\"\"\"` ends its line. The content is the lines that follow, up to a line holding only whitespace and `\"\"\"`.\n- The closing line's indentation is removed from every content line. A content line that is not blank and indented\n  less is N0011, a new code. Blank lines are empty. The newlines between content lines are `\\n`, also in a CRLF file.\n  There is no final newline.\n- No escapes: a backslash is a backslash, and `{` is a brace. N0400 does not apply.\n- The token is a `StringLiteral`, so everything after the lexer is unchanged. `fmt` moves the interior lines and the\n  closing line with the opening line's indentation, and never reflows them.\n- Not included: `embed(\"file\")`, which needs file access during checking and in the LSP and MCP. It can come later.\n\n### 2.7 Associated constants (§17)\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Oid { const RSA: [u8; 9] = [0x2A, …]; … }\nimpl Mode { const DEFAULT: u32 = 25; }\nlet a = Oid.RSA; let b = pkg.Oid.RSA;\n```\n\n- `const NAME: T = e;` is allowed in a struct body and in an inherent impl, so enums have them too. They are\n  evaluated as top-level constants are (`src/check/consts.cee`), and reached as `Type.NAME`.\n- Not allowed in a generic struct or impl: N0216, for now.\n- A constant and a method of the same type cannot share a name (N0228, as a duplicate member). The visibility rules\n  match methods'.\n\n### 2.8 `for` over an iterator (§15, the `for` statement)\n\n`for (x in e)`, where `e`'s type has a method `next(borrow mut this) -> T?`, calls `next` until it returns `null`:\n\n```ceesafe\nfor (part in text.split(\",\")) { … }\n```\n\n- `e` is evaluated once into a header temporary, which lives for the whole loop (ADR 0073 §2.1).\n- Each pass mutably borrows the iterator only for the call to `next`. The body may use what the iterator borrows,\n  `text` above, as a shared borrow.\n- `x` has type `T`, its region that of `next`'s result.\n- `break` and `continue` keep their meaning.\n- No interface is involved: the method's shape is the rule, as `drop` blocks name a shape. lib/core's `TextSplit`,\n  `TextLines` and the other `next` iterators work without change.\n\n### 2.9 Checked and saturating integer conversions (§7, §31)\n\n- `checkedTruncate<T>(e) -> T?` and `saturatingTruncate<T>(e) -",
    339 =>
        "> T` take integer operands, as well as floats:\n  `checkedTruncate<i64>(n)!` for a `usize`.\n- `as` stays lossless only. The guide's conversions table names the three spellings:\n  - `as`: never loses information;\n  - `truncate`: wraps;\n  - `checkedTruncate` and `saturatingTruncate`: check or clamp.\n- Not included: making `as` depend on the narrowest supported target (§4).\n\n### 2.10 A local may shadow a dependency (§4 of the findings; tooling.md §4)\n\n- A parameter, local, loop variable or pattern binding may have a dependency's name. In its scope, `name.x` in an\n  expression means the local's member.\n- Items and type parameters keep N0315, because they share the namespace that `dep.Item` is resolved in.\n- Types and patterns are unaffected (a local is never a type), so `tls.TlsConfig` in a type keeps naming the package.\n- The join of `dep.X` in expressions (`src/check/packages.cee:635`) becomes scope-aware. It is skipped where a local\n  of that name is in scope.\n- A shadowed dependency's values are unreachable in that scope. Where `X` is both a member of the local and a public\n  item of the dependency, warning N0401 says so.\n\n## 3. Alternatives considered\n\n- **§2.1:** a real unit type in NIR and C. It would be uniform, but would touch every pass for a value with no bits.\n  Erasing units in the checker keeps the change in one layer.\n- **§2.3:** an `Async.take(capture)` intrinsic to move one capture out explicitly. It is more to write, and a run-once\n  rule is what `move` promises a reader.\n- **§2.4:** relaxing only the caller. It is unsound (the `this.buf` example).\n- **§2.6:** `embed(\"file\")` only, or C-like `\"a\" \"b\"` concatenation. The first needs host access in every tool. The\n  second keeps escapes and quotes on every line.\n- **§2.8:** an `Iterator<T>` interface. That needs `dyn` or generic-bound dispatch, and gives nothing over the shape\n  rule today.\n- **§2.9:** `i64.fromSize(n)`, one function per pair. The two existing generic builtins cover every pair.\n\n## 4. Not in this ADR\n\n- `var` of a view type (N0246). Re-pointing a reference breaks \"assigning to a reference writes through it\", so it\n  needs its own ADR. Its workaround, a struct field holding the view, works today.\n- Generic enums instantiated at `void` (N0226), and `Some(v)` with `void`.\n- `as` checked against the narrowest target: `u64 as usize` is accepted, and it is lossy on a 32-bit target. The\n  compiler has about 3,900 `as usize`/`as isize` casts to sort first.\n- `embed(\"file\")`.\n- Consts in generic structs.\n\n## 5. Testing\n\nEvery new program runs compiled, with `--interp`, and with `--check-leaks`.\n\n- **§2.1:** run-pass:\n  - the finding's `conv(act())`;\n  - `let v = r?; return Ok(v);`;\n  - a void instance of a generic that also runs at `u32`.\n\n  check-fail: a unit local used as a value of another type.\n- **§2.2:** run-pass:\n  - `die()` in a value `match`;\n  - code after a call is unreachable.\n\n  check-fail:\n  - `never` as a parameter or field (N0226);\n  - a `never` function whose end is reachable (N0213);\n  - `return` in one.\n\n  emit-c: the `_Noreturn` signature.\n- **§2.3:** run-pass:\n  - `s.spawn` and `set.spawn` moving a non-Copy capture into a call;\n  - a capture moved on one path only (a drop flag across an `await`);\n  - a timeout body that moves its capture and one that is cancelled before it does, each with a `drop` block\n    printing the order;\n  - a race loser;\n  - a child never started (a full task set);\n  - `--check-leaks` on all.\n\n  check-fail: a second move (N0250); a `Box<dyn fn>` move literal (still N0251). The cancellation and fault sweeps run\n  over the new programs.\n- **§2.4:** run-pass:\n  - a method with its own region;\n  - the finding's lexer, `token` then `skipSpace`.\n\n  check-fail:\n  - the `this.buf` deep result (N0245);\n  - a deep result kept past the end of its region's owner (N0243);\n  - an interface method with a region (N0216).\n- **§2.5:** run-pass:\n  - `f(r.cfg)` with `Some` and `null`;\n  - `asBorrow` and `asBorrowMut` wr",
    340 =>
        "iting through;\n  - a `T` place passed to `borrow T?`.\n\n  check-fail: `asBorrowMut` on a shared place.\n- **§2.6:** lexer tests:\n  - indentation stripping;\n  - CRLF;\n  - blank lines;\n  - a content line indented less (N0011);\n  - an unterminated literal (N0005);\n  - `\"\"\"` inside a line.\n\n  Also: a run-pass printing a SQL block; `fmt` snapshots; the VS Code grammar.\n- **§2.7:** run-pass:\n  - struct and impl constants;\n  - through a package (`dep.T.C`);\n  - in an array length.\n\n  check-fail: a generic struct's constant (N0216); a name shared with a method (N0228).\n- **§2.8:** run-pass:\n  - `split`;\n  - a user iterator struct;\n  - `break` and `continue`;\n  - the body reading the iterator's source;\n  - a temporary iterator with a `drop` block destroyed after the loop.\n\n  check-fail: the body mutating the source (N0241); `next` with the wrong shape (N0205, with a hint).\n- **§2.9:** run-pass: every integer pair, at the bounds, against a Python oracle (`tools/oracles/`).\n- **§2.10:** run-pass:\n  - a local `tls` beside `tls.TlsConfig` in a type;\n  - a package test with a parameter named like a dependency.\n\n  check-fail: an item named like a dependency (N0315). warning: N0401.\n- **Every change:** the fixed point, the contract suite, and `ceesafec check` plus `ceesafec test` across CeeSafeLibs\n  and CeeSafeApps.\n\n## 6. Questions for the owner\n\n1. **All nine in one release, 0.18.0?** **Proposed: yes.** Each part has its own corpus tests, so one part can be held\n   back if it isn't ready.\n2. **§2.4 deep results**, the one change that can reject accepted code (a function returning its parameter's own\n   storage under a pointee region)? **Proposed: yes**, after searching the three repositories. The rule it replaces\n   is sound only by accident.\n3. **§2.6 raw literal form:** C#-style `\"\"\"`, with the closing line setting the indentation and no escapes?\n   **Proposed: yes.**\n4. **§2.10:** a warning (N0401) when a shadowed dependency's item has the member's name, or silent shadowing?\n   **Proposed: the warning.**\n\n## 7. Implementation (2026-11-05)\n\n### 7.1 Where\n\nFive parts were built in parallel on branches `adr0074-types`, `-moves`, `-regions`, `-syntax` and `-loops`, then\nmerged.\n\n- **§2.1, §2.2 (types):**\n  - unit locals are scope entries with no HIR local (`src/check/checker.cee`, `patterns.cee`, `options.cee`,\n    `body.cee`);\n  - `never` is in the named types, allowed only as a return type (`allowNever`, `declare.cee`);\n  - a call of a `never` function ends its NIR block with `Unreachable`;\n  - C: `_Noreturn void`.\n- **§2.3 (moves):**\n  - `StructDef.isOnceEnvironment`, set by `finishMoveLiteral` when the caller marks the literal run-once\n    (`Checker.runOnceLiteral`, in `src/check/async.cee`);\n  - `*env` is a move path in a run-once function (`src/nir/move_paths.cee`), and remaining captures are dropped at\n    `StorageDead(env)` (`src/nir/drops.cee`);\n  - the C backend and the interpreter no longer drop a run-once environment after the body.\n- **§2.4, §2.5 (regions):**\n  - the method-region refusal is lifted except for interfaces and their impls;\n  - `HirFunction.deepSources` comes from `resolveFlows`;\n  - the borrow checker gives a deep parameter an outer placeholder and a referent placeholder (`LoanEdge.dropOuter`);\n    the callee rule is `deepPlaceViolation` (N0245) and the caller rule `deepSourceEdge`;\n  - `asBorrow`/`asBorrowMut` desugar to the hand-written `match` (`src/check/options.cee`), and the argument\n    conversion is `optionalBorrowArgument` (`references.cee`).\n- **§2.6, §2.7, §2.10 (syntax):**\n  - `lexRawString` (`src/syntax/lexer.cee`), with the formatter's `verbatim` lines and the VS Code `raw-strings` rule;\n  - associated constants in `src/check/member_consts.cee`, declared as `Type.NAME`;\n  - the syntactic scope walk in `src/check/shadowing.cee`, which skips the `dep.X` join under a shadowing local and\n    reports N0401.\n- **§2.8, §2.9 (loops):**\n  - `HirStmt.ForNext` and `lowerForNext` (`src/nir/nir_bu",
    341 =>
        "ilder.cee`), with the iterator in the header scope and\n    `next`'s loan ending before the body;\n  - integer `checkedTruncate`/`saturatingTruncate` reuse the `FloatToInt` node, with exact bounds in the interpreter\n    and `appendIntFitsTest`/`appendIntSaturation` in the C backend (no UB, no mixed-sign comparison).\n\n### 7.2 Changes from the accepted text\n\n1. **§2.1:**\n   - `let x = voidCall();` stays N0224: a `let` declares a unit local only for `?`, `!` or a unit read;\n   - a bare `v;` stays N0225;\n   - `never` is allowed on struct methods as well as free functions;\n   - `let x = die();` is N0224 (0.17.0 emitted C that did not compile for `let x = Process.exit(1);`).\n2. **§2.3:**\n   - run-once is decided when the literal is checked;\n   - a local of the enclosing function cannot be moved into a `set.spawn` child, because the task set's body\n     closure only borrows it. A value made inside the body (ADR 0053's `stream` from `accept()`) can be moved.\n3. **§2.4:**\n   - slicing a shared view field still makes a place loan. The refined callee rule (a loan is a violation only if it\n     reaches no further reference) already accepts `this.text[a..b]`;\n   - a `borrow (T?)` argument converts only with `asBorrow()`, because `check-fail/optional-references.cee` requires\n     N0205 there. Places of `T?` and `T` convert as written;\n   - a method region named like its struct's region is N0202.\n4. **§2.6:** N0011 also covers text after the opening `\"\"\"`.\n5. **§2.7:**\n   - the spec text is in type-system.md §10 (Constants);\n   - enum constants come from `impl E { const … }`;\n   - array lengths stay integer literals, as for top-level constants;\n   - a bare `NAME` does not reach an associated constant, even in the type's own methods.\n6. **§2.8:** the iterator may be a `borrow mut T` (reborrowed, so its state survives the loop); a shared reference is\n   N0248.\n7. **§2.9:** the C bounds treat `isize`/`usize` as 64-bit, as the backend emits them.\n8. **§2.10:**\n   - N0401 is reported when `X` is a public item of the dependency, without checking that the local's type has `X`\n     (the warning is a syntactic pass);\n   - `dep.S { … }` struct literals keep naming the package in a shadowing scope.\n\n## 8. Amendment (2026-11-05): a reference result's own region, and deep results through a call (B18)\n\nFound by CeeSafe.Web on 0.18.0 (its `docs/ceesafe-upstream-work.md`, B18). `web_http`'s\n`fn headers(borrow this) -> borrow HeaderList<r> => borrow this.fields` was N0245, and so was every package that\ndepends on it.\n\n1. **The callee.** §25.6 elided a result's regions only when none were written. `borrow HeaderList<r>` names `r`\n   inside, so the reference's own region got no flow name. The result named only `this`'s pointee region and was\n   made a deep result of `this`, and `borrow this.fields` is a loan of the receiver's own storage. The result is a\n   reference to the receiver's storage, as Rust's `fn headers(&self) -> &HeaderList<'r>` is. Now a result reference\n   with no sources or regions written on it gets the elided region of the single scoped parameter, also when its\n   pointee names regions, so it is not deep. With several scoped parameters nothing changes: `borrow(p)` names the\n   source.\n2. **The caller.** A deep result's argument contributed less than its loans only as `&p` or a reference parameter.\n   In `o.inner().first()` the receiver of `first` is a temporary holding `inner`'s result, so it kept `o`'s own loan,\n   and `fn f<region r>(o: borrow Outer<r>) -> Slice<r, u8> => o.inner().first()` was N0245. Now a call's result that\n   passes through one reference parameter (a `borrow T` result tied to it alone, whose pointee names only regions\n   that parameter's referent holds) is followed to that argument (type-system.md §25.6; lifetimes.md §7). What the\n   result refers to holds no more than the argument's referent: the pointee names none of the parameter's own\n   regions, and a referent cannot hold a loan of its own storage. A p",
    342 =>
        "ointee that names no region yet may hold loans\n   (`-> borrow Inner`) is not followed.\n3. **Tests:** `run-pass/deep-results-through.cee` (both, with `borrow mut`, an optional and `borrow(this)`; 0.18.0\n   refuses it) and `check-fail/deep-results-through.cee` (the result stays tied to the receiver, N0240; a view still\n   blocks a push, N0241; the elided pointee is not followed, N0245).\n4. **Why the release missed it:** 0.18.0 was checked against CeeSafeLibs and CeeSafeApps, which have no result of\n   this shape, and the corpus had none.\n",
    343 =>
        "# ADR 0075 — The storage engine's findings: durable files, error conversion, an ordered map, checksums, a monotonic clock, `assert`/`panic`, `replace`, arrays as slices, `*r`, static tables, and tooling\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-06 (\"I want you to take over ~/dev/CeeSafe session and fix all the\n  findings\"). The findings come from the CosmoStore port (`CeeSafeApps/cosmostore/CEESAFE_GAPS.md`, G1–G22). Each\n  open finding was reproduced with ceesafec 0.18.2 on 2026-11-06 (§1). The owner did not answer §6 one by one. It is\n  decided as proposed, as ADR 0074 §6 was, and is open to revision before the release.\n- Scope:\n  - type-system.md §6, §13.3, §16, §17.4, §23, §26.3, §26.10, §26.11, §39.4;\n  - spec/api/core.md and spec/api/std.md;\n  - diagnostics.md;\n  - the parser, checker, NIR lowering, C backend, interpreter and runtime;\n  - lib/core and lib/std;\n  - `ceesafec test --sweep alloc` and the package C build;\n  - guide/builtins.md, guide/for-agents.md and templates/AGENTS.md;\n  - the corpus and tests/support.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nCosmoStore is an S3-compatible object store whose storage core (a journal, extents, checkpoints and crash\nrecovery) is written in CeeSafe 0.18.1. To exist at all, it needed 130 lines of C (`durable_shim.c`). It also logged\n22 findings. One is already fixed: `HashKey` for `String` and `Vector<u8>` (`lib/std/collections.cee:179`, `:187`).\nThe rest reproduce on 0.18.2:\n\n| # | Today (0.18.2) | The port's workaround |\n|---|---|---|\n| G1–G5 | `File` opens for reading, or creates **truncating**; no positional I/O, sync, length, truncate, rename or directory sync | `durable_shim.c` and a wrapper |\n| G6 | `AsyncFiles` has only `open`, `create`, `read` and `write` | the journal is synchronous |\n| G7 | `r.isOk()` is N0234; `?` from `Result<_, A>` into `Result<_, B>` is N0261 | a generic adapter per error type (`jio(f.size())?`) |\n| G8 | no ordered map | a full scan and sort for every LIST |\n| G9 | no CRC32C, CRC64NVME, MD5, SHA-256 or HMAC in the libraries | its own CRC32C and MD5 |\n| G10 | only `Timer.now()` in milliseconds | none (no latency histograms) |\n| G11 | `m.keyAt(0).name.view()` is N0247 | a local per access |\n| G12 | `assert` and `panic` are N0200 | `checkedTruncate<u32>(x)!` as an assertion |\n| G13 | `[0u8; 0]` is N0259 | `buf[0..0]` |\n| G14 | `[u8; 8]` has no `readLE`/`writeLE` (N0234) | `let w = borrow mut h[..]; w.writeLE(…)` |\n| G15 | `--sweep alloc` counts a test's own `!` as a failure (580 of 639 runs) | read the noise by hand |\n| G16 | a package's C file is compiled from a temporary copy, so `#include \"x.h\"` beside it fails | `include-dirs` |\n| G17 | a borrow error elsewhere in a function also reports N0265 \"cannot infer `T`\" | none |\n| G18 | `Vector` has no `insert`/`remove` | sorted vectors by hand |\n| G19 | moving a value out of a `borrow mut` place is N0251, and there is no `replace` | `ObjectWriter.finish` copies 128 KiB instead of moving it |\n| G20 | `a as u64 < b` is N0100 (`<` read as generic arguments) | parentheses |\n| G21 | `[AtomicU64; 4]` in a static is N0259 | a `static mut` table behind `unsafe` accessors |\n| G22 | `*r` is N0102 | `let id: u64 = r;` |\n\n## 2. Proposal\n\n### 2.1 Durable files (G1–G5; §26.3, §26.10)\n\n`File` gains three ways to open, positional I/O, two syncs, a length and a truncate. `File.rename` and\n`Directory.sync` are lib/std functions.\n\n```ceesafe\nvar journal = File.openOrCreate(path)?;           // read and write; created if missing, never truncated\njournal.writeAt(record.view(), offset)?;          // every byte at `offset`, or an error\njournal.syncData()?;                              // the data, and the metadata needed to read it back\nlet n = journal.readAt(mut buffer[..], offset)?;  // bytes read; 0 at or past the end\njournal.setLength(validEnd)?;                     // truncate a torn tail\nFile.rename(temporary, final)?;                   // atomic replace\nDirectory.sync(directory)?;                       // make the",
    344 =>
        " new names durable\n```\n\n- **Opening.**\n\n  | Function | Access | Missing file | Existing file |\n  |---|---|---|---|\n  | `File.openReadWrite(path)` | read and write | `NotFound` | opened as is |\n  | `File.openOrCreate(path)` | read and write | created | opened as is |\n  | `File.createNew(path)` | read and write | created | `AlreadyExists` |\n\n  - A file is created with mode 0666 & ~umask.\n  - On Windows, every open shares read, write and delete.\n- **Methods.** Each takes `borrow mut this` and returns `Result<…, IoError>`. `EINTR` is retried.\n  - `f.readAt(mut buffer: SliceMut<u8>, offset: u64) -> usize`: one positional read (`pread`; `ReadFile` with an\n    `OVERLAPPED` offset on Windows). It may return less than the buffer, and returns 0 at or past the end.\n  - `f.writeAt(bytes: Slice<u8>, offset: u64) -> void`: positional writes until every byte is written.\n  - `f.syncData() -> void`: the file's data, and the metadata needed to read it back, reach stable storage.\n    - Linux: `fdatasync`.\n    - macOS and iOS: `fcntl(F_FULLFSYNC)`, because `fsync` there does not flush the drive's cache. It falls back to\n      `fsync` only where the file system refuses `F_FULLFSYNC` (`ENOTSUP`, `EINVAL`).\n    - Other POSIX systems: `fsync`.\n    - Windows: `FlushFileBuffers`.\n  - `f.syncAll() -> void`: the same, with all metadata (`fsync`, or `F_FULLFSYNC` on Apple systems).\n  - `f.length() -> u64`: `fstat`'s size.\n  - `f.setLength(n: u64) -> void`: `ftruncate` (`SetFileInformationByHandle` on Windows), shrinking or\n    zero-extending.\n  - **A failed sync leaves the file's durable contents unknown.** After a failure, Linux may already have marked the\n    dirty pages clean. A later successful sync does not mean the earlier writes reached the disk. A program that\n    needs durability must treat the file as failed: rewrite it, or recover from what is known to be durable. The\n    spec says this in so many words, and so does the guide.\n  - `read` and `write` keep their sequential meaning on every `File`.\n- **`File.rename(from, to)`** (lib/std): `rename(2)`, which atomically replaces `to`. On Windows it is\n  `MoveFileExW(MOVEFILE_REPLACE_EXISTING | MOVEFILE_WRITE_THROUGH)`.\n- **`Directory.sync(path)`** (lib/std): on POSIX, `open(O_RDONLY | O_DIRECTORY)` then `fsync`, which makes the\n  directory's entries (creations, renames, removals) durable. It is `Ok` on Windows, where NTFS journals directory\n  metadata and a directory handle cannot be flushed. The spec records this.\n- **Runtime.** The C functions, each returning a value or a negated errno like the rest of\n  `runtime/hosted/ceesafe_rt_io.c`:\n  - `ceesafe_file_open`, with new modes 2, 3 and 4;\n  - `ceesafe_fd_read_at`, `ceesafe_fd_write_at`, `ceesafe_fd_sync`, `ceesafe_fd_length`, `ceesafe_fd_set_length`;\n  - `ceesafe_host_rename`, `ceesafe_host_dir_sync`.\n- **Effects.** All of these have `sleep`, as `File.open` does. In freestanding code they are N0293, as `File` is.\n- **The interpreter** runs them on the host file system, as it runs `File.open`. Its sandbox refuses them, and it\n  counts a sync as done.\n\n### 2.2 Async files (G6; spec/api/std.md `AsyncFiles`)\n\n`AsyncFiles` gains `openReadWrite`, `openOrCreate`, `createNew`, `readAt`, `writeAt`, `syncData`, `syncAll`,\n`length`, `setLength`, `rename` and `syncDirectory`, with §2.1's meaning. Each runs its blocking call on a worker\n(`Async.blocking`), as `AsyncFiles.read` does today. io_uring's `IORING_OP_FSYNC` and positional reads are an\nexecutor change for a later ADR. The API does not depend on how the work is done.\n\n### 2.3 Results and conversion in `?` (G7; §17.4, spec/api/core.md)\n\n- **Methods of `Result<T, E>`** (lib/core):\n  - `r.isOk()` and `r.isErr()` take `borrow this`;\n  - `r.ok() -> T?` and `r.err() -> E?` consume `r`;\n  - `r.map(f)` and `r.mapErr(f)` consume `r` and call `f` with the payload.\n- **Methods of `T?`:** `o.okOr(e) -> Result<T, E>` consumes `o`.\n- **Conversion in `?`.** A variant of a user enum whose single payload is the err",
    345 =>
        "or type `A` may be marked `@from`:\n\n  ```ceesafe\n  enum StoreError {\n      @from Io(IoError),\n      @from Alloc(AllocError),\n      Corrupt,\n  }\n  fn open(path: StringView) -> Result<Store, StoreError> {\n      var f = File.openReadWrite(path)?;      // an IoError becomes StoreError.Io(e)\n      …\n  }\n  ```\n\n  - In a function returning `Result<U, B>`, `e?` with `e: Result<T, A>` is allowed where `A` is `B`, or where `B`\n    is an enum with an `@from` variant whose payload type is `A`. The `Err(x)` case then returns\n    `Err(B.Variant(x))`.\n  - At most one `@from` variant per payload type (N0262 otherwise; the diagnostic names both). A `@from` variant\n    must have exactly one payload (N0262).\n  - The conversion goes one step only, never `A → B → C`.\n  - It applies to `?` only. `return Err(x)` still needs `B.Variant(x)`, so a construction always says what it\n    builds.\n  - N0261 now says which `@from` variant would fix the mismatch.\n- **Why an attribute, not an interface.** A `From`-style interface for `IoError → StoreError` would have to live in\n  the package that declares either `IoError` or the interface. That works here, but an interface adds a call the\n  reader cannot see, and generic code would have to resolve it per instance. A variant is visible where the error\n  type is declared, costs nothing at run time, and is checked once.\n\n### 2.4 An ordered map (G8; spec/api/std.md)\n\nlib/std gains `OrderedMap<K, V>`, a B-tree:\n- It is built over an arena of nodes (a `Vector` of nodes, linked by index), so it uses no `unsafe`.\n- Each node has at most 2B − 1 keys, for a fixed B of 16.\n- Keys implement a new interface, `OrderKey<K>`, with `fn compare(borrow this, other: borrow K) -> i32` (negative,\n  zero or positive). lib/std implements it for the integers, `bool`, `String` and `Vector<u8>` (bytewise).\n- `create()`.\n- `put(key, value) -> Result<V?, AllocError>`: the old value, if any.\n- `get(borrow key) -> borrow V?` and `getMut(borrow key) -> borrow mut V?`.\n- `contains(borrow key) -> bool`.\n- `remove(borrow key) -> V?`, which rebalances: borrow from a sibling, else merge.\n- `length() -> usize` and `isEmpty() -> bool`.\n- `clear()`.\n- **Ordered access with cursors.** A cursor is a plain value: a node, a slot and the map's version.\n  - `first()`, `last()`, `seek(borrow key)` (the first entry ≥ key) and `seekAfter(borrow key)` return an\n    `OrderedCursor?`.\n  - `next(cursor) -> OrderedCursor?` and `previous(cursor) -> OrderedCursor?` step it.\n  - `keyAt(cursor) -> borrow K` and `valueAt(cursor) -> borrow V` read through it, and `valueAtMut` writes.\n  - **A cursor used after the map changed panics** (\"ordered map changed under a cursor\"): a mutation bumps the\n    version. A cursor cannot outlive the map's validity silently, and the borrow checker needs no new rule.\n  - A range scan is `seek(from)` followed by `next` until `keyAt(c).compare(to) >= 0`.\n- Iteration order is the keys' order, so output never depends on insertion order or hashing.\n\n### 2.5 Checksums and hashes (G9; spec/api/std.md)\n\nlib/std gains, in pure CeeSafe:\n- `Crc32c`: Castagnoli, reflected, slicing-by-8;\n- `Crc64Nvme`: the CRC-64/NVME of the S3 checksum headers;\n- `Md5`;\n- `Sha256`;\n- `HmacSha256`.\n\nEach is incremental: `create()`, `update(bytes)`, then `finish()`, which returns a `u32`/`u64`, or a `[u8; 16]`/`[u8; 32]`\ndigest. `Crc32c.of(bytes)` and the like compute it in one call. The CRC tables are generated (`tools/gen_checksums.py`),\nas the other tables are. MD5 is for ETags and Content-MD5 only, and the API doc says it is broken as a signature.\nHardware CRC (SSE4.2 `crc32`, ARMv8 `crc32c`) needs intrinsics, which CeeSafe does not have. It is left to a later\nADR, and the API will not change. Test vectors come from Python (`hashlib`, `hmac`, and an independent CRC model in\ntools/oracles).\n\n### 2.6 A monotonic clock in nanoseconds (G10; §26.11)\n\n`Clock.monotonicNanos() -> u64` (lib/std): nanoseconds on the monotonic clock, from an arbitrary start.\n- P",
    346 =>
        "OSIX: `clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC)`.\n- Apple systems: `clock_gettime_nsec_np(CLOCK_UPTIME_RAW)`.\n- Windows: `QueryPerformanceCounter`, scaled.\n\nIt never decreases within a process. The interpreter answers its virtual clock in nanoseconds. `Timer.now()` keeps\nits milliseconds.\n\n### 2.7 A field of a returned reference is a place (G11; §13.2)\n\nA call whose result is a reference is a reference expression. A field reached through it, `m.keyAt(0).name`, is\n\"a place reached through a reference\", which N0247's text already allows. So `m.keyAt(0).name.view()` borrows\n`name` through `keyAt`'s result, and the loan is the call result's loan. The same holds for a method result\n(`e.f()`) and for chains (`a.b().c.d()`).\n\n### 2.8 `assert` and `panic` (G12; §8)\n\n- `panic(message: StringView) -> never` panics with \"panicked: \" and the message. Like every panic, it reports the\n  source position and aborts.\n- `assert(condition)` panics with \"assertion failed\" when `condition` is false. `assert(condition, message)` uses\n  the message instead.\n- These are built-in function names, so a user function named `assert` or `panic` shadows them, with no warning.\n- Assertions are always checked: there is no release mode that drops them, so a build does what its tests checked.\n- The runtime gains `CEE_PANIC_ASSERT` and `ceesafe_panic_message(position, text)`.\n\n### 2.9 Empty arrays (G13; §16)\n\n`[T; 0]` is a type and `[e; 0]` a value: the empty array.\n- Its size is 0. No C storage is emitted for it: a zero-length array member is left out of its struct, and a local\n  is a `uint8_t` placeholder that nothing reads.\n- `a[..]` is an empty slice, and any `a[i]` panics.\n- `e` in `[e; 0]` is still evaluated and then destroyed, because it is an expression with effects.\n- `[T; 0]` in a `@repr(C)` struct or an `extern \"C\"` signature is N0259, because C has no zero-length arrays.\n- `Slice<T>.empty()` and `SliceMut<T>.empty()` are also added: the empty slice, with no array behind it.\n\n### 2.10 An array's methods are its slice's (G14; §16)\n\nA method call on an array place whose name is not an array method calls the slice method on `a[..]`. It borrows\nmutably when the method needs a `SliceMut`, and needs a mutable place for that, as `a[..]` does. So `h.writeLE(0, x)`\nand `h.readLE<u32>(4)` work on `[u8; 64]`, as do `sortBy`, `swap` and the rest.\n\n### 2.11 Moving out of a mutable place: `replace` and `swap` (G19; §14)\n\n- `replace(mut place, value) -> T` puts `value` in `place` and returns the old value.\n- `swap(mut a, mut b)` exchanges two places of the same type.\n- Both are built-in functions. The place is never left empty, so neither changes any move rule. They work for\n  every type, including a field of a struct that has a `drop` block, which is how a consuming `finish` gives up a\n  buffer:\n\n  ```ceesafe\n  fn finish(this: borrow mut ObjectWriter) -> Vector<u8> => replace(mut this.inline, Vector<u8>.create());\n  ```\n\n- `swap`'s two places are two mutable loans at once, so `swap(mut a.x, mut a.x)` is N0241 as always. For two\n  elements of one slice there is `s.swap(i, j)`.\n- Lowering: `T old = *p; *p = v;` in C, with `v` moved in. Drop elaboration sees a move into `*p` and a fresh\n  value out.\n\n### 2.12 `Vector.insert`, `remove` and `swapRemove` (G18; §23)\n\n- `v.insert(at, x) -> Result<void, AllocError>` shifts later elements up. `at == length` appends.\n- `v.remove(at) -> T` shifts later elements down.\n- `v.swapRemove(at) -> T` moves the last element into `at`, which is O(1) and does not keep the order.\n- `at` past the end panics (index out of bounds). The shifting is a `memmove` in C and a loop in the interpreter.\n\n### 2.13 `as T <` (G20; grammar.md)\n\nAfter `as`, a primitive type name (`u8` … `u64`, `i8` … `i64`, `usize`, `isize`, `f32`, `f64`, `bool`, `char`)\ntakes no type arguments, so a `<` after it is the comparison operator: `a as u64 < b` is `(a as u64) < b`.\n\nFor a type that does take type arguments, `as Box<T>` is still parsed as written. If its `<` can",
    347 =>
        "not start type\narguments, N0100 now adds \"write `(e as T) < x` for a comparison\".\n\n### 2.14 Prefix `*`: reading through a reference (G22; §13.3)\n\n`*r` where `r: borrow T` or `borrow mut T`, with `T` a Copy type, is the value `r` refers to. It is a copy, of type\n`T`, so `let id = *m.keyAt(0);` declares a `u64`.\n- `*r` on a reference to a non-Copy type is N0251. The diagnostic says to borrow or `replace`.\n- `*` on a non-reference is N0205.\n- Assigning through `*r = v` is accepted where `r` is a `borrow mut T`, the same as `r = v` today.\n- Raw pointers keep `p.read()` and `p.write(v)`. `*p` on a raw pointer is N0205, which points to them, because a\n  raw read is `unsafe` and must look different.\n- The binary `*` is unchanged: a prefix `*` is one with no left operand.\n\n### 2.15 Static tables (G21; §39.4)\n\n- **Arrays of non-Copy elements, in statics only.** A static's type may be `[T; N]` with a non-Copy `T` that has no\n  `drop` block (atomics, structs of atomics). Its initialiser must be an array literal or `[e; N]` where `e` is a\n  static initialiser. `[e; N]` there means N evaluations of `e` at compile time, not a copy, so it needs no Copy.\n- `S[i]` is a place, and methods borrow through it (`SLOTS[i].fetchAdd(1)`). It cannot be moved out of, as no static\n  can.\n- Outside statics, arrays of non-Copy elements stay N0259. Locals, fields and parameters would need per-element drop\n  flags, which is a separate decision.\n- **`Mutex<T>.constant(value)`** (lib/std, `@constant`) for a `T` with a static initialiser, and\n  **`Vector<T>.empty()`**, `@constant`: an empty vector owns no memory, so it is a static initialiser. Together they\n  allow `static TABLE: Mutex<Vector<u64>> = Mutex<Vector<u64>>.constant(Vector<u64>.empty());`.\n  - A static's `Mutex` is never destroyed. Its vector's memory is reclaimed at process exit, so the leak checker\n    does not count a static's allocations.\n\n### 2.16 Tooling and diagnostics (G15, G16, G17)\n\n- **G15.** `ceesafec test --sweep alloc` puts each injected failure into one of three classes:\n  - **handled**: the test passed;\n  - **unwrapped by the test**: the panic is `!` or `?`'s unwrap at a position inside an `@test` function's own\n    body;\n  - **failed**: any other panic, a leak, a crash or a wrong result.\n\n  Only *failed* runs fail the sweep. The report shows the count of each, and lists the failed ones.\n  `--sweep-strict` keeps today's behaviour.\n- **G16.** A package's C files are compiled with their own source directory first on the quoted include path\n  (`-iquote <dir>`; `/I<dir>` on MSVC), so a header beside a `.c` file is found. They are still compiled from the\n  build copy. guide/packages.md says so.\n- **G17.** A call whose arguments include an error expression no longer reports N0265 (cannot infer `T`), and no\n  inference diagnostic comes from an error type. The first error is the one shown.\n\n## 3. Not included\n\n- io_uring for files (§2.2) and hardware CRC (§2.5): each needs its own design, and neither changes the API.\n- Arrays of non-Copy elements outside statics (§2.15).\n- An interface-based `From` (§2.3).\n- `take(mut place)` for types with a default: CeeSafe has no `Default` interface. `replace(mut p, X.create())` says\n  the same thing.\n\n## 4. Tests\n\nEach section gets run-pass and check-fail corpus programs. Every new library function gets tests/support programs\nwhose expected values come from Python oracles: hashes, CRCs and the ordered map against a sorted dict model. The file\nAPI gets a hosted test that writes, syncs, truncates, reopens and renames. The tests run with `--check-leaks`, and\nthe interpreter must give the same results.\n\n## 5. Consequences\n\n- CosmoStore can delete `durable_shim.c` and the error adapters, and its fault registry no longer needs `unsafe`.\n  It can also use the library CRC32C and MD5.\n- Programs that already have their own functions named `assert`, `panic`, `replace` or `swap` keep them, since a\n  local name wins (§2.8). There are no other source changes.\n",
    348 =>
        "\n## 6. Questions, decided as proposed\n\n1. Release: one release, 0.19.0, since the file API, `@from` and the statics are new features.\n2. `@from` over a `From` interface (§2.3).\n3. A cursor used after a mutation panics, rather than giving the borrow checker a new rule (§2.4).\n4. `Directory.sync` is `Ok` on Windows (§2.1).\n5. Assertions are always checked (§2.8).\n\n## 7. As built (2026-11-06): changes from the accepted text\n\n1. **§2.8.** The panic message has the existing format: `ceesafe panic: <message> at <file>:<line>:<column>`, not\n   \"panicked: …\". `assert` without a message says \"assertion failed\". The runtime gains `ceesafe_panic_text` and\n   `CEE_PANIC_EXPLICIT`. A freestanding environment still defines only `ceesafe_panic`, so the freestanding contract\n   (profiles.md §4.2) is unchanged.\n2. **§2.9.** `[T; 0]` is not added. C has no zero-sized object, so the type would be laid out one way in C and another\n   in CeeSafe (offsetOf, the interpreter). The empty slice is spelled `[]` where a `Slice<T>` or `SliceMut<T>` is\n   expected, rather than `Slice<T>.empty()`. That is what the finding asked for (`buf[0..0]`).\n3. **§2.3.** `map` and `mapErr` are left out. A generic method in a generic struct cannot name the struct's type\n   parameters yet (`unknown type 'T'`), and that is a checker change of its own. `isOk`, `isErr`, `ok`, `err`, `okOr`\n   and `@from` are in. `@from` is recorded per `?` expression in a checker side table, so `HirExpr.Try` keeps its\n   shape.\n4. **§2.12.** The shifting is a loop of `swap`s in lib/core's `VectorEditing<T>`, not a `memmove`. That keeps it out\n   of the C backend and the interpreter. The index check is emitted at the call, as `ByteOrder`'s is, so a panic\n   names the program's line.\n5. **§2.15.** `Mutex<T>.constant` and `Vector<T>.empty()` are left out. `Vector` is a built-in type, and making it a\n   static initialiser is its own change. Static arrays of atomics are in, and they are what CosmoStore's registry\n   needs.\n6. **§2.16, G15.** A sweep panic counts as the tests' own when it is an unwrap in any file that declares an `@test`\n   function, not only inside an `@test` body. Test helpers live in those files: CosmoStore's sweep went from 63\n   failures in 200 runs to 0, all 63 being helpers' and tests' unwraps. `ceesafec test` writes `test-spans.txt` for\n   this. `--sweep-strict` keeps the old count.\n7. **§2.16, G16.** The headers beside a package's C file are copied with it into the build's `c/` directory, instead\n   of an `-iquote` flag, so `--emit c`'s output stays self-contained. Two different headers of one name are an error.\n8. **§2.4.** `OrderKey`'s method is `compareKey`, not `compare`. A user's `impl Comparable<u8> for u8 { fn compare … }`\n   (check-pass/interfaces.cee) would otherwise clash with lib/std's impl on `u8`.\n9. **Library names (new, §5).** A package's item may now have a lib/core or lib/std item's name. Before, that was\n   N0201, and it would have made every new library name a source change. It would have broken this compiler\n   (`src/support/sha256.cee`), CeeSafeLibs' `sha256` package (`Sha256`, `HmacSha256`) and CosmoStore (`Md5`,\n   `Crc32c`). The package's item shadows the library's in that package; the libraries still find their own\n   (`packageItem` looks in the package first, a library file only in the libraries). In C the library's item is then\n   `ceeT_lib_…`, `ceeF_lib_…` and so on (`appendShadowMark`).\n10. **Library statics (new).** A library's static is emitted in C only where compiled code uses it (`usedStatics`),\n    as a library function is. Otherwise every hosted program would carry the 24 KB of checksum tables. A program's\n    own statics are still always emitted.\n11. **Corpus.** A new directive, `// expect-library-panic: reason`, covers a panic that a library raises at its own\n    line (the stale OrderedMap cursor). spec/testing.md documents it. `parse-fail/expression-errors.cee` line 6 now\n    uses `/ 2`, because `* 2` is a dere",
    349 =>
        "ference (a check error, not N0102). Four snapshots changed only because the\n    library grew: the closure numbers in check-pass `async-timeout.hir`, `effects-of.hir` and\n    `string-braces-warning.hir`, and the order of two type declarations in `emit-c/slice-bulk.c`. Each was compared\n    with numbers normalised (or sorted) before it was regenerated.\n12. **The checksum test** leaves out the million-byte \"a\" message, which took the interpreter 3 minutes (the runner\n    times out). It passed in both modes before it was removed, and the 4,096 and 8,193-byte cases still cross many\n    blocks.\n\n### Found while building (fixed after 0.19.0, §8)\n\n- **N0272 false positive.** A value of type parameter `T` taken from `this` by a generic call, or a struct literal of\n  `Item<T>` built with a Copy field read through `this`, is reported as \"a value borrowed from 'this'\". Pushing it\n  back into `this` is then N0272. The B-tree works around it. Repro: `take<T>(v: borrow mut Vector<T>) -> T` called\n  as `let x = take(mut this.a); this.b.push(x)!;` in a method of `struct Two<T>`.\n- **N0212 across `?`, kernel profile only.** In lib/core,\n  `let length = v.length; v.push(x)?; var i = length;` was \"may not have been assigned\" when checked with\n  `--profile freestanding --kernel cosmoos`, and not otherwise. `VectorEditing.insert` now computes the index after\n  the push.\n\n### Tests run (2026-11-06, macOS arm64)\n\n- `python3 tools/run_corpus.py --compiler out/ceesafec-new --corpus tests/reference/tests/corpus --jobs 8`: every\n  family passes (freestanding is \"partial\" as before). The new programs are\n  run-pass `adr0075-language`, `durable-files`, `checksums` and `ordered-map`; run-fail `panic-message`,\n  `assert-failed`, `vector-remove-past-end` and `ordered-map-stale-cursor`; and check-fail `adr0075-errors`. Each\n  also passes with `--interp`.\n- Fixed point: the new compiler built itself (gen2), gen2 built gen3, and their `--emit c` output is identical.\n- `checksums` and `ordered-map` expectations come from Python (hashlib, hmac, tools/oracles/crc_model.py, and a\n  sorted-dict model in tools/oracles/gen_ordered_map_cases.py).\n- `python3 tools/contract_tests.py --ceesafec out/ceesafec` (gen2): every suite passes; Linux-only checks were skipped\n  (fortify, io_uring). The suites are the fixed point, -Werror, asan, tsan, interp, the support, hashmap, fault and\n  cancel sweeps, mutations, packages, spec, guide, debugger and mcp api. The corpus's one failure was\n  check-fail/systems-capabilities.cee expecting N0201 for a `PhysAddr` of the program's own. That is §7.9's change:\n  the expectation was edited (a reviewed change, noted on the line) and type-system.md §2, §26.8, §29.2 and §38.5 now\n  say so.\n- `ceesafec check` is clean in all 44 packages of CeeSafeLibs and CeeSafeApps. Their 24 test suites pass, and so\n  does CosmoStore's (54 tests).\n\n## 8. Amendment (2026-11-06): the two bugs of §7, fixed after 0.19.0\n\nThe owner: \"Fix the two bugs first, then migrate CosmoStore\".\n\n1. **N0272 on a referent's own contents.**\n   - **Callee.** A result whose type is a type parameter (`fn take<T>(v: borrow mut Vector<T>) -> T`) was tied to\n     `v` by elision, so it carried `v`'s own loan. `T` cannot name the reference's elided region, so each such\n     reference parameter is now a **deep** source: the result carries only what the referent holds\n     (`src/check/references.cee`; type-system.md §25.6).\n   - **Store.** Rule 2 of §25.7 made a write through `borrow mut p` take only loans of parameters that flow into\n     `p`, and a parameter never flows into itself. Now `p`'s deep placeholder may go back into `p`: what the referent\n     held may return to it (`checkParameterWrites`, `src/nir/borrow.cee`). The loan of `p` itself (its outer\n     placeholder) and loans of `p`'s own storage still may not.\n   - **Tests.** run-pass `generic-values-back-into-this.cee` (both of §7's repros; 0.19.0 rejects it) and check-fail\n     `values-into-this-still-checked.cee` (a",
    350 =>
        " view of `this.data` stored into `this`, and another parameter's view,\n     still N0272).\n2. **N0212 under the kernel profile.** In a freestanding build, a lib/core function over `Vector` gets error types,\n   and its N0293s are dropped with the libraries' other diagnostics (main.cee). Nothing was then left to stop the\n   ownership analyses, which reported the consequences: `let length = v.length; v.push(x)?; var i = length;` was \"may\n   not have been assigned\". Library functions with an error inside them are now recorded before those diagnostics are\n   dropped (`markUncheckedLibrary`, `Checker.uncheckedLibrary`), and the NIR checks skip them. Nothing can call them:\n   a caller would have the same type errors. `VectorEditing.insert` is back to that natural form, so the kernel\n   corpus programs (check-pass and check-fail `kernel-library`, `kernel-percpu*`, check-fail `async-recursion`) test\n   it.\n3. **`File`'s durable methods take `borrow this`.** CosmoStore's journal and store read through shared references to\n   their files. `readAt`, `writeAt`, `syncData`, `syncAll`, `length` and `setLength` never move the position that\n   `read` and `write` use, as in Rust's `FileExt::read_at(&self)`, so they no longer need `borrow mut this`. This is a\n   relaxation: code written for 0.19.0 still checks. `AsyncFiles` keeps its `borrow mut File` parameters, since\n   loosening a parameter would make its callers' `mut f` arguments N0249.\n",
    351 =>
        "# ADR 0076 — A package's compiler requirement: `ceesafec = \">=0.19.1\"`\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-06 (\"yes\", to adding the key so CosmoStore's manifest can pin 0.19.1).\n  Raised by the CosmoStore port (`CeeSafeApps/cosmostore/CEESAFE_GAPS.md` G23).\n- Scope: tooling.md §4.1 and §4.5, `src/packages/manifest.cee`, guide/packages.md, the package tests.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nCosmoStore needs ceesafec 0.19.1: `File.readAt` and the other durable methods take `borrow this` from that release on\n(ADR 0075 §8). Under 0.19.0, `ceesafec check` reports 12 errors in `durable.cee`, the first one N0248, and nothing in\nthem says that a newer compiler would accept the package. A manifest has no way to state the compiler it needs.\n\n## 2. Decision\n\n1. `[package]` takes `ceesafec = \"<requirement>\"`, a version requirement in the dependencies' syntax (§4.7):\n   - comparators separated by commas, all of which must hold (`>=0.19.1`, `>=0.19.1, <0.20.0`);\n   - `1.4` or `^1.4` is a caret requirement;\n   - `*` is any version.\n2. When the manifest is read (`check`, `build`, `run`, `test`, `fetch`, and a dependency's manifest as the root's), a\n   compiler whose version does not satisfy the requirement reports N0321 at the value, before anything is checked:\n   `this package needs ceesafec >=0.19.1; this is ceesafec 0.19.0`. A requirement that does not parse is N0321\n   with the field's shape.\n3. A development version (`0.20.0-dev`) is compared as its MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH.\n4. A compiler older than this ADR reports `unknown field 'ceesafec' in [package]` (N0321) at the same line, which\n   still points the reader at the requirement.\n\n## 3. Not included\n\n- Choosing or downloading a compiler: the field states a requirement, and the reader acts on it.\n- A requirement on lib/core or lib/std alone: they ship with the compiler, so its version covers them.\n",
    352 =>
        "# ADR 0077 — `Console.flush()`, `Directory.space(path)`, and what diskio needs: `IoError.StorageFull`, `File.openAppend`, `File.modified`, `File.setPermissions`\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-06 (\"While we wait, fix CeeSafe gap you found\"). Raised by the CosmoStore\n  port (`CeeSafeApps/cosmostore/CEESAFE_GAPS.md` G24 and G25).\n- Scope: type-system.md §26.3 and §26.10, lib/std `host.cee`, the hosted runtime, the checker's `Console` members, the\n  interpreter, guide/builtins.md, the corpus.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n- **G25.** Standard output is buffered by the C library when it is not a terminal. A program killed with SIGKILL\n  loses what it wrote last, and CeeSafe has no way to flush except writing to standard error, which flushes standard\n  output first. CosmoStore's soak test lost every line of its killed runs until it moved them to standard error.\n- **G24.** A storage engine must stop writing before the volume is full. CeeSafe cannot ask a volume how much room it\n  has (`statvfs`, `GetDiskFreeSpaceExW`), so CosmoStore's ADR-0006 takes a configured capacity instead.\n\n## 2. Decision\n\n1. **`Console.flush()`** writes what standard output holds buffered and returns `void`.\n   - It is hosted only: in freestanding code it is N0293, as `Process.capture` is.\n   - It is lib/std's `HostServices.flush`, over the runtime's `ceesafe_host_console_flush` (`fflush(stdout)`). The checker\n     routes it as it routes `File.kind`.\n   - Standard error is unbuffered and needs no flush. `AsyncConsole` writes to the descriptor directly, so it needs\n     none either.\n   - The interpreter writes its buffered output.\n2. **`Directory.space(path)`**, in lib/std, returns `Result<VolumeSpace, IoError>`. `VolumeSpace { total, free,\n   available }` gives the volume's bytes for the file system that holds `path`:\n   - `total` is its size;\n   - `free` is what is unused;\n   - `available` is what an unprivileged process may use. It is at most `free`, since root may have a reserve.\n\n   On POSIX it is `statvfs`, with fragment size × `f_blocks`, `f_bfree` and `f_bavail`. On Windows it is\n   `GetDiskFreeSpaceExA` (available, total, free). A missing path is `NotFound`. The interpreter has no volume of its\n   own to describe and answers `IoError.Unsupported`, so that a run stays reproducible.\n\n## 2a. Amendment (2026-11-06): what diskio needs\n\nThe owner then asked for the mail server's `diskio` package (CeeSafeLibs) to leave C. Its session said that diskio\n\"stays in C because CeeSafe lacks a portable errno accessor and packages can't pick sources per OS\". Neither is\nneeded once lib/std covers diskio's calls, because lib/std is portable already. What lib/std still lacked:\n\n3. **`IoError.StorageFull`**, \"No space left on device\", appended after `HostNotFound` so earlier variants keep\n   their tags. It covers `ENOSPC`, `EDQUOT` where the system has it, and Windows' `ERROR_DISK_FULL` and\n   `ERROR_HANDLE_DISK_FULL`. diskio's `FileError.NoSpace` needed a portable errno for this.\n   - **Source change:** a `match` that names the first twenty variants and has no `_` no longer covers `IoError`\n     (N0256), as with ADR 0044 and ADR 0060.\n4. **`File.openAppend(path)`**: write-only, created if missing (mode 0666 & ~umask), every write at the end\n   (`O_APPEND`; `_O_APPEND` on Windows). This is mode 5 of `ceesafe_file_open`. The interpreter's model writes at\n   the file's end each time.\n5. **`File.modified(path)`**: `Result<UnixTime, IoError>`, the last modification time with nanoseconds where the\n   system keeps them (`stat`; `_stat64` on Windows).\n6. **`File.setPermissions(path, mode)`**: `chmod` of the low 12 bits. On Windows it sets the read-only attribute when\n   the owner may not write, and does nothing else. It is how a mail store keeps its files 0640 and its directories\n   0750 under any umask. It is called on the new, still empty file, so the file never holds data others could read.\n   A process-wide umask setter was rejected: diskio's calls run on `Async.blocki",
    353 =>
        "ng` worker threads, and the umask\n   would race.\n7. The interpreter answers `Unsupported` for 5 and 6, since the stage-0 compiler's library can do neither.\n\nTested: a 2 MB disk image filled with `writeAt` reports `IoError.StorageFull`, and `Directory.space` then says the\nvolume is nearly full. A file given 0o640 shows mode 640 (`stat`). Appends from two openings land in order.\n\n## 3. Tests\n\n- run-pass `console-flush` (output written before a flush) and `volume-space` (`available <= free <= total`, a missing\n  path is `NotFound`).\n- A check by hand that a flushed line survives SIGKILL with standard output redirected.\n",
    354 =>
        "# ADR 0078 — `sendFile` never raises SIGPIPE; an open file's time and kind, and `File.openRegular`\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-08 (\"Go ahead and release CeeSafe\"). Raised by CeeSafe.Web: bug B19 and\n  request U22 (`CeeSafe.Web/docs/ceesafe-upstream-work.md`), for its M9 milestone. The owner asked for both to be fixed\n  here.\n- Scope: the hosted runtime (`ceesafe_rt_async.c`, `ceesafe_rt_io.c`, `rt.h`), lib/std (`host.cee`, `async.cee`), the\n  checker's File members, the C backend, the interpreter, type-system.md §26.3 and §40's sending files,\n  guide/builtins.md, the corpus.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n- **B19.** On Linux, a program sending a file over TCP with lib/std's `sendFile` or `sendFileAll` ends with exit status\n  141 (SIGPIPE) when the peer resets the connection during the send. Every other send in the runtime passes\n  `MSG_NOSIGNAL`. `sendfile(2)` has no flags argument, and nothing in the runtime blocks or ignores SIGPIPE.\n  - macOS is not affected: its sockets get `SO_NOSIGPIPE`. Windows has no SIGPIPE.\n  - For a server, one client that disconnects in the middle of a download ends the process, and every connection with\n    it. CeeSafe.Web found it with h2load, which resets its connections when its time is up.\n  - Two ways in, both seen on Linux 6.1:\n    1. **A failed call.** Linux reports a reset once, as ECONNRESET, to whichever call on the socket comes first. A\n       server with a read pending (any HTTP/1.1 server) takes it there, and its next `sendfile` fails with EPIPE.\n    2. **A partial count.** `sendfile` moves a file in pieces. A piece after the reset raises SIGPIPE, while the call\n       returns the bytes of the pieces before it.\n- **U22.** A static-file server answers with validators (`ETag`, `Last-Modified`, 304, `If-Range`; RFC 9110 §8.8, §13),\n  and must refuse what is not a regular file. ADR 0077's `File.modified(path)` and §26.8's `File.kind(path)` read the\n  path, which can name another file by the time it is opened: a check-then-use race. Opening a FIFO for reading waits\n  for a writer, so a request for one would hold its worker.\n\n## 2. Decision: `sendFile` never raises SIGPIPE\n\n1. **On Linux, each `sendfile` call blocks SIGPIPE on its thread** (`pthread_sigmask`).\n   - SIGPIPE from a send is directed at the calling thread, so it stays pending there.\n   - If one became pending during the call and none was before it, it is taken with a zero-timeout `sigtimedwait`.\n     That holds whatever the call returned: an error, or a partial count (§1's second way).\n   - The thread's mask is then restored. The process's disposition of SIGPIPE is never changed.\n   - A thread that already blocks SIGPIPE keeps whatever the call leaves pending, as after any write of its own.\n2. **The result is the call's own.** A partial count is returned as sent, and the next step's EPIPE is\n   `IoError.BrokenPipe`. So `sendFileAll` fails with an error, as a `write` would.\n3. **Both reactors use the same step.** epoll and io_uring share it (`cee_ready_sendfile`). macOS and Windows are\n   unchanged.\n4. **Rejected:**\n   - **Ignoring SIGPIPE process-wide at startup** (nginx, Go). It changes every program, including one writing to a\n     closed pipe on standard output, which today ends quietly.\n   - **`splice` through a pipe** instead of `sendfile`. It takes no flag that suppresses the signal either.\n\n## 3. Decision: an open file's time and kind, and `File.openRegular`\n\n1. **`f.modified()`** returns `Result<UnixTime, IoError>`: the open file's last modification time, from `fstat`\n   (`_fstat64` on Windows), with nanoseconds where the system keeps them.\n2. **`f.kind()`** returns `Result<PathKind, IoError>`, from `fstat`:\n   - `File` for a regular file;\n   - `Directory` for a directory, where the system lets one be opened;\n   - `Other` for a device, FIFO or socket.\n3. **How they are built.** Both are lib/std's `FileMetadata.modified(f)` and `FileMetadata.kind(f)`, over the\n   runtime's `ceesafe_host_file_modified` and `cee",
    355 =>
        "safe_host_file_kind`, which read the File's descriptor. The checker\n   routes the methods as it routes slice algorithms, with `f` borrowed. They take `borrow this`, so a file shared by\n   reference can be asked. They are hosted only.\n4. **`File.openRegular(path)`** opens `path` for reading if it is a regular file:\n   - **POSIX:** `open` with `O_RDONLY | O_NONBLOCK | O_NOCTTY | O_CLOEXEC`, so a FIFO's open never waits and a\n     terminal never becomes the controlling one. Then `fstat` the descriptor. A regular file has `O_NONBLOCK`\n     cleared and is returned. Anything else is closed and refused.\n   - **Windows:** `_open`, then `_fstat64`. Windows refuses to open a directory (EACCES); a stat of the path, after\n     the failed open, makes that `IsDirectory`. No file is read, so this races with nothing.\n   - **Errors:** a directory is `IoError.IsDirectory`. Anything else that is not a regular file is `InvalidInput`,\n     whichever device it is. A missing path is `NotFound`, as for `File.open`.\n   - **Encoding:** it is mode 6 of `ceesafe_file_open`.\n5. **`AsyncFiles.openRegular(path)`, `AsyncFiles.modified(file)` and `AsyncFiles.kind(file)`** run these on a worker\n   thread, as `AsyncFiles.length` does, and take `borrow mut File` as it does.\n6. **The interpreter:**\n   - `f.modified()` answers `Unsupported`, as `File.modified(path)` does (the stage-0 compiler's library cannot\n     stat).\n   - `f.kind()` and `File.openRegular` use the path's kind. Its files are reopened by path for each operation\n     (ADR 0075 §2.1), and nothing else runs between the check and the use.\n   - A file whose path was removed since it was opened is `NotFound` there. Natively it still answers.\n7. **Not changed:** `File.open` keeps its behaviour. A program that opens a FIFO to wait for a writer still can.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- A Linux server that sends files survives clients that disconnect. CeeSafe.Web's benchmark server, built with this\n  runtime, served a whole 1 GiB response after 80 clients dropped in the middle of one and h2load's resets, under epoll\n  and io_uring.\n- A static-file handler can take a file's validators from the file it sends. It can refuse FIFOs and devices without\n  blocking a worker.\n- Each Linux `sendfile` costs two `pthread_sigmask` calls and two `sigpending` calls, and a `sigtimedwait` after a\n  reset. Each call moves up to the socket's free space, so this is small next to the copy.\n- `IoError` gains no variant, so no `match` over it changes.\n\n## 5. Tests\n\nRun on 2026-11-08 (macOS arm64; Debian 12, Linux 6.1, x86-64, under epoll and io_uring; Windows 11 ARM64, under IOCP\nand WSAPoll; each natively and in the interpreter):\n\n- **`run-pass/async-send-file-reset`:** §1's two ways in, in rounds whose sends must fail or finish, never signal.\n  - With ceesafec 0.21.0 on Linux it exits 141 under both reactors.\n  - So it does with a runtime that took the signal only after a failed call (exit 141, 6 runs of 6, in the\n    mid-send rounds).\n  - With this runtime it passes everywhere.\n  - It passes the allocation fault sweep (85 failure points).\n- **`run-pass/file-open-regular`:** checks, in order:\n  - a regular file opens, reads, is `File`, and its `modified()` equals `File.modified(path)` (the path-based oracle);\n  - after its path is removed, it still answers `kind()` and `modified()` (natively);\n  - a directory is `IsDirectory`;\n  - `NUL` or `/dev/null` is `InvalidInput`;\n  - a missing path is `NotFound`;\n  - a FIFO made with `mkfifo` is `InvalidInput` (POSIX);\n  - the `AsyncFiles` forms give the same answers.\n\n  A mutant runtime without `O_NONBLOCK` fails it: the FIFO's open waits until the run times out.\n- **On Windows,** `/dev/null` was a regular file on the test machine (`C:\\dev\\null`), and `openRegular` rightly opened\n  it. The test tries `NUL`, a device in every directory, first.\n",
    356 =>
        "# ADR 0079 — Imported C enums\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-08 (\"Go ahead and fix CeeSafe\"). Raised by CosmoOS's VFS port (roadmap 2.0\n  SA7): two calls through function pointers whose C type takes an `enum vnode_type`, which UBSan's function check\n  refused in CI (CosmoOS run 42469167908, both arches).\n- Scope: grammar.md §2 (`ExternEnum`), type-system.md §31.6, the parser, the formatter, the AST printer, the checker\n  (declaration, scope, FFI rules), the type table, the C backend (spelling, mangling, the layout assertion), the\n  corpus, guide/ffi.md.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n- A C header can type a parameter, a result or a member with an enum: `int (*mknod)(struct vnode *, …, enum\n  vnode_type, …)`. CeeSafe has no spelling for that type. A port writes the enum's integer (`u32`) instead.\n- C calls `enum vnode_type` and `unsigned int` compatible types (C11 §6.7.2.2 ¶4), so a prototype or a direct call\n  that mixes them compiles without a warning. A call **through a function pointer** whose type says `enum vnode_type`,\n  to a function defined with `uint32_t`, is still a call through a pointer of another type. Clang's\n  `-fsanitize=function`, part of `-fsanitize=undefined` on Linux, refuses it at run time:\n  `call to function ramfs_mknod through pointer to incorrect function type`. Two places in CosmoOS:\n  1. ramfs's operation table pointed `mknod` at ramfs.cee's export, which takes a `uint32_t`.\n  2. vfs_dir.cee called `ops->mknod` through a field it declared with a `u32` parameter, reaching C filesystems\n     whose `mknod` takes the enum.\n- The same gap made every filesystem's readdir callback (`vfs_dirent_cb`, whose last parameter is `enum vnode_type`)\n  a C trampoline in a floor: ramfs, procfs, cosmofs and the VFS each have one.\n- Today's workaround is C: a wrapper with the enum-typed signature that calls the CeeSafe function with an integer.\n  The CeeSafe rule counts that as new C (CosmoOS's share gate failed on it, 9138b975).\n\n## 2. Decision\n\n1. **Declaration.** Inside an `extern \"C\"` block, `enum name: I;` imports a C enum type, where `I` is one of `u8`,\n   `u16`, `u32`, `u64`, `i8`, `i16`, `i32`, `i64`: the integer type the C compiler gives the enum.\n   - Its C spelling is `enum name`. With `@ctype(\"cname\")` it is `cname`, for a typedef\n     (`typedef enum {…} cname;`). `name` and `cname` must be valid, unreserved C identifiers (N0292).\n   - A block that declares one needs a `@header`, as for an imported struct (N0290). `@ctype` is its one attribute\n     (N0290 for any other). The integer must be one of the eight (N0291 otherwise: `usize`, `isize`, `bool` and\n     every non-integer type are refused, since C does not give an enum any of them).\n   - Visibility is that of a struct. The name is declared in the item scope like a struct's, so it cannot be declared\n     twice (N0228 / \"already defined\").\n   - Enumerators are not imported. A value is written as an integer constant of the type,\n     `const VNODE_DIR: vnode_type = 2;`, which the C side asserts as it asserts any constant a port copies.\n2. **The type is an integer type of its own.** `vnode_type` is an integer of `I`'s width and signedness, distinct from\n   `I` and from every other type: what holds of integers holds of it (literals, arithmetic, comparison, `checked`,\n   `wrapping` and `saturating` operations, `match` on integer patterns), and a value of one integer type is not a value\n   of another without a conversion.\n   - `as` converts between it and any integer type under §13's rules: to and from `I`, and to a wider integer, are\n     lossless; anything else is `truncate`, `checkedTruncate` or `saturatingTruncate`.\n   - It is `Copy`, has no `drop`, and may be a local, a parameter, a field (of a CeeSafe struct or an imported one),\n     an array element or a type argument, as an integer may.\n3. **Across the boundary** it is FFI-safe wherever `I` is (§28.3): in `extern \"C\"` prototypes, `@export` signatures,\n   `@callback` functions, `extern \"C\" f",
    357 =>
        "n` types and imported struct fields. There it is spelled with its C spelling,\n   so a function pointer type, a prototype and a definition all say `enum vnode_type`.\n4. **Lowering** (§31.3).\n   - Every generated C file that uses the type asserts it once, before its first use:\n     `_Static_assert(sizeof(enum name) == sizeof(CI) && _Generic((enum name)0, CI: 1, default: 0), \"…\")`, where `CI`\n     is `I`'s C type (`uint32_t`). C's compatible-type rule makes `_Generic` match exactly when the compiler's\n     integer for the enum is `CI`; a header whose enum is another size or signedness fails to compile.\n   - Its mangled name is its own (`N`, the length, the name), so generic instances over it never share a C name with\n     ones over `I`.\n   - The interpreter runs it as an integer of `I`'s width.\n\n## 3. Consequences\n\n- CosmoOS's `mknod` wrappers and its four readdir trampolines can be CeeSafe: an export typed with the enum is the\n  table's entry itself, and a callback type with the enum is called directly.\n- Nothing else changes: an `enum` in an `extern \"C\"` block was a syntax error before.\n\n## 4. Rejected\n\n- **Letting `u32` stand for any enum whose compatible type is `unsigned int`.** That is what failed: C's rule for\n  compatible types and UBSan's function check disagree, and only the second is enforced at run time.\n- **Importing the enumerators** (`enum vnode_type: u32 { REG = 1, DIR = 2 }`). They would be a second copy of the\n  header's values with no way for CeeSafe to check them. Constants of the type, asserted by C, already do this.\n- **A new kind of type** beside integers. It would need every rule that holds of integers restated for it; the C\n  spelling is the only difference that matters.\n- **A type alias** (`type vnode_type = u32;`). CeeSafe has none (§13), and an alias would still be spelled `uint32_t`.\n\n## 5. Amendment (2026-11-10): the integer is the target's\n\nRunning the corpus natively on Windows (ADR 0069) showed three gaps. The rule of §2.1 stands: `I` is the integer the C\ncompiler gives the enum, and a header that disagrees fails to compile.\n\n1. **The integer depends on the target.** C11 §6.7.2.2 ¶4 leaves an enum's integer to the implementation. GCC and\n   Clang on Linux and macOS give an enum whose enumerators are all non-negative `unsigned int`, and one with a negative\n   enumerator `int`; MSVC, and Clang targeting Windows, give every enum `int`. Values of the type are C's enum in the\n   generated code, so the assertion of the width and the signedness is what keeps C's arithmetic and comparisons on\n   them CeeSafe's; a width-only check was rejected for that reason. A declaration fits every target only where the\n   header's enum has the same integer on all of them, as one with a negative enumerator does (`i32`). The corpus's\n   headers do so (`ffi/imported-enums`, `ffi/c-data`); type-system.md §31.6 and guide/unsafe-and-c.md say it.\n2. **The assertion states the width and the signedness, not the C type.** MSVC makes no enum compatible with any\n   integer type, so §2.4's `_Generic((enum name)0, CI: 1, default: 0)` fails there for every enum. The assertion is\n   now `sizeof(enum name) == sizeof(CI) && (enum name)-1 < (enum name)1` for a signed `I` (`>` for an unsigned one):\n   what CeeSafe's integer rules depend on, on every compiler. It also accepts an enum whose C type is another integer\n   of the same width and signedness (C's `char` for `i8` where `char` is signed, `long long` for `i64` where `int64_t`\n   is `long`), which behave alike.\n3. **A member's check is spelled through its address.** In `_Generic(m, enum color: 1, default: 0)` Clang targeting\n   Windows reads `enum color:` as an enum with a fixed underlying type (a Microsoft extension) and fails. A member\n   whose C type is spelled `enum name` is asserted as `_Generic(&m, enum name *: 1, default: 0)` (type-system.md\n   §31.3).\n",
    358 =>
        "# ADR 0080 — The kernel floors: exported and imported C data, constants C checks, configured code, C locals\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-08: §6's questions 1–3 as recommended (\"Yes, as proposed\", \"Accept\n  all\"), so question 4's single release too. Raised by CosmoOS's storage port (roadmap 2.0 SA7): the owner, \"fix CeeSafe so\n  that it will accept the floors\". Every SA7 tree (`kernel/block`, `kernel-services/filesystem`, `kernel-services/vfs`,\n  `kernel-services/storage`) is CeeSafe except 40 `*_floor.c` files, about 2,800 lines of C. Each construct in them was\n  classified on 2026-11-08 against this specification and ceesafec 0.23.0 (§1); small probes were compiled where the\n  answer was not plain from the text.\n- Scope: grammar.md §2 (`ExternBlock`), type-system.md §28 (§28.1's table, §28.2–§28.4), §30.1, §31.1–§31.3, §33,\n  §38, §39; c-backend.md; the parser, checker, NIR lowering, C backend and interpreter; lib/core (`CChar`, `CLong`,\n  `AtomicU8`); the corpus; guide/unsafe-and-c.md and guide/kernel.md.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nWhat keeps each floor C, by kind (constructs; files):\n\n| | Kind | Constructs | Example |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | a constant CeeSafe writes, asserted equal to a C macro, enumerator, `sizeof` or `offsetof` | ~110 assert groups; 33 files | `_Static_assert(VNODE_DIR == 2 && EINVAL == 22)` |\n| 2 | C data that other C names by symbol: an ops table, an `fs_type`, a sysctl's variable, a header's `extern` global | 16; 11 | `extern struct fs_type ramfs_fs_type;` (bootfs.c), `extern uint32_t g_pool_fault_errors;` |\n| 3 | code, fields and functions that exist in some builds only, and the stand-ins for the others | ~60; 16 | `cfs->test_commit_armed` (a `CONFIG_DEBUG` field); `lockdep_is_held` (declared only with `CONFIG_LOCKDEP`); 34 self-test stand-ins the self-test table names in every build |\n| 4 | a C object on the stack: a `struct blake2b`, a `wait_entry` for wait.h's loop, a `struct pmm_stats` | 10; 6 | `struct blake2b s; blake2b_init_key(&s, …)` |\n| 5 | a C `char` by value | 10; 3 | `cfs_mac_tag(…, char domain, …)` |\n| 6 | a C function whose name is a struct tag | 4; 4 | `cosmofs_stats`, `cfs_pace`, `bootarchive_entry` |\n| 7 | an array of raw pointers as an imported struct's member | 3; 3 | `struct blkdev *held[12]`, `struct cfs *spare[4]` |\n| 8 | C data CeeSafe reads that it cannot declare | 2; 2 | `extern const char *const procnet_names[8];` |\n| 9 | an imported struct by value as a parameter | 1; 1 | `cfs_set_extent(…, struct cfs_extent run)` |\n| 10 | a byte member read atomically; a C `unsigned long` | 1 each; 1 | `__atomic_load_n(&p->dumpable, …)`; `sched_loadavg(unsigned long out[3])` |\n\nAbout 75 more constructs need no new feature (§5): ops tables only CeeSafe addresses (`static mut` + `@callback`,\n§39.4), self-pointing list heads and wait queues (`here`, or `borrow mut S as *mut T`), `static inline` helpers (§28.2,\nADR 0030), `container_of` (`offsetOf<T>`, §30.1), `kwarn` (`KernelLog`), the readdir callbacks (ADR 0079), and config\nswitches that a folded C constant macro already gives.\n\nKind 3 is the one where this ADR reverses an earlier choice. ADR 0043 chose `@cfg` on exports only (its option A) over\nconditional items (its option B): code inside a function reads a folded constant (`static DEBUG: bool =\nCOSMO_CONFIG_DEBUG();`) and is checked in every build. Two things break that here:\n- a **member** that only some builds' struct has (`test_commit_armed`, `fbig_injected`) cannot be named in a branch the\n  C compiler must still type-check, and a **function** declared only in some builds (`lockdep_is_held`,\n  `faultinject_set`) cannot be called there either, even dead;\n- the folded constant relies on the C compiler deleting the dead call: a static is a non-`const` C object (§39.5), so\n  at `-O0` the call to a function that does not exist in that build is still emitted, and the link fails.\n\n## 2. Options for configured code\n\n| | A. Keep ADR 0043 (floors) | B",
    359 =>
        ". Configured items and blocks, checked in every build (proposed) | C. Rust-style `cfg`, checked per build |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| What | config-only members and calls stay in C floors | `@cfg` on functions, statics, extern declarations and imported fields; a `cfg(\"X\")` block lowered to `#if` | items compiled away before checking |\n| Checking | as today | every item and block is checked once, in every build, as if every `cfg` held; only C's `#if` differs | the checker must know or enumerate the build's configuration |\n| Floors left | 16 files keep C | none of kind 3 | none |\n\n**B is proposed.** CeeSafe stays one program, checked once; what changes is that C sees `#if` where C needs it. A\nconfigured item may be used only under the same condition (§3.3), which the checker can verify without knowing the\nconfiguration.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n### 3.1 C constants (kind 1)\n\nIn an `extern \"C\"` block:\n- `const NAME: T = v;` declares that the C constant `NAME` (an object-like macro or an enumerator, from the block's\n  `@header`) has the value `v`. `T` is an integer type (an imported enum included), `bool`, or `usize`/`isize`; `v` is a\n  constant expression (§10). In CeeSafe it is an ordinary constant with that value, usable everywhere a constant is\n  (patterns, other constants, the interpreter; an array length is still a literal, §16).\n- `@cvalue(\"expression\") const NAME: T = v;` names the C side by a C constant expression instead:\n  `@cvalue(\"sizeof(struct cosmo_stat)\") const STAT_SIZE: usize = 144;`,\n  `@cvalue(\"offsetof(struct vnode, mode)\")`, `@cvalue(\"CFS_DVA(0, 1)\")`. The string may not contain a line break or\n  `\"` (N0290); it is emitted only inside the assertion below, so it cannot change what the program does.\n- **Lowering.** Every generated C file that uses the constant asserts, once:\n  `_Static_assert((NAME) == (CT)(v), \"…\")` (with the `@cvalue` expression in place of `NAME`), naming the constant\n  and its declaration's file and line. A header that disagrees fails to compile; it cannot miscompile.\n- `const NAME: T;` with no value imports the C constant as C's (an object-like macro): it is a run-time value only, not\n  a constant expression (N0338 in a constant context), and its C spelling is `NAME`. `T` is an integer, `bool`, or a\n  `*CChar` (a string macro: `COSMO_VERSION_STRING`). The interpreter cannot run a read of one (a fault).\n- **A fix:** a zero-parameter `@constant fn NAME() -> T` in an extern block is emitted `NAME()` today, which does not\n  compile for an object-like macro. It stays the function-like form; the object-like form is `const NAME: T;`.\n\n### 3.2 Exported and imported C data (kinds 2 and 8)\n\n- **`@export` and `@export(\"symbol\")` on a `static` or `static mut`** (ADR 0024's \"not yet\"): the static is C data with\n  external linkage under `symbol` (default: its name), declared in `ceesafe_exports.h`. Its type must have a C\n  spelling: an integer, `bool`, an atomic (as its integer, §31.1's atomic fields), a raw pointer, an `extern \"C\" fn`\n  type, an imported C type, or an array of these (N0291). The symbol follows §28.4's rules (N0292, N0201).\n  - A `static mut` is C's `T symbol = …;`; C may read and write it, so every CeeSafe use stays unsafe (§39.3).\n  - A plain `static` is C's `const T symbol = …;`, matching a header's `extern const struct vnode_ops cfs_dir_ops;`. It\n    may be of an imported C type when exported (otherwise N0337, §39.3): C cannot write a `const` object, so CeeSafe\n    reads it as unchanging. An atomic static is never `const`.\n- **Imported statics.** In an `extern \"C\"` block, `static NAME: T;` and `static mut NAME: T;` declare C data the\n  headers define (`extern T NAME;`), with the same types. A `static mut` is used as one (unsafe); a `static` is\n  read-only, for `const` C data and for data C changes only atomically (an atomic type). `@cname(\"symbol\")` gives the C\n  name when it differs.\n\n### 3.3 Configured code (kind 3)\n\n- **`@cfg(\"NAME\")`**, and **`@cfg(\"!NAME\")`** for \"where ",
    360 =>
        "`NAME` is zero\", on: any top-level function (not only an\n  `@export`), a static, an extern function, an extern static, and a field of an imported struct or union.\n  - A configured function or static is emitted inside `#if NAME` … `#endif` (or `#if !NAME`); an extern declaration's\n    prototype likewise; a configured field's `_Generic` and `sizeof` checks (§31.3) likewise.\n  - Two functions may share a name if one is `@cfg(\"X\")` and the other `@cfg(\"!X\")` (the configured definition and its\n    stand-in): they are one function to CeeSafe callers, must have the same signature (N0201 otherwise), and both\n    bodies are checked.\n- **The `cfg` block**: `if (cfg(\"NAME\")) { … } else { … }` (`!NAME` too), a statement whose condition is the built-in\n  `cfg` with a string literal. It is emitted as `#if NAME` … `#else` … `#endif`. Both blocks are checked. It is a\n  statement only, not an expression, and `cfg` elsewhere is N0290.\n- **The rule that makes one check enough**: a configured item may be used only where its condition holds: inside a\n  function or static with the same `@cfg`, or inside a `cfg` block of that condition (N0350, new). A configured field\n  is used under the same rule. So every build's C compiles: what one build lacks is used only inside its `#if`.\n- The interpreter runs every `cfg` as false unless `--cfg NAME` is given, and refuses a call to an extern function as\n  it does today.\n\n### 3.4 C objects on the stack (kind 4)\n\n- A local `var s: T;` (no initialiser) of an imported C type, or of a C-laid-out struct (§33.1), is a **C local**: C's\n  zero-initialised object on the stack (`T s = {0};`), at a fixed place for its scope. It is never moved, copied,\n  assigned whole or returned (N0294, as for any C state); it is used through `borrow s`, `borrow mut s`, its fields,\n  and `borrow mut s as *mut T`. Its address does not outlive the scope (the borrow checker's usual rule; a raw pointer\n  made from it is the unsafe code's obligation, U-rules).\n- It is not allowed in an `async` function (its frame moves), nor in a closure's captures (N0294).\n- So wait.h's loop is CeeSafe (a lib/kernel `waitEvent` over a condition closure), and `blake2b`, `fi_stats`,\n  `cosmofs_stats` and `pmm_stats` are locals.\n\n### 3.5 Smaller items (kinds 5–7, 9, 10)\n\n- **`CChar` by value** (kind 5): a `CChar` parameter, result, local or field is C's `char`. It is an 8-bit integer whose\n  signedness is the target's, with no arithmetic: `b as CChar` from `u8` (C's conversion, which every target CeeSafe\n  supports does modulo 256) and `c as u8` (the byte) convert; `==` and `!=` compare.\n- **`@cname(\"symbol\")` on an extern function** (kind 6): its C name, when its CeeSafe name must differ (a struct of that\n  name, `struct cosmofs_stats`). The rules of §28.2 apply to the C name.\n- **Raw pointer arrays in imported structs** (kind 7): a field `[*T; N]` or `[*mut T; N]` (and of `extern \"C\" fn`\n  types), for `T *member[N]`: elements are read and assigned, not borrowed (§31.2's rule for pointer members).\n  `[*CChar; N]` imports a C string table (with §3.2's imported statics).\n- **An imported struct by value as an extern function's parameter** (kind 9): the argument must be a place of that type\n  (`Ptr.toBorrow(p)` deref, a field, a C local); C copies it. An `@export` may take one too: its body sees it as a C\n  local (§3.4).\n- **`AtomicU8`** (kind 10), as `AtomicU32` for a `uint8_t` member (§31.1's atomic fields; `__atomic_*` on a byte).\n- **`CLong` and `CULong`** (kind 10): C's `long` and `unsigned long`, integer types of the target's width (64 bits on\n  every 64-bit target CeeSafe supports, 32 on Windows), converted with `as` as `usize` is.\n\n### 3.6 What stays C\n\n- **Module export tables** (`EXPORT_SYMBOL`, blk_floor.c's 15): a section, a used attribute and a Mach-O variant;\n  ADR 0043 already leaves them to C, and they are a module ABI, not logic.\n- **C-to-C invariants** (an on-disk enum equal to a uapi one): they compare two headers and name no",
    361 =>
        " CeeSafe constant;\n  they belong in the headers.\n- **Preprocessor defaults** (`#ifndef PROCFS_COMPILER`): a header line.\n\nThe CeeSafe rule (CosmoOS's roadmap 2.0) counts these as the named C floor.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- Of the 40 SA7 floors, all but blk_floor.c's export table can go; the C left is headers and module export lines.\n- New diagnostics: N0350 (a configured item used where its condition does not hold). N0290–N0294 and N0337–N0338\n  cover the rest.\n- `ceesafe_exports.h` gains data declarations and `#if` lines; generated C gains `_Static_assert`s.\n- The compiler's own sources must still build with 0.8.0, the seed chain's last compiler (docs/release.md), so none of\n  this may be used in `src/`.\n\n## 5. Port-side work that needs nothing new\n\nRecorded so the port does not wait for the release: the 13 ops/kobject/fs tables only CeeSafe addresses, every\nself-pointing static (probed 2026-11-08: the emitted C is `{ .next = &ceeS_HEAD, … }`), `page_to_virt`/`virt_to_page`,\n`faultinject_should_fail`, `vnode_get`, `container_of`, `kwarn`, the kobject name, `cfs_max_extents`, the\ncosmofscrash readdir (ADR 0079), and `fsctl_open`/`blkctl_open` (a C-laid-out CeeSafe struct, §33.1).\n\n## 6. Questions for the owner\n\n1. Configured code as §3.3 (option B: `@cfg` on items and fields, `cfg` blocks, checked once in every build), reversing\n   ADR 0043's option A? Recommended: yes.\n2. C locals as §3.4 (zero-initialised C objects on the stack, never moved)? Recommended: yes.\n3. The rest (§3.1, §3.2, §3.5) as proposed, and §3.6's floor left in C? Recommended: yes.\n4. Release it all as ceesafec 0.24.0, then CosmoOS's pin, then the floors ported file by file with the usual\n   verification? Recommended: yes.\n\n## 7. Implementation plan (once accepted)\n\nSpec first (grammar.md, type-system.md, c-backend.md), then in this order, each with corpus tests (ffi runs with C\nhalves, check-fail for every new diagnostic) and the contract suite: §3.1 constants; §3.2 exported and imported\nstatics; §3.5's small items; §3.4 C locals; §3.3 configured code (an ffi test built twice, the macro set and unset);\nthe guide; a release. Then CosmoOS: the pin, §5's port-side work, and the floors with §3's features.\n\n## 8. Implementation notes (2026-11-08, ceesafec 0.24.0)\n\nWhat the release has, and where it differs from §3 (each a narrowing; the floors need nothing more):\n\n- §3.1 as decided. A `const` C checks is an ordinary constant, so it is usable wherever one is, but an array length is\n  still a literal (§16). The C assertion is emitted once per program, beside the imported fields' (§31.3). A\n  zero-parameter `@constant` extern stays the function-like macro form; `const NAME: T;` is the object-like one.\n- §3.2 as decided, except that an exported static may not be an array yet (N0216): C's `T name[N]` is not CeeSafe's\n  array struct. No floor exports an array (ramfs's operation tables are CeeSafe's alone). An imported or `const`\n  static's address is taken through `uintptr_t` to CeeSafe's pointer type, as for a C array member.\n- §3.3, narrowed to what C needs: `@cfg` (and `@cfg(\"!X\")`) on exports, on extern functions and on imported fields,\n  and `cfg` blocks. `@cfg` on other functions and on statics, and a pair of functions sharing a name, are not needed: a\n  stand-in is the export's `else` block, and \"A and B\" is a nested block. N0350 (not N0349, which task sets have)\n  applies to configured C functions and fields; an export's own `@cfg` does not open its body to them, since ADR\n  0043 compiles that body in every build. `cfg` as a value is N0200, an unknown function, not N0290. The structurer\n  emits the blocks as `#if`; a block left unstructured is wrapped in `#if` with its label kept. The interpreter takes\n  every macro as 0.\n- §3.4 as decided: `var s: T;` zero-fills with `s = (T){0};` where it is declared (no `memset`, so no library is\n  needed). A closure may borrow one as any local; a move of it, a moving capture included, is the in-pla",
    362 =>
        "ce checker's\n  N0294.\n- §3.5: `CChar`, `@cname`, pointer arrays, `AtomicU8` (load, store, swap, compareExchange and the ordered load and\n  store: what C's byte flags need) and `CLong`/`CULong` (asserted `sizeof(long)` where used) as decided. **An\n  imported struct by value as a parameter is not implemented.** Its one use, `cfs_set_extent`, is an internal\n  cosmofs function that only a C host test calls with a struct; CosmoOS's port changes it to take a pointer.\n- Corpus: ffi `c-data`, ffi `cfg-code`, check-fail `c-data-errors` and `cfg-code-errors`. Two expectations changed\n  where this ADR allows what they refused (`ffi-declarations.cee:17`, a `CChar` parameter; `c-arrays.cee:22`, an\n  array of pointers), and three HIR snapshots' closure ids moved with lib/core.\n",
