, and any that changed or vanished is a miss. A chan",
    372 =>
        "ged header with the same unit text never reuses an object.\n  A hit copies the object; a miss compiles and stores it.\n- The cache is content-addressed, under `$CEESAFE_HOME/cache/objects/` (Question 5). An entry is written to a temporary\n  name and renamed, so agents that build at once can share it: two writers of one key write the same bytes, and a\n  reader never sees half a file. An entry that cannot be read is a miss.\n- Each `build` works in its own temporary directory and renames its output into `out/` at the end, so two agents\n  building the same package do not overwrite each other's C.\n- The cache never changes output: the C is generated in full on every build and is byte-identical; only the C\n  compiler's run is skipped.\n\n### 2.7 Per-package checking with interface records\n\n- **An interface record** is what a dependent needs from a package that its sources cannot give cheaply: whether it\n  checked without errors; the inferred effects (§32.1) of every function another package can reach (public items, and\n  internal ones a public generic body calls); what it reaches in the libraries; the instances and variants it needs\n  from others; the coercions to `dyn I` it makes, with each impl's async frame size, and the `dyn I` async methods it\n  awaits. Declarations and bodies are **not** serialised: they are parsed again from the package's sources (0.14 s\n  for 100k lines), so there is no second format of the language to keep in step.\n- Its key is a SHA-256 of the package's sources and manifest, ceesafec's banner, the profile, and its dependencies'\n  **interface hashes**. Its own interface hash covers its declarations, effects and coercions to `dyn`, and every\n  body a dependent compiles into its own C: generic bodies, the bodies of functions with a closure parameter\n  (dependents build `ceeXS_` variants of them, §2.3) and of async functions (dependents build `ceeAS_` instances). A\n  change inside any other non-generic body does not invalidate its dependents.\n- **Async frames through `dyn I`** are the one layout that depends on the whole program (§1.5): the frame `ceeAF_` of\n  `I.m` is the union of the frames of every type coerced to `dyn I` in any package. A build computes these sizes from\n  all the records (a cheap union), and a package's C depends on the sizes of the `dyn I` methods it awaits as on its\n  dependencies' interface hashes: a coercion added in another package re-emits every package that awaits through that\n  `dyn I`, even when no interface record it depends on changed. The frame is in the unit's prelude, so the object\n  cache (§2.6) sees the change in the unit's text.\n- `ceesafec check` in a package fully checks that package; each dependency comes from its record, checked and\n  recorded first if missing. A dependency with errors is one diagnostic, at its line in the manifest. Records live\n  beside the objects, with the same atomic writes.\n- **`ceesafec check --all`**: every package of the lock, in dependency order, with up to `--jobs` independent ones\n  at once; one JSON document whose diagnostics are sorted by package, file and position, with a `\"packages\"` summary\n  (name, errors, warnings). This is the work queue for many agents.\n- **Safety stays whole-program.** `build` links only packages whose record says \"checked\" for this compiler and these\n  inputs; any doubt is a recheck. A record is a cache of a result ceesafec proved, never a promise from outside.\n  `$CEESAFE_HOME` is per user: ceesafec creates its cache with mode 0700 and ignores (as a miss) any record or object\n  not owned by the user or writable by group or others. Sharing is between that user's agents only; a process running\n  as the same user could as well replace ceesafec itself.\n\n### 2.8 The staged plan\n\n| Stage | What | Needs | Measured by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A | §2.1 naming and package paths; §2.5 `--jobs` | — | 300k split units = files; C step ≤ the hand-run 69 s |\n| B | §2.2 preludes and content names; §2.3 per-package instances ",
    373 =>
        "| A | header bytes per unit; CPU of a split build ≤ 1.5 × one file |\n| C | §2.6 object cache | B | one-body change at 700k: one unit (and maybe its package's instances) recompiled |\n| D | §2.7 interface records; `check --all` | A's paths | leaf-package check < 1 s at 1.5M; JSON grouped |\n| E | per-package C generation: emit only packages whose record, dependencies' interface hashes or awaited `dyn` frame sizes changed; drop the trial pass per unit | B, D | rebuild after a one-file change at 1.5M < 30 s |\n| F | §2.4 `--lto`; then decide `share-generics` and `@inline` | B | `benchmarks/run.py`: split + LTO within 5% of one file |\n\nStage A ships in 0.24.3. B–F are tooling work, not language, and can land with any later release (0.25.0–0.27.0\ncarry the other ADRs of this set); D comes first, because the next M0 item, the agents' work queue, needs it, then B\nand C. Split output becomes the default for package builds after B (Question 2).\n\n## 3. Alternatives considered\n\n- **One unit per package.** Inlines within a package and has 120 units, not 3,000. But `src/runtime` alone would be a\n  unit of about 200,000 lines: one core again, and any change recompiles it. Per file, with LTO for release, is what\n  Bun's own build approximates.\n- **Fix the names only and keep one header.** Restores 3,000 units, each paying the whole header: 1.2 s per unit at\n  100k lines, growing with the program (§1.2.4). It makes the split build slower than one file: on hanan, 12 units\n  of the 300k program already take 292 s and 2,353 CPU-s against one file's 225 s (§1.1).\n- **Precompiled headers.** clang and gcc each have a format, MSVC another; they are tied to the exact compiler build,\n  cannot hold `static inline` copies cheaply across many units, and leave determinism to the C compiler.\n- **One home for each instance** (the first package, in a fixed order, that uses it), or **weak symbols.** A home\n  moves when a use is added elsewhere, rebuilding unrelated packages; weak functions do not exist in MSVC's C, and a\n  stale object with an old weak body would link silently.\n- **Helpers out of line, in one unit.** Measured 1.7–1.9× slower (ADR 0022 §6).\n- **A serialised interface (HIR or a \"header\" language).** Faster to load, but a second representation of the\n  language for every change to keep exact. Parsing is cheap; checking is what the record saves.\n- **Leave caching to `ccache` and the build system.** It would cache objects, but cannot see packages, interfaces or\n  effects, so it cannot make `check` per package, and every agent would need it set up.\n\n## 4. Not in this ADR\n\n- `@inline` or any other inlining control in the language (§2.4).\n- Sharing instances between packages (`share-generics`, §2.3), and the instance unit split by size.\n- One C symbol declared in several packages: ADR 0081.\n- Separate compilation for CosmoOS's kernel images: their own build keeps passing `--split` and flags, unchanged.\n- The language server's per-package checking: it can use the records once D lands.\n- **A bug found while probing, to fix on its own (0.24.3, with stage A):** explicit type arguments on a call through a package are ignored.\n  In `evidence/p1`, `alpha.twice<u64>(5, inc)` fails with N0205 \"expected 'fn(i32) -> i32'\", and\n  `let a: u64 = alpha.pick<u64>(1, 2, true);` with \"expected 'u64', found 'i32'\"; in one file, `twice<u64>(5, inc)`\n  checks. Bun writes such calls everywhere (`crate::f::<T>()`).\n\n## 5. Testing\n\n- **Contract tests (proposed):**\n  - two packages with the same file names, and one with `a/mod.cee` and `b/mod.cee`: one unit per file, named by\n    package path; a case-only clash is N0363;\n  - every corpus program built split twice, with `--jobs 1` and `--jobs 8`, cold and warm cache: byte-identical C, and\n    the same executable where the linker is deterministic;\n  - each unit compiled alone with `-fsyntax-only` and the strict warnings (c-backend.md §1): a prelude that misses\n    anything fails here;\n  - JSON diagnostics in a ",
    374 =>
        "dependency carry `\"package\"` and an openable path; `check --all` against a reviewed file;\n  - per-package check against whole-program check: for every package of the corpus and of the scale test, the same\n    diagnostics (the whole-program check is the oracle);\n  - a stale record (sources changed, compiler banner changed, a dependency's interface changed) is never used; nor\n    is a record or object not owned by the user or writable by others;\n  - a dependent rebuilt after a change only in a body it compiles itself (a function with a closure parameter, an\n    async function): its `ceeXS_` variants and `ceeAS_` instances are regenerated;\n  - an `async` call through `dyn I` in package `a`, then a larger impl coerced to `dyn I` in package `b` only: `a` is\n    re-emitted with the larger frame, and the program runs under the sanitizers;\n  - an `@header` file changed with the same unit text: the object is a miss.\n- **Run corpus:** the `clang -O2 split` configuration stays; add `gcc -O2 split --jobs 4` and `clang -O2 split --lto`.\n- **Benchmarks:** `benchmarks/run.py` with one file, split, and split + LTO; `vector-growth` and `string-building`\n  must stay as fast as one file (ADR 0022 §6). A new one calls, in a hot loop, small functions of another package,\n  shaped like ADR 0081 §2.4's safe C wrappers: split without LTO measures what those calls cost, split + LTO must\n  match one file.\n- **Scale:** `scale_test.py` at 300k, 700k and 1.5M lines, Mac and hanan: build wall and CPU time, peak memory under\n  hanan's 12 GB cap, bytes of C per unit, binary size with and without ICF, a one-body rebuild, a leaf `check`.\n\n## 6. Questions for the owner\n\n1. **Unit layout:** one unit per source file at `c/<package>/<path>.c`, never merged, with a case-only clash an\n   error? **Proposed: yes.**\n2. **Default:** `build` of a package splits by default once stage B lands (`--single-file` keeps one `main.c`;\n   single-file builds and CosmoOS's flags unchanged)? This reverses ADR 0022 §3.4's default. **Proposed: yes**: at\n   Bun's size one file is 21 minutes on one core.\n3. **Instances:** a copy per using package, named with it, rather than one home or weak symbols? **Proposed: yes**;\n   sharing from dependencies later, if the binary sizes of §5 ask for it.\n4. **`--jobs`:** default the number of CPUs (like `make -j` in a build system, or Cargo), `--jobs 1` serial?\n   **Proposed: yes.** Output does not depend on it.\n5. **Cache location:** `$CEESAFE_HOME/cache/`, shared by every package and agent on the machine, rather than each\n   package's `out/`? **Proposed: `$CEESAFE_HOME`**: entries are content-addressed and safe to share, and a dependency\n   checked for one package is then checked for all.\n6. **Diagnostic paths:** a dependency's `file` as the path from the current directory, plus `\"package\"`? It changes\n   the text form for dependency files only. **Proposed: yes.**\n7. **`--lto`:** opt-in, used by Bun's release builds, not the default? **Proposed: yes**, until §5's benchmarks say\n   otherwise.\n8. **Order of work:** stages A and D first (the scale test and the agents' queue need them), then B, C, E, F?\n   **Proposed: yes.**\n\n## 7. Implementation notes: stage A (2026-11-09, for 0.24.3)\n\n- §2.1 as decided. `nameUnits` (`c_split.cee`) gives each user file the unit `c/<package>/<path in the package>.c`\n  from its `SourceText`'s package and path in the package; a build of files is the package `--program`, and a `..` in\n  a given path becomes `__`. N0363 is checked in `generateC` before any C is made, at the later of the two files.\n- **Identical unit paths** (after review). In a build of files, two files can give one path: files outside the working\n  directory are named by their file names (so `/p/util.cee` and `/q/util.cee` are both `c/<program>/util.c`), and\n  `../x.cee` and `__/x.cee` are both `c/<program>/__/x.c`. 0.24.3's first build called them N0363, though they do not\n  differ in letter case, and 0.24.2 built them. `unitPaths` now gives a path ",
    375 =>
        "identical to an earlier file's the suffix\n  `-2`, `-3`, … before `.c` (`c/util/util-2.c`), the first number no earlier unit has, ignoring case: in the order of\n  the files, so deterministic. N0363 stays for paths that differ only in letter case. A package's units cannot be\n  identical (§2.1), so its paths never get a suffix.\n- **`--jobs` without `--split`** is a usage error (status 2): the C is then compiled in one command, and a `--jobs`\n  that did nothing would mislead.\n- **The compilers' output.** Each C file's compiler output is printed in the order of the files' paths; an output\n  identical to an earlier file's (a bad `--cflag` makes every compile print the same error) is printed once, followed\n  by `ceesafec: the C compiler printed the same for N of the C files`. The run corpus is built with `--split --jobs 4`\n  at `-O2` in the contract suite, and `package_tests.py` §16 checks through a logging C compiler that each file is\n  compiled alone to its own object and the objects are linked, and that a failure's output keeps the paths' order.\n- **Exported data in split builds** (found by that configuration; older than stage A). The header renamed every C\n  datum with the program's name, `@export` ones too, so C code naming `data_ops` (`ffi/c-data.cee`) failed to link\n  with `--split`. An exported static now keeps its C name, as an exported function does (c-backend.md §2.4).\n- **The host's temporary directories.** `ceesafe_host_temporary`'s name counter is atomic, since parallel compiles\n  (`cl` on Windows) call it from several threads.\n- **Debuggers and dependencies** (open). `#line` names a dependency's file `alpha/src/util.cee`, by its package,\n  while the C compiler runs in the current directory, which becomes DWARF's `comp_dir`. A debugger therefore finds\n  the root package's files (`src/main.cee` from the package's directory) but looks for a dependency's at\n  `<current directory>/alpha/src/util.cee`, where it is not, unless pointed at it (`settings set target.source-map`\n  in lldb, `directory` in gdb). Emitting the path from the current directory, or an absolute one, would make the C\n  depend on where the build runs, which the reproducible C (c-backend.md §2.4, §7) rules out; a later stage fixes it\n  (each package's units compiled from its own directory, or a source map in the debug information).\n  `ceesafe_program.h` and `ceesafe_instances.c` stay at the top as before (no `ceesafe_main.c` yet: C's `main`,\n  callbacks and `@export` constants stay in `ceesafe_instances.c` until stage B's per-package instances).\n- A dependency's source path is `<package>/<path in it>` (the location table, `#line`, panics); its diagnostics name\n  it by its path from the working directory, and the JSON document gains `\"package\"` for every file of a package build.\n  The root package's paths, and lib/core's and lib/std's (`lib/core/…`), are unchanged.\n- §2.5 as decided, with `cl` too: a split build compiles each C file with `-c` (`cl /c /Fo:`, `/FS` with `-g`), at\n  most `--jobs` at once on Thread.both workers that each take the next file (the largest first), and links the\n  objects in the bytewise order of their paths; the outcomes are joined in that order. The default is the number of\n  CPUs (`getconf _NPROCESSORS_ONLN`, `NUMBER_OF_PROCESSORS` on Windows): lib/std has no query for it. On macOS a `-g`\n  split build runs `dsymutil` after the link (the debugger check's `split` case found the debug information missing\n  without it: it stayed in the removed objects). `--emit obj`\n  and builds without `--split` are unchanged.\n- **Measured** (Mac, 8 cores, Apple clang 21; the 304,176-line scale program, 821 source files): `build --split --emit\n  c` writes 821 units, one per file, plus `ceesafe_instances.c` and three runtime files, in 29.9 s. `build --split\n  --jobs 8` takes **698.7 s** wall (3,724.6 s user CPU) and the program prints `300946541`. Stage A's target, a C step\n  under the hand-run 69 s, is **not met**: the hand run compiled 12 ",
    376 =>
        "merged units, and each of today's 825 units parses\n  the 54.7 MB `ceesafe_program.h` (§1.2.4), about 4.5 CPU-s each. As §3 predicted for \"fix the names only\", more units\n  make the build slower until stage B's preludes remove the shared header; stage B is what the C step's target needs.\n\n## 8. Implementation notes: stage D (2026-11-10)\n\n§8.1 to §8.4 describe stage D as first built; two adversarial reviews then found a soundness hole, a poisoned cache\nand a check up to 7× slower than 0.27.0's, and §8.5 says what replaced it for 0.28.0: where they differ, §8.5 holds.\n\n### 8.1 What was built\n\n- **`check` in a package** checks that package fully, its tests included, and takes each dependency from its\n  interface record. A dependency is still parsed and its declarations resolved; its bodies are checked again only\n  where a dependent compiles or reads them (`keepsBody`, `src/check/interface_records.cee`): generic code and its\n  instances, async functions, functions with a closure or function parameter or `@effectsOf`, `@constant`\n  functions, closures, C functions and `dyn` methods. Every other body is skipped: it gets no HIR and no NIR, and its\n  inferred effects come from the record (`seedRecordedEffects`). A dependency without a valid record is checked\n  first, alone, in **dependency mode** (no tests, no entry function, its files and items named as dependents name\n  them), with its own dependencies from their records, level by level, `--jobs` at once. `--whole-program` is the\n  check of ADR 0013 D4, kept as the oracle; `--package <name>` checks another package of the lock; `--record` is\n  the dependency-mode check, which prints the record (what the parallel checks run).\n- **A dependency with errors** is one **N0364** at its line in the manifest, and the package's check stops where the\n  whole program's would: a record keeps the stage of its package's first error (parse, types, the per-function\n  ownership checks, the instances' checks, later), and the N0364 is added at that stage (`afterNirErrors`), so the\n  package's own diagnostics are those of `check --whole-program` in its files.\n- **`check --all`** runs each package's own check in its own process, after making the records; one JSON document\n  sorted by package, file and position, with a `packages` summary (tooling.md §5.1), or text with a line per package.\n- **The record** (`src/host/record_cache.cee`), `$CEESAFE_HOME/cache/records/<package>-<key>.rec`, is a sequence of\n  length-prefixed fields: format, key, banner, package, profile, errors, warnings, the stage of the first error,\n  opaque, the interface hash, then per skipped function its path in the package, its declaration's offset, its\n  effects, and for each of sleep, alloc, suspend and write the explanation from it (the steps' texts, its end, and its\n  full length); last, the SHA-256 of the rest. Declarations and bodies are not serialised.\n- **The key** is a SHA-256 of the format, the banner, **the executable** (size, modification time, inode), the\n  profile, the package's name, manifest and sources with their paths, and the direct dependencies' interface hashes.\n  **The interface hash** covers the sources with the skipped bodies left out (their line breaks and last line's\n  length kept, so positions after them hold), what the record says (offsets aside), and the dependencies' interface\n  hashes: `package_tests.py` §25 checks that a change inside a skipped body leaves a dependent's record valid.\n- **Trust**, as §2.7: the directories are made 0700; a record is read from the descriptor whose owner (this user) and\n  mode (no group or other write) were checked, in directories that pass the same test; a cut, altered or foreign\n  record is a miss; writes go to a new name created exclusively (0600) and are renamed. Any record a check makes is\n  also used from memory, so the cache is never needed for correctness.\n\n### 8.2 Departures from §2.7, and why\n\n1. **The record holds what `check` needs, not yet what `build` n",
    377 =>
        "eeds.** It has the effects and the status; not the\n   libraries reached, the instances and variants needed, or the coercions to `dyn I` with frame sizes. Those serve\n   stage E's per-package C. Instead a package whose skipped bodies make a `dyn` table of an interface with an async\n   method, or that embeds a file (whose contents the key does not cover, tooling.md §4.4.1), has an **opaque** record:\n   dependents check its bodies, and its own diagnostics, not N0364, say whether it fails. So does a package whose own\n   check found errors in such a dependency (its checks may have stopped early).\n2. **The key adds the executable's identity** to the banner: builds of one version share a banner, and a record one\n   build proved is not the next build's to trust.\n3. **Explanations are canonical.** The effect checker's chains (N0295, N0360) named the operation found first by the\n   fixed point, which depends on the order functions and packages are visited; a record could not reproduce the\n   whole program's. Each function's witness is now the operation with the shortest chain, the first among equals\n   (`chooseWitnesses`; type-system.md §32.1). Interface bounds are named from their declaration with their package,\n   not by whichever use came first (`interfaceDisplayName`).\n4. **Parallel checks are processes, not threads.** Thread.both's threads have the system's default stack (512 KiB on\n   macOS), and a dependency with an expression nested 900 deep overflowed it (SIGBUS); a process has the main\n   thread's stack and gives its memory back.\n5. **Where owners and modes cannot be read** (Windows, systems other than macOS and Linux on arm64 and x86-64), no\n   record is read or written; checks then run one after another in one process, each dependency's record used from\n   memory. The \"not owned by this user\" case is not in the tests: it needs a second user; the mode cases are.\n6. **`build` is unchanged**: it, `run`, `test` and `fix` still check the whole program and read no record. Using\n   records there is stage E's, with the build-side facts of item 1.\n\n### 8.3 Found on the way: four quadratic passes in the checker\n\nProfiling the 1.5M check found the declaration phase quadratic, in the whole program's check as in a package's:\n`implRegionsOf` scanned every impl for every function; an impl's overlap check, `implements` and\n`attachGenericImpls` scanned every implementation or template; `checkStructRecursion` and `computeCopyAndDrop`\ncopied their state and revisited every struct after each instantiation; an instance was found by scanning every\nstruct or enum. Each is now indexed or incremental, with the same diagnostics. The whole program's `check` of `app`\nat 1.5M went from 65.8 s to 16.4 s.\n\n### 8.4 Measured\n\nMac, 8 cores, 16 GB, shared with other work (load averages from 15 to 140 during the runs, so wall times are\napproximate; user CPU is steadier). `scale_test.generate` with 120 packages; `p000` is the leaf (no dependency),\n`p119` the package with the most (110 in its lock), `app` depends on all. Cold: an empty `$CEESAFE_HOME`; warm: the\nsame command again.\n\n| Lines | Check | Whole program | Cold | Warm |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 304,176 | leaf `p000` | 0.06 s | 0.06 s | **0.06 s** |\n| 304,176 | `p119` | 2.3 s | 13.4 s wall, 32.4 s CPU (8 jobs) | 0.63 s |\n| 304,176 | `app` | 2.7 s, 360 MB | 4.0 s | 0.67 s |\n| 304,176 | `check --all` (121 packages) | — | 24.9 s wall, 83 s CPU | 10.1 s wall, 44 s CPU |\n| 1,514,176 | leaf `p000` | 0.13 s | 0.13 s | **0.13 s** |\n| 1,514,176 | `p119` | 14.6 s, 1.3 GB | 64.6 s wall, 167 s CPU (8 jobs) | 4.0 s, 0.7 GB |\n| 1,514,176 | `app` | 16.4 s, 1.25 GB | 23.2 s | 4.4 s |\n| 1,514,176 | `check --all` | — | 129 s wall, 418 s CPU | 52.8 s wall, 226 s CPU |\n\n(`p119` cold followed `p000`'s; `app` cold followed `p119`'s, so it made only the records still missing.) Before\n§8.3 the same 1.5M program took 53.5 s for `p119`'s whole check, 19.1 s warm, 375 s cold.\n\n- **Stage D's target, a leaf-package check unde",
    378 =>
        "r 1 s at 1.5M, is met**: 0.13 s, cold or warm. The JSON of\n  `check --all` is grouped as specified. A package high in the graph pays for parsing and resolving its whole\n  closure (1.5M lines: about 1 s hashing and reading, 2 s parsing, the rest declarations), a quarter of the whole\n  program's check when warm.\n- **`check --all` warm** is about 3× the whole program's check, since each of 121 packages re-parses its closure.\n  Caching each package's own report beside its record (keyed also by its tests and the current directory) would\n  make it a lookup; not done here.\n- The records of all 120 libraries at 300k lines take 4.2 MB.\n\n### 8.5 Revised after two reviews (0.28.0)\n\nThe owner's rule: **plain `check` is never meaningfully slower than `check --whole-program`, never accepts what it\nrejects, and reports the same whatever the cache holds** (tooling.md §4.4). What changed to meet it, finding by\nfinding:\n\n- **One check, never a cascade of them.** `check` is now one check of the package and its whole closure, in one\n  process. A dependency with a valid record that has no error and is not opaque is taken from it (parsed and\n  declared, its skipped bodies not checked); every other one (no record, an opaque record, errors) is checked in full\n  in that same check. With no usable cache (Windows, an unknown `fstat` layout, a `$CEESAFE_HOME` that cannot be\n  written: review B's 0555 case) or a cold one, that is the whole program's check, at its cost: the review measured\n  7× 0.27.0's there, from one dependency-mode check per package, each parsing its closure. The child processes,\n  `--jobs` for `check`, and `checkInProcesses` are gone, and with them review A's `argv[0]`/`PATH` spawn (A3).\n- **N0364 for every failed package of the closure** (A1, critical). A package with errors is never taken from a\n  record: it is checked in full, so its errors are found at the stage they occur, and the report replaces each\n  failed package's diagnostics with one N0364 at the line of the root manifest that leads to it (the first such\n  direct dependency), whatever its depth and whether the path goes through an opaque package. The staged N0364s\n  (`afterNirErrors`, `PendingError`) are gone: the stages are the whole program's because the failed packages are in\n  the check. The N0364's message says `ceesafec check --package <name>` (which works from anywhere in the lock; the old\n  \"run 'ceesafec check' in ../mid\" failed with N0324 where only the workspace's root has a lock, B4), and its note is\n  at the failed package's first error, with its code and message. A dependency's own diagnostics, its warnings too,\n  are never repeated, so cold, warm and cacheless reports are identical (`package_tests.py` §23 checks all three\n  against `--whole-program` for every package of the fixtures, among them the new `transitive-failures`: a failure\n  through an opaque package, through an ordinary one, through both, an opaque package's own failure, two failures at\n  different stages).\n- **Records are made from the check itself.** The effect checker records every package marked for it, not only the\n  root (`recordedPackages`, `opaquePackages` per package). Once the report is printed and flushed, each dependency\n  the check checked in full whose closure has no error gets its record from what was found; three ways of making\n  `p000`…`p005`'s records (their own dependency-mode checks, from `app`, from `app`'s whole check) gave byte-identical\n  files. Before the check, a missing record low in the graph is made first by a dependency-mode check of that package\n  alone, but only if a package depending on it has had a record (in a cold cache none has), within a twentieth of the\n  whole check's estimated cost, and while it lets more records be found.\n- **The key hashes exactly the bytes checked** (A2). Each package's sources are read once (`loadTexts`); the key's\n  digest and the parse use the same bytes, so an edit during a check cannot file a record under the new sources' key",
    379 =>
        ".\n  Review A's race (`p4/race.sh`) now reports the N0295 that `--whole-program` reports. No child's record is accepted\n  any more: there is no child.\n- **The compiler's identity** (A3, A5) is the SHA-256 of its executable, read once per process and only when a record\n  is wanted, from the executable as the system knows it: `/proc/self/exe` on Linux; on macOS the kernel's path of the\n  process (`proc_info`'s `PROC_PIDPATHINFO` through `syscall`, since `_NSGetExecutablePath` would not link on Linux\n  and 0.8.0 cannot configure an extern), then `realpath`. Never `argv[0]` or `PATH`.\n- **The directories are opened once** (A4): `cache`, then `records` from its descriptor, each with\n  `O_DIRECTORY | O_NOFOLLOW` and checked by `fstat`; records are opened with `openat(records, name, O_NOFOLLOW)`,\n  written with `O_CREAT | O_EXCL | O_NOFOLLOW` and renamed with `renameat` in it. `$CEESAFE_HOME` and its parents are\n  trusted by path (tooling.md §4.4.1). Windows' `rt.h` names failing stand-ins for `fstat`, `pwrite`, `renameat`,\n  `unlinkat` and `syscall`, which `ceesafec` never calls there.\n- **The interface hash holds no position** (B2). It hashes the package's tokens by their bytes, a skipped body as one\n  `{}`, not the text with its line breaks: a comment or a moved declaration leaves the dependents' keys. Two texts\n  could still carry positions into a dependent's record, so they changed: a closure environment's type was named by\n  a global count (`{closure environment 1168}`, which also made a record depend on what was checked before it), and\n  an explanation named a closure by its line (`with the closure at main.cee:12`). Both now name it by its place in its\n  function, `{closure environment 2 in Lexer.scan}`, `with the closure 1 in main` (type-system.md §32.1).\n- **`check --all`** (B3, B5) is one check of every package of the lock, every package's tests included, reported by\n  package, with records where they cut work (a package without tests whose record has no error or warning). Where an\n  error stops it before the effect checks, it checks again the packages none of whose closure failed, so that each\n  package's own errors are still found (`borrowfail`'s ownership error behind `broken`'s type error); a package's\n  diagnostics are those of the last round that checked it. The JSON's `packages` summary counts the diagnostics by\n  their `package`, each diagnostic appears once, no N0364 is added, and the text is in the JSON's sorted order.\n- **Explanations prefer an operation to \"may do anything\"** (B6): `chooseWitnesses` ranks a chain that ends in a call\n  that may do anything (a C function or `dyn` method without an effect list, or any of them for a write) after every\n  chain that ends in an operation, then by length (`vagueEnd`); a record's chain `length` is that rank (record\n  format 2).\n- **The rest** (B7): `--help` describes `check`'s modes; `--package nope` names the lock's packages (status 2,\n  without the usage); `ceesafe_exports.h` has the `extern \"C\"` guards of ADR 0088 §2.6 and spells `_Alignas` for C++\n  (`package_tests.py` §28 compiles a C++11 caller with `-Wall -Werror` and links it); `ceesafe_lldb.py` turns off\n  lldb's `objc` category, whose `Rect` and `Point` summaries matched a dependency's `@repr(C)` structs. A child\n  that failed to start no longer exists to report.\n\nMeasured on the same Mac, again shared (load averages from 10 to 130 during the runs, so the CPU seconds are the\nfigures to compare), `scale_test.generate` with 120 packages, 0.28.0 as first built (\"before\") against this one.\nCold: an empty `$CEESAFE_HOME`; warm: the same command again; no cache: a `$CEESAFE_HOME` with mode 0555.\n\nCPU seconds (wall in brackets where the load made them differ much); \"before\" at 1.5M is §8.4's, but for no cache:\n\n| Lines | Check | Before | After |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 304,176 | `app --whole-program` | 2.7 | 2.6–3.1 |\n| 304,176 | leaf `p000` | 0.06 | 0.06–0.07 |\n| 304,176 | `app` cold | 45.6 (14.9 wall, 8 jobs) | ",
    380 =>
        "2.9–3.3 |\n| 304,176 | `app` warm | 0.65 | 0.67–0.78 |\n| 304,176 | `app` after a comment at the top of `p000` | a cold check | 0.72–0.86 |\n| 304,176 | `p119` cold / warm | 43.6 (18.1 wall) / 0.66 | 2.8–3.2 / 0.71 |\n| 304,176 | `check --all` cold / warm | 98.0 (22.4 wall) / 55.7 (9.1 wall) | 3.2–3.3 / 0.71–0.74 |\n| 304,176 | `app`, no usable cache | 26.6 | 2.8–2.9 |\n| 1,514,176 | `app --whole-program` | 16.4 | 16.1–20.4 |\n| 1,514,176 | leaf `p000` | 0.13 | 0.15 |\n| 1,514,176 | `app` cold / warm | 23.2 wall / 4.4 | 18.1–23.5 / 4.4–5.1 |\n| 1,514,176 | `app` after a comment at the top of `p000` | a cold check | 4.8–5.2 |\n| 1,514,176 | `p119` cold / warm | 167 (64.6 wall) / 4.0 | 19.7–20.2 / 4.7–5.0 |\n| 1,514,176 | `check --all` cold / warm | 418 (129 wall) / 226 (52.8 wall) | 22.6–23.2 / 5.2–5.4 |\n| 1,514,176 | `app`, no usable cache | 196.6 (217 wall) | 18.6–22.7 |\n\n- **Cold** is the whole program's check plus making the records, which comes after the report is printed and\n  flushed (5–15% more in all, at 1.5M about 1.7 s after the report); the report itself arrives within a few percent\n  of `--whole-program`'s, the keys' hashing and the effect checker's recording before it.\n- **No usable cache** is the whole program's check: 7–9× faster than before.\n- **Warm** is about a quarter of the whole program's check, as before; `check --all` warm is now one such check.\n- **A comment at the top of `p000`**, which every package depends on, used to invalidate every record; now `p000`\n  alone is checked again (in dependency mode, before the check), its interface hash is unchanged, and the check is\n  warm.\n- The cache is never pruned: every edit leaves the records of the old keys. Pruning is left to stage E's object\n  cache, which needs the same policy.\n",
    381 =>
        "# ADR 0083 — Const generic parameters: `bool`, integer and enum values as generic arguments\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-09 (\"Accept all ADRs as recommended\": every question for the owner answered as recommended), the day it was proposed; to ship in **0.26.0** (with ADR 0085 step 2 and ADR 0087 step 1), after ADR 0086\n  (0.25.0), on which §2.3 relies. Raised by the Bun port's M0 (`~/dev/CeeSafeApps/bun/ROADMAP.md` §3, M0): Bun 1.4.4\n  cannot be ported file by file while `fn pick<const TS: bool>(…)` is a syntax error. The evidence: counts measured in\n  Bun's `src/` with two Python scripts run with `python3 -I` (§1.1), eight probe programs and one benchmark compiled\n  by `out/ceesafec` 0.24.2 built from main (§1.2, §1.3), all kept in\n  `~/dev/CeeSafeApps/bun/analysis/adr-evidence/0083/`, and the specification and ADRs cited below.\n- Scope: type-system.md §20 (a new §20.5), §16.1 (array lengths), §10 (its last bullet); grammar.md `TypeParams` and\n  \"Generic arguments in expressions\"; c-backend.md's \"Generic code\" row (C names); diagnostics.md (N0352–N0354); the\n  parser, the checker (name resolution, generics, instantiation), the C backend's names (`src/backend/c_names.cee`),\n  the interpreter; guide/generics.md, guide/coming-from/rust.md and zig.md.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nBun began in Zig. Its `comptime` flags became Rust const generics in the rewrite: one parser source, compiled once\nfor TypeScript and once for JavaScript, with every `if TYPESCRIPT` folded away. The parser's state is\n`pub struct P<'a, const TYPESCRIPT: bool, const SCAN_ONLY: bool>` (`src/js_parser/p.rs:242`), its methods sit in 30\n`impl<…, const TS: bool, const SCAN: bool> P<'a, TS, SCAN>` blocks, and the entry point picks an instance from a\nrun-time option (`src/js_parser/parse/parse_entry.rs:462`):\n\n```rust\nif self.options.ts { self._parse::<true>() } else { self._parse::<false>() }\n```\n\nCeeSafe has type parameters only (type-system.md §20, ADR 0005), and `const` is a reserved word (grammar.md §1.3).\n\n### 1.1 What Bun uses (measured 2026-11-09)\n\nMethod: `measure.py` and `measure2.py` (in `analysis/adr-evidence/0083/`) walk `upstream/src` with the same `SKIP`\nset as `analysis/bun_rust_features.py`, so the file set is the 3,226 `.rs` files under `src/` outside `test`/`tests`\ndirectories. They strip comments, and match a const parameter as `[<,]\\s*const\\s+NAME\\s*:\\s*TYPE`. The item kind is\nthe nearest preceding `fn|struct|enum|impl|trait|type` keyword. Body uses are regexes over the item's brace-balanced\nbody, so they are approximate (±10%). Comments account for the difference with a reviewer's recount over the same\nfiles (1,456 parameters, 940 `bool`, 767 `const fn`): the same regex with comments kept gives 1,457, 949 and 799. The\nroadmap's 1,607 came from a coarser regex that saw only a first parameter and folded `const fn` in; the split is below.\n\n| Measure | Count |\n|---|---|\n| const generic parameters | **1,409**, in 1,156 items (947 with one, 169 with two, 40 with three or four) |\n| by type | `bool` 902 (64%); `usize` 216, `u8` 191, `u32` 7, `i32` 2 (30%); enums 91 (6%: `NodeFSFunctionEnum` 15, `Lifetime` 12, `bake::Side` 11, `StringEncoding` 7, …; Bun enables `adt_const_params`) |\n| by item | `fn` 600, `impl` 505, `struct` 242, `type` alias 50, `trait` 9, `enum` 2 |\n| top crates | `runtime` 374, `lint` 191, `js_parser` 123, `sema` 103, `jsc` 90, `collections` 81, `paths` 63 |\n| use in a `fn`'s body (600) | passed on to another const generic 244; a branch (`if`, `&&`, `match`, `==`) 205; not named (only passed through types) 143; arithmetic or `as` 19; an array length 3 |\n| integer parameters used as an array length in their item | 32 (structs 13, impls 15: `BoundedArray<T, const BUFFER_CAPACITY: usize>`, 26 uses; `SmolList`, `ShortList`, `Grouped`) |\n| defaults (`const X: bool = false`) | 15 |\n| `const fn` (separate, §4) | 762; median body 3 lines, 382 of 723 bodies ≤ 3 lines, 153 with `if`/`match`, 45 with a loop |\n\nHow",
    382 =>
        " call sites supply the values (regexes over `::<…>` and `Name<…>`):\n\n| Argument form | Count |\n|---|---|\n| `bool` literal in a turbofish (`::<true>`) | 799 (644 as the first argument); 189 more in type arguments (`P<'a, false, false>`) |\n| integer literal in a turbofish | 313 |\n| `{ Path::Variant }` or `{ path::CONST }` | 364 |\n| `{ expression }` other than a path | about 30: `{ StmtTag::COUNT + 1 }` (`src/lint/runner.rs:712`), `{ cfg!(windows) }` 6, `{ Encoding::Utf16le as u8 }`, macro arguments |\n| `{ !FLAG }` or `{ A + B }` over a const parameter (generic const expressions) | 0 (`generic_const_exprs` is enabled in 3 files, unused at these sites) |\n\nSo the values are literals, named constants, enum variants and the caller's own const parameters. Arithmetic on a\nparameter inside a generic signature does not occur. Real examples:\n\n- `src/http_jsc/websocket_client.rs:158`: `pub struct WebSocket<const SSL: bool> {`, with\n  `impl<const SSL: bool> WebSocket<SSL> {` (`:227`) and `impl<const SSL: bool> Drop for WebSocket<SSL>` (`:1835`).\n- `src/shell_parser/parse.rs:2198`: `pub struct Lexer<'bump, const ENCODING: StringEncoding> {`.\n- `src/lint/runner.rs:22`: `pub(crate) struct Grouped<const ENDS: usize> { … starts: [u32; ENDS], …`.\n- `src/install/lockfile.rs:1648`: `pub(crate) fn hoist<const METHOD: tree::BuilderMethod>(`, called as\n  `(*lf).hoist::<{ tree::BuilderMethod::Filter }>(` (`src/install/prune.rs:856`).\n\n### 1.2 What ceesafec 0.24.2 does (probes run 2026-11-09)\n\n| Probe | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `fn pick<const TS: bool>(n: u32) -> u32`, `pick<true>(1)` (`analysis/probes/g1_const_generic.cee`) | N0100 \"expected identifier, found 'const', which is a reserved word\" |\n| `const N: usize = 4;` then `let a: [u8; N] = [0u8; N];` | N0259 \"an array length must be an integer literal\" (§16.1; ADR 0074 §7 kept it so) |\n| `const D: u32 = double(2);` | N0217 \"constant expressions cannot contain calls\" (§10) |\n| an interface method without a receiver, `F.typescript()` in `pick<F: Flavor>` | N0267 \"needs a receiver\", N0200 \"cannot find 'F'\" (ADR 0086 §2.3 lifts this) |\n| marker types: `struct Ts {}`, `impl Flavor for Ts { fn typescript(borrow this) -> bool => true; }`, `fn pick<F: Flavor>(f: borrow F, n: u32)` | accepted; C has `ceeG_4pick1S2Ts` and `ceeG_4pick1S2Js` |\n| `struct Parser<F: Flavor> { flavor: F; … }`, and `struct Bare<F> { pos: usize; }` with `F` unused | accepted, runs |\n| a plain run-time `ts: bool` parameter | accepted |\n\nSo today there are two ways around: a run-time parameter, or a type parameter bounded by an interface that answers\nthe flag (marker types). Nothing gives a `usize` value in a type.\n\n### 1.3 What the flag costs at run time (measured 2026-11-09)\n\n`b_runtime.cee` and `b_marker.cee`: one recursive scanner over a 10 MB buffer of pseudo-random bytes, run 100 times,\nwith three flag tests per byte; the flag is a `bool` parameter in the first and a marker type in the second (the\nshape this ADR's instances compile to). Built with `ceesafec build` (clang 21, `-O2`, Apple arm64), three runs each:\n\n| Version | User time (s) | Mean |\n|---|---|---|\n| run-time `bool` parameter | 3.45, 3.62, 3.50 | 3.52 |\n| monomorphised (marker type) | 2.97, 2.97, 2.92 | 2.95 |\n\nThe run-time flag is **19% slower** on this branch-dense loop: clang does not clone a recursive function for a\nconstant argument at `-O2`, so every test stays. A real parser does more work per test, so its cost is smaller, but a\nstruct-level flag is worse: `P`'s methods would read `this.typescript` from memory at each test. The benchmark is a\nmicro-benchmark; it shows the direction and the order of size, not Bun's number.\n\n## 2. Proposal\n\nConst parameters of `bool`, integer and fieldless-enum types on functions, structs, enums, interfaces, interface\nmethods and impls, checked once and monomorphised like type parameters. Alternatives are in §3.\n\n### 2.1 Syntax\n\n```text\nTypeParams = '<' TypeParam (',' TypeParam)* '>'\nTypeParam  = 'region' Identifier\n  ",
    383 =>
        "         | 'const' Identifier ':' Type                       (* ADR 0083: a const parameter *)\n           | Identifier (':' Type ('+' Type)*)?\nGenericArg = Type | ConstArg\nConstArg   = 'true' | 'false' | IntLiteral | '(' Expr ')'      (* a name or E.V parses as a Type; §2.4 *)\n                                                               (* '(' Expr ')' only in a type position *)\n```\n\n- `const` stays a reserved word; it gains this one position. No new keyword.\n- Region parameters come first (§25.1); const and type parameters may then come in any order, as in Bun\n  (`fn to_json<W: fmt::Write, const STYLE: ToJsonStyle>`, `enum ShortList<T, const N: usize, L>`).\n- In a type position (`let g: Grouped<(StmtTag.COUNT + 1)>`), `<` cannot be a comparison, so every `ConstArg` is\n  allowed. Generic arguments in expressions (grammar.md) gain only `true` and `false` among their tokens, not\n  `( … )`: `f(i < (k), j > (k))` compiles today and would otherwise read as the call `i<(k), j>(k)`. A computed\n  argument in an expression (Bun's `::<{ StmtTag::COUNT + 1 }>`, about 30 sites) names a constant instead:\n  `const ENDS: usize = StmtTag.COUNT + 1;`, then `counted<ENDS>(…)`.\n- Adding `true`/`false` still changes how one shape parses: `f(i < k, true, j > (k))` compiles today, and becomes the\n  call `i<k, true, j>(k)`, an error. No program changes meaning silently: the generic reading needs `i` to name a\n  generic item and the comparison reading an ordered value, so the program stops compiling and the error points at\n  it; grammar.md's existing advice applies, `(i < k)`. Before 0.26.0, the release scans the corpus, the compiler and\n  `~/dev/CeeSafeApps` for `<` … `true|false` … `>` followed by `(`, `.` or `{` inside an argument list, as ADR 0085\n  §2.6 does for its change, and records the result.\n\n```ceesafe\nenum Encoding { Ascii, Utf16 }\n\nstruct Grouped<const ENDS: usize> {\n    starts: [u32; ENDS];\n}\n\nstruct Parser<const TS: bool, const SCAN: bool> {\n    pos: usize;\n    fn step(borrow mut this) {\n        if (TS) { this.pos += 2; } else { this.pos += 1; }\n    }\n}\n\nenum Method { Filter, All }\n\nfn hoist<const M: Method>(n: u32) -> u32 => match (M) { Method.Filter => n, Method.All => n + 1 };\n\nfn run<const TS: bool>() -> usize {\n    var p = Parser<TS, false> { pos: 0 };\n    p.step();\n    return p.pos;\n}\n\nfn parse(ts: bool) -> usize {\n    if (ts) { return run<true>(); }\n    return run<false>();\n}\n```\n\n### 2.2 Parameter types\n\nA const parameter's type is `bool`, an integer type (`i8`–`i64`, `u8`–`u64`, `isize`, `usize`), or a fieldless enum\n(§15.1) that the program or a dependency declares. Anything else (a float, `StringView`, a struct, an enum with\npayloads, an imported C enum, another parameter `T`) is **N0352 const-parameter-type**. These are the types whose\nvalues are compared with `==` exactly (ADR 0072), so two arguments name the same instance iff they are equal.\n\n### 2.3 Inside the generic item (checked once, §20.3)\n\n- A const parameter `N` is a value of its type, the same in every use: it may be read wherever a run-time value of\n  that type may (operands, arguments, `if (TS)`, `match (M)`). It is not a place: assigning or borrowing mutably is\n  N0211.\n- A `usize` const parameter may be an **array length**: `[T; N]` and `[v; N]`. In the generic body, `[T; N]` is equal\n  only to `[T; N]` with the same parameter; `a.length` is `N`.\n- It may be passed on, bare, as a const argument: `run<TS>()`, `Parser<TS, true>`, `Grouped<ENDS>`. An expression over\n  a parameter as an argument or an array length (`[u8; N + 1]`, `run<!TS>()`) is **N0353 const-argument** (§2.4): it\n  would need type equality modulo arithmetic, which Bun does not use (§1.1) and §20.4's termination rule would lose.\n- **Not a pattern.** A bare identifier in a pattern that names a const parameter in scope is N0358, whose help\n  suggests a guard (`x if (x == TS) =>`, ADR 0085 §2.2) or an `if`. The arm is checked once with the value unknown,\n  so it cannot count",
    384 =>
        " as a literal for coverage, and making it a binding would hide the parameter. ADR 0085 §2.6's\n  rule (\"a `const` in scope\") follows this one: it looks up const parameters too and reports them so. A later ADR\n  may make it a constant pattern; that only accepts more programs.\n- The body is checked once with the value unknown, so **both branches of `if (TS)` are checked**, as for type\n  parameters and as ADR 0080's `cfg` blocks. Code after `if (TS) { return 1; }` stays reachable.\n- A closure in the body reads `TS` without capturing it: it is a constant of the instance, not a local, and adds no\n  effect or loan.\n- An interface may have const parameters (`interface Pools<const SSL: bool>`), as it may have type parameters, and\n  so may an interface method, under ADR 0086 §2.3's rule for generic methods: instantiated per call through a bound\n  or on a concrete type, and a method with a const parameter makes `dyn I` N0331, naming it, as a type parameter does.\n  There is no reason to differ: a `dyn` table holds one function per method either way.\n- A generic impl follows ADR 0054: `impl<const E: Encoding> CharIter for ShellCharIter<E> { … }`; the parameter\n  appears in the target. An inherent impl names the struct's parameters in order (§21.1): `impl<const TS: bool, const\n  SCAN: bool> Parser<TS, SCAN>`.\n- **Interface constants** (ADR 0086 §2.4) are the other per-instance constant. The port maps each Rust form to its\n  own: a const generic parameter (`<const SSL: bool>`) becomes a const parameter; a trait's associated constant\n  (`const HAS_ON_DATA: bool;`, read as `H::HAS_ON_DATA`) becomes an interface constant, read as `H.HAS_ON_DATA`.\n  In generic code `H.HAS_ON_DATA` is a value, not a constant (0086 §2.4), so it cannot be a const argument or an\n  array length there (N0353); on a concrete type, `Ts.HAS_ON_DATA` is an associated constant and can be (§2.4).\n\n### 2.4 Arguments\n\nA const argument is evaluated at compile time to a value of the parameter's type (§10's rules: an overflow, or a value\nout of the type's range, is N0217). It is one of:\n- `true`, `false`, or an integer literal (typed by the parameter: `Grouped<3>` is `usize`);\n- the name of a constant (§10, an associated constant `StmtTag.COUNT` included) or an enum variant `Method.Filter`;\n- inside a generic item, one of its own const parameters, bare;\n- in a type position only (§2.1), a parenthesised constant expression (§10) that names no const parameter:\n  `let g: Grouped<(StmtTag.COUNT + 1)>`.\n\nA name in argument position is resolved by the parameter's kind: a value for a const parameter, a type for a type\nparameter. A type where a value is expected, or a value where a type is, is **N0354 generic-argument-kind**.\n\nA run-time value is **N0353 const-argument**. Its message names the dispatch that Bun itself writes:\n\n```text\nerror[N0353]: the argument for the const parameter 'TS' of 'run' must be a constant: 'ts' is a run-time value\n  = help: choose the instance at run time: `if (ts) run<true>() else run<false>()` (type-system.md §20.5)\n```\n\n### 2.5 Inference\n\nAs §20.2: a const parameter is inferred only from argument **types** it appears in (`fn sum<const N: usize>(a:\n[u8; N])` called with a `[u8; 8]`; `l: borrow Lexer<E>` with a `Lexer<Encoding.Ascii>`), and from an expected type\n(`let g: Grouped<4> = Grouped { … }`). Never from a run-time value. Otherwise N0265, \"write the generic arguments\".\n\n### 2.6 Instantiation and C names (§20.4)\n\n- Each distinct argument list is an instance: a copy with the values substituted, checked again and compiled to C.\n  `Parser<true, false>` and `Parser<false, false>` are different types. An instance that does not check is reported at\n  its use (N0222, naming the values); in particular an array length that is 0 or too large is N0259 there.\n- The 64-deep instantiation limit (N0266) applies; since arguments in generic bodies are bare parameters or\n  constants, no chain can grow without bound.\n- C names (c-backend.md \"Generic cod",
    385 =>
        "e\"): a value argument mangles as `L`, the type's code, the value in decimal (`n`\n  before a negative one; `0`/`1` for `bool`; the variant's index for an enum), then `_`. `L` is no type code today\n  (`appendMangle`, `src/backend/c_names.cee:92`), and `_` ends the number, so the codes stay prefix-free:\n  `run<true>` is `ceeG_3run1Lbool1_`, `Grouped<5>` is `ceeT_7Grouped1Lusize5_`, `hoist<Method.Filter>` is\n  `ceeG_5hoist1L` + `Method`'s code + `0_`. Members and generic methods follow\n  (`src/backend/c_names.cee:467`).\n- The rule only changes the mangled arguments, so it composes with ADR 0082 §2.3's package prefix (its stage B,\n  `ceeG_<package>_<len><name><mangled arguments>`): `run<true>` instantiated in package `parser` is\n  `ceeG_parser_3run1Lbool1_`.\n- The C of an instance holds `if (1)` or `if (0)`, and the C compiler removes the dead side at `-O2`; `-g` builds\n  (`-O0`) keep it, which is correct, since both sides were checked and compile in every instance.\n- The interpreter runs instances as it runs type instances: it never sees a parameter.\n\n### 2.7 Array lengths from named constants (amends §10 and ADR 0074)\n\n§16.1 requires an integer literal, and ADR 0074 (§2.7, its implementation notes §7) kept named constants out. With a\n`usize` parameter allowed in `[T; N]`, a `usize` constant must be allowed too, or `[u8; MAX_PATH]` stays refused while\n`[u8; N]` is accepted. Proposed: an array length is an integer literal, a `usize` constant (top-level or associated,\n`Oid.LENGTH`), or a `usize` const parameter in scope; the range rule of §16.1 applies to the value. This removes the\nlast bullet of §10 (\"Array lengths in types and repeats stay integer literals\").\n\n### 2.8 Relation to earlier decisions\n\n- **Extends** ADR 0005 and type-system.md §20: a second parameter kind, under the same \"checked once, then\n  monomorphised\" model; a new §20.5 states §2.2–§2.6.\n- **Amends** §16.1, §10 and ADR 0074 §2.7 (§2.7); grammar.md's `TypeParams` and generic-argument rule.\n- **Builds on** ADR 0086 §2.3 (generic interface methods and N0331) and §2.4 (interface constants), and sets the\n  rule ADR 0085 §2.6 follows for const parameters in patterns (§2.3).\n- **Unaffected:** ADR 0043 and ADR 0080's `@cfg`, which is about a C build's macros, not CeeSafe values; the two\n  compose (`if (cfg(\"X\"))` inside a generic body).\n- **Updates the guide:** guide/coming-from/zig.md (\"Generics, not `comptime`\") gains `comptime` flags →\n  const parameters; guide/coming-from/rust.md's list of what is missing loses const generics.\n\n## 3. Alternatives considered\n\n| | A. Const parameters: `bool`, integers, fieldless enums (proposed) | B. `bool` only, or an attribute | C. A translation rule only | D. Marker types (today; cheaper with ADR 0086) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| What | `<const N: T>` on functions, structs, enums, interfaces, their methods and impls; monomorphised like type parameters | `<const TS: bool>`; or `@specialise` on a `bool` parameter, cloned per literal at call sites | Bun's const parameters become run-time parameters or fields; the C compiler folds what it can | each flag value is a struct implementing an interface; with ADR 0086, `impl Flavor for Ts { const TS: bool = true; }` read as `F.TS`, no value passed |\n| Bun sites covered | 1,409 (aliases written out, §4) | 902 (64%); the attribute form covers functions only (600) | all, by rewriting | `bool` and enum, with noise; integers in array lengths: none |\n| Speed | as Rust | as Rust for what it covers | 19% slower in §1.3; fields cost more | as Rust |\n| Mechanical port | yes | no for 507 sites | no: signatures and struct layouts change | no: every flag becomes 2–3 items |\n| Language cost | a parameter kind, one argument rule, value codes in C names | smaller, but a second mechanism later | none | none beyond ADR 0086 |\n\n**A is proposed.** It is Bun's own construct, kept to the values Bun uses, and it reuses §20's model: checked once,\nthen one