        "# ADR 0084 — Interior mutability: `RawCell`, `Cell` and a checked `RefCell`\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-09 (\"Accept all ADRs as recommended\": every question for the owner answered as recommended), the day it was proposed, for ceesafec **0.25.0** (with ADR 0086 and ADR 0085 step 1). Raised by the Bun\n  port's M0 (`~/dev/CeeSafeApps/bun/ROADMAP.md` §3, M0): Bun 1.4.4 writes `Cell`/`RefCell` about 1,000 times, and its\n  probe `analysis/probes/g3_cell.cee` fails on ceesafec 0.24.2 (N0204). The evidence is a census of Bun's hand-written\n  Rust (§1.1, Python scripts run with `python3 -I`), eleven probes compiled and run with ceesafec 0.24.2 (§1.3), and a\n  reading of the specification and the compiler's `Send`/`Share` inference (§1.4). Scripts and probes are kept in\n  `~/dev/CeeSafeApps/bun/analysis/adr-evidence/0084/`. Probe p4 found a data race in safe code (a `StableVector`\n  shared by two threads); commit `b7c9f41` fixed it before this ADR by making `Share` stricter than `Send` (§1.4).\n  This ADR builds its cells on that rule.\n- Scope: type-system.md §38.1 (`Share`: one more entry), §38.4 (`dyn … + Share`), §39.2 (statics), a new §41\n  (cells); ownership.md B2 and T2; unsafe.md §2 (the `p.write` row and the pointers made from references);\n  optimisations.md §2 (tracked locals); diagnostics.md (N0351, N0402, a panic kind); lib/core (a new `cells.cee`) and\n  lib/std's `Mutex`; the checker (`reachesSharedChange` in `src/types/types.cee`), the interpreter;\n  guide/coming-from/rust.md §\"Things you will miss\"; the corpus. It amends ADR 0010's pointer rule; it keeps ADR 0018\n  and ADR 0024 as they are.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n### 1.1 What Bun writes\n\nCounted on 2026-11-09 by `cells.py` (run as `python3 -I cells.py upstream`) over the same files as\n`analysis/bun_rust_features.py`: `upstream/src/**/*.rs`, no test directories, no generated files. Unlike that script\nit skips comment lines, so it finds 919 `Cell`/`RefCell` types where the feature census found 1,016.\n\n| `Cell<T>` element | Uses | | `RefCell<T>` element | Uses |\n|---|---:|---|---|---:|\n| integer or float | 243 | | `Vec`, `SmallVec`, `BabyList`, FIFO | 53 |\n| `bool` | 219 | | struct or other | 45 |\n| enum, flags struct, other `Copy` value | 148 | | map or set | 14 |\n| `Option<…>` of a non-pointer | 89 | | `Option<…>` | 11 |\n| raw pointer or `NonNull` | 46 | | `String`, `Box` | 5 |\n| `Option<pointer>` | 46 | | | |\n| **total** | **791** | | **total** | **128** |\n\nOnly 18 of the 791 `Cell`s hold a value that is plainly not `Copy` (`noncopy.py`: a `Box`, `RefPtr`, `Vec` or owned\nhandle, used with `replace`/`take`); 10 cells of either kind hold a Rust reference.\n\n| Where | `Cell` | `RefCell` | `UnsafeCell` |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| struct field | 623 | 70 | 28 |\n| thread-local (`thread_local!` or `#[thread_local] static`) | 57 | 29 | 4 |\n| behind `Rc` | 0 | 4 | 0 |\n| parameter, local, alias | 111 | 25 | 19 |\n\nNone is in a plain `static` or behind an `Arc`: Rust refuses both, since `Cell` is not `Sync`. The related types:\nBun's own `JsCell<T>` (407 uses in 73 files), `RacyCell` (60), `AtomicCell` (16), `ThreadCell` (8), all over\n`UnsafeCell`; `OnceCell` 196, `OnceLock` 284, `LazyLock` 92; 55 `thread_local!` blocks and 19 `#[thread_local]`\nstatics. The calls: `.with_mut(` 841 (mostly `JsCell`), `.borrow_mut()` 168, `.borrow()` 97. For comparison the\natomics appear 1,244 times and `Mutex<` 30. Bun also writes `dyn … + Sync` 58 times (a plain grep, comments\nincluded), 25 of them `dyn Fn(usize) + Sync` and 12 `dyn Any + Send + Sync`.\n\n### 1.2 Why Bun needs them\n\nOne `VirtualMachine` per JS thread, reached by shared reference from everywhere, and **re-entrancy**: a host\nfunction calls into JS, which calls another host function that changes the same object. A `&mut` cannot be held\nacross that call, so the fields are cells.\n\n- `src/jsc/VirtualMachine.rs:389`: `pub is_inside_deferred_task_queue: core::cell::Cell<bool>,` and `:375`:\n  `pub(crate) jobs: crate::J",
    390 =>
        "sCell<crate::job::JobList>,`.\n- `src/runtime/timer/timer_object_internals.rs:242`: \"`&Self` (NOT `&mut`) — fields are `Cell`/`JsCell` so\n  re-entrant JS touching this object via another `&Self` is sound (no `noalias`).\"\n- `src/bun_core/output.rs:1044`: \"drop the RefCell borrow before calling into the writer so a flush triggered from\n  inside a print (or vice-versa) doesn't BorrowMutError.\" The checked borrow catches a real re-entrancy bug.\n- `src/ptr/js_cell.rs:27–33`: `pub struct JsCell<T>(core::cell::UnsafeCell<T>);` then\n  `unsafe impl<T> Sync for JsCell<T> {}`, which its own comment calls \"a lie to the type system that we discharge by\n  the thread-affinity invariant\". Its `get_mut` hands out `&mut T` from `&self` unchecked.\n\nIntrusive reference counts are the other common case: `src/http_jsc/websocket_client.rs:159`,\n`pub(crate) ref_count: Cell<u32>,`.\n\n### 1.3 What CeeSafe does today (ceesafec 0.24.2)\n\nProbes `p1`–`p10` and Bun's `g3_cell.cee`, checked and run with `out/ceesafec` (`run --interp`, and `build` for p4\nand p5):\n\n| Probe | Result |\n|---|---|\n| g3 `hits: Cell<u32>` in a struct | N0204: `Cell` is not a type |\n| p3 `AtomicU32`/`AtomicBool` fields, changed through `borrow Timer` | works: the atomics are today's only safe cell |\n| p5 a user `UCell<T: Copy>` whose `set(borrow this)` writes through `borrow this.value as *T as *mut T` | accepted, **and** accepted as a shared capture of both closures of `Thread.both`; built natively, two threads of 200,000 increments print 204,389, 202,460, 200,000. Still accepted after `b7c9f41`: nothing in `UCell` is on §38.1's list |\n| p2, p4 **safe code only**: two `Thread.both` closures `push` to one `StableVector<u32>` they capture shared | accepted on 0.24.2; 400,000 pushes leave a length of 214,183, 212,407, 210,627. Since `b7c9f41`: N0334, \"a `StableVector<u32>`, which is not Share\" |\n| p6 a `Reader<region r, T>` guard over a counter, written in user code like `MutexGuard` | works (with `unsafe`): a library `RefCell` needs no new syntax |\n| p7 a method named `borrow` | N0100: `borrow` is a reserved word |\n| p8 `impl<T: Copy> W<T>` (struct `W<T>` unbounded), `fn get(borrow this) -> T => this.value` | N0251: an inherent impl takes the struct's own bounds (§21.1, ADR 0064 §1), so methods cannot be limited to Copy instances (§21.1 calls the mismatch N0267; the checker reports the body instead) |\n| p9, p10 `W<borrow u32>`, and a cell of a reference set to a shorter-lived one | the generic struct is accepted; the cell's `set` is N0222 inside, since a raw pointer may not point to a type holding loans (§27.1) |\n\n### 1.4 What the specification says\n\n- **`Share` is no longer every `Send` type** (commit `b7c9f41`, type-system.md §38.1). Until then `isShare` returned\n  `isSend`, resting on ADR 0016 §3.1's \"no interior mutability (all of them in v1: the reference has no `Cell`)\".\n  But `StableVector.push` takes `borrow this` (§24.2), and a `dyn` box may hide anything: p4 was a data race in safe\n  code. Now `Share` is `Send` without a path (fields, payloads, elements, box contents) to a value changed through a\n  shared reference (`reachesSharedChange`, `types.cee:1730`): a `StableVector`, a `dyn` box (`Box<dyn I + Send>`,\n  `Box<dyn fn… + Send>`), or a type parameter without a `Share` bound. The library's `@threadSafe` types stop the\n  walk, so `Mutex<StableVector<T>>` is `Share`. The list is the hook this ADR needs; it also means no `dyn` box can\n  be shared across threads at all, where Bun writes `dyn … + Sync` 58 times.\n- **Writing behind a shared loan is invalid, even in `unsafe`.** ownership.md B2: \"If L is shared, P must also not be\n  mutated\". unsafe.md §2: `p.write(v)` needs storage \"which nothing currently borrows (no live loan of it, shared or\n  mutable)\", and ADR 0010's rule lets a pointer made from a reference write only while that loan could be live. So\n  p5's `UCell` breaks U2: **no correct cell can be written in CeeSafe today**. `MutexGuard.data`\n  (`lib/st",
    391 =>
        "d/sync.cee:51–52`, `borrow this.lock.data as *T as *mut T`) relies on a library-only argument (T3).\n- **The optimiser trusts shared loans** for its tracked locals (optimisations.md §2: \"A shared loan cannot change\n  it\", integers, `bool`, slices, views, `Vector`/`String` lengths) and already excludes `StableVector` (§2, \"their\n  length grows through a shared borrow\"). A `borrow T` is a C `const T *` (c-backend.md, the `borrow T` row), which C\n  allows to be written through when the object itself is not `const`; a `borrow mut T` may become `T *restrict`.\n- **Copy.** A struct with no `drop` whose fields are all Copy is Copy (type-system.md §14.1); `@noncopy` makes it a\n  move type anyway. A generic `W<T>` is Copy at `W<u32>`.\n- **Statics** must be `Share` (§39.2, ADR 0024 option A); diagnostics.md's N0337 already uses `static C: Cell = …` as\n  its example. Thread-locals are \"not in v1\" (ADR 0016 §4). A panic is an abort, with no unwinding and no destructors\n  (ownership.md, OD-5).\n\n## 2. Proposal\n\nLibrary cells over one compiler-known primitive (option A of §3).\n\n### 2.1 `RawCell<T>`: the primitive\n\n- A lib/core struct marked `@interiorMutable` and `@noncopy`; `@interiorMutable` is accepted only in lib/core (others:\n  N0290, as for `@threadSafe`). It has `T`'s size and alignment (one field), so a cell may be a `@repr(C)` field like\n  an atomic.\n- **Never Copy.** Without `@noncopy`, `RawCell<u32>`, hence `Cell<u32>` and `RefCell<u32>`, would be Copy (§14.1):\n  `let snap = rc;` would read the whole `RefCell` while a `CellRefMut`'s `borrow mut T` is live, a read no guard sees,\n  of storage C may treat as `restrict`. So a cell, and every struct or enum holding one, is a move type, as in Rust\n  (`Cell<T>` is not `Copy` there either). Nothing is lost: `c.get()` copies the value out, and a copy of a cell is\n  `Cell.create(c.get())`. A struct with a cell field (§2.3's `Timer`) is moved, not copied.\n- `RawCell.create(v: T) -> RawCell<T>`; `c.pointer(borrow this) -> *mut T` (safe to take, `unsafe` to use);\n  `c.getMut(borrow mut this) -> borrow mut T` (safe: the borrow is exclusive); `c.intoInner(this) -> T`.\n- **The one rule it changes** (amending ownership.md B2, unsafe.md §2's `p.write` and `Ptr.toBorrowMut` rows, and\n  ADR 0010): the `T` inside a `RawCell` may be read and written through `c.pointer()` while shared loans of the cell,\n  or of a value that holds it, are live. A shared loan freezes everything **except** the contents of a `RawCell`. A\n  `borrow mut` of the cell still excludes every other access (B1 is unchanged), and the unsafe code still must not\n  make two live `borrow mut T` from the pointer, nor write while a `borrow T` it made is live.\n- `T` may not hold loans (a reference, view or other scoped type): N0351, \"a cell of a loan-holding type\". The\n  raw-pointer rule of §27.1 already forbids the pointer; refusing at the type avoids the variance problem of p10 that\n  Rust solves with invariance. Bun has 10 such cells; the port gives them raw pointers.\n- User code may use `RawCell` (Bun's `RacyCell`, `ThreadCell`, `AtomicCell` port over it). It may not claim\n  `@threadSafe`.\n\n### 2.2 `RawCell` joins §38.1's list\n\n- `reachesSharedChange` gains one case: a `RawCell<T>` is a value changed through a shared reference. So a type is\n  **not `Share`** when a path through its fields, payloads, elements or box contents reaches a `RawCell`, unless the\n  path first passes through a `@threadSafe` type (whose own `unsafe` code argues T3). It is still `Send` when `T` is: a\n  cell may move to another thread, not be used from two at once. No other change to `Share` is needed: `b7c9f41`\n  already made it stricter than `Send`.\n- `Mutex` and `Arc` stay `Share` by `@threadSafe`. `Mutex`'s `data` field becomes a `RawCell<T>`, so\n  `MutexGuard.data` meets U2 instead of a special argument. This refuses nothing new: `Mutex<T: Send>` already\n  excludes every loan-holding `T` (N0334 and N0274 on `Mutex<borrow u32>`), so N0351 cannot fi",
    392 =>
        "re there.\n- Consequences through existing rules, with no new check: `Thread.both` refuses a cell captured shared (N0334, §38.6);\n  so does `Async.blocking`; `Arc<Cell<u32>>` fails `Arc`'s `T: Share` bound; `static X: Cell<u32>` is N0337 (§39.2,\n  as diagnostics.md already shows). ownership.md T2's sentence \"A v1 type can be changed through a shared reference\n  only if it is one of the library's `@threadSafe` types\" gains \"or holds a `RawCell` or a `StableVector`, and such a\n  type is not `Share`\".\n\n### 2.3 `Cell<T>`\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Timer {\n    flags: Cell<u32>;\n    inCallback: Cell<bool>;\n}\n\nfn fire(t: borrow Timer) {\n    t.inCallback.set(true);\n    callIntoScript(t);                 // may call fire(t) or cancel(t) again: both take `borrow Timer`\n    t.inCallback.set(false);\n    t.flags.set(t.flags.get() | 1);\n}\n```\n\n- `Cell<T: Copy>`: `Cell.create(v)`, `c.get()`, `c.set(v)`, `c.replace(v) -> T`, `c.getMut()` (`borrow mut this`),\n  `c.intoInner()`. Every method but the last two takes `borrow this`.\n- `T` is bounded `Copy` on the struct, because an inherent impl cannot add a bound of its own (§21.1, probe p8). The 18\n  non-Copy cells of §1.1 (`Cell<Option<Box<…>>>` used with `replace`/`take`) port to `RefCell<T?>`, whose `replace`\n  and `take` (§2.4) never find a guard live at those sites. Conditional methods would let one `Cell` serve both, but\n  they change §21.1 for every library; they are not needed for 2% of the cells.\n- No method lends a reference to the inside, so nothing can observe a change through a stale reference, and a\n  re-entrant call between two methods is harmless. A Copy `T` has no `drop`, so `set` runs no user code.\n- **`==`.** `Cell<T>` has `==` and `!=` when `T` has them, comparing `a.get() == b.get()`, so ADR 0087's\n  `@derive(Equal)` includes `Cell` fields, as Rust derives `PartialEq` through `Cell`. `RefCell` has no `==`: it would\n  take a read guard and could panic.\n- Plain library code over `RawCell`, with methods small enough to inline: `get` is a load, `set` a store. Cells are\n  never tracked locals (optimisations.md §2 is restated to say so), and `borrow` stays `const T *` in C.\n\n### 2.4 `RefCell<T>`: checked shared and exclusive access\n\n- `RefCell.create(v)`. Its state is one `Cell<isize>`: 0 free, `n > 0` readers, −1 writing.\n- `c.read()` returns a `CellRef<T>` and `c.write()` a `CellRefMut<T>`. (`borrow` is a reserved word, p7, so the port\n  maps `.borrow()` to `.read()` and `.borrow_mut()` to `.write()`.) Each guard is a scoped struct over a region of the\n  cell, written like `MutexGuard` (p6 shows the shape): `data(borrow this) -> borrow T`, and\n  `data(borrow mut this) -> borrow mut T` on the exclusive one. Its drop gives the access back.\n- **On conflict it panics**: `write` while any guard is live, or `read` while a `CellRefMut` is, panics with \"cell\n  already borrowed\" (a new panic kind, `CEE_PANIC_CELL_BORROWED`, for freestanding programs). Panic is abort (OD-5):\n  deterministic, reported at the call, like an index out of bounds. A reader count that would overflow panics too.\n- `c.tryRead()` and `c.tryWrite()` return the guard or `null`, for code that can wait or fall back (Bun's\n  `output.rs:1044` case). `c.replace(v)` and `c.take()` (on `RefCell<T?>`) panic as `write` does; they move the old\n  value out before returning it, so its `drop` runs in the caller with no guard live, and may re-enter the cell or\n  free its owner. `getMut` and `intoInner` need no check.\n- **Guards made in a condition.** A guard that is a temporary of an `if` or `while` condition, or of an `e is P`\n  scrutinee (ADR 0085 §2.8), must end where Rust 2024 ends it: at the end of the condition, or, when a binding of `P`\n  borrows from it, at the end of the block that uses the binding (the `if`'s block, one pass of the `while` body),\n  never in the `else` block nor across the next test. Otherwise `if (c.read().data().isEmpty()) {…} else\n  { c.write()… }` panics on a program that reads as correct. ADR 0",
    393 =>
        "085's temporaries rule should say so; if it keeps\n  \"until the end of the statement\", a guard temporary in a condition is a warning beside N0402.\n- **Why it is sound.** A guard is scoped to the cell (§25.4), so it cannot outlive it, be stored past it or cross a\n  thread (scoped types are not `Send`). The reference `data` lends is tied to the guard (§13.5), so the B-rules end it\n  before the guard's drop resets the state: the argument T3 makes for `MutexGuard`, with a counter for the lock. The\n  cell is never copied behind a guard's back (§2.1, `@noncopy`).\n- **Cost.** One word beside `T`; acquiring is a test and a store, releasing a store; the panic path is cold.\n\n### 2.5 `dyn … + Share`\n\nSince `b7c9f41`, no `dyn` box is `Share`, which is right for a box that may hold a cell but refuses Bun's 58\n`dyn … + Sync`. `dyn I + Share` and `dyn fn(…) -> R + Share` (§38.4) are `dyn` types whose boxes are `Send` and\n`Share`: `+ Share` implies `+ Send`, as `Share` implies `Send` (§38.1). A coercion to one needs `T: Share` (N0334\notherwise: a closure capturing a `Cell` cannot become one); `reachesSharedChange` stops at such a box. It converts\nto `dyn I + Send` and to `dyn I` (the same value and tables), never the other way.\n\n### 2.6 With the rest of the language\n\n| Feature | Rule |\n|---|---|\n| Borrow checker | unchanged for safe code: `set`, `read`, `write` take `borrow this`, so any number of shared loans coexist; `borrow mut` of a cell is exclusive as ever |\n| Copy | a cell and everything holding one is a move type (§2.1) |\n| Closures | a `borrow fn` may capture a cell shared and change it while the caller holds another shared loan (Bun's `&Cell<bool>` drop guards, `VirtualMachine.rs:6610`) |\n| `dyn` | a `borrow this` method behind `borrow dyn I` may change the object's cells; `dyn I + Send` needs only `Send`, `dyn I + Share` needs `Share` (§2.5) |\n| Conditions | a guard temporary ends with the condition or the block that uses its bindings (§2.4) |\n| Async | a cell in a task frame is fine (frames never move, A1). A `CellRef`/`CellRefMut` live across an `await` is warning N0402: another task resumed meanwhile panics on the same cell. Tasks that cross threads already need `Send` captures |\n| Statics | refused (N0337), as in Rust. Bun's 86 thread-local cells wait for thread-locals (§4) |\n| Profiles | all three cells are in lib/core, so in every profile, the kernel included; they have no effect |\n| Interpreter | runs them as ordinary memory; the conflict panic is the same in both back ends |\n\n## 3. Alternatives considered\n\n| | A. Library cells over one compiler-known primitive | B. Translation rule, no new types | C. Atomics as cells |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| What | `RawCell<T>` (the primitive), `Cell<T>`, `RefCell<T>` in lib/core | re-shape to `borrow mut`, or raw pointers in `unsafe` | `AtomicU32`, `AtomicBool`, … in place of `Cell` |\n| Safe code | yes | `borrow mut` cannot cross re-entrant JS calls; raw pointers are `unsafe` at ~1,000 sites | yes, for integers and `bool` (462 of 791 cells) |\n| Threads | cells are not `Share`, checked | the user's argument | atomic, so correct but paid for |\n| Cost | `Cell`: a plain load or store; `RefCell`: a flag test and an update per guard | none | sequentially consistent operations unless every site names an ordering |\n| Coverage | all of §1.1 | all, unsafely | no pointers, enums, structs, `Option`, collections |\n| Soundness | gives p5 a correct form, not `Share` | p5's `UCell` stays inferred `Share` and breaks U2 | as B |\n\n**A is proposed** (§2). B gives up the port's safety claim for the most common pattern in Bun and still leaves CeeSafe\nunable to state the rule its own `Mutex` relies on. C covers half the cases at a cost the rest of the program does\nnot need. For `dyn`, the alternative to §2.5 is to keep every box un-`Share` and port Bun's `dyn … + Sync` to\n`Arc<Mutex<…>>`: a lock per call where Bun has none.\n\n## 4. Not in this ADR\n\n- **Thread-locals** (ADR 0016 §4, \"not in v1",
    394 =>
        "\"; profiles.md lists `@threadLocal` as a profile capability). Bun's 86\n  thread-local cells and its static `VM` pointer (`VirtualMachine.rs:692`) need them; a cell is what one would hold.\n- **`OnceCell`, `OnceLock`, `LazyLock`** (572 uses). A single-thread `OnceCell` is a small library type over `RawCell`;\n  the thread-safe ones are lazy statics, ADR 0024's rejected option C. Both want their own decision.\n- **`Rc<T>`.** CeeSafe has only `Arc`. Bun's 4 `Rc<RefCell<…>>` and its intrusive counts (`ref_count: Cell<u32>`) port\n  with this ADR's `Cell`; a non-atomic `Rc` is a separate question.\n- **Implicit dereference of guards.** Guards are reached through `data()`, as `MutexGuard` is; a guard ADR may change\n  both together.\n- **An unchecked `JsCell`.** The port maps `JsCell` to `RefCell` (or `Cell` where `T` is Copy). If the checks show up\n  in Bun's benchmarks, a `RawCell` wrapper with an `unsafe` accessor is what Bun has today, and needs nothing new.\n- Weaker orderings for cells, `Cell` of a reference (§2.1), and `Share` declared by user code.\n\n## 5. Testing\n\n- **Run tests** (corpus, interpreter and native, compared): every `Cell` and `RefCell` method; a re-entrant callback\n  that sets a cell through a second shared reference (§2.3's `fire`); `RefCell<Box<…>?>`'s `take` and `replace`,\n  with a `drop` of the old value that reads the same cell; `==` on two `Cell<u32>`; `if (c.read()…) {…} else\n  { c.write()… }` does not panic; a `Box<dyn I + Share>` read from both `Thread.both` children.\n- **Panic tests**: `write` under a live `read`, `read` under a live `write`, `replace` under a guard; each with its\n  message and location, in both back ends; `tryRead`/`tryWrite` return `null` in the same cases.\n- **Check-fail tests**: a cell captured by `Thread.both` (N0334); `Arc<Cell<u32>>`; `static C: Cell<u32>` (N0337); a\n  struct holding a cell three levels deep in a `Thread.both` capture; a struct holding a cell coerced to\n  `dyn I + Share` (N0334); `let a = rc; let b = rc;` on a `RefCell<u32>` and on a struct with a `Cell<u32>` field\n  (N0250); a guard returned past its cell (N0243); `RefCell<StringView>` (N0351); `@interiorMutable` outside lib/core\n  (N0290); `Cell<Box<u32>>` (the `Copy` bound). A warning test for a guard across `await` (N0402). The existing\n  `tests/reference/tests/corpus/check-fail/share-shared-change.cee` (p4) stays.\n- **Mutation suite**: thread cases that drop the `RawCell` case from `reachesSharedChange`, or `@noncopy` from\n  `RawCell`, must be caught by the tests above.\n- **C output**: `emit_c` snapshots showing `Cell.get`/`set` as one load or store and no call; a bounds-check snapshot\n  showing that a cell's value is never a tracked fact.\n- **ThreadSanitizer**: p5 rewritten with `Mutex`/atomics, built with `-fsanitize=thread`, reports nothing.\n- **Bun**: `analysis/probes/g3_cell.cee` passes (it becomes a non-`g` probe) with `Cell.create(0)`.\n\n## 6. Questions for the owner\n\n1. Option A: one `@interiorMutable` primitive, `RawCell<T>`, and `Cell<T>`/`RefCell<T>` as library code in lib/core,\n   shipped in 0.25.0? Recommended: yes. It is the only option that keeps the port safe and lets CeeSafe state the\n   rule its own `Mutex` depends on.\n2. `RawCell` joins `reachesSharedChange`'s list (§2.2), so a cell is `Send` but not `Share`? Recommended: yes. It\n   adds one case to the rule `b7c9f41` already shipped, and no existing program holds a `RawCell`.\n3. `RawCell` is `@noncopy`, so cells and their holders are move types (§2.1)? Recommended: yes. A Copy cell can be\n   read behind a live `CellRefMut`; Rust's cells are not `Copy` either.\n4. `dyn I + Share` (§2.5), so a `dyn` over data with no cell can be shared again? Recommended: yes, in this ADR: Bun\n   writes `dyn … + Sync` 58 times, and without it each becomes a lock.\n5. A `RefCell` conflict panics (abort), with `tryRead`/`tryWrite` beside? Recommended: yes. It matches every other\n   run-time check in CeeSafe, and Bun's `BorrowMutError` is a bug to repo",
    395 =>
        "rt, not a value to handle.\n6. Names: `RawCell`, `Cell`, `RefCell`, and `read()`/`write()` returning `CellRef`/`CellRefMut` with `data()`?\n   Recommended: yes. They keep Rust's names where a port can keep them, and `borrow` is reserved.\n7. A warning (N0402, not an error) for a `RefCell` guard live across `await`, and guards in conditions ended as Rust\n   2024 does (§2.4, for ADR 0085)? Recommended: yes. Across `await` it may be deliberate in a single-task program,\n   but it is usually a panic waiting for a second task.\n8. `Cell<T: Copy>` bounded on the struct, with the 18 non-Copy cells ported to `RefCell<T?>`, rather than amending\n   §21.1 so an inherent impl may add bounds (p8)? Recommended: yes. Conditional methods are a wider change than 2% of\n   the cells justify; if another port needs them, they get their own ADR and `Cell` gains `replace`/`take` for all `T`.\n9. Cells of references refused (N0351) in v1, rather than an invariance rule for regions? Recommended: yes. Ten Bun\n   sites, all portable with raw pointers; variance can be its own ADR if a second port needs it.\n",
    396 =>
        "# ADR 0085 — Pattern guards and richer patterns: `if (…)` guards, `|`, ranges, `@`, constants, strings, slices, `is`\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-09 (\"Accept all ADRs as recommended\": every question for the owner answered as recommended), the day it was proposed. Raised by the Bun port's M0 (`~/dev/CeeSafeApps/bun/ROADMAP.md` §3, M0): the probe\n  `analysis/probes/g2_guard.cee` (`Some(n) if n > 3 => …`) fails on ceesafec 0.24.2 with N0100 \"expected '=>', found\n  'if'\". Rests on 25 probe programs compiled with ceesafec 0.24.2 on 2026-11-09 (§1.1), and on counts of Bun 1.4.4's\n  Rust (`upstream/src`, 3,238 `.rs` files, 1,743,082 lines) made by four Python scripts run with `python3 -I`\n  (§1.2), kept in `~/dev/CeeSafeApps/bun/analysis/adr-evidence/0085/`.\n- Scope: type-system.md §15.2 (patterns, scrutinee types, bindings, exhaustiveness), §15.3 (value arms), §18.4\n  (moving payloads out), §4 (literals in patterns), §9 (`if` and `while` conditions); grammar.md §1.3 (the contextual\n  word `is`), §1.7 (the token `..=`), §2.1 (`MatchExpr`, `ValueArm`, precedence); diagnostics.md (N0108, N0207, N0256,\n  N0257 and new codes); the parser, the pattern checker (`src/check/patterns.cee`), the usefulness check\n  (`src/check/usefulness.cee`), NIR lowering (`src/nir/nir_builder.cee`), the C backend, the formatter; the interpreter\n  only through NIR; guide/structs-and-enums.md; the corpus.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n### 1.1 What CeeSafe has today\n\nThe pattern grammar (type-system.md §15.2) is `_`, an integer, character or `bool` literal (an integer one may be\nnegated), `null`, and a variant `E.V(…)` / `Some(…)` / `Ok(…)` / `Err(…)` whose payloads are bindings (`x`, `var x`,\n`_`) or nested patterns (M11, G1). The parser has exactly these four forms: `PatternNode` is `Wildcard`, `Null`,\n`Literal`, `Variant` (`src/syntax/ast.cee:185`), read by `parsePatternLevel` (`src/syntax/parser.cee:1931`).\nExhaustiveness and unreachable arms use Maranget's usefulness algorithm (`useful`, `src/check/usefulness.cee:161`), and\ninteger literals never cover their type. NIR lowers a flat match to a `Switch` (`lowerMatch`,\n`src/nir/nir_builder.cee:740`) and a nested one to sequential tests that fall through to the next arm\n(`lowerNestedMatch`, `:909`, with `testPattern`, `:958`). The interpreter runs NIR, so it follows the lowering.\n\nProbes (`~/dev/CeeSafeApps/bun/analysis/adr-evidence/0085/*.cee`, ceesafec 0.24.2):\n\n| Form | Example | Today |\n|---|---|---|\n| guard | `Some(n) if n > 3 => …` | N0100 \"expected '=>', found 'if'\" |\n| or-pattern | `' ' \\| '\\t' => …` | N0100 \"expected '=>', found '\\|'\" |\n| range | `'a'..='z'`, `'a'..'z'` | N0100 \"expected '=>', found '..'\" |\n| `@` binding | `Some(n @ 5)` | N0100 \"expected ')', found '@'\" |\n| named constant | `LF => …` (`const LF: u8 = 10`) | N0205 \"a variant pattern needs an enum\" |\n| top-level binding | `n => …` on a `u8` | N0205, same message: a bare name is read as a variant |\n| struct pattern | `P { x: 1, y } => …` | N0100 |\n| slice pattern | `[first, ..] => …` | N0108 \"expected a pattern, found '['\" |\n| string literal | `\"get\" => …` | N0108; and `match` on a `StringView` is N0207 |\n| byte string | `b\"abc\" => …` | N0100: there is no byte-string literal at all |\n| `let … else` | `let Some(n) = x else { return 0; };` | N0100 |\n| `if let`, `while let` | `if (let Some(n) = x)`, `while (let …)` | N0102 \"expected expression, found 'let'\" |\n| `matches!` | `matches(c, 'a')` | N0200 |\n| works | `Tok.C(4)`, `K.A(1, _)`, `Some(true)`, a `bool` `match` expression, a duplicate literal (N0257) | |\n\nSo a guard today is a nested `if` inside the arm with the fall-through arm's body copied into its `else`, and the rest\nare chains of literal arms or `if` tests.\n\n### 1.2 What Bun's Rust uses\n\nCounted over all 3,238 `.rs` files under `upstream/src`, test directories included, by Python scripts of regular\nexpressions (`patterns.py`, `patterns2.py`, `bytestr.py`, `recount.py`, kept with th",
    397 =>
        "e probes), run with `python3 -I`.\nAn *arm* is a line `PATTERN => …` (rustfmt puts each arm head on one line); its *pattern* is the text before a\ntop-level `if`. Line-based counts are close, not exact: a multi-line or-pattern counts once; `macro_rules!` arms are\nexcluded. Without the `test`, `tests` and `testing` directories there are 95,017 arms and 6,778 guards. `matches!`\nis counted by `recount.py` per call, its parentheses balanced across lines. `src/lint` (a linter, 28,129\narms) is shown apart because it is the densest user.\n\n| Form | Arm lines (all) | outside `src/lint` | Needs |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| match arms in total | 95,140 | 67,011 | — |\n| guard `P if g =>` | 6,780 (7.1%) | 3,617 | this ADR |\n| or-pattern `A \\| B` | 6,362 | 4,296 | this ADR |\n| byte literal `b'x'` (inside any pattern) | 2,652 | 2,050 | works: `'x'` is an integer pattern |\n| byte-string `b\"…\"` (not counting 4,379 `comptime_string_map!` entries) | 2,546 | — | this ADR |\n| string literal `\"…\"` | 414 | — | this ADR |\n| integer or byte range `..=` | 352 arms; 526 lines anywhere | 270 | this ADR |\n| named constant (`UPPER_CASE` in a pattern) | 890 | 816 | this ADR |\n| slice pattern `[…]` | 764 arms + 383 `let`/`if let` | 437 | this ADR |\n| `x @ P` | 380 | 194 | this ADR |\n| tuple `(a, b) =>` | 4,058 | 2,140 | not here (§4): no tuples |\n| struct-like `V { f, .. }` | 4,446 | 2,790 | not here (§4): no named payloads |\n| `if let` / `while let` / `&& let` chains | 14,903 / 1,670 / 1,881 | — | this ADR (`is`) |\n| `matches!(…)` calls (1,667 with a guard inside) | 8,125 | — | this ADR (`is`) |\n| `let … else` | 7,806 | — | translation rule (§2.9) |\n\nWhat the 6,780 guards test: a method test (`is_*`, `contains`, `starts_with`, `len()`) 2,574; `==`/`!=` 1,719; another\ncall 1,013; a flag or other 968; a nested `matches!` 297; an ordering 209. 1,183 guard a bare binding or `_` (an\n`if`/`else` chain written as a `match`). Only 4 take `&mut` of anything, none of a binding; 3 have a visible side\neffect (`self.visited.insert(…)`); none awaits. Rust already gives a guard shared access only.\n\nExamples:\n\n```rust\nSome(wrote) if wrote == bytes.len() => {}                       // src/http_jsc/websocket_client.rs:1117\n0x5F | 0x24 | 0x61..=0x7A | 0x41..=0x5A => {                    // src/js_parser/lexer.rs:1742\nb'a'..=b'z' | b'A'..=b'Z' | b'0'..=b'9' | b'_' | b'-' => self.advance(1),   // src/css/css_parser.rs:4584\n[ref rest @ .., 0x09..=0x0D | b' '] => rest,                    // src/bun_core/string/immutable/whitespace.rs:87\nc if c == QUOTE => {     // src/js_parser/lexer.rs:815: \"const-generic param can't be a pattern; guard is fine\"\n```\n\nThe lexers and parsers (`js_parser`, `css`, `parsers/{json,json5,yaml,toml,xml}`, `shell_parser`) are almost all\nbyte `match`es with or-patterns and ranges. Ported today, each becomes an `if` chain, and the jump table is lost.\n\n### 1.3 Prior decisions\n\nThis ADR **extends** §15.2's pattern grammar and its M11 nested patterns, and **amends** one rule there: integer\nliterals never cover their type (§2.4 below lets ranges cover it). It **extends** §15.2's scrutinee types to\n`StringView`, `Slice<T>` and arrays. It keeps ADR 0072's ruling that `Slice<u8> == StringView` is N0205: a string\npattern is typed by its scrutinee, as `'k'` is (§4), and is not an `==`. Value arms (§15.3, ADR 0073 §2.3), moves out\nof a consuming match (§18.4) and pattern bindings that shadow a dependency (ADR 0074 §2.10) are unchanged.\n\n## 2. Proposal\n\n### 2.1 Grammar\n\n```text\nArm         = Pattern Guard? '=>' Block ','?               (* §15.2 *)\nValueArm    = Pattern Guard? '=>' Branch                   (* §15.3 *)\nGuard       = 'if' '(' Expr ')'\nPattern     = '|'? AltPattern ('|' AltPattern)*            (* or-pattern *)\nAltPattern  = (Identifier '@')? PrimPattern\nPrimPattern = '_' | 'null' | Literal | Literal '..=' Literal | Literal '..' Literal\n            | StringLiteral\n            | QualifiedName ('(' Payload (',' Payload)* ')')? ",
    398 =>
        "  (* a variant, or an associated constant (§2.6) *)\n            | 'var'? Identifier                            (* a constant if one is in scope, else a binding *)\n            | '[' (SliceItem (',' SliceItem)* ','?)? ']'\n            | '(' Pattern ')'\nSliceItem   = Pattern | '..' | Identifier '@' '..'          (* at most one '..' per slice pattern *)\nLiteral     = IntLiteral | '-' IntLiteral | CharLiteral | 'true' | 'false'\nIsTest      = Operand 'is' Pattern                         (* §2.8: at the precedence of '=='; 'is' is contextual *)\n```\n\n`..=` becomes one token. It is a pattern operator only; `0..=9` in an expression is N0100 with the hint \"ranges in\nexpressions are half-open: `0..10`\".\n\n### 2.2 Guards\n\n```ceesafe\nmatch (x) {\n    Some(n) if (n > 3) => { Console.writeLine(\"big\"); }\n    _ => { Console.writeLine(\"small\"); }\n}\n```\n\n- **Syntax.** `if (…)`, parenthesised like every CeeSafe condition (§9). The guard is a `bool` (N0205 otherwise).\n- **Order.** Arms are tried in order. For one arm: test the pattern (as §15.2 says: outer variant first, then nested\n  patterns left to right, depth first); if it matches, bind, then evaluate the guard; if the guard is `false`, go on to\n  the next arm. For an or-pattern with a guard, each alternative that matches, left to right, binds and runs the guard;\n  the first `true` wins (Rust's rule, so a mechanical port keeps its meaning).\n- **What the guard sees.** The arm's bindings, read-only. For a Copy payload the binding is the value. For a non-Copy\n  payload in a consuming match (§18.4), the guard reads the payload in place: the move into the binding happens only\n  after the guard is `true`. An arm whose guard fails has moved nothing.\n- **What the guard may not do (N0355).** From the start of the pattern test to the end of the guard, the scrutinee\n  carries a shared loan. The guard may read the scrutinee and the bindings, call functions, and read and write other\n  places. It may not move, assign or `mut`-lend the scrutinee, a place inside it or a binding. It may not `await`:\n  the loan would cross a suspension (no Bun guard does). The borrow checker reports these with N0355, whose message\n  names the guard.\n- **Exhaustiveness.** A guarded arm never counts toward coverage: its row is not added to the usefulness matrix. Its\n  pattern is still checked for reachability: if earlier unguarded arms cover it, it is N0257. When N0256's witness\n  would match a guarded arm, the message says \"arms with a guard do not count; add `_` or an arm without a guard\".\n\n### 2.3 Or-patterns\n\n`A | B` matches when either matches; alternatives are tested left to right. A leading `|` is allowed (rustfmt's\nlayout). An or-pattern may appear anywhere a pattern may, `Some('a' | 'b')` included. Every alternative binds the\nsame names, each with the same type and the same `var`-ness (N0356 otherwise). For usefulness each alternative is a\nrow of its own; an alternative already covered by earlier rows or earlier alternatives is N0257, pointed at that\nalternative.\n\n### 2.4 Ranges\n\n`lo..=hi` (inclusive) and `lo..hi` (half-open, as range expressions are) on an integer scrutinee or payload. Both\nbounds are integer or character literals, typed by the scrutinee as §4 types a literal (so `'a'..='z'` on a `u8`).\nAn empty range (`'z'..='a'`, `5..5`) is N0357. Floats stay N0207.\n\nCoverage **changes**: integer literals and ranges cover an integer type when together they cover every value. The\nusefulness check splits the type into the intervals the arms' bounds define (as Rust has since 1.33), so\n`0x00..=0x7F` and `0x80..=0xFF` cover a `u8`, and N0256's witness names an uncovered value (`'{'`, `128`). A lone\nliteral still needs `_` beside it, as today.\n\n### 2.5 `name @ pattern`\n\nBinds the value the pattern matched, as a payload binding would (copy, move or reference, §15.2), and tests the\npattern. `d @ '0'..='9'`, `quote @ ('\"' | '\\'')`, `Err(e @ IoError.NotFound)`. It does not change coverage.\n\nA value is moved at most once. ",
    399 =>
        "When `name` binds by move (a non-Copy value in a consuming match, §18.4, or an `is`\nthat moves, §2.8), no binding inside its pattern may also bind by move: `e @ Err(inner)` with a non-Copy payload would\nmove the payload into `inner` and again, inside the whole value, into `e`, so it is N0250 (use of a moved value), with\nthe message \"`e @ …` moves the value that `inner` also moves; bind one of them\". Bindings under it that copy (Copy\npayloads) are allowed, and so is everything on a reference scrutinee, where each binding is a reference. This is\nRust's rule for `@` (since 1.56).\n\n### 2.6 Constants and top-level bindings\n\nA bare identifier (no `.`, no parentheses) in pattern position is:\n\n- a **constant pattern** when it names a `const` in scope of an integer or `bool` type. It matches that value and\n  counts as that literal for coverage. A `const` of another type is N0358.\n  *Amended 2026-11-09:* this read \"an integer, `bool` or fieldless-enum type\", but a `const` of an enum type cannot\n  be declared (its value is not a constant expression, N0217), so the rule is the narrower one the compiler\n  implements; a fieldless enum's variant is matched as a variant pattern (`Mode.Idle`).\n- a **const generic parameter** (ADR 0083) is N0358 (\"a const generic parameter cannot be a pattern; test it in a\n  guard: `c if (c == QUOTE)`\"), never a binding, until a later ADR; ADR 0083 §4 states the same. Otherwise `QUOTE =>`\n  would silently become a catch-all binding, and as a last arm only an unused-binding warning would show it.\n- otherwise a **binding** of the value at that position, at the top level too: `n => …` binds the scrutinee (copy,\n  move or reference, §15.2), and `var n` a mutable one.\n\nThis is Rust's rule. Its known hazard is a misspelt constant becoming a catch-all binding; the arms after it are then\nN0257, and the binding warns as unused, so the mistake does not compile silently. It changes the meaning of existing\ncode in one case: a payload binding whose name is also a `const` in scope (`Some(MAX)`). The release scans the\nrepositories' corpus for it first (§5).\n\nA qualified name without parentheses (`Type.NAME`, `pkg.Type.NAME`) is a variant pattern when it names a variant, and\na constant pattern when it names an associated constant (ADR 0074 §2.7) of a type allowed above, or one an `impl` of\nan interface gives the type (ADR 0086 §2.4). `Self` may start it where `Self` is a struct or enum (`Self.TAB`,\n`Self.Idle`). Read through a type parameter (`T.NAME`, or `Self.NAME` in an interface default, ADR 0086) it is a value\nknown only per instance, not a constant, so it is N0358 there too.\n\n### 2.7 Strings and slices\n\n- **String literal patterns.** On a `StringView` scrutinee, `\"get\"` matches equal bytes, as `==` does (§22.2). On a\n  `Slice<u8>` scrutinee, the same literal matches its UTF-8 bytes: the literal is typed by the scrutinee, as `'k'` is.\n  Bun's `b\"cjs\"` is written `\"cjs\"`. A byte string that is not UTF-8 is a slice pattern of integers (below). String\n  patterns never cover their type; a duplicate is N0257. `match` on a `StringView` is no longer N0207.\n- **Slice patterns** on `Slice<T>`, `[T; N]` and references to them: `[]`, `[a, b]`, `[first, ..]`, `[.., last]`,\n  `[x, rest @ ..]`, `['\"', inner @ .., '\"']`. Each item is a pattern on one element; `..` matches any number of\n  elements, at most once (N0100 otherwise); `rest @ ..` binds them as a `Slice<T>` of the same region.\n  - **Bindings.** A slice is a view, so it never moves: a Copy element binds a copy, a non-Copy one a `borrow T`. On\n    an array by value, an element binding needs a Copy element type; otherwise N0205 with the hint \"match on\n    `a[..]`\".\n  - **Coverage.** A slice's constructors are its lengths: `[p1, …, pk]` covers length k, a pattern with `..` and k\n    items covers every length ≥ k. The check splits lengths at 0 … L and \"L or more\", L being the largest k in the\n    arms (Rust's algorithm). An array has one length, N.\n\n### 2.8 `e is ",
    400 =>
        "P`: patterns as conditions\n\nOne boolean operator replaces Rust's `matches!`, `if let`, `while let` and let chains:\n\n```ceesafe\nlet isDigit = c is '0'..='9';\nif (cache.find(key) is Some(entry) && entry.fresh) { use(entry); }\nwhile (parts.next() is Some(part)) { handle(part); }\n```\n\n- **`is` is contextual.** It is the operator only where a complete operand is followed by `is`; everywhere else it is\n  an identifier. A keyword would break `c.types.is(t, …)`, which `src/` uses throughout.\n- **Precedence.** That of `==`, non-associative. After `is` the pattern grammar reads as far as it can, so `|` there is\n  an or-pattern: `(c is 'a') | flag` needs parentheses.\n- **Meaning.** The test of `e is P` is that of `match (e) { P => true, _ => false }`: `e` is evaluated once; tests\n  only read.\n- **Bindings** are in scope only where the test is known true: in the later operands of the same top-level `&&`\n  chain, and in the `if`'s then-branch or the `while`'s body whose condition is that chain. A pattern with bindings\n  anywhere else (under `||` or `!`, in a `let` initialiser, as an argument) is N0359. Bindings copy, move or\n  reference as in §15.2.\n- **Moves happen only on success.** Unlike a consuming `match` (§18.4), `is` moves nothing when the test fails. When\n  it succeeds and binds a non-Copy payload of a value scrutinee, the scrutinee is consumed at that point, as §18.4\n  consumes it (unbound payloads go to hidden locals). The move checker sees a place scrutinee as moved on the true\n  path only, so `if (e is Some(v)) { A } else { B }` leaves `e` whole in `B`, as Rust's `if let` does.\n- **Drop points.** Bindings and hidden locals live to the end of the then-branch, or of the `while` body for that\n  iteration. When a later operand of the `&&` chain is `false`, those made so far are destroyed at once, in reverse\n  order, before the `else` branch runs or the loop exits: in `x is Some(v) && g`, a false `g` destroys `v` there.\n- **Temporaries.** A temporary scrutinee is destroyed at the end of the condition, unless a binding refers into it\n  (a reference, or a slice or view of it, §2.7); then it lives to the end of the then-branch or of this iteration's\n  body. It never lives into the `else` branch or the next iteration (Rust 2024's `if let` scope, applied to `while`\n  too). So a `RefCell` guard (ADR 0084) used to copy a value out is gone before the body, and a `.write()` in the\n  body or in `else` does not panic; one that the body still reads through is visible in the condition. A `while`\n  evaluates the scrutinee again each iteration.\n- `matches!(x, P if g)` is `x is P && g`.\n\n### 2.9 Translation rules (no language change)\n\n| Rust | CeeSafe |\n|---|---|\n| `let Some(n) = x else { return 0; };` | `let n = match (x) { Some(n) => n, _ => { return 0; } };` (works today) |\n| `b'x'` in a pattern | `'x'` |\n| `b\"abc\"` in a pattern | `\"abc\"` (§2.7) |\n| `ref x`, `ref mut x` | match on `borrow` / `borrow mut` (§15.2) |\n| `if let P = e { A } else { B }` | `if (e is P) { A } else { B }` (`e` moves only on success, §2.8) |\n| `match (a, b) { … }` | nested `match`, or `is` chains (§4) |\n| `comptime_string_map!` / `phf_map!` | a `static` view table (ADR 0066) searched by a function |\n\n### 2.10 Lowering\n\n- **Switch where it can be.** A match whose scrutinee is an integer, `bool` or fieldless enum, with no guard, and whose\n  arms are literals, constants, ranges and or-patterns of them (and a final `_` or binding), lowers through\n  `lowerMatch` to one NIR `Switch`. Ranges and or-patterns expand into cases when the expanded count is at most 1,024\n  (a fixed number, so the output is deterministic); past it, the match takes the sequential path. Bun's lexer arms\n  become a C `switch`, which C compilers turn into a jump table.\n- **Sequential tests otherwise.** Guards, strings, slices, `@` and or-patterns with bindings use\n  `lowerNestedMatch`'s per-arm test and fall-through. `testPattern` gains: a range (two comparisons), a string (length,\n  then `cee",
    401 =>
        "_slice_eq`, ADR 0072), a slice (a length test, `==` k or `>=` k, then element tests at index i or\n  `len - k + j`; the indexes are proven by the length test and emitted without bounds checks), an or-pattern (each\n  alternative's test jumps to its own bind block, since its bindings sit in other places, then to the shared body), a\n  guard (a branch after binding, to the body or to the next arm).\n- **No decision trees.** Compiling to Maranget's decision trees would test each position once but duplicate arm\n  bodies or need join blocks for bindings and moves. The C compiler already merges chains of tests on one value. This\n  can be an optimisation later, behind the same semantics.\n- **The interpreter** runs NIR and needs nothing new: ranges lower to comparisons, slice tests to `.length` and\n  indexing. **The formatter** prints `a | b`, `'a'..='z'`, `n @ P`, `P if (g) =>`, `e is P`, one space around each.\n\n### 2.11 Diagnostics\n\nCodes N0355–N0359 are this ADR's share of the set's numbering (ADRs 0081–0087). A double move through `@` (§2.5) is\nthe existing N0250 with its own message.\n\n| Code | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| N0355 | a guard moves, assigns or `mut`-lends the scrutinee or a binding, or awaits | `Some(v) if (consume(v)) =>` on a non-Copy `v` |\n| N0356 | alternatives of an or-pattern bind different names, types or `var`-ness | `Some(x) \\| null =>` |\n| N0357 | an empty range pattern | `'z'..='a'` |\n| N0358 | a constant of a type that cannot be a pattern, a const generic parameter, or `T.NAME` through a type parameter | a `const` struct or `f64`; `QUOTE =>` with `<const QUOTE: u8>` |\n| N0359 | an `is` pattern with bindings where the test is not known true | `if (!(x is Some(n))) …` |\n\nN0108's text lists the new forms; N0207 no longer applies to `StringView`, `Slice<T>` or arrays; N0256 and N0257 gain\nthe guard wording of §2.2.\n\n## 3. Alternatives considered\n\n- **Guards as `when g` (C#) or `if g` without parentheses (Rust).** `when` is a new word; a bare `if g` would be the\n  one condition in CeeSafe without parentheses. `if (g)` reuses both the keyword and the rule.\n- **`if (let P = e)` and `while (let P = e)`, plus a `matches(e, P)` built-in.** Closer to Rust's text, but two\n  constructs for one idea, and a built-in that takes a pattern as an argument is a new kind of call. `is` is one\n  operator, reads as C#'s does, and composes with `&&`.\n- **`is` as a keyword.** Simpler to parse, but it breaks the identifier `is`, used across `src/`.\n- **Keep \"integer literals never cover their type\".** A port then adds `_ => unreachable` to every split byte match.\n  An unreachable panic is weaker than a checked proof, and interval splitting is a known, small extension.\n- **Constants only when qualified (`Bytes.LF`, an associated constant, ADR 0074 §2.7), bare names always\n  bindings.** No meaning change and no misspelling hazard, but 890 Bun patterns would need renaming into namespaces,\n  and a top-level `LF =>` would bind silently. The N0257 and unused-binding backstop makes Rust's rule safe enough.\n- **A `b\"…\"` byte-string literal.** The set's rule for literal prefixes: a letter written directly before `\"` is a\n  prefix, reserved (N0100) unless an ADR defines it. ADR 0087 defines `c\"…\"`; `b` is not added here, because a string\n  pattern is typed by its scrutinee (§2.7), so patterns do not need it. Byte strings in expressions are §4's.\n- **Guards translated to nested `if`s.** That is today's rule: it copies the fall-through arm's body into each `else`,\n  6,780 times, and loses exhaustiveness checking of what follows.\n\n## 4. Not in this ADR\n\n- **Tuple patterns and `match (a, b)`** (4,058 arm lines): CeeSafe has no tuples. Translation: a nested `match`, or\n  `is` chains. If tuples come, their patterns follow §15.2's payload rules.\n- **Struct patterns and named variant fields** (`V { f, .. }`, 4,446): CeeSafe enum payloads are positional.\n  Translation: positional payloads, or field reads in a guard.\n- **`let P = e e",
    402 =>
        "lse { … };`**: a translation rule (§2.9). It may come later as `let`'s own form.\n- **Byte-string literals in expressions**, float patterns, `box`/deref patterns, patterns on `String` or `Vector`\n  values (match on their view).\n\n## 5. Testing\n\n- **The corpus, run-pass**, each also in the interpreter, outputs compared: guards (order, fall-through, a guard on a\n  consuming match that fails and moves nothing, an or-pattern whose guard runs twice), or-patterns at the top and\n  nested, ranges both ways and an exhaustive split `u8` match, `@`, constants and top-level bindings, string patterns\n  on `StringView` and `Slice<u8>`, slice patterns of every shape including `rest @ ..`, `is` in `let`, `if`, `while`\n  and `&&` chains, `matches!`-style `is` with a following `&& g`, `if (e is P) … else` using `e` in `else`, a false\n  `&&` operand destroying a binding before `else` (a `drop` that prints), a temporary destroyed at the end of the\n  condition (a `RefCell` written in the body).\n- **Check-fail**: each of N0355–N0359 (a const generic parameter and `Self.NAME` as patterns included); N0250 for\n  `e @ Err(inner)` with a non-Copy payload; N0256 with only guarded arms; N0257 for a guarded arm and for an\n  or-alternative; `..=` in an expression; two `..` in a slice.\n- **Usefulness unit tests**: witnesses for interval splits (`'{'`, `128`, `-1`), for slice lengths (`[]`, `[_, _, ..]`).\n- **Parsing and formatting**: parse-pass snapshots for `is` precedence and `|` after `is`; `ceesafec fmt` round-trips.\n- **C backend**: a snapshot that a lexer-style range match emits one `switch`, and one past 1,024 cases emits tests.\n- **The Bun probe**: `analysis/probes/g2_guard.cee` (with `if (n > 3)`) compiles and prints `big`; the five §1.2\n  examples, ported by the rules above, compile and agree with Rust's results.\n- **Meaning change scan** (§2.6): before release, the checker lists every payload binding named like a `const` in\n  scope across CeeSafe, CeeSafeLibs and CeeSafeApps; each is renamed first.\n\n## 6. Questions for the owner\n\n1. **Guard syntax `P if (g) =>`,** with parentheses. **Recommended: yes**: it is CeeSafe's condition syntax.\n2. **`e is P` as the one form for `matches!`, `if let`, `while let` and let chains,** with `is` contextual.\n   **Recommended: yes** (§3: one operator; the identifier `is` keeps working).\n3. **Integer literals and ranges cover their type when together they cover every value.** This amends §15.2.\n   **Recommended: yes**.\n4. **Bare identifiers in patterns: a constant if one is in scope, otherwise a binding, at every depth** (Rust's rule,\n   with the §5 scan). **Recommended: yes**.\n5. **String literal patterns typed by the scrutinee** (`StringView` or `Slice<u8>`), no `b\"…\"` token here.\n   **Recommended: yes**.\n6. **An or-pattern with a guard runs the guard once per matching alternative**, as Rust does. **Recommended: yes**, for\n   a faithful port; the order is still fixed.\n7. **Release in two steps:** 0.25.0 with guards, or-patterns, ranges, `@`, constants and top-level bindings (§2.2–§2.6,\n   what the lexers need); 0.26.0 with strings, slices and `is` (§2.7–§2.8). **Recommended: yes**: the first step is\n   mostly parser and usefulness work, the second touches scoping and new scrutinee types.\n\n## 7. Implementation notes (step 2, 0.26.0)\n\nStep 2 (§2.7, §2.8 and their parts of §2.10, §2.11 and §5) is implemented as written, with these precisions:\n\n- **`&&` chains outside a condition.** An operand's bindings are in scope in the chain's later operands, so\n  `let big = x is Some(v) && v > 3;` is allowed; the last operand's bindings would have no scope and are N0359, as are\n  those under `!` or `||`, of a whole `let` initialiser and of an argument. Parentheses around a chain's operand do not\n  change its place in the chain. Such bindings end at the end of their statement, with its temporaries (decided\n  2026-11-09, after review): in `let ok = c.tryRead() is Some(g) && g.data() == 5;` the guard is re",
    403 =>
        "leased before the\n  next statement.\n- **Temporaries.** When a binding borrows from what a condition's temporaries hold (a test of a reference or view\n  that is not a place, such as `c.read().data().view() is [first, ..]`, or `rest @ ..` on an array that is not a\n  place; a field of a temporary, `make().arr`, is the temporary's), all of that condition's temporaries live to the\n  end of the then-branch or of the body's pass; otherwise all end with the condition. In both cases none lives into the `else` branch or the next test (ADR 0084 §41.3).\n- **`var` on a slice element that is not Copy** is N0246, the existing code for `var` on a binding by reference.\n- **Arrays.** String patterns apply to `StringView` and `Slice<u8>` only, not to `[u8; N]` (match on `a[..]`). Arrays\n  of non-Copy elements do not exist yet (N0259, type-system.md §16.1), so §2.7's rule for element bindings of such an\n  array held by value is checked but cannot arise.\n- **Witnesses.** N0256 writes `_` for a position no arm constrains (a slice's elements: `[_, _, ..]`) rather than the\n  lowest value of its type.\n- **Flexible values.** A value branch whose value is a flexible literal (a value block ending in `3`) is checked\n  after the other branches, which fix its type, with the names it saw in scope: its arm's or its condition's\n  bindings (fixed 2026-11-09; before, a block that used them reported N0200).\n- **Payload views.** A pattern on a payload that is a view (`Some(…)` on a `StringView?` or `Slice<u8>?`) may be a\n  string or slice pattern, an or-pattern of them (`Some(\"x\" | \"y\")`), or `name @` one (`Some(s @ \"zz\")`), which binds\n  the view itself.\n- **Const-length arrays.** On `[T; N]` with N a const parameter (ADR 0083), the generic version checks slice and\n  array patterns as on a slice; in an instance, an arm whose count N cannot have is never taken, not an error\n  (ADR 0083 §7).\n",
    404 =>
        "# ADR 0086 — Interfaces: default methods, `Self`, associated types and constants, and an `Iterator<T>` interface\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-09 (\"Accept all ADRs as recommended\": every question for the owner answered as recommended), the day it was proposed. Raised by the Bun port's M0 (`~/dev/CeeSafeApps/bun/ROADMAP.md` §3, M0: \"default\n  interface methods; decide on associated types and an iterator trait\"). It rests on three things: counts of Bun\n  1.4.4's 540 traits and 5,070 trait impls, measured on 2026-11-09 with small Python scripts (§1.1); probes compiled\n  with ceesafec 0.24.2 the same day (§1.2); and the current checker, parser and C backend sources (cited by line).\n- Scope: type-system.md §20.1 (generic methods), §20.2 (inference), §21.1–§21.2 (interfaces, bounds), §10 (associated\n  constants), §16.4 (`for` over an iterator), §37.1 and §37.4 (dyn compatibility, tables); grammar.md §1.3 (`Self`)\n  and §2 (`InterfaceDecl`, `ImplDecl`, the `T.Item` type); diagnostics.md (N0267, N0331, N0265); the parser\n  (`src/syntax/parser.cee`), the checker (`src/check/interfaces.cee`, `declare.cee`, `arrays.cee`, `dyn.cee`), the C\n  backend's tables (`src/backend/c_dyn.cee`), the interpreter; lib/core (`Iterator<T>`, `text.cee`'s iterators); the\n  corpus; guide/coming-from/rust.md and guide/for-agents.md.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n### 1.1 What Bun uses\n\nMethod: a walk of `upstream/src/**/*.rs` that skips `test`/`tests` directories and generated files, like\n`analysis/bun_rust_features.py` (3,186 files). Comments and string literals are stripped, then trait bodies are found\nby brace matching and their items classified at depth 1. Run with `python3 -I traits.py upstream` (and\n`dyntraits.py`, `supers.py`) from the port's root; the scripts and probes are in\n`~/dev/CeeSafeApps/bun/analysis/adr-evidence/0086/`. The counts are regex-based, so they are approximate, to a few\nper cent, and cover only that file set: a count over every `.rs` file, tests and comments included, finds a little\nmore (547 traits, 5,120 `impl … for`, 49 `impl Iterator for` in one reviewer's count).\n\n| What | Count | Notes |\n|---|---:|---|\n| Trait definitions / `impl … for` | 540 / 5,070 | 5,082 with comments counted (the census) |\n| Required / default trait methods | 1,389 / 555 | 124 traits have defaults: 58 have one, 35 have 2–4, 31 have 5 or more |\n| Defaults that call a required method of their trait | 122 | `self.m(…)` or `Self::m(…)` |\n| Defaults in traits with no required method | 136 | all-hooks traits (the regex validator's `Handler`: 36 defaults) and extension traits |\n| Trait functions without `self` (static) | 454 | 23% of trait functions; constructors `fn new(…) -> Self` |\n| Trait functions naming `Self` in their signature | 496 | 117 return `Self`, `Option<Self>` or `Result<Self, _>` |\n| Generic methods in traits | 75 | including `impl Trait` arguments |\n| Associated types | 83 in 53 traits | 39 bounded, 10 with a default, 15 generic (GATs) |\n| Associated constants | 166 in 87 traits | 58 with a default |\n| Traits with supertraits | 247 | names: `Sized` 119, `Copy` 73, `Sync` 19, `Send` 10; 54 name a first-party trait |\n| Traits used as `dyn` | 34 | 17 have defaults; at most 2 have a member a table cannot hold (one, a `Handler`, has 12 constants and a type), and both trait names are shared with other traits, so a `dyn` use may be the other trait |\n| `impl Iterator for` / `impl IntoIterator for` | 51 / 25 | `lint` 15, `bun_core` 7, `format` 6, `collections` 6 |\n| Functions returning `impl Iterator` | 270 | `lint` 141, `sema` 31, `format` 25 |\n| `Iterator<Item = …>` / `IntoIterator<Item = …>` | 373 / 59 | in bounds and `impl Iterator<…>` results |\n| Iterator sources (`.iter()`, `.chars()`, `.split(…)`, `.keys()`, …) | 15,772 | 10,452 followed by an adapter; 445 by 3 or more |\n\nAdapter chains, by the number of chains that use each adapter: `map` 2,038, `any` 2,019, `enumerate` 1,405, `collect`\n1,295, `filter` ",
    405 =>
        "888, `zip` 710, `all` 701, `find` 658, `count` 451, `filter_map` 420, `take_while` 399, `copied` 398,\n`rev` 368, `position` 338, `flatten` 229, `chain` 227, `find_map` 164, `flat_map` 162, `skip` 144, `for_each` 137,\n`fold` 102, `take` 100; the rest under 75 each.\n\nExamples:\n- A default that calls a required method, `src/watcher/Watcher.rs:146`–`156`:\n  `fn on_error(&mut self, err: sys::Error);` then\n  `fn on_watch_error(&mut self, err: sys::Error) { self.on_error(err); }`.\n- The lint `Rule` trait (892 impls), `src/lint/rule.rs:638`: `const META: Meta;` (639), `type State<'a>;` (647),\n  `fn new(options: &Options) -> Self;` (652), and 27 empty hooks such as\n  `fn expr<'a>(&self, _expr: Expr<'a>, _cx: &mut Cx<'a, Self>) {}` (675), of which each rule overrides a few.\n- uWS's socket handler, `src/uws_sys/vtable.rs:38`: `type Ext;` (40) and `const HAS_ON_DATA: bool = false;` (46):\n  constants with defaults that select code at compile time.\n- An iterator, `src/lint/types/symbol.rs:107`: `impl<'a> Iterator for SymbolIter<'a> { type Item = TsSymbol<'a>;\n  fn next(&mut self) -> Option<TsSymbol<'a>> { … } }`.\n\n### 1.2 What CeeSafe does today (ceesafec 0.24.2, probes of 2026-11-09)\n\n| Probe | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `impl Named for A` without the interface's `greet`, which has a body (Bun's `analysis/probes/g4_default_method.cee`) | 0.24.2: N0267 \"'impl Named for A' is missing method 'greet'\", then N0200 at `a.greet()`; since `b7c9f41`: N0100 at the body |\n| An interface method body that returns `\"not a number\"` from a `u32` method (`p1_body_unchecked.cee`) | 0.24.2: **accepted with no error**, the body parsed and never checked; since `b7c9f41`: N0100 \"expected ';' after an interface method's signature … an interface method has no body\" |\n| `fn copyOf(borrow this) -> Self;` | N0105 \"expected type, found 'Self'\" |\n| `type Item;` in an interface | N0100, N0101 |\n| `interface B: A { … }` | N0100 \"expected '{', found ':'\" |\n| `fn make() -> u32;` in an interface | N0267 \"interface method 'make' needs a receiver\" (`src/check/interfaces.cee:112`) |\n| `fn visit<T>(…)` in an interface and its impl | N0216 (`src/check/declare.cee:620`; ADR 0064 §1) |\n| `for (x in it)` with `it: I`, `I: Iter<u32>` (`p6`, `p8`) | N0205 (`src/check/arrays.cee:553`): the structural rule needs a struct |\n| `for (x in it)` with `it: borrow mut dyn Iter<u32>` (`p9`) | N0205 |\n| The same loop written with `loop`/`match (i.next())` through the bound (`p6b`) | works (prints 10) |\n| `impl<region r> Iter<StringView<r>> for Words<r>` (`p11`) | works: a scoped item type is fine as an interface argument |\n\nSo the parts are mostly there. An impl's methods are declared on the target by `declareFunctionIn`\n(`src/check/interfaces.cee:704`) and recorded as `ImplMethod`s (`:713`, looked up by `implMethodFor`, `:752`). Bound\ncalls and `dyn` tables both find methods there: `emitDynTables` emits one adapter per HIR method in `table.methods`\n(`src/backend/c_dyn.cee:183`–`200`). Generic impls (ADR 0054) already check a method once and instantiate it per\ntarget instance.\n\n### 1.3 Earlier decisions on the subject\n\n- type-system.md §21.1: every interface method has a receiver, methods have no type parameters, \"v0 has no `Self`\n  type\", and an impl \"must define exactly the interface's methods\". Since commit `b7c9f41` (after the probes above),\n  a signature ends with `;`: a body, a block or `=> e`, is N0100 at parse time, which closed the unchecked-body hole\n  of `p1`. The same commit made every `dyn` box not `Share` (§38.1); nothing here changes that.\n- ADR 0052 chose `HashKey<K>` with the bound `K: HashKey<K>` because there is no `Self`; `src/support/map.cee:5` says\n  the same.\n- ADR 0064 §1 did not propose generic methods in interfaces, because \"a table cannot hold an unbounded family of\n  instances\".\n- ADR 0074 §2.7 added associated constants to structs and inherent impls, read as `Type.NAME`, but not to\n  interfaces. ADR 0074 §2.8 made `for` structural over `next(borrow mut ",
    406 =>
        "this) -> T?`, and rejected an `Iterator<T>`\n  interface because it \"gives nothing over the shape rule today\". The probes above show what it would give:\n  iteration over a bound or a `dyn`.\n\nThis ADR **extends** §21 and ADR 0074 §2.7–§2.8. It **amends** §21.1's body rule (`b7c9f41`'s N0100), its receiver\nrule and its rule against generic methods, ADR 0064 §1, and §37.1's N0331 rule (one rule for every member a table\ncannot hold). It reverses none of them: every program accepted today keeps its meaning.\n\n## 2. Proposal\n\n### 2.1 Default methods\n\n```ceesafe\ninterface WatcherContext {\n    fn onError(borrow mut this, err: IoError);\n    fn onWatchError(borrow mut this, err: IoError) { this.onError(err); }   // a default\n}\n```\n\n- An interface method with a body (`{ … }` or `=> e;`) is a **default**: this lifts the N0100 that `b7c9f41` gives\n  a body on an interface method (§21.1). An impl may define it (an **override**, with the same signature: N0267\n  otherwise, as today) or leave it out (the impl **inherits** it). A missing method\n  that has no default is still N0267.\n- **Checking, once.** The default is checked at the interface, as a generic body (§20.3) with one type parameter,\n  the implementing type `Self` (§2.2), bounded by the interface itself and its supertraits (§2.6). `this` is `Self`,\n  `borrow Self` or `borrow mut Self`, as the receiver says. The body may call any method of the interface (required\n  or default) and of its supertraits, and read its constants (§2.4). It cannot use fields or `Self`'s other methods\n  (N0234). An error in a default is reported once, at the interface, whether or not any impl inherits it, so `p1`'s\n  ill-typed body, N0100 today, becomes a type error at the interface, never an unchecked body.\n- **Instantiation, per impl.** For every impl that inherits it, the default is instantiated with `Self` = the target\n  (and, in a generic impl, once per instance, as the impl's own methods are: ADR 0054). The instance is an ordinary\n  method of the target, recorded as the impl's methods are. So `a.onWatchError(e)` works on the concrete type; a call\n  through a bound and a `dyn` table each name the instance, and dispatch stays static (§21.2). An instance that does\n  not check for its target is N0222 at the impl, as for any instantiation (§20.4).\n- **Names.** An inherited default is a member of the target like a written one. If the target already has a method\n  of that name (its own, or another impl's), that is N0228 at the impl, as today for two impls that write one. An\n  override does not help, since the written method would clash the same way, and there is no qualified call\n  (`I.method(x)`). This is today's limit (one method name per type, §21.1), not a new one: a type cannot implement\n  two interfaces that share a method name, default or not. The port renames one of the two methods where a Bun type\n  implements both (not measured; Bun's shared trait *names*, §1.1, are a different matter). Qualified calls, if the\n  port finds the renames costly, are a later ADR.\n- **Effects.** The interface's `@effects` list bounds the default as it bounds every impl (§37.3). The instance has\n  its own body's effects.\n- **`dyn`.** A default is in the table like any method (§37.4). An inherited one's slot holds its instance's\n  adapter, so nothing in `c_dyn.cee` changes. Of Bun's 34 `dyn` traits, 17 have defaults.\n- There is no call from an override to the default it replaces (Rust has none either).\n- **Derived impls** (ADR 0087's `@derive(Equal, HashKey, OrderKey)`) are ordinary impls: the compiler writes the\n  required methods, the impl inherits the interface's defaults, and the written code may use `Self` (§2.2).\n\n### 2.2 `Self`\n\n- `Self` is already a keyword (grammar.md §1.3) with no meaning; this ADR gives it one. Declaring an item, a type\n  parameter or a local named `Self` stays N0100 (\"a reserved word\"), as for `const`.\n- **In an interface**, `Self` is the implementing type: opaque in the inte",
    407 =>
        "rface's signatures and defaults, and\n  substituted by each impl's target. An impl writes either the target or `Self` where the interface has `Self`.\n- **In an `impl` block (inherent or for an interface) and in a struct or enum body**, `Self` is the target type with\n  its parameters (`Pair<T>`, `View<r>`). It is valid wherever that type's name is: a type, a struct literal\n  (`Self { first: a, second: b }`), a static call (`Self.make()`) and a constant (`Self.MAX`). Elsewhere it is N0105\n  in a type and N0200 in an expression.\n- With `Self`, ADR 0052's `interface HashKey<K>` could become `interface HashKey`. Nothing here requires that change.\n\n### 2.3 Static functions and generic methods in interfaces\n\n- An interface may declare a function **without a receiver** (`fn create(options: borrow Options) -> Self;`), with\n  or without a default. Through a bound it is called as `T.create(o)`, in a default as `Self.create(o)`, and on a\n  concrete type as `Rule.create(o)`. This lifts §21.1's \"needs a receiver\" N0267.\n- An interface method may have **type parameters of its own** (§20.1). Through a bound and on a concrete type, it is\n  instantiated per call, as a struct's generic method is (ADR 0064 §1). This lifts N0216 for interfaces. Const\n  parameters on an interface method (ADR 0083, from 0.26.0) follow the same rule as type parameters: allowed,\n  instantiated per call, and N0331 for `dyn`.\n- **One dyn-compatibility rule** (§37.1, amended): `dyn I` is N0331, naming the member, if any method of `I` or of its\n  supertraits takes `this` by value (as today), has no receiver, has type or const parameters, or names `Self`\n  outside its receiver, or if `I` or a supertrait has an associated type (§2.5) or a constant (§2.4). Defaults do not\n  affect it. The rule is what keeps `T = dyn I` sound: `dyn I` implements `I` for bounds (§37.2, ADR 0064 §3.1;\n  `fn g<T: I>(x: borrow T)` takes a `borrow dyn I` today: a probe of 2026-11-09 prints 7), and generic code may read\n  `T.NAME`, call `T.create(…)` or name `T.Item`, none of which a table without a value can answer. Every member\n  N0331 lets through is a method with a receiver, which the table holds. Bun's measure: of 34 `dyn` traits, at most 2\n  have such a member (the constants add none: the one `dyn` trait with constants, a `Handler`, already has static\n  functions), so the rule costs almost nothing in the port.\n\n### 2.4 Constants in interfaces\n\n- An interface may declare `const NAME: T;` or `const NAME: T = v;` (a default). An impl gives\n  `const NAME: T = v;`; a missing constant without a default is N0267. The value is a constant expression (§10). A\n  default may not use `Self`.\n- Each constant becomes an associated constant of the target (ADR 0074 §2.7), read as `Type.NAME`, so the rules\n  for names (N0228) and visibility are ADR 0074's. In generic code and defaults, it is read as `T.NAME` or\n  `Self.NAME`. There it is a value, not a constant expression (not an array length or a pattern), because it is\n  known only per instance. Once instantiated it is the constant, so `if (H.HAS_ON_DATA) { … }` costs nothing at run\n  time.\n- An interface with a constant has no `dyn` type (N0331, §2.3), so a constant is never read through a table and\n  `T.NAME` never meets `T = dyn I`.\n- As in ADR 0074, a generic impl cannot define one (N0216), but its instances get the interface's defaults.\n\n### 2.5 Associated types\n\n```ceesafe\ninterface Encoding {\n    type Unit: Copy;\n    fn decode(borrow this, bytes: Slice<u8>) -> Self.Unit?;\n}\nimpl Encoding for Utf16 { type Unit = u16; fn decode(borrow this, bytes: Slice<u8>) -> u16? { … } }\nfn first<E: Encoding>(e: borrow E, b: Slice<u8>) -> E.Unit? => e.decode(b);\n```\n\n- An interface may declare `type Name;` or `type Name: B1 + B2;` (bounds as in §21.2). Each impl must define\n  `type Name = T;` (N0267 if it is missing or extra). The impl's type and region parameters may appear in `T`\n  (`impl<region r> Source for Words<r> { type Item = StringView<r>; }",
    408 =>
        "`). `T` must meet the bounds (N0268, at the\n  impl).\n- **Projection.** In a type, `P.Name` names the associated type, where `P` is `Self` inside the interface or a type\n  parameter whose bounds (with their supertraits) declare exactly one associated type `Name`. Zero is N0200; two or\n  more is N0267, ambiguous, as for bound methods. In generic code, `P.Name` is an opaque type with the declared\n  bounds (§20.3). Instantiation substitutes the impl's type. A dependency's name cannot be a type parameter\n  (N0315), so `P.Name` never reads as `package.Type`.\n- `Encoding.Unit` on a concrete type is not proposed: the port writes the type itself there.\n- Not proposed: defaults, bounds that fix an associated type (`E: Encoding<Unit = u16>`), and associated types with\n  parameters of their own (§4). An interface whose callers need to fix a type takes it as a parameter instead, as\n  `Iterator<T>` does (§2.7).\n\n### 2.6 Supertraits\n\n- `interface B: A + Copy { … }`: every supertrait is an interface instantiation or `Copy`. `impl B for T` requires\n  `T` to implement each one (N0268, at the impl). A bound `T: B` implies `T: A`: the methods, constants and\n  associated types of `A` are reachable through it (§21.2's ambiguity rule applies to the union).\n- `dyn B`'s table holds `A`'s methods after `B`'s, in declaration order, so `A`'s methods can be called through it, and\n  `dyn B` implements `A` for bounds as well as `B` (ADR 0064 §3.1). There is still no conversion from `dyn B` to\n  `dyn A` (§37.2).\n- A cycle of supertraits is N0227. Rust's `Sized` supertrait (119 in Bun) translates to nothing: a CeeSafe type\n  parameter is sized unless it is given a `dyn` (§37.2).\n\n### 2.7 Iteration: `Iterator<T>` in lib/core, and loops instead of adapters\n\n- lib/core declares `public interface Iterator<T> { fn next(borrow mut this) -> T?; }`. lib/core's iterators\n  (`TextSplit`, `TextLines` and the other `next` structs in `lib/core/text.cee`) and lib/std's implement it.\n- **`for` (§16.4, extended).** Beyond the structural rule, which stays (no impl is needed for a concrete struct),\n  `for (x in e)` accepts an `e` of type `I` (or `borrow mut I`), where `I` is a type parameter bounded by\n  `Iterator<T>`, and a `borrow mut dyn Iterator<T>` or a mutable place of `Box<dyn Iterator<T>>`. `x` has type `T`.\n  The header, borrowing and destruction rules are §16.4's.\n- **The item is a parameter, not an associated type.** Callers fix it directly (`I: Iterator<u8>`), with no\n  equality bound, and `dyn Iterator<T>` stays dyn-compatible (§2.3). Rust's 373 `Iterator<Item = X>` become\n  `Iterator<X>`.\n- **Inference from a bound (§20.2, extended).** A type parameter that the arguments do not determine is inferred\n  from a bound `I: J<…T…>` when `I`'s argument implements exactly one instantiation of `J`. Otherwise it is N0265, as\n  today. So `fn count<T, I: Iterator<T>>(it: I) -> usize` is called as `count(text.split(\",\"))`. For `Iterator`, the\n  instantiation is always unique: two impls on one type would both define `next` (N0228).\n- **No `IntoIterator`.** `for` already takes arrays, slices, vectors and iterators. A type with something to iterate\n  gets an `iter()` method that returns its iterator struct (Bun's 25 `IntoIterator` impls).\n- **No adapter library in this ADR.** A lazy `Map<I, F>` would hold a closure in a field, and a closure in a field\n  cannot be called (AGENTS.md, \"Common mistakes\"). Without `impl Iterator` results, every chain would also need a\n  named type. The port follows its roadmap (§3, \"iterator chains become loops\"), with this translation:\n\n| Adapter (chains) | Loop form |\n|---|---|\n| `map`, `filter`, `filter_map`, `copied`, `cloned` (≈4,100) | the body, with `if` or `match` for the filter; copies are implicit for Copy elements |\n| `collect` (1,295) | a `Vector` the loop pushes into (`?` on each `push`) |\n| `any`, `all` (2,720) | lib/core's slice `any`/`all` (§36.4) on a slice, else a loop with `break` |\n| `find`, `position`, `find_",
    409 =>
        "map`, `count`, `fold`, `sum`, `max`, `min` (≈1,800) | a loop with a result local, and `break` where Rust short-circuits |\n| `enumerate`, `zip`, `rev`, `skip`, `take`, `step_by` (≈2,750) | `for (i in 0..n)` over indexes; `rev` counts down with `while` |\n| `take_while`, `skip_while` (409) | `break`, or a flag |\n| `chain`, `flatten`, `flat_map` (618) | two loops, or nested loops |\n| `peekable` (61) | a local holding the looked-ahead optional |\n\n- A function returning `impl Iterator` (270) returns a named iterator struct implementing `Iterator<T>`. If its\n  chain has closures, the struct does their work in its `next`.\n\n## 3. Alternatives considered\n\n- **One shared default body, called through a table** (as Java and Swift do). It makes one C function, but every\n  call on a concrete type or through a bound becomes indirect, against §21.2's static dispatch, and a `borrow this` of\n  an unknown size needs a table even where no `dyn` exists. Monomorphising matches generic impls (ADR 0054), and the\n  C compiler folds empty defaults like `Rule`'s hooks.\n- **Defaults as free generic functions** (`fn onWatchError<W: WatcherContext>(w: borrow mut W, …)`), which work\n  today (probe `p10`). Every caller has to know which half of the API is which (ADR 0064 §1's complaint). It also\n  cannot be overridden, and Bun's lint rules override some of 27 hooks.\n- **Associated types as extra interface parameters only.** `trait Handler { type Ext; }` would become\n  `interface Handler<E>`, and every `H: Handler` would become `<E, H: Handler<E>>` in every signature and struct\n  that holds one. With §2.7's inference rule this works, but it spreads a parameter through 53 traits' users. Both\n  are proposed: associated types where the impl chooses the type, a parameter where callers fix it.\n- **`Iterator` with an associated `Item` and equality bounds** (Rust's design). It needs `I: Iterator<Item = u8>`,\n  a new bound form, and makes `dyn Iterator` need `dyn Iterator<Item = u8>`. The parameter gives the same\n  uniqueness for free (N0228 on `next`).\n- **Keep `for` structural only** (ADR 0074 §2.8). Then generic and `dyn` iteration stays a `loop`/`match`, which\n  the port would write about 25 times for `dyn Iterator` and wherever `I: Iterator` is a bound (32).\n- **Lazy adapters in lib/core, with closures as type parameters.** This needs callable closure fields and opaque\n  result types, two language changes larger than this ADR, for chains the roadmap already turns into loops.\n- **Rust's `where Self: Sized`, to keep a few methods out of the table.** It is more to learn for the ≤2 `dyn`\n  traits that need it.\n\n## 4. Not in this ADR\n\n- Associated types with their own type or region parameters (15 GATs). The largest user is `Rule`'s\n  `type State<'a>` (581 impls define it). That needs region-parameterised associated types and its own ADR; until\n  then the port translates `Rule` by hand (open question for M1).\n- Defaults for associated types (10), and bounds that fix one (`E: Encoding<Unit = u16>`, about 40 outside the\n  iterator traits).\n- Blanket impls, `impl<T: A> B for T` (24 in Bun). They need a new overlap rule. Extension traits translate to\n  generic free functions or to defaults (§2.1).\n- Opaque result types (`-> impl Iterator`), callable closure fields, and lazy adapters.\n- A conversion from `dyn B` to `dyn A`; `dyn I` of an interface with associated types, constants or static functions.\n- Constants in generic impls (ADR 0074 §4, unchanged); a default constant that uses `Self`.\n- `Send` and `Sync` as supertraits (Bun: 29). Threads take `dyn I + Send` (§38); whether `Send` becomes a general\n  bound is for the thread model, not here.\n- Changing `HashKey<K>` to use `Self` (§2.2).\n- The compiler's own sources: they must still build with the seed chain's compiler (ADR 0080 §4), so none of this\n  is used in `src/`.\n\n## 5. Testing\n\nRun (2026-11-09, `~/dev/CeeSafe/out/ceesafec` 0.24.2, `check` or `run --interp`, the probes in\n`~/dev/CeeSafeApps/bun/an",
    410 =>
        "alysis/adr-evidence/0086/`): the twelve probes of §1.2, with the results shown there. Bun's\nown probe `analysis/probes/g4_default_method.cee` gave N0267 and N0200, as stated in the port's roadmap; rerun after\n`b7c9f41`, it and `p1` give N0100 at the body. A `borrow dyn I` passed to `g<T: I>` prints 7;\n`struct Self` is N0100 \"a reserved word\".\n\nProposed (corpus, each run in the interpreter and built through C with `--check-leaks`):\n- **run-pass `interface-defaults`:** an inherited default calls a required method; an override wins; a default calls\n  another default; a generic impl's instances inherit; the same calls through a bound and through\n  `Box<dyn I>`/`borrow mut dyn I` (the inherited slot); a default with a move-type `Self` whose instance drops it.\n  Expected output written by hand from the program text.\n- **run-pass `interface-self-static`:** `fn create(…) -> Self` through `T.create`; `Self` in an impl as a type,\n  literal, static call and constant; a generic interface method instantiated at two types.\n- **run-pass `interface-assoc`:** constants with and without defaults, read as `T.NAME` and `Type.NAME`, and a\n  compile-time switch; an associated type with a bound used in a generic function; supertrait methods through a bound\n  and through `dyn B`.\n- **run-pass `iterator-interface`:** `for` over `I: Iterator<T>`, `borrow mut dyn Iterator<T>` and\n  `Box<dyn Iterator<T>>`; `count(text.split(\",\"))` inferred from the bound; lib/core's `TextSplit` through the\n  interface; destruction order as in §16.4.\n- **check-fail `interface-defaults-errors`:** an ill-typed default reported at the interface (`p1`, N0100 since\n  `b7c9f41`, becomes a type error); a field read in a default (N0234); an override with another signature (N0267);\n  an inherited default that clashes with an inherent method (N0228); a missing constant or associated type (N0267);\n  `Self` outside an impl (N0105/N0200); a type named `Self` (N0100); an ambiguous projection (N0267); a supertrait the\n  target does not implement (N0268); a supertrait cycle (N0227); `dyn I` with a static function, a generic method, a\n  const parameter, a `Self` result, an associated type or a constant (N0331, naming the member).\n- The contract suite and the fixed point, unchanged. HIR snapshots may move with lib/core's new interface; each such\n  change is a reviewed edit.\n- Port side: rerun Bun's `g4_default_method.cee` (expected to pass), and translate `src/watcher/Watcher.rs`'s\n  `WatcherContext` and one `lint` rule as the first users.\n\n## 6. Questions for the owner\n\n1. Default methods as §2.1: checked once at the interface, instantiated per impl that inherits them, in `dyn` tables\n   like any method? Recommended: **yes**. It is the generic-impl machinery reused, and dispatch stays static.\n2. `Self` as §2.2: the reserved keyword given a meaning, the implementing type in interfaces and an alias of the\n   target in impls and type bodies? Recommended: **yes**. Defaults and constructors cannot be written without it, and the alias makes a\n   mechanical port shorter.\n3. Static functions and generic methods (type and const parameters) in interfaces, with §2.3's single N0331 rule for\n   `dyn`, constants included? Recommended: **yes**. 23% of Bun's trait functions are static, and almost no `dyn`\n   trait is affected.\n4. Constants in interfaces (§2.4) and associated types without defaults, fixing bounds or parameters (§2.5), with\n   `P.Name` projections? Recommended: **yes**. GATs (Rule's `State<'a>`) get their own ADR when M1 reaches `lint`.\n5. Supertraits (§2.6), limited to interfaces and `Copy`, with flattened `dyn` tables? Recommended: **yes**.\n6. `Iterator<T>` in lib/core with the item as a parameter, `for` over bounds and `dyn`, and inference from a unique\n   bound (§2.7)? Recommended: **yes**. It extends ADR 0074 §2.8 without changing a structural loop.\n7. No adapter library for now: the port translates chains to loops by §2.7's table, recorded in the port's\n   translation ru",
    411 =>
        "les. Recommended: **yes**. Lazy adapters need closure fields and opaque results first.\n8. Release all of it as one minor release (0.25.0), then the Bun port's pin? Recommended: **yes**. The parts share the\n   checker's impl code, and N0331's amended rule should arrive in one step. 0.25.0 also carries ADR 0084 and step 1\n   of ADR 0085 (after 0.24.3's ADR 0081 and 0082 stage A; ADR 0083 follows in 0.26.0).\n",
    412 =>
        "# ADR 0087 — Macros: none in the language; translation rules, `@derive`, `debugAssert`, `c\"…\"`, `embed`\n\n- Status: **Accepted** by the owner, 2026-11-09 (\"Accept all ADRs as recommended\": every question for the owner answered as recommended), the day it was proposed. Raised by the Bun port's M0 (`~/dev/CeeSafeApps/bun/ROADMAP.md` §3, M0, \"Macro\n  plan\"). It rests on a census of Bun 1.4.4's hand-written Rust made for this ADR (§1.1), on the source of Bun's four\n  proc-macro crates and its codegen scripts, and on probes compiled with ceesafec 0.24.2 (`out/ceesafec` from main)\n  on 2026-11-09.\n- Scope: grammar.md §1.6 (`c\"…\"`); type-system.md §6 (`==` on derived types), §8 (`debugAssert`), §10, §10.1 and\n  §39.4 (constant `+`, `embed`), §16.2 (equality of elements), §24.3 and ADR 0075 §2.4 (derived `HashKey`,\n  `OrderKey`), §28.1 (`@derive`), §32.1 (the write fact of §2.5); tooling.md (`--debug-assertions`, `[embed] roots`);\n  the checker, NIR lowering, the C backend and the interpreter; lib/core (`CStr`, formatting helpers); the corpus and\n  the guide.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nCeeSafe has no macros: `macro` is reserved (grammar.md §1.3), and language-spec.md §6 lists macros as a deliberate\nomission. Bun's Rust calls one every 38 lines.\n\n### 1.1 Method and totals\n\n`macro_census.py` (run with `python3 -I`; location in §5) walks `upstream/src` as `analysis/bun_rust_features.py` does,\nskipping test directories and generated files. A small lexer drops comments and the contents of string and char\nliterals. It then counts `name!(`, `name![` and `name!{`, each `#[derive]` trait, each attribute path and each\n`macro_rules!` name. `shapes.py` classifies arguments on raw text, so its counts are approximate.\n\n| | This census | ROADMAP §1 (raw regex) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Invocations / distinct macros | 36,282 / 657 | 37,247 / 671 |\n| `macro_rules!` definitions (names) | 697 (601) | 707 |\n\nThe roadmap's regex also matched comments. The formatter's IR defines its own `write!` and `format_args!`\n(`src/format/ir/macros.rs:8`, `:22`); 1,315 calls in `src/format` are those. So about **13,040** invocations are of\nBun's own `macro_rules!`, and about **23,240** are of std, third-party crates and Bun's function-like proc macros.\n\n### 1.2 Standard and third-party macros\n\n| Macro | Uses | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `matches!` | 8,125 | 3,761 with `\\|`, 1,667 with an `if` guard (calls counted with balanced parentheses across lines, ADR 0085's `recount.py`; `shapes.py`, by line, gives 8,106 and 1,639) |\n| `format_args!` (std) | ~2,940 | `global.throw(format_args!(\"Expected a connected socket\"))` (`runtime/socket/socket_body.rs:5178`) |\n| `debug_assert!`, `_eq!`, `_ne!` | 2,355 | 60 may have side effects (§1.6) |\n| `assert_eq!`/`_ne!` / `assert!` | 1,520 / 929 | `assert_eq!` mostly in 83 test-heavy files |\n| `vec!` | 1,496 | 384 are `vec![x; n]` |\n| `write!` (std), `writeln!` | ~1,140, 175 | onto `Vec<u8>` and `fmt::Formatter` |\n| `format!` | 807 | 708 use only `{}` or `{name}` |\n| `unreachable!` / `panic!` | 749 / 307 | `todo!`, `unimplemented!`, `dbg!`: none |\n| `cfg!` | 429 | `windows` 164, `debug_assertions` 148; also 5,082 `#[cfg]` attributes |\n| `addr_of_mut!`, `addr_of!` / `offset_of!` | 343 / 157 | |\n| `concat!`, `stringify!`, `concatcp!`, `formatcp!` | 169, 72, 61, 9 | compile-time text |\n| `include_bytes!`, `include_str!` | 99, 17 | 26 files: `root_certs.der` (27,583 bytes), `typescript_libs.bin`, ESLint shims |\n| `smallvec!`, `bitflags!`, `defer!`, `thread_local!` | 167, 86, 81, 56 | |\n| `env!`, `asm!`, `compile_error!`, `line!`/`file!` | 15, 13, 11, 6 | |\n\nFormat specifiers other than `{}`/`{name}`, over all literals: `{:?}` 282, hex 172, `{:p}` 45, `{:.N}` 43, widths and\nalignment about 120, `{:o}` 17.\n\n### 1.3 Derives\n\n16,258 trait names in 5,373 `#[derive]` lists: `Clone` 4,275 (474 without `Copy`), `Copy` 3,802, `PartialEq` 2,082,\n`Eq` 1,977, `Debug` 1,543, `Default` 1,443 (232 `#[default]` variants), `Hash` 288, strum `IntoStaticS",
    413 =>
        "tr` 234,\nthiserror `Error` 134 (955 `#[error]`), `PartialOrd`/`Ord` 69 each, css_derive's six 157, Bun's ref-counting three 68,\n`EnumTag` 13, `JsAffine` 11. Hand-written impls: `Default` 548, `Display` 210, `Clone` 106, `PartialEq` 90, `Debug` 71,\n`Hash` 40. Typical: `#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Hash, Debug)] pub struct Indent(usize);`\n(`src/parsers/yaml.rs:243`). `Debug` is derived 1,543 times but formatted with `{:?}` only 282 times.\n\n### 1.4 Attribute and proc macros\n\nFour crates hold them (`src/jsc_macros`, `css_derive`, `bun_core_macros`, `clap_macros`; 4,067 lines):\n\n- `#[bun_jsc::host_fn]`, with `(method|getter|setter)` or `(export = \"Name\")` (533 uses): it emits an `extern \"C\"`\n  shim (`extern \"sysv64\"` on Windows x64) that turns JSC's raw pointers into references and calls\n  `host_fn_result(__g, || name(__g, __f))` (`src/jsc_macros/lib.rs:81-102`). `#[JsClass]` (38), `#[uws_callback]`\n  (45) and `#[host_call]` (19) do the same for classes, uWS and other ABIs.\n- Bun generates the rest in TypeScript: `src/codegen/generate-host-exports.ts` scrapes `// HOST_EXPORT(Name)` comment\n  markers and writes every thunk into one file (its lines 1-9); 12 of the 27 scripts in `src/codegen` emit Rust.\n- Function-like: `pretty_fmt!` (183), `comptime_string_map!` (102, `src/options_types/bundle_enums.rs:59`), clap's\n  `param!`/`parse_param!` (625).\n\n### 1.5 Bun's own `macro_rules!`, top 20\n\n| Macro | Uses | Defined in | What it does | Treatment (§2.6) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `t!` | 1,201 | `http_types/MimeType.rs:17` | MIME literal to table entry | generate (table) |\n| `write!` (formatter IR) | 1,147 | `format/ir/macros.rs:22` | `Format::fmt(&args, f)` | expand, then port |\n| `scoped_log!` | 857 | `bun_core/output.rs:1499` | debug-only scoped log | library + rule |\n| `pretty_errorln!` | 451 | `bun_core/output.rs:2166` | `<red>…<r>` markup to ANSI, stderr | library + generate |\n| `pretty!` | 396 | `bun_core/output.rs:2118` | the same, stdout | library + generate |\n| `param!` | 394 | `clap/lib.rs:32` | parses a help line at compile time | generate (table) |\n| `no_state!` | 314 | `lint/rule.rs:737` | `impl` lines of a stateless rule | default methods (ADR 0086) |\n| `log!` | 307 | per file | forwards to `scoped_log!` | library + rule |\n| `parse_param!` | 231 | `clap/lib.rs` | as `param!` | generate (table) |\n| `cat!` | 218 | `sema/check/mod.rs:8` | `[&a[..], &b[..]].concat()` | expand, then port |\n| `zstr!` | 217 | `bun_core/util.rs:562` | NUL-terminated literal | `c\"…\"` (§2.4) |\n| `debug!` | 203 | `bun_core/output.rs` | debug-only log | library + rule |\n| `prettyln!` | 201 | `bun_core/output.rs:2129` | `pretty!` and a newline | library + generate |\n| `declare_scope!` | 176 | `bun_core/output.rs:1474` | `pub static X: ScopedLogger` | rule (a `static`) |\n| `format_args!` (formatter IR) | 168 | `format/ir/macros.rs:8` | tuple of `&dyn Format` | expand, then port |\n| `w!` | 128 | `bun_core/string/immutable.rs:1354` | ASCII literal as `&[u16]` | generate (array) |\n| `node_entry!` | 122 | `resolve_builtins/HardcodedModule.rs:353` | builtin-module table row | generate (table) |\n| `of!` | 116 | `lint/estree/schema.rs:160` | `match` with early `return None` | expand, then port |\n| `mark_binding!` | 114 | `bun_core/Global.rs:426` | debug trace of the caller's file | library + rule |\n| `pretty_fmt_const!` | 113 | `runtime/test_runner/pretty_format.rs` | markup to a constant | generate |\n\nBy purpose, over all 601 names: colour markup about 1,940 uses, logging about 1,700, tables and generated items about\n2,400 (also `opaque_ffi!` 105, `env_var::new!` 109, `property_helper!` 100), literals 345, `impl` boilerplate about\n600, and local syntax sugar (`cat!`, `of!`, `col_ref!`, `bail!`, …) most of the rest. 17 are never invoked.\n\n### 1.6 `debug_assert!` and side effects\n\nRelease builds drop a `debug_assert!` with its arguments, so an argument with an effect changes behaviour between\nbuilds; this caused one of Bun's regressions after its",
    414 =>
        " rewrite (ROADMAP §3, M0). 60 of the 2,355 have arguments that\nmay write (assignment, `&mut`, `next`, `pop`, `try_lock`, `fetch_*`, `unsafe`). For example:\n\n- `debug_assert!(unsafe { (*first).debug_mutex.try_lock() });` (`runtime/bake/dev_server/hot_reload_event.rs:294`,\n  and three more sites) takes a lock that release builds never take;\n- `debug_assert!(events.next().is_none());` (`install/PackageInstaller.rs:1478`; also `install/yarn.rs:573`)\n  advances an iterator;\n- `debug_assert_eq!(unsafe { us_nq_vtable_size() }, …)` (`lsquic_sys/lib.rs`) calls C.\n\n### 1.7 CeeSafe today (probes, ceesafec 0.24.2)\n\n| Probe | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `a == b` on a struct; `@derive(Eq)`; `let macro = 1;` | N0207; N0290 (unknown attribute); N0100 (reserved word) |\n| `match (t) { Tok.Plus => true, Tok.Minus => true, _ => false }`; `Tok.Num(v) => v > 2` | runs: `true`, `true` |\n| `assert(bump(mut n), \"…\")` | allowed; the write happens in every build (§8: \"assertions are always checked\") |\n| `debugAssert(…)`; `embed(\"f\")` | N0200 (unknown function) |\n| `strlen(\"abc\\0\".ptr as *CChar)` | 3, interpreted and native; `\"hello\\0\".length` is 6 |\n| `const MSG: StringView = PREFIX + \"error\";` | N0207 |\n| `if (cfg(\"BUN_DEBUG\")) {…} else {…}` in a hosted `main` | prints `off`; with `--cflag -DBUN_DEBUG=1`, `on` |\n\nPrior decisions: ADR 0072 gave `==` to integers, `bool`, fieldless enums, slices and arrays, and deferred \"an equality\ninterface\" (§3, §4). `HashKey<K>` is ADR 0052, `OrderKey<K>` ADR 0075 §2.4, configured code ADR 0080 §3.3, `@test` ADR\n0049. `embed(\"file\")` was deferred twice (ADR 0072 §4; ADR 0074 §2.6 and §4) because checking would need file access in\nthe LSP and MCP. This set's ADR 0085 proposes `e is P`, `|`, ranges and guards; ADR 0086 proposes default methods and\n`Self`; ADR 0084 proposes `Cell` and `RefCell`.\n\n## 2. Proposal\n\n### 2.1 No general macros\n\nCeeSafe does not add macros, neither pattern macros nor procedural ones, and `macro` stays reserved. Why:\n\n- **A reader sees what runs.** Bun's `pretty!` dispatches through `__pretty_dispatch!` with a pool of 32 identifiers\n  (`bun_core/output.rs:2103-2116`). Port reviewers compare CeeSafe with Rust line by line, which works for expanded\n  code and fails for a second language inside the first.\n- **Diagnostics and tools stay exact.** Everything the checker reports, `fmt` formats and the LSP resolves is in the\n  file.\n- **The census doesn't need them.** About 20,000 invocations are standard forms with a fixed CeeSafe spelling (§2.2).\n  Most of Bun's 13,000 are libraries, tables or boilerplate that the port generates (§2.6). The rest needs five fixed\n  features, each specified once (§2.3–§2.5, §2.7, §2.8).\n- **Generation stays in the open.** The compiler already does it this way: `tools/gen_*.py` write sources that say\n  they are generated (AGENTS.md). Bun does too, with 12 codegen scripts.\n\n### 2.2 Translation rules (the porting process; no compiler change)\n\nThese go into the port's `PORTING.md` (ROADMAP M1) and the reviewers' checklist.\n\n| Rust | CeeSafe |\n|---|---|\n| `matches!(e, P)`; `matches!(e, P if g)` | from 0.26.0, `e is P`; `e is P && g` (ADR 0085 §2.8). In 0.25.0, `match (e) { P => true, _ => false }` with 0085's `\\|` and ranges, and `P if (g) => true` for a guard. The 1,639 guarded calls (§1.2) need 0085's guards; before 0.25.0, one arm per alternative and the arm `P(x) => g` |\n| `format!`, `write!`, `writeln!` | appends: `var s = String.create(); s.append(\"a \")?; s.appendNumber(x)?;` (`VectorBytes` for bytes); `writeln!` appends `\"\\n\"` |\n| `format_args!` passed to a function | the callee takes a `StringView` built by the caller in a local `String`; on a log path, inside the gate (§2.6) |\n| `{:x}`, `{:08x}`, `{:.3}`, `{:>8}`; `{:?}` | §2.9's helpers; the generated `appendDebug` (§2.3) |\n| `print!`, `println!`, `eprintln!` (43) | `Console.write…` |\n| `vec![a, b]` / `vec![x; n]` | `Vector.withCapacity(2)?` and pushes / `withCapacity(n)?` then `resize(n,",
    415 =>
        " x)?` (§23.1) |\n| `assert!(c, \"m\")`; with format arguments | `assert(c, \"m\")`; `if (!c) { var m = …; panic(m.view()); }` |\n| `assert_eq!(a, b)` | in a `@test`, lib/std's `Check` (ADR 0049); elsewhere `assert(a == b, \"a == b\")` |\n| `debug_assert!(c)` | `debugAssert(c)` (§2.5) |\n| `unreachable!()`, `panic!(\"m\")` | `panic(\"unreachable\")`, `panic(\"m\")` |\n| `concat!`, `stringify!` | one literal, folded by the porting tool; with constants, `+` (§2.7) |\n| `cfg!(x)`; `#[cfg(debug_assertions)]` code | `if (cfg(\"X\")) { … }` (ADR 0080 §3.3); `debug_assertions` is `BUN_DEBUG`, defined by Bun's build |\n| `offset_of!`; `addr_of!`, `addr_of_mut!` | `offsetOf<T>(f)` (§30.1); `borrow x as *T`, `borrow mut x as *mut T` (§27.2) |\n| `defer!`; `include_bytes!`, `include_str!` | `defer` (§14.7); `embed` (§2.8) |\n| `env!(\"X\")`; `line!`, `file!` (6) | a constant in a `.cee` file Bun's build generates; written out |\n| `quote!`, `compile_error!` | not ported (proc-macro crates and `#[cfg]` guards) |\n\nA Rust type that is not `Copy` keeps its move semantics. The port adds `@noncopy` when every field is Copy and the\ntype has no `drop` block; otherwise §14.1 would make it Copy. `Clone` beside `Copy` (3,806) needs nothing.\n\n### 2.3 `@derive(Equal, HashKey, OrderKey)`\n\nThese three are in the compiler because a hand-written or script-written version drifts silently: add a field and it\nstill compiles, but compares wrongly. A stale `Clone` or `Default` fails to compile, since a struct literal names every\nfield (§12), and a stale `Debug` is harmless. So those three stay generated source (table below).\n\n`@derive(…)` on a struct or enum takes one or more of these names, and no others (N0290):\n\n- **`Equal`** gives the type `==` and `!=`. The fields are compared in declaration order, joined by `&&`. An enum\n  compares the variant, then its payloads. Each field's type must have `==`:\n  - ADR 0072's types (integers, `bool`, fieldless enums, slices and arrays of them);\n  - `StringView`, `String`, `Vector<u8>`;\n  - another `Equal` type, and slices, arrays and vectors of one (this extends ADR 0072 §2.4);\n  - `T?` of these (two `null`s are equal), `Box<T>` and `borrow T` (the referent), raw pointers (the address);\n  - floats, compared as IEEE values (**Question 2**);\n  - ADR 0084's `Cell<T>`, compared by `get()`, if 0084 gives `Cell<T: Copy>` `==` (Rust derives `PartialEq` through\n    `Cell`); otherwise a `Cell` field is N0207 like any field without `==`. `RefCell` and `RawCell` fields are N0207.\n\n  A field without `==` is N0207 at the attribute, naming the field. A generic type has `==` for the instances whose\n  arguments have it, checked at the use; in generic code over an opaque `T` it is N0207. User code still cannot\n  define `==` (language-spec.md §6: no operator overloading). Bun's 90 hand-written `PartialEq` impls become named\n  methods, and `Eq` is dropped.\n- **`HashKey`** writes `impl HashKey<S> for S` (§24.3), and needs `Equal` (N0290 otherwise). `hash` feeds each field in\n  order: through its `HashKey`, or a fieldless enum's variant index as a `u32`. An enum's index comes before its\n  payloads. `sameAs` is `==`. A field type with neither is N0268.\n- **`OrderKey`** writes `impl OrderKey<S> for S`: `compareKey` compares the fields lexicographically, in declaration\n  order. For enums it compares the variant index first. ADR 0072 §2.3 refused declaration order as a default\n  ordering; here the author asks for it. There is still no `<`.\n\nEach impl is ordinary checked code: `--dump-hir` shows it, and the guide prints the expansion for a two-field struct.\n\nGenerated source for the other derives, from the port's generator:\n\n| Rust | Generated CeeSafe |\n|---|---|\n| `Clone` without `Copy` (474) | `public fn clone(borrow this) -> Result<S, AllocError>` |\n| `Default` (1,443) | `public static fn default() -> S`; with ADR 0086, an interface may replace it later |\n| `Debug` (1,543) | `appendDebug(bor