    464 =>
        "# Formatting (M10 T1)\n\n`ceesafec fmt` rewrites CeeSafe source into one canonical style. It has no options: two programmers who run it get the same\nspacing, indentation, blank lines, brace placement and comment alignment. Where the style leaves a choice to the author\n(§3), it is a choice of line breaks, which the formatter keeps.\n\nThe formatter changes only whitespace, and the trailing commas of §3.3. A formatted file parses to the same tree as\nthe original (the same AST, apart from spans) and keeps every comment, in order. Formatting a formatted file changes\nnothing.\n\n## 1. Command\n\n```sh\nceesafec fmt file.cee...          # rewrite each file in place (only if it changes)\nceesafec fmt --check file.cee...  # change nothing; list the files that would change, exit 1 if any\nceesafec fmt --stdout file.cee    # print the formatted text of one file\n```\n\nA file with a syntax error is not formatted: its diagnostics are printed, the file is left as it is, and the exit\nstatus is 1. A directory argument means every `.cee` file under it.\n\n## 2. Lines and indentation\n\n- The text is UTF-8 with LF line endings; it ends with exactly one newline. No line has trailing whitespace.\n- Indentation is 4 spaces per level; there are no tabs.\n- A level is opened by a `{` that ends its line (a block, a struct, enum, interface or impl body, a match, a\n  multi-line struct or array literal) and by a broken list (§3.3). Its contents are indented one level, and its\n  closing delimiter is on its own line at the opening line's indentation.\n- **Blank lines:** at most one in a row. None directly after an opening `{` or before a closing `}`, and none at the\n  start or end of the file. Elsewhere, between two items, two members or two statements, a blank line is kept if\n  the source has one or more, and not added if it has none.\n- **Line width:** 120 columns, counting the code and not a trailing comment. A line longer than that is broken by §3.4\n  where it can be. A line that cannot be broken (a long string literal, a long name) stays long.\n- **Raw literals** (`grammar.md` §1.6, ADR 0074 §2.6): the content lines and the closing line of a `\"\"\"` literal move\n  with the line its opening `\"\"\"` ends, by the change in that line's indentation, and are never reflowed or trimmed:\n  their trailing spaces are part of the value. Fewer columns are removed than the change when the closing line's\n  indentation is narrower, since removing more would change the value. Their width does not count.\n\n## 3. Line breaks\n\n### 3.1 What the author decides\n\nThe formatter keeps these choices and only lays out the result:\n- whether a block, struct, enum, interface or impl body, or match is written on one line (`{ return x; }`) or across\n  lines (anywhere between its braces);\n- whether a list (arguments, parameters, struct fields, array elements, attribute arguments) is broken, and, in a\n  broken list, which elements start a new line (so a literal can keep several fields per line);\n- whether an expression body (`=> expr`) starts on the line of the `=>` or on the next one;\n- where a long binary expression or a chain of member calls (a `.` after a call, an index, `!` or `?`) is broken,\n  and which `else`s of an `if` expression (`if (c) a else b`) start a new line;\n- whether an attribute is followed by a line break.\n\nAny other line break in the source is removed: two tokens on different lines that no rule here separates are joined\nwith their §4 spacing. In particular `else` always follows the `}` before it on the same line; only an `if` expression whose value is not a\nblock may start an `else` on a new line (§3.4).\n\nA one-line form is kept only if it fits in 120 columns and holds no line comment; otherwise it is broken, as if the\nauthor had written it across lines.\n\n### 3.2 One-line forms\n\n- A one-line block has one space inside each brace: `{ x += 1; }`, `{ break; }`. An empty block is `{}`.\n- A one-line struct body is `struct Point { x: i32; y: i32; }`; a one-line enum is `enum E { A, B(i3",
    465 =>
        "2) }`.\n- A one-line match is `match (v) { 0 => \"zero\", _ => \"many\" }`, and as a statement\n  `match (o) { Some(v) => { use(v); } null => {} }`.\n- A one-line struct literal is `Point { x: 1, y: 2 }`; an array literal is `[1, 2, 3]`; argument and parameter lists\n  are `f(a, b)` and `fn f(a: i32, b: i32)`. One-line lists have no trailing comma.\n- **An if/else chain is laid out as a whole:** if one of its blocks is broken, or the author started an `else` on a new\n  line, every block of the chain is broken.\n\n```ceesafe\nif (c) { a(); } else { b(); }      // fits: kept on one line\nif (c) {                           // otherwise: every block broken\n    a();\n} else {\n    b();\n}\n```\n\n### 3.3 Broken lists\n\nStatements, members, enum variants and match arms of a broken body are one per line. A list that is broken (by its\nauthor, or by §3.4) is laid out as a block:\n- the opening delimiter ends its line;\n- the elements are indented one level; each element that the author started on a new line starts a new line, and\n  the others follow the element before them after `, `; a list broken by §3.4 has one element per line;\n- the closing delimiter starts its own line at the opening line's indentation;\n- the last element is followed by a comma, where the grammar allows one (grammar.md §2: parameters, arguments, struct\n  field initialisers, array elements, attribute arguments, enum variants, and the arms of a match expression, except\n  an arm whose value is a block). The commas between the arms of a match statement are left as written. Type\n  parameters, generic arguments, closure parameters, function-type parameters and interface method parameters are\n  never broken.\n\n```ceesafe\nlet bodies = [\n    Body { x: 0.0, y: 0.0, z: 0.0, vx: 0.0, vy: 0.0, vz: 0.0, mass: SOLAR_MASS },\n    Body {\n        x: 4.84143144246472090e+00, y: -1.16032004402742839e+00, z: -1.03622044471123109e-01,\n        vx: 1.66007664274403694e-03 * DAYS_PER_YEAR, vy: 7.69901118419740425e-03 * DAYS_PER_YEAR,\n    },\n];\n```\n\n### 3.4 Breaking a line that is too long\n\nWhen a line does not fit, the formatter breaks one construct that starts on it and lays the file out again, until\nthe line fits or nothing on it is left to break. It takes the first kind in this order, and of that kind the\noutermost:\n1. a function's expression body (`=> e`): the expression moves to the next line, indented one level;\n2. a list or a one-line block or body (§3.3);\n3. the value of a match arm or a closure (`=> e`), moved to the next line;\n4. an initialiser or an assigned value (`= e`), moved to the next line;\n5. an `if` expression whose then-value is not a block: before each `else` of its chain at once, every `else` one\n   level in (an `else if` chain is indented once, not once per `if`);\n6. a binary expression: after its operator, with the right operand indented one level;\n7. a chain of member calls: before a `.`, indented one level.\n\n```ceesafe\n@header(\n    \"kernel/blk.h\", \"kernel/errno.h\", \"kernel/gpt.h\", \"kernel/kmalloc.h\", \"kernel/page.h\", \"kernel/part.h\",\n    \"kernel/ramblk.h\",\n)\nlet which = if (kind == VNODE_DIR) OPS_DIR\n    else if (kind == VNODE_LNK) OPS_LNK\n    else OPS_FILE;\n```\n\nThe author's breaks of the same kinds (§3.1) are laid out by the same rules.\n\n## 4. Spaces\n\n- One space around binary operators (`a + b`, `x == y`, `a && b`), around `=` and the compound assignments, around\n  `->` and `=>`, after `,`, `;` (inside a one-line form) and `:` (`x: i32`, `x: 1`). In patterns (ADR 0085), one space\n  around the `|` of an or-pattern (`' ' | '\\t'`) and the `@` of `name @ P` or `rest @ ..`, none around `..=`\n  (`'a'..='z'`), and a guard is written `P if (g) =>`. A slice pattern is written like an array literal, its `..` an\n  item: `[first, .., last]`, `['\"', inner @ .., '\"']`.\n- No space around `..` (`0..n`, `a[i..j]`, `a[..]`), `.`, inside parentheses, brackets and generic arguments\n  (`f(a)`, `[1, 2]`, `Vector<i32>`), before `(` in a call or declaration, after a unary operator (`-x`, `!b`,\n  `*p`)",
    466 =>
        ", before `?` and `!` (`f()?`, `r!`), or before `,` and `;`.\n- Keywords: one space after `if`, `while`, `for`, `match`, `return` (with a value), `fn` (in a declaration), `let`,\n  `var`, `borrow`, `mut`, `move`, `as` and the visibility keywords; `if (c) {`, `} else {`, `} else if (d) {`.\n- One space before an opening `{` that follows code on its line.\n- Attributes are followed by one space or a line break, as written: `@context(atomic) fn f()`.\n\n## 5. Comments\n\n- Comments keep their text and order. A comment on a line of its own is indented like the code that follows it (or,\n  at the end of a block, like the code before the `}`).\n- A comment after code on the same line (a trailing comment) follows it after one space. Trailing comments on\n  consecutive lines are aligned: each starts one space after the longest code among those lines. A line without a\n  trailing comment, or a blank line, ends the group.\n- Doc comments (`///`) stay directly before their item; a blank line between a doc comment and its item is removed,\n  since it would detach the comment (grammar.md §1.2).\n- A block comment (`/* … */`) inside a line keeps one space on each side.\n\n## 6. Guarantees and their tests\n\n- **Same tree:** for every file of the repository that parses (the corpus included), the formatted text parses\n  without error to an AST equal to the original's, spans aside.\n- **Comments:** the comments of the formatted text are those of the original, in order.\n- **Same tokens:** apart from commas, the tokens are the original's; a raw literal is compared by its value, which its\n  re-indentation keeps.\n- **Fixed point:** formatting the formatted text changes nothing. `tools/fmt_snapshots.py` checks the reviewed\n  snapshots in `tests/formatting/` (`NAME.cee` formats to `NAME.formatted.cee`, which formats to itself).\n- **The repository's own code** (`lib/`, `programs/`, `benchmarks/`, `integrations/`, the README's examples) is kept\n  formatted: `ceesafec fmt --check` runs over it in the test suite. The test corpus is not reformatted, since its\n  annotations name lines and some files test unusual layout; it is the formatter's test input.\n",
    467 =>
        "# CeeSafe Grammar — M1 subset\n\nStatus: normative for Milestone 1 (increment 1a implements the lexer and parser for exactly\nthis grammar). Later milestones extend it. Any syntax not listed here is rejected\nby the parser, even if `language-spec.md` shows it for a later milestone.\n\nNotation: EBNF. `'x'` is a literal token, `X?` optional, `X*` zero or more, `X+` one or more.\n\n## 1. Lexical grammar\n\n### 1.1 Source text\n- UTF-8. A leading BOM is ignored. Line terminators: `\\n`, `\\r\\n`, `\\r`.\n- Columns in diagnostics are 1-based and count UTF-16 code units. Lines are 1-based.\n- Non-ASCII characters may appear only inside comments, string literals and char\n  literals.\n\n### 1.2 Whitespace and comments\n- Whitespace: space, tab, line terminators, form feed.\n- `// ...` to end of line. `/* ... */` block comments do **not** nest. An unterminated block\n  comment is error N0003.\n- Comments and whitespace are discarded by the parser, except doc comments (M8a). The lexer records every comment's span\n  for the formatter (M10 T1, formatting.md).\n- **Doc comments** (M8a): a line comment that starts with exactly `///` (not `////`). A run of doc\n  comment lines, with only whitespace between them, is attached to the declaration whose first token\n  follows the run directly. Its text is each line after `///` with leading spaces removed. Doc\n  comments elsewhere are ordinary comments. Used by the `Safety:` rule (type-system.md §27.4).\n\n### 1.3 Identifiers and keywords\n```\nIdentifier = IdentStart IdentContinue*      (not a keyword)\nIdentStart = 'A'..'Z' | 'a'..'z' | '_'\nIdentContinue = IdentStart | '0'..'9'\n```\nA lone `_` is the token `Underscore` (reserved; no meaning in M1).\n\nKeywords (all reserved, whether or not M1 uses them):\n```\nas borrow break const continue defer drop else enum extern false fn for if impl\nimport in interface internal let loop match move mut namespace null own private\npublic return Self static struct this true type unsafe using var where while\nclass async await yield macro\n```\nPrimitive type names (`i32`, `u64`, `bool`, `void`, …) are **identifiers**,\nresolved by name resolution, not keywords.\n\n`is` (ADR 0085 §2.8) is **contextual**: it is the operator of `e is P` (§2.1) only where a complete operand is followed\nby the identifier `is`; everywhere else it is an identifier, so `c.types.is(t, k)` and a local named `is` keep working.\n\n`Self` (ADR 0086 §2.2) names a type: in an interface, the implementing type; in an `impl` block and in a struct's or\nenum's body, the target type with its parameters. It is written where a type name is, as the first part of a\n`QualifiedName` in a type (`Self`, `Self.Item`, also as a type argument: `Vector<Self>.create()`), as a primary\nexpression (`Self { … }`, `Self.create(…)`, `Self.MAX`) and as the first part of a pattern's qualified name\n(`Self.Idle`, `Self.Busy(n)`, `Self.TAB`). Declaring anything named `Self` is N0100, as for every keyword.\n\n### 1.4 Integer literals\n```\nIntLiteral = (Decimal | '0x' HexDigits | '0b' BinDigits | '0o' OctDigits) IntSuffix?\nIntSuffix  = 'i8'|'i16'|'i32'|'i64'|'isize'|'u8'|'u16'|'u32'|'u64'|'usize'\n```\n- `_` separators are allowed only *between* digits (`1_000`, `0xFF_FF`). A separator\n  at the start or end of the digit sequence, or doubled, is error N0010.\n- The value must fit in 64 bits (unsigned), otherwise N0004. Whether it fits the\n  *type* is checked later (type checking).\n- A digit invalid for the radix is N0008. A missing digit after a prefix is N0008. An unknown suffix is N0009.\n- Decimal literals have no leading-zero restriction, and there is no octal-by-leading-zero:\n  `010` is ten.\n\n### 1.5 Float literals (typed since M11 G7: type-system.md §35.2)\n```\nFloatLiteral = Decimal '.' Decimal Exponent? FloatSuffix? | Decimal Exponent FloatSuffix?\nExponent = ('e'|'E') ('+'|'-')? Decimal\nFloatSuffix = 'f32' | 'f64'\n```\n`1.` and `.5` are not float literals. `1..2` lexes as `1` `..` `2`.\n\n### 1.6 String and char literals\n- `\"...\"` on one line. Escapes: `\\n",
    468 =>
        " \\r \\t \\\\ \\\" \\' \\0 \\{ \\} \\u{H..H}` (1–6 hex digits,\n  a Unicode scalar value). `\\{` and `\\}` are the braces themselves; `\\{` marks braces that are meant as text, so\n  that N0400 does not warn (ADR 0073 §2.4). Other escapes are N0006. A newline or EOF before the closing quote is N0005.\n- `\"\"\"` begins a raw literal, which may span lines (ADR 0074 §2.6):\n\n  ```ceesafe\n  let query = \"\"\"\n      SELECT id, name\n      FROM users\n      \"\"\";\n  ```\n\n  - The opening `\"\"\"` ends its line: anything but spaces and tabs after it is N0011.\n  - The content is the lines that follow, up to the first line holding only spaces or tabs and then `\"\"\"`. That\n    closing line's `\"\"\"` ends the literal; tokens may follow it on the line. A content line may hold `\"\"\"` among\n    other text.\n  - The closing line's indentation is removed from every content line. A content line that is not blank and does not\n    start with that indentation is N0011. A blank line (only spaces and tabs) is empty.\n  - Content lines are joined by `\\n`, also in a CRLF file. There is no final newline: the value above is\n    `SELECT id, name\\nFROM users`.\n  - There are no escapes: `\\` is a backslash, and `{` is a brace (N0400 does not apply).\n  - EOF before the closing line is N0005.\n  - The token is a string literal, the same as `\"...\"` after the lexer.\n- `c\"...\"` is a C string (ADR 0087 §2.4): a `\"...\"` literal with the same escapes, whose value is a lib/core\n  `CStr<static>` (`type-system.md` §22.3). The compiler appends the NUL byte that ends it, so a `\\0` in it is N0006.\n  `c` must touch the `\"`: `c \"x\"` is the name `c` followed by a literal.\n- **Literal prefixes.** A single letter immediately before `\"`, not part of a longer identifier, is a reserved literal\n  prefix: N0100 for any but `c`, and for `c` before a raw literal (`c\"\"\"`). `b\"…\"` is not defined: string patterns\n  are typed by the scrutinee (ADR 0085 §2.7).\n- `'c'`: exactly one Unicode scalar value or one escape. Otherwise N0007. Its value is the scalar value, as an\n  integer literal (`type-system.md` §4, M11); it may also be a pattern.\n\n### 1.7 Punctuation and operators\n```\n( ) { } [ ] , ; : . .. ..= @ ? ?? -> =>\n+ - * / % ! & | ^ ~ && || << \n= == != < <= > >=\n+= -= *= /= %= &= |= ^= <<=\n```\nThe lexer **never** produces `>>` or `>>=`. It emits single `>` (and `>=`) tokens.\nThe parser forms the shift operator `>>` from two *adjacent* `>` tokens, and `>>=` from `>`\nimmediately followed by `>=`. That keeps nested generic arguments\n(`A<B<C>>`) unambiguous in later milestones.\n\n`..=` is one token (ADR 0085 §2.4): the inclusive range of a pattern, `'a'..='z'`, and only that. In an expression it\nis N0100 with the hint that ranges there are half-open (`0..10`).\n\n`??` is one token (ADR 0072): the fallback operator in an expression (§2.1), and two `?` in a type (`T??`, an\noptional of an optional). The expression `x??`, `?` applied twice, is written `(x?)?`: a `??` that no operand follows\nis N0100 with that hint.\n\nAny other character is N0001 (unexpected character).\n\n## 2. Syntactic grammar (M1)\n\n```\nCompilationUnit = NamespaceDecl? ImportDecl* Item* EOF\nNamespaceDecl   = 'namespace' QualifiedName ';'     (* retired by ADR 0013: accepted, no effect *)\nImportDecl      = 'import' Identifier ('.' Identifier)? ';'   (* M10 T5: a dependency, or one of its items; type-system.md §2 *)\nQualifiedName   = Identifier ('.' Identifier)*\n\nItem       = Attribute* Visibility? (FunctionDecl | ConstDecl | StaticDecl | StructDecl | EnumDecl | ExternBlock)\nAttribute  = '@' Identifier ('(' AttributeArg (',' AttributeArg)* ')')?   (* M8b; type-system.md §28.1 *)\nAttributeArg = Identifier | StringLiteral | IntLiteral\nExternBlock = 'extern' StringLiteral '{' (ExternFn | ExternStruct | ExternUnion | ExternEnum | ExternConst | ExternStatic)* '}'  (* M8b; §28.2 *)\nExternStruct = Attribute* Visibility? 'struct' Identifier ('{' (ExternField | AnonymousUnion)* '}' | ';')   (* C6b; type-system.md §31 *)\nExternUnion = Attribute* Visibility? 'unio",
    469 =>
        "n' Identifier ('{' ExternField+ '}' | ';')   (* ADR 0041; type-system.md §31.1 *)\nExternEnum = Attribute* Visibility? 'enum' Identifier ':' Type ';'   (* ADR 0079; type-system.md §31.6 *)\nExternConst = Attribute* Visibility? 'const' Identifier ':' Type ('=' Expr)? ';'   (* ADR 0080; type-system.md §28.2 *)\nExternStatic = Attribute* Visibility? 'static' 'mut'? Identifier ':' Type ';'   (* ADR 0080; type-system.md §39.6 *)\nAnonymousUnion = 'union' '{' ExternField+ '}'                               (* ADR 0041: a C11 anonymous union member *)\nExternField = Attribute* FieldDecl                                           (* C9: @cname; type-system.md §31.1 *)\nExternFn   = Attribute* Visibility? 'fn' Identifier '(' (Param (',' Param)* (',' '...')? ','?)? ')' ('->' Type)? ';'   (* `...`: ADR 0041 *)\nTypeParams = '<' TypeParam (',' TypeParam)* '>'               (* M6a; type-system.md §20 *)\nInterfaceDecl = 'interface' Identifier TypeParams? (':' Type ('+' Type)*)? '{' (MethodSig | InterfaceConst | AssocTypeDecl)* '}'\n                                            (* M6b; §21; supertraits: ADR 0086 §2.6 *)\nInterfaceConst = 'const' Identifier ':' Type ('=' Expr)? ';'   (* ADR 0086 §2.4: the value is a default *)\nAssocTypeDecl = 'type' Identifier (':' Type ('+' Type)*)? ';'   (* ADR 0086 §2.5 *)\nMethodSig  = Attribute* 'fn' Identifier TypeParams? '(' ParamList? ')' ('->' Type)? (';' | FunctionBody)\n                                            (* attributes: M13 G2, `@effects`, §37.3; a FunctionBody is a default, ADR 0086 §2.1 *)\nImplDecl   = 'impl' Type 'for' Type '{' (Attribute* Visibility? FunctionDecl | ConstDecl | AssocTypeDef)* '}'\n                                            (* M6b; §21; attributes C7a; ConstDecl and AssocTypeDef: ADR 0086 §2.4, §2.5 *)\nAssocTypeDef = 'type' Identifier '=' Type ';'\n           | 'impl' Type '{' (Attribute* Visibility? FunctionDecl | Visibility? ConstDecl)* '}'  (* inherent: ADR 0064 §1; ConstDecl ADR 0074 §2.7 *)\n           | 'impl' TypeParams? Type '{' (Attribute* Visibility? FunctionDecl)* '}'   (* inherent: ADR 0064 §1; §21.1 *)\nMemberCall = Expr '.' Identifier ('<' GenericArg (',' GenericArg)* '>')? '(' Args? ')'   (* type arguments: ADR 0064 §1 *)\nTypeParam  = 'region' Identifier                               (* M6x: a region, before any type parameter; §25.1 *)\n           | 'const' Identifier ':' Type                       (* ADR 0083: a const parameter; type-system.md §20.5 *)\n           | Identifier (':' Type ('+' Type)*)?                (* bounds: M6b *)\n(* `region` is contextual: only `region` followed by a name declares a region. In a type, a region\n   argument is a region name or `static`, written first in the argument list (`Token<src>`,\n   `StringView<r>`, `Slice<r, T>`) or in `borrow(r) T`; type-system.md §25.2. *)\nEnumDecl   = 'enum' Identifier TypeParams? '{' (Variant (',' Variant)* ','?)? '}'        (* M2d *)\nVariant    = ('@' 'from')? Identifier ('(' Type (',' Type)* ','? ')')?   (* `@from`: ADR 0075 §2.3 *)\nVisibility = 'public' | 'internal' | 'private'\n\nFunctionDecl = 'unsafe'? 'async'? 'fn' Identifier TypeParams? '(' ParamList? ')' ('->' Type)? FunctionBody\n                                            (* 'unsafe': M8a; 'async': L1, type-system.md §40, ADR 0031 *)\nParamList    = (Receiver | Param) (',' Param)* ','?\nReceiver     = ('borrow' 'mut'?)? 'this'   (* M2a: by value; M2b: reference receivers *)\nParam        = Identifier ':' Type\n\nStructDecl   = 'struct' Identifier TypeParams? '{' Member* '}'          (* M2a; TypeParams M6a *)\nMember       = Visibility? FieldDecl | Attribute* Visibility? FunctionDecl | DropBlock   (* method attributes: C7a, type-system.md §32 *)\n             | Visibility? ConstDecl                                (* ADR 0074 §2.7: an associated constant, type-system.md §10 *)\nDropBlock    = 'drop' Block                (* M2c *)\nFieldDecl    = Identifier ':' Type ';'\nFunctionBody = Block | '=>' Expr ';'\n\nConstDecl = 'const' Identifier '",
