    476 =>
        "re\n  unreachable and are not lowered. Blocks with no predecessors are not emitted.\n- Constants are already folded in HIR. NIR performs no optimisation in M1.\n\n- References (M2b):\n  - an explicit or implicit borrow becomes `t = Ref(k, place)`;\n  - auto-deref reads `(*r)`;\n  - a use of a `borrow mut` variable becomes `Ref(Mut, (*r))` (reborrow);\n  - a call-site borrow of a value spills it into a temporary, which gets `StorageDead` at the end of\n    the statement;\n  - for a `borrow mut this` receiver, the receiver's `Ref` is emitted after the other arguments (B5);\n  - every statement carries a source span for borrow diagnostics.\n- The **borrow checker** (`src/nir/borrow.cee`, `lifetimes.md`) runs on NIR inside\n  `Compilation.Create`, so `ceesafec check` reports borrow errors too.\n- **Moves and destruction (M2c).**\n  - Scopes hold locals *and* deferred statements. Leaving a scope (its end, `break`/`continue`,\n    or `return`) emits, in reverse order of position, `StorageDead` for locals and a fresh\n    lowering of each deferred statement.\n  - By-value parameters form the outermost scope.\n  - `return e` computes `e` into an unscoped temporary first, then ends the statement's\n    temporaries and every scope, then returns.\n  - **Move paths** (`src/nir/move_paths.cee`): each local is a tree, expanded field by field\n    wherever a field is assigned or moved; all siblings are expanded, so children partition their\n    parent. A forward dataflow over the leaves tracks maybe-init, maybe-uninit and maybe-moved.\n    `MoveChecker` uses it for N0212/N0250–N0253.\n  - **Drop elaboration** (`src/nir/drops.cee`) runs only when every check passed:\n    - it inserts `Drop` before `StorageDead` of a local that needs drop, and before an assignment\n      that overwrites one; the assignment becomes `t = rvalue; Drop(p); p = t`;\n    - what is dropped depends on the state: whole, nothing, a flagged drop, or per field for a\n      partially moved struct;\n    - one bool flag per leaf is updated after every statement that changes that leaf's state.\n    - In a run-once function (an `async move` child, type-system.md §36.5, ADR 0074 §2.3), `*env` (local 0's\n      referent) is a move path, tracked from the entry as a box's contents are, but without an allocation leaf. At\n      `StorageDead(env)` the captures still initialised are dropped, whole, flagged or field by field; the backends\n      no longer drop the child's environment after the body, only that of a child that never started.\n\n    The C backend emits drop glue `ceeD_<len><Name>(T *)`: the `drop` block, then each field that\n    needs drop, in declaration order.\n\n- **Arrays, slices and `for` (M2d).**\n  - A slice type is a reference to the unsized `[T]`; `s[i]` is the place `(*s)[i]`.\n  - An index is evaluated into a `usize` temporary (or kept as a constant), then checked with\n    `BoundsCheck` against the array's constant length or the slice's `Length`, then used as an\n    `Index` projection. Element places are never move paths: an element's init state is its\n    array's.\n  - An assignment evaluates its target's indexes (and checks them) before the value.\n  - `a[lo..hi]` evaluates the base place, then the bounds left to right, then `RangeCheck` (none\n    for `a[..]`), then `Slice`.\n  - **Bulk operations (M9b).** `d.copyFrom(s)` lowers the receiver slice, then the source slice, each into a\n    temporary, then `LengthCheck(Length(s), Length(d))`, then the built-in `SliceCopy(d, s)`, which copies\n    with `memmove` semantics. `d.fill(v)` is the built-in `SliceFill(d, v)`. Neither is two-phase.\n    `d.swap(i, j)` (M11 G8c) lowers the slice and both indices, then `BoundsCheck(i, Length(d))` and\n    `BoundsCheck(j, Length(d))`, then the built-in `SliceSwap(d, i, j)`. `v.retain(keep)` lowers the vector\n    reference and the closure, then a temporary `SliceMut` reborrowed from the vector's elements, a call of\n    lib/core's `SliceAlgorithms<T>.retain` on it and the closure, and `VectorTruncate(v, kept)` (type-",
    477 =>
        "system §36.4).\n  - `for` evaluates its bounds, or its sequence place and length, once into locals that live until\n    the loop exits. A `usize` counter is compared with the end at the head. The body binds the\n    variable in its own scope; `continue` jumps to the counter's increment, and `break` to the\n    exit, which ends the header's locals.\n\n- **Results (M3a).**\n  - `e?` and `e!` move `e` into a temporary, switch on its tag, and in the `Ok` block read\n    the payload `t as Ok .0`.\n  - On `Err`, `?` builds `Err(t as Err .0)` of the function's return type and returns through the\n    same path as `return`; `!` ends in `Panic`.\n  - **Optionals (M6c)** lower the same way with `Some` (tag 1) as the success variant: on `null`,\n    `?` returns the `null` of the function's return type and `!` ends in `Panic(UnwrapNull)`.\n    `o == null` (HIR `IsNull`) takes a shared borrow of `o` (or of a temporary), reads\n    `Discriminant(*r)` and compares it with 0, so `o` is not consumed.\n  - **Vectors and strings (M6d).** A vector dereferences, like a box, to its unsized elements: `v[i]`\n    is the place `v.*[i]`, with a `BoundsCheck` against `Length(v)`, and views are `Ref` of `v.*`.\n    The deref is not a move path, so moving an element out is a move out of a deref (N0251).\n    `push`/`append` lower their argument before the receiver's `Ref mut` (B5). `for (x in v)`\n    iterates over a view held in a header temporary, so the vector stays borrowed for the whole\n    loop. When the value written to a vector element contains a call, it is evaluated into a\n    temporary and the element's index is checked again just before the write (the call may have\n    shrunk the vector).\n  - **Slot maps and stable vectors (M6e)** are built-in calls on a borrow of the container\n    (`insert`, `getMut` and `remove` take `Ref mut`, with `insert` two-phase); `get`'s optional\n    reference carries the map's loan like any built-in result. A stable vector dereferences like\n    a vector, so `sv[i]` is the place `sv.*[i]`; the C backend renders its elements through the\n    chunk table. Liveness treats the targets of `Unpack` as definitions (fixed in M6e: a reference\n    payload bound by a consuming match used to be live from the function's start).\n  - **Region parameters (M6x).** A loan-carrying value stored into a field or element is lowered\n    into a temporary first, so the borrow checker sees a local whose loans flow into the target.\n  - **String views (M6c).** A literal is a constant operand of type `StringView` (no loans); a place\n    is needed only when it is spilled into a temporary. `s[lo..hi]` always emits a `RangeCheck`\n    with `Text`; `s.bytes()` is a `Cast` to `Slice<u8>` that keeps the view's loans.\n  - Moving a variant payload out of a local (only these lowerings do that) uninitialises the whole\n    local in the move dataflow, so the consumed result is not destroyed again.\n  - **Open temporaries.** The builder records the temporaries of every statement still being\n    lowered, with the scope they belong to. An early exit (`return`, `break`, `continue`, `?`)\n    ends them together with that scope, before its locals. Before M3a the temporaries of an\n    enclosing statement, such as a `match` scrutinee, were never ended on such paths.\n  - An expression statement whose value needs drop assigns it to a statement temporary, which is\n    destroyed at the end of the statement (type-system.md §17.5).\n\n- **Boxes and consuming matches (M3c).**\n  - Move paths go through box derefs (`NirPlace.IsMovePath`). Once a box's contents are tracked,\n    the box node has two children: its contents (fields below), then an *allocation* leaf that is\n    initialised while the box owns its memory.\n  - Drop elaboration destroys a partially moved box as: the initialised fields of its contents,\n    then `BoxFree` of the box. `BoxFree` is flagged if the allocation is only maybe initialised.\n  - A match whose arms are literals, ranges, variants without a payload list and or-patterns o",
    478 =>
        "f these (and a final\n    `_` or binding), with no guard, lowers to one `Switch` (ADR 0085 §2.10): ranges and or-patterns expand into one\n    case per value, an earlier arm's value staying with it, while the cases number at most 1,024; past that, and for\n    any other match with nested patterns, guards, `@` or or-patterns that bind, each arm tests its pattern in turn\n    (a range with two comparisons) and falls through to the next. An or-pattern that binds is tried one alternative\n    after another, each binding in its own places, then the arm's body; a guard is a `Branch` after the bindings, to\n    the body or to the next alternative or arm. There are no decision trees.\n  - A consuming `match` moves its scrutinee into a temporary. An arm that lists payloads starts\n    with one `Unpack`, which moves every payload into its binding or into a hidden local of the\n    arm and consumes the temporary as a whole. Other arms leave the value in the temporary, which\n    is destroyed at the end of the statement.\n\n- **Generic code (M6a).** NIR is built for generic versions too (type parameters are move types\n  that may need drop), so the move and borrow checkers run on them. Instantiations skip those\n  checks, which would only repeat the generic version's diagnostics. Drop elaboration and C\n  emission skip generic versions.\n\nPrinted by `ceesafec build --dump-nir`.\n\n## 4. LIR — deferred\n\n`architecture.md` §2.4 plans a C-shaped LIR between NIR and C. In M1 the C emitter consumes NIR\ndirectly. The C-level decisions (type names, helper selection, temporaries) are made in one place,\n`src/backend/c_emitter.cee`, because NIR operands are already flat. LIR will be introduced when a\nsecond consumer appears or when drop elaboration makes the emitter non-trivial (M2/M7).\nThis is a recorded deviation from the plan, not an omission.\n\n## 5. The NIR reference interpreter (M7c)\n\n`ceesafec run --interp <files> [-- <arguments>]` executes a program's NIR directly\n(`src/interpreter/`). It serves as an oracle for the C backend. The C backend and\nthe interpreter consume the same checked NIR, after drop elaboration. Agreement between them\ntherefore tests the C backend and its runtime, not NIR lowering.\n\n### 5.1 Observable behaviour\n\nThe interpreter must produce exactly what the native program produces:\n\n- the bytes written to standard output and standard error, in the same order: every write to\n  standard error first flushes standard output (`ceesafe_rt_hosted.c`);\n- the exit status:\n  - `main`'s result, or `Process.exit`'s code (the operating system keeps the low 8 bits);\n  - 134 after a panic, as `abort()` gives natively;\n- the panic message (`ceesafe panic: <reason> at <file>:<line>:<column>`);\n- the allocation counters: `CEESAFE_FAIL_ALLOC_AT` and the `CEESAFE_ALLOC_REPORT` line. The interpreter\n  counts every attempt that `ceesafe_alloc` would. This includes vector growth, stable-vector chunks,\n  slot-map slots, arena and scratch chunks, pool slots, and `Process.run`'s argument copies. The\n  allocation-failure sweep therefore sees the same attempts.\n\nTo get these, the runtime's algorithms are reimplemented step for step:\n- `ceesafe_rt_core.c`: growth (doubling, at least 4), arena chunks with their 16-byte header,\n  scratch chunks of 64 KiB, the pool slot size, and the panic and report texts;\n- the emitter's helpers (`c-backend.md` §2): push, pop and clear, slot-map generations, and the\n  drop-glue order.\n\n### 5.2 Memory model\n\nMemory is **byte-addressed**. Values have the target layout of `TypeTable.LayoutOf`, which is the\nsame layout the generated C asserts. Pointers are 8-byte addresses, and null is 0. Memory is a set of\n*allocations*, each with a base address aligned to 16 (as `malloc` gives) and a size:\n\n| Kind | Created by | Ends at |\n|---|---|---|\n| local | a function's entry (every local, zero-filled, as the C locals are) | `StorageDead`, or the function's return |\n| heap | `ceesafe_alloc` | `ceesafe_free` |\n| static | a string literal (read-only",
    479 =>
        "), the empty-vector sentinel (size 0) | never |\n\n`StorageDead` ends a local's allocation. The next write to that local (a whole assignment, or a\nwrite or borrow through one of its fields) gives it a *new* allocation, so an old reference to it\nstays dead.\n\n**Interpreter faults.** Each of the following stops the program with\n`ceesafe interp: fault: <what> at <file>:<line>:<column>` on standard error and exit status 70:\n- an access to an ended allocation, or outside an allocation's bounds;\n- a dereference of null;\n- a misaligned access through a raw pointer (M8a): the address is not a multiple of the pointee's\n  alignment;\n- a write to static memory;\n- `ceesafe_free` of anything but the base of a live heap allocation;\n- destroying a stack-placed box whose slot has already ended (M9e), the placed counterpart of a double free; and\n  running a placed site again while its previous box is alive (`memory-model.md` §5.1);\n- a *use* of uninitialised bytes. Heap memory starts uninitialised. Each byte carries an\n  initialised bit, which copies carry along: copying a struct with padding is not a use.\n  Reading a scalar to compute with it, to branch on it, to compare it, as a tag or as an address\n  is a use.\n- a bounds check that bounds-check elimination removed, and that fails (M9c, `optimisations.md` §2.8): the\n  interpreter evaluates every check, and the compiled program would not have made this one\n  (`an eliminated bounds check fails at …`). `ceesafec run --no-bce` turns the analysis off. Likewise an operation whose\n  overflow check was eliminated (`optimisations.md` §3) and that would overflow faults (`an eliminated overflow check\n  fails at …`); `ceesafec run --no-oce` turns that off.\n\nNo correct program can fault. A fault means the compiler, its runtime, or its checks let an\nunsafe program through, or (since M8a) that unsafe code broke its contract (`unsafe.md` §2). This makes the interpreter a dynamic safety oracle: liveness, bounds and\ninitialisation. It does not track borrows (no Stacked Borrows), so it does not catch aliasing\nviolations that stay in bounds.\n\n### 5.3 Execution\n\n- Calls recurse on the host stack. The interpreter runs on its own thread with a 1 GiB stack.\n- Drop glue and the runtime helpers are interpreted in the same order as the emitted C. A user\n  `drop` function, for example, runs before its fields' drops.\n- Arithmetic follows `IntegerSemantics` (type-system.md §6–7), the same table the checked C helpers\n  implement.\n\n### 5.4 The host\n\n- **Output.** Standard output, standard error and standard input are streams supplied by the\n  caller. The CLI uses the process's own.\n- **Arguments.** `argv[0]` is the first source file's path. The arguments after `--` follow it.\n- **Configuration.** The CLI reads `CEESAFE_FAIL_ALLOC_AT` and `CEESAFE_ALLOC_REPORT` as\n  `ceesafe_platform_config` does (leading digits).\n- **Files and sockets** use the host C library through P/Invoke (`open`, `read`, `write`,\n  `close`, `unlink`, `socket`, …) on Linux and macOS, with the same flags and errno mapping as\n  `ceesafe_rt_io.c`. On Windows, I/O is an interpreter fault (\"unsupported\").\n- **Working directory.** An optional working directory resolves relative file paths and is\n  where child processes start. Tests use it, since they run in parallel in one process. The CLI\n  uses the process's own directory.\n- **`Process.run`** starts the program with lib/std's `Process.run`, after flushing standard output.\n  When the interpreter's output streams are the process's own, the child inherits them. When the\n  caller captures output (tests), the child's output is copied into the captured streams.\n\n**C code (M8b).** The interpreter runs no C. A call to an `extern \"C\"` function is interpreted only\nfor this model of the C library, used when the declaration's parameter and result types have the\nexpected shape (integers, `bool` or pointers of the right widths):\n`strlen` (reads bytes up to a zero byte, each access checked), `puts` (writes the ",
    480 =>
        "bytes and a\nnewline to standard output, returns 0), `abs`, `malloc` and `free` (C5: a heap allocation of the\ninterpreter's memory, not counted as CeeSafe's), `memcmp`, `memcpy` and `memset` (checked accesses;\n`memcpy` with overlapping ranges is a fault, as it is undefined in C). Any other extern call is the\nfault `unsupported: call to C function '<name>'`. Exported functions are ordinary CeeSafe functions.\n**User allocators (C5).** A box with a user allocator gets its memory from the allocator's\n`allocate` method and returns it through `deallocate`, run as CeeSafe code; `this` is a one-byte\nread-only allocation per allocator type.\n**Function pointers (M8d).** The C address of an extern or exported function is a value in a range\nno allocation uses (from `0xFFFF000000000000`); a call through it runs the exported function, or\nthe C model for an extern. A call through null or any other value is a fault. Volatile accesses\nare performed like other accesses (the interpreter does every access once, in order).\n\n### 5.5a Placement and the drop trace (M5)\n\n- `InterpreterOptions.Placement` (on by default since M9e, like the C backend's) applies the same\n  `PlacementAnalysis` as the C backend. A stack-placed site stores the value in a new *local* allocation, which is\n  not counted. If the site's previous allocation is still live when it runs again, that is a fault: natively the site\n  has one slot (`memory-model.md` §5.1).\n- A `Drop` of the site's box local destroys the contents, then ends that allocation. A `BoxFree`\n  just ends it. Ending it there, where C's slot lives on until the function returns, makes a\n  late use a fault: a stronger check.\n- `InterpreterOptions.DropTrace`, when given a list, records every executed `Drop` statement as\n  `<function> <line>:<column> <type>`, in order. M5's drop-timing check compares the traces\n  with and without placement.\n\n### 5.5 Agreement tests\n\n- `tools/run_corpus.py --interp` runs every run-pass and run-fail program with `ceesafec run --interp` (`testing.md`\n  §1). Standard output, standard error\n  and exit status must equal the corpus expectations. The native runs check the same\n  expectations under every C configuration.\n- The allocation-failure sweep runs the interpreter beside the native ASan build, for the\n  baseline and at every failure point. The outcomes must be equal.\n",
    481 =>
        "# CeeSafe Language Specification — Initial Draft (v0)\n\nStatus: Milestone 0. Defines the **MVP surface** (milestones 1–4) and marks\n*future proposals* explicitly. Semantics of ownership and borrowing are in\n`ownership.md` (normative); storage and allocators in `memory-model.md`.\nThe formal grammar will be written in `grammar.md` during M1. Until then this\ndocument is the reference.\n\nLegend used in every example heading:\n- **[MVP-M1]** … **[MVP-M6]**: accepted for that milestone.\n- **[FUTURE]**: proposal only; not to be implemented without an approved ADR.\n\n## 1. Lexical structure (summary)\n\n- UTF-8 source. Identifiers `[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*` (ASCII in v0).\n- Comments `//`, `/* */` (non-nesting), doc comments `///`.\n- Integer literals: decimal, `0x`, `0b`, `0o`, `_` separators, optional suffix\n  (`10u8`, `3i64`). An unsuffixed literal takes its type from context; with no\n  context it defaults to `i32`. A literal that doesn't fit its type is a compile error.\n- Float literals `1.0`, `1e9`, suffix `f32`/`f64` (default `f64`).\n- String literals `\"...\"` (escapes `\\n \\t \\\\ \\\" \\0 \\u{...}`) have type\n  `StringView` with static region. Char literals `'a'` are integer literals of the character's Unicode\n  scalar value, `u32` by default, ASCII in a byte (M11; `type-system.md` §4). There is no `char` type.\n- Keywords: `namespace import public private internal fn let var struct enum\n  interface impl static const if else while for in loop break continue return\n  match using defer own borrow mut move unsafe extern true false null this Self\n  as type where drop`. Reserved: `class async await yield macro`.\n\n## 2. Types (MVP)\n\n| Type | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| `i8 i16 i32 i64 u8 u16 u32 u64` | Fixed-width two's complement; overflow panics (`ownership.md` §0) |\n| `isize usize` | Pointer-sized, per *target* |\n| `f32 f64` | IEEE 754; C `float`/`double` |\n| `bool` | `true`/`false`; C `_Bool` |\n| `char` | Unicode scalar value (u32 with validity invariant) |\n| `void` | Unit return type |\n| `Array<T, N>` / `[T; N]` | Fixed-size inline array (Copy if T is Copy) |\n| `Slice<T>` | Scoped: borrowed `(ptr, len)` view; `SliceMut<T>` exclusive |\n| `StringView` | Scoped: borrowed UTF-8 `(ptr, len)` |\n| `String<A>` | Owned UTF-8, allocator `A` |\n| `Box<T, A>` | Owned single heap object |\n| `Vector<T, A>` | Owned growable array |\n| `T?` | Optional. Niche-optimised for `Box` and references, otherwise `{bool, T}` |\n| `Result<T, E>` | Tagged union |\n| `borrow T`, `borrow mut T` | References (scoped). Never null |\n| `*T`, `*mut T` | Raw pointers (unsafe to dereference) |\n| `fn(A, B) -> R` | Plain function pointer (no closure environment) |\n\nDefault allocator type parameter: in the **userland** profile `A` defaults to\n`SystemHeap`. In the **kernel** profile there is no default; it must be explicit\n(`profiles.md`).\n\nConversions: no implicit numeric conversions except widening of an integer\n**literal**. `as` performs only lossless numeric conversions (`u8 as u32`,\n`i32 as i64`, `i32 as f64`). Lossy conversions use `truncate<T>()`,\n`checkedCast<T>()` or `f.toIntSaturating<T>()`.\n\n## 3. Declarations and statements (MVP)\n\n- `let x = e;` immutable binding. `var x = e;` mutable binding. A type annotation\n  is optional (`let x: u64 = 0;`). Bindings must be definitely initialised before\n  use (`var x: i32;` followed by assignment on all paths is allowed).\n- `fn name(p: T, ...) -> R { ... }`, `fn name(...) -> R => expr;`\n- Parameter modes: *by value* (default: copy if Copy, otherwise move),\n  `borrow T`, `borrow mut T`. `own T` is an explicit spelling of by-value for\n  move types, accepted for readability.\n- Call-site borrowing: an argument for a `borrow T` parameter is borrowed\n  implicitly. An argument for a `borrow mut T` parameter must be written\n  `mut x` (as with C# `ref`), so mutation is visible at the call site.\n  Explicit borrow expressions elsewhere: `borrow x`, `borrow mut x`.\n- `move x` explicitly moves (optional; moves of move types are implicit).\n- `if`/`else`,",
    482 =>
        " `while`, `loop`, `for i in a..b` (half-open integer range),\n  `for x in slice` (borrowing iteration), `break`, `continue`, `return`.\n- `match` over enums and integers, exhaustive.\n- `using x = e;`: pinned scoped binding (`ownership.md` R3).\n- `defer stmt;`: runs `stmt` at scope exit on every path (in reverse order of\n  `defer` statements). The deferred statement may not `return`/`break`/`?`.\n- Error propagation `e?`: on `Result<T,E>` yields `T` or returns `Err(e)`\n  converted by `From<E>` (M6; v0 requires identical `E`); on `T?` yields\n  `T` or returns `null`. Postfix `!` unwraps or panics.\n- Evaluation order: strictly left to right, receiver before arguments,\n  arguments before call, and right-hand side of assignment **after** the\n  left-hand side place is evaluated (as in C#). This is specified and the\n  C backend must not delegate it to C's unspecified order (`c-backend.md` §4).\n\n## 4. Example programs\n\n### Ex1 — Functions and arithmetic [MVP-M1]\n\n```ceesafe\nnamespace Demo;\n\nfn gcd(a: u64, b: u64) -> u64 {\n    var x = a;\n    var y = b;\n    while (y != 0) {\n        let t = x % y;\n        x = y;\n        y = t;\n    }\n    return x;\n}\n\nfn square(x: i64) -> i64 => x * x;      // overflow panics\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    Console.writeLine(gcd(1071, 462));  // 21\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Ex2 — Structs and methods (Copy value type) [MVP-M2]\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Point {\n    public x: f64;\n    public y: f64;\n\n    public fn distance(this, other: Point) -> f64 {\n        let dx = this.x - other.x;\n        let dy = this.y - other.y;\n        return Math.sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy);\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let p = Point { x: 3.0, y: 4.0 };\n    let q = p;                            // Copy: p still usable\n    Console.writeLine(p.distance(Point { x: 0.0, y: 0.0 }));\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n`this` alone = by value (copy for Copy types); `borrow this` and\n`borrow mut this` are the reference receivers.\n\n### Ex3 — Move-only resource with `drop` [MVP-M2]\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Fd {\n    raw: i32;\n\n    drop {                      // runs exactly once, when the owner dies\n        unsafe { Sys.close(this.raw); }\n    }\n}\n\nfn consume(fd: own Fd) -> void { }        // fd dropped at end of consume\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let fd = Fd { raw: 3 };\n    consume(fd);\n    // consume(fd);   // error[O3]: use of moved value `fd`\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nA struct with `drop` is never Copy. Fields are dropped after the `drop`\nblock, in declaration order.\n\n### Ex4 — Equivalent of classes: owned heap reference type [MVP-M3]\n\nCeeSafe v0 has **no `class` keyword** (reserved). The C#/TS \"object on the heap with\nidentity\" idiom is written as a `struct` behind `Box`:\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Account {\n    id: u64;\n    balance: i64;\n\n    public fn deposit(borrow mut this, amount: i64) -> void {\n        this.balance += amount;\n    }\n}\n\nfn open(id: u64) -> Result<Box<Account>, AllocError> {\n    return Box.create(Account { id: id, balance: 0 });   // A = SystemHeap (userland)\n}\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    var acct = open(7)!;          // panics on allocation failure\n    acct.deposit(100);            // auto-deref + implicit mut reborrow of *acct\n    return 0;\n}                                  // Box dropped: Account dropped, memory freed\n```\n\n*[FUTURE]* `class Account { ... }` as sugar for a non-Copy type that is always\n`Box`-allocated (OD-4).\n\n### Ex5 — Heap allocation with an explicit allocator [MVP-M3]\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct User { id: u64; age: u32; }\n\nfn createUser<A: Allocator>(alloc: A) -> Result<Box<User, A>, AllocError> {\n    return Box.createIn(alloc, User { id: 100, age: 25 });\n}\n```\n\n`A` is an *allocator type* (`memory-model.md` §3). For free-standing allocators\n(`SystemHeap`, `KernelHeap`) the value is a zero-sized token, so\n`Box<User, KernelHeap>` is one pointer wide.\n\n### Ex6 — Borrowing [MVP-M2]\n\n```ceesafe\nfn inspect(buffer: borrow Buffer) -> usize {\n    return buffer.length;\n}\n\nfn modify(buffer: borrow mut Buffer) -> void {\n    buffer.clear();\n}\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n  ",
    483 =>
        "  var b = Buffer.create()!;\n    let n = inspect(b);             // implicit shared borrow\n    modify(mut b);                  // explicit mutable borrow at call site\n    return n as i32;                // error: usize → i32 is not lossless; use checkedCast\n}\n```\n\n### Ex7 — Returning a borrow [MVP-M2]\n\n```ceesafe\nfn first(values: Slice<i32>) -> borrow i32 {     // elided: derived from `values`\n    return borrow values[0];                     // bounds-checked\n}\n\nfn longer(a: StringView, b: StringView) -> borrow(a, b) StringView {\n    return if (a.length >= b.length) a else b;   // region ⊆ both loans\n}\n```\n\n### Ex8 — Fixed arrays and slices [MVP-M2]\n\n```ceesafe\nfn sum(values: Slice<i64>) -> i64 {\n    var total: i64 = 0;\n    for v in values { total += v; }\n    return total;\n}\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    var data: [i64; 4] = [1, 2, 3, 4];\n    data[2] = 10;\n    Console.writeLine(sum(data[..]));      // whole-array slice\n    Console.writeLine(sum(data[1..3]));    // checked sub-slice\n    // data[4] = 0;                        // compile error: constant index out of range\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Ex9 — Vectors and invalidation [MVP-M6]\n\n```ceesafe\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    var v = Vector<i32>.create();         // no allocation until first push\n    v.push(1)!;                            // push returns Result<void, AllocError>\n    v.push(v.length as i32)!;              // two-phase borrow (B5)\n\n    let r = borrow v[0];\n    // v.push(3)!;                         // error[B1]: `v` is borrowed by `r`\n    Console.writeLine(r);\n    v.push(3)!;                            // fine: `r` is dead (NLL)\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Ex10 — Strings [MVP-M6]\n\n```ceesafe\nfn greet(name: StringView) -> Result<String, AllocError> {\n    var s = String.withCapacity(name.length + 7)?;\n    s.append(\"Hello, \")?;\n    s.append(name)?;\n    return Ok(s);\n}\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let msg = greet(\"CeeSafe\")!;\n    Console.writeLine(msg.view());         // StringView borrowed from msg\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Ex11 — Error handling [MVP-M3]\n\n```ceesafe\nenum ParseError { Empty, BadDigit(u8), Overflow }\n\nfn parseU32(text: StringView) -> Result<u32, ParseError> {\n    if (text.length == 0) { return Err(ParseError.Empty); }\n    var value: u32 = 0;\n    for c in text.bytes() {\n        if (c < '0' as u8 || c > '9' as u8) { return Err(ParseError.BadDigit(c)); }\n        value = value.checkedMul(10)?.checkedAdd((c - '0' as u8) as u32)\n                     .okOr(ParseError.Overflow)?;\n    }\n    return Ok(value);\n}\n```\n\n(`checkedMul` returns `u32?`; `?` on an optional inside a function returning\n`Result` is an error in v0. The example converts it with `okOr` and then uses `?`.)\n\n### Ex12 — Generics with interface constraints [MVP-M6]\n\n```ceesafe\ninterface Comparable<T> {\n    fn compare(borrow this, other: borrow T) -> i32;\n}\n\nfn max<T: Comparable<T>>(a: borrow T, b: borrow T) -> borrow(a, b) T {\n    return if (a.compare(b) >= 0) a else b;\n}\n```\n\nGenerics are monomorphised. Interfaces are static constraints in v0. Dynamic\ndispatch (`dyn Interface`) is *[FUTURE]* (OD-10).\n\n### Ex13 — Interfaces and implementations [MVP-M6]\n\n```ceesafe\ninterface Shape {\n    fn area(borrow this) -> f64;\n}\n\nstruct Circle { r: f64; }\nstruct Rect { w: f64; h: f64; }\n\nimpl Shape for Circle { fn area(borrow this) -> f64 => 3.141592653589793 * this.r * this.r; }\nimpl Shape for Rect   { fn area(borrow this) -> f64 => this.w * this.h; }\n\nfn total<S: Shape>(shapes: Slice<S>) -> f64 {\n    var t = 0.0;\n    for s in shapes { t += s.area(); }\n    return t;\n}\n```\n\n### Ex14 — Recursive structure (owned tree) [MVP-M3]\n\n```ceesafe\nenum Tree {\n    Leaf,\n    Node(Box<TreeNode>),\n}\n\nstruct TreeNode { value: i32; left: Tree; right: Tree; }\n\nfn insert(t: own Tree, v: i32) -> Result<Tree, AllocError> {\n    match (t) {\n        Tree.Leaf => return Ok(Tree.Node(Box.create(TreeNode {\n            value: v, left: Tree.Leaf, right: Tree.Leaf })?)),\n        Tree.Node(var n) => {\n            if (v < n.value) { n.left = i",
    484 =>
        "nsert(move n.left, v)?; }\n            else             { n.right = insert(move n.right, v)?; }\n            return Ok(Tree.Node(n));\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nMoving out of `n.left` (a field of a uniquely owned `Box` contents whose type\nhas no `drop`) is a partial move (O2) immediately repaired by assignment.\nThe checker requires the field to be re-initialised before `n` is used whole.\nOn the `?` error path `n` is partially moved: drop elaboration drops only `n.right`\nand `n.value` (trivial), then frees the box. This is a key M3 test.\n\nDestruction of a deep tree is recursive in v0. A stack-depth concern is recorded\nin `risks.md` (R-9).\n\n### Ex15 — Scoped arena [MVP-M4]\n\n```ceesafe\nfn process(input: Slice<u8>) -> Result<u64, AllocError> {\n    using arena = Arena.create(SystemHeap, chunkSize: 64 * 1024)?;\n\n    let counts = arena.allocArray<u32>(256)?;     // scoped to `arena`, zero-filled\n    for b in input { counts[b as usize] += 1; }\n\n    var distinct: u64 = 0;\n    for c in counts { if (c != 0) { distinct += 1; } }\n    return Ok(distinct);                          // returns a u64, not arena memory\n}                                                 // arena freed here; no live loans\n```\n\n`allocArray<T>` requires `T` without `drop` (R2).\n\n### Ex16 — Custom allocator [MVP-M4]\n\n```ceesafe\n// A fixed-buffer allocator over caller-provided storage.\nfn parseHeaders(raw: Slice<u8>) -> Result<usize, AllocError> {\n    var storage: [u8; 4096] = [0; 4096];\n    using fba = FixedBufferAllocator.over(mut storage);  // scoped to `storage`\n    let fields = fba.allocArray<Slice<u8>>(32)?;          // error[E3]: arena element type is scoped\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nThe error is intentional: storing borrowed slices inside region memory needs\nlifetime-parameterised containers (OD-3). The accepted v0 form stores\n`(offset, length)` pairs:\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Span { start: u32; len: u32; }\nlet fields = fba.allocArray<Span>(32)?;\n```\n\nA user-defined allocator implements the `Allocator` interface\n(`memory-model.md` §3.2) inside `unsafe` code with a documented contract.\n\n### Ex17 — C interoperability [M8b; `type-system.md` §28]\n\n```ceesafe\nextern \"C\" {\n    @effects(sleep)\n    fn write(fd: i32, buf: *u8, count: usize) -> isize;\n}\n\n/// Safety: `bytes` is a valid borrowed slice; write() does not retain the pointer.\npublic fn writeAll(fd: i32, bytes: Slice<u8>) -> bool {\n    var off: usize = 0;\n    while (off < bytes.length) {\n        let rest = bytes[off..];\n        let n = unsafe { write(fd, rest.ptr, rest.length) };\n        if (n < 0) { return false; }\n        off += truncate<usize>(n); // n >= 0 here\n    }\n    return true;\n}\n\n/// Safety: the caller passes `len` readable bytes at `data` (the C side's contract).\n@export(\"geo_checksum\")             // C-callable symbol; `ceesafe_` names belong to the runtime\npublic unsafe fn checksum(data: *u8, len: usize) -> u32 {\n    let s = Slice.fromRaw(data, len);\n    var h: u32 = 2166136261;\n    for (b in s) { h = (h ^ b as u32).wrappingMul(16777619); }\n    return h;\n}\n```\n\nM8b changed this example from its M0 form: `@export` alone makes a function C-callable (no\n`extern \"C\"` on definitions), and an export that reads through a pointer is `unsafe fn`, since a\nsafe CeeSafe caller could pass any pointer. `IoError.fromErrno()`, `checkedCast` and `wrappingMul` are\nnot built in yet. `run-pass/ffi-libc.cee` is the running form of this example, with `puts`, `strlen`\nand `memcpy` in place of `write`, which is not in every C library (MSVC calls it `_write`).\n\n### Ex18 — Unsafe systems operations: MMIO [M8d; `type-system.md` §29]\n\n```ceesafe\n@repr(C)\nstruct UartRegs {\n    data: Volatile<u32>;\n    status: Volatile<u32>;\n}\n\nstruct Uart {\n    regs: MmioPtr<UartRegs>;\n\n    /// Safety: `base` must be the mapped address of a PL011-compatible UART that no other `Uart`\n    /// value aliases.\n    public unsafe fn attach(base: usize) -> Uart => Uart { regs: MmioPtr<UartRegs>.at(base) };\n\n    public fn putByte(borrow mut this, b: u8) -> void {",
    485 =>
        "\n        while ((this.regs.get().status.read() & 0x20) != 0) { Cpu.spinHint(); }\n        this.regs.getMut().data.write(b as u32);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n`Volatile<T>`, `MmioPtr<T>`, `PhysAddr` and `VirtAddr` come from `lib/core`, CeeSafe sources bundled\nwith the compiler (§29.2). The address types are distinct, with no implicit conversions\n(`cosmoos/kernel-memory-and-lifetimes.md`); `DmaAddr` comes with the kernel profile. M8d changed\nthis example from its M0 form: `attach` takes the address as a `usize`, and register access goes\nthrough `get()` / `getMut()`, whose results borrow the `MmioPtr`. It runs as\n`run-pass/mmio.cee` and, freestanding, as `freestanding/uart.cee`.\n\n### Ex19 — Kernel-context effects [FUTURE, C7]\n\n```ceesafe\n@context(irq)            // implies @noalloc @nosleep, transitively checked\nfn timerIrq(ctx: borrow mut IrqContext) -> void {\n    ctx.ackTimer();\n    // KernelHeap.create(...);   // error: allocation not permitted in irq context\n}\n```\n\n## 5. Packages and visibility (M10, ADR 0013)\n\n- A *package* is a directory with `ceesafe.toml`; its sources are the `.cee` files under `src/` (`tooling.md` §4).\n- All files of a package share one scope. Another package's `public` items are named with that package's name,\n  `geometry.Point`, or brought into a file's scope with `import geometry;` or `import geometry.Point;`\n  (`type-system.md` §2).\n- `public` (visible to other packages), `internal` (default: visible within the package), `private` (visible within\n  the declaring file).\n- `namespace X;` declarations are retired: accepted, with no effect.\n\n## 6. Not in the language (v0) — deliberate omissions\n\nExceptions, implicit reference counting, tracing GC, inheritance, implicit\nnumeric conversions, null references, `async/await` (reserved; post-M9),\nclosures that capture (M6+, OD-9), operator overloading (M6+), macros.\n",
    486 =>
        "# References and the Borrow Checker (M2b)\n\nStatus: normative for increment 2b. This document turns `ownership.md` invariants B1–B5 and E1–E3\ninto an algorithm on NIR. The surface rules for references are in `type-system.md` §13.\nWhere this document is stricter than `ownership.md`, it records a v0 simplification that later\nmilestones may relax.\n\n## 1. What makes the v0 problem small\n\nThe algorithm can be simple and **exact for v0** because of four language rules (type-system §13):\n\n1. **References are values of type `borrow T` / `borrow mut T` held only in locals and temporaries.**\n   They never appear in struct fields (E3) or constants.\n2. **A reference binding is immutable and initialised at its declaration** (`let r = …;`; no\n   `var r: borrow T`). Assigning to `r` writes *through* it (`*r = v`) and never re-points it.\n   So each reference local is assigned exactly once, which makes the points-to sets below\n   flow-insensitive without losing precision.\n3. **No nested references** (`borrow borrow T` is rejected).\n4. **A use of a `borrow mut T` value is an implicit reborrow** of its referent (§4). Two usable\n   mutable references to one place can therefore never coexist.\n\nTogether with lexical scoping, (1) and (2) mean a reference cannot outlive its referent *inside*\na function, except through a **temporary** borrowed at a call site, or through **`return`**. Both\nare checked explicitly (§6, §7). Conflicting accesses while a loan is live (B1/B2) are the\nremaining work, and they need liveness (§5).\n\n## 2. NIR additions\n\n```text\nprojection = Field(f) | Deref | Downcast(V) | Index(operand)\nplace      = local projection*                 -- (*r).f is  r Deref Field(f)\nrvalue    += Ref(Shared | Mut, place)           -- creates a loan\n           | Slice(Shared | Mut, place, lo, hi) -- M2d: also a loan, of the whole array or slice viewed\nstmt      += StorageDead(local)                 -- the local's storage ends (scope exit, statement end for temps)\n```\n\nLowering facts the checker relies on:\n- Every `Ref` is assigned directly to a local or temporary: `t = Ref(k, p)`.\n- A temporary lives until the end of the source statement that created it. At that point the\n  builder emits `StorageDead(t)`. The condition of an `if` or `while` counts as its own statement\n  (M15 S2): its temporaries end once it is evaluated, before the branch, and each evaluation of a\n  `while` condition ends its own. A borrow taken in a condition therefore does not reach the body.\n- User locals get `StorageDead` at the end of their block, and on `break`/`continue` for the\n  blocks being left. (`return` ends every local; references escaping through `return` are §7's job.)\n- A use of a `borrow mut T` operand at a call argument, an initialiser or a return is lowered as\n  `Ref(Mut, (*r))`, a reborrow. A coercion of `borrow mut T` to `borrow T` is `Ref(Shared, (*r))`.\n\n## 3. Loans and points-to sets\n\n- Each `t = Ref(k, p)` at program point P creates a **loan** `L = (k, p, P)`.\n- Each reference local `r` has a set `loans(r)` of the loans it may refer to (directly or through\n  reborrows). It is the least solution of:\n\n| NIR | Constraint |\n|---|---|\n| `t = Ref(k, p)` | `L ∈ loans(t)`; and if `p` contains `Deref` of a reference local `q`, then `loans(q) ⊆ loans(t)` (a reborrow keeps the original loan alive) |\n| `t = Slice(k, p, lo, hi)` | as `Ref(k, p)`: a slice is a reference to `[T]`, and its loan covers all of `p`, whatever the range |\n| `t = Use(Place(r))` with `r` of reference type | `loans(r) ⊆ loans(t)` |\n| `t = Call(f, args)` where `f` returns a reference | `loans(arg_i) ⊆ loans(t)` for every parameter `i` that `f`'s signature says the result is derived from (§7) |\n| reference **parameter** `p_i` | `Param_i ∈ loans(p_i)`, a placeholder loan for \"whatever the caller lent\" |\n\nBecause every reference local is assigned once (§1), solving these subset constraints by\nfixpoint iteration gives the same result as a flow-sensitive analysis.\n\n**Loan-holdin",
    487 =>
        "g locals (M4a).** Since M4a, every local of a *scoped* type holds loans\n(`type-system.md` §19.1), not only reference locals. The constraints generalise:\n- **Copies and moves.** When an assignment's rvalue reads a place rooted at a loan-holding local\n  `q` (`Use`, aggregates, enum values, built-in calls), then `loans(q) ⊆ loans(t)`. This covers a\n  payload read such as `t = (q as Ok).0`.\n- **`Unpack`.** `Unpack(q, …)` gives each target `loans(q)`.\n- **Borrowing a loan holder.** `Ref(k, p)` where `p` is rooted at a loan-holding local `q` and does\n  not go through a deref: `loans(q) ⊆ loans(t)`. A reference to a scoped value keeps that\n  value's loans alive.\n- Liveness (§5) and the return check (§7) consider all loan-holding locals.\n\n## 4. Accesses\n\nEach NIR statement and terminator performs **accesses** to places, in evaluation order:\n\n| Construct | Access |\n|---|---|\n| operand `Place(p)` | **Read** p |\n| `Ref(Shared, p)` | **BorrowShared** p |\n| `Ref(Mut, p)` | **BorrowMut** p |\n| `Assign(p, …)` | the rvalue's accesses, then **Write** p |\n| `StorageDead(l)` | **Dead** l |\n\nTwo places **overlap** if they have the same root local and one projection list is a prefix of\nthe other. `a` overlaps `a.f`, `a.f` does not overlap `a.g`, and `(*r)` overlaps `(*r).f`.\nTwo `Index` projections always compare equal (M2d): `a[i]` overlaps `a[j]`, because v0 does no\nindex reasoning. Two mutable slices of one array therefore cannot be live at once, even if\ntheir ranges are disjoint.\nPlaces with different roots never overlap. That is correct because aliasing between different\nroots only arises through loans, and every loan is checked when it is created.\n\n**Conflicts** (B1, B2):\n\n| Live loan \\ access | Read | Write | BorrowShared | BorrowMut | Dead |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Shared | ok | **conflict** | ok | **conflict** | **conflict** |\n| Mut | **conflict** | **conflict** | **conflict** | **conflict** | **conflict** |\n\n## 5. Liveness (NLL)\n\n- A reference local is **live** at a point if some path from that point reads it (as an operand\n  or as the root of a place) before the function ends. Reference locals are never redefined\n  (§1), so there is no kill except at the definition. This is standard backward dataflow over the\n  NIR CFG.\n- The **live loans after point P** are the union of `loans(r)` over every reference local `r` live\n  after P.\n- **Check:** for every access `a` at point P to place `q`, and every loan `L` live after P with\n  `L` not created at P, it is an error if `L.place` overlaps `q` and the table in §4 says\n  conflict. The errors are N0240 (write), N0241 (borrow), N0242 (read) and N0243 (Dead: the\n  place stops existing while borrowed).\n\nThis accepts `let r = borrow x; print(r); x = 5;`, because `r` is dead after `print`. It rejects\n`let r = borrow x; x = 5; print(r);`.\n\n**Overwriting a reference is not a use of its referent (M11, G3).** A **Write** or **Dead** of a local `r` whose\ntype is a reference does not conflict with a loan whose place goes through `*r`, such as `(*r).next`. The write\nreplaces which object `r` points to; it does not change, move or end the object it pointed to. That object stays\nprotected by the loans the new reference inherited from `r` when it was made (§3: a reborrow `Ref(k, (*r)…)` gets\n`loans(r)`). A box is not a reference: overwriting a box destroys its contents, so a loan through `*b` still conflicts.\nThis accepts a cursor walking a list, `node = cursor!; … cursor = next_of(node);`, and a temporary that is reassigned\nin each iteration of a loop while a reference made through the previous value lives on.\n\n**Writes happen after the value (M2d).** In `Assign(p, rvalue)` the rvalue is evaluated first and\n`p` is written last. The **Write** access is therefore checked against the loans live *after*\nthe statement; the rvalue's own accesses use the loans live before it. This accepts\n`a[0] = sum(a[..])`: the slice is dead once `sum` has returned, before `a[0]` is written.\nRust's MIR gets th",
    488 =>
        "e same result by writing a call's result in a separate step.\n\n**B5, two-phase borrows (as implemented; ADR 0073 §2.2).** For a method call `e.m(args)` whose receiver is\n`borrow mut this` and `e` is a place, the receiver's loan is created **after** `args` are\nevaluated, right before the call. Arguments may therefore read `e` (`v.push(v.len())`), but\nthey cannot hold a borrow of `e` across the call. The place itself, with its index values and their bounds\nchecks, is found where the receiver stands, left to right: `ps[i].set(bump(mut i))` borrows the element `i` named\nbefore `bump` ran (before ADR 0073, after). The loan exists only for the call, which is exactly the interval in\nwhich it must be exclusive.\n\nEvery other argument `mut p` (`p` a place) and every `borrow mut` local passed on to a `borrow mut` parameter (B4) is\ntwo-phase too, in calls of functions, methods, closures and function values: its place is found where it stands, and\nits loan made at the call. Unlike a receiver it is **reserved** in between: when a later argument does more than\nread (a call, a borrow, a move), a shared loan of the place is held from the argument to the call, so a later\nargument may read the place (`fail(mut s, s.count)`) but a write, a move or a mutable borrow of it conflicts (N0240,\nN0241), and so does a shared borrow still live at the call (`f(mut s, s.name.view())`). Reads in later arguments\nof what a reserved loan may cover are taken where they stand, before the loans are made.\n\n## 6. Temporaries\n\n`f(makeValue())` with a `borrow T` parameter borrows a temporary that dies at the end of the\nstatement (`StorageDead`). If the result of `f` is derived from that argument and is stored in a\nreference local used after the statement, the `Dead` access conflicts with the live loan: N0243\n(\"temporary dropped while still borrowed\"). The fix is to bind the value to a local first. The same holds for a\ntemporary made for a receiver or a slice (ADR 0073 §2.1): `make().view()` is valid within its statement, and N0243\nonce the view is used after it.\n\n## 7. Returning references (E1, E2)\n\n- **Signature.** A function whose return type is a reference must name the parameters the result\n  may be derived from. With exactly one reference parameter (a `borrow`/`borrow mut` receiver\n  counts), that parameter is implied (elision). Otherwise the return type must be written\n  `borrow(a, b) T` or `borrow mut(a) T`, naming reference parameters (N0245 if missing or wrong).\n  `borrow mut` results may be derived from `borrow mut` parameters only.\n- **Body check at each `Return(op)`.** For every loan `L ∈ loans(op)`:\n  - `L = Param_i`: `i` must be among the named parameters (N0245);\n  - `L` is a real loan whose place starts with `Deref` of reference parameter `p_i`: allowed if\n    `i` is named (N0245 otherwise). Examples: `borrow this.items` with `this: borrow Self`, or\n    `borrow (*a).f`;\n  - any other real loan borrows a local or temporary of this function: N0244 (\"returns a reference\n    to local data\").\n- **Deep results** (type-system.md §25.6, ADR 0074 §2.4). A reference parameter that is not a view has two\n  placeholder loans: `Outer_i`, the loan of the reference itself, and `Param_i`, what its referent holds. A value read\n  through `*p_i` carries `Param_i` and not `Outer_i`; so does a borrow of a place beyond another reference stored in\n  the referent. If the result is a deep result of `i`, the body check also rejects (N0245) `Outer_i`, and a real loan\n  whose place starts with `Deref` of `p_i` and passes no other reference: the referent's own storage.\n- **Callers** use the signature as the `Call` constraint in §3. For a deep result of parameter `i` whose argument is\n  `t = &p` (`&mut p`), the result takes `p`'s local's loans, less `Outer` placeholders of a parameter `p` is reached\n  through, instead of `t`'s; for an argument that is a reference parameter, its loans less its `Outer`; for an\n  argument `t = f(a)`, the only assignment of `t`, where `f`'s result ",
    489 =>
        "passes through its parameter `a`\n  (type-system.md §25.6, B18), what `a` contributes as that argument, followed again if `a` is defined the same way.\n\n## 8. What the v0 checker deliberately does not do\n\n- No references in aggregates, and no reference re-pointing — **relaxed in M6x** (OD-3,\n  `type-system.md` §25): aggregates with region parameters, scoped collections and re-pointable\n  reference fields. The checker keeps one loan set per value; writes into fields, elements and\n  through references add the written loans to the holder and to what its mutable loans point to\n  (iterated to a fixpoint); calls apply their signature flows; and a loan conflicts only at points\n  reachable from where it was created (holders such as a vector receive loans after their own\n  definition). Destroying a value whose `drop` may read its loans is a use of them.\n- Index-sensitive disjointness (`a[i]` versus `a[j]`) came in 2d with arrays, conservatively: all elements\n  overlap. Since M13 G5, two different **constant** indices are different places, so `borrow mut a[0]` and\n  `borrow mut a[2]` may be live together. That is sound because a constant index names one element whatever the\n  array's length. Any index that is not a constant still overlaps every element.\n- Moves and drops are 2c. The `StorageDead` points emitted now are where drops will go.\n- **`await` adds no rule** (L1, type-system.md §40.2). An awaited call is a call in NIR\n  (`intermediate-representation.md` §3), so its arguments' loans last until the task returns, as for any call. A frame\n  never moves, so a loan of a local across an `await` needs nothing more. A scope's child is an element of a scoped\n  collection (§40.5), so its loans last until the scope returns.\n\n## 9. Diagnostics\n\nEvery borrow error names the conflicting access (primary span) and carries notes for the loan's\ncreation point and for a later use of the reference that keeps it live, when one exists.\n",
    490 =>
        "# CeeSafe Memory Model and Allocation Architecture (v0)\n\nStatus: Milestone 0 proposal. Safety rules referenced as O*/B*/E*/R*/H*/U* are\ndefined in `ownership.md`. Goose findings are in `research/goose.md` (commit\n`f605b5a`). Other prior art is in `research/comparative.md`. The CosmoOS kernel\nmapping is in `cosmoos/kernel-memory-and-lifetimes.md`.\n\n## 1. Candidate architectures\n\n### A. Ownership-based stack + heap (Rust-like)\n\nValues live on the native stack by default. Long-lived and dynamically sized data\nlive in owned heap objects (`Box`, `Vector`, `String`). Safety comes entirely\nfrom ownership and borrowing. Arenas are ordinary library types built with\n`unsafe` plus lifetime-carrying APIs.\n\n### B. Region-based + owned heap (Cyclone/Tofte–Talpin-like)\n\nEvery allocation is placed in a *region*. Regions are lexically scoped (stack frames,\ndynamic regions) or the heap region. Pointer types carry a region, and an\noutlives/effect system prevents dangling. Owned heap objects exist as unique\npointers in the heap region. Region inference reduces annotations.\n\n### C. Hybrid: ownership + explicit regions + conservative escape analysis\n\nArchitecture A's ownership/borrow core is *the only* safety mechanism. Regions\n(arenas, scratch stacks, fixed buffers) are **explicit library values whose\nallocations are loans of the region** (R1), so the borrow checker covers them\nwith no separate region type system. A conservative escape/placement analysis\n(M5) may move allocations to cheaper storage only when the move is\n**unobservable** (§5). Goose-derived techniques (data stacks for scoped\ntemporaries, construction in place, grow-only containers, typed pools, BCE)\nare adopted as **implementation strategies and library types**, not as the\nsafety argument.\n\n## 2. Evaluation\n\n| Criterion | A: Ownership | B: Regions | C: Hybrid |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Safety argument | One mechanism (affine + XOR borrows). Proven for the λRust core | Region type system + uniqueness. Proven for core calculi, but **two** interacting mechanisms in practice | Same as A (one mechanism). Regions reduce to loans. Escape analysis is outside the trusted base |\n| Kernel fit (no hidden allocation, failure values) | Good | Good if regions are explicit. Poor with inference (hidden placement) | Good: placement changes require the profile to permit them (§5), and the kernel profile forbids implicit ones |\n| Ergonomics for C#/TS users | Annotation burden for structs of references. v0 limits mitigate (E3) | Many annotations, or inference with unpredictable lifetimes (MLKit space leaks) | As A, plus `using arena = ...` for batch lifetimes. Diagnostics explain placement |\n| Implementation complexity | High (NLL borrow checker) | High (region inference + effects) **plus** uniqueness | A's complexity + an *optional* M5 pass. Region support costs nearly nothing extra |\n| Runtime cost | `malloc`/`free` per heap object unless the user picks an arena | Cheap bump allocation everywhere; risk of space leaks | Same as A by default. Arena and scratch where the user or proven-safe analysis selects them |\n| Predictability | High | Low with inference | High: placement is either explicit or reported by `--explain-allocations` |\n| Goose techniques usable | Some (in-place construction, BCE) | Data stacks map naturally to lexical regions | All the \"cheap, local\" ones (goose.md §9 key cautions) |\n| Failure mode if the analysis is wrong | Unsound borrow check → safety bug | Unsound region inference → safety bug | Unsound borrow check → safety bug. Unsound placement → **caught**, because placement output is re-validated by the checker (`architecture.md` §1) |\n\n### Recommendation: **C — Hybrid**\n\n1. It keeps **one** safety mechanism to specify, test and eventually verify. Goose's\n   own history shows the cost of a second, fragile mechanism: its lifetime checker is graded\n   \"C/D, structurally fragile\" by its own inventory, and roughly half of its\n   recent commits fixed holes (goose.md §9.4",
    491 =>
        "). Option B would add a region type\n   system *alongside* uniqueness. Option C reduces regions to borrows.\n2. Arenas, scratch regions and fixed buffers are first-class from M4 with no\n   new type-system concept: \"an allocation borrows its region\" is checked by\n   the same B1/B2/E1 rules as `borrow x`.\n3. Escape analysis stays outside the safety argument (master prompt rules 10–11),\n   and its effects are visible and reportable.\n4. It fits CosmoOS: every allocation in the kernel profile names its allocator\n   type or region value, so nothing allocates implicitly.\n\nCost: the v0 restriction E3 (no references in user structs) is felt most\nfor arena-heavy code (e.g. AST nodes pointing to other arena nodes). The v0\nanswer is indices or `Span` offsets (Ex16), and the long-term answer is OD-3.\n\n## 3. Storage categories\n\n### 3.1 Native stack (C automatic storage)\n- Scalars, fixed-size structs/enums/arrays, references, `Box` pointers, temporaries.\n- Size limit per value: `maxStackValue` per profile (userland default 64 KiB,\n  kernel default 512 B). Larger fixed-size locals are a compile error suggesting\n  `Box` or a region. This avoids unbounded native-stack consumption.\n- No variable-length arrays (C VLAs and `alloca` are never emitted).\n- Kernel profile: per-function frame-size estimates are reported, and the deepest\n  call chain is checked against the kernel stack budget (C4/C7; see\n  `profiles.md`).\n\n### 3.2 Heap (owned)\n\nImplemented in C5 (`type-system.md` §30, `lib/core/alloc.cee`): `Layout` and `Allocator` with\n`allocate` and an `unsafe deallocate` (no `reallocate` yet), user allocators as field-less structs, for\n`Box` only.\n\n```ceesafe\n// core library (sketch)\npublic struct Layout { size: usize; align: usize; }      // align: power of two\n\npublic interface Allocator {\n    // `this` is a free-standing allocator value: ZST token or a 'static handle.\n    unsafe fn allocate(borrow this, layout: Layout) -> Result<*mut u8, AllocError>;\n    unsafe fn deallocate(borrow this, ptr: *mut u8, layout: Layout) -> void;\n    unsafe fn reallocate(borrow this, ptr: *mut u8, old: Layout, newSize: usize)\n        -> Result<*mut u8, AllocError>;                 // default: alloc+copy+free\n}\n```\n\n- *Free-standing allocator types* (`SystemHeap`, `KernelHeap<Flags>`,\n  `SlabCache<T>`-backed allocator singletons) are zero-sized or `'static`.\n  Only these may parameterise `Box<T, A>`, `Vector<T, A>`, `String<A>` (H1).\n  This keeps `Box` one pointer wide and makes \"Box outlives allocator\"\n  trivially true.\n- *Instance allocators* (arenas, pools, fixed buffers) are values. Their\n  allocations are scoped values carrying a loan of the instance (R1). A\n  `Pool<T>` hands out `PoolBox<T>` (scoped, individually freeable). An `Arena`\n  hands out `borrow mut T` / `SliceMut<T>` (freed in bulk).\n- **Allocator lifetime**: free-standing allocators live for the whole program\n  (or kernel lifetime). Instance allocators cannot be dropped while allocations\n  are live (B2). Outstanding allocations therefore can never outlive their\n  allocator (master prompt §5.5).\n- **Ownership of allocations**: always exactly one owner (O1). Freeing with the wrong\n  allocator is impossible because the allocator is part of the type (`A`) or of the loan.\n- **Thread safety**: an allocator type declares `Sync` if it may be used\n  concurrently (`SystemHeap`, `KernelHeap`: yes; `Arena`, `FixedBuffer`: no).\n  Enforced once threads exist (M-later).\n- **Failure**: every allocating API returns `Result<_, AllocError>`.\n  Convenience `...OrPanic` / `!` usage is allowed in userland and **rejected by\n  a lint (error) in the kernel profile**.\n- **Testability**: `FailingAllocator<A>(failAfter: n)` and a fault-injection\n  hook in `SystemHeap` (env var in hosted test builds) make every failure path\n  testable (M3 acceptance).\n  - *As implemented in M3b (ADR 0003):* `FailingAllocator` is a built-in, zero-sized allocator\n    type over the platform heap, steered by `FailingAllocator.failAfter(n)` and `.res",
    492 =>
        "et()`\n    (a process-wide countdown).\n  - The runtime counts every allocation attempt when asked to (ADR 0059). `CEESAFE_FAIL_ALLOC_AT=k` fails attempt *k*,\n    and `CEESAFE_ALLOC_REPORT=1` prints `ceesafe alloc: attempts=<n> live=<m>` when `main` returns. Either one, or\n    `ceesafec test`, turns counting on; without them the runtime counts nothing.\n  - **In a host program** (ADR 0088 §2.2), whose `main` is not CeeSafe's, the same settings come from `ceesafe_init`'s\n    config (`ceesafe/embed.h`): `fail_alloc_at` and `alloc_report`, or with `CEESAFE_INIT_ENVIRONMENT` the two\n    variables (the environment's `CEESAFE_FAIL_ALLOC_AT` wins over the config's when set). The report is printed by\n    `ceesafe_shutdown`, which the host calls (in its exit path), and which returns `live`; the generated `main` calls\n    `ceesafe_init` with the environment flag before anything else and `ceesafe_shutdown` when `main` returns. Counting\n    cannot start under live blocks: a config asking for counting after the runtime has allocated is\n    `CEESAFE_INIT_LATE`, and changes nothing (without counting, it succeeds; when only the environment asks, it\n    succeeds without counting and says so on stderr). The generated `main` counts regardless,\n    as before `ceesafe_init`, when a C constructor has allocated through CeeSafe. A host that never calls it gets no counting, no failure injection and no report, whatever the\n    environment says. `ceesafe_init`'s copy of `argv` is not counted.\n  - **With a host's allocator** (ADR 0088 §2.5), `ceesafe_init`'s `allocator` replaces the C library's under this\n    accounting: attempts are counted and failed before it is called, so the injected attempt never reaches it, and\n    `live` and `foreign held` count the same blocks. `CEE_ALLOC_FOREIGN` and `CEE_ALLOC_FOREIGN_INIT` blocks come from\n    it too.\n  - **With threads.** The counters are atomic, so every attempt, whatever its thread, gets its own number in one\n    order, and attempt *k* is the only one that fails. That order is the order in which the threads' attempts reach\n    the counter. A sweep (`k` = 1, 2, …) therefore fails every attempt of a run that allocates from one thread at a\n    time in a fixed order, as before. Where threads allocate concurrently, the attempt numbered *k* may be a different\n    allocation from one run to the next: the sweep still fails one allocation per run and never races, but it may not\n    reproduce, and it may miss an allocation that is never numbered *k* in its run. `FailingAllocator`'s countdown is\n    atomic in the same way (`type-system.md` §18.1). The same holds for a host's threads calling exports at once.\n  - C code's allocations through `CEE_ALLOC_FOREIGN` (ADR 0060, type-system.md §28.6) count in `attempts` and can be\n    failed, but not in `live`: when there were any, a second line, `ceesafe alloc: foreign held=<k>`, gives the blocks\n    C still holds, and no tool reads it as a leak. `CEE_ALLOC_FOREIGN_INIT` (ADR 0061), for a C library's one-time\n    initialisation, is neither counted in `attempts` nor failed; its blocks are in `foreign held`.\n  - The test runner's sweep uses both (`testing.md`).\n\n### 3.3 Arenas (regions)\n\n- `Arena.create(parent: A, chunkSize)`, an instance allocator over a free-standing\n  parent. Bump allocation in chunks, with all chunks released at drop.\n- v0 contents must have no `drop` (R2), so bulk release equals destruction.\n  Arena-with-drop-list is OD-8.\n- `reset(borrow mut this)` requires no live loans (B2), so it is safe by\n  construction.\n- An arena is **not** the native stack: it is heap-backed (master prompt rule 9).\n\n### 3.4 Scratch regions (Goose-style data stacks)\n\nFrom goose.md §9.1/§9.2: a per-thread, compiler-known **scratch stack** used\nfor dynamically sized temporaries with nested lifetimes:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn render(n: usize) -> Result<void, AllocError> {\n    using frame = Scratch.enter();                 // records watermark\n    let buf = frame.allocArray<u8>(n)?;       ",
    493 =>
        "     // bump; scoped to `frame`\n    ...\n}                                                  // watermark restored: one store\n```\n\n- Semantically an arena with LIFO discipline. Safety follows from R1 + R3\n  (`using` frames cannot be moved, and their allocations cannot escape).\n- Storage: hosted/userland uses a reserved virtual region with guard pages,\n  plus an explicit capacity check. Exhaustion returns `AllocError`\n  **unless** the frame was created with `Scratch.enterOrHeap()`, in which case it falls back\n  to the parent heap. Kernel: a fixed per-thread buffer of profile-defined size\n  with explicit checks. No reliance on page faults.\n- Nested frames must be strictly LIFO. *Corrected by ADR 0004:* `using` alone does not ensure\n  this. Nested frames come from `parent.enter()`, which mutably borrows the parent, and only\n  one root frame per thread may be live (a second `Scratch.enter()` panics).\n  Goose's \"one growable on top\" rule is **not** adopted in v0. The scratch region\n  hands out fixed-size allocations only. Growable scratch arrays are a future\n  extension (OD-12).\n\n### 3.5 Fixed-buffer and pool allocators\n- `FixedBufferAllocator.over(mut storage)` borrows its backing storage mutably\n  for its whole lifetime, so the backing array cannot be touched directly while it is used.\n- *As implemented in M4c (ADR 0004):* `Pool<T>` is a fixed-capacity pool whose `take(v)` returns a\n  scoped `PoolBox<T>`: a box that holds a loan of the pool and returns its slot when destroyed\n  (`type-system.md` §19.6). `SlotMap` with generation-checked handles is still M6.\n- `Pool<T>` / `SlotMap<T>`: typed slots with generation counters (goose.md §9.8\n  plus Vale). Handles are free-standing `Handle<T>` values (index + generation)\n  validated on access (`get(h) -> borrow T?`).\n\n### 3.6 Static data\nString literals and `static let` constants (immutable). `static var` is\n`unsafe` to access in v0 (data races); safe statics with synchronisation come with\nconcurrency.\n\n## 4. Collections and invalidation\n\n| Type | Ownership | Growth | Element references | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `[T; N]` | value | — | loans of the array | Copy iff T Copy |\n| `Slice<T>` / `SliceMut<T>` | scoped borrow | — | reborrows | bounds-checked |\n| `Vector<T, A>` (M6d, type-system §23.1) | owns buffer | reallocates (amortised ×2) | loans of the vector. Growth requires `borrow mut` → **rejected while element loans are live** | contiguous; **no address stability promise** |\n| `StableVector<T, A>` (M6e, type-system §24.2) | owns chunks | appends a chunk, never moves | loans with *shared* access permitted during `push` (grow-only: goose.md §9.3) | push takes `borrow this` with interior bump. Not `Sync` |\n| `SlotMap<T, A>` (M6e, type-system §24.1) | owns slots | may reallocate | `Handle<T>` (not a loan) validated by generation | stable *identity*, not stable addresses |\n| `String<A>` / `StringView` | owns / scoped | as Vector | as Vector | UTF-8 invariant |\n| `Box<T, A>` | owns one object | — | loans of the box | `T?` niche |\n\nIterator invalidation is impossible in safe code: iterators are scoped values\nholding a loan of the collection.\n\n## 5. Placement analysis (M5; on by default since M9e) — rules\n\nThe analysis may change *where* a value lives only under these conditions:\n\n1. **Box → native stack / scratch**: allowed iff (a) the Box is created and\n   dropped in the same function on all paths, (b) it is never moved into a\n   callee or returned, (c) its address is not exposed to `unsafe`/FFI, (d)\n   the profile marks `A` as *placement-transparent* (`SystemHeap` in userland: yes;\n   **`KernelHeap`: no**, because kernel allocation is observable through\n   accounting, and failure injection must remain meaningful), and (e) the size is\n   below the profile's stack limit. Failure behaviour: the original program could fail\n   with `AllocError`, and the optimised one cannot. This is permitted only because the\n   placement-transparent contract declares allocation failure",
    494 =>
        " of that allocator\n   \"may spuriously succeed\".\n2. **Never** the reverse: a scoped/borrowed value is never promoted to the heap to\n   make a program type-check (master prompt §7 and rule 11). There is no such\n   code path, because placement runs after the borrow check on accepted programs only.\n3. Destruction order and timing are preserved (master prompt rule 12): drops of\n   demoted Boxes run at exactly the same NIR points.\n4. Every decision is reported by `--explain-allocations`:\n   `src/a.cee:12:9: Box<Node> placed on stack (does not escape; SystemHeap is placement-transparent)`\n   `src/a.cee:20:9: Box<Node> stays on heap: moved into call to 'insert' at 21:5`\n\n### 5.1 The M5 implementation (`PlacementAnalysis`)\n\nThe analysis works per function on NIR after drop elaboration. It is deliberately simple and\nconservative: a box it cannot follow stays on the heap. Rule 1's conditions become the\nfollowing checks.\n\n- **Sites.** A site is `R = Box.create(v)` or `Box.createIn(a, v)`, with `R` a whole local. The\n  site is **eligible** when:\n  - the allocator is `SystemHeap`, the only placement-transparent allocator so far (rule 1d).\n    `FailingAllocator` is not transparent: its failures are its purpose;\n  - `T` is at most 1 KiB, and the function's stack-placed boxes total at most 4 KiB (rule 1e);\n  - each site has at most one box alive at a time, so one storage slot per site suffices. A site in\n    no loop of the function's graph has this by construction. A site in a loop has it when every\n    local of its flow set `F` (below) ends, by its `StorageDead`, on every path from the site back to\n    itself (M9e): no local can then hold one execution's box when the site runs again, and since the box\n    does not escape, it has been dropped by then. A local that is not ended inside the loop keeps the\n    box on the heap (\"may outlive its loop iteration\"). The interpreter checks this rule: a site whose\n    previous box is still alive when it runs again is an interpreter fault.\n- **The flow set** `F` of a site is the set of locals that may hold its box: `R` itself, and every\n  whole local assigned (moved or copied) from a place rooted in `F` whose type is the box type or\n  `R`'s result type. That includes the unpacked `Ok` payload and copies of the result. The\n  analysis iterates until `F` stops growing.\n- **Escapes.** The box stays on the heap, with the first reason found, if:\n  1. a local in `F` has more than one definition. Its drops could then see a box from elsewhere;\n  2. a value of the box type or of `R`'s type, read from a place rooted in `F`, flows anywhere\n     but into a local of `F`: a call or built-in argument, an aggregate, a return, or a\n     non-local place (rule 1b);\n  3. a place rooted in `F` of the box type is borrowed **mutably** (`borrow mut Box<T>`). The\n     borrower could replace the box, and drop ours with the heap's drop glue. Borrowing the box\n     shared, or borrowing its contents either way, is fine: the borrow checker ends those\n     borrows before the box's drop;\n  4. a `Drop` destroys a place rooted in `F` that is neither a whole local of the box type nor\n     inside its contents. Destroying contents in place is fine: drop elaboration does that to\n     the fields left after a move out. A result holding the box, though, would be destroyed by\n     the heap's drop glue. A result *may* be destroyed where it cannot hold the box: in a block\n     dominated by a non-`Ok` target of a switch on that result's own discriminant, where the target\n     is entered only from the switch. That is the error path of `?` and `!`, whose drop glue sees\n     an `Err`.\n\n  Reads through the box (`b.f`, `b.f = x`, moving a field out) and `discriminant` of the result\n  are not escapes. Rule 1c (unsafe and FFI), from M8a: a function that makes a raw pointer from a reference, or a\n  reference or slice from a raw pointer, keeps all its boxes on the heap (reason \"address exposed\";\n  `type-system.md` §27.5). Rule 1a (created and\n  dropped in o",
    495 =>
        "ne function) follows from rules 2 and 4.\n- **Demotion.** A stack-placed site writes `v` into a per-site slot of the function's frame and\n  makes `Ok(&slot)` without allocating; that allocation cannot fail.\n  - A `Drop` of a box local in `F` destroys the contents only, and does not free.\n  - A `BoxFree` of one does nothing.\n  - Drops therefore run at the same NIR points with the same contents (rule 3).\n\n## 6. No-GC baseline and cycles\n\nNo tracing GC, no implicit RC. Ownership is a forest (H3). Graph-shaped data\nuses one of: `SlotMap` handles, arena + indices, or (future, OD-13) an explicit\n`Rc<T>`/`Weak<T>` library type with documented cycle-leak behaviour. In\nthe kernel, explicit refcounted *kernel objects* wrap CosmoOS's own refcount\nprotocol (`cosmoos/kernel-memory-and-lifetimes.md`). That is a different\ntype (`KRef<T>`) with type-specific release, not a generic `free`.\n\n## 7. Goose technique adoption summary\n\n| Goose technique (goose.md §9) | CeeSafe adoption | Milestone |\n|---|---|---|\n| Data stacks for scoped temporaries | `Scratch` regions (§3.4), with heap fallback and a no-MMU kernel mode | M4/M5 |\n| One-growable-on-top non-moving growth | Not adopted; `StableVector` instead | M6 |\n| Grow-only vs grow-shrink split | `StableVector`, monotone lengths feed BCE | M6/M9 |\n| Root inference by monomorphisation | **Rejected** (whole-program, fragile). NLL + E1–E4 instead | — |\n| Construction into destination / guaranteed NRVO | NIR destination places + guaranteed RVO for single-named-result functions | M2 (RVO), M7 |\n| Inline variable-size layouts | Future opt-in `@flat` types (OD-14) | post-M9 |\n| Relative references + verifier | Future library feature for zero-copy formats | post-M9 |\n| Type-safe pools | `Pool<T>`/`SlotMap<T>` with generations | M6 |\n| BCE with index types, 2^48 size cap, `checkfree` | Adopt the size cap in the spec now (all `len ≤ 2^48`), BCE + `@checkfree` in M9 | M0 (spec) / M9 |\n| `return … from` | Not adopted; `Result` + `?` | — |\n| Share-nothing workers | Possible library model later | post-M9 |\n\n**Spec rule adopted now (from Goose BCE):** every object, slice and collection\nlength is `≤ 2^48 − 1` elements. Allocators reject larger requests with `AllocError`. This makes\n`len * sizeof(T)` overflow-free for `sizeof(T) < 2^15` and lets index arithmetic\non `usize` be proven non-overflowing.\n",
    496 =>
        "# Optimisations — preconditions and checks\n\nStatus: **normative** from M9c (2026-10-25); §3 from M9d; §4 from M9e. Master prompt §16: an optimisation needs safety preconditions, tests,\ncorrectness checks, debugging support and measurements, against a correct baseline. This document states each\noptimisation's preconditions, which must hold for every program the checker accepts, and how the toolchain checks\nthat the optimisation changes nothing a program can observe. Results are in `milestone-9.md`.\n\n## 1. Rules for every optimisation\n\n- It must not change what a program does: its output, exit status, panics (which one, and where), and its\n  allocations as counted by the runtime. A removed check must be one that cannot fail. The one exception is\n  placement (§4), which removes allocations of a placement-transparent allocator by design.\n- It has a switch that turns it off (`--no-…`), so that the unoptimised build stays available as an oracle and for\n  measuring the gain.\n- It is decided from facts the compiler already proves (types, loans, checked arithmetic), never from heuristics\n  about what a program \"probably\" does, and never by weakening a check of `ownership.md`.\n- The interpreter checks it on every program it runs, where it can (below), and the run corpus and the fuzzer run\n  with it both on and off.\n- Its output is deterministic, like the rest of the compiler.\n\n## 2. Bounds-check elimination (M9c)\n\n### 2.1 What is removed\nA `BoundsCheck(i < L)` (`intermediate-representation.md`; the index check of `type-system.md` §16.3) whose condition\nholds every time the statement is reached. The C backend then emits no `cee_check_index` for it. Nothing else is\nremoved: range checks (`a[lo..hi]`), length checks (`copyFrom`), overflow checks and every other panic stay. Because a\nremoved check never fails, which panic a program reaches, and where, does not change.\n\nThe analysis is `src/backend/check_elimination.cee`, which also decides overflow checks (§3). It runs on each function's final NIR (after drop\nelaboration), and its result is the set of checks it proved. The C backend and the interpreter consult that set.\nThe NIR itself is not changed.\n\n### 2.2 Terms and facts\nA **term** is an integer the analysis can name at a program point:\n- the current value of a tracked local (§2.3);\n- an integer constant;\n- the length of a tracked slice, string view, vector or string local.\n\nA **fact** relates two terms: `a = b`, `a ≤ b`, `a < b`. Terms are mathematical integers and facts are integer\nrelations. That is sound because CeeSafe's arithmetic is checked: `+`, `-`, `*` panic rather than wrap (§6), and `as`\nis lossless (§7). A **definition** `x = a op b` records how a local was computed, for `op` among `+`, `-`, `%`, `&`\nand `>>` on unsigned integers. A **condition** `c = a cmp b` records the comparison that produced a `bool` local.\n\n### 2.3 Tracked locals\nA fact may mention only a tracked local:\n- an integer, `bool`, slice or `StringView` local, for its value (and a slice's or view's length);\n- a `Vector` or `String` local, for its length.\n\nA tracked local changes only through an assignment to it, which the analysis sees, or through a mutable loan of its\nown storage: `borrow mut i`, or `push`, `pop`, `clear` and `append`, which take `borrow mut` of a vector. While such a\nloan is live, every other use of the local is rejected (B1–B2), so no new fact about it can be made. After the loan,\nnothing may write the local through a pointer made from it (U2, ADR 0010). A shared loan cannot change it. A cell\n(`RawCell`, `Cell`, `RefCell`, type-system.md §41) is a struct, so never a tracked local, and nothing inside one is a\nfact: its contents change through shared loans. So facts\n\nabout a local end at each mutable `Ref` or `Slice` of a place rooted at it without a dereference, and a value or\nlength read after that is a new fact.\n\nA tracked local's facts end at:\n- an assignment to a place rooted at the local without a dereference;\n- a mu",
    497 =>
        "table borrow of such a place, as above;\n- being a target of `Unpack`;\n- its `StorageDead`.\n\nA local that is moved out is uninitialised until it is assigned again, and that assignment ends its facts.\n\nA fact that ends with a local but also holds of a term known to be equal to it is kept, rewritten to that term.\nSo `t = length(s); m = t - 1; StorageDead(t)` still knows `m < len(s)`.\n\n### 2.4 Where facts come from\n- **Copies**: `x = y` and `x = y as T` (lossless) give `x = y`. `x = length(s)` gives `x = len(s)`. `x = c` gives\n  `x = c`, and `x = c op d` for constants and `+`, `-`, `*` gives `x` its exact value when that is in `x`'s range (M9d).\n  `x = e as T` where `e` is not a term (a field, an element) gives `min(E) ≤ x ≤ max(E)` for `e`'s type `E` (M9d).\n- **Definitions**: `x = a op b` with unsigned operands, unless `x` is itself one of `a`, `b` (as in `i = i + 1`, where\n  the old value is gone). If a definition `y = a op b` of the same terms already holds, `x = y` holds after the\n  assignment, even when `a` or `b` is `x`'s old value: `i = i + 1` after a test of `i + 1 < n` gives `i < n`.\n- **Comparisons**: a `bool` local `c = a cmp b` comparing integers (`<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`) gives two implications:\n  if `c` is true, the relation holds; if false, its negation (`a < b` false is `b ≤ a`). A copy of `c` or `!c`\n  carries them, and a `Branch` on `c` adds the relation of the edge it takes. A `bool` local assigned a constant is\n  known to hold it.\n- **Passed checks**: after `BoundsCheck(i < L)`, `i < L`; after `RangeCheck(lo, hi, L)`, `lo ≤ hi` and `hi ≤ L`;\n  after `LengthCheck(a, b)`, `a = b`.\n\nDerived facts:\n- a < b implies a ≤ b;\n- ≤ and < are transitive (and a ≤ b < c gives a < c);\n- constants compare as integers;\n- equal definitions (the same operation on equal terms) have equal results;\n- x = a − b gives x ≤ a and b ≤ a (the subtraction did not panic), and x < a when b is a constant ≥ 1;\n- t = L − o with a constant c ≥ 1 and c ≤ t gives o < L (some length remains after o);\n- x = a % n gives x < n;\n- x = a & m gives x ≤ m and x ≤ a;\n- x = a >> k gives x ≤ a;\n- x = a + b gives a ≤ x and b ≤ x, and a < x when b is a constant ≥ 1;\n- x = o + i, with i < t (or i ≤ t) and t = L − o for the same `o`, gives x < L (or x ≤ L): an offset plus an index\n  below the remaining length stays below the length.\n- x = a + m and y = b + m (the same `m`) with a ≤ b give x ≤ y, and with a < b, x < y.\n\nA check `BoundsCheck(i < L)` is removed when `i < L` is derived from the facts that hold before it.\n\n### 2.5 The analysis\nForward dataflow over the control-flow graph. The facts at a block's entry are those holding on every incoming edge;\na block not yet reached holds everything. At a join of two paths the analysis keeps:\n- the facts of both paths;\n- an implication \"if `c` is `w`, then F\" of one path, if the other path knows `c` is not `w` (it holds vacuously\n  there) or knows F itself;\n- a comparison or definition F of one path as \"if `c` is not `w`, then F\", if the other path knows `c` is `w`.\n\n`a && b` and `a || b` lower to joins of a path that computed `b` and a path that assigned a constant, so\n`while (i < s.length && s[i] != 0)` and `if (k == 0 || n - k < 4) { return; }` keep what their tests showed.\n\nEvery rule keeps only facts that hold on both paths, so each fact in a block's entry holds on every execution that\nreaches it. Loops are iterated until no entry changes. The join is not a plain intersection, so the iteration is\nbounded (64 visits per block on average). A function that reaches the bound has nothing removed. Implications use a\nlength rather than a compiler temporary known to equal it, since the temporary may be gone where the implication is\nused. Derived facts are computed by saturation, which does not depend on the order facts were found, so the result\nis deterministic.\n\nA proof considers at most **128 facts** (M15 S3); where more hold, the check stays. The cost of saturation grows",
    498 =>
        " with\nthe number of facts, cubically, and a long straight-line function adds one at each step, so a sum of 300 terms took\n17 s to compile before the budget. That covers both this section's index checks and §3's overflow checks. The most\nfacts any proof needs in the repository's programs, benchmarks and libraries, or in CosmoOS's CeeSafe sources, is 44\n(measured 2026-10-30). Their generated C was byte-identical with and without the budget.\n\n### 2.6 Unsafe code\nThe rule of ADR 0010 is the only assumption about `unsafe` code. The unsafe code in this repository (`lib/core`,\n`integrations/cosmoos/**`, `tests/corpus/**`, `programs/`, `benchmarks/`) was audited for writes through a pointer\nmade from a reference after its target was used directly (2026-10-25): none, as recorded in `milestone-9.md` (9c).\n\n### 2.7 Switch and diagnostics\n- On by default. `ceesafec build --no-bce` and `ceesafec run --no-bce` keep every check (`CEmitOptions.BoundsCheckElimination`,\n  `InterpreterOptions.BoundsCheckElimination`).\n- `ceesafec build --explain-bounds-checks` prints each index check of the package, in source order, with\n  `removed` or `kept`.\n\n### 2.8 How it is checked\n- **The interpreter** evaluates every bounds check, removed or not. If a removed one would fail, it stops with an\n  interpreter fault (exit status 70, \"an eliminated bounds check fails\"): a compiler bug. Every interpreter run checks\n  this: the corpus agreement tests, the fault-injection sweep, the property tests and the fuzzer's oracle.\n- **The run corpus** builds every program with the analysis (every configuration) and without it (`clang -O2 no-elim`),\n  so output and panics are compared both ways. So does the fuzzer's oracle.\n- **`tests/corpus/bounds-checks/*.cee`**: snapshots of `--explain-bounds-checks`\n  (`.bce`) for loops that must be proved, and for near misses that must keep their check (a reassigned index, a\n  mutated vector, a borrowed index, `<=` bounds, a loop bounded by another slice's length, a stable vector, whose\n`push` takes only `borrow this`).\n\nStable vectors (`type-system.md` §24.2) are not tracked: their length grows through a shared borrow.\n\n### 2.9 Character boundaries in ASCII text (after M13)\nA slice of a `StringView` (`v[lo..hi]`, type-system §22.2) checks the range and that `lo` and `hi` are character\nboundaries. In text whose bytes are all ASCII (each below 0x80), every index is a boundary, so the boundary check\ncannot fail. When the text is a local every assignment of which is a string literal of ASCII bytes, or a copy of\nsuch a local, the C backend checks the range only (`cee_check_range` for `cee_check_str_range`). The range check stays.\n- The analysis is per function and flow-insensitive, like §5.1's; it is part of `CheckElimination` and runs under the\n  same switch (`--no-bce` keeps the boundary check too). `--explain-bounds-checks` lists each slice of a view with\n  `boundary removed` or `boundary kept`.\n- The interpreter checks boundaries on every slice; if one whose check was removed is not a boundary, it stops with\n  an interpreter fault (\"an eliminated boundary check fails\"), as for §2.8.\n- The run corpus's and the fuzzer's `clang -O2 no-elim` configuration builds without it.\n\n## 3. Overflow-check elimination (M9d)\n\n### 3.1 What is removed\nThe overflow check of an integer `+`, `-` or `*` (`type-system.md` §6) or of a negation, when the exact result is in\nthe range of its type every time the operation runs. The C backend then writes the operation without a check\n(`(T)(a + b)`, with no undefined behaviour, since the exact result fits). Nothing else is removed: division and\nremainder keep their zero-divisor and `MIN / -1` checks, shifts their amount checks, and conversions their range\nchecks. A removed check never fails, so which panic a program reaches, and where, does not change.\n\nIt is part of the same analysis as §2 (`src/backend/check_elimination.cee`), with the same facts at the same program\npoints, and it adds no new kind of fact.",
    499 =>
        " §2's facts treat checked arithmetic as exact; that stays true, since an\noperation whose check is removed was proved not to overflow.\n\n### 3.2 Bounds of a term\nEvery term has a range that holds whatever the facts say: a local's is its type's (`i32`: −2³¹ to 2³¹ − 1), a\nlength's is that of `usize`, and a constant's is itself. From the facts before the operation, a term `a` has:\n- an **upper bound**, the least of `max(u)` over the terms `u` with `a ≤ u` derived (§2.4), and of `max(u) − 1`\n  over those with `a < u` derived;\n- a **lower bound**, the greatest of `min(l)` over the terms `l` with `l ≤ a`, and of `min(l) + 1` over those with\n  `l < a`.\n\nAn operand that is not a term (an element read from memory, a field) has its type's range.\n\n### 3.3 When an operation cannot overflow\nWith the operands' bounds `[lo(a), hi(a)]` and `[lo(b), hi(b)]` and the result type's range `[MIN, MAX]`, the check is\nremoved when one of these holds:\n- **by interval**: every combination of the bounds stays in `[MIN, MAX]`: for `a + b`, `lo(a) + lo(b) ≥ MIN` and\n  `hi(a) + hi(b) ≤ MAX`; for `a - b`, `lo(a) − hi(b) ≥ MIN` and `hi(a) − lo(b) ≤ MAX`; for `a * b`, the four products of\n  the bounds; for `-a`, `−hi(a) ≥ MIN` and `−lo(a) ≤ MAX`;\n- **an unsigned subtraction** `a - b` with `b ≤ a` derived;\n- **an unsigned addition** `a + b` whose sum `s`, added to the facts as the definition `s = a + b` (exact: §2.4's\n  rules for `+` hold of the mathematical sum), has an upper bound of at most `MAX`. That covers an offset plus an\n  index below the remaining length (`to + i < L`, §2.4), which the interval rule does not.\n\nAn operation whose operands are all constants is decided by its exact result alone, whatever the facts (M11, found by\nthe G8c fuzz campaign). Under a condition that is never true, such as `if (1u16 < 0u16)`, the facts contradict and\nevery rule above would hold vacuously. The check would then be removed from code that never runs, which is sound,\nbut the C would hold an overflowing constant expression such as `1 + 2147483647`, which C compilers reject under\n`-Werror`.\n\nThe bound rule is what makes a counter safe: in `for (i in 0..n)` the increment `i + 1` runs after `i < n`, so\n`hi(i) ≤ max(n) − 1` and `i + 1` does not overflow. A loop over a bound held in a constant (`for (s in 0..100)`) is\nthe same.\n\n### 3.4 Switch and diagnostics\n- On by default. `ceesafec build --no-oce` and `ceesafec run --no-oce` keep every overflow check\n  (`CEmitOptions.OverflowCheckElimination`, `InterpreterOptions.OverflowCheckElimination`). `--no-bce` and `--no-oce`\n  are independent.\n- `ceesafec build --explain-overflow-checks` prints each checked `+`, `-`, `*` and negation of the package, in source\n  order, with `removed` or `kept`.\n\n### 3.5 How it is checked\n- **The interpreter** evaluates every operation exactly, removed or not. If a removed one would overflow, it stops\n  with an interpreter fault (exit status 70, \"an eliminated overflow check fails\"). As for §2, every interpreter run\n  checks this.\n- **The run corpus and the fuzzer** build every program without either elimination (`clang -O2 no-elim`) as well as\n  with both, and compare.\n- **`tests/corpus/overflow-checks/*.cee`**: snapshots of `--explain-overflow-checks`\n  (`.oce`) for operations that must be proved (counters, constant bounds, `b ≤ a` subtractions, offsets below a\n  length, widened operands) and near misses that must keep their check (a counter bounded by `<=` the type's\n  maximum, a sum of two unbounded values, a subtraction tested the wrong way, a signed negation that may be `MIN`).\n\n## 4. Placement (M5; on by default from M9e)\n\n### 4.1 What changes\nA `Box.create` site that `PlacementAnalysis` places (`memory-model.md` §5) keeps its value in a slot of the\nfunction's frame instead of the heap. The rules and preconditions are `memory-model.md` §5 and §5.1; they are not\nrepeated here. Since M9e a site in a loop may be placed when its box ends within ",
    500 =>
        "the iteration (§5.1).\n\n### 4.2 What a program can observe\nOutput, exit status, panics and drop timing are unchanged. Allocation counts are not. A placed box is no allocation\nattempt, so the allocation report (`CEESAFE_ALLOC_REPORT`) shows fewer attempts, and fault injection\n(`CEESAFE_FAIL_ALLOC_AT`) cannot make a placed box fail. That is allowed only for an allocator whose contract says\nallocation \"may spuriously succeed\": `SystemHeap` in userland (memory-model.md §5, rule 1d). `FailingAllocator`,\n`KernelHeap` and user-defined allocators are never placed, so counting and failure injection stay exact for them.\nA test that needs every `SystemHeap` attempt counted builds with `--no-placement`.\n\n### 4.3 Switch and diagnostics\n- On by default (M9e). `ceesafec build --no-placement` and `ceesafec run --no-placement` keep every box on the heap\n  (`CEmitOptions.Placement`, `InterpreterOptions.Placement`). `--placement` is still accepted.\n- `ceesafec build --explain-allocations` prints every allocation site of the package with its decision and reason. The\n  libraries' sites are left out, as in §2.7's listings, since the libraries are part of every package.\n\n### 4.4 How it is checked\n- **The interpreter** applies the same decisions. A placed box lives in its own allocation, which ends at the box's\n  drop, so a use after it is a fault. A site whose previous box is still alive when it runs again is a fault (\"a\n  stack-placed box's slot is reused while its previous box is alive\"), since natively the site has one slot.\n- **The run corpus and the fuzzer** build every program with placement (every configuration) and without it\n  (`clang -O2 no-placement`), and compare.\n- **What must hold**: the same output, panics and drop trace with and without placement, never more allocation\n  attempts, and native code that agrees with the interpreter on everything, the allocation report included.\n  `tests/corpus/placement/*.alloc` are reviewed snapshots of `--explain-allocations`.\n\n## 5. Closure-call specialisation (OD-9a; ADR 0019)\n\n### 5.1 What changes\nA call through a closure (`CallClosure`, type-system §36.2) whose closure is *known* becomes a direct call to the\nclosure's body function, which the C compiler may inline. A call that passes a known closure to a function's\nclosure-typed parameter goes to a **variant** of that function: a copy in which that parameter is bound to the\nclosure's body, so the calls through it inside are direct too.\n\n- **Known.** In a function, a local holds a known closure with body `K` when every assignment to it is one of:\n  a reference to field 0 of an environment local every assignment of which is an aggregate whose field 0 is\n  `K`'s address (a closure literal, or an `fn` value's adapter, made in this function); a copy, cast or reborrow of a\n  local holding a known closure with body `K`; or, in a variant, the parameter bound to `K`. Any other assignment\n  makes the local unknown. The analysis is per function, flow-insensitive, and iterated to a fixed point.\n- **Variants.** One per (function, bindings), where the bindings map each bound parameter index to a body. They are\n  made from a worklist in the order the program's functions and their calls are visited, so the result is\n  deterministic. A recursive call with the same known argument goes to the same variant. Generic functions are\n  specialised as their instances (§20.4); a function with no NIR (an `extern`) is never specialised.\n- **Not specialised:** a closure read from a field, an element or a `dyn fn` (§37), a `Box<dyn fn…>`, a closure whose\n  value depends on a branch, and a `Thread.both` argument (called through the runtime, c-backend §2).\n- A variant is the same NIR with call targets fixed: the same statements, checks and frame. Borrow and effect\n  checking run before the pass and are not repeated.\n- **Async instances are not variants** (type-system.md §32.5, ADR 0037). A call with an async closure for an\n  `@effectsOf` parameter needs its callee's inst",
    501 =>
        "ance, which awaits the closure, so it is made always, after drop\n  elaboration and before the effect checker, whatever `--no-closure-specialisation` says. Async functions, instances\n  among them, are not specialised (their frames are laid out once).\n\n### 5.2 What a program can observe\nNothing: output, exit status, panics and allocations are unchanged. Only the C function a call goes to changes, and the\ngenerated C has one more function per variant (`ceeXS<n>_<name>`, c-backend §2.1).\n\n### 5.3 Switch and diagnostics\n- On by default. `ceesafec build --no-closure-specialisation` and `ceesafec run --no-closure-specialisation` keep every\n  closure call indirect (`CEmitOptions.ClosureSpecialisation`, `InterpreterOptions.ClosureSpecialisation`).\n- `ceesafec build --explain-closure-calls` prints each call through a closure with its function and line, and whether it\n  became direct (and to which body) or why not, then each variant made.\n\n### 5.4 How it is checked\n- **The interpreter** runs the specialised NIR when the pass is on, so the corpus's comparison of interpreter and\n  native output covers it.\n- **The run corpus and the fuzzer** build every program with the pass (every configuration) and without it\n  (`clang -O2 no-closure-specialisation`), and compare.\n- **What must hold**: the C of `benchmarks/sort` has no indirect comparator call left, and the run corpus\n  gives the same output with and without the pass. `tests/corpus/closure-calls/*.calls` are reviewed snapshots of\n  `--explain-closure-calls`.\n",
    502 =>
        "# Ownership, Borrowing and Lifetimes — Formal Safety Design (v0)\n\nStatus: Milestone 0 proposal. This document is normative for Milestone 2, unless\nan item is marked *future*. **B1–B5 and E1–E3 are implemented as specified in `lifetimes.md` (M2b),**\nwhich records the v0 simplifications: immutable, once-initialised reference bindings; and B5\nimplemented as \"the receiver's loan starts after the arguments\". Related: `memory-model.md`, `language-spec.md`,\n`intermediate-representation.md`, `unsafe.md` (M8a).\n\n## 0. Scope of the guarantee\n\n**Memory safety** for CeeSafe means: no execution of a well-typed program that\ncontains no `unsafe` code (and links only correctly-specified `unsafe` libraries)\nperforms any of:\n\n1. use-after-free; 2. double free; 3. dereference of a dangling reference;\n4. out-of-bounds access; 5. read of uninitialised memory; 6. type-confused\naccess; 7. invalid ownership transfer; 8. unsynchronised concurrent access where\nat least one access is a write (data race; only once threads exist, M-later).\n\n**Not** included in memory safety, but each with defined behaviour:\n\n| Condition | Defined behaviour |\n|---|---|\n| Integer overflow (`+ - *`, negation, `abs`) | **Panic** in all profiles (OD-6). `wrapping*`, `saturating*`, `checked*` methods available |\n| Division / remainder by zero, `MIN / -1` | Panic |\n| Shift by ≥ bit width | Panic (`wrappingShl` masks) |\n| Out-of-bounds index | Panic (`get(i)` returns `T?`) |\n| Narrowing numeric cast | `as` is **only** allowed where lossless; `truncate<T>(x)`, `checkedCast<T>(x)` explicit |\n| `T?` unwrap of `null` | Panic (`!` postfix operator) |\n| Allocation failure | **Value** (`Result<_, AllocError>`); panicking convenience APIs exist only in the userland profile |\n| Stack overflow | Profile-defined: userland relies on guard page → process termination; kernel profile uses static stack-depth checking (see `profiles.md`) |\n| Resource exhaustion, deadlock, logic errors, leaks via `forget` | Not prevented |\n| Panic | **Abort**, no unwinding (OD-5). Destructors do not run on panic. Userland: print + `abort()`; kernel: CosmoOS `panic()` |\n\nLeaks are memory-safe (as in Rust), but the safe standard library must not leak\nin ordinary use, and no CeeSafe feature may use leaking as a substitute for\nlifetime soundness (master prompt §5.6).\n\n## 1. Vocabulary\n\n- **Place**: a storage location expression: a local, a field of a place\n  (`p.f`), an element of a place (`p[i]`), or the target of a reference\n  (`*r`, written implicitly in surface syntax).\n- **Copy type**: a type whose values may be duplicated bitwise: primitives,\n  `borrow T` (shared), raw pointers, and `struct`/`enum` types all of whose\n  fields are Copy and which declare no `drop` (and are not marked `@noncopy`).\n  Everything else is a **move type** (affine).\n- **Loan**: the creation of a reference to a place. Kinds: `shared`\n  (`borrow T`) and `mut` (`borrow mut T`).\n- **Region**: a set of points in a function's control-flow graph (NIR)\n  during which a loan must remain valid.\n- **Scoped type**: any type that contains a reference (`borrow T`,\n  `borrow mut T`, `Slice<T>`, `StringView`, arena handles, guard-protected\n  handles), transitively, including through generic arguments. A non-scoped type is\n  **free-standing**: it may outlive any particular stack frame.\n- **Owner**: the place that holds a value of a move type. Destroying the owner\n  destroys the value.\n\n## 2. Invariants\n\nThe checker establishes these properties on the NIR (control-flow IR) of every\nfunction. \"At point p\" means on entry to the NIR statement at p.\n\n### Ownership\n\n- **O1 — Unique owner.** Every live value of a move type is held by exactly one\n  initialised place.\n- **O2 — Move de-initialises.** Moving from a place makes it *uninitialised*.\n  A move out of a place is permitted only if the place is a local, or a field\n  path rooted at a local, whose type has no `drop` (partial move). It is\n  never permitted through a reference, an index, or out ",
    503 =>
        "of a `drop`-implementing\n  struct.\n- **O3 — Definite initialisation.** A place may be read, borrowed, or have a\n  field assigned only if it is *definitely initialised* on every path reaching\n  that point. (Forward must-dataflow over NIR. The same analysis implements\n  use-after-move detection.)\n- **O4 — Exactly-once destruction.** At every point where a place goes out of\n  scope (`StorageDead`), and before every assignment that overwrites an\n  initialised place, the compiler inserts `drop(place)` if the place is\n  definitely initialised, or a guarded drop if it is *maybe* initialised (a drop\n  flag). A definitely uninitialised place gets no drop. Drop order: locals\n  in reverse declaration order; struct fields in declaration order; vector\n  elements in index order.\n- **O5 — Partial initialisation.** Struct literals initialise all fields\n  atomically from the program's point of view. Their C lowering constructs\n  in place, and no partially built struct is observable. A partially *moved*\n  struct (O2) drops only its remaining initialised fields.\n\n### Borrowing\n\n- **B1 — XOR aliasing.** At any point, for any place P, the live loans that\n  overlap P are either all shared, or exactly one is `mut`. Overlap is\n  path-prefix based: `a` overlaps `a.f`; `a.f` does not overlap `a.g`;\n  `a[i]` overlaps `a[j]` (no index disjointness in v0).\n- **B2 — Loan validity.** While a loan L of P is live (inside its region),\n  P must not be moved, dropped, go out of scope, or be assigned. If L is\n  shared, P must also not be mutated or mutably borrowed, except for the\n  contents of a `RawCell` in P, which unsafe code may change through the\n  cell's pointer (type-system.md §41.1; ADR 0084). If L is `mut`,\n  P must not be accessed at all except through L.\n- **B3 — Liveness regions (NLL).** A loan's region is the smallest set of\n  CFG points containing every point at which some reference derived from the\n  loan (transitively, through reborrows and copies of shared refs) may still be\n  used. It is computed by backward liveness over NIR, then by solving outlives\n  constraints. A shared reference copied into another variable propagates the loan.\n- **B4 — Reborrowing.** `borrow mut T` passed to a `borrow mut T` parameter\n  is implicitly reborrowed. The original is unusable until the reborrow dies\n  and becomes usable again afterwards (no move).\n- **B5 — Two-phase borrows.** For method calls `recv.m(args)` with a\n  `borrow mut this` receiver, the mutable loan of `recv` is *reserved* while\n  `args` are evaluated (permitting shared reads) and *activated* at the call.\n  Required for `v.push(v.length)`. ADR 0073 §2.2 extends it to every `mut p` argument, whose later arguments may\n  only read `p` (`lifetimes.md` §5).\n\n### Escape (lifetime) rules\n\n- **E1 — No escape beyond the owner.** A value of scoped type may flow only to:\n  (a) locals of the same function; (b) callee arguments; (c) the function's\n  return value, if the elision rule E2 links it to a parameter; (d) fields of\n  scoped *local* aggregates (tuples/anonymous temporaries), which then become\n  scoped.\n- **E2 — Return elision.** A function returning a scoped type must have exactly one\n  `borrow`/`borrow mut` parameter (including `this`), or must annotate the\n  source: `fn pick(a: borrow T, b: borrow T) -> borrow(a) T`. The returned\n  value's region is constrained to be within the named parameter's loan at the call\n  site. A function with no borrow parameters cannot return a scoped type, except\n  `static` data (string literals, `static let`), which have the `static` region.\n- **E3 — Scoped fields only with region parameters.** In v0, user `struct`/`enum`\n  declarations could not contain scoped types. Since M6x (OD-3, ADR 0006) a struct\n  or enum may hold scoped fields if it declares region parameters\n  (`struct Parser<region src> { input: StringView<src> }`), and generic instances\n  with scoped type arguments are scoped themselves (`type-system.md` §25.3–§25.4).\n  Region valu",
    504 =>
        "es and pool boxes still stay in locals.\n- **E4 — No scoped globals or heap.** Scoped values may not be stored in\n  `static`s, constants, `Box`, pools, or arrays. Since M6x, `Vector`,\n  `StableVector`, `SlotMap` and region memory may hold scoped values; the container\n  is then scoped and carries their loans (`type-system.md` §25.4, §25.7).\n\nE1–E4 deliberately trade expressiveness for an implementation of tractable size\n(see \"Why not full Rust lifetimes\" below).\n\n### Regions (arenas) and guards\n\n- **R1 — Allocation borrows the region.** `arena.alloc(...)` takes `borrow this`\n  and returns a scoped value carrying a *shared loan of the arena*. Therefore:\n  - the arena cannot be dropped, moved, reset, or mutably borrowed while any\n    allocation from it is live (B2);\n  - arena allocations cannot escape the arena's scope (E1–E4).\n- **R2 — Trivially-destructible contents (v0).** Arena allocation is\n  permitted only for types with no `drop` (transitively). Bulk release is\n  then semantically equivalent to individual destruction. Arena-with-drop-list is\n  *future* (OD-8).\n- **R3 — Pinned scoped bindings.** `using x = e;` declares a binding that cannot\n  be moved, reassigned, or returned; it is dropped exactly at the end of the\n  enclosing block. Arenas, lock guards and quiescence guards are normally bound\n  with `using`. (Types may require `using` by declaration: `@scopedOnly`.)\n- **R4 — Guard-protected borrows.** An API may return values scoped to a\n  guard (`table.lookup(borrow guard, key) -> Obj?` where `Obj` is scoped to\n  `guard`). By B2/E1 these cannot outlive the guard. This is the mechanism for\n  epoch/quiescence-protected kernel lookups (`cosmoos/kernel-memory-and-lifetimes.md`).\n\n### Heap\n\n- **H1 — Box ownership.** `Box<T, A>` is a move type that owns exactly one\n  allocation of `T` obtained from allocator `A`. Its drop runs `T`'s drop, then\n  `A.free`. `A` must be a free-standing *allocator type* (§3 of\n  `memory-model.md`). Allocations from non-free-standing allocators are scoped\n  values (R1).\n- **H2 — Collections.** `Vector<T, A>` owns its buffer. Element access returns\n  loans of the vector (`borrow this`/`borrow mut this`), so growth (which takes\n  `borrow mut this`) is rejected while element borrows are live (B1/B2).\n  Stable references into collections use `SlotMap<T>`/`Handle<T>` with\n  generation validation (library type, M6).\n- **H3 — No owning cycles.** Only `Box` and owning collections own, and their\n  ownership graph is a forest by construction (O1). Back-references and\n  graphs use indices, handles, or arenas.\n\n### Threads (M13 G4; ADR 0016, type-system.md §38)\n\n- **T1 — Only `Send` values cross.** A value reaches another thread only by being moved into a spawned closure, or\n  returned from one through `join`. Each is checked `Send` (N0334). A `Send` value holds no loan and no raw pointer,\n  so after the move the spawner reaches nothing the new thread owns.\n- **T2 — Sharing is through `Arc` of `Share` types.** The only safe way for two threads to reach the same memory is\n  an `Arc<T>` with `T: Share`, which lends only `borrow T`. A v1 type can be changed through a shared reference only\n  if it is one of the library's `@threadSafe` types: `Mutex<T>` (whose guard lends `borrow mut T` to one thread at a\n  time) and the atomics (whose operations are atomic), or holds a `RawCell` or a `StableVector`, and such a type is\n  not `Share` (type-system.md §38.1, §41). So two threads can write the same memory only through a mutex or an\n  atomic: there is no data race in safe code.\n\n- **T3 — The library's `@threadSafe` types** keep T2 in their `unsafe` code. `Mutex.lock` takes the platform lock\n  before lending. The guard's drop releases it, after the guard's loan has ended (B-rules). `Arc`'s count changes\n  only by atomic operations, and only the owner that drops it to zero destroys the value. A spawned thread is\n  joined before `main` returns, so no thread outlives the values `main` dropped ",
    505 =>
        "(O2). `@threadSafe` is accepted only\n  in `lib/std`, so user code cannot claim it (N0290).\n- **T4 — Fork-join borrows end with the call** (`Thread.both`, §38.6; ADR 0018). A closure passed to `Thread.both`\n  may reach the caller's values through references, because it runs only during the call and the call returns only\n  after the thread running it has ended. Both closures' loans are live for the whole call, so the B-rules keep them\n  from reaching one value in conflicting ways. A value reached from both threads is reached shared by both and is\n  `Share`; one reached mutably is reached by one thread only and is `Send`. So T2's conclusion holds: there is no data\n  race in safe code.\n\n### Async tasks (L1; ADR 0031, type-system.md §40)\n\n- **A1 — Frames never move.** A task's frame is placed once (§40.6) and freed only after the task returns, never while\n  it is suspended. A reference into a frame, held across an `await`, therefore points to live storage, and a buffer\n  lent to the kernel by a suspended task stays valid until the kernel is done with it.\n- **A2 — No task outlives what it borrows.** A task is awaited where it is called, or spawned into a scope that\n  returns only after its children (§40.5). Its loans are those of a call (an awaited task) or of an element of a\n  scoped collection (a child), so the B-rules end them only after the task returns.\n- **A3 — Tasks stay on their thread.** A task never moves to another thread, so its frame needs no `Send`. A value\n  crosses threads only through T1–T4 (`Async.blocking` checks its captures as `Thread.both` does).\n- **A4 — Cancellation destroys nothing.** Cancelling sets a flag that the library's waiting operations turn into\n  `Err(AsyncError.Cancelled)`. The task then returns by ordinary code, and its locals are dropped in order. There is\n  no path on which a suspended frame is dropped.\n\n### Unsafe\n\n- **U1 — Capabilities.** Raw pointer dereference, pointer arithmetic, calls to\n  `extern` or `unsafe fn`, `transmute`, access to `static mut`, MMIO and inline\n  assembly are allowed only inside `unsafe { }` or an `unsafe fn`.\n- **U2 — Contract.** Every `unsafe` block must preserve O1–O5, B1–B5, E1–E4,\n  R1–R4, H1–H3 and T1–T3 *as observed by safe code*. Every `unsafe fn` and every safe\n  function containing `unsafe` must state its contract in a `/// Safety:` doc\n  comment. A lint enforces that the comment exists.\n- **U3 — Raw pointers are inert.** Raw pointers (`*T`, `*mut T`) are Copy,\n  carry no loan, and are ignored by the borrow checker. Converting a raw pointer\n  to a reference (`unsafe { Ptr.toBorrow(p) }`; `type-system.md` §27.3) creates a reference with an\n  *unconstrained* region, which must be immediately bounded by a safe wrapper's\n  signature (as with Rust's unbounded lifetimes; this is the main hazard in\n  unsafe code).\n\n## 3. How the compiler rejects invalid programs\n\nPass order (see `architecture.md`):\n\n1. **Type check (HIR)**: kinds (Copy / move / scoped), E2 annotations,\n   E3/E4 structural checks, `unsafe` context (U1), `using` restrictions (R3),\n   arena element constraint (R2).\n2. **NIR construction**: explicit `StorageLive/StorageDead`, `Move`, `Copy`,\n   `Borrow(kind, place)`, two-phase reservation markers, and the `drop` sites\n   still unelaborated.\n3. **Definite-initialisation / move analysis** (O2–O3): forward dataflow, with a\n   per-place bitset of {init, uninit, maybe}.\n4. **Borrow check** (B1–B5, E1–E2, R1, R4):\n   a. compute liveness of every reference-typed local;\n   b. generate loans at `Borrow` statements and region variables for every\n      reference type occurrence;\n   c. generate outlives constraints from assignments, calls (signature\n      instantiation), and returns (E2);\n   d. solve regions as sets of points (NLL-style fixpoint);\n   e. for every access, report a conflict if any live loan in the solved region at\n      that point conflicts (B1/B2).\n5. **Drop elaboration** (O4): inserts static drops and drop",
    506 =>
        " flags. Runs after the\n   borrow check, so drops are checked as accesses in step 4 using the\n   *unelaborated* drop sites.\n\nDiagnostics name the loan's creation point, the conflicting access, and the\nlater use that keeps the loan alive, as three labelled spans. This is the\nsingle most important usability feature of the checker (see `diagnostics.md`).\n\nExample compile-fail cases (each becomes a test in `tests/corpus/compile-fail/`):\n\n```ceesafe\nfn dangling() -> borrow i32 {         // error E2: no borrow parameter to derive from\n    let x = 5;\n    return borrow x;\n}\n\nfn useAfterMove(b: Box<Node>) -> void {\n    consume(b);\n    print(b.value);                    // error O3: `b` used after move at line 2\n}\n\nfn invalidate(v: borrow mut Vector<i32>) -> void {\n    let first = v[0];                  // Copy of i32: fine\n    let r = borrow v[0];               // shared loan of *v\n    v.push(1);                         // error B1: mutable loan while `r` live\n    print(r);                          //   ... `r` used here\n}\n\nfn arenaEscape() -> Slice<u8> {        // error E2: no borrow parameter\n    using arena = Arena.create(SystemHeap)!;\n    return arena.allocArray<u8>(16)!;\n}\n```\n\n## 4. Why not full Rust lifetimes in v0\n\nNamed lifetime parameters on aggregates, higher-ranked bounds and variance are\nwhere most of Rust's borrow-checker complexity and annotation burden live.\nE1–E4 keep the analysis entirely **intraprocedural** (signatures summarise\ncallees completely), with no lifetime parameters on types. That is enough for:\nborrowing parameters, slices, string views, iterators over a borrowed collection\n(as a local), arena-scoped computations, and guard-scoped kernel lookups. It is\nnot enough for: long-lived parsers holding `StringView`s, structs of\nreferences, or zero-copy data structures. Those are OD-3, designed in M6f\n(`od3-design.md`) so that v0 programs remain valid.\n\n## 5. Behaviours requiring runtime checks\n\n| Check | Elidable when | Pass |\n|---|---|---|\n| Array/slice bounds | Index proven `< len` by range analysis (M9) | Lowering emits `ceesafe_check_index` |\n| Integer overflow | Value ranges proven (M9) | `ceesafe_add_i32` etc. |\n| Division by zero / `MIN/-1` | Divisor is a non-zero, non-minus-one constant | — |\n| Shift width | Constant shift amount | — |\n| Optional unwrap `!` | Flow-typing after `!= null` | — |\n| Drop flags | Static init state known (most cases) | Drop elaboration |\n| Allocation failure | Never elided (it is a value) | — |\n| Handle generation check (`SlotMap`) | Never in v0 | Built-in (M6e; library in M8) |\n| Kernel: stack depth | Static call-graph bound (kernel profile) | Link-time report |\n\n## 6. Testing obligations (M2 acceptance)\n\n- Every invariant above has at least two compile-fail tests and one compile-pass\n  test that is \"close\" to failing (for example NLL cases where a loan ends\n  before the conflicting access).\n- Mutation suite: for each compile-pass test, automated mutants (swap statement\n  order, remove a scope, duplicate a move) whose expected verdict is computed\n  independently, by hand-labelling or a reference interpreter, not by the\n  checker itself.\n- Generated C for compile-pass tests is run under ASan + UBSan in CI\n  (supporting evidence only).\n",
    507 =>
        "# CeeSafe Compilation Profiles for CosmoOS (+ Freestanding Runtime Design)\n\nStatus: C0 proposal (deliverables 3 and 7); §4, the freestanding profile, is normative from M8c. Facts are cited from\n`docs/research/cosmoos-platform-audit.md` **[PA §n]** and\n`docs/research/cosmoos-kernel-audit.md` **[KA §n]**.\n\nA *profile* fixes three things: (1) the language features permitted, (2) the\nruntime library linked, (3) the C flags and link recipe. Profiles are selected\nwith `ceesafec build --profile <p> --target <t>`. A profile is **not** \"userland\nwith the standard library switched off\" (prompt §2). Each feature is either\nallowed or not, and the list below is normative.\n\n## 1. Profile matrix\n\n| Feature | `hosted` (Linux/macOS dev) | **A: `cosmoos-user`** | **B: `cosmoos-kernel`** | `freestanding` (generic; M8c, §4) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Libraries | core + std | core + std(cosmoos) | core + kernel | core |\n| Default allocator type param | `SystemHeap` (malloc) | `SystemHeap` (CosmoOS libc malloc) | **none**: must be explicit | none |\n| Panicking allocation helpers (`!` on `AllocError`, `create().orPanic`) | allowed | allowed | **lint error** | lint error |\n| `f32`/`f64` | yes | yes (no `<math.h>` in libc: the math methods are portable C in `rt.h`, type-system §35.8) | **forbidden** (`-mgeneral-regs-only`) [KA §7–8]; enforced by `ceesafec --no-float` (N0297, §35.9) | target-defined |\n| Threads, `Send`/`Sync` | later | via `cosmo_thread_*` only [PA impl 4] | kernel threads via KThread wrapper (C7) | no |\n| Async (`async fn`, `Async`; type-system.md §40) | yes (L1 A1–A2; `Async.runWorkers`, ADR 0038) | planned (L1 A4) | the freestanding executor over the kernel's hooks (ADR 0039) | the executor over the environment's hooks, §4.2 (ADR 0039) |\n| Native stack value limit | 64 KiB | 64 KiB | **512 B** per value; **2 KiB per frame** (CosmoOS `-Wframe-larger-than=2048`, error) [KA impl 21] | configurable |\n| Whole-call-chain stack budget | — | — | report + error against **16 KiB** (shared with IRQs) [KA §8] | configurable |\n| Scratch regions | reserved VM | reserved VM via `mmap` | fixed per-thread buffer, explicit checks | fixed buffer |\n| Panic | stderr + `abort()` | stderr + `abort()` via libc | CosmoOS `panic()` | user-provided `ceesafe_panic` |\n| Context effects (`@context`, `@effects`, `sleep`/`alloc`) | checked | checked | checked | checked (C7a, type-system §32: obligations arise only where declared) |\n| `extern \"C\"` imports | any | libc public headers only (L1, OD-15) | bindings file (exports only for modules) | any |\n| `static var`, raw pointers, MMIO | unsafe | unsafe | unsafe (+ MMIO types) | unsafe |\n| TLS (`@threadLocal`) | yes | yes (`__thread` works) [PA §4] | **no** (no TLS in the kernel) | no |\n| Recursion | yes | yes | allowed, but makes the stack bound unprovable → the function must be `@unboundedStack` and is excluded from IRQ/atomic contexts | yes |\n\n## 2. Profile A — `cosmoos-user`\n\n### 2.1 Target and code generation [PA §3, impl 1]\n- Triples: `x86_64-unknown-none-elf`, `aarch64-unknown-none-elf`. LP64, max\n  alignment 16, `long double` = 16 bytes (unused by CeeSafe), `char` signedness\n  differs (irrelevant: CeeSafe never uses `char`).\n- C flags: CosmoOS `USER_CFLAGS` exactly (`build/toolchain.mk:27-61`,\n  `libc/libc.mk:12-18`): `-std=c11 -ffreestanding -nostdinc -fno-pic -fno-pie\n  -fno-stack-protector`, no unwind tables, CosmoOS warning set with `-Werror`.\n  CeeSafe does **not** maintain its own copy of these flags. It reads them from the CosmoOS\n  build (`make print-USER_CFLAGS`-style query or `compile_commands.json`; see\n  `build-integration.md`).\n- Link: static ET_EXEC at 0x400000 with `userland/user.ld`, `crt0.o`, `libc.a`.\n  No `.init_array` support exists, so **CeeSafe must never emit constructors**. The\n  `.note.cosmo` note from `crt0` must be present; without it the binary silently\n  runs as a Linux binary [PA impl 6]. The CeeSafe build checks the note with `llvm-readelf -n`.\n\n### 2.2 ",
    508 =>
        "Runtime: `runtime/cosmoos/user/`\n- `main(int argc, char** argv, char** envp)` is generated. It wraps argv into\n  `Slice<StringView>` without copying, calls CeeSafe `main`, runs no `atexit`, and returns the\n  exit code. Deterministic cleanup has completed when CeeSafe `main` returns.\n- `SystemHeap` = `malloc`/`free`/`realloc`. Over-aligned (`> 16`) layouts are\n  served by over-allocating and storing the original pointer in a header (no\n  `aligned_alloc` in libc) [PA impl 3].\n- Arenas and scratch regions take chunks from `mmap(MAP_ANONYMOUS)` (a public libc\n  function, consistent with L1).\n- Console/IO: `write`/`read` on fds. Files: `open/read/write/close/stat/dirent`.\n  Processes: `spawn*` + `waitpid` (no fork/exec). **Implemented in C3** (type-system.md §26,\n  `runtime/hosted/ceesafe_rt_io.c`): files (`open/read/write/close/unlink`), TCP over IPv4, `spawnvp` +\n  `waitpid`, and argv; `stat`/`dirent` and time are not yet. Time: `clock_gettime`\n  (monotonic + realtime). Sockets: CosmoOS-shaped socket API. Absent in libc:\n  math, poll/select, DNS, `setjmp`, sigaltstack [PA impl 2]. CeeSafe's std\n  omits them in v0.\n- **Async** (type-system.md §40; ADR 0040): the hosted executor, with no reactor. With no task ready it waits on its\n  own `cosmo_cond_t`, timed to the earliest deadline, and another thread's wake signals it; channels use\n  `cosmo_mutex_t` and `cosmo_cond_t`. CosmoOS has no `poll` and no `fcntl` to make a descriptor non-blocking (it\n  waits or not per call, `MSG_DONTWAIT`, or through its own asynchronous I/O), so a descriptor counts as ready, as\n  consoles and files do on Windows: a console write or an `AsyncConsole` read may block its worker for as long as it\n  takes. Opening an async socket (listen, accept, connect) fails with `ENOSYS`, which the library reports as\n  `AsyncError.Io`. `integrations/cosmoos/c1.py --corpus` runs every run-pass and run-fail program in the guest but the\n  three that use async sockets.\n- **L1 policy** (OD-15): the CeeSafe runtime calls **only public libc functions**, never\n  raw syscalls, until the CosmoOS owner rules on whether CeeSafe's runtime counts as \"library\".\n\n### 2.3 Alternative A-musl (not recommended for C1)\nCosmoOS already builds musl 1.2.5 + compiler-rt and ships musl programs under\nthe Linux personality [PA impl 7]. CeeSafe could target it for richer POSIX\ncoverage, but it is not \"native CosmoOS\" (Invariant 7) and is kept as a\nlater option (OD-16). The C backend needs no changes for it, only a different\nprofile runtime.\n\n## 3. Profile B — `cosmoos-kernel`\n\n### 3.1 Permitted language features (normative)\n- All of core: structs, enums, generics, slices, `Option`/`Result`, borrowing,\n  `Box<T, A>` with an **explicit** kernel allocator type, `using`, `defer`.\n- No floats, no TLS, no implicit allocation (no default `A`; no allocating\n  operators; string formatting writes into caller-provided buffers or explicit\n  `KernelHeap` strings).\n- No panicking allocation. Panics in general are **discouraged**: the kernel\n  profile lint `kernel-panic-sites` reports every panic site (bounds, overflow,\n  unwrap). `@noPanic` on a function makes any remaining panic site a compile\n  error. That is the kernel analogue of Goose's `checkfree` (goose.md §9.9).\n- Context effects enforced (`kernel-memory-and-lifetimes.md` §6).\n- Two sub-modes: `--kernel-link=builtin` (internal APIs allowed, KQ-5) and\n  `--kernel-link=module` (exported symbols only; generates `COSMO_MODULE`\n  metadata, `init/shutdown`) [KA impl 23].\n\n### 3.2 Code generation [KA §7, impl 20–22]\n- clang only. `-std=c11 -ffreestanding -nostdinc`, `-Werror` with the kernel\n  warning set, `-Wframe-larger-than=2048`, `-fno-strict-aliasing`, non-PIC.\n  - x86-64: `-mcmodel=kernel -mno-red-zone -mgeneral-regs-only`.\n  - AArch64: `-mcmodel=small -mgeneral-regs-only -mno-outline-atomics -ffixed-x18`.\n- Flags are taken from CosmoOS's build per **configuration and arch**.\n  `CONFIG_*` macros change inline header behaviour, so CeeSafe objects",
    509 =>
        " are never shared between debug\n  and release builds [KA impl 22].\n- **Kernel struct layouts come from the kernel's own headers** (ADR 0008; [KA impl 26]). CeeSafe never\n  writes down the layout of `spinlock_t`, `struct kobject`, `struct cosmoboot_info` or any other C\n  type. It imports them: an `extern \"C\"` block with `@header(\"kernel/bootinfo.h\")` declares\n  `struct cosmoboot_info { fb_phys: u64; … }` with only the fields CeeSafe uses, by their C names\n  (type-system §31; implemented in C6b, whose `c6/fbvalid.cee` does exactly this).\n  Generated C `#include`s the headers of the exact module ABI (currently 11) and accesses the real\n  members, compiled with the build's own flags, so the layout is always the one of this\n  configuration and architecture. Each declared field is checked at C compile time (it exists, and\n  has exactly the declared type), so a configuration that changes a field fails to build, naming it,\n  instead of miscompiling. Imported types are used behind pointers and references only, since their\n  size is the C compiler's, not CeeSafe's. `@repr(C)` mirrors remain for types CeeSafe itself defines and\n  exports (type-system §28.5).\n- The mechanism is not CosmoOS-specific (ADR 0008): the same declarations import Linux kernel or C\n  library structs from those platforms' headers (§5).\n\n### 3.3 Freestanding runtime: `runtime/core/` + `runtime/cosmoos/kernel/`\n\n| Runtime need | Freestanding core (no libc) | CosmoOS kernel binding |\n|---|---|---|\n| `ceesafe_panic(kind, src)` | plain `extern` (no weak symbols), which the profile must define | calls CosmoOS `panic(\"ceesafe: %s at %s:%u\")` |\n| `ceesafe_mem_copy/move/set/cmp` | `__builtin_mem*` (may lower to calls) | kernel exports `memcpy/memmove/memset/memcmp` (string functions exported [KA impl 20]) |\n| Checked arithmetic | `static inline` in `rt.h`, no libcalls | same |\n| Allocation | none (allocators are library types over externs) | `kmalloc/kfree`, `kmem_cache_*`, … via bindings |\n| Source locations | static `const` tables | same (in `.rodata`; no relocations beyond the allowed set) |\n| Stack metadata | `-fstack-usage` output parsed by `ceesafec` | call-graph depth check against 16 KiB |\n\nThe core runtime is ~300 lines of header plus a few C functions. It has **no global\nmutable state** (so no TLS or per-CPU questions) and **no constructors**.\n\nAcceptance (C4): `llvm-nm -u` on a kernel-profile object lists only symbols\nfrom the profile's allowlist (`memcpy`, `memset`, `memmove`, `memcmp`,\n`panic`, plus bound kernel APIs); `llvm-readelf -r` shows only relocation types\naccepted by the module loader [KA §6.4]; there are no `.init_array`, `.tdata`/`.tbss`,\n`.eh_frame` or COMMON symbols.\n\n## 4. The `freestanding` profile (M8c; normative)\n\n`ceesafec build --profile freestanding` compiles a package for an environment with no C library and\nno operating system: a kernel, firmware, or a module that another runtime links. `--profile hosted`\nis the default and is what every earlier milestone built. (`cosmoos-user` is hosted with CosmoOS's\nflags, C1–C3; `cosmoos-kernel` is C4–C8 and builds on this profile.)\n\n### 4.1 What freestanding code may use\nEverything in the language that needs no runtime service: integers, `bool`, structs, enums,\narrays, slices, `StringView`, optionals, `Result` and `AllocError`, references, generics and\ninterfaces, `unsafe` and raw pointers (type-system §27), the C FFI (§28), `FixedBufferAllocator`\nover caller-provided storage, `Ptr`, `Slice.fromRaw`, and `truncate`.\n\nThese need a hosted runtime and are rejected in freestanding code (N0293), wherever they are\nnamed (a type, a call, a struct literal):\n\n| Name | Why |\n|---|---|\n| `Console`, `Process` | standard streams, exit, arguments and processes are operating-system services |\n| `File`, `TcpListener`, `TcpStream`, `IoError` | files and sockets |\n| `SystemHeap`, `FailingAllocator` | there is no system heap; `FailingAllocator` wraps it |\n| `Box`, `PoolBox`, `Pool`, `Arena`, `Scratch`, `Ve",
    510 =>
        "ctor`, `String`, `SlotMap`, `StableVector` | each allocates from one of the two allocators above (`Scratch` from the runtime's heap-backed stack) |\n| `Async.scope`, `Async.blocking`, `Async.runWorkers`, `s.spawnSend`; lib/std's async I/O | a scope's children come from the system heap (use `Async.scopeIn`), and there are no threads or descriptors (ADR 0039) |\n\nAsync tasks run (ADR 0039): `Async.run`, `await`, `Async.scopeIn` with a user allocator, `spawn`, `race`, `all`,\n`shield`, cancellation, `Async.boxed` with a user allocator, lib/core's `Timer` and `AsyncSignal`. The `IoError` type\nmay be named, since `AsyncError` carries it, but no value of it can be made.\n\nA library function that takes or returns one of these (lib/core's `VectorBytes.pushU8BE`, which takes a `Vector`) is\nN0293 at its call too: its signature names a hosted type, and the library's own diagnostics are not reported.\n\nExempt, since they need no runtime: `Box.createIn` with a user allocator (C5, type-system §30) and `Box.intoRaw` / `Box.fromRaw`\n(C7d, type-system §34.2).\n\nA `main` function is N0293 too: there is no C `main` to call it. A freestanding package is entered\nthrough its `@export` functions (§28.4; custom entry points are M8d). Allocators that a freestanding\nenvironment provides (CosmoOS's `KernelHeap`, C5) will come with user-defined allocators.\n\n### 4.2 Runtime and the panic hook\n- The program is the generated `main.c`, `ceesafe/rt.h`, and the core runtime `ceesafe_rt_core.c` compiled\n  with `CEE_FREESTANDING` defined, which leaves out everything that needs a platform service (output,\n  exit, heap accounting, arenas, scratch frames, pools and collections). What remains has no\n  mutable global state and no constructors: the fixed-buffer allocator, UTF-8 validation, and the\n  checked arithmetic in `rt.h`.\n- **`ceesafe_panic` is the environment's.** Generated code calls\n  `void ceesafe_panic(cee_panic_kind kind, cee_loc loc)` (declared in `ceesafe/rt.h`) for every panic:\n  overflow, bounds, unwrap, and the rest. The freestanding runtime does not define it; the code that\n  links the object must, and it must not return (panic = abort, OD-5). `kind` is an\n  `cee_panic_kind`; `loc.file` is the CeeSafe source path (a zero-terminated string), `loc.line` and\n  `loc.column` 1-based. The core's `ceesafe_panic_reason(kind)` gives the same text the hosted runtime\n  prints (\"integer overflow\", …). CosmoOS's kernel binding is `integrations/cosmoos/c4/ceesafe_rt_kernel.c` (C4).\n- The C compiler may itself emit calls to `memcpy`, `memmove`, `memset` and `memcmp` (struct copies,\n  zeroing), as it may in any freestanding C (C11 §4 and the compilers' documentation); the\n  environment provides them. (On a Darwin host, LLVM makes a zeroing `memset` a call of `bzero`; the CosmoOS\n  triples do not.)\n- **The async executor's hooks** (ADR 0039). A program that uses async also gets `ceesafe_rt_async.c`, compiled with\n  `CEE_FREESTANDING`: the hosted executor's portable part, which has no global state. The environment defines four\n  functions, declared in `ceesafe/rt.h`:\n  - `uint64_t ceesafe_env_now_ms(void)`: a monotonic clock in milliseconds;\n  - `void ceesafe_env_idle(void *executor, uint64_t deadline_ms)`: block until the deadline (`UINT64_MAX`: none) or a\n    wake;\n  - `void ceesafe_env_wake(void *executor)`: end that idling, from any thread or an interrupt handler, keeping a wake\n    that comes before the idle;\n  - `void **ceesafe_env_slot(void)`: the calling thread's slot for its executor, since the profile has no\n    thread-local storage. `Async.run` keeps its executor in its own frame and names it there.\n  A child's frame comes from `Async.scopeIn`'s allocator. With no task able to run and nothing to wait for,\n  the executor panics with `CEE_PANIC_ASYNC`. It finds deadlines by walking its parked tasks, since it has no heap of\n  its own. `tools/freestanding_check.py`'s `freestanding/async` runs it over a host harness of the four hooks, and\n  CosmoOS's self-test ",
    511 =>
        "`cee-async` runs it in the kernel.\n\n### 4.3 `--emit obj`\n`ceesafec build --emit obj -o out.o` compiles each C file with `-c` (and the flags given with\n`--cflag`) and joins the objects into one relocatable object: with `<linker> -r` when `--ld <linker>`\nis given (`ld.lld` for the ELF cross builds, as CosmoOS links), otherwise with the C compiler's `-r`\n(passing it the `--target=…` flag). It works in\nboth profiles; with `--profile freestanding` it is the normal output.\n\nFreestanding builds always add `-ffreestanding -fno-stack-protector -fno-asynchronous-unwind-tables\n-fno-unwind-tables` (c-backend §1).\n\n### 4.4 The symbol allowlist (acceptance, M8 criterion 1)\nThe undefined symbols of a freestanding object (`llvm-nm -u`) are only:\n`ceesafe_panic`, `memcpy`, `memmove`, `memset`, `memcmp`, the functions the package declares in\n`extern \"C\"` blocks, and, for a program that uses async, the four `ceesafe_env_*` hooks (§4.2). Checked by `tools/freestanding_check.py` for the host and for both CosmoOS triples\n(`x86_64-unknown-none-elf`, `aarch64-unknown-none-elf`).\n\nOn the CosmoOS triples the objects also have (M8e): no constructor or destructor\nsections (`.init_array`, `.fini_array`, `.ctors`, `.dtors`), which CosmoOS never runs; no TLS\n(`.tdata`, `.tbss`), which the kernel lacks; no unwind tables (`.eh_frame`); no COMMON symbols; and no\nmutable global state (`.data` and `.bss` empty) except the program's static items (type-system §39, ADR 0024),\nwhose thread-safety the checker requires (`Share`, or `unsafe` for a `static mut`). Until M14 CeeSafe had no statics,\nso this said \"no mutable global state\" without the exception.\n\n## 5. Other C codebases (Linux and others)\n\nCeeSafe is multi-platform: nothing in the language, the C backend or the profiles above is specific to\nCosmoOS. The freestanding profile (§4), the C FFI (type-system §28) and imported C types (ADR 0008)\nwork against any C codebase, the Linux kernel included:\n- **Flags** come from the codebase's own build, as they come from CosmoOS's Make here (for Linux,\n  from Kbuild for the configured kernel), never copied by hand.\n- **Types** come from its headers, imported as in §3.2, so a field CeeSafe uses is checked against the\n  configured kernel's real layout.\n- **Symbols** are its exports (for Linux, `EXPORT_SYMBOL`/`EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL` for modules), checked like\n  CosmoOS's in C4.\n- **Panics** go to the codebase's own reporting (`ceesafe_panic`, §4.2; for Linux, a `BUG()`-style report\n  or a recoverable error path, decided with that integration).\n\nA Linux kernel-module profile is a future integration (not scheduled); it needs only a binding of\n`ceesafe_panic`, the build's flags, and the headers, as C4 and C5 did for CosmoOS.\n\n`integrations/linux/mq` demonstrates this without a profile (2026-11-10): a misc-device module whose C is generated\nby `ceesafec build --emit c` in the freestanding profile and compiled by Kbuild, on Linux 6.1: AArch64 with gcc 12.2,\nAArch64 with clang 14 on a kernel with lockdep, KASAN and kmemleak, and x86-64 with gcc 12.2.\nWhat it had to supply is what a profile would hold:\n- the freestanding standard headers in the kernel's own types (`<stdint.h>`, `<stdbool.h>`, `<stddef.h>` with\n  `max_align_t`, an empty `<float.h>`), since Linux builds with `-nostdinc` and defines `int64_t` as `long long`\n  where a compiler's `<stdint.h>` may say `long`;\n- `ceesafe_panic` bound to `BUG()`;\n- a C function for each kernel macro CeeSafe calls (`mutex_init`, `spin_lock_init`, `GFP_KERNEL`, `pr_info`);\n- on x86-64, `ceesafe_panic`'s definition in the same translation unit as its callers, since objtool learns that a\n  function does not return from its body or from its own list of names, and reports every caller otherwise.\nIts README records the runs and what was not run.\n\n",
    512 =>
        "# Testing\n\nStatus: describes CeeSafe's tests. Tests that were run are recorded, with dates and results, in the repository's\n`ROADMAP.md` §10 and the plans under `docs/`; they are never mixed with proposals.\n\nSanitizer results are supporting evidence, never proof of the language's safety.\n\n## 1. Test kinds\n\n| Kind | Where | Oracle |\n|---|---|---|\n| Corpus (§2) | `tests/reference/tests/corpus/*`, `tools/run_corpus.py` | the files' own annotations and snapshots |\n| Support library | `tests/support`, `tools/fault_sweep.py` | independent oracles (Python, .NET) for each structure, and an allocation-failure sweep |\n| Fixed point | `tools/fixed_point.py`, `bootstrap/fixed-point/` | ceesafec built by itself emits byte-identical C, generation after generation |\n| Mutation suite (M2) | `tools/mutation_suite.py` | mutants of seed programs; the expected (code, line) diagnostics come from an independent oracle, not from the checker |\n| Fault-injection sweep (M3) | `tools/fault_injection.py` | every run-pass program that allocates, built with ASan+UBSan, run once per allocation attempt *k* with attempt *k* failing; each run returns from `main` with `live=0` or panics with \"unwrapped an Err value\", with no sanitizer report |\n| Collection properties (M6) | `tools/collection_properties.py` | a model of each built-in collection predicts the exact output of random operation programs (values, lengths, the growth rule of `type-system.md` §23.1, stale handles, drop order), also under the fault sweep |\n| Freestanding | `tools/freestanding_check.py` | the freestanding programs build with `-ffreestanding -nostdinc` for `x86_64-` and `aarch64-unknown-none-elf` and run against their C harnesses |\n| Debugging | `tools/debugger_check.py` | a `-g` build under lldb or gdb with `editors/debug`'s commands (`tooling.md` §3) |\n| CosmoOS (C1–C9, K3) | `integrations/cosmoos/c*.py`, `k3.py` | ceesafec's C under CosmoOS's own flags, host tests, fuzz targets and module loader rules, and in its QEMU boots; CosmoOS's tree is only read |\n| Same C on another host | `tools/host_c_check.py` | ceesafec built on Windows (MSVC, clang) emits the same C as on macOS and Linux |\n| Specification (§4) | `tools/spec_check.py`, `tools/guide_check.py` | every diagnostic code is explained; the guide's examples do what the pages say; links resolve |\n| Frame reports (L1 A3) | `tools/run_corpus.py`, family `frames` | `--explain-frames` against its `.frames` snapshot; every build asserts each frame's size in C |\n| Interpreter agreement (L1 A1) | `tools/run_corpus.py --interp` | every run-pass and run-fail program run with `ceesafec run --interp` meets the same expectations (output, exit, panic, leak check) as its C build |\n| Library diagnostics (Decision 0008) | `ceesafec check --library-diagnostics` on an empty program | `lib/` reports no diagnostic: since lib/ is CeeSafe's, its errors are no longer assumed to be porting gaps |\n| Cancellation sweep (L1 A2b) | `tools/cancel_sweep.py` | every async run-pass program, built with ASan+UBSan, run once per suspension point *k* with `CEESAFE_CANCEL_AT=k`; each run exits cleanly with `live=0` and no sanitizer report |\n\n`tools/contract_tests.py --ceesafec PATH` runs all of these but CosmoOS and the host check, against one ceesafec.\n\n`tools/package_tests.py --compiler PATH` (in the contract suite) builds packages that link a C static library it makes,\nthrough two levels of dependency, and checks the link file, an unset variable, the locked hash and N0321 (ADR 0047).\n\n`tools/dns_check.py` (ADR 0048's timed and sanitizer cases) and `tools/wake_check.py` (a cancellation wakes only the\ntasks it reaches: 200 races beside 100 idle sockets wake at most 200) are in the contract suite too.\n\n`tools/test_command_check.py` runs `ceesafec test` (ADR 0049) on `tests/packages/test-command`, whose tests have every\noutcome, and compares each with `expected.tsv` (reviewed by hand), natively and with `--interp`; it also checks\n`--list`, both sweeps against the ",
    513 =>
        "harness's own counts, and that `build` leaves the tests out.\n\nBoth sweeps also take an executable package instead, built in its directory with its manifest:\n`tools/cancel_sweep.py --compiler PATH --package DIR` (repeatable) and `tools/fault_sweep.py --compiler PATH\n--package DIR`. That is how a package outside this repository (CeeSafe.Web's gates) uses them.\n\n## 2. Corpus format\n\nRunner: `tools/run_corpus.py --compiler out/ceesafec --corpus tests/reference/tests/corpus`.\n\n- `parse-pass/*.cee`: must parse with **no** diagnostics. The AST dump (`ceesafec parse --dump-ast`) must equal the\n  sibling `.ast` snapshot. **Every new or changed snapshot is reviewed by hand before it is committed.** A snapshot is\n  an expected value, not a recording.\n- `parse-fail/*.cee`: annotate each expected diagnostic on the line where it is reported, as `// error[N0100]`, or\n  `// error[N0100]@14` to also pin the column. A missing **or extra** diagnostic fails the test, so it also checks\n  error recovery.\n- `check-pass/*.cee`: must type-check with no diagnostics. The HIR dump (`ceesafec check --dump-hir`) must equal the\n  sibling `.hir` snapshot.\n- `check-fail/*.cee`: must **parse** cleanly and produce exactly the annotated semantic diagnostics.\n- A file with a line `// profile: freestanding` is checked in the freestanding profile (`profiles.md` §4), and one with\n  `// option: no-float` as if built with `--no-float` (`type-system.md` §35.9).\n- `run-pass/*.cee` and `run-fail/*.cee` are compiled to C, built with strict warnings and `-Werror`, and run.\n  Directives:\n  - `// expect-stdout: text`: one per output line, in order; `// expect-stdout:` alone is an empty line.\n  - `// expect-exit: N`: the exit status (default 0).\n  - `// expect-panic: reason`: placed on the line where the panic must be reported. The test requires exit status\n    134 (SIGABRT) and `ceesafe panic: <reason> at <file>:<line>:` on standard error.\n  - `// expect-library-panic: reason`: the same for a panic an embedded library raises at its own line (ADR 0075\n    §4: an OrderedMap cursor used after a change), so `ceesafe panic: <reason> at lib/`.\n- `ffi/` programs are linked with their C harness (`NAME.c`, and `NAME.types.h` for imported C types,\n  `type-system.md` §31); `freestanding/` programs with their environment.\n- `emit-c/*.cee` have reviewed snapshots of the generated `main.c`; `placement/`, `bounds-checks/`,\n  `overflow-checks/` and `closure-calls/` have snapshots of the `--explain-…` reports (`optimisations.md`).\n- The C configurations: the contract suite builds the run families under the host's `cc` with `-Werror`, with\n  `-O2 --split --jobs 4` (each file's unit compiled to its own object, four at once; ADR 0082), and under clang\n  `-O1` with ASan+UBSan, `-fno-sanitize-recover=all` and the runtime's allocation report (`--check-leaks`: a program\n  that exits normally with live allocations fails). On Linux, also under gcc with `-O2 -D_FORTIFY_SOURCE=2\n  -Wall -Werror`, the flags Ubuntu's gcc applies by default: glibc's `warn_unused_result` and gcc's own flow warnings\n  then reach the runtime and the generated C. Every configuration must meet the expectations, so the run corpus is\n  also a differential test.\n\nA change to the language adds corpus files, with snapshots written by ceesafec run from the corpus root. Adding\nlibrary code (`lib/`, Decision 0008) can renumber closures in `.hir` snapshots and reorder types in `.c` ones, since\nlibrary items are declared first: such an update is a reviewed edit that changes nothing else (L1 A1: `check-pass/effects-of.hir`,\n`emit-c/slice-bulk.c`).\n\n## 3. Snapshots\n\nThe corpus's snapshots are CeeSafe's own: an intended change to what ceesafec emits updates them by a reviewed edit,\nrecorded with the change, and never by regenerating them to make a run pass. `tools/run_corpus.py --reference PATH`\nalso requires a second compiler to give identical observable outcomes.\n\n## 4. The specification's own checks\n\n- `tools/spec_check.py`: every",
    514 =>
        " diagnostic code `ceesafec` reports has a row that `ceesafec explain` finds; every\n  relative link in `spec/` and `guide/` resolves; `spec/api/core.md` is what `ceesafec doc` writes for `lib/core`; no\n  tracked file uses a retired name.\n- `tools/guide_check.py`: every `ceesafe` code block of `guide/` is compiled as a file named `main.cee`:\n  - a block must type-check; with a `main`, it is also run in the interpreter, and a `text` block right after it must be\n    exactly its output;\n  - an HTML comment directly before the block changes that:\n    - `<!-- ceesafe: fail N0241 -->`: the block must fail with exactly these codes, and a `text` block after it must be\n      exactly the compiler's messages;\n    - `<!-- ceesafe: freestanding -->`: checked with the freestanding profile, not run;\n    - `<!-- ceesafe: check -->`: type-checked, not run (it calls C);\n    - `<!-- ceesafe: panics -->`, `<!-- ceesafe: exits 1 -->`: the run's exit status;\n    - `<!-- ceesafe: skip -->`: not checked, for a block that cannot stand alone (a second package's file, a fragment).\n      Use it sparingly, and check the example some other way.\n\n## 5. Not yet done\n\nA random program generator (M7d, K7) and a 24-hour differential campaign with it are ROADMAP P9 items.\n",
    515 =>
        "# Tooling (M10)\n\nThe commands of `ceesafec` beside `build`, `check` and `run`. The formatter has its own document (`formatting.md`).\n\n## 1. Documentation generator (T2)\n\n```sh\nceesafec doc file.cee...                 # Markdown for the files' public items, on standard output\nceesafec doc lib/core -o spec/api/core.md --title \"lib/core\"\nceesafec doc --missing file.cee...       # list the public items without a doc comment; exit 1 if there are any\n```\n\nThe files are one package, as for `check` (a directory means every `.cee` file under it, in ordinal path order).\n`lib/core` is documented only when it is named. A file with a syntax error stops the command: its diagnostics are\nprinted and nothing is written. The package does not have to type-check: documentation is made from the syntax, so it\ncan be written for code that does not build yet.\n\n### 1.1 What is documented\n\n- Every item declared `public`: a function, a constant, a struct, an enum, an interface, and a function in an\n  `extern \"C\"` block.\n- Inside a public struct: its `public` fields, its `public` methods, and whether it has a `drop` block. Inside a public\n  enum: every variant. Inside a public interface: every associated type, constant and method (ADR 0086 §2.4,\n  §2.5), in source order.\n- An `impl I for T` block, listed under `T` as \"Implements `I`\" when `T` is a public struct or enum of the package.\n\nNothing else is listed. Items in a `namespace` are listed under their simple names, as the language names them today\n(type-system.md §1).\n\n### 1.2 The page\n\nThe output is deterministic: the same files give the same bytes.\n- A title (`--title`, or the first file's name without its extension) as a level-1 heading, then a list of the\n  documented items, each a link to its section.\n- One level-2 section per item, in the order of the files as given and of the items in each file. Its heading is the\n  item's kind and name (`struct Volatile`, `fn sortBy`).\n- The item's **signature** in a `ceesafe` code block: the item's attributes and its declaration up to its body (the\n  `{ … }` or `=> …` of a function, the members of a struct), as `ceesafec fmt` prints them (formatting.md). A struct's\n  block lists its public fields; an enum's, its variants; an interface's, its associated types, constants and\n  methods.\n- The item's **doc comment** (grammar.md §1.2): its lines as written, which are Markdown. An item without one has\n  only its signature.\n- A struct's public methods follow as level-3 sections (`StructName.method`), each with its signature and doc\n  comment; then \"Implements `I`\" for each `impl`.\n\n### 1.3 Missing documentation\n\n`--missing` writes nothing but a line per public item without a doc comment, `path:line: kind name`, and exits 1 if\nthere is any. It is a lint for a package's public surface; nothing requires it to pass.\n\n### 1.4 In the repository\n\n`spec/api/core.md` is generated from `lib/core` with `ceesafec doc lib/core -o spec/api/core.md --title \"lib/core\"`, run\non the embedded `lib/core` (its sources under CeeSafe's names). `tools/spec_check.py` fails if it is out of date.\n\n## 2. Language server (T3)\n\n```sh\nceesafec lsp        # a Language Server Protocol server on standard input and output\n```\n\nThe server speaks LSP 3.17 over JSON-RPC 2.0, with `Content-Length` framing, on standard input and output. It is part\nof `ceesafec` and has no dependency beyond CeeSafe's own libraries. The editor starts it; `editors/vscode` is a VS Code\nextension that does (§2.4).\n\n### 2.1 What a document is checked with\n\nA document that is a source of a package (a `ceesafe.toml` above it whose sources include it) is checked with its\npackage, as `ceesafec check` in the package would check it (§4.4): the editor's text for the document, the package's\nother files from disk, and the dependencies its `ceesafe.lock` names, never fetching; the manifest's profile and `float`\napply. Any other document, or one whose package does not resolve, is checked as a package of its own, with\n`lib/core`,",
    516 =>
        " as `ceesafec check file.cee` would check it, using the text the editor holds. Its profile is hosted, unless\nits first line is `// profile: freestanding` (the corpus convention, `testing.md` §3).\n\nChecking reads the files the document's `embed` and `embedText` calls name (`type-system.md` §10.1, ADR 0087 §2.8),\nunder the same rules as the command line: inside the package's directory and its `[embed] roots`, or, for a document\nchecked alone, below the document's own directory. A document whose URI is not a file on disk embeds nothing. An\nembedded file is read again at each check, so a change to it shows at the document's next change or save.\n\n### 2.2 Capabilities\n\n| Request or notification | What the server does |\n|---|---|\n| `initialize` | Answers with the capabilities below: full-text synchronisation, hover, definition, document symbols, formatting, quick fixes |\n| `textDocument/didOpen`, `didChange`, `didClose` | Keeps the text; after an open or change, checks the document (§2.1) and publishes its diagnostics. A close clears them |\n| `textDocument/publishDiagnostics` (sent) | Every diagnostic of the check: its range, severity, code (`N0205`) and message, with each note as related information |\n| `textDocument/hover` | On a name or an expression: its type, as CeeSafe writes it. On a local or parameter, `let x: T` or `var x: T`; on a function or method, its signature; on a field, `name: T` |\n| `textDocument/definition` | On a local, parameter, function, method, field, struct, enum or interface name: the location of its declaration, if it is in the document |\n| `textDocument/documentSymbol` | The document's items and their members, as a tree: functions, structs (fields and methods), enums (variants), interfaces, constants |\n| `textDocument/formatting` | The document formatted by `ceesafec fmt` (`formatting.md`), as one edit; nothing if it has a syntax error |\n| `textDocument/codeAction` | The fixes (§5.2) of the diagnostics in the requested range, as `quickfix` actions (§5.5) |\n| `shutdown`, `exit` | Stops |\n\nAnything else is answered with the JSON-RPC error `MethodNotFound` (requests) or ignored (notifications).\n\nPositions are LSP positions: zero-based lines, and columns in UTF-16 code units, which the server converts to and from\nthe documents' UTF-8 bytes. Hover and definition use the typed tree of the last check; if the document does not parse,\nthey answer null and only the diagnostics are shown.\n\n### 2.3 Errors\n\nThe server never stops on a bad document. A request that fails inside the compiler is answered with the JSON-RPC\nerror `InternalError` and the exception's message, and the server goes on. A message that is not valid JSON-RPC is\nanswered with `ParseError` when it has an id.\n\n### 2.4 The VS Code extension\n\n`editors/vscode` holds:\n- a TextMate grammar for `.cee` files (keywords, literals, comments, attributes), whose keyword list is the\n  compiler's (`editors/vscode/test/keywords.test.mjs` compares them);\n- a language configuration (comments, brackets, auto-closing pairs);\n- the client, which starts `ceesafec lsp` (the `ceesafe.server.path` setting, default `ceesafec` on the `PATH`) through the\n  `vscode-languageclient` library, bundled with it into `dist/extension.cjs`.\n\nIt is built with [Vite+](https://viteplus.dev/) (`vp`), not with `ceesafec`:\n\n```sh\ncd editors/vscode\nvp install            # the dependencies, pinned by pnpm-lock.yaml\nvp test               # the grammar and the server tests below\nvp run check          # lint and formatting\nvp run package        # bundle the client (vp pack) and make ceesafe-language-0.1.0.vsix (vsce)\n```\n\nIts tests: `test/grammar.test.mjs` tokenizes CeeSafe lines with `vscode-textmate`, as VS Code does, and checks the scope\neach token gets; `test/server.test.mjs` drives a built `ceesafec lsp` through `vscode-jsonrpc`, the library under VS\nCode's client (diagnostics, hover, definition, an unknown method, shutdown and exit).\n\n## 3. Debugging (T4)\n\nA CeeSafe program is debugged a",
    517 =>
        "s the C it compiles to, with the C mapped back to CeeSafe lines. No debugger needs to\nknow about CeeSafe.\n\n### 3.1 A debug build\n\n`ceesafec build -g file.cee -o program` writes `#line` directives into the C (`c-backend.md`, source maps) and compiles\nit with `-g -O0`. A debugger then shows CeeSafe files and lines: breakpoints set by CeeSafe line stop there, and stepping\ngoes from one CeeSafe statement to the next. A line can take more than one step, since one CeeSafe statement can be several\nC statements (a checked addition, a temporary).\n\nA debug build also turns debug assertions on (ADR 0087 §2.5): `debugAssert(…)` is evaluated, and a false one panics.\n`--no-debug-assertions` turns them off in a `-g` build, `--debug-assertions` on in any `build` or native `run`.\n`ceesafec test` and the interpreter (`run --interp`) evaluate them unless given `--no-debug-assertions`. The C names the\nchoice once, `#define CEE_DEBUG_ASSERTIONS 1` (or `0`), in a program that has a `debugAssert`; each assertion is in an\n`#if CEE_DEBUG_ASSERTIONS` block.\n\n| | lldb | gdb |\n|---|---|---|\n| Stop at a CeeSafe line | `breakpoint set -f demo.cee -l 12` (`b demo.cee:12`) | `break demo.cee:12` |\n| Stop when a check fails, before the program aborts | `b ceesafe_panic` | `break ceesafe_panic` |\n| Where am I | `bt` | `bt` |\n\n### 3.2 Names\n\nThe C names follow `c-backend.md` §2.1, so a CeeSafe name can be found in the debugger:\n\n| CeeSafe | C |\n|---|---|\n| function `length` | `ceeF_length` |\n| method `Counter.bump` | `ceeM_7Counter_bump` (the type's name, prefixed by its length) |\n| local or parameter `sum` | `v_sum_1` (a number makes each local's name unique in its function) |\n| a temporary the compiler made | `t_3` |\n| field `x` | `f_x` |\n| struct `Point` | `ceeT_Point` |\n\n### 3.3 CeeSafe names in the debugger\n\n`editors/debug/ceesafe_lldb.py` and `editors/debug/ceesafe_gdb.py` add three commands, which print with CeeSafe names:\n- `ceesafe-locals`: the current frame's locals and parameters, `name = value`, without the compiler's temporaries; struct\n  values show their fields by their CeeSafe names;\n- `ceesafe-bt`: the backtrace, one frame a line, `CeeSafe name at file:line` (`Counter.bump at demo.cee:3`); an async\n  fn's resume function shows as the fn (`async closure` for a closure);\n- `ceesafe-tasks`: the stopped thread's async tasks (type-system.md §40), one a line, from the runtime's\n  `ceesafe_async_tasks()` (c-backend.md §6): `gather at demo.cee:18 (waiting for its scope)`, a scope's children\n  indented under it.\n\nLoad them with `command script import editors/debug/ceesafe_lldb.py` (lldb) or `source editors/debug/ceesafe_gdb.py` (gdb).\n\nSplit C (`--split`, c-backend.md §2.4) renames the program's functions to `ceeF_<program>_…`: set `CEESAFE_PROGRAM` to\nthe program's name before starting the debugger, and the helpers read them as before.\n\n### 3.4 What is checked\n\n`tools/debugger_check.py` builds a program with `-g` and runs it under lldb (macOS) or gdb (Linux), in batch mode. A breakpoint\nat a CeeSafe line must stop at that line, `ceesafe-locals` must show the locals by their CeeSafe names, and a breakpoint on\n`ceesafe_panic` must give a backtrace whose frames are CeeSafe functions at CeeSafe lines. In an async program stopped\ninside a child task, `ceesafe-tasks` must list the awaiting task, the running child and its waiting sibling at the\nlines they wait at. On a machine with neither debugger,\nthe test does nothing, and the run log says which debugger ran.\n\n## 4. Packages (T6, ADR 0013)\n\n### 4.1 The manifest\n\nA package is a directory with `ceesafe.toml`. Its sources are the `.cee` files under the directories `sources` names\n(default `src`), in ordinal path order. The manifest is a subset of TOML: `[table]` headers, `key = value` lines\nwhose value is a string, a boolean, an array of strings or an inline table of strings, and `#` comments.\n\n```toml\n[package]\nname = \"app\"                 # required: an identifier, not a keyword and not \"core\"\nversi",
    518 =>
        "on = \"0.1.0\"            # optional; required to publish (§4.8), MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH\nkind = \"executable\"          # \"executable\" (default), \"library\", or \"object\" (profiles.md §4.3)\nprofile = \"hosted\"           # or \"freestanding\"\nfloat = true                 # false is --no-float (type-system.md §35.9)\nkernel = \"cosmoos\"           # with profile = \"freestanding\": include lib/kernel, as --kernel cosmoos (ADR 0025)\ntrusted = true               # its types may be declared @threadSafe (ADR 0025 §3.2); listed for reviewers\nsources = [\"src\"]\nc = [\"src/shim.c\"]           # C files compiled and linked with an executable (type-system.md §28.6)\ntests = [\"tests\"]            # test directories (§10): default \"tests\"; their files are sources for check and test only\nceesafec = \">=0.20.0\"        # the compiler this package needs (ADR 0076): a requirement, as a dependency's (§4.7)\nlinks = [\"ssl\", \"crypto\"]    # system libraries to link (ADR 0047): -lssl -lcrypto; ssl.lib crypto.lib with cl\nlib-dirs = [\"$OPENSSL_DIR/lib\"]          # where they are; absolute, or relative to the package\ninclude-dirs = [\"$OPENSSL_DIR/include\"]  # headers for this package's own C files only\n\n[target.windows]             # also [target.macos] and [target.linux]: added after [package]'s on that system\nlinks = [\"libssl\", \"libcrypto\"]\n\n[embed]                      # where `embed` may read besides the package's directory (ADR 0087 §2.8)\nroots = [\"../build/codegen\"]\n\n[dependencies]\ngeometry = { path = \"../geometry\" }\nlz4 = { git = \"https://example.org/lz4-cee.git\", tag = \"v1.2.0\" }      # or rev = \"<commit>\", or branch = \"main\"\nshapes = \"2.1\"               # from the registry (§4.6): a version requirement\njson = { version = \"0.9\", index = \"https://example.org/my-index.git\" }   # from another index\n```\n\n**The compiler requirement** (ADR 0076). When a manifest is read (the root's, and each dependency's, by every command\nthat reads it), a ceesafec whose version does not satisfy `ceesafec` reports N0321 at the value, before anything is\nchecked: `this package needs ceesafec >=0.20.0; this is ceesafec 0.19.1`. A development version (`0.20.0-dev`) counts\nas its MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH. A compiler older than the field reports it as an unknown field at the same line.\n\nA dependency's key is its package's `name`: a dependency is known by the name its own manifest gives it. A\ndependency of kind `executable` is N0321: only a library's items are used. Errors in the manifest are N0320 (its\nsyntax) and N0321 (a missing, unknown or invalid field), with the line.\n\n**System libraries** (ADR 0047):\n- `links` names libraries: letters, digits, `_`, `.`, `+` and `-`, not starting with `-`. `lib-dirs` and `include-dirs`\n  are directories, absolute or relative to the package, not starting with `-`.\n- A directory may start with `$NAME` or `${NAME}`: the environment variable's value, read when the manifest is. An\n  unset one is N0321. Nothing runs (ADR 0013), and the hash (§4.3) is of the manifest's text, not the value.\n- A `[target.OS]` table (`linux`, `macos` or `windows`) holds the same three fields. The host's is added after\n  `[package]`'s; every one is checked.\n- The libraries and library directories of every package in the build reach the executable's link, a library's\n  included. Packages are taken dependents first (by their longest path from the root). A library named twice keeps\n  its last place, so it follows what uses it; a directory keeps its first.\n- A package with `include-dirs` has its own C files compiled apart, with `-c` and those directories, and the objects\n  join the build. No other C file sees its directories.\n- `--emit c` writes the link line as `ceesafe_link.txt` beside the C, and `--emit obj` and `--emit lib` as\n  `<output>.link.txt`, when the\n  manifests name any: one argument per line, each separately compiled C file after its include directories, then the\n  library directories and the libraries (`cl`'s spelling when `--cc` is `cl`).\n\n**Embed roots** (ADR 0087 §2.8, ",
    519 =>
        "`type-system.md` §10.1). A package's files may embed files inside the package's\ndirectory (the one holding `ceesafe.toml`), and inside each directory `[embed] roots` lists:\n- A root is relative to the package's directory, with `/` separators, and nothing in it is expanded: an absolute path,\n  a drive, a `\\`, `:`, `$` or `%`, or a leading `~` is N0321 at the array, and so is any other field in `[embed]`.\n  It may use `..`. Roots are resolved when a file is embedded, not when the manifest is read: one that does not exist\n  (yet: a build's generated directory) reaches nothing, and an `embed` below it is N0362 until it does.\n- A root is its real path: every symbolic link in it is followed, so a root that is a symbolic link stands for the\n  directory it leads to.\n- **Only the root package and the path dependencies it reaches through path dependencies alone may have roots\n  outside their directory.** They are code the user works on, whose manifests the user wrote. A git or registry\n  dependency, and every package reached through one (its path dependencies, at any depth), embeds only inside that\n  checkout: a root counts only when its real path is inside it, so a checkout reaches nothing outside itself, through\n  `..`, through a symbolic link it holds, or through a path dependency it names. So does a package whose directory\n  lies in the package cache (`$CEESAFE_HOME`, §4.3), even as the root package: the language server going to a\n  definition in a checkout, or `check` run there, gives it no more than the build that fetched it, and the path\n  dependencies such a root reaches are kept inside its directory, wherever they lie.\n\n\nFrom the package being built, every dependency is resolved, and then its dependencies, recursively:\n- `path` is a directory relative to the manifest that names it, holding the dependency's `ceesafe.toml`;\n- `git` is a repository; `rev` names a commit, `tag` and `branch` are resolved to one (§4.3). The commit is\n  checked out into the cache, `$CEESAFE_HOME/git/checkouts/<repository>/<commit>` (default `~/.ceesafe`), and the\n  dependency is the package at the checkout's root.\n\nA package name reached by two different sources (two paths, two commits, a path and a commit) is N0326, naming both\ndependents. A package that depends on itself, directly or not, is N0327. A missing directory or manifest is N0322.\nNothing runs during resolution except `git` (ADR 0013 D5).\n\n### 4.3 The lock file\n\n`ceesafe.lock`, beside `ceesafe.toml`, records the resolution so that every build uses the same code:\n\n```toml\n# Written by ceesafec fetch. Commit it with ceesafe.toml.\n[[package]]\nname = \"lz4\"\nsource = \"git+https://example.org/lz4-cee.git\"\ncommit = \"3f2a9c0d…\"         # the full commit id\nhash = \"sha256:…\"            # of the checkout's manifest and sources\n\n[[package]]\nname = \"geometry\"\nsource = \"path+../geometry\"\n```\n\n- `ceesafec fetch` resolves every dependency, fetching git repositories and resolving tags and branches (`git\n  ls-remote`, `git fetch`), and writes the lock. A `rev` is used as written; a `tag` or `branch` is the commit it names\n  at that moment, until the next `ceesafec fetch`.\n- Every other command reads the lock. It fails with N0324 if the manifest has a dependency the lock lacks or\n  records differently (run `ceesafec fetch`), and with N0325 if a git checkout's manifest and sources no longer have the\n  lock's hash. A missing checkout of a locked commit is fetched, unless `--offline` is given (N0323 if it cannot be).\n- A path dependency has no hash: it is code being worked on beside the package.\n- The hash is SHA-256 over, for the manifest and each source file in ordinal order, the path relative to the package\n  root, a zero byte, the file's bytes and a zero byte.\n\n### 4.4 Commands\n\n- `ceesafec new <name>` makes `<name>/ceesafe.toml` and `<name>/src/main.cee` (a `main` that prints a line); `--lib`\n  makes a library with `src/lib.cee` instead. It also writes `.gitignore` (`out/`) and the agent instructions",
    520 =>
        " of\n  §7: `AGENTS.md` by default, others with `--agent`, none with `--no-agent`.\n- `ceesafec agents [--agent …]` writes or refreshes the agent instructions of the package in the current directory or\n  above it (§7.3).\n- `ceesafec fetch [--offline] [--update [name]]` resolves and writes `ceesafe.lock` (§4.7).\n- `ceesafec publish` checks that the package can be published and prints its index entry (§4.8).\n- `ceesafec test` runs the package's `@test` functions (§10).\n- `ceesafec build`, `check`, `run`, `doc` and `fmt` without file arguments use the package of the nearest `ceesafe.toml` in\n  the current directory or above it. `build` writes `out/<name>` (an executable, or `out/<name>.o` for `object`), the\n  C sources with `--emit c`, or `out/<name>.a` with `--emit lib` (§8.3); the manifest's profile, `float` and C files apply, and command-line options add to\n  them. `fmt` formats the package's own sources, not its dependencies'.\n- `ceesafec check` in a package (ADR 0082 §2.7, §8.5) is one check of that package, its tests included, and of every\n  package it depends on. A dependency whose **interface record** (§4.4.1) is valid is parsed and its declarations\n  resolved, but its bodies are checked again only where a dependent compiles or reads them (generic code, async\n  functions, functions with a closure or function parameter or `@effectsOf`, `@constant` functions); every other\n  body's effects come from the record. Any other dependency is checked in full in the same check: one without a\n  record, one whose record is opaque, and one with errors, which is never taken from a record.\n  - **Never slower, never weaker than `--whole-program`.** `check --whole-program` checks every package in full as one\n    program (ADR 0013 D4) and reads no record. Plain `check` does no more work than it: where there is no usable cache\n    (no owners and modes to check, Windows, a `$CEESAFE_HOME` that is not writable) or no record is valid (a cold\n    cache), it is that same check. It rejects whatever `--whole-program` rejects: the oracle\n    `tools/package_tests.py` §23 compares the two for every package of its fixtures, cold, warm and without a cache.\n  - **The same report whatever the cache holds.** The diagnostics are the package's own, as `--whole-program` gives\n    them, and, for each package of its closure with errors, at any depth, one N0364 at the line of the package's\n    manifest that leads to it (the first such dependency), with a note at its first error and the command that\n    shows them all, `ceesafec check --package <name>`. A dependency's own diagnostics, its warnings too, are not\n    repeated. As in any check, errors before the effect checks stop what follows: a dependency's type error hides\n    another's ownership error, as it does in `--whole-program`.\n  - **The records are made by the checks.** Once the report is out, each dependency the check checked in full whose\n    closure has no error gets its record, from what the check found: the effect checker records every such\n    package's functions, so the next check finds them. Before the check, a few missing records low in the graph are\n    made first (each such dependency checked alone, without its tests or entry function), but only while that costs\n    at most a twentieth of the whole check and lets the cache's other records be found: after an edit in a package\n    that leaves its interface (§4.4.1), such as a comment or a body, the records of the packages above it hold again.\n  - `--package <name>` checks another package of the lock as from its directory; `--record` makes the package's\n    record in that way and prints it. `check` runs in one process and takes no `--jobs`.\n- `ceesafec check --all` checks every package of the lock together, each one's tests included, and prints one report\n  (§5.1): its diagnostics sorted by package, file and position, in both forms, then, as text, a line per package with\n  errors or warnings, or as JSON a `packages` summary of the diagno",
    521 =>
        "stics by their package. Where errors stop the\n  check before the effect checks, it checks again the packages none of whose closure has errors, so that each\n  package's own errors are found as its own check would find them; a package's diagnostics are those of the last\n  round that checked it. No N0364 is added: the failing package's own diagnostics are in the report. It exits with 1\n  if any package has an error. Its cost is about one `--whole-program` check (ADR 0082 §8.5).\n- `build`, `run`, `test` and `fix` check every package as one program and read no record: a record is a cache of a\n  result `check` proved, and only `check` uses it (ADR 0082 §2.7, §8).\n- The language server (§2.1) checks a document that belongs to a package (a `ceesafe.toml` above it, whose sources\n  include it) with that package: the open document's text, the package's other files from disk, and its locked\n  dependencies. Without a lock, or outside a package, the document is checked alone as before.\n\n### 4.4.1 Embedded files and what is cached\n\n`embed` and `embedText` (`type-system.md` §10.1) make a program depend on files that are not sources. Every command\nthat checks a package reads them afresh, so a file changed after `check` is what `build` compiles, and the language\nserver sees a change at its next check. Within one compilation each file is read once, by its real path. Neither the\nlock's hash (§4.3), which covers a package's manifest and sources, nor `fetch` looks at embedded files.\n\n**Interface records** (ADR 0082 §2.7, §8.5) are what `check` keeps of a package between runs, in\n`$CEESAFE_HOME/cache/records/<package>-<key>.rec`:\n- **The key** is a SHA-256 of the record format, `ceesafec --version`'s banner, the identity of the `ceesafec` that\n  runs (the SHA-256 of its executable, read once per process: builds of one version share a banner), the profile\n  (hosted or freestanding, `--kernel`, `--no-float`), the package's name, manifest and sources (each with its path in\n  the package), as read for the check that uses or makes the record, and its direct dependencies' interface hashes.\n  Any change to one of them is another key: a miss, and a check in full.\n- **The record** says whether the package checked and the stage of its first error; and for each function whose body\n  dependents skip, where it is declared, its inferred effects (`type-system.md` §32.1) and the explanation of each\n  (the steps an N0295 or N0360 names through it, and its rank).\n- **The interface hash** covers the package's tokens, with those skipped bodies left out, what the record says, and\n  its dependencies' interface hashes. It holds no position: a comment, a blank line, or a declaration moved leaves it,\n  and so every dependent's key, as it was, while the diagnostics, made from the sources as they are, keep their\n  positions. Nothing a dependent's check can say of the package holds one either: an explanation names a closure by\n  its place in its function (`{closure environment 2 in Lexer.scan}`), not by its line. A change inside a skipped body\n  that changes neither the effects nor whether the package checks leaves the hash as it was too.\n- **Opaque records.** A package that embeds a file, or whose skipped bodies make a `dyn` table of an interface with an\n  async method (whose frames, and recursion through `await`, belong to the whole program), has an opaque record: its\n  dependents check its bodies. Whether it fails is said the same way as for any other package: one N0364.\n- **Trust.** A record is a cache of a result this compiler proved, never a promise from outside, and any doubt is a\n  miss. `$CEESAFE_HOME/cache` and `cache/records` are made with mode 0700, then opened once, each never through a\n  symbolic link (`O_NOFOLLOW`), and used only if both belong to the user running `ceesafec` and no one else may write\n  to them; `$CEESAFE_HOME` itself and the directories above it are trusted as they are named. A record is opened from\n  the descriptor of `records`, never ",
    522 =>
        "through a symbolic link, and read only if it belongs to that user and no one\n  else may write to it, from the descriptor whose owner and mode were checked; one that cannot be read, is cut short\n  (its last field is the SHA-256 of the rest), or names another key, banner, profile or package is a miss. A record\n  is written to a new name created for it alone (mode 0600) in that directory and renamed there, so agents that check\n  at once share the cache and none reads half a record. The compiler is identified by its own executable as the\n  system knows it (`/proc/self/exe` on Linux, the kernel's path of the process on macOS), never by the name it was\n  started with. Owners and modes are read where `ceesafec` knows `fstat`'s layout, macOS and Linux on arm64 and\n  x86-64; elsewhere, Windows included, no record is read or written, and `check` is the whole program's check.\n\n### 4.5 Errors\n\n| Code | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| N0320 | `ceesafe.toml` or `ceesafe.lock` is not valid in the TOML subset of §4.1 |\n| N0321 | A manifest field is missing, unknown, or has an invalid value |\n| N0322 | A path dependency's directory or manifest is missing, or a dependency is named differently from its key |\n| N0323 | A git dependency cannot be fetched or resolved, or is needed with `--offline` and absent |\n| N0324 | The lock file is missing or does not match the manifest: run `ceesafec fetch` |\n| N0325 | A git checkout's manifest and sources do not match the lock's hash |\n| N0326 | One package name reached through two different sources |\n| N0327 | A dependency cycle |\n| N0328 | An invalid version, version requirement, or index entry |\n| N0329 | No versions satisfy the requirements: the chain of requirements that conflict |\n| N0335 | `ceesafec publish`: the package cannot be published as it is |\n\n### 4.6 The registry (M13 G6, ADR 0017)\n\nA **registry dependency** names a package and a **version requirement**. The package is found in an **index**: a git\nrepository, by default `https://github.com/CeeSafe/CeeSafe-index`, or `$CEESAFE_INDEX`, or the dependency's `index`.\n\n- **Versions** are `MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH`, three decimal numbers with no leading zeros, compared numerically. There\n  are no pre-release or build suffixes in v1.\n- **Requirements** are one or more comparators separated by commas, all of which must hold:\n  - `\"1.4\"`, `\"^1.4\"` and `\"^1.4.2\"` are caret requirements: at least that version, below the next major version, or\n    below the next minor for `0.x` (`\"0.3\"` is `>=0.3.0, <0.4.0`) and the next patch for `0.0.x`.\n  - `\"=1.4.2\"` means exactly that version.\n  - `\">=1.4\"`, `\">1.4\"`, `\"<2\"` and `\"<=1.9.3\"` compare. A missing minor or patch is 0.\n  - `\"*\"` means any version.\n- **The index** holds `packages/<name>.toml`, with one `[[version]]` table per published version, in publishing order:\n  ```toml\n  [[version]]\n  version = \"1.4.0\"\n  git = \"https://github.com/someone/geometry.git\"\n  commit = \"3f2a9c0d…\"                      # a full commit id\n  hash = \"sha256:…\"                         # of the commit's manifest and sources (§4.3)\n  dependencies = { shapes = \"2.1\" }         # its registry requirements, as its ceesafe.toml writes them\n  yanked = false                            # optional; a yanked version is never chosen for a new resolution\n  ```\n  Entries are only appended. A published package's dependencies are all registry dependencies, and those without\n  an `index` come from the index that published the package.\n\n### 4.7 Version solving\n\n`ceesafec fetch` resolves path and git dependencies as before (§4.2), gathering the registry requirements of the root\nand of every path and git package. It then chooses one version of each registry package, transitively, from the\nindex's entries:\n\n- The **newest** version that satisfies every requirement on it is chosen, and so on through its own dependencies.\n  On a conflict, the solver backtracks.\n- A version the lock already records is preferred while it satisfies the requirements, so `ceesafec fe",
    523 =>
        "tch` changes\n  nothing that needs no change. `--update` prefers the newest for every package, and `--update <name>` for that one.\n- **One version per package name** (OD-26). A name reached through a path or git dependency and through the registry\n  is N0326.\n- If no choice satisfies every requirement, N0329 shows the requirements on the package that could not be chosen,\n  and where each came from:\n  `no version of 'shapes' satisfies ^2.1 (from geometry 1.4.0) and ^1.0 (from plot 0.3.0)`.\n- Each chosen version's commit is checked out as for a git dependency (§4.2). Its hash must be the index's (N0325),\n  and its manifest's name and version must be the entry's (N0328).\n- The lock records each as `source = \"registry+<index>\"` with `version`, `repository` (the git URL), `commit` and\n  `hash`. Other commands use the lock: an entry whose version no longer satisfies the manifest's requirement is\n  N0324.\n\n### 4.8 Publishing\n\n`ceesafec publish` (no network but `git`, and nothing written) checks the package in the current directory:\n\n- it is a library with a valid `version`;\n- every dependency is a registry dependency;\n- it type-checks with its dependencies;\n- its git working tree is clean, and its commit is on a remote branch.\n\nIt then prints the index entry for the version: the remote's URL, the commit, the hash and the requirements. To\npublish, add that entry to `packages/<name>.toml` in the index by pull request. The index's continuous integration\nruns `ceesafec index-check`, which checks every new entry:\n- the commit exists;\n- its manifest has the entry's name and version;\n- its hash and dependencies are the entry's;\n- the version is newer than every earlier entry.\n\nA new package name is reviewed by the index's maintainers. Later versions from the same repository merge after the\nchecks pass.\n\n## 5. Diagnostics for programs (M12 A1)\n\nPeople read diagnostics as text (`diagnostics.md`, \"Format\"). Programs, AI agents and editors among them, read the\nsame diagnostics as JSON, and can apply the fixes the compiler is sure of.\n\n### 5.1 `--diagnostics-format=json`\n\n`check`, `build`, `run` and `parse` accept `--diagnostics-format=json` (or `=text`, the default). With `json`,\ndiagnostics are not written to standard error; instead one JSON document is written to standard output, as one line,\nafter the command's own work. `build` still writes its outputs, and `run` still runs the program, if there are no\nerrors; the program's own output then precedes the document. The exit status is unchanged. `\"severity\"` is `\"error\"` or `\"warning\"`,\nand `\"warnings\"` counts the warnings (`diagnostics.md`, \"Warnings\"): a successful `build` or `run` writes the document\nonly when there are warnings, and a warning never changes the exit status unless `--deny-warnings` is given.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"version\": 1,\n  \"errors\": 1,\n  \"warnings\": 0,\n  \"diagnostics\": [\n    {\n      \"code\": \"N0249\",\n      \"severity\": \"error\",\n      \"file\": \"src/main.cee\",\n      \"package\": \"app\",\n      \"start\": { \"line\": 7, \"column\": 10 },\n      \"end\": { \"line\": 7, \"column\": 11 },\n      \"message\": \"this parameter is 'borrow mut': write 'mut' before the argument to let the call modify it\",\n      \"summary\": \"`mut` argument marker missing or misplaced\",\n      \"notes\": [ { \"file\": \"src/main.cee\", \"start\": { … }, \"end\": { … }, \"message\": \"…\" } ],\n      \"fixes\": [\n        { \"title\": \"write 'mut' before the argument\",\n          \"edits\": [ { \"file\": \"src/main.cee\", \"start\": { \"line\": 7, \"column\": 10 }, \"end\": { \"line\": 7, \"column\": 10 },\n                       \"newText\": \"mut \" } ] }\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n- `version` is the format's version: fields may be added within a version, never removed or changed.\n- Positions are 1-based lines and columns, columns in UTF-16 code units, as in the text form. `end` is exclusive.\n- `file` is the path as the text form prints it: a path given on the command line, or a file of the root package, as\n  given (relative to the current directory when it is under it); ",
    524 =>
        "a dependency's file as its path from the current\n  directory, `../alpha/src/util.cee`, so that it can be opened (ADR 0082 §2.1).\n- `package` (added in version 1 by ADR 0082 §2.1) is the name of the package the file belongs to, in package mode\n  (`build`, `check` or `run` without file arguments): the root package's or a dependency's. It is left out for a build\n  of files, and for lib/core's and lib/std's files.\n- `summary` is the code's \"Meaning\" in `diagnostics.md`, which `ceesafec` embeds.\n- Diagnostics are in the order the text form prints them. Keys are written in the order shown.\n- A package of the closure with errors (N0364, §4.4) is reported in the manifest of the package checked: `file` is\n  `ceesafe.toml` from the current directory, and `package` that package's name; its note is at the failing package's\n  first error.\n- `check --all` (§4.4; added in version 1 by ADR 0082 §2.7) writes one document for every package of the lock. Its\n  diagnostics are sorted by `package`, then `file`, then position (a diagnostic's order of discovery breaks ties), the\n  order the text form prints them in too, and `errors` and `warnings` count them all. Each diagnostic is there once,\n  and none is an N0364. After `diagnostics` comes `packages`: one entry per package, by name, with its `name` and the\n  number of `errors` and `warnings` among the diagnostics whose `package` it is:\n\n```json\n  \"packages\": [ { \"name\": \"app\", \"errors\": 1, \"warnings\": 0 }, { \"name\": \"mid\", \"errors\": 0, \"warnings\": 0 } ]\n```\n\n### 5.2 Fixes\n\nA fix is a list of edits that the compiler is sure resolves the diagnostic. It is offered only where the\nintent is unambiguous; a diagnostic with several plausible repairs offers none. Applying a fix removes that\ndiagnostic and introduces none at the edited place. The kinds:\n\n| Code | When | Edit |\n|---|---|---|\n| N0249 | an argument for a `borrow mut` parameter lacks `mut`, and is a place that can be borrowed mutably | insert `mut ` before the argument; if the place is a `let` local, also `let` → `var` at its declaration (one fix) |\n| N0249 | an argument for a shared parameter has `mut` | delete the `mut ` |\n| N0211 | assignment to a `let` local declared in the function | `let` → `var` at its declaration |\n| N0248 | `borrow mut` (or a `borrow mut this` call) of a `let` local declared in the function | `let` → `var` at its declaration |\n| N0208 | `e as T` between integer types that can lose information | `truncate<T>(e)` (wrap-around, as the message says) |\n| N0201 | an extern declaration whose C name a dependency's `public` extern declaration has, identical to it in CeeSafe (type-system.md §28.2; ADR 0081 §2.2) | remove the declaration, with its doc comment and attributes; its call sites are left as they are |\n| N0100 | `expected ';'`, with no earlier error in the file, after a declaration, assignment, `return`, or a call statement (not after an expression that cannot be a statement, N0225) | insert `;` after the previous token |\n\n### 5.3 `ceesafec fix`\n\n`ceesafec fix [FILES]` (or the package, without files) applies every fix, writes the files, checks again, and repeats\nuntil no fix applies or after 8 rounds. It then prints the remaining diagnostics, as text or with\n`--diagnostics-format=json`, and exits 1 if errors remain. With `--dry-run` it changes no file and prints the\nedits of the first round. Edits to one file never overlap: when two fixes would, the later one waits for the next\nround.\n\n### 5.4 `ceesafec explain`\n\n`ceesafec explain N0249` prints the code's row of `diagnostics.md` (its meaning and example) and the paragraphs of\n`diagnostics.md` that mention it. An unknown code is exit status 2.\n\n### 5.5 In the language server\n\n`textDocument/codeAction` returns the fixes of the diagnostics that touch the requested range as `quickfix` actions,\neach with its diagnostic and a `WorkspaceEdit` for the document (§2.2). A fix that would edit another file is not\noffered.\n\n## 6. The MCP server (M12 A2)\n\n`ceesafec mcp`",
    525 =>
        " serves the [Model Context Protocol](https://modelcontextprotocol.io) on standard input and output, so an\nAI agent's host (Claude Code, an IDE, any MCP client) can use CeeSafe's compiler, interpreter and documentation as\ntools. It is part of `ceesafec`, with no other dependency, and serves the documents embedded in that `ceesafec`, so the\nanswers always match the compiler that gives them.\n\n```json\n{ \"mcpServers\": { \"ceesafe\": { \"command\": \"ceesafec\", \"args\": [\"mcp\"] } } }\n```\n\n### 6.1 Protocol\n\n- JSON-RPC 2.0, one message per line of UTF-8 on standard input and output; nothing else is written to standard\n  output. The server stops at the end of its input.\n- `initialize` negotiates the protocol version: the client's, if it is one the server supports (`2025-06-18`,\n  `2025-03-26`, `2024-11-05`), otherwise the newest of those. The server declares `tools`, `resources` and `prompts`,\n  none of which change while it runs, and gives brief usage `instructions`.\n- `ping`, `tools/list`, `tools/call`, `resources/list`, `resources/read`, `prompts/list` and `prompts/get` are\n  answered; other requests are `MethodNotFound` (-32601); notifications are ignored.\n- With version `2025-06-18`, each tool declares an `outputSchema` and its result carries `structuredContent`, with the\n  same data as JSON text in `content`. With earlier versions, only `content`.\n- A tool call that the tool cannot carry out (a file it cannot read, a source that does not parse when it needs to)\n  is a result with `isError: true` and the reason. Unknown tools and invalid arguments are `InvalidParams` (-32602).\n  The server never stops on a bad request.\n\n### 6.2 Sources\n\nEvery tool that compiles takes its program as either\n- `files`: an array of `{ \"path\": \"main.cee\", \"text\": \"…\" }`, compiled as one package, with nothing read from disk; or\n- `paths`: an array of `.cee` file paths, read from disk relative to the server's working directory;\n\nplus `profile` (`hosted`, the default, or `freestanding`) and `noFloat` (default false). Paths in results are the\npaths given.\n\n`embed` and `embedText` (`type-system.md` §10.1) read files relative to the source's file on disk: a `paths` file\nembeds below its own directory, and a `files` source, which is no file, embeds nothing (N0362). The server reads\nnothing else for them.\n\n### 6.3 Tools\n\n| Tool | Input (besides §6.2) | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| `ceesafe_check` | | the diagnostics document of §5.1, with fixes |\n| `ceesafe_fix` | | `files`: each file's text with every fix applied, over rounds as `ceesafec fix` does (§5.3); `applied`: the number of fixes; `diagnostics`: what remains. Nothing is written to disk |\n| `ceesafe_format` | `text`, `path` (optional) | `text`: formatted (`formatting.md`); or an error with the syntax diagnostics |\n| `ceesafe_run` | `stdin` (text), `args`, `maxSteps` (default 10,000,000), `maxOutputBytes` (default 65,536) | `outcome` (`exited`, `panicked`, `fault`, `not-compiled`), `exitCode`, `stdout`, `stderr`, `truncated`, and the diagnostics if it did not compile |\n| `ceesafe_emit_c` | `explain`: any of `allocations`, `bounds-checks`, `overflow-checks` | `files`: the generated C (`main.c`, and `ceesafe_exports.h` when there is one; not the runtime); `explanations`: the `--explain-*` text |\n| `ceesafe_explain` | `code` | `code`, `meaning`, `example`, `paragraphs` of `diagnostics.md` (§5.4) |\n| `ceesafe_spec_search` | `query`, `limit` (default 10) | `results`: the sections whose heading or text contain every word of the query, best first: `document`, `heading`, `snippet`, `uri` |\n| `ceesafe_spec_section` | `document` (`type-system`, `grammar`, …), `section` (`31.2`, or heading text) | `text` of that section, with its subsections |\n| `ceesafe_symbol` | `path`, `line`, `column` (1-based) | the type or signature under the position, and its declaration's location, as hover and definition give them (§2.2) |\n| `ceesafe_api` | `name`: a type (`TextWriter`), a member (`TextWriter.decimal`) or a bare member nam",
    526 =>
        "e (`decimal`) | `results`: the entries of `spec/api/core.md`, `spec/api/std.md` and `guide/builtins.md` that document the name (`document`, `heading`, `text`): a type's whole section, a member's entry, or the built-in table rows naming it. With no entry, `suggestions`: up to eight names, the closest by edit distance, the type's real members for `Type.member`, and the entries whose text mentions the member's name |\n\n`ceesafe_run` runs the program in the interpreter (`run --interp`) **sandboxed**:\n- files, sockets and processes are refused (a fault that names the refusal);\n- `maxSteps` statements at most (a fault), and `maxOutputBytes` of each output kept (`truncated` says if more was\n  written; the program goes on);\n- 256 MiB of interpreter memory at most: the heap then reports out of memory, which the program sees as\n  `AllocError.OutOfMemory`; a local or static allocation beyond it is a fault;\n- standard input is `stdin`, the arguments are `args`, and the run has no access to the server's environment.\n\nA fault's exit code is 70 and a panic's 134, as for `run --interp`.\n\n### 6.4 Resources\n\nEach embedded document is a resource `ceesafe://docs/<path>` (for example `ceesafe://docs/docs/type-system.md`,\n`ceesafe://docs/site/guide/basics.md`), `text/markdown`, named by its first heading. `resources/read` returns its text.\nAn unknown URI is the error -32002 (resource not found).\n\n### 6.5 Prompts\n\n| Prompt | Arguments | What it asks |\n|---|---|---|\n| `write-ceesafe` | `task` | To write CeeSafe for the task: the agent guide (`guide/for-agents.md`), then the task, with the edit-check loop of `ceesafe_check` and `ceesafe_fix` |\n| `port-c-to-ceesafe` | `file`, `api` | To port a C file to CeeSafe as C6 and C9 did: imported C structs from the header, safe code over slices, `unsafe` only at the exported boundary, and the C's own tests, a differential test and a benchmark (`cosmoos/c9-report.md`) |\n\n## 7. Agent instructions in packages (M12 A4)\n\nAI coding agents read a file of instructions at the root of a project. `ceesafec new` writes one, so that an agent\nworking in a new CeeSafe package knows from the start what CeeSafe is, which commands check its work, and the mistakes to\navoid. The convention follows Vite+'s (`vp create`).\n\n### 7.1 The CeeSafe section\n\n`AGENTS.md` holds the **CeeSafe section**, between the lines `<!--CEESAFE START-->` and `<!--CEESAFE END-->`: what CeeSafe is and\nwhere its guide for agents is; the package's layout; the commands (`check`, `fix`, `run --interp`, `build`, `fmt`,\n`explain`, `--diagnostics-format=json`); how to use the MCP server; a checklist before finishing; and the common\nmistakes of the agent guide. The text is embedded in `ceesafec` (`templates/AGENTS.md`) and is the same for every\npackage. Everything outside the markers belongs to the package's authors: ceesafec never changes it.\n\n### 7.2 Agents\n\n`--agent` takes one or more of these names, separated by commas or by repeating the option; the default is `agents`.\nEach name writes the files below; `AGENTS.md` is written whenever any agent is chosen, since the others refer to it.\n\n| Name | Agent | Instructions | MCP configuration (`ceesafec mcp`, §6) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `agents` | Codex, Amp, OpenCode and others that read `AGENTS.md` | `AGENTS.md` | — |\n| `claude` | Claude Code | `CLAUDE.md`: `@AGENTS.md` (Claude Code's import) | `.mcp.json` |\n| `gemini` | Gemini CLI | `GEMINI.md`: `@AGENTS.md` (Gemini CLI's import) | `.gemini/settings.json` |\n| `copilot` | GitHub Copilot | `.github/copilot-instructions.md`: a copy of the CeeSafe section | `.vscode/mcp.json` |\n| `cursor` | Cursor | `.cursor/rules/ceesafe.mdc`: a copy of the CeeSafe section, applied always | `.cursor/mcp.json` |\n\nAn unknown name is a usage error (exit status 2), and so is `--agent` with `--no-agent`. The MCP configurations name\nthe server `ceesafe`, with command `ceesafec` and argument `mcp`, in each tool's format: `{\"mcpServers\": {\"ceesafe\": …}}`, or\n`{\"servers\": {\"ceesafe\": {\"typ",
    527 =>
        "e\": \"stdio\", …}}}` for VS Code.\n\n### 7.3 `ceesafec agents`\n\nIn a package, `ceesafec agents [--agent …]` writes the chosen agents' files (default `agents`):\n- a missing file is written as `ceesafec new` writes it;\n- in an instructions file that has the markers, the text between them is replaced with the current CeeSafe section,\n  and the rest is kept; a file without them gets the CeeSafe section appended, after a blank line; a file that only\n  imports `AGENTS.md` (`@AGENTS.md`) is left as it is;\n- an MCP configuration that exists is left as it is, and the command says so: it may hold the authors' other servers.\n\nIt prints what it wrote and what it left. Run it after updating `ceesafec`, so the instructions match the compiler.\n\n## 8. The C compiler (`ceesafec build`)\n\n`ceesafec build` writes C and gives it to a C compiler. It needs no configuration when a usual compiler is installed.\n\n### 8.1 Which compiler\n\n`--cc <compiler>` names it; otherwise `$CC`; otherwise:\n- on Linux and macOS, `cc`;\n- on Windows, the first of these that exists: `clang` on `PATH`; `%ProgramFiles%\\LLVM\\bin\\clang.exe` (where the LLVM\n  installer, `winget install LLVM.LLVM`, puts it); `gcc` on `PATH` (MinGW-w64); `cl` on `PATH`; Visual Studio's `cl`\n  (§8.2). clang and gcc are fully supported; MSVC is best effort (`c-backend.md` §1.1).\n\nWith none, the error names what was looked for and how to install one.\n\n### 8.2 Visual Studio's environment (Windows)\n\nclang on Windows and `cl` compile against Visual Studio's C library and the Windows SDK, which they find through the\nenvironment that Visual Studio's developer prompt sets (`INCLUDE`, `LIB`, `PATH`). When that environment is missing\n(`INCLUDE` unset) and the compiler is clang or `cl`, `ceesafec build` sets it up for the compiler, as the developer prompt\nwould:\n1. find Visual Studio, or its Build Tools, with the C++ tools for this machine: `vswhere -latest -products *\n   -requires Microsoft.VisualStudio.Component.VC.Tools.ARM64` on an ARM64 Windows,\n   `…VC.Tools.x86.x64` otherwise (other products that `vswhere` knows, such as SQL Server Management Studio, have no\n   C++ tools and are not taken);\n2. run its `VC\\Auxiliary\\Build\\vcvarsall.bat` for the machine's architecture (`arm64` or `x64`) and take the\n   environment it leaves;\n3. run the compiler in that environment. The user's own environment is not changed.\n\nWithout Visual Studio, clang still works where the environment is already set; otherwise the error says to install\nVisual Studio's \"Desktop development with C++\" workload (or the Build Tools), or MinGW-w64.\n\n### 8.3 Flags\n\n| Compiler | Flags |\n|---|---|\n| clang, gcc, `cc` | `c-backend.md` §1's: `-std=c11`, the strict warnings, `-ffp-contract=off -fno-math-errno`, `-O2` (or `-g -O0`), `--cflag`s, the C files and the separately compiled packages' objects, the manifests' `-L<dir>` and `-l<name>` (§4.1), `-o <output>`; on Windows also the 8 MiB stack, `-Wl,/STACK:8388608` for clang and `-Wl,--stack,8388608` for gcc. With `--split`, each C file is compiled alone with the same flags and `-c`, at most `--jobs` at once, and the objects are linked with `-g` (for `-g`), the `--cflag`s, the libraries and the stack (`c-backend.md` §2.4) |\n| `cl` (MSVC) | `/nologo /std:c11 /W3 /O2` (or `/Zi /Od` with `-g`), `--cflag`s, `/Fo:` the build directory (so no `.obj` lands in the project), `/Fe:<output>`, `/link /STACK:8388608` (`c-backend.md` §1.1), then the manifests' `/LIBPATH:<dir>` and `<name>.lib` (§4.1) |\n\nA compiler is MSVC when its file name is `cl` or `cl.exe`. `--emit obj` (one relocatable object) needs clang or gcc.\n\n**`--emit lib`** (ADR 0088 §2.1) writes a static library for a host program whose `main` is not CeeSafe's\n(type-system.md §28.6): each C file of the build compiled with `-c` and `--emit obj`'s flags (`--cflag`s included),\nthen archived with `<archiver> rcs` into `<output>.partial.<pid>` (ceesafec's process id, so two builds never share\nit), made afresh, and renamed over the output, s",
    528 =>
        "o a failing\narchiver (`ceesafec: the archiver '<path>' failed`) leaves an earlier archive as it was. The members are one per C\nfile: the runtime's keep their names (`ceesafe_rt_core.o`, …); every other one is `<program>_` and the C file's path\nwithout `.c`, each `_` written `__` and each directory separator `_s` (`main.c` gives `<program>_main.o`;\nwith `--split`, `c/embed/src/lib.c` gives `<program>_c_sembed_ssrc_slib.o`), so two paths never give one name; a\npackage's separately compiled C files are `<program>_ceesafe_package_<k>.o`. Two C files whose member names differ\nonly in case would be one file on macOS and Windows, so such a build fails (status 1) naming the member. The default\noutput is `<name>.a` (`out/<name>.a` in a package). Unlike `-r`, an archive keeps the host's LTO: with `--cflag\n-flto=thin` (or `-flto`) every member is bitcode, which the host's link optimises with its own code (a ThinLTO host\nthat builds its own C with `-fno-split-lto-unit`, as Bun does, needs `--cflag -fno-split-lto-unit` too). One archive\nper host binary: two archives built separately each carry the runtime and clash as two objects do (ADR 0088 §2.7).\n- **The C `main`.** A package with a CeeSafe `main` gets no C `main` in its archive, since the host has its own (two\n  would not link); `--lib-main` keeps it, for an archive linked into an executable alone.\n- **The host's headers**, with `--emit lib`, are written beside the output: `ceesafe/embed.h` (its\n  freestanding form in a freestanding build), and, when the program has exports, the exports header, named by\n  `--exports-header <name>` (default `ceesafe_exports.h`). `-o build/libtext.a` gives `build/ceesafe/embed.h` and\n  `build/ceesafe_exports.h`, so a host compiles with `-I build`. Each starts with `/* Generated by ceesafec`, and a\n  file already at either path is replaced only if it starts so too: otherwise the build fails (status 1) before\n  compiling, naming the file, and leaves it as it was. `--emit obj` writes no header, as in 0.27.0.\n- `--ar <archiver>` names the archiver, a path or a name looked up on `PATH`; by default it is `llvm-ar` beside an\n  LLVM clang (`clang-18` beside `llvm-ar-18`; the C compiler found as for `--emit obj`, on `PATH` when it has no\n  directory), else `ar` on `PATH` (Apple's `ar` indexes bitcode members).\n- Usage errors (status 2): `--ar` or `--lib-main` without `--emit lib`; `--ld` with `--emit lib`, which archives\n  instead of linking; `--emit lib` when no archiver is found, or `--ar`'s is not, naming `--ar`.\n\n`--jobs N` (`build --split`, ADR 0082 §2.5) compiles at most `N` C files at once; the default is the number of CPUs\n(`getconf _NPROCESSORS_ONLN`, or `NUMBER_OF_PROCESSORS` on Windows), and `--jobs 1` compiles them one at a time in the\nbytewise order of their paths. The objects are linked in that order, and the C compiler's output is printed in it,\nwhatever finishes first, so neither the C nor the output depends on `N`; an output identical to an earlier file's\n(a bad `--cflag`'s error, printed by every compile) is printed once, then `ceesafec: the C compiler printed the same\nfor <n> of the C files`. `--jobs` without `--split` is a usage error (status 2), since the C is then one command.\nWith `cl`, each file is compiled with `/c`, `/Fo:` its own object and, for `-g`, `/FS`, and the objects are linked by\n`cl /Fe:`. On macOS a `-g` build's debug\ninformation is gathered into `<output>.dSYM` by `dsymutil` after the link, as the compiler driver does when it compiles\nand links in one command, since the objects are removed with the build directory. A build without `--split` is one\n`main.c` and the runtime's files in one compiler command, as before.\n\n### 8.4 The output\n\nThe executable is `out/<name>` in a package, or `-o <path>`. On Windows, a path without an extension gets `.exe`, so\n`ceesafec build` makes `out\\hello.exe`. The output's directory is created if it does not exist.\n\n## 9. Porting C to CeeSafe: `c2cee`\n\nCosmoOS's roadmap 2.0 moves kernel C int",
    529 =>
        "o CeeSafe one file at a time (SA2, SA3), and\nevery port repeats the same mechanical steps: map the C types, declare each function the file calls, choose `@header`s\nthat do not include the port's own header back, find what must stay in a C floor, rename members that are keywords,\nwire the build, and run the same checks. `integrations/cosmoos/c2cee.py` does the analysis and the checks, and drafts\nthe `.cee` file, the floor and the build lines (§9.4). Since 2026-10-31 CosmoOS's kernel language\nis CeeSafe: a finished port lands in its tree as a `.cee` file, and its build generates the C with the ceesafec it pins\n(its `scripts/cee-gen.py` and `build/cee.mk`; the C is not committed). It reads C through clang's own front end (`-Xclang -ast-dump=json`), so\nit needs nothing but clang and Python 3, and never edits a file. It parses with the kernel's include path (CosmoOS's `KERNEL_CFLAGS`), the headers the build generates\nfrom CeeSafe (`out/cee/kernel/include`, written by `make cee`) first.\n\n### 9.1 `c2cee.py analyse <file.c> [--header <h>] [--cosmoos <tree>] [--arch x86_64|aarch64] [--json]`\n\nParses `<file.c>` twice, as a debug build and as a release one (`CONFIG_DEBUG=1`, `0`), with CosmoOS's include paths\nfor `--arch` (default x86_64), and reports:\n\n- **Functions** the file defines: their C and CeeSafe signatures, and whether a module export (`EXPORT_SYMBOL`) names them.\n- **Calls**, each callee with the header that declares it and what that means for the port:\n  - a function: an `extern \"C\"` declaration;\n  - `static inline` in a header: callable through that header (type-system §28.2);\n  - an ordinary function in one build and `static inline` in the other (lockdep's hooks): it **must** be declared\n    through its header, or the build where it is inline fails to link (CosmoOS #499);\n  - variadic (`panic`, `klog`): a C floor;\n  - through a function pointer: a C floor when the pointer is a member of an imported struct (§31.1).\n- **Builtins**: `__builtin_return_address` (CeeSafe's `callerAddress()` in an export), `__atomic_*` (an atomic view of the\n  field; a `bool` field needs a `u32`, since lib/core's `AtomicBool` is four bytes), `__atomic_thread_fence` (a floor:\n  CeeSafe has no fence).\n- **Macros** expanded in the file's functions, from their expansion locations: `wait_event*` (wait_floor.c's helpers),\n  `list_for_each_entry*` (a loop over lib/kernel's `List` or `offsetOf`), `might_sleep` (`wait_floor_check_sleep`),\n  `KASSERT`, `WARN`, `EXPORT_SYMBOL` (a floor).\n- **Locals of struct type** that hold C state (a wait entry, a timer): a CeeSafe local cannot (§33.1), so a floor helper.\n- With `--header <h>`, the **structs it defines**, field by field: the CeeSafe type, members whose names are CeeSafe keywords\n  (renamed, or `@cname` on an imported struct), `const` pointer members (a `@repr(C)` struct's carry no `const`),\n  fields used atomically, and every other file in the tree that names a member.\n- **`@header` candidates**: the headers that declare what the file uses, each marked unusable when it includes `<h>`,\n  directly or not, since the generated types' header is included from `<h>` (ADR 0030; `thread.h` → `completion.h` →\n  `wait.h`).\n\nThe report is text, or JSON with `--json`. It exits 0 unless clang fails.\n\n### 9.2 `c2cee.py verify [--cosmoos <tree>] [--arch x86_64|aarch64|both] [--no-boot] [--max-load <n>] [--ceesafec <path>]`\n\nRuns a port's checks in a CosmoOS tree, in order, with `CEESAFEC` set to `--ceesafec` (default `$CEESAFEC`, else\n`ceesafec` on `PATH`, else `~/.ceesafe/bin/ceesafec`: the release CosmoOS pins, which its Make runs on the tree's\nCeeSafe sources), and prints one line per step and a summary. A tree without `scripts/cee-gen.py` (from before\nCosmoOS's move to CeeSafe) is refused.\n1. `make cee-check` (every CeeSafe source compiles with the pinned ceesafec, and the negative sources are refused as\n   annotated);\n2. `make host-test`, with every recorded trace digest it prints;\n3. `make check-docs`;\n4. p",
    530 =>
        "er architecture: the debug and the release build, `make analyze` (and, for the first architecture, `make\n   fuzz-build`, whose shims define some of a port's functions over its types), and unless `--no-boot` a debug and a release boot\n   (`make test`). Before each boot it waits until the one-minute load average is under `--max-load` (default 8),\n   since the boot tests' time bounds fail on a loaded host. A boot that fails after the load average reached the\n   bound at any point during it (another job started meanwhile; it is sampled every five seconds) is run once more\n   on a quiet host: both lines are printed, and the second\n   decides.\n\nIt exits 1 if any step failed, naming the first.\n\n### 9.3 Checks of the tool\n\n`c2cee.py selftest` analyses `integrations/cosmoos/c2cee/fixture.c` with `fixture.h` and compares the report with\n`fixture.expected.json`, and drafts its port (§9.4) and compares that with `fixture.draft.cee` and\n`fixture_floor.draft.c`. The fixture holds one of each case of §9.1.\n\n### 9.4 `c2cee.py port <file.c> --header <h> --out <dir> [--cosmoos <tree>] [--arch …]`\n\nWrites a **draft** of the port into `<dir>`, never into the tree: `<name>.cee`, `<name>_floor.c` and `BUILD.md` (the\n`ceesafec check` of the finished port, CosmoOS's `cee-gen.py`, `kernel.mk` and `host.mk` lines, where the `.cee` file goes\nand the C it replaces, and what to change in\n`<h>`). The draft is unsafe CeeSafe that mirrors the\nC, statement by statement, for a person to finish and review; every place it could not translate, or translated with a\ndecision someone must check, carries `TODO(c2cee)` with the reason, and it never drops a construct silently.\n\n- **Names** stay C's (functions, parameters, locals, statics), so the draft reads beside the original; a name that is a\n  CeeSafe keyword gets `_` appended. Each function's body is one `unsafe` block, and its doc comment has a `Safety:` line\n  marked `TODO(c2cee)`. A function `EXPORT_SYMBOL` names, or that the header declares, is `@export(\"name\")`; one the\n  file passes as a function pointer is `@callback`.\n- **Types**: `<h>`'s structs become `@repr(C)` structs (a keyword member renamed, `fn` to `callback`, `class` to\n  `lock_class`, otherwise with `_` appended, and listed in `BUILD.md`); every other struct the file uses is imported,\n  with the fields it reads; typedefs of integers are their CeeSafe integer, of structs `@ctype` imports.\n- **Declarations**: an `extern \"C\"` block per kind: callees whose headers may be `@header`s (§9.1), and the rest without\n  a header; the floor's functions; `@constant` macros used in static initialisers.\n- **Statics**: integer, `bool`, pointer and array statics, and those initialised by a C constant macro (`SPINLOCK_INIT`),\n  are CeeSafe `static mut`s; a static of a C struct type, or one whose initialiser names the static itself\n  (`WAITQUEUE_INIT(name)`), stays in the floor, and the draft reaches it through a floor function that returns its\n  address.\n- **Statements and expressions**: blocks, declarations, `if`, `while`, `for` (as a `while`, unless its body has a\n  `continue`), `do` (as a `loop`), `return`, `break`, `continue`, assignments and compound assignments, `++`/`--` as\n  statements, calls, `?:` (an `if` expression), casts (`as` when it cannot lose information, else `truncate`), pointer\n  comparisons with null (`isNull()`), `*p` (`read`/`write`), `p->f` (`Ptr.toBorrow`, and for a write a `let` of\n  `Ptr.toBorrowMut` first), `&x` (`borrow mut … as *mut T`), `sizeof` (`layoutOf`), string literals, the `__atomic`\n  builtins on integers and pointers (an atomic view), `__builtin_return_address` (`callerAddress()`). Anything else,\n  `switch`, `goto` and `continue` in a `for` among them, is a `TODO(c2cee)` with the C text.\n- **Macros**: `KASSERT(c)` becomes `if (!(c))` and a floor call that panics with the condition's text; `might_sleep()`\n  `wait_floor_check_sleep`; `wait_event(q, c)` and `wait_event_timeout(q, c, t)` `wait_floor_until(_timeout)` with t",
    531 =>
        "he\n  condition as a `@callback` over a context struct of the locals it reads; other macros are translated from their\n  expansion.\n- **The floor**: one C function per call of a variadic function (`panic`, `klog`, `kwarn`), with the format fixed and\n  the other arguments as parameters; the statics that stay in C, with their accessors; file-scope `STATIC_ASSERT`s; the\n  `EXPORT_SYMBOL` lines.\n- **Builds**: a function that differs between the debug and the release build (an `#if CONFIG_DEBUG` inside it, or\n  present in one build only) is drafted from the debug build with a `TODO(c2cee)` naming the difference: a draft from\n  one build alone would change the other.\n\n## 10. Tests: `ceesafec test` (ADR 0049)\n\n### 10.1 Declaring tests\n\nA test is a top-level function marked `@test` (type-system.md §28.1): no parameters, not generic, not exported,\n`async` or not, returning `void` or `Result<void, E>`, where an `Err` is its failure. Any other shape, and a call of a\ntest from anything but the generated harness, is N0348.\n\n- **Where tests are.** Beside the code in the package's sources, or in its test directories (`tests = [\"tests\"]` in\n  `[package]`, by default `tests`). They are part of the same package and see its private items.\n- **Build and check.** `check` checks the test directories too. `build` leaves them out, and emits no `@test`\n  function. Only the root package's tests are run; a dependency's are not.\n- **Checks** (lib/std's `testing.cee`): `Check.isTrue`, `isFalse`, `equalU64`, `equalI64`, `equalText`, `equalBytes`\n  and `fail`, and `CheckResult<T, E>.isOk` and `isErr`. Each returns `Result<void, TestFailure>`, used with `?`; a\n  failure holds its message. Generic equality waits for an equality interface.\n\n### 10.2 The command\n\n`ceesafec test [--filter TEXT] [--list] [--interp] [--jobs N] [--timeout SECONDS] [--sweep alloc|cancel]\n[--format text|json]`, in a package:\n\n1. It checks the package with its tests and writes the harness and the list of tests to `out/ceesafe-test/`.\n2. It builds the harness once, as `out/<name>-tests`.\n3. It runs each test whose name contains `--filter` (all by default), in the order of their names (bytewise), `--jobs`\n   at a time (default 1), each as its own process. `--list` prints the names instead. A `--filter` that no name\n   contains is an error (exit status 1), so a mistyped filter cannot pass. `ceesafec test --help` lists the options.\n\nEach test's outcome:\n\n| Outcome | When |\n|---|---|\n| passed | it returned `Ok`, or `void` |\n| failed | it returned `Err`; its message is shown (`IoError`'s and `AsyncError`'s message, a `TestFailure`'s, or for the test's own enum the variant: `returned Err(Mine.Broke)`), and its captured output |\n| panicked | the runtime's panic report, with its location |\n| crashed | a signal or exception: `abort`, a fault (a status of 129 to 192 on POSIX, as `Process.capture` reports a signal; an exception's code on Windows) |\n| exited | the test ended the process itself with another status (`Process.exit(3)`) |\n| timed out | it ran past `--timeout` (default 60 s) |\n| leaked | it passed, but allocations were live at the end (`CEESAFE_ALLOC_REPORT`) |\n| skipped | with `--interp`, it calls C, which the interpreter cannot run |\n\n- **Exit status.** 1 if any test is not passed or skipped, else 0.\n- **JSON.** `--format json` prints one document: each test's name, outcome, milliseconds, message, standard output and\n  error; the sweeps' failures and runs; and the counts.\n- **`--interp`** runs each test with `ceesafec run --interp` on the harness: the virtual clock and the seeded run order\n  of type-system.md §40.8. The timeout holds there too, and so does the leak check: the interpreter counts allocations as\n  the runtime does. The sweeps are native only: `--interp --sweep` is a usage error (exit status 2).\n- **`--sweep alloc`** runs each passing test again once per allocation attempt, the k-th failing. **`--sweep cancel`**\n  runs it once per suspension point, the k-th cancelling it",
    532 =>
        "s root task, sampled past 200 as `tools/cancel_sweep.py`\n  is. A run may fail; it may not panic, crash, time out or leak.\n\n### 10.3 The harness protocol (version 1)\n\nThe generated function `ceesafeTestMain` is the harness's entry. An executable package's own `main` is checked and\ncompiled but not run.\n\n- **Listing.** `--ceesafe-list` prints `ceesafe-test 1` and then one name a line.\n- **Running.** `--ceesafe-test <name> <fail-alloc-at> <cancel-at> <timeout-ms>` runs one test (0: none for the last\n  three):\n  - lib/std's `TestRun.begin` turns on the runtime's allocation and cancellation reports and the two points\n    (`ceesafe_test_begin`), and arms a watchdog thread (`ceesafe_thread_watchdog`). The watchdog prints\n    `ceesafe-test 1 timeout` and ends the process with status 124.\n  - The harness then writes `ceesafe-test 1 passed`, or `ceesafe-test 1 failed`, the message and `ceesafe-test 1 end`,\n    to standard error.\n  - The runner reads these lines, the panic report, the allocation report (`ceesafe alloc: attempts=… live=…`), the\n    cancellation report (`points=…`) and the exit status.\n",
    533 =>
        "# Type System and Static Semantics — M1 subset\n\nStatus: normative for Milestone 1 (increment 1b implements it). Later milestones extend these\nrules. Where `language-spec.md` describes a later feature, it does not apply until then. Error\ncodes are listed in `diagnostics.md`.\n\n## 1. Types\n\n| Type | Values | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `i8 i16 i32 i64` | two's complement, −2^(n−1) … 2^(n−1)−1 | |\n| `u8 u16 u32 u64` | 0 … 2^n−1 | |\n| `isize` / `usize` | pointer width of the **target** (64 on every M1 target) | from `TargetInfo`, never the host |\n| `bool` | `true`, `false` | |\n| `void` | no value | function return type only |\n| `never` | no value; the expression does not complete | a function's return type only (§15.3, ADR 0074 §2.2) |\n\nNot in M1 (error N0216): floats, `char`, strings as values, user-defined types, references. Floats came in M11\n(`f32`, `f64`, §35); character literals are integers (§4).\nIn M1 a string literal was allowed **only** as a direct argument of `Console.write`/`writeLine`;\nsince M6c it is a `StringView` value (§22.2).\n\n`void` may appear only as a return type (writing `-> void` is the same as omitting the\nreturn type). A *never* expression is accepted wherever any type is expected.\n\n`never` (ADR 0074 §2.2) names the bottom type, and only as the return type of a function or of a struct's method: a\ncall of one is a call of type *never* (§9), as `Process.exit` is. Anywhere else (a parameter, a field, a local, a type\nargument, a function type, an `async` function, an interface method, an `extern` function) it is N0226: \"'never' can\nonly be a function's return type\". A `never` function does not return: the end of its body must be unreachable\n(N0213), and `return` in it is N0215. A `let` whose initializer has type *never* has no value to bind (N0224). In C,\nthe function is `_Noreturn void` (`c-backend.md`); in NIR, a call of one ends its block with `Unreachable`. `never` is\na type name, not a keyword: a value named `never` is unaffected.\n\n## 2. Names and scopes\n\nA **package** is the unit of compilation and of naming (ADR 0013, `tooling.md` §4): its files, its dependencies, and\n`lib/core`. A build checks the package being built together with every package it depends on, directly or not.\n\n- **Package scope.** All files of a package share one item scope. Items are referenced by simple name. Two items of\n  one package with the same name are an error (N0201), even in different files. `namespace` declarations are accepted\n  and have no effect (retired by ADR 0013; they will become an error).\n- **Dependencies** (M10 T5). A package names its dependencies in its manifest (`tooling.md` §4). A dependency's\n  **public** items are reached by qualifying them with the dependency's name:\n  - in a type, `geometry.Point`, `geometry.Pair<u8>`;\n  - in an expression, `geometry.area(p)`, `geometry.LIMIT`, `geometry.Shape.Circle(2)`, a struct literal\n    `geometry.Point { x: 1, y: 2 }`; in a pattern, `geometry.Shape.Circle(r)`.\n\n  A static function of a generic type of a dependency is called through an import (`Pair<u8>.make()`), since\n  `geometry.Pair<u8>.make()` does not parse (grammar.md §2.1: in an expression, generic arguments follow a simple\n  name only).\n- **Dependency names and items.** In a package, no item or type parameter may have the name of one of its\n  dependencies (N0315): they share the namespace `geometry.X` is resolved in. A parameter, local, loop variable,\n  closure parameter or pattern binding may (ADR 0074 §2.10):\n  - in its scope, `geometry.x` in an expression is the local's member, and the dependency's values are unreachable;\n  - types and patterns are unaffected (a local is never a type), so `geometry.Point` in a type, a struct literal\n    `geometry.Point { … }` and a pattern `geometry.Shape.Circle(r)` keep naming the package's items;\n  - where `x` is also a public item of the dependency, `geometry.x` is warning N0401, since the item is hidden.\n\n  Elsewhere `geometry.x` means the depend",
    534 =>
        "ency's item, and the compiler resolves it before type checking\n  (`PackageNames`), with a syntactic scope walk to find the locals.\n- **Imports.** `import geometry;` at the top of a file brings every public item of the dependency `geometry` into\n  that file's scope; `import geometry.Point;` brings one (N0311 if it has none of that name, N0312 if it is not\n  public). An import applies to its file only. The name before the `.` must be a dependency of the package (N0310).\n- **Visibility between packages.** Only `public` items are visible to other packages, and of a public struct only its\n  `public` fields and methods; `internal`, the default, keeps an item within its package, and `private` within its\n  file (§12). Naming another package's non-public item is N0312. A public function, constant, field or struct whose\n  signature names a type its dependents cannot see (a public function returning an internal struct) is N0314. An\n  `impl I for T` applies wherever `I` and `T` are both visible.\n- **Built-in scope** (outside the package scope, so a user item hides a built-in): `Console.write`,\n  `Console.writeLine`, `Process.exit`, `truncate`, and the primitive type names.\n- **`lib/core`** (and, hosted, `lib/std`) is in scope in every package, unqualified. A package may declare an item with\n  one of their names (ADR 0075 §5; before it, N0201): the package's item shadows the library's in that package, while\n  the libraries' own code still finds its own. So a name a release adds to a library is not a source change. In C\n  the library's item is then emitted as `ceeT_lib_…`, `ceeF_lib_…` and the like.\n- **Function scope.** Parameters, then nested block scopes. A local is visible from the end of\n  its declaration to the end of its block.\n- **No shadowing of locals.** Declaring a local or parameter whose name is already a local or\n  parameter in scope is an error (N0203), as in C#. Shadowing an *item* with a local is allowed.\n- **Lookup order** for a simple name: locals (innermost first), the package's items, the file's imports, `lib/core`,\n  built-ins. Two imports that both provide a name are an error where the name is used (N0313), not where it is\n  imported. For `a.b`: if `a` is a local or an item of the package or of `lib/core`, `.b` is a member as before;\n  otherwise, `a` being a dependency (and so not an item, N0315), `a.b` is that dependency's item `b`, unless a local\n  `a` is in scope there (ADR 0074 §2.10). `T.NAME`, with `T` a struct or an enum, is its associated constant (§10).\n- **Messages and C names** write an item of a dependency with its package's name, `geometry.Point`; the C backend\n  gives it a prefixed name, `ceeT_8geometry_Point` (`c-backend.md` §2.1).\n\n## 3. Bindings and mutability\n\n- `let x = e;` declares an immutable local and **requires** an initialiser (N0220).\n- `let _ = e;` (ADR 0072) binds nothing: `e`, any expression, is evaluated and its value destroyed at the end of the\n  statement (a non-Copy value is consumed). `_` may be used any number of times; `var _` and `let _: T` are N0100.\n- `var x = e;` / `var x: T = e;` / `var x: T;` declare a mutable local. Without an initialiser\n  the type is required (N0221), and the variable must be definitely assigned before every read (§7).\n- Without an annotation, a local's type is the type of its initialiser. An initialiser of type\n  `void` is an error (N0224).\n- Parameters and `const` items are immutable. Assigning to them, or to a `let`, is N0211.\n- Assignment targets in M1 are locals only. Member targets (`a.b = …`) are N0216.\n\n## 4. Integer literals\n\n- A suffixed literal (`7u8`) has the suffix's type.\n- An unsuffixed literal takes the **expected type** from context (§5) if that type is an\n  integer type. With no integer expectation it defaults to `i32`.\n- The value must be representable in the literal's type (N0206). A literal directly under\n  unary minus is checked as a negative value, so `-128i8` and `-9223372036854775808` (as `i64`) are valid.\n- **Character",
    535 =>
        " literals** (M11, G4a). `'k'` is an integer literal whose value is the character's Unicode scalar value\n  (`grammar.md` §1.6, escapes included: `'\\n'` is 10). It takes its type from context like an unsuffixed literal,\n  so `c == 'k'` with `c: u8` compares bytes, and `'0'..'9'` is a range. With no integer expectation it is a `u32`.\n  In an 8-bit type it must be ASCII (at most `0x7F`), since a larger scalar value is more than one byte in UTF-8 and\n  would not equal any byte of the text (N0206). It is a pattern too (§15.2). CeeSafe has no `char` type: text is UTF-8\n  bytes (§22.2), and a character type is left for when text gains character iteration.\n\n## 5. Expected types (bidirectional checking)\n\nEach expression is checked against an optional expected type:\n\n- local initialiser → the annotated type; assignment value → the target's type;\n  argument → the parameter type; `return` value → the return type; `const` value → its\n  declared type; `if`/`while` condition → `bool`; each value branch of an `if` or `match` expression → the\n  expression's expected type (§15.3).\n- An expression's *actual* type must equal the expected type, unless it is *never* (N0205).\n- **Flexible operands.** An expression is *flexible* if it consists only of unsuffixed integer\n  literals combined with unary `-`, parentheses and the arithmetic/bitwise binary operators, or (M11) if it is an\n  `if` or `match` expression whose expression branches are all flexible (§15.3). Since M11 (G7), unsuffixed float\n  literals combined the same way are flexible too; they take an expected `f32` or `f64` instead (§35.2).\n  For a binary operator whose operands must have the same type:\n  1. if the operator's result type is the operand type and an integer type is expected, both\n     operands are checked against it;\n  2. otherwise, if exactly one operand is flexible, the other operand is checked first\n     (without expectation) and its type becomes the flexible operand's expected type;\n  3. otherwise both are checked without expectation (flexible ones default to `i32`).\n\n  So `x + 1` with `x: u8` types `1` as `u8`, and `let y: u64 = 2 * 3;` types both literals as `u64`.\n\n## 6. Operators\n\nAll binary operators require both operands to have the **same type**. There are no implicit\nconversions.\n\n| Operators | Operand types | Result | Runtime behaviour (implemented in 1c) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `+ - *` | integer | same | overflow → panic |\n| `/ %` | integer | same | zero divisor → panic. `MIN / -1` → panic (overflow). `/` truncates toward zero, `%` has the sign of the dividend, and `MIN % -1` is `0` |\n| `& \\| ^` | **unsigned** integer | same | |\n| `<< >>` | left: **unsigned** integer; right: any integer type | left type | amount `< 0` or `≥ bit width` → panic. `>>` is logical |\n| `== !=` | integer or `bool`; also floats, views, pointers, fieldless enums, slices and arrays (§16.2), `String` (its text), and a type `@derive(Equal)` gave equality (§28.7) | `bool` | |\n| `< <= > >=` | integer | `bool` | |\n| `&& \\|\\|` | `bool` | `bool` | short-circuit |\n| unary `-` | **signed** integer | same | `-MIN` → panic |\n| unary `!` | `bool` | `bool` | |\n\n**Explicit overflow behaviour** (M11, G4b; OD-6). The operators panic on overflow. Where a program wants another\nbehaviour, every integer type has nine methods. Each takes one argument of the receiver's type, and a flexible\nliteral adapts:\n\n| Method | Result | On overflow |\n|---|---|---|\n| `x.wrappingAdd(y)`, `x.wrappingSub(y)`, `x.wrappingMul(y)` | `T` | the result modulo 2^bits, in two's complement for signed types |\n| `x.checkedAdd(y)`, `x.checkedSub(y)`, `x.checkedMul(y)` | `T?` | `null` (otherwise `Some` of the result) |\n| `x.saturatingAdd(y)`, `x.saturatingSub(y)`, `x.saturatingMul(y)` | `T` | the nearest of `T`'s minimum and maximum |\n\nTwo more take one argument the same way and cannot overflow (ADR 0063): `x.min(y)` and `x.max(y)`, of result `T`,\nthe smaller and the larger operand (`x` when they are equal).\n\nO",
    536 =>
        "n signed types (ADR 0063 §2.5), `x.abs()` is the magnitude as a `T`. It panics at `T`'s minimum, whose magnitude\n`T` cannot hold, exactly as `-x` does, reported at the call. `x.unsignedAbs()` is the magnitude in the unsigned type\nof the same width and never panics. Neither exists on unsigned types (N0234).\n\n**Bit methods** (ADR 0071 §2.3). On every unsigned type, `x.rotateLeft(n)`, `x.rotateRight(n)`, `x.wrappingShl(n)` and\n`x.wrappingShr(n)` take a `u32` amount (a flexible literal adapts), taken modulo the bit width, and are of type `T`;\n`x.leadingZeros()`, `x.trailingZeros()` and `x.countOnes()` are a `u32` (the width for a zero `x`). On a signed type\nthey are N0234, as the bit operators are restricted to unsigned types. `x.byteSwap()` is on every integer type: the\nbytes in the other order, of type `T` (two's complement for signed types; a one-byte type is unchanged).\n\nNone of them panics except `abs`. Their names are reserved on integer types: an `impl` for an integer type cannot define a method\nwith one of these names (N0228). In C they are `static inline` helpers of `rt.h` with no undefined behaviour: a\nwrapping operation computes in the unsigned type of the same width and converts back with `cee_as_iN`\n(`c-backend.md` §6).\n\nBitwise operators and shifts are restricted to unsigned types in M1. This avoids the\nimplementation-defined and undefined parts of C's signed bit operations. Reinterpret\nwith `truncate<u32>(x)`. Relaxing this later is backward compatible. A shift amount that is a\nflexible literal is typed as `u32`. Other mismatches are N0207.\n\n## 7. Conversions\n\n- `e as T` is allowed only when **every** value of the source type is representable in `T`\n  (N0208 otherwise):\n  - identity;\n  - `uN → uM` and `iN → iM` for M ≥ N;\n  - `uN → iM` for M > N;\n  - `bool →` any integer type (`false`→0, `true`→1).\n  `isize`/`usize` use the target width. Floats (M11, §35.4): `f32 → f64`, and the integers that a float type holds\n  exactly.\n- `truncate<T>(e)`: `e` of any integer type, `T` any integer type. The result is `e`\n  modulo 2^bits(T), reinterpreted in `T` (two's complement). It is always defined. Use it for\n  narrowing and for signed↔unsigned reinterpretation.\n- `checkedTruncate<T>(e)` and `saturatingTruncate<T>(e)` (ADR 0074 §2.9): `e` of any integer type, `T` any integer\n  type. `checkedTruncate` gives `T?`: `e`'s value when `T` holds it, else `null`. `saturatingTruncate` gives `T`:\n  `e`'s value when `T` holds it, else `T`'s least value for an `e` below `T`'s range and its greatest for one above.\n  Both are always defined. They also convert floats (§35.4).\n- The three spellings say what may happen to the value: `as` never loses information; `truncate` wraps;\n  `checkedTruncate` and `saturatingTruncate` check or clamp (`checkedTruncate<i64>(n)!` for a `usize` `n`).\n\n## 8. Calls and statements\n\n- A callee must be a package function, or one of the built-ins below (N0210 otherwise).\n  Functions are not values in M1 (since M11 they are, §36.1). The argument count must match (N0209), and each argument is\n  checked against its parameter type.\n- Built-ins:\n  - `Console.write(x)` and `Console.writeLine(x)`: `x` is an integer, a `bool`, or a string\n    literal. `writeLine()` with no argument writes a newline. Both return `void`.\n  - `Process.exit(code: i32)`: has type *never*.\n  - `truncate<T>(e)`: see §7. Exactly one type argument (N0216 for other generic arguments).\n  - `panic(message: StringView)` (ADR 0075 §2.8): has type *never*. It panics with the program's message:\n    `ceesafe panic: <message> at <file>:<line>:<column>`. In a freestanding program the environment's `ceesafe_panic`\n    gets `CEE_PANIC_EXPLICIT` (\"explicit panic\") and the message is not shown.\n  - `assert(condition)` and `assert(condition, message)`: `void`. When the condition is false, they panic with\n    \"assertion failed\" or the message. The message is evaluated only then. Assertions are always checked; no build\n    leaves them out",
    537 =>
        " (`debugAssert`, below, is the one that release builds leave out).\n  - `debugAssert(condition)` and `debugAssert(condition, message)` (ADR 0087 §2.5): `void`. They are checked in every\n    build, like any call, but evaluated only when debug assertions are on: under `build -g` (unless\n    `--no-debug-assertions`) or `--debug-assertions` (`build`, `run`), under `ceesafec test`, and in the interpreter\n    (unless `run --interp --no-debug-assertions`). When they are off, nothing of the call is compiled: the C is\n    `#if CEE_DEBUG_ASSERTIONS`, which the program defines as 0 or 1. A failure panics as `assert` does. Since the call\n    may vanish, its arguments may change nothing that exists before it (N0360):\n    - no assignment, `mut` argument or `borrow mut this` call on a place not declared inside the arguments, no move of\n      one (`debugAssert(consume(v))`), and no way out of the arguments but their end: no exit from the function\n      (`?`), no `break` or `continue` to a loop outside them, and no process exit (`Process.exit`), directly or\n      through a call. A move of a place used after the call is N0360 at the call as well as N0250 at the use;\n    - no write through a raw pointer or to a `static mut` (reading one is allowed);\n    - no `sleep` or `suspend` effect (§32.1): no I/O, no `await`. Allocating is allowed, and so is freeing what the\n      arguments allocated;\n    - no call of a C function, even one declared `@effects()`, and no call through a closure, a function value or a\n      `dyn` method. lib/core's atomic loads (`AtomicU32.load`, …) and `Cell.get` only read and are allowed; atomic\n      stores, swaps, fetches and compare-exchanges, lock acquisition and `RefCell.read`/`write` are not;\n    - a call is allowed when its callee's body, and everything it calls or drops, keeps these rules, writes through\n      its own `borrow mut` parameters aside (the caller's `mut` argument is what is checked). Drops count: a temporary\n      the arguments make is dropped inside the call, so its `drop` block is checked as a call. The checker infers\n      this per function with §32.1's machinery, as an internal fourth fact that no `@effects` list names.\n\n    To assert through something the rules refuse, bind the value to a local before the call, or put an `assert` in\n    `if (cfg(\"NAME\")) { … }` (ADR 0080 §3.3).\n  - `replace(mut place, value) -> T` (ADR 0075 §2.11): puts `value` in the place and returns the old value.\n    `swap(mut a, mut b)` exchanges two places of one type (N0205 otherwise). Each argument is `mut` and a place\n    (N0249, N0247). Neither leaves a place moved-from, so both work for any type, a field of a struct with a `drop`\n    block included: `replace(mut this.buffer, Vector<u8>.create())` is how a method gives a field up.\n  - A package's own function or item of one of these names shadows the built-in (§2).\n- An expression statement must be a call (N0225). `1 + 2;` is rejected; `let _ = 1 + 2;` discards any value (§3).\n- `break`/`continue` must be inside `while` or `loop` (N0214).\n- `return;` only in a `void` function. `return e;` only in a non-`void` function (N0215), except that `return v;` of a\n  unit local is `return;` (§20.4). Neither is allowed in a `never` function (§1), which does not return (N0215).\n\n## 9. Control flow facts\n\n- **Definite assignment.** A local may be read only if it is assigned on every path from its\n  declaration to the read (N0212). Compound assignment (`x += 1`) reads `x`. Code that is\n  unreachable is not checked. M1 has no moves, so the analysis is a forward must-analysis\n  over the structured statements; it moves to NIR in M2.\n- **Termination.** A statement *may complete* unless it is a `return`, `break` or\n  `continue`, or a call of type *never*. It also may not complete if it is a block containing\n  such a statement, an `if` with an `else` whose two branches both cannot complete, or a `loop`\n  with no `break` that targets it. A `while` loop may always complete, whatever",
    538 =>
        " its condition:\n  write `loop` for infinite loops. An `if` or `match` expression (§15.3) cannot complete when none of its\n  branches can: its condition or scrutinee does not complete, or every branch is a block that cannot complete.\n- **Missing return.** If the end of a non-`void` function body may be reached, that is N0213. For a function\n  returning `never` (§1), the end must be unreachable too: every path ends in a call of type *never* or a `loop`\n  without `break`.\n\n## 10. Constants\n\n- `const N: T = e;` where `e` is a constant expression: literals, other constants, and unary,\n  binary, `as` and `truncate` applied to constant expressions. Calls are not constant (N0217), except `embed` and\n  `embedText` (§10.1) and a constant view's `.bytes()` (below). `==` and `!=` on constant views compare their bytes\n  (`\"a\" + \"b\" == \"ab\"` is `true`).\n- Constants are evaluated at compile time with the runtime semantics of §6. An operation that\n  would panic at runtime (overflow, division by zero, bad shift) is a compile error (N0217).\n- A constant that depends on itself is N0218.\n- Uses of a constant are replaced by its value, typed as the constant's declared type.\n- A constant's type may be a `StringView` (ADR 0064 §2) or a lib/core `CStr` (§22.3, ADR 0087 §2.4), whose value is\n  a string literal or a `c\"…\"`, or a `Slice<u8>` of static bytes (below); other struct types are N0216.\n- **Constant arrays** (C9, ADR 0014): `T` may be `[E; N]` with `E` an integer type, `bool`, `StringView` or\n  `Slice<u8>` (static bytes, below), and\n  `e` an array literal `[e1, …, eN]` or repeat `[v; N]` of constant expressions. A use is the whole array's value,\n  like an array literal: it can be indexed and iterated, its `.length` read (ADR 0072), and bound to a local to be\n  sliced or borrowed. A table to\n  be sliced where it is named is a `static` instead (§39.2, ADR 0066). Arrays of other types, and of arrays, are\n  N0216.\n- **Constant views** (ADR 0064 §2): `T` may be `StringView` (or an array of them, above), with string literals as\n  the constant expressions. The value is static data, so a constant view holds no loans. The other reference types a\n  constant may have are `CStr`, `Slice<u8>` and an array of `Slice<u8>`, all holding static data; any other is N0246.\n- **Constant bytes** (ADR 0087 §2.8, §9). A constant's `Slice<u8>` is static bytes: an `embed(path)` (§10.1), or the\n  `.bytes()` of a constant view (a string literal, a `StringView` constant, an `embedText` or a constant `+`), which\n  is CeeSafe's spelling of a byte-string literal (`const MAGIC: Slice<u8> = \"\\u{7f}ELF\".bytes();`). Nothing else gives\n  that type a constant value: another call, or `.bytes()` of a view that is not constant, is N0217 (N0338 in a\n  static), and `as` cannot make one (N0208).\n- **Constant concatenation** (ADR 0087 §2.7). In a constant's value or a static initialiser (§39.4), `a + b` whose\n  operands are both constant views (a string literal, a constant's name, an `embedText`, §10.1, or another such `+`)\n  is a constant view: `a`'s bytes, then `b`'s. `A + B + C` folds left to right, and a constant of another package\n  (`dep.PREFIX`) is a constant view like any. With a `c\"…\"` (or a `CStr` constant) as the right operand, the result is\n  a `CStr<static>` whose text is `a`'s then the literal's: a NUL byte in `a` is N0006 at the `+`, as inside a `c\"…\"`.\n  A `CStr` on the left is N0207. An operand that is not constant is N0217 in a constant and N0338 in a static, and\n  so is a `+` whose result is longer than 16 MiB (16,777,216 bytes), `embed`'s limit (§10.1).\n  Anywhere else, `+` on views stays N0207: text is joined at run time by appending to a `String`.\n- `@export` on a constant makes it C data (§28.4).\n- **Associated constants** (ADR 0074 §2.7): `const NAME: T = e;`, with a visibility as a method's, may stand in a\n  struct body or an inherent `impl` block (§21.1), so an enum has them too (`impl Mode { const DEFAULT: u32 = 25; }`,\n  a block with c",
    539 =>
        "onstants only). They follow the rules above and are reached as `Type.NAME`, and from a dependent\n  package as `dep.Type.NAME` when public (N0312 otherwise). A bare `NAME` does not reach one, inside the type's\n  methods either.\n  - A constant whose name is a field, method or variant of its type, or another of its constants, is N0228.\n  - A generic struct or `impl` has none yet (N0216).\n  - An associated constant takes no attributes (N0290).\n  - An interface's constants (§21.1, ADR 0086 §2.4) become associated constants of each impl's target.\n  - An array length in a type or a repeat may name a `usize` constant, top-level or associated (§16.1, ADR 0083\n    §2.7): `[u8; LENGTH]` and `[u8; Oid.LENGTH]` are accepted.\n\n### 10.1 Embedded files (ADR 0087 §2.8)\n\n`embed(path)` is a file's bytes as a `Slice<u8>`, and `embedText(path)` the same bytes as a `StringView`, read when\nthe package is checked: `static ROOT_CERTS: Slice<u8> = embed(\"certs/root.der\");`,\n`const RUNTIME_JS: StringView = embedText(\"../js/runtime.js\");`. They are built-in functions, which a package's own\nitem of the same name shadows.\n\n- **The value** is static data, like a string literal's: it holds no loan, may be a constant's or a static's value\n  (a constant or a non-`mut` static may have the type `Slice<u8>` for it, or an array of them; §10, §39.2), is used\n  anywhere a literal is, and\n  `embedText(…)` is a constant view that constant `+` joins (§10). A use of the constant is the same bytes.\n- **The path** is a string literal or a constant view (N0362 otherwise), relative to the directory of the source file\n  that names it, with `/` separators on every system. Nothing in it is expanded. N0362 for: an empty path; an\n  absolute one (`/…`, `\\…`, `X:`); a `\\`, a `:`, a `$` or a `%` anywhere; a `~` first.\n- **Where it may lead.** The compiler resolves the path fully, every symbolic link and `..`, as the system does\n  (`realpath`; on Windows the final path of the opened